window.LESSONS = {"s1":[{"id":"s1-l1","session":"s1","title":"Source to Contract as a decision chain","summary":"Explain why value in Source to Contract is decided in the early steps, map the 11 steps of the course to the reference models, and diagnose where your own sourcing events skip the decisions that matter.","from":0,"to":25,"audio":"audio/s1-l1.mp3","duration":1636.0,"segs":[[0,0.6,22.87,"Welcome to Session 1 of the Source to Contract course. I am glad you are here. Over four sessions we will work on one question that sits underneath every sourcing project you will ever run: how do we choose the right route to market, and then run it well? Today's session is called WHAT are we sourcing? Its subtitle tells you the journey: from request to category insight."],[0,23.37,65.3,"This first video lesson covers two things. First, the opening of the session: how the course is built, what you will produce, and the real project you will carry through all four sessions. Second, module 1.1, where we look at Source to Contract as a decision chain. We will compare how the main reference authors frame the process, CIPS, van Weele, O'Brien and Monczka, and we will walk through the 11 steps we use in this course. At the end you will do a short, honest exercise on your own last three sourcing events. The video will pause from time to time with a question. Take a moment with each one. They are there to help you apply the ideas, not to catch you out."],[1,66.25,73.59,"Let me start with the sentence that carries the whole course. Source to Contract is a decision chain, not a document chain."],[1,74.09,134.34,"What does that mean? A document chain is what many teams actually run. A request arrives, someone opens a template, writes a request for quotation, collects bids, signs a contract. Every step produces a document, and when the contract is signed, the job looks done. A decision chain is different. Each step answers a question, and the answer shapes the next step. Look at the order on the slide. Category insight comes first. Then the power position: how strong are we, and how strong is the supply market? Then the sourcing strategy: how many suppliers, what kind of relationship, what competitive mechanism. And only then, at the very end, the tool. The request for quotation, the tender, the e-auction in Coupa, the negotiation. The tool is the last decision, not the first. Keep this sentence in mind. We will come back to it at the end of every module, because almost every problem we will discuss in this course comes from reversing that order."],[2,135.29,142.2,"Here is how the four sessions are built. Each one asks a question, and each one adds a part to your own Sourcing Case."],[2,142.7,182.97,"Session 1, today, asks WHAT are we sourcing? We look at the need, the category, the supply market and the power position. Session 2 asks WHICH route to market? That is the sourcing strategy itself: the six strategic decisions and the choice of route. Session 3 asks HOW do we run the event? Request for information, request for proposal, evaluation, and e-auctions in Coupa. Session 4 asks HOW do we close it well? Negotiation, the contract and the handover into daily buying. Notice the logic. The first two sessions are about deciding. The last two are about executing. We deliberately start with the decisions."],[2,183.47,206.96,"Look at the footer. About four weeks after Session 4 there is a capstone. You present your Sourcing Case to a panel, and that panel includes a business owner and a colleague from Risk or Compliance. So think of the panel from today. Would a business owner recognise the need you describe? Would a Risk colleague find the regulatory questions answered? That is the standard we are building towards."],[3,207.91,234.43,"So what will you do today? Six things. You will map how your last sourcing events really ran. You will run a structured intake and a regulatory triage. You will build a category fact base and a total cost of ownership baseline. You will read the supply market and how suppliers see us as a customer. You will position your project on the Kraljic matrix and on the Purchasing Chessboard. And you will leave with part 1 of your Sourcing Case."],[3,234.93,253.49,"Notice something about this list. Every item ends in your own project. We will use a fictional insurer called Nordvia throughout the course to illustrate the ideas, but Nordvia is only the practice ground. The real work happens on your project, with your data, your stakeholders and your contract dates."],[4,254.44,291.88,"Here is the plan for the live session, which these video lessons follow. After the opening, module 1.1 is the decision chain, the topic of this lesson. Module 1.2 is intake and need validation. Module 1.3 is knowing the category: spend, contracts, demand drivers and total cost of ownership. Module 1.4 is knowing the market. Module 1.5 is power decides strategy, where Kraljic, supplier preferencing and the Chessboard come together. Then the first case and a briefing for the fieldwork you will do before Session 2."],[5,292.83,302.79,"Now, your Sourcing Case. This is the thread that runs through the whole course, so let us be precise about what makes a good one. There are four criteria on the slide."],[5,303.29,359.42,"First, one real project. An expiring contract or a new need that will go to market in the next 6 to 18 months. Why that window? Because if it is less than six months away, the decisions are probably already made, and you will only be documenting them. If it is more than 18 months away, nobody will give you data or time. Second, big enough to matter. As a guide, above 250 thousand euros over the term of the contract. Below that, the full decision chain is often more effort than the value deserves. Third, a business owner who will give you an hour between sessions. Without that person, you cannot validate the need, you cannot get the demand drivers, and the capstone panel will see it immediately. Fourth, still open. The supplier and the price must not already be decided. If the business has already chosen the supplier, you are not sourcing, you are paperwork."],[6,360.37,394.62,"Before we go into the theory, a quick reflection. How do most sourcing events in your team start today? With a request for quotes from the business? With a contract about to expire? With a category plan? With a supplier proposal? Or with an urgency? Pause for a second and pick your honest answer. In most indirect teams, the answer is either a request that already names a supplier, or a contract that is about to expire with very little time left. Keep your answer in mind. It tells you where your decision chain starts, and often, where it is already broken."],[7,395.57,404.59,"This slide puts the two ways of running Source to Contract side by side. On the left, the document chain. On the right, the decision chain."],[7,405.09,432.32,"Read the left column with me. The request arrives with a supplier already in mind. Three quotes are collected, whatever the category. The same template is used every time. And success is defined as a contract signed. Now the right column. The need is validated and triaged first. The category and the market are understood. The strategy is chosen from the power position. And the tool is chosen to fit the strategy."],[7,432.82,482.93,"Here is a small story to make it concrete. A marketing team asks Procurement for three quotes for a new creative agency. They have already met one agency and they like it. In the document chain, the buyer finds two more agencies, collects quotes, and the favourite wins, because the brief was written around its pitch. In the decision chain, the buyer first asks: how many agencies do we already use? Six, it turns out, with overlapping briefs and no rate card. The real need is not a seventh agency. It is a structure: a small panel with a rate card and clear rules for who gets which brief. Same request, very different outcome. If you are honest, which column describes your last event? Most experienced buyers say the left one. That is not a criticism. It is the starting point of this course."],[8,483.88,492.01,"Where is the course heading? Towards four basic routes to market. This is only a preview. Today we earn the right to choose between them."],[8,492.51,536.95,"The first route is one supplier by tender. Think of cleaning, security or soft facilities management, retendered about every three years. The second is a panel of preferred suppliers. That fits consulting, IT services and agencies, with a rate card and mini-competitions for each assignment. The third is a sole-source negotiation. This is the incumbent software vendor, where there is no real competition today, and the levers are your best alternative to a negotiated agreement, usage data and timing. The fourth is a request for quotation, an e-auction or a catalogue. That fits commodities with enough bidders, and the long tail of small purchases through catalogues."],[8,537.45,555.2,"Look at how different these are. A tender for cleaning and a negotiation with a software vendor have almost nothing in common, except that both end in a contract. A team that uses one tool for all four is not being efficient. It is using the wrong tool three times out of four."],[9,556.15,568.38,"Let us now open module 1.1: Source to Contract as a decision chain. Two questions guide this module. Where is value decided? And how do the reference models frame the process?"],[10,569.33,592.65,"Let us start by placing Source to Contract next to its neighbour, Procure to Pay. On the left, Source to Contract: need, category and market analysis, then the sourcing strategy, then the request, evaluation and negotiation, and finally award and contract. On the right, Procure to Pay: requisition and approval, purchase order, receipt, invoice and payment."],[10,593.15,631.38,"Here is the key idea. Value is decided in Source to Contract. Procure to Pay protects it. If you sign a contract at the wrong price, with the wrong supplier, or with a specification that is far more than you need, no amount of clean invoicing will fix it. On the other hand, a great contract that nobody uses, because buyers keep ordering outside it, loses its value in Procure to Pay. So both matter. But the decisions that create value sit on the left. Coupa covers both sides, which is exactly why the handover between them matters so much. We will come back to that handover in Session 4."],[11,632.33,645.41,"Now the reference models, starting with CIPS, the Chartered Institute of Procurement and Supply. CIPS describes a Procurement and Supply Cycle with 13 stages. The slide groups them into three blocks."],[11,645.91,675.05,"Stages 1 to 4 are specification, analysis, strategy and a market test. That is the insight and strategy part of Source to Contract. Stages 5 to 9 are documentation, criteria, tender, evaluation and contract. That is the event itself. Stages 10 to 13 are delivery, performance, supplier management and asset management. That is contract management and supplier relationship management, after the contract is signed."],[11,675.55,708.22,"Now look at the footer, because it is the most important line on the slide. At the Strategy stage, CIPS chooses between competitive tendering and other approaches, based on supply position. Read that again. A tender is not the default. It is one option, and the choice depends on the supply position: how many credible suppliers there are, how much power they have, how attractive we are to them. That idea is the bridge to module 1.5 later today. Supply position comes before the tender decision."],[12,709.17,721.05,"The second reference is Arjan van Weele, in the eighth edition of his textbook with Frank Rozemeijer, published in 2022. Van Weele splits the purchasing process into two parts."],[12,721.55,742.18,"Tactical purchasing comes first: specify, select the supplier, contract. Operational purchasing comes after: order, expedite, evaluate. The lesson he draws is simple and powerful. The specification locks in most of the cost and the risk. And the consequence: mistakes upstream cannot be repaired downstream."],[12,742.68,787.12,"Think about what this means in an indirect category. Take office cleaning. If the specification says every office is cleaned every night, regardless of occupancy, you have locked in the cost before a single supplier has seen the tender. The best negotiator in the world can win you a few percent on the hourly rate. But a specification based on outputs, cleaning by need and by occupancy, can change the volume itself. Or take software. If the specification says every employee needs the premium licence tier, the negotiation is already lost. So here is a question for you. When did you last change a specification rather than a price? If you cannot remember, that is van Weele's point exactly."],[13,788.07,803.54,"The third reference is Jonathan O'Brien, whose book Category Management in Purchasing is now in its fifth edition, published in 2024. O'Brien describes category management in five stages, often called the five i's."],[13,804.04,824.8,"Initiation is setting up: the team, the scope and the sponsor. Insight is the analysis: data, market, stakeholders and power. Innovation is where the sourcing strategy is created. Implementation is the event, the contract and the transition. And Improvement is performance management and, eventually, renewal."],[13,825.3,858.0,"Notice where the strategy sits. It is the output of Innovation, and Innovation cannot start without Insight. You cannot invent a good strategy for a category you do not understand. That gives a name to everything we do today. Today is the Insight stage. The intake, the spend cube, the contract inventory, the market analysis and the power position are all Insight. Session 2 is Innovation. And the request for proposal, the auction and the contract in Sessions 3 and 4 are Implementation."],[14,858.95,872.14,"The fourth reference is the strategic sourcing model taught by Monczka, Handfield, Giunipero and Patterson in their textbook Purchasing and Supply Chain Management. Their model has four ideas on this slide."],[14,872.64,898.09,"First, it is iterative. You plan, profile the spend, assess the market and develop the strategy, and you may go round that loop more than once as you learn. Then you execute: request, negotiate, award, transition and measure. Third, segmentation. The portfolio position of the category drives the sourcing approach. And fourth, the failure mode they warn against: one approach for every category."],[14,898.59,932.83,"Step back and compare the four authors. CIPS says the strategy depends on supply position. Van Weele says early decisions lock in cost. O'Brien says insight before innovation. Monczka says segment first, and do not use one approach for everything. That is the same message Peter Kraljic gave in his famous 1983 article on purchasing portfolios. Four different vocabularies, one shared conclusion: understand the category and its power position before you choose how to go to market."],[15,933.78,943.64,"Now let me show you the 11 steps we use in this course. This slide has the first five. Each row has the step, the key decision, and where it touches Coupa."],[15,944.14,1000.34,"Step 1 is need validation. The key decisions: is the need real, is there an existing contract, and what does the regulatory triage say? In Coupa, this is the intake request. Step 2 is category and spend analysis. The decision is about boundaries: what is in the category and what is out, and should we bundle demand across areas or entities? In Coupa, spend analytics and the contract repository. Step 3 is supply market analysis. How much power do the suppliers have, and how attractive are we to them? Coupa offers the request for information event as a tool here. Step 4 is portfolio positioning: Kraljic, supplier preferencing and the Chessboard. That gives you the direction, recorded in the category strategy. And step 5 is the sourcing strategy itself, with six decisions and a route to market, written up in a strategy paper."],[15,1000.84,1014.58,"Steps 1 to 4 are today. Step 5 is the whole of Session 2. Notice that steps 1 to 4 are all about knowing, and step 5 is about choosing. You cannot do step 5 well if steps 1 to 4 are guesses."],[16,1015.53,1071.51,"Here are the remaining six steps. Step 6 is the design of the request. The specification, the lots and the evaluation weights are fixed before the event is issued, using an event template and lots in Coupa. Step 7 is supplier engagement: equal treatment of all bidders and a fair question and answer process, through the Message Center. Step 8 is evaluation: the shortlist, the handling of abnormally low bids, and due diligence, using scoring and Supplier Information Management. Step 9 is negotiation: your best alternative, the rounds, and a best and final offer or an auction phase. Step 10 is award and contract: approval, clauses and registers, in contract lifecycle management. And step 11 is the handover: making sure the contract is usable and actually used, through catalogues and contract-backed buying."],[16,1072.01,1093.33,"Steps 6 to 8 are Session 3. Steps 9 to 11 are Session 4. One thing to notice on this slide: step 11 is part of Source to Contract. A contract that sits in a drawer while the business keeps buying elsewhere has not delivered its value. Handover is the last link of the decision chain, not an administrative afterthought."],[17,1094.28,1104.77,"Here is an uncomfortable truth. Steps 1 to 5 produce almost nothing a supplier sees. That is why they get skipped. And that is where the route to market is decided."],[17,1105.27,1148.82,"Think about the pressure a buyer is under. The business wants a contract. Steps 6 to 10 produce visible things: a tender pack, bids, a scoring sheet, a signed contract. Everyone can see progress. Steps 1 to 5 produce a validated need, a spend analysis, a market view and a strategy paper. Useful, but invisible to the outside world, and easy to sacrifice when time is short. Which step produces the fewest documents in your team? It is usually step 4 or step 5, the positioning and the strategy. And those are precisely the steps where the choice between a tender, a panel, a negotiation or an auction is made. When they are skipped, the choice is made by habit."],[18,1149.77,1168.56,"Let me introduce the course case. Nordvia is a fictional mid-sized insurer, part of a European insurance group. Its Procurement team of 9 runs about 60 sourcing events a year in Coupa. And until now, most of them were requests for quotation with three quotes, whatever the category."],[18,1169.06,1194.13,"Look at the three examples on the slide. Office cleaning: three quotes, and the contracts have been rolled over since 2019. IT consulting: 38 call-offs and no framework, so every engagement is a separate small purchase. Core software from a vendor called Kelvar: renewed with a 9 percent uplift. And the last line sums it up. Same tool, three very different power positions."],[18,1194.63,1243.43,"Why does that matter? Because for cleaning, three quotes is too little: a well-designed tender with an output-based specification could change the whole cost base. For IT consulting, three quotes per call-off is the wrong level entirely: the problem is the missing structure of a panel and a rate card. And for Kelvar, three quotes is meaningless, because there is only one vendor who can renew those licences. What Nordvia needed there was a negotiation strategy, with usage data and a credible alternative. Nordvia's team is not lazy or incompetent. It is using one familiar tool everywhere. This is the starting point of the course case. At the end of the session we will return to Nordvia's eight pipeline projects and decide which one to start first."],[19,1244.38,1257.18,"What does good look like? Here is a benchmark from The Hackett Group, a consultancy, published in 2024. It compares what Hackett calls Digital World Class procurement organisations with their peers."],[19,1257.68,1275.6,"Four numbers. 23 percent shorter sourcing cycle times. Twice the spend-cost-reduction savings. 59 percent less maverick buying, meaning less buying outside contracts and agreed channels. And 3.7 times fewer suppliers per billion dollars of spend."],[19,1276.1,1314.16,"Now, a word of caution, and the footer says it clearly. This is consultancy benchmark data, not peer-reviewed research. So treat these numbers as a direction, not as targets you promise your management. But look at the first number with me, because it holds a lesson. The best teams have shorter cycle times. Does that mean they skip the analysis? No. They are faster because they know the category before the request arrives. They already have the spend cube, the contract dates and the market view. When the business calls, they are not starting from zero. Insight is not what slows sourcing down. Missing insight is."],[20,1315.11,1321.72,"Now let us bring it home to our own Group. These figures are anonymised and rounded, but they tell a clear story."],[20,1322.22,1366.32,"First, only about 2 percent of addressable spend is under a Group contract today. That is a very low number, and it means there is a lot of demand we buy separately that we could buy together. Second, about 800 supplier relationships above 50 thousand euros a year carry about 88 percent of the spend. That is the negotiable universe. That is where a decision chain pays back. Third, 60 to 70 percent of a mature sourcing plan comes from expiring contracts. In other words, most of next year's work is already visible in today's contract end dates, if anybody looks. And fourth, about 6 percent per contract cycle is a planning assumption for procurement value."],[20,1366.82,1401.66,"Be careful with that last number. The footer explains it: it is a planning assumption, made up of about 2 percent cost savings plus 4 percent cost avoidance over a three-year contract cycle. It is not a result anyone has measured. It is useful for sizing a pipeline, not for promising savings. And what about the thousands of small suppliers below 50 thousand euros? For them, the question is not which sourcing strategy, but which channel: a catalogue, a spot buy, a purchasing card. The tail is a channel question, not a sourcing one."],[21,1402.61,1432.82,"Another moment of reflection. Who usually chooses the sourcing tool, the request for quotation, the request for proposal or the auction, in your team today? Is it the business, before Procurement is even called? The buyer, by habit or by template? The buyer, after a category analysis? A policy threshold? Or nobody explicitly? Think about your honest answer. If it is anything other than the buyer after a category analysis, the tool is being chosen before the strategy."],[22,1433.77,1453.7,"And one more. Which step of Source to Contract does your team skip or rush most often? Need validation? Spend and contract analysis? Market analysis? Positioning and strategy? Or the handover to Procure to Pay? Hold that answer, because the exercise that follows asks you to check it against real events."],[23,1454.65,1468.75,"Now it is your turn. This is the exercise for module 1.1, and it is in your workbook, under module 1.1 in the Modules tab. The question is: how did your last three sourcing events really run?"],[23,1469.25,1491.8,"Here is what to do. First, name your last three sourcing events, the category and the year. Second, for each of the 11 steps, mark whether it was done properly, rushed, or skipped. Third, look across the three events and find the step you skip most often. Fourth, note one event where the tool came before the strategy, and write down what a better route would have been."],[23,1492.3,1512.4,"Be honest. Nobody sees this unless you choose to share it, and the value of the exercise is entirely in its honesty. In the live session this takes about 20 minutes: 12 minutes on your own and 8 minutes comparing in groups of three. If you want to do it now, pause the video, open your workbook, and come back when you are done."],[24,1513.35,1530.93,"Welcome back. Let us debrief with four questions. Which steps were most often rushed or skipped? What did skipping them cost you: price, risk, time or relationship? Where was the tool chosen before the strategy? And what would have to change for steps 1 to 5 to happen every time?"],[24,1531.43,1590.15,"When groups do this exercise, a pattern usually appears. Steps 2 to 5 dominate the skipped column: the spend analysis, the market analysis, the positioning and the strategy. Step 1 is often rushed, because the need arrives already formed. Steps 6 to 10 are almost always done, because they produce the documents. The costs are also typical. A price that was higher than it needed to be, because a framework already existed. A risk that appeared late, because nobody asked the regulatory question at the start. Time lost, because a notice period was missed and the contract rolled over. And sometimes a relationship strained, because the incumbent was treated as interchangeable when it was not. And the last question, what would have to change, usually leads to two answers: earlier involvement, and having the category insight ready before the request arrives. That is exactly what the rest of today is about."],[25,1591.1,1616.66,"Let me recap module 1.1 in three lines. First, Source to Contract is a decision chain of 11 steps, and value is decided early. Second, CIPS, van Weele and O'Brien agree: insight comes before strategy, and Monczka and Kraljic add that you must segment before you choose an approach. Third, Nordvia's habit, three quotes for everything, is the problem this course sets out to solve."],[25,1617.16,1634.76,"So where does the decision chain begin? With step 1, the intake. In the next lesson we look at how to start right: how to validate a need, check whether it is already covered, think in total contract value, and run the regulatory triage that every insurer needs on day one. See you there."]],"questions":[{"after":5,"q":"A colleague proposes, as their Sourcing Case, a marketing tool renewal that goes to market in three months, where the CMO has already agreed the price with the incumbent. What is the main problem?","options":["The value is probably too low for the course","The project is not still open: supplier and price are already decided","Marketing projects are not suitable for Source to Contract","There is no Risk colleague on the panel"],"answer":1,"explain":"A good Sourcing Case must still be open. If the supplier and the price are already agreed, the decisions have been made and the case would only document them. The short time window is a warning sign for the same reason.","t":359.42},{"after":8,"q":"Nordvia uses a core software vendor for which no credible alternative can be switched to in the short term. Which of the four routes is the most likely fit?","options":["One supplier by tender","A panel of preferred suppliers","A sole-source negotiation built on usage data, timing and an alternative","An e-auction among three bidders"],"answer":2,"explain":"With an incumbent and no real competition today, there is nothing to auction or tender. The route is a sole-source negotiation, using usage data, timing to the renewal, and a credible alternative as leverage.","t":555.2},{"after":12,"q":"A Facilities manager asks you to negotiate a 5 percent reduction in the cleaning hourly rate. Following van Weele, which question should you raise first?","options":["Whether the specification, for example cleaning frequency, matches real occupancy","Whether the supplier can invoice monthly instead of quarterly","Whether the purchase order approval workflow is correct","Whether the incumbent will accept a longer term"],"answer":0,"explain":"Van Weele's point is that the specification locks in most of the cost and risk. Changing what is cleaned and how often can move the cost far more than a rate negotiation, and it must be decided before going to market.","t":787.12},{"after":14,"q":"Which statement best captures what CIPS, van Weele, O'Brien and Monczka have in common?","options":["A competitive tender is always the safest route","Procure to Pay is where most value is created","Understand the category and its power position before choosing how to go to market","Every category should use the same template to ensure fairness"],"answer":2,"explain":"All four frameworks put insight and segmentation before the choice of approach. CIPS chooses based on supply position, van Weele stresses early decisions, O'Brien puts Insight before Innovation, and Monczka warns against one approach for all.","t":932.83},{"after":16,"q":"A buyer is deciding whether to bundle demand for advisory services across three entities before any market activity. Which of the 11 steps is this decision part of?","options":["Step 2, category and spend analysis","Step 6, RFx design","Step 9, negotiation","Step 11, handover"],"answer":0,"explain":"Category boundaries and whether to bundle demand across areas or entities are the key decisions of step 2. They come long before the request is designed or any negotiation starts.","t":1093.33},{"after":18,"q":"Nordvia's IT consulting spend runs through 38 separate call-offs with no framework. Why is collecting three quotes for each call-off the wrong answer?","options":["Three quotes is too few; it should be five","The real problem is a missing structure, such as a panel and a rate card, not a missing quote","IT consulting should always be sole-sourced","Call-offs cannot be managed in Coupa"],"answer":1,"explain":"More quotes per call-off would repeat the same small, unstructured purchase. The decision chain points to a structural answer for the whole category, such as a panel with a rate card and clear call-off rules.","t":1243.43},{"after":20,"q":"Your manager wants to promise the board 6 percent savings on every contract in next year's pipeline. Based on the Group figures, what is the best response?","options":["Agree, because 6 percent is a measured Group result","Suggest promising 23 percent, as in the Hackett benchmark","Recommend focusing effort on suppliers below 50 thousand euros instead","Explain that 6 percent is a planning assumption combining savings and avoidance over a cycle, useful for sizing, not a promise"],"answer":3,"explain":"The 6 percent is a planning assumption of about 2 percent savings plus 4 percent avoidance over three years, not a result. Hackett figures are consultancy benchmarks, and the tail below 50 thousand euros is a channel question, not a sourcing one.","t":1401.66},{"after":24,"q":"In the exercise, you find that in all three of your last events the tool was chosen before any market or power analysis. What does this most directly put at risk?","options":["The route to market, because it was chosen by habit rather than from the power position","The invoice matching in Procure to Pay","The equal treatment of bidders in the question and answer phase","The legal validity of the contract"],"answer":0,"explain":"Steps 1 to 5 are where the route to market is decided. If the tool comes first, the choice between a tender, a panel, a negotiation or an auction is made by habit, and may be the wrong one for the category's power position.","t":1590.15}],"cues":[[0.6,3.98,"Welcome to Session 1 of the Source to Contract course."],[3.98,5.55,"I am glad you are here."],[5.55,15.67,"Over four sessions we will work on one question that sits underneath every sourcing project you will ever run: how do we choose the right route to market, and then run it well?"],[15.67,18.43,"Today's session is called WHAT are we sourcing?"],[18.71,22.87,"Its subtitle tells you the journey: from request to category insight."],[23.37,26.04,"This first video lesson covers two things."],[26.04,35.42,"First, the opening of the session: how the course is built, what you will produce, and the real project you will carry through all four sessions."],[35.42,40.16,"Second, module 1.1, where we look at Source to Contract as a decision chain."],[40.44,51.01,"We will compare how the main reference authors frame the process, CIPS, van Weele, O'Brien and Monczka, and we will walk through the 11 steps we use in this course."],[51.01,56.55,"At the end you will do a short, honest exercise on your own last three sourcing events."],[56.55,59.72,"The video will pause from time to time with a question."],[60.0,61.67,"Take a moment with each one."],[61.67,65.3,"They are there to help you apply the ideas, not to catch you out."],[66.25,69.45,"Let me start with the sentence that carries the whole course."],[69.45,73.59,"Source to Contract is a decision chain, not a document chain."],[74.09,75.09,"What does that mean?"],[75.09,78.53,"A document chain is what many teams actually run."],[78.53,85.81,"A request arrives, someone opens a template, writes a request for quotation, collects bids, signs a contract."],[85.81,91.27,"Every step produces a document, and when the contract is signed, the job looks done."],[91.27,93.2,"A decision chain is different."],[93.48,97.18,"Each step answers a question, and the answer shapes the next step."],[97.18,99.81,"Look at the order on the slide."],[99.81,101.38,"Category insight comes first."],[101.38,106.59,"Then the power position: how strong are we, and how strong is the supply market?"],[106.59,112.98,"Then the sourcing strategy: how many suppliers, what kind of relationship, what competitive mechanism."],[113.26,115.83,"And only then, at the very end, the tool."],[115.83,121.2,"The request for quotation, the tender, the e-auction in Coupa, the negotiation."],[121.2,124.29,"The tool is the last decision, not the first."],[124.29,126.17,"Keep this sentence in mind."],[126.45,134.34,"We will come back to it at the end of every module, because almost every problem we will discuss in this course comes from reversing that order."],[135.29,137.66,"Here is how the four sessions are built."],[137.66,142.2,"Each one asks a question, and each one adds a part to your own Sourcing Case."],[142.7,145.65,"Session 1, today, asks WHAT are we sourcing?"],[145.65,150.02,"We look at the need, the category, the supply market and the power position."],[150.02,152.73,"Session 2 asks WHICH route to market?"],[152.73,158.38,"That is the sourcing strategy itself: the six strategic decisions and the choice of route."],[158.38,161.2,"Session 3 asks HOW do we run the event?"],[161.48,167.23,"Request for information, request for proposal, evaluation, and e-auctions in Coupa."],[167.23,170.18,"Session 4 asks HOW do we close it well?"],[170.18,174.38,"Negotiation, the contract and the handover into daily buying."],[174.38,175.62,"Notice the logic."],[175.62,178.53,"The first two sessions are about deciding."],[178.53,180.55,"The last two are about executing."],[180.83,182.96,"We deliberately start with the decisions."],[183.47,184.54,"Look at the footer."],[184.54,188.16,"About four weeks after Session 4 there is a capstone."],[188.16,195.45,"You present your Sourcing Case to a panel, and that panel includes a business owner and a colleague from Risk or Compliance."],[195.45,197.58,"So think of the panel from today."],[197.58,200.62,"Would a business owner recognise the need you describe?"],[200.9,204.41,"Would a Risk colleague find the regulatory questions answered?"],[204.41,206.96,"That is the standard we are building towards."],[207.91,209.42,"So what will you do today?"],[209.42,210.01,"Six things."],[210.01,213.87,"You will map how your last sourcing events really ran."],[213.87,217.68,"You will run a structured intake and a regulatory triage."],[217.68,222.37,"You will build a category fact base and a total cost of ownership baseline."],[222.37,226.6,"You will read the supply market and how suppliers see us as a customer."],[226.88,231.71,"You will position your project on the Kraljic matrix and on the Purchasing Chessboard."],[231.71,234.43,"And you will leave with part 1 of your Sourcing Case."],[234.93,236.76,"Notice something about this list."],[236.76,239.11,"Every item ends in your own project."],[239.11,247.49,"We will use a fictional insurer called Nordvia throughout the course to illustrate the ideas, but Nordvia is only the practice ground."],[247.49,253.49,"The real work happens on your project, with your data, your stakeholders and your contract dates."],[254.44,259.95,"Here is the plan for the live session, which these video lessons follow."],[259.95,265.65,"After the opening, module 1.1 is the decision chain, the topic of this lesson."],[265.65,268.31,"Module 1.2 is intake and need validation."],[268.31,275.82,"Module 1.3 is knowing the category: spend, contracts, demand drivers and total cost of ownership."],[276.1,278.44,"Module 1.4 is knowing the market."],[278.44,286.19,"Module 1.5 is power decides strategy, where Kraljic, supplier preferencing and the Chessboard come together."],[286.19,291.88,"Then the first case and a briefing for the fieldwork you will do before Session 2."],[292.83,294.62,"Now, your Sourcing Case."],[294.62,300.35,"This is the thread that runs through the whole course, so let us be precise about what makes a good one."],[300.35,302.79,"There are four criteria on the slide."],[303.29,305.12,"First, one real project."],[305.12,310.33,"An expiring contract or a new need that will go to market in the next 6 to 18 months."],[310.33,312.25,"Why that window?"],[312.25,319.58,"Because if it is less than six months away, the decisions are probably already made, and you will only be documenting them."],[319.58,324.22,"If it is more than 18 months away, nobody will give you data or time."],[324.5,326.34,"Second, big enough to matter."],[326.34,331.13,"As a guide, above 250 thousand euros over the term of the contract."],[331.13,336.84,"Below that, the full decision chain is often more effort than the value deserves."],[336.84,340.9,"Third, a business owner who will give you an hour between sessions."],[341.18,349.54,"Without that person, you cannot validate the need, you cannot get the demand drivers, and the capstone panel will see it immediately."],[349.54,350.71,"Fourth, still open."],[350.71,354.25,"The supplier and the price must not already be decided."],[354.25,359.42,"If the business has already chosen the supplier, you are not sourcing, you are paperwork."],[360.37,363.17,"Before we go into the theory, a quick reflection."],[363.17,366.73,"How do most sourcing events in your team start today?"],[366.73,369.53,"With a request for quotes from the business?"],[369.53,371.66,"With a contract about to expire?"],[371.66,372.74,"With a category plan?"],[372.74,373.95,"With a supplier proposal?"],[373.95,375.55,"Or with an urgency?"],[375.55,378.27,"Pause for a second and pick your honest answer."],[378.55,387.88,"In most indirect teams, the answer is either a request that already names a supplier, or a contract that is about to expire with very little time left."],[387.88,389.78,"Keep your answer in mind."],[389.78,394.61,"It tells you where your decision chain starts, and often, where it is already broken."],[395.57,399.94,"This slide puts the two ways of running Source to Contract side by side."],[399.94,402.36,"On the left, the document chain."],[402.36,404.59,"On the right, the decision chain."],[405.09,406.95,"Read the left column with me."],[406.95,410.41,"The request arrives with a supplier already in mind."],[410.41,413.49,"Three quotes are collected, whatever the category."],[413.49,416.19,"The same template is used every time."],[416.19,419.26,"And success is defined as a contract signed."],[419.26,420.94,"Now the right column."],[420.94,423.76,"The need is validated and triaged first."],[424.04,426.63,"The category and the market are understood."],[426.63,429.6,"The strategy is chosen from the power position."],[429.6,432.31,"And the tool is chosen to fit the strategy."],[432.82,435.37,"Here is a small story to make it concrete."],[435.37,440.22,"A marketing team asks Procurement for three quotes for a new creative agency."],[440.22,443.21,"They have already met one agency and they like it."],[443.21,451.85,"In the document chain, the buyer finds two more agencies, collects quotes, and the favourite wins, because the brief was written around its pitch."],[452.13,457.72,"In the decision chain, the buyer first asks: how many agencies do we already use?"],[457.72,461.47,"Six, it turns out, with overlapping briefs and no rate card."],[461.47,464.34,"The real need is not a seventh agency."],[464.34,469.82,"It is a structure: a small panel with a rate card and clear rules for who gets which brief."],[469.82,472.44,"Same request, very different outcome."],[472.72,475.97,"If you are honest, which column describes your last event?"],[475.97,478.86,"Most experienced buyers say the left one."],[478.86,480.69,"That is not a criticism."],[480.69,482.94,"It is the starting point of this course."],[483.88,485.32,"Where is the course heading?"],[485.32,487.88,"Towards four basic routes to market."],[487.88,488.92,"This is only a preview."],[488.92,492.01,"Today we earn the right to choose between them."],[492.51,495.19,"The first route is one supplier by tender."],[495.19,501.52,"Think of cleaning, security or soft facilities management, retendered about every three years."],[501.52,504.62,"The second is a panel of preferred suppliers."],[504.62,511.86,"That fits consulting, IT services and agencies, with a rate card and mini-competitions for each assignment."],[512.14,514.99,"The third is a sole-source negotiation."],[514.99,526.97,"This is the incumbent software vendor, where there is no real competition today, and the levers are your best alternative to a negotiated agreement, usage data and timing."],[526.97,531.17,"The fourth is a request for quotation, an e-auction or a catalogue."],[531.45,536.95,"That fits commodities with enough bidders, and the long tail of small purchases through catalogues."],[537.45,539.61,"Look at how different these are."],[539.61,547.74,"A tender for cleaning and a negotiation with a software vendor have almost nothing in common, except that both end in a contract."],[547.74,552.12,"A team that uses one tool for all four is not being efficient."],[552.12,555.2,"It is using the wrong tool three times out of four."],[556.15,560.23,"Let us now open module 1.1: Source to Contract as a decision chain."],[560.23,562.72,"Two questions guide this module."],[562.72,564.0,"Where is value decided?"],[564.0,568.37,"And how do the reference models frame the process?"],[569.33,574.94,"Let us start by placing Source to Contract next to its neighbour, Procure to Pay."],[574.94,586.25,"On the left, Source to Contract: need, category and market analysis, then the sourcing strategy, then the request, evaluation and negotiation, and finally award and contract."],[586.53,592.65,"On the right, Procure to Pay: requisition and approval, purchase order, receipt, invoice and payment."],[593.15,594.83,"Here is the key idea."],[594.83,597.36,"Value is decided in Source to Contract."],[597.36,598.75,"Procure to Pay protects it."],[598.75,609.45,"If you sign a contract at the wrong price, with the wrong supplier, or with a specification that is far more than you need, no amount of clean invoicing will fix it."],[609.73,617.93,"On the other hand, a great contract that nobody uses, because buyers keep ordering outside it, loses its value in Procure to Pay."],[617.93,619.45,"So both matter."],[619.45,622.54,"But the decisions that create value sit on the left."],[622.54,628.5,"Coupa covers both sides, which is exactly why the handover between them matters so much."],[628.78,631.38,"We will come back to that handover in Session 4."],[632.33,638.75,"Now the reference models, starting with CIPS, the Chartered Institute of Procurement and Supply."],[638.75,642.88,"CIPS describes a Procurement and Supply Cycle with 13 stages."],[642.88,645.41,"The slide groups them into three blocks."],[645.91,651.21,"Stages 1 to 4 are specification, analysis, strategy and a market test."],[651.21,654.73,"That is the insight and strategy part of Source to Contract."],[654.73,661.14,"Stages 5 to 9 are documentation, criteria, tender, evaluation and contract."],[661.14,663.11,"That is the event itself."],[663.11,669.06,"Stages 10 to 13 are delivery, performance, supplier management and asset management."],[669.34,675.05,"That is contract management and supplier relationship management, after the contract is signed."],[675.55,680.11,"Now look at the footer, because it is the most important line on the slide."],[680.11,687.21,"At the Strategy stage, CIPS chooses between competitive tendering and other approaches, based on supply position."],[687.21,689.0,"Read that again."],[689.0,690.7,"A tender is not the default."],[690.98,702.06,"It is one option, and the choice depends on the supply position: how many credible suppliers there are, how much power they have, how attractive we are to them."],[702.06,705.25,"That idea is the bridge to module 1.5 later today."],[705.25,708.21,"Supply position comes before the tender decision."],[709.17,717.51,"The second reference is Arjan van Weele, in the eighth edition of his textbook with Frank Rozemeijer, published in 2022."],[717.51,721.05,"Van Weele splits the purchasing process into two parts."],[721.55,726.48,"Tactical purchasing comes first: specify, select the supplier, contract."],[726.48,730.82,"Operational purchasing comes after: order, expedite, evaluate."],[730.82,734.2,"The lesson he draws is simple and powerful."],[734.2,737.5,"The specification locks in most of the cost and the risk."],[737.5,742.18,"And the consequence: mistakes upstream cannot be repaired downstream."],[742.68,745.55,"Think about what this means in an indirect category."],[745.55,747.2,"Take office cleaning."],[747.2,757.24,"If the specification says every office is cleaned every night, regardless of occupancy, you have locked in the cost before a single supplier has seen the tender."],[757.24,762.2,"The best negotiator in the world can win you a few percent on the hourly rate."],[762.48,769.24,"But a specification based on outputs, cleaning by need and by occupancy, can change the volume itself."],[769.24,770.85,"Or take software."],[770.85,777.92,"If the specification says every employee needs the premium licence tier, the negotiation is already lost."],[777.92,778.95,"So here is a question for you."],[778.95,783.56,"When did you last change a specification rather than a price?"],[783.84,787.12,"If you cannot remember, that is van Weele's point exactly."],[788.07,797.74,"The third reference is Jonathan O'Brien, whose book Category Management in Purchasing is now in its fifth edition, published in 2024."],[797.74,803.54,"O'Brien describes category management in five stages, often called the five i's."],[804.04,808.14,"Initiation is setting up: the team, the scope and the sponsor."],[808.14,813.02,"Insight is the analysis: data, market, stakeholders and power."],[813.02,816.43,"Innovation is where the sourcing strategy is created."],[816.43,820.52,"Implementation is the event, the contract and the transition."],[820.52,824.8,"And Improvement is performance management and, eventually, renewal."],[825.3,827.33,"Notice where the strategy sits."],[827.33,832.33,"It is the output of Innovation, and Innovation cannot start without Insight."],[832.33,836.98,"You cannot invent a good strategy for a category you do not understand."],[836.98,839.77,"That gives a name to everything we do today."],[839.77,841.94,"Today is the Insight stage."],[842.22,849.26,"The intake, the spend cube, the contract inventory, the market analysis and the power position are all Insight."],[849.26,851.7,"Session 2 is Innovation."],[851.7,858.01,"And the request for proposal, the auction and the contract in Sessions 3 and 4 are Implementation."],[858.95,869.29,"The fourth reference is the strategic sourcing model taught by Monczka, Handfield, Giunipero and Patterson in their textbook Purchasing and Supply Chain Management."],[869.29,872.13,"Their model has four ideas on this slide."],[872.64,874.16,"First, it is iterative."],[874.16,882.91,"You plan, profile the spend, assess the market and develop the strategy, and you may go round that loop more than once as you learn."],[882.91,886.97,"Then you execute: request, negotiate, award, transition and measure."],[886.97,889.16,"Third, segmentation."],[889.16,893.1,"The portfolio position of the category drives the sourcing approach."],[893.38,898.09,"And fourth, the failure mode they warn against: one approach for every category."],[898.59,901.49,"Step back and compare the four authors."],[901.49,904.4,"CIPS says the strategy depends on supply position."],[904.4,907.82,"Van Weele says early decisions lock in cost."],[907.82,910.88,"O'Brien says insight before innovation."],[910.88,915.44,"Monczka says segment first, and do not use one approach for everything."],[915.72,922.07,"That is the same message Peter Kraljic gave in his famous 1983 article on purchasing portfolios."],[922.07,932.83,"Four different vocabularies, one shared conclusion: understand the category and its power position before you choose how to go to market."],[933.78,937.07,"Now let me show you the 11 steps we use in this course."],[937.07,939.39,"This slide has the first five."],[939.39,943.64,"Each row has the step, the key decision, and where it touches Coupa."],[944.14,946.4,"Step 1 is need validation."],[946.4,953.89,"The key decisions: is the need real, is there an existing contract, and what does the regulatory triage say?"],[953.89,956.24,"In Coupa, this is the intake request."],[956.24,959.16,"Step 2 is category and spend analysis."],[959.44,967.96,"The decision is about boundaries: what is in the category and what is out, and should we bundle demand across areas or entities?"],[967.96,971.57,"In Coupa, spend analytics and the contract repository."],[971.57,973.07,"Step 3 is supply market analysis."],[973.07,978.28,"How much power do the suppliers have, and how attractive are we to them?"],[978.56,982.37,"Coupa offers the request for information event as a tool here."],[982.37,988.05,"Step 4 is portfolio positioning: Kraljic, supplier preferencing and the Chessboard."],[988.05,992.34,"That gives you the direction, recorded in the category strategy."],[992.62,1000.34,"And step 5 is the sourcing strategy itself, with six decisions and a route to market, written up in a strategy paper."],[1000.84,1002.72,"Steps 1 to 4 are today."],[1002.72,1005.55,"Step 5 is the whole of Session 2."],[1005.55,1010.97,"Notice that steps 1 to 4 are all about knowing, and step 5 is about choosing."],[1010.97,1014.58,"You cannot do step 5 well if steps 1 to 4 are guesses."],[1015.53,1017.5,"Here are the remaining six steps."],[1017.5,1020.49,"Step 6 is the design of the request."],[1020.49,1029.27,"The specification, the lots and the evaluation weights are fixed before the event is issued, using an event template and lots in Coupa."],[1029.55,1038.49,"Step 7 is supplier engagement: equal treatment of all bidders and a fair question and answer process, through the Message Center."],[1038.49,1047.83,"Step 8 is evaluation: the shortlist, the handling of abnormally low bids, and due diligence, using scoring and Supplier Information Management."],[1048.11,1055.94,"Step 9 is negotiation: your best alternative, the rounds, and a best and final offer or an auction phase."],[1055.94,1062.81,"Step 10 is award and contract: approval, clauses and registers, in contract lifecycle management."],[1063.09,1071.52,"And step 11 is the handover: making sure the contract is usable and actually used, through catalogues and contract-backed buying."],[1072.01,1073.56,"Steps 6 to 8 are Session 3."],[1073.56,1076.84,"Steps 9 to 11 are Session 4."],[1076.84,1080.38,"One thing to notice on this slide: step 11 is part of Source to Contract."],[1080.38,1087.36,"A contract that sits in a drawer while the business keeps buying elsewhere has not delivered its value."],[1087.36,1093.32,"Handover is the last link of the decision chain, not an administrative afterthought."],[1094.28,1096.31,"Here is an uncomfortable truth."],[1096.31,1100.16,"Steps 1 to 5 produce almost nothing a supplier sees."],[1100.16,1101.69,"That is why they get skipped."],[1101.69,1104.78,"And that is where the route to market is decided."],[1105.27,1107.39,"Think about the pressure a buyer is under."],[1107.39,1110.18,"The business wants a contract."],[1110.18,1116.29,"Steps 6 to 10 produce visible things: a tender pack, bids, a scoring sheet, a signed contract."],[1116.29,1118.27,"Everyone can see progress."],[1118.27,1124.64,"Steps 1 to 5 produce a validated need, a spend analysis, a market view and a strategy paper."],[1124.92,1130.45,"Useful, but invisible to the outside world, and easy to sacrifice when time is short."],[1130.45,1133.67,"Which step produces the fewest documents in your team?"],[1133.67,1138.62,"It is usually step 4 or step 5, the positioning and the strategy."],[1138.62,1145.85,"And those are precisely the steps where the choice between a tender, a panel, a negotiation or an auction is made."],[1146.13,1148.82,"When they are skipped, the choice is made by habit."],[1149.77,1151.69,"Let me introduce the course case."],[1151.69,1157.35,"Nordvia is a fictional mid-sized insurer, part of a European insurance group."],[1157.35,1162.14,"Its Procurement team of 9 runs about 60 sourcing events a year in Coupa."],[1162.14,1168.56,"And until now, most of them were requests for quotation with three quotes, whatever the category."],[1169.06,1172.01,"Look at the three examples on the slide."],[1172.01,1177.54,"Office cleaning: three quotes, and the contracts have been rolled over since 2019."],[1177.54,1184.12,"IT consulting: 38 call-offs and no framework, so every engagement is a separate small purchase."],[1184.12,1188.94,"Core software from a vendor called Kelvar: renewed with a 9 percent uplift."],[1189.22,1190.97,"And the last line sums it up."],[1190.97,1194.13,"Same tool, three very different power positions."],[1194.63,1195.74,"Why does that matter?"],[1195.74,1204.56,"Because for cleaning, three quotes is too little: a well-designed tender with an output-based specification could change the whole cost base."],[1204.56,1213.49,"For IT consulting, three quotes per call-off is the wrong level entirely: the problem is the missing structure of a panel and a rate card."],[1213.77,1221.61,"And for Kelvar, three quotes is meaningless, because there is only one vendor who can renew those licences."],[1221.61,1228.09,"What Nordvia needed there was a negotiation strategy, with usage data and a credible alternative."],[1228.09,1230.52,"Nordvia's team is not lazy or incompetent."],[1230.52,1233.4,"It is using one familiar tool everywhere."],[1233.68,1236.39,"This is the starting point of the course case."],[1236.39,1243.42,"At the end of the session we will return to Nordvia's eight pipeline projects and decide which one to start first."],[1244.38,1245.64,"What does good look like?"],[1245.64,1250.52,"Here is a benchmark from The Hackett Group, a consultancy, published in 2024."],[1250.52,1257.18,"It compares what Hackett calls Digital World Class procurement organisations with their peers."],[1257.68,1258.77,"Four numbers."],[1258.77,1261.91,"23 percent shorter sourcing cycle times."],[1261.91,1264.64,"Twice the spend-cost-reduction savings."],[1264.64,1271.2,"59 percent less maverick buying, meaning less buying outside contracts and agreed channels."],[1271.2,1275.6,"And 3.7 times fewer suppliers per billion dollars of spend."],[1276.1,1279.57,"Now, a word of caution, and the footer says it clearly."],[1279.57,1283.73,"This is consultancy benchmark data, not peer-reviewed research."],[1283.73,1288.72,"So treat these numbers as a direction, not as targets you promise your management."],[1288.72,1292.52,"But look at the first number with me, because it holds a lesson."],[1292.52,1295.34,"The best teams have shorter cycle times."],[1295.62,1297.77,"Does that mean they skip the analysis?"],[1297.77,1298.52,"No."],[1298.52,1302.45,"They are faster because they know the category before the request arrives."],[1302.45,1307.31,"They already have the spend cube, the contract dates and the market view."],[1307.31,1309.89,"When the business calls, they are not starting from zero."],[1309.89,1312.72,"Insight is not what slows sourcing down."],[1312.72,1314.16,"Missing insight is."],[1315.11,1317.35,"Now let us bring it home to our own Group."],[1317.35,1321.72,"These figures are anonymised and rounded, but they tell a clear story."],[1322.22,1327.43,"First, only about 2 percent of addressable spend is under a Group contract today."],[1327.43,1335.08,"That is a very low number, and it means there is a lot of demand we buy separately that we could buy together."],[1335.08,1342.19,"Second, about 800 supplier relationships above 50 thousand euros a year carry about 88 percent of the spend."],[1342.47,1344.66,"That is the negotiable universe."],[1344.66,1347.42,"That is where a decision chain pays back."],[1347.42,1353.27,"Third, 60 to 70 percent of a mature sourcing plan comes from expiring contracts."],[1353.27,1360.43,"In other words, most of next year's work is already visible in today's contract end dates, if anybody looks."],[1360.71,1366.32,"And fourth, about 6 percent per contract cycle is a planning assumption for procurement value."],[1366.82,1368.77,"Be careful with that last number."],[1368.77,1378.76,"The footer explains it: it is a planning assumption, made up of about 2 percent cost savings plus 4 percent cost avoidance over a three-year contract cycle."],[1378.76,1381.36,"It is not a result anyone has measured."],[1381.36,1385.68,"It is useful for sizing a pipeline, not for promising savings."],[1385.96,1390.37,"And what about the thousands of small suppliers below 50 thousand euros?"],[1390.37,1398.37,"For them, the question is not which sourcing strategy, but which channel: a catalogue, a spot buy, a purchasing card."],[1398.37,1401.66,"The tail is a channel question, not a sourcing one."],[1402.61,1404.4,"Another moment of reflection."],[1404.4,1412.78,"Who usually chooses the sourcing tool, the request for quotation, the request for proposal or the auction, in your team today?"],[1412.78,1416.35,"Is it the business, before Procurement is even called?"],[1416.35,1418.14,"The buyer, by habit or by template?"],[1418.14,1421.02,"The buyer, after a category analysis?"],[1421.02,1422.17,"A policy threshold?"],[1422.45,1423.62,"Or nobody explicitly?"],[1423.62,1425.17,"Think about your honest answer."],[1425.17,1432.82,"If it is anything other than the buyer after a category analysis, the tool is being chosen before the strategy."],[1433.77,1434.71,"And one more."],[1434.71,1439.1,"Which step of Source to Contract does your team skip or rush most often?"],[1439.1,1440.59,"Need validation?"],[1440.59,1442.84,"Spend and contract analysis?"],[1442.84,1444.26,"Market analysis?"],[1444.26,1446.07,"Positioning and strategy?"],[1446.07,1448.21,"Or the handover to Procure to Pay?"],[1448.21,1453.7,"Hold that answer, because the exercise that follows asks you to check it against real events."],[1454.65,1456.25,"Now it is your turn."],[1456.25,1464.35,"This is the exercise for module 1.1, and it is in your workbook, under module 1.1 in the Modules tab."],[1464.35,1468.75,"The question is: how did your last three sourcing events really run?"],[1469.25,1470.47,"Here is what to do."],[1470.47,1474.97,"First, name your last three sourcing events, the category and the year."],[1474.97,1480.61,"Second, for each of the 11 steps, mark whether it was done properly, rushed, or skipped."],[1480.61,1485.34,"Third, look across the three events and find the step you skip most often."],[1485.62,1491.8,"Fourth, note one event where the tool came before the strategy, and write down what a better route would have been."],[1492.3,1492.97,"Be honest."],[1492.97,1500.0,"Nobody sees this unless you choose to share it, and the value of the exercise is entirely in its honesty."],[1500.0,1507.19,"In the live session this takes about 20 minutes: 12 minutes on your own and 8 minutes comparing in groups of three."],[1507.47,1512.4,"If you want to do it now, pause the video, open your workbook, and come back when you are done."],[1513.35,1514.1,"Welcome back."],[1514.1,1516.61,"Let us debrief with four questions."],[1516.61,1519.41,"Which steps were most often rushed or skipped?"],[1519.41,1524.23,"What did skipping them cost you: price, risk, time or relationship?"],[1524.23,1527.17,"Where was the tool chosen before the strategy?"],[1527.17,1530.93,"And what would have to change for steps 1 to 5 to happen every time?"],[1531.43,1535.14,"When groups do this exercise, a pattern usually appears."],[1535.14,1542.88,"Steps 2 to 5 dominate the skipped column: the spend analysis, the market analysis, the positioning and the strategy."],[1542.88,1547.46,"Step 1 is often rushed, because the need arrives already formed."],[1547.46,1552.14,"Steps 6 to 10 are almost always done, because they produce the documents."],[1552.42,1554.36,"The costs are also typical."],[1554.36,1559.48,"A price that was higher than it needed to be, because a framework already existed."],[1559.48,1564.89,"A risk that appeared late, because nobody asked the regulatory question at the start."],[1564.89,1569.6,"Time lost, because a notice period was missed and the contract rolled over."],[1569.88,1576.58,"And sometimes a relationship strained, because the incumbent was treated as interchangeable when it was not."],[1576.58,1587.05,"And the last question, what would have to change, usually leads to two answers: earlier involvement, and having the category insight ready before the request arrives."],[1587.33,1590.15,"That is exactly what the rest of today is about."],[1591.1,1594.27,"Let me recap module 1.1 in three lines."],[1594.27,1600.46,"First, Source to Contract is a decision chain of 11 steps, and value is decided early."],[1600.46,1610.62,"Second, CIPS, van Weele and O'Brien agree: insight comes before strategy, and Monczka and Kraljic add that you must segment before you choose an approach."],[1610.9,1616.66,"Third, Nordvia's habit, three quotes for everything, is the problem this course sets out to solve."],[1617.16,1619.29,"So where does the decision chain begin?"],[1619.29,1620.97,"With step 1, the intake."],[1620.97,1633.67,"In the next lesson we look at how to start right: how to validate a need, check whether it is already covered, think in total contract value, and run the regulatory triage that every insurer needs on day one."],[1633.67,1634.76,"See you there."]]},{"id":"s1-l2","session":"s1","title":"Start right: intake and need validation","summary":"Run a structured intake on a sourcing request: challenge the need, check existing contracts and Group frameworks, size the total contract value, and triage outsourcing, DORA, data and staff questions before going to market.","from":26,"to":44,"audio":"audio/s1-l2.mp3","duration":1500.4,"segs":[[26,0.6,17.79,"Welcome back. In the last lesson we saw that Source to Contract is a decision chain, and that the first five steps are where the route to market is decided. Now we start at the very beginning of that chain, with module 1.2: Start right, intake and need validation."],[26,18.29,61.91,"This lesson covers three questions, and they are on the slide. Is the need real? Is it already covered by a contract we have? And what does regulation say about it? We will go through six intake questions, look at how to challenge a need without offending the business, learn to think in total contract value, and then spend a good part of the lesson on the regulatory triage: Solvency II outsourcing, DORA for ICT services, personal data and staff transfer. We will apply it to two Nordvia projects, a claims IT managed service and office cleaning, and finish with how this can be built into the sourcing request in Coupa. At the end, you will run the intake on your own Sourcing Case."],[27,62.86,74.61,"Let me start with two sentences that are worth remembering. The cheapest saving is the purchase that does not happen. And the cheapest risk control is the one applied before the market is approached."],[27,75.11,110.64,"Think about the first sentence. If a business area is about to buy 500 licences and really needs 350, no negotiation will ever save as much as simply buying 350. That is demand management, and in the Kearney Purchasing Chessboard, the book by Schuh and colleagues that we will use later today, demand management is one of the levers in the most basic strategy, manage spend. It is also the most underused lever in indirect spend. Why? Because it feels like questioning the business, and buyers are often called in too late to do it."],[27,111.14,141.73,"Now the second sentence. Imagine you discover, after the bids are in, that the service is outsourcing of a critical function. Suddenly you need a detailed examination of the provider, specific contract clauses, and a notification to the supervisor. Some bidders cannot meet the requirements. The timeline slips. Asking the question at intake costs five minutes. Asking it at signature can cost a re-tender. Intake is where both of these sentences are put into practice."],[28,142.68,147.95,"Here are the six intake questions. Each one has a reason, shown in the right-hand column."],[28,148.45,190.12,"Question 1: is the need real, and how much of it? That is demand management before buying. Question 2: does a contract or a Group framework already cover it? That stops duplicate sourcing. Question 3: buy, make, or keep in-house? This decides whether a sourcing event is needed at all, and whether the arrangement could be outsourcing. Question 4: what is the value, and what is the term? That sets the approval levels and the effort the project deserves. Question 5: what does the regulatory triage say? That decides the governance path and the lead time. And question 6: who owns it, and by when? You need a named owner and a real date."],[28,190.62,224.88,"Let me give you a Nordvia example for question 6, because it is the one people underestimate. Nordvia's network and telecom contract with a provider called Telmo has been rolled over twice. Not because anyone decided that rolling over was the best option, but because nobody owned the renewal. Nobody had the date in their calendar. Each time, the notice period passed, and the default happened. A named owner and a date counted back from the notice period would have prevented that. The other questions each get a Nordvia example on the next slides."],[28,225.38,264.98,"And a quick example for question 3, buy, make or keep in-house. Nordvia scans claims documents with a small internal team. The claims director asks Procurement to find a cheaper scanning provider. Before any market activity, the intake asks: are we really choosing between providers, or between keeping the work in-house and handing it to a business process outsourcing provider? If it is the second, the regulatory triage becomes essential, because claims handling support may be part of a critical function. The same request can be a simple purchase or an outsourcing decision. Intake is where you find out which."],[29,265.93,274.42,"So how do you challenge a need? The slide gives four angles, and each one is a question you can ask the business without telling them they are wrong."],[29,274.92,305.8,"The first is volume. Do we need this many? Fewer licences, fewer studies, fewer consulting days. The second is frequency. How often do we need it? Clean by need rather than by a fixed schedule. Produce fewer reports. The third is specification. Could a standard product do the job instead of a bespoke one? Could we specify outputs, the result we want, instead of inputs, the hours and the people? The fourth is duplication. Are two areas buying the same thing separately?"],[29,306.3,333.88,"Here is a Nordvia example of duplication. Nordvia's marketing team uses four market research agencies, and two of the tracking studies they run measure almost the same thing. Nobody planned that. Each study was commissioned by a different team, at a different time, for a good reason. A buyer who asks, could one study serve both teams, may remove a whole contract. That is a real saving, and it never appears in a bid comparison."],[29,334.38,358.28,"One practical tip. Ask these questions in the language of the business, not in the language of savings. Do not say, I want to cut your volume. Say, help me understand how many users will actually use this in the first year. Most business owners are happy to answer, and many have never been asked. Before moving on, think of one example from your own area for each of the four cards."],[30,359.23,364.82,"Intake question 2 asks whether the need is already covered. Here is a Nordvia example."],[30,365.32,390.15,"Nordvia buys devices and end-user support, laptops, desk support and the like, for 7.8 million euros a year from a local provider. A Group framework already exists for the same service, at lower rates. The local contract ends in March 2027. So the first question at intake is not how do we tender this. It is: why not call off from the Group framework?"],[30,390.65,424.83,"There may be good reasons not to. The framework may not cover the local language, or the service levels may not fit. But those reasons must be stated and checked, not assumed. Remember the Group figure from the last lesson: only about 2 percent of addressable spend is under a Group contract today. That means checking frameworks first is one of the quickest wins available to any buyer. It saves the cost of running a tender, it uses volume the Group has already negotiated, and it frees your team for the projects that really need a full decision chain."],[31,425.78,434.36,"Intake question 4 is about value. And here the key message is in the title: value means total contract value, not annual spend."],[31,434.86,470.64,"Let us walk through the table. Nordvia's office cleaning, project P1, costs 2.4 million euros a year. If the term is three years plus two optional one-year extensions, the total value is about 12 million euros. Security and reception, project P6, costs 1.6 million euros a year. Over three years plus a two-year option, that is about 8 million euros. And the Kelvar core software licences, project P3, cost 11.2 million euros a year. Over three years, that is about 34 million euros."],[31,471.14,515.02,"Why does this matter? Because approval levels follow the total, not the annual figure. A buyer who says cleaning is a 2.4 million euro project understates it by a factor of five. That has two consequences. First, the project may be approved at the wrong level, and a committee that should have seen it never does. Second, the project gets less effort than it deserves. A 12 million euro decision deserves a proper fact base, a market analysis and a strategy paper. One more point from the footer: these terms are illustrative choices. The actual term is one of the strategic decisions we make in Session 2. But you need an estimate now, at intake, to set the right governance."],[32,515.97,527.32,"Now we come to intake question 5, the regulatory triage. This is where an insurer differs from most other buyers. There are four questions, and each one has a legal reference."],[32,527.82,572.37,"The first question: is this outsourcing of a critical or important function? The reference is Solvency II, Article 49, and Article 274 of the Solvency II Delegated Regulation. The second: is it an ICT service, and does it support a critical or important function? The reference is DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act, in particular Articles 28 and 30. The third: will the supplier process personal data? That is the General Data Protection Regulation, GDPR. And the fourth: could it involve a staff transfer, or agency work? The reference is the Acquired Rights Directive, together with national law."],[32,572.87,613.94,"A word on the last two questions, because they are often forgotten in indirect categories. Personal data appears in more places than people expect: an events agency holds attendee lists, a print supplier handles policyholder mailings, a consulting firm sees employee data. When the answer is yes, you need a data processing agreement, a security annex and checks on where the data is stored and transferred. And the staff question matters for any labour-intensive service, such as cleaning, security, reception or staff augmentation in IT, where national rules on transfers and agency work can shape the contract and the savings."],[32,614.44,641.26,"Here is the important nuance. Procurement does not decide these questions alone. The determination of whether something is a critical or important function belongs to the insurer, with Risk, Compliance, Legal or Information Security involved. But Procurement must ask the questions on day one, and record who answered. If nobody asks, nobody answers, and the question appears later at the worst possible moment."],[33,642.21,650.94,"What happens if the answer to the first question is yes, and the service is outsourcing of a critical or important function? Four consequences."],[33,651.44,698.82,"First, full responsibility. Under Article 49 of Solvency II, the insurer remains fully responsible for the function it outsources. You can outsource the work, but not the accountability. Second, provider selection. Article 274 requires a detailed examination of the provider: its ability and capacity to deliver, any conflicts of interest, and its security arrangements. That is not a weighted score in a tender. It is a requirement. Third, a written agreement. It must include access and audit rights, termination rights, and a notice period long enough for the insurer to find an alternative. Fourth, the supervisor. The supervisor must be notified before the outsourcing starts."],[33,699.32,715.18,"Look at that last card with a planner's eye. Supervisor notification adds lead time to the project. If you only discover at contract stage that it is needed, the start date moves. That is why the question belongs at intake, not at contract stage."],[34,716.13,733.7,"Now the second question. What if it is an ICT service under DORA? DORA has applied since 17 January 2025, and it changes the life of anyone buying IT, data or network services in a financial institution. Four cards again."],[34,734.2,795.52,"First, the pre-contract assessment, in Article 28, paragraph 4. Before entering the contract, the insurer must assess whether the service supports a critical or important function, identify the relevant risks including ICT concentration risk, carry out due diligence on the prospective provider, and look at conflicts of interest. Second, the register of information. Every ICT contractual arrangement is recorded, and I want to stress the word every. The register covers all ICT arrangements, not only the critical ones. A small SaaS tool for the marketing team still goes in. Third, contract clauses. Article 30 sets clauses for all ICT contracts, and additional ones where the service supports a critical or important function. Fourth, the exit strategy. For critical or important functions, Article 28, paragraph 8 requires an exit strategy that is documented and sufficiently tested."],[34,796.02,815.39,"Notice how each card lands at a different point of our 11 steps. The assessment shapes the market analysis and the evaluation. The clauses shape the draft contract that goes out with the request. The register and the exit plan shape the award and the contract. None of them can be added comfortably at the end."],[35,816.34,827.59,"A short but important correction. The EIOPA guidelines on outsourcing to cloud service providers were withdrawn, with effect from 17 January 2025."],[35,828.09,872.89,"EIOPA, the European insurance supervisory authority, withdrew them to avoid duplication with DORA. So for ICT, the reference today is DORA, plus the Solvency II outsourcing rules where the arrangement is also outsourcing of a critical or important function. Why does this deserve a slide? Because older training material, old policies and some contract templates still cite the cloud guidelines as current. If you see them quoted as the rule to follow, correct it, politely. You can still treat them as historical background. And for any question about how the local supervisor treats cloud aspects outside ICT, check the local supervisory guidance rather than assuming."],[36,873.84,884.81,"So which purchases are outsourcing of a critical function? Here is a practical guide in two columns. Remember, this is a guide for asking the question, not a legal opinion."],[36,885.31,914.49,"On the left, the purchases that are usually not: office cleaning, marketing and creative agencies, print and office supplies, business travel, and one-off expert advice. On the right, the purchases that may be, and must be assessed and documented: IT services for claims or policy administration, data storage and cloud, software support for core systems, and some business process outsourcing, for example claims handling or document scanning."],[36,914.99,953.93,"Look at the footer. The determination is the insurer's own documented assessment. And EIOPA's Questions and Answers, number 1283, gives a useful rule of thumb: a one-off expert appointment may not necessarily amount to outsourcing, and the more substantial or frequent the service, the more likely it is outsourcing. So a single piece of actuarial advice probably is not. A permanent team running a regulatory process on your behalf probably is. Your skill here is not to give legal opinions. It is to ask the right question, route it to the right person, and record the answer."],[37,954.88,961.24,"Why insist on asking at intake? This table shows what a yes changes downstream, step by step."],[37,961.74,1007.5,"At step 3, the market analysis, you must look at concentration risk and subcontracting chains. How many alternatives exist, and does every provider depend on the same underlying platform? At step 5, the strategy, exit feasibility becomes a criterion, and multi-vendor options may need to be considered. At step 6, the design of the request, the mandatory clauses go into the draft contract, and a security questionnaire goes into the request. At step 8, evaluation, due diligence becomes a gate, not a weighted score. A provider that fails it is out, however good its price. And at step 10, the contract, you need the register entries, the supervisor notification and the exit plan."],[37,1008.0,1036.4,"Now imagine finding out at step 10. The draft contract that went to bidders did not have the clauses. The evaluation scored security instead of treating it as a gate. Bidders priced without the audit rights. You are left with two bad options: renegotiate the contract with the winner, from a weak position, or re-tender. That is why a late triage forces a re-tender or a contract renegotiation. The triage at intake is cheap insurance."],[38,1037.35,1050.0,"Let us apply the triage to a real case. This is a worked example on Nordvia's project P8, the claims IT managed service provided by Northbyte, worth 19.4 million euros a year."],[38,1050.5,1093.13,"Go down the list with me. Is it an ICT service? Yes. It is a managed service, and the provider has access to Nordvia's systems. Does it support a critical function? Yes: claims handling is a core insurance function. Is it outsourcing under Solvency II? Very likely, but that is to be confirmed with Risk. Notice the wording: Procurement records very likely and names who confirms. Personal data? Yes, claims data, which includes sensitive information about policyholders. And then the gap: there is no tested exit plan, which DORA Article 28, paragraph 8 requires for ICT services supporting critical or important functions."],[38,1093.63,1131.88,"Now the last line, which is the real lesson. Lead time: start now for a December 2027 expiry. Why so early? Because an exit plan has to be written and tested, the market analysis must include concentration risk, the request must carry the full set of clauses, due diligence takes time, and the supervisor must be notified. Add the notice period on the current contract, and a project that looks two years away is already urgent. The triage does not only add requirements. It drives the timeline. Northbyte will come back in Session 4 as our exit-plan case."],[39,1132.83,1137.8,"Now a contrast. The same triage, applied to office cleaning, project P1."],[39,1138.3,1170.03,"Outsourcing of a critical function? No. ICT service? No. Personal data? Limited: access badges and data about the staff on site. Staff transfer? Possible, if the supplier changes. Under the Acquired Rights Directive and national rules, cleaning staff may transfer from the old supplier to the new one. So check the national rules. The consequence is concrete: staff data must go into the tender pack, so bidders can price correctly, and mobilisation will take longer."],[39,1170.53,1204.94,"Here is the lesson of this slide. A no is also an answer worth recording. When the triage says no to outsourcing and no to DORA, the process can stay light. You do not need a Risk committee for a cleaning contract. But you only know that because you asked. And the staff transfer question, which many buyers forget for cleaning and security, can change the savings case. If bidders assume they will inherit the current staff at current terms, the price gap between them may shrink. Better to know that at intake than to promise savings that cannot materialise."],[40,1205.89,1213.77,"How can all of this be built into daily practice? Through the sourcing request in Coupa. The slide shows good practice in four cards."],[40,1214.27,1260.52,"First, classification questions in the intake form itself: outsourcing, ICT, critical function, personal data, staff. Short yes, no or not sure questions that a requester can answer. Second, automatic routing. A yes or a not sure sends the request to Risk, Compliance or Information Security, without the buyer having to remember. Third, reuse in onboarding. The same questions appear again in Supplier Information Management, when the supplier is onboarded, so the answers stay consistent from intake to contract. Fourth, and this is important, check your tenant. Which intake form and which routing your entity uses is a configuration choice. Some entities have built this, others have not."],[40,1261.02,1277.49,"So do not assume your Coupa does this today. Confirm it in your Coupa tenant after this lesson. And if it does not, the four questions of the triage can still go into your own intake checklist tomorrow morning. The tool helps, but the discipline does not depend on it."],[41,1278.44,1303.74,"A moment of reflection. In your entity, when is the regulatory question, outsourcing or DORA, usually asked? At intake? When drafting the request? At contract stage? When Risk or Legal raises it? Or rarely? Think of your last ICT purchase and pick your honest answer. If it was anything later than intake, you now know the cost: requirements arriving after bidders have already priced."],[42,1304.69,1316.66,"Now the exercise for module 1.2: intake and triage for your Sourcing Case. It is in your workbook under module 1.2 in the Modules tab, and it takes about 20 minutes."],[42,1317.16,1350.62,"Here are the five steps. First, write the need in business terms, as an outcome, not as a supplier name. Not, we need a new contract with this agency. But, we need creative support for six campaigns a year, at a predictable cost. Second, challenge volume, frequency and specification. Third, check existing contracts and Group frameworks. Fourth, estimate the total contract value, and the latest date to start the sourcing. Fifth, tick the regulatory triage questions that are a yes or a possible yes."],[42,1351.12,1383.12,"One tip from facilitators who run this exercise. The most common gap is the notice period and the auto-renewal date. People know the end date of their contract, but not the notice period. So find it. Count back: the end date, minus the notice period, minus the time the sourcing will take. That is your latest start date. Mark everything you do not know yet. Those gaps become questions for your business owner during the fieldwork. Pause the video now if you want to do the exercise, and come back when you are ready."],[43,1384.07,1400.41,"Welcome back. Four debrief questions. Whose need statement still names a supplier? Who found an existing contract or framework? Who ticked a DORA or outsourcing question, and who will confirm it? And how many found that their real start date is already close?"],[43,1400.91,1441.53,"Let me comment on each. If your need statement still names a supplier, rewrite it. A need that names a supplier has already chosen the route to market. If you found an existing contract or framework, congratulations. That may feel like losing your project, but it is a real saving, achieved at almost no cost, and it is exactly what intake is for. If you ticked a DORA or outsourcing question, write down the name of the person who will confirm it, and a date. And if you discovered that your real start date is close, or already passed, you have learned the most valuable thing in this module. Better to learn it now than when the auto-renewal triggers."],[44,1442.48,1485.11,"Let me recap module 1.2 in three lines. First, validate the need and check existing contracts before going to market. The cheapest saving is the purchase that does not happen, and the quickest win is often a Group framework that already exists. Second, think in total contract value, and count back from the notice period. Approval levels follow the total, and the notice period sets the real deadline. Third, triage outsourcing, DORA, personal data and staff at intake, not at signature. A yes changes the market analysis, the strategy, the request, the evaluation and the contract. A no keeps the process light. Either way, record it."],[44,1485.61,1499.22,"With a validated need, we are ready for the next step of the chain. In the next lesson, we build the category fact base: the spend cube, the contract inventory, the demand drivers and the total cost of ownership. See you there."]],"questions":[{"after":28,"q":"Nordvia's network contract has rolled over twice without anyone reviewing it. Which intake question would most directly have prevented this?","options":["Is the need real, and how much of it?","Buy, make or keep in-house?","What does the regulatory triage say?","Who owns it, and by when?"],"answer":3,"explain":"The rollovers happened because nobody owned the renewal or tracked the date. A named owner and a date counted back from the notice period stop the default from happening by accident.","t":264.98},{"after":29,"q":"Marketing asks you to tender a new tracking study. You discover another team already runs a study measuring almost the same thing. Which angle of the need challenge applies?","options":["Duplication","Frequency","Specification","Volume"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two areas buying the same thing separately is duplication. Asking whether one study could serve both teams may remove a purchase entirely, a saving no bid comparison would show.","t":358.28},{"after":31,"q":"A buyer presents a security contract at 1.6 million euros a year, with a three-year term plus a two-year option, as a 1.6 million euro project. What is the main risk?","options":["The supplier will refuse the option years","The project is approved at the wrong level and gets less effort than its roughly 8 million euro total deserves","The annual figure breaks the GDPR assessment","The contract cannot be loaded into Coupa"],"answer":1,"explain":"Approval levels and effort follow the total contract value, not the annual spend. Quoting the annual figure understates this project about five times.","t":515.02},{"after":33,"q":"A business process outsourcing provider will handle part of Nordvia's claims processing, and Risk confirms it is outsourcing of a critical function. Which statement is correct?","options":["Responsibility for the function passes to the provider under the contract","Supervisor notification can be done after the contract is running","Nordvia remains fully responsible, and the supervisor must be notified before the outsourcing starts","Provider examination can be replaced by a weighted quality score"],"answer":2,"explain":"Under Solvency II Article 49 the insurer keeps full responsibility, and the supervisor must be notified before the outsourcing starts. Article 274 requires a detailed examination of the provider, not just a score.","t":715.18},{"after":35,"q":"A colleague's cloud contract template still says it complies with the EIOPA cloud outsourcing guidelines. What should you tell them?","options":["Those guidelines were withdrawn from 17 January 2025; the reference is DORA, plus Solvency II outsourcing rules where relevant","The guidelines replaced DORA for insurers","The guidelines still apply, but only to critical functions","No regulation applies to cloud contracts below one million euros"],"answer":0,"explain":"EIOPA withdrew the cloud guidelines with effect from 17 January 2025 to avoid duplication with DORA. For ICT the reference is DORA, together with Solvency II outsourcing rules where the arrangement is also critical outsourcing.","t":872.89},{"after":37,"q":"In an ICT tender supporting a critical function, a bidder has the best price but fails the due diligence. Following the triage logic, what happens?","options":["The bidder loses some points on security but can still win","The bidder is excluded, because due diligence is a gate, not a weighted score","The bidder wins and is asked to fix the gaps after signature","The tender is cancelled automatically"],"answer":1,"explain":"When the triage says yes, due diligence becomes a gate at the evaluation step. A provider that fails it is out, whatever its price.","t":1036.4},{"after":39,"q":"For Nordvia's office cleaning tender, the triage says no to outsourcing and no to DORA. Which point still needs attention at intake?","options":["A DORA exit plan","Supervisor notification","A possible staff transfer, so staff data goes in the tender pack and mobilisation is planned longer","An entry in the register of information"],"answer":2,"explain":"Cleaning is usually not critical outsourcing and not ICT, so the process stays light. But a change of supplier may trigger a staff transfer under national rules, which affects pricing, the tender pack and mobilisation time.","t":1204.94},{"after":40,"q":"You want the triage questions to route automatically to Risk in Coupa. What is the right first step?","options":["Promise the business it is already configured","Confirm in your Coupa tenant which intake form and routing your entity uses","Wait until the supplier is onboarded to ask","Ask suppliers to fill in the triage themselves"],"answer":1,"explain":"Intake forms and routing are configuration choices that differ by entity. Confirm what your tenant does, and use your own intake checklist in the meantime.","t":1277.49},{"after":43,"q":"Your contract ends on 31 December 2027, has a six-month notice period, and you estimate the sourcing will take nine months. When is your latest start date?","options":["31 December 2027","30 June 2027","End of March 2027","Around the end of September 2026"],"answer":3,"explain":"Count back from the end date: minus six months of notice gives 30 June 2027, minus nine months of sourcing gives about the end of September 2026. The notice period, not the end date, sets the real deadline.","t":1441.53}],"cues":[[0.6,1.35,"Welcome back."],[1.35,11.16,"In the last lesson we saw that Source to Contract is a decision chain, and that the first five steps are where the route to market is decided."],[11.16,17.79,"Now we start at the very beginning of that chain, with module 1.2: Start right, intake and need validation."],[18.29,22.04,"This lesson covers three questions, and they are on the slide."],[22.04,23.48,"Is the need real?"],[23.48,26.11,"Is it already covered by a contract we have?"],[26.11,28.27,"And what does regulation say about it?"],[28.55,47.88,"We will go through six intake questions, look at how to challenge a need without offending the business, learn to think in total contract value, and then spend a good part of the lesson on the regulatory triage: Solvency II outsourcing, DORA for ICT services, personal data and staff transfer."],[48.16,58.1,"We will apply it to two Nordvia projects, a claims IT managed service and office cleaning, and finish with how this can be built into the sourcing request in Coupa."],[58.1,61.9,"At the end, you will run the intake on your own Sourcing Case."],[62.86,66.27,"Let me start with two sentences that are worth remembering."],[66.27,70.03,"The cheapest saving is the purchase that does not happen."],[70.03,74.61,"And the cheapest risk control is the one applied before the market is approached."],[75.11,76.86,"Think about the first sentence."],[76.86,87.76,"If a business area is about to buy 500 licences and really needs 350, no negotiation will ever save as much as simply buying 350."],[88.04,102.01,"That is demand management, and in the Kearney Purchasing Chessboard, the book by Schuh and colleagues that we will use later today, demand management is one of the levers in the most basic strategy, manage spend."],[102.01,105.14,"It is also the most underused lever in indirect spend."],[105.14,105.19,"Why?"],[105.47,110.64,"Because it feels like questioning the business, and buyers are often called in too late to do it."],[111.14,112.81,"Now the second sentence."],[112.81,119.02,"Imagine you discover, after the bids are in, that the service is outsourcing of a critical function."],[119.02,127.07,"Suddenly you need a detailed examination of the provider, specific contract clauses, and a notification to the supervisor."],[127.07,130.25,"Some bidders cannot meet the requirements."],[130.25,131.41,"The timeline slips."],[131.69,134.91,"Asking the question at intake costs five minutes."],[134.91,138.11,"Asking it at signature can cost a re-tender."],[138.11,141.73,"Intake is where both of these sentences are put into practice."],[142.68,144.81,"Here are the six intake questions."],[144.81,147.95,"Each one has a reason, shown in the right-hand column."],[148.45,151.68,"Question 1: is the need real, and how much of it?"],[151.68,154.35,"That is demand management before buying."],[154.35,159.26,"Question 2: does a contract or a Group framework already cover it?"],[159.26,161.5,"That stops duplicate sourcing."],[161.5,164.13,"Question 3: buy, make, or keep in-house?"],[164.41,171.45,"This decides whether a sourcing event is needed at all, and whether the arrangement could be outsourcing."],[171.45,173.92,"Question 4: what is the value, and what is the term?"],[173.92,177.84,"That sets the approval levels and the effort the project deserves."],[177.84,181.33,"Question 5: what does the regulatory triage say?"],[181.61,184.53,"That decides the governance path and the lead time."],[184.53,187.77,"And question 6: who owns it, and by when?"],[187.77,190.12,"You need a named owner and a real date."],[190.62,196.3,"Let me give you a Nordvia example for question 6, because it is the one people underestimate."],[196.3,202.78,"Nordvia's network and telecom contract with a provider called Telmo has been rolled over twice."],[202.78,209.05,"Not because anyone decided that rolling over was the best option, but because nobody owned the renewal."],[209.33,211.52,"Nobody had the date in their calendar."],[211.52,215.26,"Each time, the notice period passed, and the default happened."],[215.26,220.85,"A named owner and a date counted back from the notice period would have prevented that."],[220.85,224.88,"The other questions each get a Nordvia example on the next slides."],[225.38,229.22,"And a quick example for question 3, buy, make or keep in-house."],[229.22,233.6,"Nordvia scans claims documents with a small internal team."],[233.6,238.5,"The claims director asks Procurement to find a cheaper scanning provider."],[238.78,250.01,"Before any market activity, the intake asks: are we really choosing between providers, or between keeping the work in-house and handing it to a business process outsourcing provider?"],[250.01,258.17,"If it is the second, the regulatory triage becomes essential, because claims handling support may be part of a critical function."],[258.45,262.76,"The same request can be a simple purchase or an outsourcing decision."],[262.76,264.98,"Intake is where you find out which."],[265.93,267.66,"So how do you challenge a need?"],[267.66,274.42,"The slide gives four angles, and each one is a question you can ask the business without telling them they are wrong."],[274.92,276.34,"The first is volume."],[276.34,277.7,"Do we need this many?"],[277.7,281.48,"Fewer licences, fewer studies, fewer consulting days."],[281.48,282.59,"The second is frequency."],[282.59,284.69,"How often do we need it?"],[284.69,287.65,"Clean by need rather than by a fixed schedule."],[287.65,288.94,"Produce fewer reports."],[288.94,291.58,"The third is specification."],[291.58,294.93,"Could a standard product do the job instead of a bespoke one?"],[295.21,300.97,"Could we specify outputs, the result we want, instead of inputs, the hours and the people?"],[300.97,303.07,"The fourth is duplication."],[303.07,305.79,"Are two areas buying the same thing separately?"],[306.3,308.55,"Here is a Nordvia example of duplication."],[308.55,317.5,"Nordvia's marketing team uses four market research agencies, and two of the tracking studies they run measure almost the same thing."],[317.5,318.75,"Nobody planned that."],[318.75,324.2,"Each study was commissioned by a different team, at a different time, for a good reason."],[324.48,329.8,"A buyer who asks, could one study serve both teams, may remove a whole contract."],[329.8,333.89,"That is a real saving, and it never appears in a bid comparison."],[334.38,335.55,"One practical tip."],[335.55,340.99,"Ask these questions in the language of the business, not in the language of savings."],[340.99,343.63,"Do not say, I want to cut your volume."],[343.63,348.74,"Say, help me understand how many users will actually use this in the first year."],[348.74,353.09,"Most business owners are happy to answer, and many have never been asked."],[353.37,358.28,"Before moving on, think of one example from your own area for each of the four cards."],[359.23,362.98,"Intake question 2 asks whether the need is already covered."],[362.98,364.82,"Here is a Nordvia example."],[365.32,375.03,"Nordvia buys devices and end-user support, laptops, desk support and the like, for 7.8 million euros a year from a local provider."],[375.03,380.22,"A Group framework already exists for the same service, at lower rates."],[380.22,382.2,"The local contract ends in March 2027."],[382.2,387.27,"So the first question at intake is not how do we tender this."],[387.55,390.15,"It is: why not call off from the Group framework?"],[390.65,392.55,"There may be good reasons not to."],[392.55,397.54,"The framework may not cover the local language, or the service levels may not fit."],[397.54,401.62,"But those reasons must be stated and checked, not assumed."],[401.62,408.83,"Remember the Group figure from the last lesson: only about 2 percent of addressable spend is under a Group contract today."],[409.11,414.42,"That means checking frameworks first is one of the quickest wins available to any buyer."],[414.42,424.83,"It saves the cost of running a tender, it uses volume the Group has already negotiated, and it frees your team for the projects that really need a full decision chain."],[425.78,428.29,"Intake question 4 is about value."],[428.29,434.36,"And here the key message is in the title: value means total contract value, not annual spend."],[434.86,436.36,"Let us walk through the table."],[436.36,442.1,"Nordvia's office cleaning, project P1, costs 2.4 million euros a year."],[442.1,450.43,"If the term is three years plus two optional one-year extensions, the total value is about 12 million euros."],[450.43,455.08,"Security and reception, project P6, costs 1.6 million euros a year."],[455.36,460.97,"Over three years plus a two-year option, that is about 8 million euros."],[460.97,466.94,"And the Kelvar core software licences, project P3, cost 11.2 million euros a year."],[466.94,470.64,"Over three years, that is about 34 million euros."],[471.14,472.66,"Why does this matter?"],[472.66,476.29,"Because approval levels follow the total, not the annual figure."],[476.29,482.77,"A buyer who says cleaning is a 2.4 million euro project understates it by a factor of five."],[482.77,483.56,"That has two consequences."],[483.56,490.77,"First, the project may be approved at the wrong level, and a committee that should have seen it never does."],[491.05,494.8,"Second, the project gets less effort than it deserves."],[494.8,501.39,"A 12 million euro decision deserves a proper fact base, a market analysis and a strategy paper."],[501.39,505.96,"One more point from the footer: these terms are illustrative choices."],[505.96,510.8,"The actual term is one of the strategic decisions we make in Session 2."],[511.08,515.03,"But you need an estimate now, at intake, to set the right governance."],[515.97,520.25,"Now we come to intake question 5, the regulatory triage."],[520.25,523.68,"This is where an insurer differs from most other buyers."],[523.68,527.32,"There are four questions, and each one has a legal reference."],[527.82,533.68,"The first question: is this outsourcing of a critical or important function?"],[533.68,542.33,"The reference is Solvency II, Article 49, and Article 274 of the Solvency II Delegated Regulation."],[542.33,548.49,"The second: is it an ICT service, and does it support a critical or important function?"],[548.77,555.59,"The reference is DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act, in particular Articles 28 and 30."],[555.59,559.2,"The third: will the supplier process personal data?"],[559.2,562.92,"That is the General Data Protection Regulation, GDPR."],[562.92,567.93,"And the fourth: could it involve a staff transfer, or agency work?"],[568.21,572.37,"The reference is the Acquired Rights Directive, together with national law."],[572.87,578.39,"A word on the last two questions, because they are often forgotten in indirect categories."],[578.39,591.07,"Personal data appears in more places than people expect: an events agency holds attendee lists, a print supplier handles policyholder mailings, a consulting firm sees employee data."],[591.35,599.01,"When the answer is yes, you need a data processing agreement, a security annex and checks on where the data is stored and transferred."],[599.29,613.94,"And the staff question matters for any labour-intensive service, such as cleaning, security, reception or staff augmentation in IT, where national rules on transfers and agency work can shape the contract and the savings."],[614.44,616.47,"Here is the important nuance."],[616.47,619.64,"Procurement does not decide these questions alone."],[619.64,630.31,"The determination of whether something is a critical or important function belongs to the insurer, with Risk, Compliance, Legal or Information Security involved."],[630.31,635.09,"But Procurement must ask the questions on day one, and record who answered."],[635.37,641.26,"If nobody asks, nobody answers, and the question appears later at the worst possible moment."],[642.21,649.99,"What happens if the answer to the first question is yes, and the service is outsourcing of a critical or important function?"],[649.99,650.94,"Four consequences."],[651.44,653.52,"First, full responsibility."],[653.52,661.42,"Under Article 49 of Solvency II, the insurer remains fully responsible for the function it outsources."],[661.42,664.66,"You can outsource the work, but not the accountability."],[664.66,666.82,"Second, provider selection."],[667.1,678.35,"Article 274 requires a detailed examination of the provider: its ability and capacity to deliver, any conflicts of interest, and its security arrangements."],[678.35,681.0,"That is not a weighted score in a tender."],[681.0,682.57,"It is a requirement."],[682.57,684.59,"Third, a written agreement."],[684.87,692.98,"It must include access and audit rights, termination rights, and a notice period long enough for the insurer to find an alternative."],[692.98,695.18,"Fourth, the supervisor."],[695.18,698.83,"The supervisor must be notified before the outsourcing starts."],[699.32,701.87,"Look at that last card with a planner's eye."],[701.87,705.65,"Supervisor notification adds lead time to the project."],[705.65,710.92,"If you only discover at contract stage that it is needed, the start date moves."],[710.92,715.17,"That is why the question belongs at intake, not at contract stage."],[716.13,717.73,"Now the second question."],[717.73,720.85,"What if it is an ICT service under DORA?"],[720.85,732.28,"DORA has applied since 17 January 2025, and it changes the life of anyone buying IT, data or network services in a financial institution."],[732.28,733.71,"Four cards again."],[734.2,738.81,"First, the pre-contract assessment, in Article 28, paragraph 4."],[739.09,756.15,"Before entering the contract, the insurer must assess whether the service supports a critical or important function, identify the relevant risks including ICT concentration risk, carry out due diligence on the prospective provider, and look at conflicts of interest."],[756.15,758.42,"Second, the register of information."],[758.7,764.92,"Every ICT contractual arrangement is recorded, and I want to stress the word every."],[764.92,768.92,"The register covers all ICT arrangements, not only the critical ones."],[768.92,773.75,"A small SaaS tool for the marketing team still goes in."],[773.75,775.04,"Third, contract clauses."],[775.32,784.67,"Article 30 sets clauses for all ICT contracts, and additional ones where the service supports a critical or important function."],[784.67,786.28,"Fourth, the exit strategy."],[786.28,795.52,"For critical or important functions, Article 28, paragraph 8 requires an exit strategy that is documented and sufficiently tested."],[796.02,800.02,"Notice how each card lands at a different point of our 11 steps."],[800.02,804.29,"The assessment shapes the market analysis and the evaluation."],[804.29,808.61,"The clauses shape the draft contract that goes out with the request."],[808.61,812.44,"The register and the exit plan shape the award and the contract."],[812.44,815.39,"None of them can be added comfortably at the end."],[816.34,818.52,"A short but important correction."],[818.52,827.58,"The EIOPA guidelines on outsourcing to cloud service providers were withdrawn, with effect from 17 January 2025."],[828.09,835.56,"EIOPA, the European insurance supervisory authority, withdrew them to avoid duplication with DORA."],[835.56,846.67,"So for ICT, the reference today is DORA, plus the Solvency II outsourcing rules where the arrangement is also outsourcing of a critical or important function."],[846.67,848.66,"Why does this deserve a slide?"],[848.94,856.01,"Because older training material, old policies and some contract templates still cite the cloud guidelines as current."],[856.01,860.5,"If you see them quoted as the rule to follow, correct it, politely."],[860.5,863.38,"You can still treat them as historical background."],[863.66,872.9,"And for any question about how the local supervisor treats cloud aspects outside ICT, check the local supervisory guidance rather than assuming."],[873.84,877.84,"So which purchases are outsourcing of a critical function?"],[877.84,880.09,"Here is a practical guide in two columns."],[880.09,884.81,"Remember, this is a guide for asking the question, not a legal opinion."],[885.31,896.15,"On the left, the purchases that are usually not: office cleaning, marketing and creative agencies, print and office supplies, business travel, and one-off expert advice."],[896.43,914.5,"On the right, the purchases that may be, and must be assessed and documented: IT services for claims or policy administration, data storage and cloud, software support for core systems, and some business process outsourcing, for example claims handling or document scanning."],[914.99,916.04,"Look at the footer."],[916.04,920.21,"The determination is the insurer's own documented assessment."],[920.21,936.54,"And EIOPA's Questions and Answers, number 1283, gives a useful rule of thumb: a one-off expert appointment may not necessarily amount to outsourcing, and the more substantial or frequent the service, the more likely it is outsourcing."],[936.82,940.22,"So a single piece of actuarial advice probably is not."],[940.22,944.98,"A permanent team running a regulatory process on your behalf probably is."],[944.98,947.89,"Your skill here is not to give legal opinions."],[947.89,953.93,"It is to ask the right question, route it to the right person, and record the answer."],[954.88,957.14,"Why insist on asking at intake?"],[957.14,961.24,"This table shows what a yes changes downstream, step by step."],[961.74,968.03,"At step 3, the market analysis, you must look at concentration risk and subcontracting chains."],[968.03,974.15,"How many alternatives exist, and does every provider depend on the same underlying platform?"],[974.15,982.24,"At step 5, the strategy, exit feasibility becomes a criterion, and multi-vendor options may need to be considered."],[982.52,991.83,"At step 6, the design of the request, the mandatory clauses go into the draft contract, and a security questionnaire goes into the request."],[991.83,997.02,"At step 8, evaluation, due diligence becomes a gate, not a weighted score."],[997.02,1000.55,"A provider that fails it is out, however good its price."],[1000.83,1007.51,"And at step 10, the contract, you need the register entries, the supervisor notification and the exit plan."],[1008.0,1009.9,"Now imagine finding out at step 10."],[1009.9,1014.34,"The draft contract that went to bidders did not have the clauses."],[1014.34,1018.52,"The evaluation scored security instead of treating it as a gate."],[1018.52,1021.02,"Bidders priced without the audit rights."],[1021.02,1028.12,"You are left with two bad options: renegotiate the contract with the winner, from a weak position, or re-tender."],[1028.4,1033.27,"That is why a late triage forces a re-tender or a contract renegotiation."],[1033.27,1036.4,"The triage at intake is cheap insurance."],[1037.35,1039.94,"Let us apply the triage to a real case."],[1039.94,1050.0,"This is a worked example on Nordvia's project P8, the claims IT managed service provided by Northbyte, worth 19.4 million euros a year."],[1050.5,1051.85,"Go down the list with me."],[1051.85,1053.76,"Is it an ICT service?"],[1053.76,1054.64,"Yes."],[1054.64,1059.06,"It is a managed service, and the provider has access to Nordvia's systems."],[1059.06,1061.23,"Does it support a critical function?"],[1061.23,1064.84,"Yes: claims handling is a core insurance function."],[1064.84,1067.72,"Is it outsourcing under Solvency II?"],[1067.72,1070.89,"Very likely, but that is to be confirmed with Risk."],[1071.17,1075.89,"Notice the wording: Procurement records very likely and names who confirms."],[1075.89,1077.29,"Personal data?"],[1077.29,1082.33,"Yes, claims data, which includes sensitive information about policyholders."],[1082.33,1093.13,"And then the gap: there is no tested exit plan, which DORA Article 28, paragraph 8 requires for ICT services supporting critical or important functions."],[1093.63,1097.71,"Now the last line, which is the real lesson."],[1097.71,1101.27,"Lead time: start now for a December 2027 expiry."],[1101.27,1102.36,"Why so early?"],[1102.64,1116.66,"Because an exit plan has to be written and tested, the market analysis must include concentration risk, the request must carry the full set of clauses, due diligence takes time, and the supervisor must be notified."],[1116.66,1123.05,"Add the notice period on the current contract, and a project that looks two years away is already urgent."],[1123.33,1126.05,"The triage does not only add requirements."],[1126.05,1127.93,"It drives the timeline."],[1127.93,1131.89,"Northbyte will come back in Session 4 as our exit-plan case."],[1132.83,1134.12,"Now a contrast."],[1134.12,1137.8,"The same triage, applied to office cleaning, project P1."],[1138.3,1140.86,"Outsourcing of a critical function?"],[1140.86,1142.31,"No."],[1142.31,1143.04,"ICT service?"],[1143.04,1142.07,"No."],[1142.07,1143.04,"Personal data?"],[1143.04,1146.96,"Limited: access badges and data about the staff on site."],[1146.96,1147.97,"Staff transfer?"],[1147.97,1150.67,"Possible, if the supplier changes."],[1150.67,1159.16,"Under the Acquired Rights Directive and national rules, cleaning staff may transfer from the old supplier to the new one."],[1159.44,1161.3,"So check the national rules."],[1161.3,1170.03,"The consequence is concrete: staff data must go into the tender pack, so bidders can price correctly, and mobilisation will take longer."],[1170.53,1172.54,"Here is the lesson of this slide."],[1172.54,1174.81,"A no is also an answer worth recording."],[1174.81,1180.5,"When the triage says no to outsourcing and no to DORA, the process can stay light."],[1180.5,1183.73,"You do not need a Risk committee for a cleaning contract."],[1183.73,1186.27,"But you only know that because you asked."],[1186.55,1193.49,"And the staff transfer question, which many buyers forget for cleaning and security, can change the savings case."],[1193.49,1199.96,"If bidders assume they will inherit the current staff at current terms, the price gap between them may shrink."],[1199.96,1204.94,"Better to know that at intake than to promise savings that cannot materialise."],[1205.89,1208.51,"How can all of this be built into daily practice?"],[1208.51,1210.91,"Through the sourcing request in Coupa."],[1210.91,1213.76,"The slide shows good practice in four cards."],[1214.27,1222.43,"First, classification questions in the intake form itself: outsourcing, ICT, critical function, personal data, staff."],[1222.43,1226.76,"Short yes, no or not sure questions that a requester can answer."],[1226.76,1228.84,"Second, automatic routing."],[1229.12,1237.1,"A yes or a not sure sends the request to Risk, Compliance or Information Security, without the buyer having to remember."],[1237.1,1238.07,"Third, reuse in onboarding."],[1238.07,1249.3,"The same questions appear again in Supplier Information Management, when the supplier is onboarded, so the answers stay consistent from intake to contract."],[1249.58,1252.48,"Fourth, and this is important, check your tenant."],[1252.48,1257.63,"Which intake form and which routing your entity uses is a configuration choice."],[1257.63,1260.53,"Some entities have built this, others have not."],[1261.02,1263.73,"So do not assume your Coupa does this today."],[1263.73,1266.95,"Confirm it in your Coupa tenant after this lesson."],[1266.95,1273.98,"And if it does not, the four questions of the triage can still go into your own intake checklist tomorrow morning."],[1273.98,1277.49,"The tool helps, but the discipline does not depend on it."],[1278.44,1279.92,"A moment of reflection."],[1279.92,1285.47,"In your entity, when is the regulatory question, outsourcing or DORA, usually asked?"],[1285.47,1286.56,"At intake?"],[1286.56,1288.24,"When drafting the request?"],[1288.24,1289.69,"At contract stage?"],[1289.69,1291.82,"When Risk or Legal raises it?"],[1291.82,1292.94,"Or rarely?"],[1292.94,1296.49,"Think of your last ICT purchase and pick your honest answer."],[1296.77,1303.74,"If it was anything later than intake, you now know the cost: requirements arriving after bidders have already priced."],[1304.69,1310.57,"Now the exercise for module 1.2: intake and triage for your Sourcing Case."],[1310.57,1316.66,"It is in your workbook under module 1.2 in the Modules tab, and it takes about 20 minutes."],[1317.16,1318.74,"Here are the five steps."],[1318.74,1324.23,"First, write the need in business terms, as an outcome, not as a supplier name."],[1324.23,1326.98,"Not, we need a new contract with this agency."],[1326.98,1332.13,"But, we need creative support for six campaigns a year, at a predictable cost."],[1332.13,1335.7,"Second, challenge volume, frequency and specification."],[1335.98,1339.42,"Third, check existing contracts and Group frameworks."],[1339.42,1345.0,"Fourth, estimate the total contract value, and the latest date to start the sourcing."],[1345.0,1350.61,"Fifth, tick the regulatory triage questions that are a yes or a possible yes."],[1351.12,1354.3,"One tip from facilitators who run this exercise."],[1354.3,1358.48,"The most common gap is the notice period and the auto-renewal date."],[1358.48,1363.32,"People know the end date of their contract, but not the notice period."],[1363.32,1363.68,"So find it."],[1363.68,1369.59,"Count back: the end date, minus the notice period, minus the time the sourcing will take."],[1369.59,1371.6,"That is your latest start date."],[1371.88,1374.1,"Mark everything you do not know yet."],[1374.1,1378.22,"Those gaps become questions for your business owner during the fieldwork."],[1378.22,1383.12,"Pause the video now if you want to do the exercise, and come back when you are ready."],[1384.07,1384.83,"Welcome back."],[1384.83,1385.95,"Four debrief questions."],[1385.95,1388.87,"Whose need statement still names a supplier?"],[1388.87,1392.1,"Who found an existing contract or framework?"],[1392.1,1396.46,"Who ticked a DORA or outsourcing question, and who will confirm it?"],[1396.46,1400.41,"And how many found that their real start date is already close?"],[1400.91,1402.59,"Let me comment on each."],[1402.59,1406.28,"If your need statement still names a supplier, rewrite it."],[1406.28,1410.37,"A need that names a supplier has already chosen the route to market."],[1410.37,1414.73,"If you found an existing contract or framework, congratulations."],[1415.01,1422.83,"That may feel like losing your project, but it is a real saving, achieved at almost no cost, and it is exactly what intake is for."],[1422.83,1430.16,"If you ticked a DORA or outsourcing question, write down the name of the person who will confirm it, and a date."],[1430.44,1438.2,"And if you discovered that your real start date is close, or already passed, you have learned the most valuable thing in this module."],[1438.2,1441.53,"Better to learn it now than when the auto-renewal triggers."],[1442.48,1444.85,"Let me recap module 1.2 in three lines."],[1444.85,1449.8,"First, validate the need and check existing contracts before going to market."],[1449.8,1458.61,"The cheapest saving is the purchase that does not happen, and the quickest win is often a Group framework that already exists."],[1458.89,1463.93,"Second, think in total contract value, and count back from the notice period."],[1463.93,1469.58,"Approval levels follow the total, and the notice period sets the real deadline."],[1469.58,1475.23,"Third, triage outsourcing, DORA, personal data and staff at intake, not at signature."],[1475.51,1481.61,"A yes changes the market analysis, the strategy, the request, the evaluation and the contract."],[1481.61,1483.66,"A no keeps the process light."],[1483.66,1485.11,"Either way, record it."],[1485.61,1489.31,"With a validated need, we are ready for the next step of the chain."],[1489.31,1498.15,"In the next lesson, we build the category fact base: the spend cube, the contract inventory, the demand drivers and the total cost of ownership."],[1498.15,1499.22,"See you there."]]},{"id":"s1-l3","session":"s1","title":"Know the category: spend, contracts, TCO","summary":"Build a category fact base with a spend cube, a contract inventory and demand drivers, and a total cost of ownership baseline that shows what a purchase really costs beyond the price.","from":45,"to":62,"audio":"audio/s1-l3.mp3","duration":1457.9,"segs":[[45,0.6,12.55,"Welcome back. We have validated the need and run the triage. Now we move to module 1.3: Know the category. Spend, contracts, stakeholders and total cost of ownership."],[45,13.05,59.58,"The subtitle sums up the questions we will answer: what is bought, from whom, under which contracts, and what it really costs. In this lesson we will build the four parts of a category fact base. First the spend cube and its three axes, with some real patterns from our Group. Then the contract inventory, and why the notice period is the real deadline. Then demand drivers, which tell you whether you have a price problem or a demand problem. And finally total cost of ownership, using Lisa Ellram's framework, with a worked example on Nordvia's office cleaning. We will close with Nordvia's IT consulting spend, where the fact base tells the whole story before any market analysis, and then you will build the fact base for your own Sourcing Case."],[46,60.53,64.9,"No strategy without insight. That is the whole module in four words."],[46,65.4,107.14,"Remember O'Brien's five stages from the first lesson. Insight comes before Innovation. And O'Brien describes Insight very concretely: know what is bought, from whom, at what price, under which contracts, and why. Notice the last word. Why. It is not enough to know that Nordvia spends 6 million euros on IT consulting. You need to know why it spends that much, what drives the spend up or down, and who decides. This module produces the fact base that your whole Sourcing Case will rest on. When the capstone panel asks why you chose a panel rather than a tender, or a negotiation rather than an auction, the answer will come from this fact base."],[47,108.09,112.08,"So what is in the category fact base? Four parts, on four cards."],[47,112.58,137.62,"First, the spend cube. What is bought, from whom, and who buys it, over 24 months. Second, the contract inventory. For each contract: the value, the dates, the notice period, the indexation, and the regulatory flags. Third, the demand drivers. What makes the spend go up or down. Fourth, the total cost of ownership baseline. Every cost the purchase causes, not only the price."],[47,138.12,163.75,"There is a fifth element, the stakeholder map. You built that in the first course of the academy: the budget owner, the demand owner, the specification owner, and the gatekeepers such as Risk, Legal and Information Security. We do not repeat it here, but it belongs in your fact base. A category where the demand owner and the budget owner are different people behaves very differently from one where they are the same person."],[47,164.25,190.41,"Think of Nordvia's creative agencies, project P7. The budget sits with the marketing director, but the briefs are written by several brand managers, and every campaign tends to be briefed to a favourite agency. If you only talk to the budget owner, you will design a rate card that nobody follows. The demand owners decide who gets the work. So map them, and bring at least one of them into your fieldwork conversations."],[48,191.36,196.67,"Let us start with the spend cube. It has three axes, and the slide shows them as a flow."],[48,197.17,215.66,"The first axis is what: the category and sub-category. The second is from whom: the supplier, grouped by its parent company. The third is who buys: the business area, the site, the entity. And the cube is built over at least 24 months, so that one unusual year does not mislead you."],[48,216.16,247.68,"Why three axes? Because each combination reveals something. Look along the supplier axis and you see concentration: is the spend with one supplier, or spread across many? Look across the buyer axis and you see fragmentation and duplication: are five areas buying the same thing from five different suppliers, at five different prices? That is exactly what the cube is for. It shows concentration, fragmentation and duplication, and each of those points to a different strategy."],[48,248.18,278.09,"One practical point that facilitators insist on. Group suppliers by their parent. The same firm often appears under several supplier records: a national subsidiary, a regional office, an old name before a merger, a typo in the vendor master. If you analyse records instead of parents, a supplier that takes 20 percent of your spend can look like four suppliers with 5 percent each. Your view of concentration, and of your own buying power, will be wrong."],[49,279.04,290.02,"This chart shows where the negotiable spend sits in our Group. The figures are anonymised and rounded. Each bar is a band of supplier size, by annual spend with that supplier."],[49,290.52,320.39,"Read the bars with me. Suppliers below 50 thousand euros a year carry 12 percent of the spend. Suppliers from 50 thousand to 250 thousand euros carry 25 percent. From 250 thousand to 1 million euros, 34 percent. And above 1 million euros, 29 percent. Now the footer adds the key fact. Suppliers below 50 thousand euros are 86 percent of all supplier records, but only 12 percent of the spend."],[49,320.89,350.34,"What does that tell us? That most of your supplier list is the tail, and most of your spend is not. The tail is a channel question: catalogues, spot buys, purchasing cards, perhaps a marketplace. You do not run a decision chain for a 3 thousand euro purchase. The sourcing effort belongs above 50 thousand euros, where the remaining 88 percent of the spend sits. If your team spends its time chasing small suppliers, this chart is the argument for changing that."],[49,350.84,379.25,"Here is how this looks in practice. Nordvia's office supplies and print spend, project P5, is 2.3 million euros a year across 31 suppliers, with about 1,900 purchase orders a year below 1,000 euros. Running tenders for each of those small orders would be absurd. The right answer is a catalogue with a standard range, so that the small orders flow through one channel, and the buyer's time goes where the value is."],[50,380.2,388.63,"Now look at the who buys axis across the whole Group. This is anonymised Group data, and it is one of the most striking facts in the course."],[50,389.13,420.77,"About 90 supplier groups work for two or more of our entities. Together, around 50 million euros is managed in some 360 separate local relationships. Think about that. The same supplier groups, serving several entities, each one negotiating on its own, with its own contract, its own price and its own terms. Here is a concrete example. Advisory firms: about 12 million euros spent in 9 entities, through 60 different supplier records, and no Group agreement."],[50,421.27,458.05,"In the language of the Purchasing Chessboard, bundling this demand is volume bundling, combining demand to get better terms, and co-sourcing, buying together with other units. Both sit in the manage spend strategy. What is remarkable is that this is demand power we already have, and do not use. We do not need to grow the business or find new suppliers. We only need to see the demand together. When you build your own spend cube, ask: do other entities buy this from the same supplier group? This question matters again in module 1.5, when we look at our power position."],[51,459.0,468.05,"The second part of the fact base is the contract inventory. Each row of this table is a field you need for every contract, and the reason it matters."],[51,468.55,509.39,"Value and term give you the total contract value and the approval level. The end date and the notice period give you the real deadline. Auto-renewal tells you the default: what happens if nobody acts. Price and indexation tell you where the uplifts come from. Is the price linked to an inflation index, a wage index, or a fixed annual increase? Service levels and KPIs give you the baseline for the new specification. If you do not know how the current supplier performs, you cannot specify what you want next time. And the regulatory flags tell you whether the contract is in the outsourcing register or the DORA register of information."],[51,509.89,538.47,"Here is a Nordvia example of what the inventory reveals. Nordvia's security and reception contract, project P6, saw its hourly rates indexed twice in two years, and it has no KPIs at all. Both facts come straight from the inventory. Together they tell you that the price has risen without any evidence of performance. That is a strong argument for a retender with a proper performance framework, and you know it before talking to a single supplier."],[51,538.97,575.74,"A word on auto-renewal, because it is where many contracts quietly lose value. An auto-renewal clause is not bad in itself. For a low-risk service that works well, it can save effort. The problem is when it becomes the default by accident. Imagine a facilities contract that renews for another three years because the notice letter was due during the summer holidays and nobody had the date. The price, the indexation and the service levels from five years ago now run for three more years. The inventory exists so that every renewal is a decision, not an accident."],[52,576.69,582.96,"Now a sentence to write on a sticky note. The notice period, not the end date, is the real deadline."],[52,583.46,619.9,"Look at Nordvia's Kelvar contract. The renewal is in June 2027. The notice period is nine months. And there is an auto-renewal clause. So what is the real deadline? If Nordvia wants to change anything, to reduce licences, renegotiate the price, or even keep the option of switching, it must give notice nine months before June 2027. That means by September 2026. Miss that date, and the contract renews automatically, on the vendor's terms. Remember that the last renewal came with a 9 percent uplift."],[52,620.4,659.0,"And the notice date is still not your start date. You need to work back further. Here is the formula: the end date, minus the notice period, minus the sourcing lead time. For Kelvar, you need a usage analysis, a benchmark, a credible alternative and a negotiation plan before you give notice. If that preparation takes six months, as an illustration, the work must begin around March 2026. Try it now for your own Sourcing Case. Take your end date, subtract your notice period, subtract a realistic lead time. Many people discover that the real start date is much closer than they thought."],[53,659.95,669.38,"The third part of the fact base is demand drivers. What makes the spend go up or down? The table shows drivers and levers for five indirect categories."],[53,669.88,716.26,"For cleaning, the drivers are the number of sites, the square metres, the cleaning frequency and the occupancy. The levers are the specification and the frequency. For software, the drivers are users, modules and the licence metrics. The lever is a usage audit to find shelfware, licences that are paid for but not used. For IT consulting, the drivers are projects, roles and seniority. The levers are demand management and discipline in writing statements of work. For creative agencies, the drivers are campaigns, briefs and formats. The levers are brief discipline and a rate card. And for office supplies and print, the drivers are headcount and ordering habits. The levers are a catalogue and a standard range."],[53,716.76,751.9,"Here is the rule that makes this table useful. If the driver is demand, manage demand. If it is price, negotiate. And most categories have both. Take Kelvar again. The case data estimates that 20 to 25 percent of the named-user licences are not used. That is a demand problem, and no price negotiation will fix it. If you negotiate a 5 percent discount on licences that a quarter of your users never open, you have optimised the wrong thing. The usage audit comes first. Then you negotiate the price on what you really need."],[53,752.4,766.41,"So for every category, ask two questions in your fact base. What would happen to the spend if we bought the same volume at a better price? And what would happen if we bought less, or bought differently? The second question is often the bigger one."],[54,767.36,779.7,"Now the fourth part, and the one that most changes how a buyer thinks: total cost of ownership. The reference is Lisa Ellram, in her work of 1993 and 1995."],[54,780.2,799.61,"Ellram describes total cost of ownership as both a methodology and a philosophy. The methodology is a structured way to count costs. The philosophy is simply this: look beyond the price. The widely used version of her framework groups the costs into three phases, around the transaction itself."],[54,800.11,825.92,"Pre-transaction costs come before the purchase: writing the specification, researching the market, qualifying suppliers, onboarding them, and mobilising the service. Transaction costs are the ones linked to the purchase itself: the price, the fees, delivery, ordering and invoicing. And post-transaction costs come after: quality failures, managing the contract, support, and in the end, switching and exit."],[54,826.42,858.17,"Ellram also studied how companies actually use total cost of ownership. In her 1995 article, based on case studies of eleven firms, she distinguished models that put a money value on every cost from models that use weighted scores for factors that are hard to price, and she found them used both for selecting suppliers and for evaluating them over time. For us the practical message is simple. Put a money value on what you can, use a clear judgement for the rest, and always state your assumptions."],[54,858.67,879.54,"Why does this matter so much in indirect procurement? Because in services, many of the biggest costs never appear on the supplier's invoice. They appear as your own people's time, as rework, as complaints, as a painful migration three years later. A buyer who compares only prices compares only the middle row of this table."],[55,880.49,886.93,"Let us make this concrete for indirect categories. The table lists what sits beyond the price in four of them."],[55,887.43,923.94,"For cleaning: mobilisation of a new supplier, a possible staff transfer, quality failures, and extras ordered outside the contract. For IT consulting: expenses on top of day rates, the internal effort to manage many firms, and the knowledge that walks out of the door when a consultant leaves. For software and SaaS: the implementation project, shelfware, uplifts on support fees, and the cost of migration and exit when you eventually leave. For agencies: third-party costs passed through, mark-ups on production, and the rights to use the creative work."],[55,924.44,952.81,"Here is a short story about the last one. A company runs a competitive pitch for a creative agency and picks the one with the lowest fee. A year later it wants to reuse a successful campaign in another market, and discovers that the image rights were only licensed for one country and one year. The rights cost more than the fee saving. Nothing on the original bid showed it. Before moving on, add one hidden cost from your own category to this list."],[56,953.76,965.73,"Now a worked example: a total cost of ownership baseline for Nordvia's office cleaning. Please note the footer. Only the contract price is case data. The other figures are illustrative."],[56,966.23,1008.96,"Let us walk down the table. The contract price for the three sites is 2.40 million euros a year. That is a transaction cost. Extras outside the contract, such as deep cleans or event set-ups, add 0.15 million euros. Also transaction. Internal contract management, half a full-time equivalent, adds 0.04 million euros. That is post-transaction. Complaints and re-cleaning add 0.03 million euros, also post-transaction. And mobilisation, if the supplier changes, spread over each year of the contract, adds 0.05 million euros. That one is pre-transaction."],[56,1009.46,1059.9,"Add it up. The total is about 2.67 million euros a year, so in this illustration the price is about 90 percent of the real cost, and around 0.27 million euros a year sits outside the price. Now the point of the exercise. Suppose a bidder offers a price 5 percent lower than today, a saving of about 0.12 million euros. But its method doubles complaints, and it needs a full mobilisation. Suddenly the saving is much smaller, and in a weak bid it can disappear altogether. A cheaper bid that doubles complaints or needs a costly mobilisation may not be cheaper. That is why the baseline matters. It gives you something to compare bids against that is more honest than the price alone, and in Session 3 it will help you design the price and quality weighting."],[57,1060.85,1070.11,"Now let us look at a Nordvia fact base that already tells a story. This is project P2, IT consulting. The data is fictional case data."],[57,1070.61,1104.45,"Four numbers. 6.1 million euros a year, with no framework. 12 firms, through 38 call-offs. 61 percent of the spend with the top three firms. And for the same senior profile, day rates range from 820 euros to 1,450 euros. Pause on that last one. The same profile, at the same company, costs between 820 and 1,450 euros a day, depending on which firm and which call-off. That is a difference of more than 75 percent."],[57,1104.95,1122.83,"Notice that we have not done any market analysis yet. We have not spoken to a single supplier. And yet the fact base already tells us a great deal. The spend is concentrated in a few firms, but it is not structured. Prices are not managed. The work is going to whoever is called."],[57,1123.33,1153.96,"Think about what this means for Nordvia's IT director. Every time a project needs a senior developer, someone calls a firm they know. The firm quotes a rate. Nobody checks it against the other eleven firms. Over 38 call-offs, that pattern adds up. If, as an illustration, a third of the senior days were bought at the top of the range instead of the middle, the cost would be hundreds of thousands of euros a year higher than necessary, without anyone ever making a bad decision on purpose."],[58,1154.91,1159.16,"So what does the P2 fact base say? Let us read it as a worked example."],[58,1159.66,1222.98,"First, the problem is not a missing tender. It is a missing structure. Running one big tender for all IT consulting would not solve the problem, because the needs are different every time. Second, there is no rate card, so every call-off becomes a new price discussion. That explains the range from 820 to 1,450 euros. Third, there are no call-off rules, so favourites get the work. The case data adds that many of the call-offs were placed without any competition. Fourth, the demand is real, but unplanned. That is a demand-management question. IT projects start, and consultants are called, without a forecast of roles and seniority. And fifth, labour law and DORA apply where consultants access Nordvia's systems. An IT consultant with system access is part of an ICT service, so the register of information and the contract clauses come into play, and long-running staff augmentation may raise national agency-work rules."],[58,1223.48,1241.02,"This prepares the discussion in Session 2, where we will look at panels of preferred suppliers. But do not design the panel yet. The discipline of the decision chain is to finish the Insight first. We still need to understand the supply market and our power position before we choose the route."],[59,1241.97,1266.61,"A moment of reflection. Which data is hardest to get for your Sourcing Case? Clean spend by supplier? Contract terms and notice periods? Demand drivers and forecasts? Internal costs beyond the price? Or market and supplier information? Think about it for a moment. Whatever your answer, that data goes on your fieldwork list, and your business owner is often the best person to help you find it."],[60,1267.56,1281.66,"Now the exercise for module 1.3: the category fact base and the total cost of ownership baseline. It is in your workbook under module 1.3 in the Modules tab, and it takes about 25 minutes."],[60,1282.16,1317.73,"There are five steps. First, fill the fact base with the data you pulled for the pre-work: category boundaries, spend over the last 12 months, number of suppliers and the share of the top supplier, the buying areas, the contract dates and notice, the last sourcing event, the demand drivers and the key stakeholders. Second, list five or more cost elements beyond the price. Third, assign each to a phase: pre-transaction, transaction or post-transaction. Fourth, estimate each one and name your source. Fifth, write the one insight that will shape your strategy."],[60,1318.23,1335.72,"Two tips. Estimates are fine, as long as you label them as estimates. A labelled estimate is far more useful than a blank cell. And missing data is not a failure. It goes on your fieldwork list. Pause the video now if you want to build your fact base, and come back when you are done."],[61,1336.67,1350.81,"Welcome back. Four debrief questions. What surprised you in your own spend data? Which cost beyond the price is the biggest? Is your category a price problem or a demand problem? And what data will you ask for in the fieldwork?"],[61,1351.31,1383.79,"On the insight question, here is the difference between a weak insight and a good one. A weak insight is general: our spend is high and we should negotiate. A good insight is specific and surprising. For example: two thirds of our spend in this category goes to one supplier group, under three separate contracts, with three different indexation clauses. Or: a quarter of our licences have not been used in twelve months. A good insight points directly to a lever. If yours does not yet, sharpen it during the fieldwork."],[61,1384.29,1412.45,"On the price or demand question, many people answer both, and that is usually right. The useful follow-up is: which one is bigger? If your demand drivers show that volume grew by a third while the price per unit stayed flat, your strategy should start with demand. If volume is stable and the price rose through indexation, start with the commercial model. Write down your answer, because it shapes the six strategic decisions you will make in Session 2."],[62,1413.4,1440.11,"Let me recap module 1.3 in three lines. First, the spend cube, the contract inventory, the demand drivers and the total cost of ownership are the four parts of insight, with the stakeholder map from the first course alongside them. Second, the notice period is the real deadline: end date, minus notice, minus lead time. Third, total cost of ownership stops a cheap price from becoming an expensive purchase."],[62,1440.61,1456.65,"Notice that everything in this module was about our side: what we buy, how we buy it, what it costs us. We now know our side. In the next lesson we turn to the supplier's side: how to read the supply market, and how suppliers see us as a customer. See you there."]],"questions":[{"after":47,"q":"A buyer wants to start the cleaning strategy using only last year's invoices. Which part of the fact base would show whether the price can rise automatically next year?","options":["The spend cube","The contract inventory, with price and indexation terms","The demand drivers","The stakeholder map"],"answer":1,"explain":"Indexation sits in the contract inventory. Invoices show what was paid, not the mechanism that will change next year's price.","t":190.41},{"after":48,"q":"Your spend cube shows a consulting firm under four supplier records, each with 5 percent of the spend. What should you do before drawing conclusions?","options":["Treat them as four competing suppliers","Exclude them as tail spend","Group the records by parent, which may reveal one supplier with about 20 percent","Send each record a separate request for quotation"],"answer":2,"explain":"The same firm often appears under several records. Grouping by parent shows the true concentration and your real buying power with that supplier.","t":278.09},{"after":50,"q":"Three entities each buy from the same audit and advisory group under separate local contracts. Which Chessboard levers does this point to?","options":["Volume bundling and co-sourcing","Sole-source negotiation","An e-auction for each entity","Moving the spend to purchasing cards"],"answer":0,"explain":"Combining demand that is already spread across entities is volume bundling and co-sourcing, both in the manage spend strategy. It uses demand power the Group already has.","t":458.05},{"after":52,"q":"A contract ends on 30 June 2027 with a nine-month notice period and an auto-renewal clause. What happens if nobody acts by the end of September 2026?","options":["The contract ends on 30 June 2027 and a new tender is required","The contract renews automatically on the supplier's terms","The supplier must offer a discount to keep the business","The contract becomes month-to-month"],"answer":1,"explain":"With nine months' notice, the decision must be taken by the end of September 2026. After that, the auto-renewal clause becomes the default, which is why the notice period is the real deadline.","t":659.0},{"after":53,"q":"A usage audit shows that 20 to 25 percent of Nordvia's Kelvar named-user licences are unused. What should come first?","options":["Negotiate a larger discount on the full licence count","Reduce the licence count to real usage, then negotiate the price on what is needed","Run a three-quote request for quotation","Accept the uplift to protect the relationship"],"answer":1,"explain":"Shelfware is a demand problem: the driver is volume, not price. Manage the demand first, then negotiate on the right volume.","t":766.41},{"after":55,"q":"When choosing a new SaaS tool, which cost would Ellram's framework place in the post-transaction phase?","options":["Data migration and exit at the end of the contract","The monthly subscription fee","Market research before the selection","The implementation project at the start"],"answer":0,"explain":"Post-transaction costs come after the purchase, including support, quality failures, switching and exit. The subscription is a transaction cost, while research and implementation come before, in the pre-transaction phase.","t":952.81},{"after":56,"q":"In the cleaning example, a new bidder is 5 percent cheaper on price but needs a full mobilisation and its method is likely to double complaints. What is the right conclusion?","options":["Award it, because price is the main cost","Reject it, because mobilisation costs always outweigh savings","Compare it on total cost of ownership, because the price saving may shrink or disappear","Ask the incumbent to match the price"],"answer":2,"explain":"The TCO baseline adds mobilisation, complaints and management to the price. A lower price can turn into a higher total cost, so bids must be compared on the full picture.","t":1059.9},{"after":58,"q":"Nordvia's IT consulting fact base shows 12 firms, 38 call-offs, no rate card and day rates from 820 to 1,450 euros for the same profile. What is the core problem?","options":["Too few suppliers in the market","A missing structure: no rate card, no call-off rules and unplanned demand","Labour law prevents any competition","The top three firms charge too little"],"answer":1,"explain":"The fact base shows that the issue is not the lack of a single tender but the lack of structure. Without a rate card and call-off rules, every call-off is a new price discussion and favourites get the work.","t":1241.02},{"after":61,"q":"Which of these is the strongest insight to bring from your fact base into the strategy?","options":["Our spend in this category is high and we should negotiate harder","Suppliers in this market are generally expensive","We should run a tender soon","Two thirds of our spend goes to one supplier group under three contracts with three different indexation clauses"],"answer":3,"explain":"A good insight is specific and surprising, and it points to a lever. Here it points to consolidating the contracts and fixing one indexation mechanism.","t":1412.45}],"cues":[[0.6,1.57,"Welcome back."],[1.57,4.29,"We have validated the need and run the triage."],[4.29,8.29,"Now we move to module 1.3: Know the category."],[8.29,12.55,"Spend, contracts, stakeholders and total cost of ownership."],[13.05,20.79,"The subtitle sums up the questions we will answer: what is bought, from whom, under which contracts, and what it really costs."],[20.79,24.81,"In this lesson we will build the four parts of a category fact base."],[24.81,29.92,"First the spend cube and its three axes, with some real patterns from our Group."],[30.2,34.93,"Then the contract inventory, and why the notice period is the real deadline."],[34.93,40.83,"Then demand drivers, which tell you whether you have a price problem or a demand problem."],[40.83,48.57,"And finally total cost of ownership, using Lisa Ellram's framework, with a worked example on Nordvia's office cleaning."],[48.85,59.58,"We will close with Nordvia's IT consulting spend, where the fact base tells the whole story before any market analysis, and then you will build the fact base for your own Sourcing Case."],[60.53,62.49,"No strategy without insight."],[62.49,64.9,"That is the whole module in four words."],[65.4,68.8,"Remember O'Brien's five stages from the first lesson."],[68.8,71.33,"Insight comes before Innovation."],[71.33,79.38,"And O'Brien describes Insight very concretely: know what is bought, from whom, at what price, under which contracts, and why."],[79.38,81.05,"Notice the last word."],[81.05,81.95,"Why."],[81.95,86.84,"It is not enough to know that Nordvia spends 6 million euros on IT consulting."],[87.12,92.93,"You need to know why it spends that much, what drives the spend up or down, and who decides."],[92.93,97.68,"This module produces the fact base that your whole Sourcing Case will rest on."],[97.96,107.13,"When the capstone panel asks why you chose a panel rather than a tender, or a negotiation rather than an auction, the answer will come from this fact base."],[108.09,110.26,"So what is in the category fact base?"],[110.26,112.08,"Four parts, on four cards."],[112.58,114.02,"First, the spend cube."],[114.02,117.88,"What is bought, from whom, and who buys it, over 24 months."],[117.88,120.18,"Second, the contract inventory."],[120.18,126.81,"For each contract: the value, the dates, the notice period, the indexation, and the regulatory flags."],[126.81,129.08,"Third, the demand drivers."],[129.08,131.16,"What makes the spend go up or down."],[131.44,134.32,"Fourth, the total cost of ownership baseline."],[134.32,137.63,"Every cost the purchase causes, not only the price."],[138.12,140.88,"There is a fifth element, the stakeholder map."],[140.88,151.91,"You built that in the first course of the academy: the budget owner, the demand owner, the specification owner, and the gatekeepers such as Risk, Legal and Information Security."],[151.91,155.42,"We do not repeat it here, but it belongs in your fact base."],[155.7,163.74,"A category where the demand owner and the budget owner are different people behaves very differently from one where they are the same person."],[164.25,167.27,"Think of Nordvia's creative agencies, project P7."],[167.27,177.99,"The budget sits with the marketing director, but the briefs are written by several brand managers, and every campaign tends to be briefed to a favourite agency."],[177.99,183.07,"If you only talk to the budget owner, you will design a rate card that nobody follows."],[183.35,185.74,"The demand owners decide who gets the work."],[185.74,190.41,"So map them, and bring at least one of them into your fieldwork conversations."],[191.36,193.66,"Let us start with the spend cube."],[193.66,196.67,"It has three axes, and the slide shows them as a flow."],[197.17,201.0,"The first axis is what: the category and sub-category."],[201.0,205.54,"The second is from whom: the supplier, grouped by its parent company."],[205.54,209.04,"The third is who buys: the business area, the site, the entity."],[209.04,215.67,"And the cube is built over at least 24 months, so that one unusual year does not mislead you."],[216.16,217.02,"Why three axes?"],[217.02,219.78,"Because each combination reveals something."],[219.78,227.38,"Look along the supplier axis and you see concentration: is the spend with one supplier, or spread across many?"],[227.66,238.43,"Look across the buyer axis and you see fragmentation and duplication: are five areas buying the same thing from five different suppliers, at five different prices?"],[238.43,240.98,"That is exactly what the cube is for."],[240.98,247.67,"It shows concentration, fragmentation and duplication, and each of those points to a different strategy."],[248.18,251.47,"One practical point that facilitators insist on."],[251.47,253.78,"Group suppliers by their parent."],[253.78,264.67,"The same firm often appears under several supplier records: a national subsidiary, a regional office, an old name before a merger, a typo in the vendor master."],[264.95,273.63,"If you analyse records instead of parents, a supplier that takes 20 percent of your spend can look like four suppliers with 5 percent each."],[273.63,278.09,"Your view of concentration, and of your own buying power, will be wrong."],[279.04,283.33,"This chart shows where the negotiable spend sits in our Group."],[283.33,285.28,"The figures are anonymised and rounded."],[285.28,290.03,"Each bar is a band of supplier size, by annual spend with that supplier."],[290.52,292.3,"Read the bars with me."],[292.3,298.01,"Suppliers below 50 thousand euros a year carry 12 percent of the spend."],[298.01,303.77,"Suppliers from 50 thousand to 250 thousand euros carry 25 percent."],[303.77,307.17,"From 250 thousand to 1 million euros, 34 percent."],[307.17,310.14,"And above 1 million euros, 29 percent."],[310.14,312.66,"Now the footer adds the key fact."],[312.94,320.39,"Suppliers below 50 thousand euros are 86 percent of all supplier records, but only 12 percent of the spend."],[320.89,322.07,"What does that tell us?"],[322.07,326.5,"That most of your supplier list is the tail, and most of your spend is not."],[326.5,333.62,"The tail is a channel question: catalogues, spot buys, purchasing cards, perhaps a marketplace."],[333.62,337.38,"You do not run a decision chain for a 3 thousand euro purchase."],[337.66,344.31,"The sourcing effort belongs above 50 thousand euros, where the remaining 88 percent of the spend sits."],[344.31,350.33,"If your team spends its time chasing small suppliers, this chart is the argument for changing that."],[350.84,352.86,"Here is how this looks in practice."],[352.86,364.89,"Nordvia's office supplies and print spend, project P5, is 2.3 million euros a year across 31 suppliers, with about 1,900 purchase orders a year below 1,000 euros."],[364.89,370.34,"Running tenders for each of those small orders would be absurd."],[370.62,379.26,"The right answer is a catalogue with a standard range, so that the small orders flow through one channel, and the buyer's time goes where the value is."],[380.2,383.38,"Now look at the who buys axis across the whole Group."],[383.38,388.63,"This is anonymised Group data, and it is one of the most striking facts in the course."],[389.13,392.98,"About 90 supplier groups work for two or more of our entities."],[392.98,399.23,"Together, around 50 million euros is managed in some 360 separate local relationships."],[399.23,400.31,"Think about that."],[400.31,409.95,"The same supplier groups, serving several entities, each one negotiating on its own, with its own contract, its own price and its own terms."],[410.23,412.14,"Here is a concrete example."],[412.14,420.77,"Advisory firms: about 12 million euros spent in 9 entities, through 60 different supplier records, and no Group agreement."],[421.27,432.23,"In the language of the Purchasing Chessboard, bundling this demand is volume bundling, combining demand to get better terms, and co-sourcing, buying together with other units."],[432.23,434.76,"Both sit in the manage spend strategy."],[434.76,440.04,"What is remarkable is that this is demand power we already have, and do not use."],[440.32,443.79,"We do not need to grow the business or find new suppliers."],[443.79,446.22,"We only need to see the demand together."],[446.22,452.52,"When you build your own spend cube, ask: do other entities buy this from the same supplier group?"],[452.52,458.05,"This question matters again in module 1.5, when we look at our power position."],[459.0,462.55,"The second part of the fact base is the contract inventory."],[462.55,468.05,"Each row of this table is a field you need for every contract, and the reason it matters."],[468.55,473.15,"Value and term give you the total contract value and the approval level."],[473.15,476.93,"The end date and the notice period give you the real deadline."],[476.93,481.04,"Auto-renewal tells you the default: what happens if nobody acts."],[481.04,485.0,"Price and indexation tell you where the uplifts come from."],[485.28,490.98,"Is the price linked to an inflation index, a wage index, or a fixed annual increase?"],[490.98,494.89,"Service levels and KPIs give you the baseline for the new specification."],[494.89,501.73,"If you do not know how the current supplier performs, you cannot specify what you want next time."],[502.01,509.39,"And the regulatory flags tell you whether the contract is in the outsourcing register or the DORA register of information."],[509.89,513.49,"Here is a Nordvia example of what the inventory reveals."],[513.49,522.17,"Nordvia's security and reception contract, project P6, saw its hourly rates indexed twice in two years, and it has no KPIs at all."],[522.17,524.42,"Both facts come straight from the inventory."],[524.42,530.9,"Together they tell you that the price has risen without any evidence of performance."],[531.18,538.48,"That is a strong argument for a retender with a proper performance framework, and you know it before talking to a single supplier."],[538.97,544.15,"A word on auto-renewal, because it is where many contracts quietly lose value."],[544.15,547.39,"An auto-renewal clause is not bad in itself."],[547.39,551.04,"For a low-risk service that works well, it can save effort."],[551.04,554.8,"The problem is when it becomes the default by accident."],[555.08,564.44,"Imagine a facilities contract that renews for another three years because the notice letter was due during the summer holidays and nobody had the date."],[564.44,570.63,"The price, the indexation and the service levels from five years ago now run for three more years."],[570.91,575.73,"The inventory exists so that every renewal is a decision, not an accident."],[576.69,579.18,"Now a sentence to write on a sticky note."],[579.18,582.96,"The notice period, not the end date, is the real deadline."],[583.46,585.81,"Look at Nordvia's Kelvar contract."],[585.81,587.63,"The renewal is in June 2027."],[587.63,590.94,"The notice period is nine months."],[590.94,593.68,"And there is an auto-renewal clause."],[593.68,595.71,"So what is the real deadline?"],[595.99,607.86,"If Nordvia wants to change anything, to reduce licences, renegotiate the price, or even keep the option of switching, it must give notice nine months before June 2027."],[607.86,610.7,"That means by September 2026."],[610.7,616.06,"Miss that date, and the contract renews automatically, on the vendor's terms."],[616.34,619.9,"Remember that the last renewal came with a 9 percent uplift."],[620.4,623.82,"And the notice date is still not your start date."],[623.82,624.86,"You need to work back further."],[624.86,630.97,"Here is the formula: the end date, minus the notice period, minus the sourcing lead time."],[630.97,638.87,"For Kelvar, you need a usage analysis, a benchmark, a credible alternative and a negotiation plan before you give notice."],[639.15,645.73,"If that preparation takes six months, as an illustration, the work must begin around March 2026."],[645.73,648.8,"Try it now for your own Sourcing Case."],[648.8,653.47,"Take your end date, subtract your notice period, subtract a realistic lead time."],[653.47,658.99,"Many people discover that the real start date is much closer than they thought."],[659.95,662.96,"The third part of the fact base is demand drivers."],[662.96,665.15,"What makes the spend go up or down?"],[665.15,669.38,"The table shows drivers and levers for five indirect categories."],[669.88,677.42,"For cleaning, the drivers are the number of sites, the square metres, the cleaning frequency and the occupancy."],[677.42,680.74,"The levers are the specification and the frequency."],[680.74,684.61,"For software, the drivers are users, modules and the licence metrics."],[684.61,690.19,"The lever is a usage audit to find shelfware, licences that are paid for but not used."],[690.47,695.51,"For IT consulting, the drivers are projects, roles and seniority."],[695.51,700.2,"The levers are demand management and discipline in writing statements of work."],[700.2,704.91,"For creative agencies, the drivers are campaigns, briefs and formats."],[704.91,708.26,"The levers are brief discipline and a rate card."],[708.54,713.39,"And for office supplies and print, the drivers are headcount and ordering habits."],[713.39,716.26,"The levers are a catalogue and a standard range."],[716.76,719.49,"Here is the rule that makes this table useful."],[719.49,722.62,"If the driver is demand, manage demand."],[722.62,724.63,"If it is price, negotiate."],[724.63,726.93,"And most categories have both."],[726.93,727.4,"Take Kelvar again."],[727.4,734.32,"The case data estimates that 20 to 25 percent of the named-user licences are not used."],[734.32,738.37,"That is a demand problem, and no price negotiation will fix it."],[738.65,746.55,"If you negotiate a 5 percent discount on licences that a quarter of your users never open, you have optimised the wrong thing."],[746.55,748.71,"The usage audit comes first."],[748.71,751.9,"Then you negotiate the price on what you really need."],[752.4,755.47,"So for every category, ask two questions in your fact base."],[755.47,760.19,"What would happen to the spend if we bought the same volume at a better price?"],[760.19,763.74,"And what would happen if we bought less, or bought differently?"],[763.74,766.42,"The second question is often the bigger one."],[767.36,775.02,"Now the fourth part, and the one that most changes how a buyer thinks: total cost of ownership."],[775.02,779.69,"The reference is Lisa Ellram, in her work of 1993 and 1995."],[780.2,785.6,"Ellram describes total cost of ownership as both a methodology and a philosophy."],[785.6,788.78,"The methodology is a structured way to count costs."],[788.78,792.17,"The philosophy is simply this: look beyond the price."],[792.17,799.61,"The widely used version of her framework groups the costs into three phases, around the transaction itself."],[800.11,810.45,"Pre-transaction costs come before the purchase: writing the specification, researching the market, qualifying suppliers, onboarding them, and mobilising the service."],[810.45,818.14,"Transaction costs are the ones linked to the purchase itself: the price, the fees, delivery, ordering and invoicing."],[818.42,825.93,"And post-transaction costs come after: quality failures, managing the contract, support, and in the end, switching and exit."],[826.42,831.05,"Ellram also studied how companies actually use total cost of ownership."],[831.33,849.27,"In her 1995 article, based on case studies of eleven firms, she distinguished models that put a money value on every cost from models that use weighted scores for factors that are hard to price, and she found them used both for selecting suppliers and for evaluating them over time."],[849.55,852.02,"For us the practical message is simple."],[852.02,858.17,"Put a money value on what you can, use a clear judgement for the rest, and always state your assumptions."],[858.67,861.74,"Why does this matter so much in indirect procurement?"],[861.74,867.45,"Because in services, many of the biggest costs never appear on the supplier's invoice."],[867.45,874.54,"They appear as your own people's time, as rework, as complaints, as a painful migration three years later."],[874.82,879.54,"A buyer who compares only prices compares only the middle row of this table."],[880.49,883.57,"Let us make this concrete for indirect categories."],[883.57,886.93,"The table lists what sits beyond the price in four of them."],[887.43,895.73,"For cleaning: mobilisation of a new supplier, a possible staff transfer, quality failures, and extras ordered outside the contract."],[895.73,906.18,"For IT consulting: expenses on top of day rates, the internal effort to manage many firms, and the knowledge that walks out of the door when a consultant leaves."],[906.46,916.68,"For software and SaaS: the implementation project, shelfware, uplifts on support fees, and the cost of migration and exit when you eventually leave."],[916.68,923.93,"For agencies: third-party costs passed through, mark-ups on production, and the rights to use the creative work."],[924.44,926.99,"Here is a short story about the last one."],[926.99,933.53,"A company runs a competitive pitch for a creative agency and picks the one with the lowest fee."],[933.53,943.23,"A year later it wants to reuse a successful campaign in another market, and discovers that the image rights were only licensed for one country and one year."],[943.51,945.85,"The rights cost more than the fee saving."],[945.85,948.29,"Nothing on the original bid showed it."],[948.29,952.82,"Before moving on, add one hidden cost from your own category to this list."],[953.76,959.4,"Now a worked example: a total cost of ownership baseline for Nordvia's office cleaning."],[959.4,961.11,"Please note the footer."],[961.11,963.74,"Only the contract price is case data."],[963.74,965.73,"The other figures are illustrative."],[966.23,968.48,"Let us walk down the table."],[968.48,972.5,"The contract price for the three sites is 2.40 million euros a year."],[972.5,975.59,"That is a transaction cost."],[975.59,983.09,"Extras outside the contract, such as deep cleans or event set-ups, add 0.15 million euros."],[983.09,983.83,"Also transaction."],[983.83,989.82,"Internal contract management, half a full-time equivalent, adds 0.04 million euros."],[990.1,991.79,"That is post-transaction."],[991.79,997.79,"Complaints and re-cleaning add 0.03 million euros, also post-transaction."],[997.79,1007.13,"And mobilisation, if the supplier changes, spread over each year of the contract, adds 0.05 million euros."],[1007.13,1008.96,"That one is pre-transaction."],[1009.46,1010.25,"Add it up."],[1010.25,1023.61,"The total is about 2.67 million euros a year, so in this illustration the price is about 90 percent of the real cost, and around 0.27 million euros a year sits outside the price."],[1023.61,1025.24,"Now the point of the exercise."],[1025.24,1032.69,"Suppose a bidder offers a price 5 percent lower than today, a saving of about 0.12 million euros."],[1032.97,1037.07,"But its method doubles complaints, and it needs a full mobilisation."],[1037.07,1042.5,"Suddenly the saving is much smaller, and in a weak bid it can disappear altogether."],[1042.5,1048.19,"A cheaper bid that doubles complaints or needs a costly mobilisation may not be cheaper."],[1048.19,1050.32,"That is why the baseline matters."],[1050.6,1059.9,"It gives you something to compare bids against that is more honest than the price alone, and in Session 3 it will help you design the price and quality weighting."],[1060.85,1065.61,"Now let us look at a Nordvia fact base that already tells a story."],[1065.61,1067.93,"This is project P2, IT consulting."],[1067.93,1070.11,"The data is fictional case data."],[1070.61,1071.66,"Four numbers."],[1071.66,1075.29,"6.1 million euros a year, with no framework."],[1075.29,1078.32,"12 firms, through 38 call-offs."],[1078.32,1081.86,"61 percent of the spend with the top three firms."],[1081.86,1089.33,"And for the same senior profile, day rates range from 820 euros to 1,450 euros."],[1089.33,1091.05,"Pause on that last one."],[1091.33,1101.37,"The same profile, at the same company, costs between 820 and 1,450 euros a day, depending on which firm and which call-off."],[1101.37,1104.45,"That is a difference of more than 75 percent."],[1104.95,1108.64,"Notice that we have not done any market analysis yet."],[1108.64,1111.14,"We have not spoken to a single supplier."],[1111.14,1114.36,"And yet the fact base already tells us a great deal."],[1114.36,1118.46,"The spend is concentrated in a few firms, but it is not structured."],[1118.46,1120.41,"Prices are not managed."],[1120.41,1122.83,"The work is going to whoever is called."],[1123.33,1126.72,"Think about what this means for Nordvia's IT director."],[1126.72,1131.67,"Every time a project needs a senior developer, someone calls a firm they know."],[1131.67,1133.37,"The firm quotes a rate."],[1133.37,1137.0,"Nobody checks it against the other eleven firms."],[1137.0,1139.46,"Over 38 call-offs, that pattern adds up."],[1139.74,1153.97,"If, as an illustration, a third of the senior days were bought at the top of the range instead of the middle, the cost would be hundreds of thousands of euros a year higher than necessary, without anyone ever making a bad decision on purpose."],[1154.91,1157.14,"So what does the P2 fact base say?"],[1157.14,1159.16,"Let us read it as a worked example."],[1159.66,1162.24,"First, the problem is not a missing tender."],[1162.24,1165.12,"It is a missing structure."],[1165.12,1173.26,"Running one big tender for all IT consulting would not solve the problem, because the needs are different every time."],[1173.26,1179.04,"Second, there is no rate card, so every call-off becomes a new price discussion."],[1179.04,1182.34,"That explains the range from 820 to 1,450 euros."],[1182.62,1187.2,"Third, there are no call-off rules, so favourites get the work."],[1187.2,1191.78,"The case data adds that many of the call-offs were placed without any competition."],[1191.78,1194.31,"Fourth, the demand is real, but unplanned."],[1194.31,1197.27,"That is a demand-management question."],[1197.27,1202.95,"IT projects start, and consultants are called, without a forecast of roles and seniority."],[1203.23,1208.56,"And fifth, labour law and DORA apply where consultants access Nordvia's systems."],[1208.56,1222.98,"An IT consultant with system access is part of an ICT service, so the register of information and the contract clauses come into play, and long-running staff augmentation may raise national agency-work rules."],[1223.48,1229.19,"This prepares the discussion in Session 2, where we will look at panels of preferred suppliers."],[1229.19,1231.07,"But do not design the panel yet."],[1231.07,1235.81,"The discipline of the decision chain is to finish the Insight first."],[1235.81,1241.02,"We still need to understand the supply market and our power position before we choose the route."],[1241.97,1243.49,"A moment of reflection."],[1243.49,1247.07,"Which data is hardest to get for your Sourcing Case?"],[1247.07,1248.29,"Clean spend by supplier?"],[1248.29,1250.82,"Contract terms and notice periods?"],[1250.82,1252.69,"Demand drivers and forecasts?"],[1252.69,1255.15,"Internal costs beyond the price?"],[1255.15,1257.53,"Or market and supplier information?"],[1257.53,1259.08,"Think about it for a moment."],[1259.36,1266.61,"Whatever your answer, that data goes on your fieldwork list, and your business owner is often the best person to help you find it."],[1267.56,1275.02,"Now the exercise for module 1.3: the category fact base and the total cost of ownership baseline."],[1275.02,1281.66,"It is in your workbook under module 1.3 in the Modules tab, and it takes about 25 minutes."],[1282.16,1283.61,"There are five steps."],[1283.61,1300.93,"First, fill the fact base with the data you pulled for the pre-work: category boundaries, spend over the last 12 months, number of suppliers and the share of the top supplier, the buying areas, the contract dates and notice, the last sourcing event, the demand drivers and the key stakeholders."],[1301.21,1305.46,"Second, list five or more cost elements beyond the price."],[1305.46,1310.89,"Third, assign each to a phase: pre-transaction, transaction or post-transaction."],[1310.89,1314.22,"Fourth, estimate each one and name your source."],[1314.22,1317.73,"Fifth, write the one insight that will shape your strategy."],[1318.23,1318.69,"Two tips."],[1318.69,1322.54,"Estimates are fine, as long as you label them as estimates."],[1322.54,1325.44,"A labelled estimate is far more useful than a blank cell."],[1325.44,1329.09,"And missing data is not a failure."],[1329.09,1330.51,"It goes on your fieldwork list."],[1330.51,1335.72,"Pause the video now if you want to build your fact base, and come back when you are done."],[1336.67,1337.45,"Welcome back."],[1337.45,1338.58,"Four debrief questions."],[1338.58,1341.22,"What surprised you in your own spend data?"],[1341.22,1344.09,"Which cost beyond the price is the biggest?"],[1344.09,1348.36,"Is your category a price problem or a demand problem?"],[1348.36,1350.81,"And what data will you ask for in the fieldwork?"],[1351.31,1356.6,"On the insight question, here is the difference between a weak insight and a good one."],[1356.6,1360.86,"A weak insight is general: our spend is high and we should negotiate."],[1360.86,1363.68,"A good insight is specific and surprising."],[1363.96,1373.8,"For example: two thirds of our spend in this category goes to one supplier group, under three separate contracts, with three different indexation clauses."],[1373.8,1377.47,"Or: a quarter of our licences have not been used in twelve months."],[1377.47,1380.42,"A good insight points directly to a lever."],[1380.42,1383.78,"If yours does not yet, sharpen it during the fieldwork."],[1384.29,1389.66,"On the price or demand question, many people answer both, and that is usually right."],[1389.66,1392.28,"The useful follow-up is: which one is bigger?"],[1392.28,1400.72,"If your demand drivers show that volume grew by a third while the price per unit stayed flat, your strategy should start with demand."],[1401.0,1406.54,"If volume is stable and the price rose through indexation, start with the commercial model."],[1406.54,1412.45,"Write down your answer, because it shapes the six strategic decisions you will make in Session 2."],[1413.4,1416.66,"Let me recap module 1.3 in three lines."],[1416.66,1428.61,"First, the spend cube, the contract inventory, the demand drivers and the total cost of ownership are the four parts of insight, with the stakeholder map from the first course alongside them."],[1428.61,1434.29,"Second, the notice period is the real deadline: end date, minus notice, minus lead time."],[1434.57,1440.11,"Third, total cost of ownership stops a cheap price from becoming an expensive purchase."],[1440.61,1446.8,"Notice that everything in this module was about our side: what we buy, how we buy it, what it costs us."],[1446.8,1448.44,"We now know our side."],[1448.44,1455.58,"In the next lesson we turn to the supplier's side: how to read the supply market, and how suppliers see us as a customer."],[1455.58,1456.65,"See you there."]]},{"id":"s1-l4","session":"s1","title":"Know the market","summary":"Read a supply market with five forces, PESTLE, cost drivers and capacity, decide when an RFI is worth running, and judge with evidence how a supplier sees your account.","from":63,"to":79,"audio":"audio/s1-l4.mp3","duration":1476.2,"segs":[[63,0.6,34.37,"Welcome back. This lesson covers module 1.4 of Session 1: know the market. So far in this session we have looked inward. We validated the need, we triaged the regulation, and we built a fact base of spend, contracts, demand drivers and total cost of ownership. All of that describes our side of the table. In this lesson we turn our chair around and look at the other side. Three questions guide us. How many suppliers can really deliver what we need? How do those suppliers make their money? And how much do they actually want our business?"],[63,34.87,71.95,"Here is the plan. We start with Porter's five forces, seen from the buyer's chair. Then a short PESTLE scan, the cost drivers of typical indirect categories, and the effect of capacity. After that we look at the RFI, the request for information, as a research tool rather than a ritual. Then we move to the model that many buyers have never used: Steele and Court's supplier preferencing, which tells us how the supplier sees us. We will apply it to two Nordvia projects, add the one market question that DORA makes compulsory for ICT, and finish with your own market snapshot."],[64,72.9,84.23,"Let's start with the sentence on this slide. Spend analysis shows our side. Market analysis shows theirs. And the consequence underneath: supply power cannot be read from our own data."],[64,84.73,132.44,"Think about what your spend cube can tell you. It tells you how much you spend, with whom, in which entity, and how that changed over two years. It cannot tell you whether there are two credible alternatives or twenty. It cannot tell you whether the incumbent is running at full capacity and turning clients away, or whether it is desperate for volume. It cannot tell you whether the supplier regards you as a valued client or as a source of easy revenue. Those facts live outside our systems. Why does this matter so much? Because in the next module we will position each project by comparing our power with the supplier's power. If we only know our half, the positioning is guesswork, and a strategy built on guesswork tends to fall apart at the first serious negotiation."],[64,132.94,175.91,"There is a second reason. Our own data describes the past. A market can change between two renewals. A competitor is bought, a new entrant appears, wages rise, or a technology makes the old specification obsolete. If we only look at our invoices, we will negotiate the next contract with the picture of the last one. Market analysis does not need to be a research project of several months. For most indirect categories, a few hours of desk research, two or three conversations with suppliers and colleagues in other entities, and a short structured summary are enough. The aim is not perfection. The aim is to replace assumptions with evidence wherever the strategy depends on it."],[65,176.86,207.11,"Our first tool is Michael Porter's five forces. Porter designed it to judge how attractive an industry is for the firms competing in it. We borrow it and read it from the buyer's chair: each force tells us something about how much power the suppliers have over us. The slide compares two Nordvia categories side by side, office cleaning, project P1, and core insurance software, project P3. Same insurer, same buyer, opposite markets. Let's walk down the rows."],[65,207.61,259.2,"First, rivalry among suppliers. In cleaning it is high. There are many local firms, and they compete hard for multi-site contracts. In core software it is low. There are only a few platforms that can run an insurer's policy and claims processes. Second, new entrants. In cleaning, medium: a new firm can start with a van, staff and equipment, although winning a multi-site client takes a track record. In core software, low, because building an insurance platform takes years of research and development. Third, substitutes. Is there another way to meet the need? In cleaning, yes, to some degree: robotics for floor care at night, or an output specification that pays for clean floors rather than for hours. In software, other architectures exist, but only in the long term."],[65,259.7,290.0,"Fourth, supplier power. Low to medium in cleaning. High in core software, and the reason is on the slide: switching takes 18 to 24 months. When a migration takes two years, the vendor knows you cannot walk away at the next renewal. Fifth, our buyer power. Medium to high in cleaning, because 2.4 million euros across three sites is an attractive contract for a local cleaner. Low in software, unless we bundle with other entities of the Group."],[65,290.5,329.61,"Now read the two columns as a whole. In cleaning, almost every force points in our favour. In core software, almost every force points against us. That is why the route to market cannot be the same. A competitive tender with an auction at the end can work well for cleaning. Send the same tender to Kelvar's market and you will get one serious answer, from the incumbent, at the incumbent's price. A practical tip: when you use the five forces, write one line of evidence for each force. Not high or low on its own, but high because we counted eleven firms that bid for similar contracts in our region last year."],[66,330.56,358.61,"The second lens is PESTLE: Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Legal and Environmental factors. It scans the world outside the market. The danger with PESTLE is the generic list. Everybody has seen a slide that says digitalisation, inflation, sustainability. That list changes nothing. So the rule for this course is simple: ask for concrete factors, and if a factor does not change the strategy, drop it."],[66,359.11,407.03,"Look at the four cards. Economic and legal: collective wage agreements set the floor in cleaning and security. When a new agreement raises wages, the supplier's cost rises, and your indexation clause decides who pays. Technological: AI in creative production changes what an agency should charge for routine adaptations, and robotics changes cleaning methods. Legal: DORA, concentration risk and data location for ICT services. Environmental: ESG reporting duties, which push the Group to ask suppliers for data on emissions and labour conditions. Each of these factors would change something concrete, a clause, a specification or an evaluation criterion. That is the test."],[67,407.98,419.97,"Next, cost drivers: how suppliers make money. If you understand the supplier's cost structure, you know which part of the price is negotiable and which part is not. Let's go down the table."],[67,420.47,463.91,"Cleaning and security. The main driver is labour hours multiplied by the collective wage, plus on-costs such as social contributions, then supervision, consumables and margin. Labour dominates. In many labour-intensive service contracts it is by far the largest share of the price, and you should check that share in your own bids. The consequence is important: a bid below the wage floor is a red flag. If the hours offered multiplied by the minimum wage are more than the price, the supplier is either planning to cut hours, cutting corners on staff, or losing money. None of those is good for you. We return to abnormally low bids in Session 3."],[67,464.41,533.71,"IT consulting. The drivers are role rates, the seniority pyramid and utilisation. Two firms with the same day rate can produce very different costs if one staffs the project with seniors and the other with juniors. Remember Nordvia's P2: the same senior profile ranges from 820 to 1,450 euros a day. Part of that spread is the pyramid, part is simple lack of competition. Software. The drivers are the licence metric, the support uplift and the recovery of research and development. Once the software is built, the cost of one more licence is close to zero, so price is driven by what the market will bear and by how locked in you are. Print. Paper, make-ready and run cost. Make-ready, the setup of the press, is a fixed cost per job, which is why many small print orders cost so much more per unit than a few bundled ones. Each row tells you where to push. In cleaning, push on method and supervision, never below the wage. In consulting, push on the pyramid and on rate cards. In software, push on the metric and on usage."],[68,534.66,553.88,"The fourth market lens is capacity, and it changes behaviour more than most buyers expect. On the left, a market with spare capacity. Suppliers compete for volume, price moves in competition, and auctions can work, because every supplier has idle people or machines it wants to fill."],[68,554.38,598.74,"On the right, a market at capacity. Now suppliers choose their clients. Attractiveness matters more than volume. Negotiation and relationship matter more than competition. Think of a period when experienced cloud engineers or data specialists were scarce. A buyer who ran a tough auction in that market often received few bids, or bids from the firms that had nothing better to do. The best firms simply did not answer. Here is a question to ask yourself about your own category: which side is it on today, and was it different three years ago? Markets move. A category that was a buyer's market when the contract was signed can be a seller's market at renewal, and the strategy has to move with it."],[69,599.69,619.45,"So far we have used desk research. Sometimes desk research is not enough, and we need to ask the market directly. That is the RFI, the request for information. It collects information without binding prices. The key message of this slide is that the RFI is a research tool, not a ritual."],[69,619.95,665.09,"When should you use it? To discover a new market, for example a category Nordvia has never bought. To pre-qualify suppliers before a framework tender. And to build an alternative to an incumbent. That last use matters a lot. An RFI to alternative providers is how you build a BATNA, your best alternative to a negotiated agreement, against a supplier who believes you have no choice. We use that idea in Session 2 and again in the negotiation session. What should you pre-qualify on? The points that knock suppliers out: certifications, data location, the subcontracting chain and financial standing. For an insurer, data location and subcontracting are often the decisive ones."],[69,665.59,691.67,"When should you not use it? When the market is well known, because an RFI then just adds weeks to the timeline. As free consulting, asking suppliers to design your solution with no intention of buying. And when you have no next step to offer, because suppliers remember buyers who waste their time. In Coupa, an RFI event collects qualitative information through forms or attachments, where pricing is not the point."],[70,692.62,722.76,"If you decide to run one, keep it short. Five steps. First, objectives: what must we learn? Write two or three questions you need answered to shape the strategy. Second, the questionnaire: short, with knock-out questions first. If a supplier cannot host data where we need it, there is no point in reading its twenty pages on innovation. Third, the invitation list: broad, and include new entrants. The RFI is your chance to discover the firm you have never heard of."],[70,723.26,763.33,"Fourth, issue it with a clear deadline. Two to three weeks is common practice, a rule of thumb rather than a rule. Fifth, score the answers and give feedback. Record the rationale for who goes forward, and tell every supplier what happens next. A small story to make this concrete. Imagine Nordvia's Facilities team wants to test robotic floor care before the cleaning tender. A three-week RFI with six questions, sent to the three incumbents and five other firms, tells them which suppliers can offer it, at what scale, and with which references. That knowledge then shapes the specification. That is research, not ritual."],[71,764.28,783.27,"Now we come to the heart of this module. Steele and Court, in their 1996 book Profitable Purchasing Strategies, describe the supplier preferencing model. It asks a question most buyers never ask out loud: how does the supplier see us? This is the supplier's half of the power picture."],[71,783.77,825.61,"Look at the axes. Horizontally, the relative value of our account to the supplier: what share of its business we represent. Vertically, the attractiveness of our account. Four quadrants. Bottom left, Nuisance: low value and unattractive. The supplier has little interest. Expect weak service and no flexibility. Top left, Development: low value today, but attractive. The supplier invests to grow with us, perhaps with good people and sharp prices, because it sees a future. Top right, Core: high value and attractive. The supplier defends the account with service, flexibility and innovation. This is where we want to be with important suppliers."],[71,826.11,868.59,"And bottom right, highlighted, Exploitable: high value, but unattractive. The supplier wants the revenue, but will not invest in the relationship. What do you see in practice? Firm prices, regular uplifts, slow responses, and little interest in improving. Why is this highlighted? Because Exploitable is the dangerous quadrant for an insurer buying from large software and telecom vendors. The amount is big enough to matter to us, but the supplier has no reason to go the extra mile. The important discipline with this model is honesty. Buyers like to believe their suppliers love them. The model asks you to look at behaviour, not at what the account manager says over lunch."],[72,869.54,891.43,"What makes our account attractive? Four factors. Growth: a bigger or growing share in the future. A supplier will invest in a client that is expanding, or that could open doors to other entities of the Group. Reference value: a name the supplier can use in the market. Being the first insurer to go live on a new module has value for the vendor."],[72,891.93,926.24,"Ease of doing business: clear specifications, quick decisions, few surprises. A client that changes scope every month and takes six weeks to approve a change request is expensive to serve, and suppliers price that in. And payment behaviour. Here the note is precise. Paying on time does not make us a customer of choice; it is hygiene. But paying late makes us less attractive. If your accounts payable process is slow, that is a sourcing problem too. The good news in this list is that most of these factors are partly in our hands."],[72,926.74,969.56,"Here is a small example from General Services. Imagine a print supplier that receives 140 small orders a year from Nordvia, each with a different contact person, a vague brief, and payment after 75 days. For that supplier, Nordvia is a nuisance, even though the total spend is not trivial. Now imagine the same spend channelled through one catalogue, with standard formats, one contact and payment on agreed terms. Nothing about the volume has changed, but the account has become easier to serve and more attractive. That is why preferencing is not only a diagnosis. It also tells us what we can change about ourselves before we go to market."],[73,970.51,988.23,"Let's apply the model to a Nordvia project. P3, the Kelvar core software licences. Remember that Nordvia is fictional. The facts: 11.2 million euros a year. A 9 percent uplift at the last renewal. No usage data. An auto-renewal clause."],[73,988.73,1015.62,"Now think like Kelvar's account manager. Here is a customer that never challenges. It accepted 9 percent without a fight. It does not know how many licences it uses. Its contract renews automatically unless someone acts in time. For a vendor, that is easy revenue. So the likely position is Exploitable: the account is valuable, but the vendor has no reason to invest in it, because the revenue arrives anyway."],[73,1016.12,1067.28,"But notice the word likely. The last line on the slide lists the evidence to check. Roadmap access: does Nordvia have any say in what Kelvar develops? Flexibility: has Kelvar ever agreed to reduce licences or change the metric? Service response: how fast are critical incidents resolved? If Kelvar invests in Nordvia, gives it roadmap influence and resolves incidents fast, the position may in fact be Core. The rule is: write the evidence, not the feeling. I think they like us is not evidence. Uplift history, response times and roadmap access are. And there is one more lesson here. Nordvia's own behaviour helped create the Exploitable position. A buyer who never collects usage data and lets contracts auto-renew teaches the supplier that the account needs no effort."],[74,1068.23,1093.75,"Now the contrast. P1, office cleaning. 2.4 million euros a year across three local cleaners. For a local cleaning firm, a large multi-site client like Nordvia is significant revenue, and a good reference. So the likely position is Core or Development. The consequence is that real competition is possible. Several firms will want this contract and will compete for it."],[74,1094.25,1124.67,"But look at the last line. Quality complaints at headquarters show that the account is not managed. The contracts have rolled over since 2019, with no KPIs that anyone enforces. So even an attractive account can get poor service when nobody manages it. Put the two lenses together: leverage for us, attractive for them. That is the classic setting for a competitive tender. And the complaints give us something precise to put into the specification and the KPIs."],[74,1125.17,1168.97,"So what would a good buyer do here? First, use the complaints as evidence in the need validation: which areas, which times, what kind of failure. Second, move from paying for hours towards an output or hybrid specification, so that the supplier is paid for clean, safe buildings rather than for presence. Third, define a small set of KPIs with service credits and a regular review, so that the next contract is managed. And fourth, test whether bundling the three sites into one contract, or keeping lots by site, gives the better competition. We decide that in Session 2. For now, the important point is that the market and the preferencing position both tell us that competition will work."],[75,1169.92,1192.56,"One more market question, and for ICT services it is compulsory. DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act, adds concentration risk. Article 29 asks financial entities to assess ICT concentration risk, and that assessment feeds the pre-contract assessment we met in module 1.2. Four questions on the cards."],[75,1193.06,1235.05,"Alternatives: how many credible providers exist? Dependency: how much do we, and the Group as a whole, rely on one provider? If three entities run on the same platform, the concentration is a Group issue, not a local one. Subcontracting chain: who sits behind the provider? Your network provider may rely on a data centre operator, which relies on a cloud platform. And exit: could we move within a reasonable transition period? For Nordvia, this applies to Telmo, to Kelvar and above all to Northbyte. Notice how naturally this fits the five forces. Concentration is rivalry and supplier power seen through a regulator's eyes."],[75,1235.55,1262.2,"In practice, this means that the market snapshot for an ICT category is not complete until you have talked to Risk or Information Security. They may already know the Group's dependency on a provider from the register of information. Ask them early. It is much easier to design a strategy around a concentration risk you know about at the start than to discover it at contract stage, when the only option left is to accept it."],[76,1263.15,1291.91,"Time for a moment of reflection. The poll asks how the incumbent of your own Sourcing Case probably sees your account. Core, Development, Exploitable, Nuisance, or is there no incumbent at all? Take a few seconds and pick your honest answer. Then ask yourself one follow-up question. What behaviour of the supplier makes you think so? If you cannot name one fact, your answer is still a feeling, and the next exercise will help you turn it into evidence."],[77,1292.86,1323.5,"Now it is your turn. This exercise takes about 25 minutes and lives under Module 1.4 in your workbook. First, fill the eight boxes of the market snapshot: the number of credible suppliers, rivalry and spare capacity, switching cost and time, the main cost drivers, substitutes and new entrants, the PESTLE factors that matter, regulatory and concentration risk, and our buyer power. Tick assumed wherever you are guessing. Those ticks become research questions."],[77,1324.0,1359.99,"Then place the incumbent, or the most likely supplier, on the preferencing model, and write the evidence for that position. Finally, decide whether you need an RFI, and why: to discover the market, to pre-qualify on regulatory points, or to build an alternative to the incumbent. If you are working with a colleague, try this: one of you plays the incumbent's account manager and explains how they see the client. It is often a revealing conversation. Pause the video now if you want to complete the exercise in your workbook, in the Modules tab, and continue when you are ready."],[78,1360.94,1376.06,"Welcome back. Let's debrief with four questions. Who has a supply market with real competition? Who found that they are Nuisance or Exploitable for the incumbent? What evidence did you use? And who needs an RFI, and for what purpose?"],[78,1376.56,1410.42,"In groups that do this exercise, a pattern appears. Exploitable positions are common for software and telecom. Facilities services tend to show real competition. And the most common weak spot is the evidence line: many people write a quadrant but no fact behind it. If that was you, go back and add one observable fact, such as an uplift, a response time or a share of the supplier's revenue. Keep a note of any Exploitable or Nuisance position. Those are the danger zone we deal with in module 1.5."],[79,1411.37,1434.12,"Let's close module 1.4 in three lines. One: five forces, PESTLE, cost drivers and capacity tell us about supply power. Two: the RFI is a research tool, not a ritual. Use it to learn something specific, and give suppliers a next step. Three: Steele and Court show how the supplier sees us, which is half of the power picture."],[79,1434.62,1456.98,"And that word half is the bridge to the next lesson. We now have our view of the category from the fact base, and the supplier's view from the market and from preferencing. In module 1.5 we combine both halves. We bring in Kraljic's matrix and the Purchasing Chessboard, and we turn the balance of power into a strategic direction for every project. See you there."],[79,1457.48,1475.03,"Before you move on, take one minute with your own Sourcing Case. Write one sentence that says how much supply power your market has, and one sentence that says how the incumbent sees you, with the evidence. If you can write both sentences, you are ready for the positioning work of the next module."]],"questions":[{"after":65,"q":"Nordvia's CIO suggests running the same three-quote competitive process for the Kelvar renewal that works well for office cleaning. Based on the five forces comparison, what is the strongest objection?","options":["Software has few platforms and an 18 to 24 month switch, so a competitive process would draw one serious answer at the incumbent's price","Cleaning has more substitutes, so it needs a longer process","Software suppliers are not allowed to answer RFQs under DORA","Cleaning has higher supplier power, so it needs more quotes"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rivalry, entry barriers and switching cost all favour the software vendor. When the forces point the opposite way, the route to market must differ: competition works in cleaning but not against a locked-in incumbent.","t":329.61},{"after":67,"q":"In the HQ cleaning tender, one bid is far below the others. You multiply the hours it offers by the collective wage and on-costs, and the result exceeds the bid price. What does the cost driver analysis tell you?","options":["It is the best value bid and should win on price","The supplier must have better robotics, so the bid is credible","The bid is a red flag: the supplier will likely cut hours or staff conditions, or lose money","Collective wages do not apply to subcontractors, so it does not matter"],"answer":2,"explain":"Labour dominates cleaning costs, so the wage floor sets the floor of any credible bid. A price below it signals a delivery or compliance risk, which Session 3 treats as an abnormally low bid.","t":533.71},{"after":69,"q":"Which situation is the best reason for Nordvia to run an RFI?","options":["Renewing office supplies from a well-known catalogue market","Getting suppliers to design the solution with no plan to buy","Satisfying a template that says every event starts with an RFI","Building a credible alternative to the Kelvar incumbent before the renewal negotiation"],"answer":3,"explain":"An RFI is a research tool. Building an alternative to an incumbent is a strong use because it creates a BATNA. In a well-known market it only adds weeks, and using it as free consulting damages supplier trust.","t":691.67},{"after":71,"q":"A global telecom provider earns a large sum from your entity, but your requests get slow answers, every renewal brings an uplift, and it never proposes improvements. Where does your account most likely sit in Steele and Court's model?","options":["Core","Exploitable","Development","Nuisance"],"answer":1,"explain":"High value but unattractive is Exploitable: the supplier wants the revenue but will not invest. Firm prices, uplifts and weak responsiveness are the typical behaviour.","t":868.59},{"after":73,"q":"A colleague says Kelvar sees Nordvia as Core because the account manager is very friendly. What is the best response?","options":["Accept it: the relationship is what matters","Place it in Nuisance to be safe","Ask for evidence such as uplift history, roadmap access, flexibility and incident response times before deciding the position","Skip preferencing, since Kraljic already covers it"],"answer":2,"explain":"Preferencing positions must rest on observable behaviour, not feelings. The 9 percent uplift points to Exploitable; investment, roadmap influence or flexibility would be the counter-evidence for Core.","t":1067.28},{"after":75,"q":"Nordvia and two other Group entities all run claims processing on the same ICT provider. Under DORA's concentration question, what should you conclude?","options":["Dependency is a Group issue, and alternatives, the subcontracting chain and exit feasibility must be assessed","Concentration is only assessed per entity, so it is not relevant","The provider must be replaced immediately","Concentration risk only applies to cloud hyperscalers"],"answer":0,"explain":"DORA Article 29 asks about ICT concentration risk, including how much we and the Group rely on one provider. The assessment feeds the pre-contract assessment and shapes the strategy, it does not force an automatic switch.","t":1262.2},{"after":78,"q":"In your market snapshot you ticked assumed on the number of credible suppliers and on switching time. What should those ticks become?","options":["Nothing: assumptions are fine for positioning","A reason to choose an auction","Evidence that the incumbent is Core","Research questions for fieldwork, and possibly the objectives of a short RFI"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ticking assumed separates facts from guesses. Each guess becomes a question to answer before positioning, through desk research, the business owner meeting or an RFI.","t":1410.42}],"cues":[[0.6,1.35,"Welcome back."],[1.35,5.2,"This lesson covers module 1.4 of Session 1: know the market."],[5.2,8.36,"So far in this session we have looked inward."],[8.36,18.05,"We validated the need, we triaged the regulation, and we built a fact base of spend, contracts, demand drivers and total cost of ownership."],[18.05,20.8,"All of that describes our side of the table."],[21.08,25.12,"In this lesson we turn our chair around and look at the other side."],[25.12,26.56,"Three questions guide us."],[26.56,29.56,"How many suppliers can really deliver what we need?"],[29.56,31.85,"How do those suppliers make their money?"],[31.85,34.37,"And how much do they actually want our business?"],[34.87,36.16,"Here is the plan."],[36.16,40.14,"We start with Porter's five forces, seen from the buyer's chair."],[40.14,47.03,"Then a short PESTLE scan, the cost drivers of typical indirect categories, and the effect of capacity."],[47.03,53.66,"After that we look at the RFI, the request for information, as a research tool rather than a ritual."],[53.94,62.57,"Then we move to the model that many buyers have never used: Steele and Court's supplier preferencing, which tells us how the supplier sees us."],[62.57,71.95,"We will apply it to two Nordvia projects, add the one market question that DORA makes compulsory for ICT, and finish with your own market snapshot."],[72.9,75.64,"Let's start with the sentence on this slide."],[75.64,77.53,"Spend analysis shows our side."],[77.53,79.61,"Market analysis shows theirs."],[79.61,84.23,"And the consequence underneath: supply power cannot be read from our own data."],[84.73,87.49,"Think about what your spend cube can tell you."],[87.49,93.55,"It tells you how much you spend, with whom, in which entity, and how that changed over two years."],[93.55,97.7,"It cannot tell you whether there are two credible alternatives or twenty."],[97.98,106.43,"It cannot tell you whether the incumbent is running at full capacity and turning clients away, or whether it is desperate for volume."],[106.43,112.46,"It cannot tell you whether the supplier regards you as a valued client or as a source of easy revenue."],[112.46,115.19,"Those facts live outside our systems."],[115.19,116.86,"Why does this matter so much?"],[117.14,123.06,"Because in the next module we will position each project by comparing our power with the supplier's power."],[123.06,132.44,"If we only know our half, the positioning is guesswork, and a strategy built on guesswork tends to fall apart at the first serious negotiation."],[132.94,134.31,"There is a second reason."],[134.31,136.79,"Our own data describes the past."],[136.79,139.57,"A market can change between two renewals."],[139.57,147.13,"A competitor is bought, a new entrant appears, wages rise, or a technology makes the old specification obsolete."],[147.13,153.14,"If we only look at our invoices, we will negotiate the next contract with the picture of the last one."],[153.42,157.71,"Market analysis does not need to be a research project of several months."],[157.71,168.64,"For most indirect categories, a few hours of desk research, two or three conversations with suppliers and colleagues in other entities, and a short structured summary are enough."],[168.64,170.72,"The aim is not perfection."],[171.0,175.91,"The aim is to replace assumptions with evidence wherever the strategy depends on it."],[176.86,180.21,"Our first tool is Michael Porter's five forces."],[180.21,185.17,"Porter designed it to judge how attractive an industry is for the firms competing in it."],[185.17,192.84,"We borrow it and read it from the buyer's chair: each force tells us something about how much power the suppliers have over us."],[193.12,202.18,"The slide compares two Nordvia categories side by side, office cleaning, project P1, and core insurance software, project P3."],[202.18,205.15,"Same insurer, same buyer, opposite markets."],[205.15,207.11,"Let's walk down the rows."],[207.61,210.06,"First, rivalry among suppliers."],[210.06,210.95,"In cleaning it is high."],[210.95,216.43,"There are many local firms, and they compete hard for multi-site contracts."],[216.43,218.68,"In core software it is low."],[218.68,224.43,"There are only a few platforms that can run an insurer's policy and claims processes."],[224.43,225.72,"Second, new entrants."],[226.0,234.4,"In cleaning, medium: a new firm can start with a van, staff and equipment, although winning a multi-site client takes a track record."],[234.4,241.02,"In core software, low, because building an insurance platform takes years of research and development."],[241.02,242.68,"Third, substitutes."],[242.68,244.8,"Is there another way to meet the need?"],[245.08,254.6,"In cleaning, yes, to some degree: robotics for floor care at night, or an output specification that pays for clean floors rather than for hours."],[254.6,259.2,"In software, other architectures exist, but only in the long term."],[259.7,261.44,"Fourth, supplier power."],[261.44,263.38,"Low to medium in cleaning."],[263.38,269.64,"High in core software, and the reason is on the slide: switching takes 18 to 24 months."],[269.64,275.03,"When a migration takes two years, the vendor knows you cannot walk away at the next renewal."],[275.03,276.96,"Fifth, our buyer power."],[277.24,285.66,"Medium to high in cleaning, because 2.4 million euros across three sites is an attractive contract for a local cleaner."],[285.66,290.0,"Low in software, unless we bundle with other entities of the Group."],[290.5,292.92,"Now read the two columns as a whole."],[292.92,296.36,"In cleaning, almost every force points in our favour."],[296.36,300.23,"In core software, almost every force points against us."],[300.23,302.5,"That is why the route to market cannot be the same."],[302.5,307.72,"A competitive tender with an auction at the end can work well for cleaning."],[308.0,315.44,"Send the same tender to Kelvar's market and you will get one serious answer, from the incumbent, at the incumbent's price."],[315.44,321.14,"A practical tip: when you use the five forces, write one line of evidence for each force."],[321.42,329.61,"Not high or low on its own, but high because we counted eleven firms that bid for similar contracts in our region last year."],[330.56,338.13,"The second lens is PESTLE: Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Legal and Environmental factors."],[338.13,340.37,"It scans the world outside the market."],[340.37,343.63,"The danger with PESTLE is the generic list."],[343.63,349.2,"Everybody has seen a slide that says digitalisation, inflation, sustainability."],[349.2,350.98,"That list changes nothing."],[351.26,358.62,"So the rule for this course is simple: ask for concrete factors, and if a factor does not change the strategy, drop it."],[359.11,360.53,"Look at the four cards."],[360.53,366.26,"Economic and legal: collective wage agreements set the floor in cleaning and security."],[366.26,373.53,"When a new agreement raises wages, the supplier's cost rises, and your indexation clause decides who pays."],[373.81,384.68,"Technological: AI in creative production changes what an agency should charge for routine adaptations, and robotics changes cleaning methods."],[384.68,389.58,"Legal: DORA, concentration risk and data location for ICT services."],[389.86,398.07,"Environmental: ESG reporting duties, which push the Group to ask suppliers for data on emissions and labour conditions."],[398.07,405.67,"Each of these factors would change something concrete, a clause, a specification or an evaluation criterion."],[405.67,407.03,"That is the test."],[407.98,411.51,"Next, cost drivers: how suppliers make money."],[411.51,418.41,"If you understand the supplier's cost structure, you know which part of the price is negotiable and which part is not."],[418.41,419.97,"Let's go down the table."],[420.47,422.17,"Cleaning and security."],[422.17,432.89,"The main driver is labour hours multiplied by the collective wage, plus on-costs such as social contributions, then supervision, consumables and margin."],[432.89,434.36,"Labour dominates."],[434.64,442.97,"In many labour-intensive service contracts it is by far the largest share of the price, and you should check that share in your own bids."],[442.97,447.82,"The consequence is important: a bid below the wage floor is a red flag."],[448.1,458.67,"If the hours offered multiplied by the minimum wage are more than the price, the supplier is either planning to cut hours, cutting corners on staff, or losing money."],[458.67,460.64,"None of those is good for you."],[460.64,463.91,"We return to abnormally low bids in Session 3."],[464.41,465.34,"IT consulting."],[465.34,470.84,"The drivers are role rates, the seniority pyramid and utilisation."],[470.84,480.56,"Two firms with the same day rate can produce very different costs if one staffs the project with seniors and the other with juniors."],[480.56,487.13,"Remember Nordvia's P2: the same senior profile ranges from 820 to 1,450 euros a day."],[487.41,491.77,"Part of that spread is the pyramid, part is simple lack of competition."],[491.77,493.02,"Software."],[493.02,498.8,"The drivers are the licence metric, the support uplift and the recovery of research and development."],[499.08,508.91,"Once the software is built, the cost of one more licence is close to zero, so price is driven by what the market will bear and by how locked in you are."],[508.91,509.39,"Print."],[509.39,511.48,"Paper, make-ready and run cost."],[511.76,520.6,"Make-ready, the setup of the press, is a fixed cost per job, which is why many small print orders cost so much more per unit than a few bundled ones."],[520.6,522.8,"Each row tells you where to push."],[522.8,527.19,"In cleaning, push on method and supervision, never below the wage."],[527.19,530.7,"In consulting, push on the pyramid and on rate cards."],[530.98,533.71,"In software, push on the metric and on usage."],[534.66,540.57,"The fourth market lens is capacity, and it changes behaviour more than most buyers expect."],[540.57,543.84,"On the left, a market with spare capacity."],[543.84,553.88,"Suppliers compete for volume, price moves in competition, and auctions can work, because every supplier has idle people or machines it wants to fill."],[554.38,556.87,"On the right, a market at capacity."],[556.87,559.38,"Now suppliers choose their clients."],[559.38,562.2,"Attractiveness matters more than volume."],[562.2,566.5,"Negotiation and relationship matter more than competition."],[566.5,571.68,"Think of a period when experienced cloud engineers or data specialists were scarce."],[571.96,579.55,"A buyer who ran a tough auction in that market often received few bids, or bids from the firms that had nothing better to do."],[579.55,582.27,"The best firms simply did not answer."],[582.27,589.9,"Here is a question to ask yourself about your own category: which side is it on today, and was it different three years ago?"],[589.9,590.59,"Markets move."],[590.87,598.74,"A category that was a buyer's market when the contract was signed can be a seller's market at renewal, and the strategy has to move with it."],[599.69,602.2,"So far we have used desk research."],[602.2,607.82,"Sometimes desk research is not enough, and we need to ask the market directly."],[607.82,610.95,"That is the RFI, the request for information."],[610.95,614.21,"It collects information without binding prices."],[614.21,619.44,"The key message of this slide is that the RFI is a research tool, not a ritual."],[619.95,621.2,"When should you use it?"],[621.2,626.01,"To discover a new market, for example a category Nordvia has never bought."],[626.01,629.35,"To pre-qualify suppliers before a framework tender."],[629.35,631.97,"And to build an alternative to an incumbent."],[631.97,633.97,"That last use matters a lot."],[634.25,644.77,"An RFI to alternative providers is how you build a BATNA, your best alternative to a negotiated agreement, against a supplier who believes you have no choice."],[644.77,649.64,"We use that idea in Session 2 and again in the negotiation session."],[649.64,651.44,"What should you pre-qualify on?"],[651.72,659.91,"The points that knock suppliers out: certifications, data location, the subcontracting chain and financial standing."],[659.91,665.1,"For an insurer, data location and subcontracting are often the decisive ones."],[665.59,666.92,"When should you not use it?"],[666.92,672.34,"When the market is well known, because an RFI then just adds weeks to the timeline."],[672.34,677.56,"As free consulting, asking suppliers to design your solution with no intention of buying."],[677.56,683.77,"And when you have no next step to offer, because suppliers remember buyers who waste their time."],[684.05,691.66,"In Coupa, an RFI event collects qualitative information through forms or attachments, where pricing is not the point."],[692.62,694.88,"If you decide to run one, keep it short."],[694.88,695.75,"Five steps."],[695.75,697.85,"First, objectives: what must we learn?"],[697.85,702.7,"Write two or three questions you need answered to shape the strategy."],[702.7,707.08,"Second, the questionnaire: short, with knock-out questions first."],[707.36,714.02,"If a supplier cannot host data where we need it, there is no point in reading its twenty pages on innovation."],[714.02,718.82,"Third, the invitation list: broad, and include new entrants."],[718.82,722.77,"The RFI is your chance to discover the firm you have never heard of."],[723.26,725.62,"Fourth, issue it with a clear deadline."],[725.62,730.38,"Two to three weeks is common practice, a rule of thumb rather than a rule."],[730.38,733.13,"Fifth, score the answers and give feedback."],[733.13,738.6,"Record the rationale for who goes forward, and tell every supplier what happens next."],[738.6,740.92,"A small story to make this concrete."],[741.2,747.48,"Imagine Nordvia's Facilities team wants to test robotic floor care before the cleaning tender."],[747.48,758.44,"A three-week RFI with six questions, sent to the three incumbents and five other firms, tells them which suppliers can offer it, at what scale, and with which references."],[758.44,761.19,"That knowledge then shapes the specification."],[761.47,763.33,"That is research, not ritual."],[764.28,766.99,"Now we come to the heart of this module."],[766.99,774.68,"Steele and Court, in their 1996 book Profitable Purchasing Strategies, describe the supplier preferencing model."],[774.68,780.43,"It asks a question most buyers never ask out loud: how does the supplier see us?"],[780.43,783.27,"This is the supplier's half of the power picture."],[783.77,785.07,"Look at the axes."],[785.07,792.2,"Horizontally, the relative value of our account to the supplier: what share of its business we represent."],[792.2,795.07,"Vertically, the attractiveness of our account."],[795.07,796.01,"Four quadrants."],[796.01,799.02,"Bottom left, Nuisance: low value and unattractive."],[799.02,801.18,"The supplier has little interest."],[801.18,803.89,"Expect weak service and no flexibility."],[804.17,807.52,"Top left, Development: low value today, but attractive."],[807.52,814.54,"The supplier invests to grow with us, perhaps with good people and sharp prices, because it sees a future."],[814.54,817.47,"Top right, Core: high value and attractive."],[817.47,822.49,"The supplier defends the account with service, flexibility and innovation."],[822.77,825.61,"This is where we want to be with important suppliers."],[826.11,830.95,"And bottom right, highlighted, Exploitable: high value, but unattractive."],[830.95,835.22,"The supplier wants the revenue, but will not invest in the relationship."],[835.22,836.88,"What do you see in practice?"],[836.88,842.05,"Firm prices, regular uplifts, slow responses, and little interest in improving."],[842.05,843.58,"Why is this highlighted?"],[843.86,850.74,"Because Exploitable is the dangerous quadrant for an insurer buying from large software and telecom vendors."],[850.74,856.92,"The amount is big enough to matter to us, but the supplier has no reason to go the extra mile."],[856.92,860.4,"The important discipline with this model is honesty."],[860.4,863.32,"Buyers like to believe their suppliers love them."],[863.6,868.59,"The model asks you to look at behaviour, not at what the account manager says over lunch."],[869.54,872.0,"What makes our account attractive?"],[872.0,872.67,"Four factors."],[872.67,876.15,"Growth: a bigger or growing share in the future."],[876.15,882.83,"A supplier will invest in a client that is expanding, or that could open doors to other entities of the Group."],[882.83,886.71,"Reference value: a name the supplier can use in the market."],[886.99,891.43,"Being the first insurer to go live on a new module has value for the vendor."],[891.93,897.6,"Ease of doing business: clear specifications, quick decisions, few surprises."],[897.6,906.02,"A client that changes scope every month and takes six weeks to approve a change request is expensive to serve, and suppliers price that in."],[906.02,907.66,"And payment behaviour."],[907.66,909.4,"Here the note is precise."],[909.68,914.2,"Paying on time does not make us a customer of choice; it is hygiene."],[914.2,916.77,"But paying late makes us less attractive."],[916.77,921.72,"If your accounts payable process is slow, that is a sourcing problem too."],[921.72,926.24,"The good news in this list is that most of these factors are partly in our hands."],[926.74,929.78,"Here is a small example from General Services."],[929.78,940.5,"Imagine a print supplier that receives 140 small orders a year from Nordvia, each with a different contact person, a vague brief, and payment after 75 days."],[940.5,946.73,"For that supplier, Nordvia is a nuisance, even though the total spend is not trivial."],[947.01,955.17,"Now imagine the same spend channelled through one catalogue, with standard formats, one contact and payment on agreed terms."],[955.17,961.66,"Nothing about the volume has changed, but the account has become easier to serve and more attractive."],[961.66,965.08,"That is why preferencing is not only a diagnosis."],[965.36,969.56,"It also tells us what we can change about ourselves before we go to market."],[970.51,973.71,"Let's apply the model to a Nordvia project."],[973.71,976.37,"P3, the Kelvar core software licences."],[976.37,979.42,"Remember that Nordvia is fictional."],[979.42,982.43,"The facts: 11.2 million euros a year."],[982.43,985.16,"A 9 percent uplift at the last renewal."],[985.16,986.58,"No usage data."],[986.58,988.24,"An auto-renewal clause."],[988.73,991.38,"Now think like Kelvar's account manager."],[991.38,994.16,"Here is a customer that never challenges."],[994.16,996.85,"It accepted 9 percent without a fight."],[996.85,999.59,"It does not know how many licences it uses."],[999.59,1003.7,"Its contract renews automatically unless someone acts in time."],[1003.7,1006.2,"For a vendor, that is easy revenue."],[1006.48,1015.61,"So the likely position is Exploitable: the account is valuable, but the vendor has no reason to invest in it, because the revenue arrives anyway."],[1016.12,1017.74,"But notice the word likely."],[1017.74,1021.19,"The last line on the slide lists the evidence to check."],[1021.19,1025.81,"Roadmap access: does Nordvia have any say in what Kelvar develops?"],[1025.81,1031.24,"Flexibility: has Kelvar ever agreed to reduce licences or change the metric?"],[1031.24,1035.43,"Service response: how fast are critical incidents resolved?"],[1035.71,1043.2,"If Kelvar invests in Nordvia, gives it roadmap influence and resolves incidents fast, the position may in fact be Core."],[1043.2,1046.69,"The rule is: write the evidence, not the feeling."],[1046.69,1049.15,"I think they like us is not evidence."],[1049.15,1053.24,"Uplift history, response times and roadmap access are."],[1053.24,1055.08,"And there is one more lesson here."],[1055.36,1059.3,"Nordvia's own behaviour helped create the Exploitable position."],[1059.3,1067.28,"A buyer who never collects usage data and lets contracts auto-renew teaches the supplier that the account needs no effort."],[1068.23,1069.53,"Now the contrast."],[1069.53,1070.95,"P1, office cleaning."],[1070.95,1074.92,"2.4 million euros a year across three local cleaners."],[1074.92,1083.33,"For a local cleaning firm, a large multi-site client like Nordvia is significant revenue, and a good reference."],[1083.33,1086.25,"So the likely position is Core or Development."],[1086.25,1089.95,"The consequence is that real competition is possible."],[1090.23,1093.75,"Several firms will want this contract and will compete for it."],[1094.25,1095.81,"But look at the last line."],[1095.81,1101.11,"Quality complaints at headquarters show that the account is not managed."],[1101.11,1106.55,"The contracts have rolled over since 2019, with no KPIs that anyone enforces."],[1106.55,1110.72,"So even an attractive account can get poor service when nobody manages it."],[1110.72,1115.33,"Put the two lenses together: leverage for us, attractive for them."],[1115.61,1119.08,"That is the classic setting for a competitive tender."],[1119.08,1124.67,"And the complaints give us something precise to put into the specification and the KPIs."],[1125.17,1127.76,"So what would a good buyer do here?"],[1127.76,1134.33,"First, use the complaints as evidence in the need validation: which areas, which times, what kind of failure."],[1134.33,1144.26,"Second, move from paying for hours towards an output or hybrid specification, so that the supplier is paid for clean, safe buildings rather than for presence."],[1144.54,1151.84,"Third, define a small set of KPIs with service credits and a regular review, so that the next contract is managed."],[1151.84,1159.83,"And fourth, test whether bundling the three sites into one contract, or keeping lots by site, gives the better competition."],[1159.83,1161.87,"We decide that in Session 2."],[1162.15,1168.97,"For now, the important point is that the market and the preferencing position both tell us that competition will work."],[1169.92,1174.54,"One more market question, and for ICT services it is compulsory."],[1174.54,1179.81,"DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act, adds concentration risk."],[1179.81,1190.58,"Article 29 asks financial entities to assess ICT concentration risk, and that assessment feeds the pre-contract assessment we met in module 1.2."],[1190.58,1192.55,"Four questions on the cards."],[1193.06,1196.37,"Alternatives: how many credible providers exist?"],[1196.37,1201.66,"Dependency: how much do we, and the Group as a whole, rely on one provider?"],[1201.66,1207.89,"If three entities run on the same platform, the concentration is a Group issue, not a local one."],[1207.89,1211.3,"Subcontracting chain: who sits behind the provider?"],[1211.58,1217.92,"Your network provider may rely on a data centre operator, which relies on a cloud platform."],[1217.92,1221.35,"And exit: could we move within a reasonable transition period?"],[1221.35,1226.7,"For Nordvia, this applies to Telmo, to Kelvar and above all to Northbyte."],[1226.7,1229.73,"Notice how naturally this fits the five forces."],[1230.01,1235.05,"Concentration is rivalry and supplier power seen through a regulator's eyes."],[1235.55,1244.94,"In practice, this means that the market snapshot for an ICT category is not complete until you have talked to Risk or Information Security."],[1244.94,1250.53,"They may already know the Group's dependency on a provider from the register of information."],[1250.53,1251.23,"Ask them early."],[1251.51,1262.2,"It is much easier to design a strategy around a concentration risk you know about at the start than to discover it at contract stage, when the only option left is to accept it."],[1263.15,1265.31,"Time for a moment of reflection."],[1265.31,1271.3,"The poll asks how the incumbent of your own Sourcing Case probably sees your account."],[1271.3,1276.0,"Core, Development, Exploitable, Nuisance, or is there no incumbent at all?"],[1276.0,1278.93,"Take a few seconds and pick your honest answer."],[1278.93,1281.73,"Then ask yourself one follow-up question."],[1282.01,1284.79,"What behaviour of the supplier makes you think so?"],[1284.79,1291.91,"If you cannot name one fact, your answer is still a feeling, and the next exercise will help you turn it into evidence."],[1292.86,1294.44,"Now it is your turn."],[1294.44,1300.24,"This exercise takes about 25 minutes and lives under Module 1.4 in your workbook."],[1300.52,1318.71,"First, fill the eight boxes of the market snapshot: the number of credible suppliers, rivalry and spare capacity, switching cost and time, the main cost drivers, substitutes and new entrants, the PESTLE factors that matter, regulatory and concentration risk, and our buyer power."],[1318.71,1321.04,"Tick assumed wherever you are guessing."],[1321.32,1323.5,"Those ticks become research questions."],[1324.0,1332.14,"Then place the incumbent, or the most likely supplier, on the preferencing model, and write the evidence for that position."],[1332.14,1341.62,"Finally, decide whether you need an RFI, and why: to discover the market, to pre-qualify on regulatory points, or to build an alternative to the incumbent."],[1341.9,1350.27,"If you are working with a colleague, try this: one of you plays the incumbent's account manager and explains how they see the client."],[1350.27,1352.57,"It is often a revealing conversation."],[1352.57,1359.99,"Pause the video now if you want to complete the exercise in your workbook, in the Modules tab, and continue when you are ready."],[1360.94,1361.75,"Welcome back."],[1361.75,1364.32,"Let's debrief with four questions."],[1364.32,1367.27,"Who has a supply market with real competition?"],[1367.27,1371.26,"Who found that they are Nuisance or Exploitable for the incumbent?"],[1371.26,1373.2,"What evidence did you use?"],[1373.2,1376.07,"And who needs an RFI, and for what purpose?"],[1376.56,1380.09,"In groups that do this exercise, a pattern appears."],[1380.09,1384.24,"Exploitable positions are common for software and telecom."],[1384.24,1387.88,"Facilities services tend to show real competition."],[1387.88,1394.16,"And the most common weak spot is the evidence line: many people write a quadrant but no fact behind it."],[1394.44,1403.78,"If that was you, go back and add one observable fact, such as an uplift, a response time or a share of the supplier's revenue."],[1403.78,1406.76,"Keep a note of any Exploitable or Nuisance position."],[1406.76,1410.42,"Those are the danger zone we deal with in module 1.5."],[1411.37,1414.48,"Let's close module 1.4 in three lines."],[1414.48,1420.69,"One: five forces, PESTLE, cost drivers and capacity tell us about supply power."],[1420.69,1423.68,"Two: the RFI is a research tool, not a ritual."],[1423.68,1428.74,"Use it to learn something specific, and give suppliers a next step."],[1429.02,1434.11,"Three: Steele and Court show how the supplier sees us, which is half of the power picture."],[1434.62,1437.58,"And that word half is the bridge to the next lesson."],[1437.58,1445.4,"We now have our view of the category from the fact base, and the supplier's view from the market and from preferencing."],[1445.4,1447.61,"In module 1.5 we combine both halves."],[1447.89,1455.89,"We bring in Kraljic's matrix and the Purchasing Chessboard, and we turn the balance of power into a strategic direction for every project."],[1455.89,1456.98,"See you there."],[1457.48,1461.1,"Before you move on, take one minute with your own Sourcing Case."],[1461.1,1470.04,"Write one sentence that says how much supply power your market has, and one sentence that says how the incumbent sees you, with the evidence."],[1470.04,1475.04,"If you can write both sentences, you are ready for the positioning work of the next module."]]},{"id":"s1-l5","session":"s1","title":"Power decides strategy","summary":"Combine Kraljic, supplier preferencing and the Purchasing Chessboard to position a project and name its strategic direction before choosing any sourcing tool.","from":80,"to":98,"audio":"audio/s1-l5.mp3","duration":1514.4,"segs":[[80,0.6,31.05,"Welcome to module 1.5: power decides strategy. This is the last step of the Insight stage of Session 1, and in many ways the most important one. In the previous lessons we built two halves of a picture. Our half came from the fact base: spend, contracts, demand drivers and total cost of ownership. The supplier's half came from the market analysis and from Steele and Court's preferencing model. In this lesson we put the two halves together and turn them into a direction."],[80,31.55,67.05,"We will use three lenses, together. Kraljic's matrix gives the buyer's view. Steele and Court give the supplier's view. And the Kearney Purchasing Chessboard turns the balance of power into concrete levers. We will look at each lens, at its limits, and at how they combine. Then we will position three Nordvia projects, see what the Group adds to our demand power, preview the routes to market of Session 2, and finish with your own positioning. Remember the sentence of the course: Source to Contract is a decision chain. This lesson is where the chain makes its central decision."],[81,68.0,94.11,"Let's start with history. In 1983 Peter Kraljic published an article in the Harvard Business Review with a provocative title: Purchasing must become supply management. His motivation sounds very familiar. He observed that purchasing was attached to routine, to yearly negotiations with established suppliers, at a time when supply could be, in his words, upset virtually overnight."],[81,94.61,125.33,"Think about what that means for us. Routine is the habit of treating every purchase the same way. At Nordvia, that habit had a name: the RFQ with three quotes, whatever the category. Kraljic's argument was that different purchases carry different risks and different value, and so they deserve different strategies. More than forty years later, that is still the core idea of category management, and it is exactly what we are trying to do in this course. Stop treating every purchase with the same routine."],[81,125.83,153.7,"Kraljic also gave us a useful attitude. He did not say that purchasing should do more analysis for its own sake. He said that the effort a buyer spends on a purchase should match what is at stake. A box of pens and a claims platform do not deserve the same process, the same time or the same people. That sounds obvious. Yet in many teams, the effort is driven by the template and the approval threshold, not by the risk and the value."],[82,154.65,183.54,"Here is the matrix that made Kraljic famous. Two axes. Horizontally, profit impact: how much the category affects our costs, our results or our service. Volume of spend is part of it, but so is the effect on the business, for example on claims handling or on customer experience. Vertically, supply risk: how hard it is to secure supply. That depends on the number of suppliers, on switching costs, on scarcity and on regulation."],[82,184.04,222.14,"Four quadrants. Bottom left, non-critical: low impact and low risk. The direction is to simplify and reduce effort: catalogues, standard terms, purchasing cards. Bottom right, highlighted, leverage: high impact and low risk. Here we use our buying power, with competition, bundling and target prices. Top left, bottleneck: low impact but high risk. Secure supply, accept a premium if needed, and build alternatives over time. Top right, strategic: high impact and high risk. Here we look for partnership, joint development, and, just as important, exit planning."],[82,222.64,257.44,"Why is leverage highlighted? Because most of an insurer's indirect spend sits in leverage and non-critical. Cleaning, security, print, mobile telecom and temporary staff are leverage for most entities. That is good news: these are categories where competition works. But the interesting cases, the ones that need the most thought, are the exceptions. A proprietary software platform. A managed service that supports claims. A data feed that only one provider can supply. Those are the ones where a routine approach does real damage."],[83,258.39,283.58,"This table makes it concrete for an insurer. Leverage: cleaning, security, print, mobile telecom, temporary staff. Strategic: a claims IT managed service, a lead creative agency, core platforms. Bottleneck: niche data feeds, regulatory reporting tools, proprietary software. Non-critical: office supplies, promotional items, small one-off purchases."],[83,284.08,336.16,"Now look at the footnote, because it is important. Typical, not fixed. The same category can move with the market or with our dependency. Here is an example. Mobile telecom is leverage for most entities: several operators, standard tariffs, easy switching. But imagine an entity that has built its field claims app on one operator's private network. For that entity, mobile telecom may have moved towards bottleneck. Or think of a creative agency. For one entity it is the lead agency that holds the brand, strategic. For another it is a production shop for brochures, leverage. So do not copy the table. Use it as a starting point and ask, for each category, why it sits where it does in your own entity. If you can name one category that sits differently in your entity, you have understood the footnote."],[83,336.66,368.94,"One more observation on this table. Where does regulation fit? It mostly pushes categories up the supply risk axis. A service that supports a critical or important function under Solvency II, or an ICT service under DORA, carries a supply risk that is not only commercial. If it fails, the regulator is interested, and the exit takes planning. That is why claims IT managed services and core platforms sit in strategic, even when the market has several capable providers."],[84,369.89,400.08,"Many people know the matrix. Fewer know that Kraljic's article describes a method in four phases, and that the matrix is only the first one. Phase one, classify: place the purchases by profit impact and supply risk. Phase two, analyse the market: supplier strength and availability. That is the work we did in module 1.4. Phase three, position: compare our bargaining strength against the suppliers'. Phase four, act: action plans for each quadrant."],[84,400.58,445.26,"The footnote adds a nuance. For strategic items, Kraljic's approach is often summarised as a choice between exploit, balance or diversify, depending on that balance of power. If we are stronger, we exploit our position. If the power is roughly equal, we balance. If the supplier is stronger, we diversify, which means reducing our dependency. That summary is widely used, but check the wording against the original article before you quote it in a paper. The practical lesson is this: many teams stop at phase one. They colour a matrix and think the strategy is done. The matrix only tells you where to look. The power comparison and the action plan are where the strategy actually lives."],[85,446.21,467.65,"Kraljic is powerful, but it has limits, and a good buyer knows them. Four of them. First, it is the buyer's view only. It says nothing about how the supplier sees us. A category can be strategic for us and still be irrelevant to the supplier. Second, it is static. Positions move with markets and with our dependency, as we just saw."],[85,468.15,503.34,"Third, it works at category level, which hides differences between suppliers. Within IT consulting, one firm may be a commodity provider of developers while another holds the only expert in an old claims system. Fourth, it gives no levers. It names a direction, use your buying power for example, but not the concrete moves that get you there. The good news is that each limit is answered by another lens. Preferencing gives the supplier's view. The Chessboard gives the levers. And a regular review, at least before each renewal, deals with the static problem."],[85,503.84,530.8,"Here is how that plays out in a real conversation. A buyer presents a Kraljic matrix to the CIO and says: Kelvar is strategic, so we need a partnership. The CIO answers: we have tried, but Kelvar does not return our calls. The matrix was right about our dependency, and silent about the vendor's interest. Only when you add the supplier's view and the levers do you get a strategy that survives that conversation."],[86,531.75,554.97,"So let's look at the Chessboard. The Purchasing Chessboard was developed at Kearney by Schuh, Kromoser, Strohmer, Romero P\u00e9rez and Triplat. Its key move is to change the axes. Instead of profit impact and supply risk, it puts two powers on the axes. Horizontally, our demand power. Vertically, the supply power. Comparing the two gives four basic strategies."],[86,555.47,579.75,"Bottom left, both powers low: manage spend. The category is not worth much to either side, so we manage it efficiently. Bottom right, high demand power and low supply power: leverage competition among suppliers. This is the classic tender. Top right, both powers high: seek joint advantage with the supplier. Neither side can dictate, so value comes from working together."],[86,580.25,611.14,"And top left, highlighted, high supply power and low demand power: change the nature of demand. This is the quadrant most people find hardest, because the natural reaction is to try to compete, and competition does not work when the supplier holds the power. The answer is not to push harder on price. The answer is to change what we buy, or how much we buy, or how we depend on it, so that the power balance itself changes. We will see exactly what that means with Kelvar in a few minutes."],[87,612.09,624.23,"Each basic strategy opens four levers, sixteen in total. The full Chessboard has 64 methods, but the sixteen levers are enough for this course. Let's walk through them, row by row."],[87,624.73,644.5,"Manage spend: demand management, which questions whether the demand is justified at all. Co-sourcing, buying together with other entities on one contract. Volume bundling, putting fragmented volume together. And commercial data mining, looking in our data for spend, prices and terms we did not know we had."],[87,645.0,668.88,"Leverage competition: tendering, the structured competitive process. Supplier pricing review, comparing prices across suppliers and against benchmarks. Globalisation, widening the supplier base beyond the usual local firms. And target pricing, which means estimating what the service should cost from its cost drivers. You will meet that as should-cost later in the course."],[87,669.38,721.03,"Seek joint advantage: value partnership, cost partnership, integrated operations planning and value chain management. These are the levers of a long-term relationship, where both sides share data and improvements. Change the nature of demand: innovation breakthrough, re-specification, technical data mining and risk management. Technical data mining means analysing how we actually use what we buy, for example how many licences are really used. Re-specification means changing the requirement so that more suppliers can meet it. Risk management means protecting ourselves against a dominant supplier, with exit plans and alternatives. A practical tip: when you position a project, do not try to use all four levers. Pick the two or three that your evidence supports, and test them."],[88,721.98,743.98,"What drives each power? On the left, our demand power. Volume and its growth. Bundling across areas and entities. Freedom to change the specification. And a credible ability to switch. On the right, supply power. Market concentration. Proprietary technology or intellectual property. Switching cost and time. And scarce capacity."],[88,744.48,783.91,"Now look at the two lists again. The right-hand list is mostly given by the market. We cannot easily change how concentrated a market is. But the left-hand list is partly in our hands. Bundling can be created: we can combine the demand of several entities. Specification freedom can be created: we can stop insisting on one brand or one method. A credible ability to switch can be created: we can run an RFI to alternatives, or qualify a second supplier. In other words, demand power is not only something we have. It is something we can build. That idea is the engine of the whole module."],[88,784.41,812.51,"A short example. Imagine a reception service where the specification insists on one particular visitor management system that only the incumbent operates. Supply power is high, not because the market is concentrated, but because our own specification made it so. Remove that requirement, specify the outcome instead, and suddenly five firms can bid. That is re-specification turning supply power into demand power."],[89,813.46,835.09,"Now let's combine Kraljic and preferencing, the buyer's view and the supplier's view. Five combinations on this table. First row: leverage for us, and the supplier sees us as Core or Development. The direction is to exploit competition, firm and fair. Suppliers want the business, so a well-run tender will produce good offers."],[89,835.59,864.08,"Second row: leverage for us, but we are Nuisance for the supplier. Our spend is interesting in theory, but too fragmented or too small for each supplier. The direction is to aggregate volume, for example across the Group or through co-sourcing, to become attractive, and then compete. Third row: bottleneck, and the supplier sees us as Exploitable or Nuisance. Secure supply, protect the contract, and work on alternatives over time."],[89,864.58,899.67,"Fourth row: strategic, and we are Core for the supplier. This is a good place to be. Partnership, joint value, open book. Fifth row: strategic, and we are Exploitable or Nuisance. This is the danger zone. We depend on the supplier, and the supplier does not care much about us. The direction is to raise our attractiveness or to reduce our dependency, in Chessboard terms, change the nature of demand. Which row do you think most insurer software and some ICT services sit in? The last one. That is why the next example matters."],[90,900.62,930.64,"Let's position P3, the Kelvar licences, 11.2 million euros a year. Kraljic: strategic, or bottleneck if the profit impact is judged lower. Either way the supply risk is high, with two credible alternative platforms and a migration of 18 to 24 months. Chessboard: high supply power, low demand power. Preferencing: likely Exploitable, as we saw in module 1.4. Put them together and Kelvar is in the danger zone."],[90,931.14,972.57,"So the strategy is to change the nature of demand. Which levers? First, technical data mining. In the case, an estimated 20 to 25 percent of named-user licences are unused. That is shelfware, and it is our first source of power, because we can stop paying for licences nobody uses, or trade them for something we need. Second, re-specification. Could a different licence metric, or a smaller module set, serve the business just as well? Third, risk management: protect ourselves against the 9 percent uplift pattern with caps on uplifts, a review of the auto-renewal clause and the notice period, and a credible alternative."],[90,973.07,1001.68,"Notice what is not on this slide: an RFQ with three quotes, or an auction. There is only one supplier who can renew these licences. Sending an RFQ would tell Kelvar that we have no strategy. The route here is a single-source negotiation, prepared with usage data, benchmarks and a BATNA, for example an RFI to the two alternative platforms. Session 2 and Session 4 build that negotiation step by step."],[91,1002.63,1020.42,"Second example, P5, office supplies and print. 2.3 million euros with 31 suppliers, and 1,900 orders a year below 1,000 euros. Kraljic: non-critical, close to leverage on volume print. Chessboard: manage spend."],[91,1020.92,1066.07,"The levers: volume bundling, putting the fragmented spend with fewer suppliers. Demand management, questioning whether all those orders are needed, for example printed brochures that could be digital. And commercial data mining: who orders what, at which price, from whom. The direction is to consolidate and to channel through a catalogue. The note on the slide says it well: the tail is channelled, not sourced event by event. Think about what 1,900 small orders cost in process time, approvals and invoices. Running a separate competitive event for each would cost more than the savings. The value is in the channel, a catalogue in Coupa with one or two suppliers, not in squeezing each order."],[91,1066.57,1080.8,"There is also a people benefit. When General Services stops spending its days raising small orders and chasing invoices, it has time to manage the categories that matter. That is part of the business case of channelling the tail."],[92,1081.75,1105.73,"Third example, P2, IT consulting, 6.1 million euros across 12 firms and 38 call-offs, with no framework. Kraljic: leverage for standard profiles, strategic for scarce expertise. Chessboard: leverage competition, once demand is bundled. Preferencing: probably Core for the top three firms, which together take most of the spend."],[92,1106.23,1149.93,"The levers: volume bundling, supplier pricing review, which makes sense when the same senior profile costs anything from 820 to 1,450 euros a day, and demand management. The direction: structure the spend, with a panel route to be designed in Session 2. Why not one supplier? Because consulting is rarely one quadrant. Developers and project managers are leverage. The one expert who knows the old claims system is closer to strategic. A panel of preferred suppliers, with rate cards and mini-competitions for larger projects, handles that mix. The same logic applies to creative agencies, P7, which we will see in the case."],[93,1150.88,1172.25,"Now the Group lens. Some of the demand power we need is something we already have, if we look beyond our own entity. The cards give the picture across the Group. About 90 supplier groups serve two or more entities. Around 50 million euros sits in some 360 separate local relationships. That is fragmented demand power."],[93,1172.75,1212.2,"The lever is co-sourcing: buying together with other entities on one contract. And the effect can be dramatic. An entity that is a nuisance for a supplier on its own can become an attractive account when it buys together with three others. A category that was manage spend can move to leverage competition. Bundling across entities is often the fastest way to change a position. So before you finish your positioning, ask a simple question. Did anyone check the contracts of other entities with the same supplier? For Kelvar, for example, if other entities of the Group run the same platform, the demand power picture changes completely."],[93,1212.7,1242.89,"The Group data offers another example. Advisory firms are used in nine entities through some 60 separate supplier records, for about 12 million euros, with no Group agreement. Each entity negotiates alone, and each is a small client for a very large firm. Together, they would be a significant one. You do not need a Group contract to start using this. Even knowing what rates other entities pay is commercial data mining, and it strengthens your next negotiation."],[94,1243.84,1273.33,"This slide is a preview of Session 2: from direction to route. Read the flow as three pairs. First pair: leverage, attractive account, leads to one supplier chosen by tender. That is cleaning and security. Second pair: leverage to strategic, with mixed needs, leads to a panel of preferred suppliers. That is consulting and agencies. Third pair: strategic, high supply power, leads to a single-source negotiation. That is Kelvar."],[94,1273.83,1300.09,"Today we name the direction. The route to market is decided in Session 2. Why the discipline? Because the moment people see the word tender, they start designing the RFP: criteria, weights, timelines. That is the old habit of jumping to the tool. Resist it. A direction like leverage competition on an output specification is a decision. The RFP is a consequence, and it comes later."],[94,1300.59,1315.82,"There is a fourth situation the flow does not show, the tail. For non-critical spend like P5, the route is often not a sourcing event at all, but a catalogue or a framework the business can order from. Keep that in mind when you look at the case."],[95,1316.77,1338.56,"Let's pause for a quick reflection. The poll asks where you would place the Kelvar licence renewal on the Chessboard: manage spend, leverage competition among suppliers, seek joint advantage with the supplier, or change the nature of demand. Think about your answer before the next slide, and about the evidence behind it: who has the power, and why."],[96,1339.51,1375.46,"Now apply all of this to your own Sourcing Case. The exercise takes about 20 minutes and lives under Module 1.5 in your workbook. First, score profit impact and supply risk from 1 to 5. Second, score our demand power and the supply power, also from 1 to 5. Third, choose the Kraljic quadrant and the Chessboard strategy. Fourth, pick up to four levers to test. And fifth, write the direction in one sentence, for example: leverage, attractive account, exploit competition through a single-supplier tender on an output specification."],[96,1375.96,1403.46,"Use the evidence from Practices 1.3 and 1.4. And remember that positions are judgements, not calculations. Scores from 1 to 5 are a way to make your reasoning visible, not a formula. If you work in a trio, ask your colleagues to challenge your positioning, and write down what they challenged. Pause the video now if you want to complete the exercise in your workbook, in the Modules tab, and continue when you are ready."],[97,1404.41,1430.61,"Welcome back. Let's debrief with four questions. Whose project sits in the danger zone, strategic for us and Exploitable or Nuisance for the supplier? Where did Kraljic and the Chessboard disagree? That happens, for example when a category looks leverage on Kraljic but the supplier holds the power on the Chessboard. When they disagree, the disagreement is usually telling you something about the supplier's view that Kraljic ignores."],[97,1431.11,1448.66,"Which levers had you never considered? For many buyers, it is technical data mining or demand management. And the last question is the bridge to the case. Does your direction still fit an RFQ with three quotes? For some projects it might. For many, the honest answer is no."],[98,1449.61,1468.54,"Let's close module 1.5 in three lines. One: Kraljic gives our view, Steele and Court the supplier's view, and the Chessboard the levers. Two: demand power can be built, through bundling, specification freedom and alternatives. Three: name the direction before choosing any tool."],[98,1469.04,1486.49,"In the next lesson we put all of this to work on the Nordvia case: eight projects, one team of nine, and eight positions. You will position the whole pipeline, decide which projects to start first, and then prepare the fieldwork that takes your own Sourcing Case into Session 2. See you there."],[98,1486.99,1513.21,"Before you move on, check your own Sourcing Case against these three lines. Can you say where it sits on Kraljic, how the supplier sees you, and which Chessboard strategy follows? Can you name one way to build more demand power? And have you written the direction in one sentence, without mentioning a tool? If yes, you have completed the Insight stage for your project. That is a real achievement, because it is the stage most teams skip."]],"questions":[{"after":82,"q":"Nordvia's security and reception service is 1.6 million euros, with several capable providers in each region and easy switching at contract end. Where does it most likely sit on Kraljic's matrix?","options":["Leverage","Strategic","Bottleneck","Non-critical"],"answer":0,"explain":"Meaningful spend with low supply risk is leverage, so the direction is to use buying power through competition and bundling.","t":257.44},{"after":84,"q":"A team has coloured every category on a Kraljic matrix and declares the strategy finished. What is missing according to Kraljic's own method?","options":["Nothing, the matrix is the strategy","A reverse auction for each leverage category","A RACI chart for each quadrant","The market analysis, the comparison of bargaining strength and the action plans"],"answer":3,"explain":"Classification is only phase one of four. The strategy comes from analysing the market, comparing power and writing action plans per quadrant.","t":445.26},{"after":86,"q":"Which situation calls for the Chessboard strategy change the nature of demand?","options":["Many suppliers compete for our large volume","Both we and the supplier are strong and depend on each other","A proprietary vendor holds the power and our demand power is low","Small fragmented spend with many suppliers"],"answer":2,"explain":"High supply power with low demand power means competition will not work. The answer is to change what or how much we buy, or our dependency, so the power balance shifts.","t":611.14},{"after":88,"q":"Your entity buys a niche analytics tool and has low demand power. Which action builds demand power most directly?","options":["Accept the supplier's terms to keep the relationship","Bundle the demand with other Group entities and relax a brand-specific requirement","Ask for a bigger discount without changing anything","Move the category to non-critical in the matrix"],"answer":1,"explain":"Demand power grows with bundling, specification freedom and a credible ability to switch. Those are partly in our hands, unlike market concentration.","t":812.51},{"after":90,"q":"For the Kelvar renewal, which first lever creates the most negotiating power?","options":["An RFQ to three resellers","A reverse auction among Kelvar's partners","Accepting the 9 percent uplift in exchange for goodwill","Technical data mining to quantify the unused named-user licences"],"answer":3,"explain":"An estimated 20 to 25 percent of licences are unused. Quantifying that shelfware is technical data mining, a change the nature of demand lever that gives Nordvia facts to negotiate with.","t":1001.68},{"after":93,"q":"Nordvia is a small, unattractive client for a facilities supplier that also serves three other Group entities. What is often the fastest way to change that position?","options":["Co-sourcing with the other entities on one contract","Paying invoices faster than agreed","Changing to a different quadrant label","Running more frequent RFQs"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bundling across entities turns fragmented demand into an attractive account and can move a category from manage spend to leverage competition.","t":1242.89},{"after":97,"q":"Your Kraljic analysis says leverage, but on the Chessboard the supplier clearly holds the power. What should you do?","options":["Trust Kraljic and run an auction","Average the two positions","Investigate the disagreement, since it often reveals the supplier's view or a dependency Kraljic ignores","Drop the Chessboard, it is less established"],"answer":2,"explain":"Positions are judgements. When lenses disagree, the evidence behind the disagreement, often supply power or the supplier's view, is what should shape the direction.","t":1448.66}],"cues":[[0.6,2.89,"Welcome to module 1.5: power decides strategy."],[2.89,10.8,"This is the last step of the Insight stage of Session 1, and in many ways the most important one."],[10.8,14.26,"In the previous lessons we built two halves of a picture."],[14.26,20.67,"Our half came from the fact base: spend, contracts, demand drivers and total cost of ownership."],[20.95,26.58,"The supplier's half came from the market analysis and from Steele and Court's preferencing model."],[26.58,31.05,"In this lesson we put the two halves together and turn them into a direction."],[31.55,33.55,"We will use three lenses, together."],[33.55,36.23,"Kraljic's matrix gives the buyer's view."],[36.23,39.02,"Steele and Court give the supplier's view."],[39.02,43.95,"And the Kearney Purchasing Chessboard turns the balance of power into concrete levers."],[43.95,48.15,"We will look at each lens, at its limits, and at how they combine."],[48.43,58.64,"Then we will position three Nordvia projects, see what the Group adds to our demand power, preview the routes to market of Session 2, and finish with your own positioning."],[58.64,63.59,"Remember the sentence of the course: Source to Contract is a decision chain."],[63.59,67.05,"This lesson is where the chain makes its central decision."],[68.0,69.56,"Let's start with history."],[69.56,79.74,"In 1983 Peter Kraljic published an article in the Harvard Business Review with a provocative title: Purchasing must become supply management."],[79.74,82.46,"His motivation sounds very familiar."],[82.74,94.11,"He observed that purchasing was attached to routine, to yearly negotiations with established suppliers, at a time when supply could be, in his words, upset virtually overnight."],[94.61,96.45,"Think about what that means for us."],[96.45,100.38,"Routine is the habit of treating every purchase the same way."],[100.38,106.36,"At Nordvia, that habit had a name: the RFQ with three quotes, whatever the category."],[106.36,113.7,"Kraljic's argument was that different purchases carry different risks and different value, and so they deserve different strategies."],[113.98,122.05,"More than forty years later, that is still the core idea of category management, and it is exactly what we are trying to do in this course."],[122.05,125.33,"Stop treating every purchase with the same routine."],[125.83,128.1,"Kraljic also gave us a useful attitude."],[128.1,132.7,"He did not say that purchasing should do more analysis for its own sake."],[132.7,138.34,"He said that the effort a buyer spends on a purchase should match what is at stake."],[138.34,145.12,"A box of pens and a claims platform do not deserve the same process, the same time or the same people."],[145.12,146.29,"That sounds obvious."],[146.57,153.69,"Yet in many teams, the effort is driven by the template and the approval threshold, not by the risk and the value."],[154.65,157.48,"Here is the matrix that made Kraljic famous."],[157.48,158.17,"Two axes."],[158.17,165.47,"Horizontally, profit impact: how much the category affects our costs, our results or our service."],[165.47,173.49,"Volume of spend is part of it, but so is the effect on the business, for example on claims handling or on customer experience."],[173.77,177.21,"Vertically, supply risk: how hard it is to secure supply."],[177.21,183.54,"That depends on the number of suppliers, on switching costs, on scarcity and on regulation."],[184.04,185.18,"Four quadrants."],[185.18,188.78,"Bottom left, non-critical: low impact and low risk."],[188.78,194.97,"The direction is to simplify and reduce effort: catalogues, standard terms, purchasing cards."],[194.97,199.19,"Bottom right, highlighted, leverage: high impact and low risk."],[199.19,204.18,"Here we use our buying power, with competition, bundling and target prices."],[204.46,207.86,"Top left, bottleneck: low impact but high risk."],[207.86,213.11,"Secure supply, accept a premium if needed, and build alternatives over time."],[213.11,216.93,"Top right, strategic: high impact and high risk."],[216.93,222.14,"Here we look for partnership, joint development, and, just as important, exit planning."],[222.64,224.41,"Why is leverage highlighted?"],[224.41,229.08,"Because most of an insurer's indirect spend sits in leverage and non-critical."],[229.08,235.82,"Cleaning, security, print, mobile telecom and temporary staff are leverage for most entities."],[235.82,239.79,"That is good news: these are categories where competition works."],[240.07,245.27,"But the interesting cases, the ones that need the most thought, are the exceptions."],[245.27,247.26,"A proprietary software platform."],[247.26,250.0,"A managed service that supports claims."],[250.0,253.44,"A data feed that only one provider can supply."],[253.44,257.44,"Those are the ones where a routine approach does real damage."],[258.39,262.09,"This table makes it concrete for an insurer."],[262.09,266.41,"Leverage: cleaning, security, print, mobile telecom, temporary staff."],[266.41,272.69,"Strategic: a claims IT managed service, a lead creative agency, core platforms."],[272.69,278.38,"Bottleneck: niche data feeds, regulatory reporting tools, proprietary software."],[278.66,283.59,"Non-critical: office supplies, promotional items, small one-off purchases."],[284.08,287.57,"Now look at the footnote, because it is important."],[287.57,288.65,"Typical, not fixed."],[288.65,292.62,"The same category can move with the market or with our dependency."],[292.62,294.09,"Here is an example."],[294.09,300.87,"Mobile telecom is leverage for most entities: several operators, standard tariffs, easy switching."],[301.15,307.72,"But imagine an entity that has built its field claims app on one operator's private network."],[307.72,311.66,"For that entity, mobile telecom may have moved towards bottleneck."],[311.66,313.42,"Or think of a creative agency."],[313.42,318.2,"For one entity it is the lead agency that holds the brand, strategic."],[318.2,322.35,"For another it is a production shop for brochures, leverage."],[322.63,324.43,"So do not copy the table."],[324.43,330.79,"Use it as a starting point and ask, for each category, why it sits where it does in your own entity."],[330.79,336.16,"If you can name one category that sits differently in your entity, you have understood the footnote."],[336.66,338.86,"One more observation on this table."],[338.86,340.77,"Where does regulation fit?"],[340.77,344.53,"It mostly pushes categories up the supply risk axis."],[344.53,355.45,"A service that supports a critical or important function under Solvency II, or an ICT service under DORA, carries a supply risk that is not only commercial."],[355.73,360.27,"If it fails, the regulator is interested, and the exit takes planning."],[360.27,368.94,"That is why claims IT managed services and core platforms sit in strategic, even when the market has several capable providers."],[369.89,371.72,"Many people know the matrix."],[371.72,379.3,"Fewer know that Kraljic's article describes a method in four phases, and that the matrix is only the first one."],[379.3,384.77,"Phase one, classify: place the purchases by profit impact and supply risk."],[384.77,389.09,"Phase two, analyse the market: supplier strength and availability."],[389.09,391.56,"That is the work we did in module 1.4."],[391.84,397.38,"Phase three, position: compare our bargaining strength against the suppliers'."],[397.38,400.08,"Phase four, act: action plans for each quadrant."],[400.58,402.38,"The footnote adds a nuance."],[402.38,412.12,"For strategic items, Kraljic's approach is often summarised as a choice between exploit, balance or diversify, depending on that balance of power."],[412.12,415.08,"If we are stronger, we exploit our position."],[415.08,417.99,"If the power is roughly equal, we balance."],[418.27,423.68,"If the supplier is stronger, we diversify, which means reducing our dependency."],[423.68,430.74,"That summary is widely used, but check the wording against the original article before you quote it in a paper."],[430.74,434.7,"The practical lesson is this: many teams stop at phase one."],[434.7,437.79,"They colour a matrix and think the strategy is done."],[438.07,440.38,"The matrix only tells you where to look."],[440.38,445.26,"The power comparison and the action plan are where the strategy actually lives."],[446.21,450.25,"Kraljic is powerful, but it has limits, and a good buyer knows them."],[450.25,451.36,"Four of them."],[451.36,453.97,"First, it is the buyer's view only."],[453.97,456.81,"It says nothing about how the supplier sees us."],[456.81,461.83,"A category can be strategic for us and still be irrelevant to the supplier."],[461.83,463.17,"Second, it is static."],[463.45,467.65,"Positions move with markets and with our dependency, as we just saw."],[468.15,473.34,"Third, it works at category level, which hides differences between suppliers."],[473.34,482.38,"Within IT consulting, one firm may be a commodity provider of developers while another holds the only expert in an old claims system."],[482.38,483.72,"Fourth, it gives no levers."],[484.0,489.92,"It names a direction, use your buying power for example, but not the concrete moves that get you there."],[489.92,493.98,"The good news is that each limit is answered by another lens."],[493.98,496.55,"Preferencing gives the supplier's view."],[496.55,498.52,"The Chessboard gives the levers."],[498.52,503.33,"And a regular review, at least before each renewal, deals with the static problem."],[503.84,507.58,"Here is how that plays out in a real conversation."],[507.58,514.11,"A buyer presents a Kraljic matrix to the CIO and says: Kelvar is strategic, so we need a partnership."],[514.11,518.5,"The CIO answers: we have tried, but Kelvar does not return our calls."],[518.5,524.3,"The matrix was right about our dependency, and silent about the vendor's interest."],[524.58,530.81,"Only when you add the supplier's view and the levers do you get a strategy that survives that conversation."],[531.75,533.42,"So let's look at the Chessboard."],[533.42,540.75,"The Purchasing Chessboard was developed at Kearney by Schuh, Kromoser, Strohmer, Romero P\u00e9rez and Triplat."],[540.75,542.93,"Its key move is to change the axes."],[542.93,547.75,"Instead of profit impact and supply risk, it puts two powers on the axes."],[547.75,550.04,"Horizontally, our demand power."],[550.04,551.93,"Vertically, the supply power."],[552.21,554.96,"Comparing the two gives four basic strategies."],[555.47,558.06,"Bottom left, both powers low: manage spend."],[558.06,563.21,"The category is not worth much to either side, so we manage it efficiently."],[563.21,569.59,"Bottom right, high demand power and low supply power: leverage competition among suppliers."],[569.59,571.43,"This is the classic tender."],[571.43,575.8,"Top right, both powers high: seek joint advantage with the supplier."],[576.08,579.75,"Neither side can dictate, so value comes from working together."],[580.25,586.81,"And top left, highlighted, high supply power and low demand power: change the nature of demand."],[586.81,596.65,"This is the quadrant most people find hardest, because the natural reaction is to try to compete, and competition does not work when the supplier holds the power."],[596.65,599.32,"The answer is not to push harder on price."],[599.6,607.68,"The answer is to change what we buy, or how much we buy, or how we depend on it, so that the power balance itself changes."],[607.68,611.14,"We will see exactly what that means with Kelvar in a few minutes."],[612.09,616.27,"Each basic strategy opens four levers, sixteen in total."],[616.27,621.77,"The full Chessboard has 64 methods, but the sixteen levers are enough for this course."],[621.77,624.23,"Let's walk through them, row by row."],[624.73,630.43,"Manage spend: demand management, which questions whether the demand is justified at all."],[630.43,634.81,"Co-sourcing, buying together with other entities on one contract."],[634.81,638.36,"Volume bundling, putting fragmented volume together."],[638.36,644.51,"And commercial data mining, looking in our data for spend, prices and terms we did not know we had."],[645.0,649.76,"Leverage competition: tendering, the structured competitive process."],[649.76,654.96,"Supplier pricing review, comparing prices across suppliers and against benchmarks."],[654.96,659.59,"Globalisation, widening the supplier base beyond the usual local firms."],[659.59,665.95,"And target pricing, which means estimating what the service should cost from its cost drivers."],[666.23,668.87,"You will meet that as should-cost later in the course."],[669.38,677.07,"Seek joint advantage: value partnership, cost partnership, integrated operations planning and value chain management."],[677.07,683.35,"These are the levers of a long-term relationship, where both sides share data and improvements."],[683.63,691.12,"Change the nature of demand: innovation breakthrough, re-specification, technical data mining and risk management."],[691.12,699.6,"Technical data mining means analysing how we actually use what we buy, for example how many licences are really used."],[699.6,704.69,"Re-specification means changing the requirement so that more suppliers can meet it."],[704.97,711.79,"Risk management means protecting ourselves against a dominant supplier, with exit plans and alternatives."],[711.79,717.42,"A practical tip: when you position a project, do not try to use all four levers."],[717.42,721.03,"Pick the two or three that your evidence supports, and test them."],[721.98,723.32,"What drives each power?"],[723.32,725.17,"On the left, our demand power."],[725.17,726.8,"Volume and its growth."],[726.8,729.17,"Bundling across areas and entities."],[729.17,731.9,"Freedom to change the specification."],[731.9,734.56,"And a credible ability to switch."],[734.56,736.2,"On the right, supply power."],[736.2,737.24,"Market concentration."],[737.24,740.5,"Proprietary technology or intellectual property."],[740.5,742.33,"Switching cost and time."],[742.61,743.98,"And scarce capacity."],[744.48,746.35,"Now look at the two lists again."],[746.35,750.16,"The right-hand list is mostly given by the market."],[750.16,753.23,"We cannot easily change how concentrated a market is."],[753.23,756.24,"But the left-hand list is partly in our hands."],[756.24,760.89,"Bundling can be created: we can combine the demand of several entities."],[761.17,767.25,"Specification freedom can be created: we can stop insisting on one brand or one method."],[767.25,774.48,"A credible ability to switch can be created: we can run an RFI to alternatives, or qualify a second supplier."],[774.48,779.04,"In other words, demand power is not only something we have."],[779.04,780.73,"It is something we can build."],[781.01,783.91,"That idea is the engine of the whole module."],[784.41,785.62,"A short example."],[785.62,794.58,"Imagine a reception service where the specification insists on one particular visitor management system that only the incumbent operates."],[794.58,801.71,"Supply power is high, not because the market is concentrated, but because our own specification made it so."],[801.99,808.04,"Remove that requirement, specify the outcome instead, and suddenly five firms can bid."],[808.04,812.51,"That is re-specification turning supply power into demand power."],[813.46,819.19,"Now let's combine Kraljic and preferencing, the buyer's view and the supplier's view."],[819.19,820.97,"Five combinations on this table."],[820.97,826.43,"First row: leverage for us, and the supplier sees us as Core or Development."],[826.43,830.51,"The direction is to exploit competition, firm and fair."],[830.79,835.09,"Suppliers want the business, so a well-run tender will produce good offers."],[835.59,839.65,"Second row: leverage for us, but we are Nuisance for the supplier."],[839.65,845.51,"Our spend is interesting in theory, but too fragmented or too small for each supplier."],[845.51,853.77,"The direction is to aggregate volume, for example across the Group or through co-sourcing, to become attractive, and then compete."],[854.05,859.3,"Third row: bottleneck, and the supplier sees us as Exploitable or Nuisance."],[859.3,864.07,"Secure supply, protect the contract, and work on alternatives over time."],[864.58,867.93,"Fourth row: strategic, and we are Core for the supplier."],[867.93,869.71,"This is a good place to be."],[869.71,872.59,"Partnership, joint value, open book."],[872.59,876.75,"Fifth row: strategic, and we are Exploitable or Nuisance."],[876.75,877.54,"This is the danger zone."],[877.54,882.63,"We depend on the supplier, and the supplier does not care much about us."],[882.91,890.83,"The direction is to raise our attractiveness or to reduce our dependency, in Chessboard terms, change the nature of demand."],[890.83,896.1,"Which row do you think most insurer software and some ICT services sit in?"],[896.1,897.25,"The last one."],[897.25,899.68,"That is why the next example matters."],[900.62,906.27,"Let's position P3, the Kelvar licences, 11.2 million euros a year."],[906.27,910.29,"Kraljic: strategic, or bottleneck if the profit impact is judged lower."],[910.29,918.85,"Either way the supply risk is high, with two credible alternative platforms and a migration of 18 to 24 months."],[918.85,922.57,"Chessboard: high supply power, low demand power."],[922.85,927.47,"Preferencing: likely Exploitable, as we saw in module 1.4."],[927.47,930.64,"Put them together and Kelvar is in the danger zone."],[931.14,934.12,"So the strategy is to change the nature of demand."],[934.12,935.4,"Which levers?"],[935.4,937.89,"First, technical data mining."],[937.89,942.58,"In the case, an estimated 20 to 25 percent of named-user licences are unused."],[942.58,952.43,"That is shelfware, and it is our first source of power, because we can stop paying for licences nobody uses, or trade them for something we need."],[952.71,954.73,"Second, re-specification."],[954.73,960.29,"Could a different licence metric, or a smaller module set, serve the business just as well?"],[960.29,972.57,"Third, risk management: protect ourselves against the 9 percent uplift pattern with caps on uplifts, a review of the auto-renewal clause and the notice period, and a credible alternative."],[973.07,978.27,"Notice what is not on this slide: an RFQ with three quotes, or an auction."],[978.27,982.12,"There is only one supplier who can renew these licences."],[982.12,986.0,"Sending an RFQ would tell Kelvar that we have no strategy."],[986.28,996.78,"The route here is a single-source negotiation, prepared with usage data, benchmarks and a BATNA, for example an RFI to the two alternative platforms."],[996.78,1001.68,"Session 2 and Session 4 build that negotiation step by step."],[1002.63,1006.36,"Second example, P5, office supplies and print."],[1006.36,1013.95,"2.3 million euros with 31 suppliers, and 1,900 orders a year below 1,000 euros."],[1013.95,1018.3,"Kraljic: non-critical, close to leverage on volume print."],[1018.3,1020.42,"Chessboard: manage spend."],[1020.92,1026.13,"The levers: volume bundling, putting the fragmented spend with fewer suppliers."],[1026.13,1033.62,"Demand management, questioning whether all those orders are needed, for example printed brochures that could be digital."],[1033.62,1038.31,"And commercial data mining: who orders what, at which price, from whom."],[1038.59,1042.42,"The direction is to consolidate and to channel through a catalogue."],[1042.42,1048.29,"The note on the slide says it well: the tail is channelled, not sourced event by event."],[1048.29,1053.88,"Think about what 1,900 small orders cost in process time, approvals and invoices."],[1053.88,1059.54,"Running a separate competitive event for each would cost more than the savings."],[1059.82,1066.07,"The value is in the channel, a catalogue in Coupa with one or two suppliers, not in squeezing each order."],[1066.57,1068.52,"There is also a people benefit."],[1068.52,1077.46,"When General Services stops spending its days raising small orders and chasing invoices, it has time to manage the categories that matter."],[1077.46,1080.8,"That is part of the business case of channelling the tail."],[1081.75,1089.92,"Third example, P2, IT consulting, 6.1 million euros across 12 firms and 38 call-offs, with no framework."],[1089.92,1095.88,"Kraljic: leverage for standard profiles, strategic for scarce expertise."],[1095.88,1100.1,"Chessboard: leverage competition, once demand is bundled."],[1100.38,1105.73,"Preferencing: probably Core for the top three firms, which together take most of the spend."],[1106.23,1118.55,"The levers: volume bundling, supplier pricing review, which makes sense when the same senior profile costs anything from 820 to 1,450 euros a day, and demand management."],[1118.55,1124.55,"The direction: structure the spend, with a panel route to be designed in Session 2."],[1124.55,1125.57,"Why not one supplier?"],[1125.57,1128.78,"Because consulting is rarely one quadrant."],[1129.06,1132.02,"Developers and project managers are leverage."],[1132.02,1137.15,"The one expert who knows the old claims system is closer to strategic."],[1137.15,1144.12,"A panel of preferred suppliers, with rate cards and mini-competitions for larger projects, handles that mix."],[1144.12,1149.92,"The same logic applies to creative agencies, P7, which we will see in the case."],[1150.88,1152.24,"Now the Group lens."],[1152.24,1158.77,"Some of the demand power we need is something we already have, if we look beyond our own entity."],[1158.77,1162.15,"The cards give the picture across the Group."],[1162.15,1166.0,"About 90 supplier groups serve two or more entities."],[1166.0,1169.9,"Around 50 million euros sits in some 360 separate local relationships."],[1169.9,1172.26,"That is fragmented demand power."],[1172.75,1177.62,"The lever is co-sourcing: buying together with other entities on one contract."],[1177.62,1179.73,"And the effect can be dramatic."],[1179.73,1187.58,"An entity that is a nuisance for a supplier on its own can become an attractive account when it buys together with three others."],[1187.58,1191.65,"A category that was manage spend can move to leverage competition."],[1191.93,1196.46,"Bundling across entities is often the fastest way to change a position."],[1196.46,1200.4,"So before you finish your positioning, ask a simple question."],[1200.4,1204.45,"Did anyone check the contracts of other entities with the same supplier?"],[1204.73,1212.2,"For Kelvar, for example, if other entities of the Group run the same platform, the demand power picture changes completely."],[1212.7,1215.24,"The Group data offers another example."],[1215.24,1224.59,"Advisory firms are used in nine entities through some 60 separate supplier records, for about 12 million euros, with no Group agreement."],[1224.59,1230.39,"Each entity negotiates alone, and each is a small client for a very large firm."],[1230.39,1232.77,"Together, they would be a significant one."],[1233.05,1236.12,"You do not need a Group contract to start using this."],[1236.12,1242.89,"Even knowing what rates other entities pay is commercial data mining, and it strengthens your next negotiation."],[1243.84,1247.51,"This slide is a preview of Session 2: from direction to route."],[1247.51,1249.82,"Read the flow as three pairs."],[1249.82,1255.38,"First pair: leverage, attractive account, leads to one supplier chosen by tender."],[1255.38,1257.57,"That is cleaning and security."],[1257.57,1263.74,"Second pair: leverage to strategic, with mixed needs, leads to a panel of preferred suppliers."],[1264.02,1266.33,"That is consulting and agencies."],[1266.33,1272.16,"Third pair: strategic, high supply power, leads to a single-source negotiation."],[1272.16,1273.33,"That is Kelvar."],[1273.83,1275.68,"Today we name the direction."],[1275.68,1278.74,"The route to market is decided in Session 2."],[1278.74,1280.15,"Why the discipline?"],[1280.15,1287.57,"Because the moment people see the word tender, they start designing the RFP: criteria, weights, timelines."],[1287.57,1290.27,"That is the old habit of jumping to the tool."],[1290.27,1291.24,"Resist it."],[1291.52,1296.6,"A direction like leverage competition on an output specification is a decision."],[1296.6,1300.09,"The RFP is a consequence, and it comes later."],[1300.59,1304.3,"There is a fourth situation the flow does not show, the tail."],[1304.3,1313.38,"For non-critical spend like P5, the route is often not a sourcing event at all, but a catalogue or a framework the business can order from."],[1313.38,1315.82,"Keep that in mind when you look at the case."],[1316.77,1318.92,"Let's pause for a quick reflection."],[1318.92,1332.17,"The poll asks where you would place the Kelvar licence renewal on the Chessboard: manage spend, leverage competition among suppliers, seek joint advantage with the supplier, or change the nature of demand."],[1332.45,1338.55,"Think about your answer before the next slide, and about the evidence behind it: who has the power, and why."],[1339.51,1342.28,"Now apply all of this to your own Sourcing Case."],[1342.28,1348.44,"The exercise takes about 20 minutes and lives under Module 1.5 in your workbook."],[1348.44,1352.09,"First, score profit impact and supply risk from 1 to 5."],[1352.09,1357.53,"Second, score our demand power and the supply power, also from 1 to 5."],[1357.53,1361.44,"Third, choose the Kraljic quadrant and the Chessboard strategy."],[1361.72,1364.4,"Fourth, pick up to four levers to test."],[1364.4,1375.46,"And fifth, write the direction in one sentence, for example: leverage, attractive account, exploit competition through a single-supplier tender on an output specification."],[1375.96,1378.78,"Use the evidence from Practices 1.3 and 1.4."],[1378.78,1383.02,"And remember that positions are judgements, not calculations."],[1383.02,1388.16,"Scores from 1 to 5 are a way to make your reasoning visible, not a formula."],[1388.16,1395.76,"If you work in a trio, ask your colleagues to challenge your positioning, and write down what they challenged."],[1396.04,1403.46,"Pause the video now if you want to complete the exercise in your workbook, in the Modules tab, and continue when you are ready."],[1404.41,1405.17,"Welcome back."],[1405.17,1406.92,"Let's debrief with four questions."],[1406.92,1413.66,"Whose project sits in the danger zone, strategic for us and Exploitable or Nuisance for the supplier?"],[1413.66,1417.06,"Where did Kraljic and the Chessboard disagree?"],[1417.06,1424.04,"That happens, for example when a category looks leverage on Kraljic but the supplier holds the power on the Chessboard."],[1424.32,1430.61,"When they disagree, the disagreement is usually telling you something about the supplier's view that Kraljic ignores."],[1431.11,1433.2,"Which levers had you never considered?"],[1433.2,1437.75,"For many buyers, it is technical data mining or demand management."],[1437.75,1440.73,"And the last question is the bridge to the case."],[1440.73,1444.33,"Does your direction still fit an RFQ with three quotes?"],[1444.33,1446.04,"For some projects it might."],[1446.04,1448.67,"For many, the honest answer is no."],[1449.61,1452.77,"Let's close module 1.5 in three lines."],[1452.77,1458.97,"One: Kraljic gives our view, Steele and Court the supplier's view, and the Chessboard the levers."],[1458.97,1465.23,"Two: demand power can be built, through bundling, specification freedom and alternatives."],[1465.23,1468.53,"Three: name the direction before choosing any tool."],[1469.04,1476.45,"In the next lesson we put all of this to work on the Nordvia case: eight projects, one team of nine, and eight positions."],[1476.45,1485.43,"You will position the whole pipeline, decide which projects to start first, and then prepare the fieldwork that takes your own Sourcing Case into Session 2."],[1485.43,1486.49,"See you there."],[1486.99,1491.14,"Before you move on, check your own Sourcing Case against these three lines."],[1491.14,1497.65,"Can you say where it sits on Kraljic, how the supplier sees you, and which Chessboard strategy follows?"],[1497.65,1500.77,"Can you name one way to build more demand power?"],[1500.77,1504.85,"And have you written the direction in one sentence, without mentioning a tool?"],[1505.13,1508.76,"If yes, you have completed the Insight stage for your project."],[1508.76,1513.21,"That is a real achievement, because it is the stage most teams skip."]]},{"id":"s1-l6","session":"s1","title":"Case 1: positioning Nordvia's pipeline","summary":"Position a pipeline of eight sourcing projects, choose which to start first, and prepare the need-validation fieldwork that carries your own Sourcing Case into Session 2.","from":99,"to":112,"audio":"audio/s1-l6.mp3","duration":1460.5,"segs":[[99,0.6,29.79,"Welcome to the last lesson of Session 1. This lesson covers Case 1, Nordvia's sourcing pipeline, then the fieldwork you will do before Session 2, and finally a recap of the whole session. The case brings together everything we have learned: the intake and regulatory triage, the fact base, the market analysis, supplier preferencing, Kraljic and the Chessboard. The subtitle sets the scene. Eight projects, one team of nine, and a habit of three quotes."],[99,30.29,55.51,"As always, Nordvia Insurance is fictional. It is a mid-sized insurer of about 4,200 employees, part of a European insurance group, with 186 million euros of indirect spend a year. The situations in the case are realistic, and you will probably recognise several of them from your own entity. That is the point. The case is a rehearsal for your own pipeline."],[99,56.01,79.88,"Here is how the lesson runs. First we read the situation and the eight projects. Then you position them yourself, in the Case tab of your workbook. After that I show one possible answer and we debrief it. Then we prepare the fieldwork, with the key questions for your meeting with the business owner. And we close with a recap of the five big ideas of Session 1 and a look ahead to Session 2."],[100,80.83,100.29,"Let's start with the situation. Nordvia's Procurement team runs about 60 sourcing events a year in Coupa. Most of them were RFQs with three quotes, whatever the category. Cleaning, software, consulting, creative agencies: the same template, the same three quotes, the same lowest price wins."],[100,100.79,133.47,"Why is that a problem? Think back to everything we saw in this session. Three quotes work well when the market is competitive, the specification is clear, and the supplier wants our business. They work badly when the incumbent holds the power, when the need has not been validated, or when the real issue is fragmented demand. A three-quote RFQ on a software renewal tells the vendor that we have no strategy. A three-quote RFQ on 1,900 small print orders costs more in process than it saves."],[100,133.97,154.82,"So the new Head of Procurement sets a new rule: every project in the pipeline is positioned before anyone chooses a tool. There are eight projects in the pipeline, worth 55 million euros of annual spend in total. That figure is simply the sum of the eight projects in the case. Your task, in a moment, is to position all eight."],[100,155.32,192.48,"Before we look at the projects, picture the team. Nine buyers, 60 events a year. That is fewer than seven events per buyer, and each event takes weeks of work. If every event gets the same template, the team spends the same effort on a box of toner as on a 19 million euro managed service. Positioning is not extra work on top of sourcing. It is how a small team decides where to spend its limited time. A project positioned as manage spend may need a catalogue and an afternoon. A project in the danger zone may need three months of preparation and the CIO in the room."],[101,193.43,203.16,"Here is the first half of the pipeline. Take a moment to read it. I will walk you through each row, and as I do, start forming a view on where each project sits."],[101,203.66,246.4,"P1, office cleaning across three sites in two countries. 2.4 million euros. Three local cleaners, with contracts rolled over since 2019, and quality complaints at headquarters. What does that tell you? A competitive market, as we saw in module 1.4, an attractive account for local firms, but no management of the contracts. P2, IT consulting. 6.1 million euros spread across 12 firms and 38 call-offs, with no framework. Remember the day rates for the same senior profile: from 820 to 1,450 euros. That spread is a sign of fragmented demand and missing competition."],[101,246.9,281.03,"P3, the Kelvar licences. 11.2 million euros, a 9 percent uplift at the last renewal, no usage data and an auto-renewal clause. We positioned it in module 1.5: the danger zone. P4, the Telmo network. 4.9 million euros, rolled over twice, and, here is the key phrase, used by claims systems. A network service that claims systems depend on is an ICT service under DORA, and it may support a critical function. Yet nobody has reviewed it for years. Keep that in mind."],[101,281.53,317.12,"Look also at who owns these four projects. Facilities owns P1. IT owns P2, P3 and P4. That concentration matters. IT is the largest area of Nordvia's spend, and the CIO will be your most important stakeholder for this pipeline. If the CIO sees Procurement only as the team that sends RFQs, she will not involve you early in the Kelvar renewal or in the Telmo review. If she sees Procurement as the team that brings usage data, benchmarks and a DORA view, the conversation is very different."],[102,318.07,354.47,"The second half of the pipeline. P5, office supplies and print. 2.3 million euros with 31 suppliers and 1,900 purchase orders a year below 1,000 euros. We met it in module 1.5: the tail. P6, security and reception. 1.6 million euros, one provider, hourly rates indexed twice in two years, and no KPIs. A labour-intensive service with a single provider, two indexations and no measurement of performance. What does that suggest? An account that pays more every year without knowing what it gets."],[102,354.97,408.82,"P7, creative agencies. 7.2 million euros with six agencies, no rate card, and every campaign briefed to a favourite. Overlapping briefs mean Nordvia may be paying two agencies to think about the same problem. And P8, the Northbyte claims IT managed service. 19.4 million euros, the largest project. It supports a critical function, it is in the DORA register of information, and it has no tested exit plan. Under DORA, exit strategies for ICT services that support critical or important functions must be documented and tested, so that gap is not optional. And because it is an outsourced critical function, the Solvency II outsourcing rules must also be checked. The full table, with owners and expiry dates, is in the Case tab of your workbook."],[102,409.32,448.59,"Now step back and look at both halves together. Three projects are with a single provider: P3 Kelvar, P4 Telmo and P8 Northbyte, plus P6 for security. Those single-provider situations carry very different risks. Security has several capable alternatives in the market. Northbyte supports a critical function and would take a long transition to replace. Two projects, P2 and P7, have the opposite problem: too many suppliers and no structure. And one, P5, is a long tail of small orders. Already, before any matrix, you can see that one template cannot fit all eight."],[103,449.54,487.88,"Now the exercise. It takes about 30 minutes, and it lives in the Case tab of your workbook. For each of the eight projects, you decide five things. First, the Kraljic quadrant and the Chessboard strategy. Second, how the main supplier probably sees Nordvia, using Steele and Court. Third, the regulatory flag from the triage: none, personal data, staff transfer or labour law, DORA ICT, DORA ICT plus critical function, or Solvency II outsourcing. Fourth, the direction, in a few words. And then the team question: which two projects should Nordvia start first, and why?"],[103,488.38,514.92,"When you choose the first two, use three criteria: value, risk and timing. Value is how much money or quality is at stake. Risk is what happens if nothing is done, including regulatory risk. Timing is how close the contract end or the notice period is, and how long the right process will take. A software negotiation, for example, needs months of preparation before the notice deadline, not weeks."],[103,515.42,546.32,"If you are short of time, go deep on two projects and fill the other six more quickly, but do fill all eight, because the comparison between them is where the learning happens. If you are in a live group, you will present in three minutes per team. If you are working alone, imagine you are presenting to the Head of Procurement. Pause the video now if you want to complete the exercise in the Case tab of your workbook, and continue when you are ready. The next slide shows one possible answer, so try it before you look."],[104,547.27,557.46,"Here is one possible answer. Note the word possible. Positions are judgements, and other answers are acceptable where the evidence supports them. Let's walk through it."],[104,557.96,588.6,"P1 cleaning and P6 security: leverage. The market is competitive, the cost drivers are clear, and for local firms Nordvia is an attractive account. The direction: compete, with one supplier chosen by tender. For P1 the regulatory flag is staff transfer and labour law, because cleaning staff may move with the contract. For P6 the main gap is the missing KPIs. Both projects gain from an output specification and a managed contract."],[104,589.1,623.28,"P5 supplies and print: non-critical, close to leverage on volume print. The direction: consolidate and channel through a catalogue. The tail is channelled, not sourced event by event. P2 consulting and P7 creative agencies: leverage moving to strategic. Each contains standard work and scarce expertise. The direction: structure the spend, through a panel of preferred suppliers with rate cards. For creative work there is one more point to remember for later sessions: no auctions for creative work."],[104,623.78,645.8,"P3 Kelvar: bottleneck or strategic, with high supply power, and likely Exploitable. The direction: change the nature of demand, with usage data, re-specification and a credible alternative, leading to a single-source negotiation. The regulatory flag is DORA ICT, because core software is an ICT service."],[104,646.3,703.03,"P4 Telmo: leverage or bottleneck, depending on how specific the network is. The direction: review it now. The regulatory flag is DORA ICT, and because the claims systems use it, Risk must confirm whether it supports a critical function. And P8 Northbyte: strategic, a DORA critical function, with Solvency II outsourcing to check. The direction: partnership with a tested exit. That may sound like a contradiction, but it is not. We want a close relationship with a provider we depend on, and we need a credible, tested plan to leave if we must. The exit plan is the gap, and it is also our insurance in the next negotiation. Now compare this table with the starting point. Of the eight projects, how many would an RFQ with three quotes have served well? Perhaps none of them fully, and certainly not P3, P4, P5 or P8."],[104,703.53,744.15,"Let me add the Chessboard and preferencing view for a few of these, because the table only shows Kraljic. For P1 and P6, the Chessboard strategy is leverage competition among suppliers, and the levers are tendering, supplier pricing review and target pricing, using the wage floor as a check. For P5, it is manage spend, with volume bundling and demand management. For P2 and P7, the top firms probably see Nordvia as Core, but the smaller ones may see it as Nuisance, and the levers are volume bundling, supplier pricing review and demand management, such as stopping overlapping briefs to two agencies."],[104,744.65,779.06,"For P3, as we saw, the strategy is change the nature of demand, with technical data mining, re-specification and risk management. For P8, the Chessboard strategy is closer to seek joint advantage with the supplier, with levers like value partnership and integrated operations planning, combined with risk management through the exit plan. And for P4, the first lever is simply commercial data mining: find out what Telmo actually provides, at what price, and under which terms, before deciding whether to compete it."],[105,780.01,806.6,"Let's debrief the case with four questions. First: which projects would three quotes have served badly? Kelvar, because there is only one supplier and an RFQ shows we have no strategy. Northbyte, because the issue is dependency and exit, not price. The print tail, because 1,900 small events cost more than they save. And the agencies, because creative quality cannot be bought on three quotes."],[105,807.1,852.05,"Second: where did teams disagree, and on what evidence? Common disagreements are Telmo, leverage or bottleneck, and Kelvar, bottleneck or strategic. That is fine, as long as each team can show the evidence. Third: which projects did you start first, value, risk or timing? In practice, P4 and P8 usually come first on risk and timing. Telmo's contract has already been extended past its original end, and Northbyte lacks a tested exit plan for a critical function. P3 often comes first on value, and because a renewal with a nine-month notice period needs early preparation. All three choices are defensible, as long as you say which criterion drove them."],[105,852.55,878.43,"And fourth: what does Telmo's rollover tell us about ownership? Think about it. A service that the claims systems depend on was rolled over twice without review. That usually means nobody owned it. IT assumed Procurement would act, Procurement assumed IT was happy, and the contract simply continued. The lesson for your own pipeline is to look for the contracts nobody owns. They are often where the biggest risks hide."],[105,878.93,902.08,"There is a broader lesson in this debrief. The three quotes habit is not wrong because RFQs are bad. An RFQ is the right tool for a well-specified purchase in a competitive market. It is wrong because it was applied without a decision. The case shows that the same team, with the same tool set, can choose very different routes once it has positioned each project."],[106,903.03,928.85,"Time for a quick reflection. The poll asks which Nordvia project the team should start first: P1 office cleaning, P2 IT consulting, P3 Kelvar licences, P4 Telmo network, or P8 Northbyte claims IT. Choose your answer, and then say to yourself which criterion drove it: value, risk or timing. If you can name the criterion and the evidence, your answer is defensible."],[107,929.8,949.21,"Now let's move from the case to your own work. Between Session 1 and Session 2 you do Fieldwork 1. Five steps. First, meet: hold a 45-minute meeting with the business owner of your Sourcing Case to validate the need. This is the most important item, and we will look at it in detail in a moment."],[107,949.71,978.86,"Second, collect: 24 months of spend by supplier, the current contracts with notice periods, indexation and auto-renewal clauses, and any Group framework for the category. Remember the Kelvar auto-renewal: a notice period you do not know is a decision you will miss. Third, confirm the regulatory triage with Risk, Compliance or Information Security, wherever you ticked a yes or a possible yes. Do not wait until the contract stage to ask."],[107,979.36,1001.93,"Fourth, update your fact base, your TCO baseline and your positioning in Practices 1.3 to 1.5 with what you learned. Expect some positions to move. That is a sign the fieldwork worked. And fifth, share your one-page category profile with your trio, and collect one challenge. The full meeting guide is in the Fieldwork tab of your workbook."],[107,1002.43,1020.75,"A practical tip on timing. Book the business owner meeting this week, even if the date is two weeks away. Diaries fill quickly, and the meeting shapes everything else. Do the data collection before the meeting if you can, so that you can show the business owner what you already know and ask about what you do not."],[108,1021.7,1045.05,"Here are the key questions for the need-validation meeting. The full guide has eleven, and these six carry the most weight. First: what outcome do you need over the next two to three years? You are asking about results, not about suppliers or products. Second: what is not working today? Show me an example. The words show me matter. An example is evidence; a general complaint is not."],[108,1045.55,1064.72,"Third: what will change in volume, sites, users or scope? A new office, a merger of teams or a digital project can change the need completely. Fourth: which requirements are truly fixed, and why? This is where you discover specification freedom, which, as we saw, is a source of demand power."],[108,1065.22,1104.5,"Fifth: have you already spoken to suppliers? What was promised? This question often reveals a pre-commitment. Imagine the Head of Marketing says: yes, we told our favourite agency they would get the next campaign. Better to know that now than after the tender is out. And sixth: does the service touch a critical function or customer data? That checks your regulatory triage directly with the person who knows how the service is used. The other questions in the guide cover who else depends on the service, who must approve, whether staff would move with the contract, the budget expectation, and the date the new arrangement must be live."],[108,1105.0,1131.23,"A word on how to use these questions. Do not read them out like a survey. Pick the ones that matter most for your project, and let the conversation flow. Send the business owner two or three of them before the meeting, so they can prepare. And end with the question about timing: when do you need the new arrangement live, and what happens if it is late? That answer tells you how much time you have for the route to market we design in Session 2."],[109,1132.18,1163.24,"How should you behave in the meeting? On the left, what to do. Bring your fact base: the spend, the contracts, the triage. It shows that you have done your homework and it anchors the conversation in facts. Ask for the interest behind each position. When the business owner says we need supplier X, ask what X gives them that matters. Perhaps it is speed, perhaps it is one person they trust, perhaps it is a feature. Note their words, not your interpretation. And agree the next contact before you leave."],[109,1163.74,1191.54,"On the right, what not to do. Do not propose a route to market yet. That is Session 2. Do not promise a saving. Do not debate the incumbent: if the business owner loves or hates the current supplier, listen and note it. And do not leave without the data you need. The distinction between interests and positions comes from Fisher and Ury's Getting to Yes. It starts here, with your internal client, and we use it fully in the negotiation session."],[109,1192.04,1230.83,"Here is what that looks like in practice. Imagine you meet Nordvia's Head of Real Estate about the cleaning contracts. She says: just get me a new cleaner, the current one is terrible. That is a position. You ask: what does terrible look like? Show me an example. She describes washrooms that are not checked during the day and complaints from the executive floor. Now you have interests: daytime presence in key areas and a fast response to complaints. Those interests can go straight into an output specification and KPIs. You did not propose a route, you did not promise a saving, and you left with exactly what you need."],[110,1231.78,1258.49,"One last reflection for this session. How confident are you that your Sourcing Case project is the right one? Very, fairly, or not yet, because you need to check with the business owner. Any answer is fine. If it is not yet, the fieldwork meeting is exactly the place to find out. A Sourcing Case that changes scope after a good conversation with the business is not a failure. It is the need validation doing its job."],[110,1258.99,1271.28,"And if you discover that a different project would make a better Sourcing Case, talk to your trio and to the facilitator before Session 2, so that you arrive with a project you can carry through all four sessions."],[111,1272.23,1288.72,"Let's recap the whole of Session 1 in five lines. One: Source to Contract is a decision chain. Insight first, then strategy, then the tool. We started with that sentence, and the case showed why it matters: the habit of three quotes jumps straight to the tool."],[111,1289.22,1326.79,"Two: validate the need and triage regulation at intake. Is the demand justified? Does the service touch an outsourced critical function under Solvency II, an ICT service under DORA, personal data, or a staff transfer? Asking those questions at the start shapes the whole process; asking them at contract stage leaves few options. Three: know the category. Spend, contracts, demand drivers and total cost of ownership, using Ellram's three phases of pre-transaction, transaction and post-transaction costs. The price is only part of the cost."],[111,1327.29,1365.85,"Four: know the market and how suppliers see us. Five forces, PESTLE, cost drivers and capacity tell us about supply power, and Steele and Court's preferencing model tells us how attractive our account is. And five: power decides strategy. Kraljic gives our view, preferencing gives the supplier's view, and the Chessboard turns the balance of power into levers. Demand power can be built, and the direction is named before any tool is chosen. Before you close the session, try this: say the direction of your own project in one sentence, out loud. If you can, Session 1 has done its job."],[111,1366.35,1394.04,"Look at how those five lines connect. Each one feeds the next. Without a validated need, the fact base describes the wrong scope. Without a fact base, we cannot judge our demand power. Without market analysis and preferencing, we cannot judge the supplier's power. And without both, the direction is a guess. That is what decision chain means. If one link is weak, the decision at the end is weak too, no matter how good the RFx template is."],[112,1394.99,1418.35,"Next session asks the question: which route to market? Session 2 turns your direction into a sourcing strategy. How many suppliers? A single supplier by tender, a panel of preferred suppliers, or a single-source negotiation? What contract architecture, what term, and what competitive mechanism? You will write a sourcing strategy paper for your own project."],[112,1418.85,1436.2,"So bring three things: your validated need, your fact base and your positioning. And above all, hold the meeting with your business owner. Of all the fieldwork items, it is the one that makes the biggest difference to Session 2. Thank you for your work in this session, and see you in Session 2."],[112,1436.7,1459.25,"In Session 2 we will also return to several Nordvia projects from this case. The IT consulting spend will become a panel design. The cleaning contracts will become a tender strategy. And Kelvar will become the start of a negotiation plan. So the positioning you did today is not a one-off exercise. It is the foundation for the rest of the course."]],"questions":[{"after":101,"q":"Nordvia's network service from Telmo has been rolled over twice and is used by claims systems. Which regulatory flag should the triage raise first?","options":["None, because telecom is a leverage category","Staff transfer and labour law","DORA ICT, with Risk confirming whether it supports a critical function","Personal data only"],"answer":2,"explain":"A network used by claims systems is an ICT service under DORA and may support a critical function. The triage must flag it so that Risk confirms the classification and the requirements follow.","t":317.12},{"after":103,"q":"Your team must choose the first two projects to start. Which reasoning is strongest?","options":["Choose based on value, risk and timing, and state which criterion drove each choice","Start with the two smallest projects to get quick wins","Start alphabetically to be fair to all owners","Start with the projects that fit a three-quote RFQ"],"answer":0,"explain":"Value, risk and timing are the criteria for sequencing. Any choice is defensible if the criterion and the evidence are explicit.","t":546.32},{"after":104,"q":"Why is partnership with a tested exit a coherent direction for Northbyte rather than a contradiction?","options":["Because the partnership will end soon anyway","Because exit plans are only paperwork","Because partnership means we can skip competition forever","Because we want a close relationship with a provider we depend on, while keeping a credible plan to leave that meets DORA and protects us in negotiation"],"answer":3,"explain":"Strategic dependency calls for partnership, and regulation plus prudence call for a tested exit. The two work together: the exit plan keeps the partnership honest.","t":779.06},{"after":105,"q":"Telmo's contract was rolled over twice without review although claims systems use it. What is the most likely root cause, and the lesson?","options":["The supplier hid the contract; switch suppliers","Nobody owned the contract; look for unowned contracts in your own pipeline","Rollovers are always cheaper; keep rolling over","The service is non-critical; no action needed"],"answer":1,"explain":"Silent rollovers usually reveal missing ownership between the business and Procurement. Unowned contracts often hide the biggest risks.","t":902.08},{"after":107,"q":"During fieldwork, you discover the Kelvar contract has a nine-month notice period before auto-renewal. Why does this matter for planning?","options":["It does not matter until the expiry date","It means the contract cannot be renegotiated","It sets the real deadline: usage analysis, benchmarks and a BATNA must be ready well before notice must be given","It allows Nordvia to run an auction"],"answer":2,"explain":"With an auto-renewal clause, the notice date, not the expiry date, is the decision point. Preparation for a single-source negotiation must finish before it.","t":1020.75},{"after":109,"q":"The business owner insists, we need the current security firm. What is the best response in the meeting?","options":["Ask what that firm gives them that matters, and note the interest behind the position","Explain that a tender will choose a cheaper firm","Debate the incumbent's weaknesses with data","Promise that the incumbent will be kept"],"answer":0,"explain":"Following Fisher and Ury, you ask for the interest behind the position. The interest, such as continuity of trusted staff, can then be built into the strategy.","t":1230.83},{"after":111,"q":"Which statement best summarises the decision chain of Session 1?","options":["Choose the tool, then gather data to justify it","Run three quotes for everything, then analyse the results","Position suppliers first, then validate the need","Validate the need, build insight on the category and market, position the power balance, name the direction, and only then choose the tool"],"answer":3,"explain":"Source to Contract is a decision chain: insight, then strategy, then tool. The direction comes from the power position, not from the template.","t":1394.04}],"cues":[[0.6,3.28,"Welcome to the last lesson of Session 1."],[3.28,12.91,"This lesson covers Case 1, Nordvia's sourcing pipeline, then the fieldwork you will do before Session 2, and finally a recap of the whole session."],[13.19,23.84,"The case brings together everything we have learned: the intake and regulatory triage, the fact base, the market analysis, supplier preferencing, Kraljic and the Chessboard."],[23.84,25.96,"The subtitle sets the scene."],[25.96,29.79,"Eight projects, one team of nine, and a habit of three quotes."],[30.29,33.3,"As always, Nordvia Insurance is fictional."],[33.3,43.17,"It is a mid-sized insurer of about 4,200 employees, part of a European insurance group, with 186 million euros of indirect spend a year."],[43.17,51.15,"The situations in the case are realistic, and you will probably recognise several of them from your own entity."],[51.15,52.43,"That is the point."],[52.71,55.51,"The case is a rehearsal for your own pipeline."],[56.01,57.84,"Here is how the lesson runs."],[57.84,60.73,"First we read the situation and the eight projects."],[60.73,64.95,"Then you position them yourself, in the Case tab of your workbook."],[64.95,67.89,"After that I show one possible answer and we debrief it."],[67.89,73.95,"Then we prepare the fieldwork, with the key questions for your meeting with the business owner."],[74.23,79.88,"And we close with a recap of the five big ideas of Session 1 and a look ahead to Session 2."],[80.83,82.69,"Let's start with the situation."],[82.69,87.48,"Nordvia's Procurement team runs about 60 sourcing events a year in Coupa."],[87.48,91.86,"Most of them were RFQs with three quotes, whatever the category."],[91.86,100.29,"Cleaning, software, consulting, creative agencies: the same template, the same three quotes, the same lowest price wins."],[100.79,102.29,"Why is that a problem?"],[102.29,105.2,"Think back to everything we saw in this session."],[105.2,112.16,"Three quotes work well when the market is competitive, the specification is clear, and the supplier wants our business."],[112.44,121.8,"They work badly when the incumbent holds the power, when the need has not been validated, or when the real issue is fragmented demand."],[121.8,127.62,"A three-quote RFQ on a software renewal tells the vendor that we have no strategy."],[127.62,133.48,"A three-quote RFQ on 1,900 small print orders costs more in process than it saves."],[133.97,141.55,"So the new Head of Procurement sets a new rule: every project in the pipeline is positioned before anyone chooses a tool."],[141.55,147.94,"There are eight projects in the pipeline, worth 55 million euros of annual spend in total."],[147.94,151.59,"That figure is simply the sum of the eight projects in the case."],[151.87,154.82,"Your task, in a moment, is to position all eight."],[155.32,157.96,"Before we look at the projects, picture the team."],[157.96,160.72,"Nine buyers, 60 events a year."],[160.72,165.42,"That is fewer than seven events per buyer, and each event takes weeks of work."],[165.42,173.86,"If every event gets the same template, the team spends the same effort on a box of toner as on a 19 million euro managed service."],[174.14,177.26,"Positioning is not extra work on top of sourcing."],[177.26,181.58,"It is how a small team decides where to spend its limited time."],[181.58,186.47,"A project positioned as manage spend may need a catalogue and an afternoon."],[186.47,192.49,"A project in the danger zone may need three months of preparation and the CIO in the room."],[193.43,195.58,"Here is the first half of the pipeline."],[195.58,197.27,"Take a moment to read it."],[197.27,203.16,"I will walk you through each row, and as I do, start forming a view on where each project sits."],[203.66,207.64,"P1, office cleaning across three sites in two countries."],[207.64,210.03,"2.4 million euros."],[210.03,216.1,"Three local cleaners, with contracts rolled over since 2019, and quality complaints at headquarters."],[216.1,218.25,"What does that tell you?"],[218.53,228.04,"A competitive market, as we saw in module 1.4, an attractive account for local firms, but no management of the contracts."],[228.04,229.73,"P2, IT consulting."],[229.73,235.36,"6.1 million euros spread across 12 firms and 38 call-offs, with no framework."],[235.36,241.93,"Remember the day rates for the same senior profile: from 820 to 1,450 euros."],[242.21,246.4,"That spread is a sign of fragmented demand and missing competition."],[246.9,249.17,"P3, the Kelvar licences."],[249.17,256.69,"11.2 million euros, a 9 percent uplift at the last renewal, no usage data and an auto-renewal clause."],[256.69,260.78,"We positioned it in module 1.5: the danger zone."],[260.78,262.62,"P4, the Telmo network."],[262.62,269.26,"4.9 million euros, rolled over twice, and, here is the key phrase, used by claims systems."],[269.54,277.0,"A network service that claims systems depend on is an ICT service under DORA, and it may support a critical function."],[277.0,279.66,"Yet nobody has reviewed it for years."],[279.66,281.04,"Keep that in mind."],[281.53,285.28,"Look also at who owns these four projects."],[285.28,286.63,"Facilities owns P1."],[286.63,287.98,"IT owns P2, P3 and P4."],[287.98,290.28,"That concentration matters."],[290.28,299.36,"IT is the largest area of Nordvia's spend, and the CIO will be your most important stakeholder for this pipeline."],[299.64,309.36,"If the CIO sees Procurement only as the team that sends RFQs, she will not involve you early in the Kelvar renewal or in the Telmo review."],[309.36,317.12,"If she sees Procurement as the team that brings usage data, benchmarks and a DORA view, the conversation is very different."],[318.07,320.78,"The second half of the pipeline."],[320.78,323.28,"P5, office supplies and print."],[323.28,329.83,"2.3 million euros with 31 suppliers and 1,900 purchase orders a year below 1,000 euros."],[329.83,333.06,"We met it in module 1.5: the tail."],[333.06,335.79,"P6, security and reception."],[335.79,342.65,"1.6 million euros, one provider, hourly rates indexed twice in two years, and no KPIs."],[342.93,349.24,"A labour-intensive service with a single provider, two indexations and no measurement of performance."],[349.24,350.84,"What does that suggest?"],[350.84,354.47,"An account that pays more every year without knowing what it gets."],[354.97,357.4,"P7, creative agencies."],[357.4,364.09,"7.2 million euros with six agencies, no rate card, and every campaign briefed to a favourite."],[364.09,370.91,"Overlapping briefs mean Nordvia may be paying two agencies to think about the same problem."],[370.91,373.82,"And P8, the Northbyte claims IT managed service."],[373.82,377.82,"19.4 million euros, the largest project."],[378.1,385.23,"It supports a critical function, it is in the DORA register of information, and it has no tested exit plan."],[385.23,395.95,"Under DORA, exit strategies for ICT services that support critical or important functions must be documented and tested, so that gap is not optional."],[396.23,403.31,"And because it is an outsourced critical function, the Solvency II outsourcing rules must also be checked."],[403.31,408.82,"The full table, with owners and expiry dates, is in the Case tab of your workbook."],[409.32,412.61,"Now step back and look at both halves together."],[412.61,419.52,"Three projects are with a single provider: P3 Kelvar, P4 Telmo and P8 Northbyte, plus P6 for security."],[419.52,424.15,"Those single-provider situations carry very different risks."],[424.15,428.09,"Security has several capable alternatives in the market."],[428.37,433.98,"Northbyte supports a critical function and would take a long transition to replace."],[433.98,439.74,"Two projects, P2 and P7, have the opposite problem: too many suppliers and no structure."],[439.74,443.86,"And one, P5, is a long tail of small orders."],[443.86,448.6,"Already, before any matrix, you can see that one template cannot fit all eight."],[449.54,451.05,"Now the exercise."],[451.05,455.11,"It takes about 30 minutes, and it lives in the Case tab of your workbook."],[455.11,458.64,"For each of the eight projects, you decide five things."],[458.64,462.49,"First, the Kraljic quadrant and the Chessboard strategy."],[462.49,467.57,"Second, how the main supplier probably sees Nordvia, using Steele and Court."],[467.85,480.4,"Third, the regulatory flag from the triage: none, personal data, staff transfer or labour law, DORA ICT, DORA ICT plus critical function, or Solvency II outsourcing."],[480.4,481.2,"Fourth, the direction, in a few words."],[481.2,487.88,"And then the team question: which two projects should Nordvia start first, and why?"],[488.38,493.64,"When you choose the first two, use three criteria: value, risk and timing."],[493.64,496.72,"Value is how much money or quality is at stake."],[496.72,501.43,"Risk is what happens if nothing is done, including regulatory risk."],[501.43,508.09,"Timing is how close the contract end or the notice period is, and how long the right process will take."],[508.37,514.92,"A software negotiation, for example, needs months of preparation before the notice deadline, not weeks."],[515.42,525.66,"If you are short of time, go deep on two projects and fill the other six more quickly, but do fill all eight, because the comparison between them is where the learning happens."],[525.66,529.71,"If you are in a live group, you will present in three minutes per team."],[529.99,534.47,"If you are working alone, imagine you are presenting to the Head of Procurement."],[534.47,542.31,"Pause the video now if you want to complete the exercise in the Case tab of your workbook, and continue when you are ready."],[542.31,546.31,"The next slide shows one possible answer, so try it before you look."],[547.27,549.26,"Here is one possible answer."],[549.26,550.12,"Note the word possible."],[550.12,556.17,"Positions are judgements, and other answers are acceptable where the evidence supports them."],[556.17,557.47,"Let's walk through it."],[557.96,560.69,"P1 cleaning and P6 security: leverage."],[560.69,568.09,"The market is competitive, the cost drivers are clear, and for local firms Nordvia is an attractive account."],[568.09,572.24,"The direction: compete, with one supplier chosen by tender."],[572.24,580.13,"For P1 the regulatory flag is staff transfer and labour law, because cleaning staff may move with the contract."],[580.41,582.96,"For P6 the main gap is the missing KPIs."],[582.96,588.6,"Both projects gain from an output specification and a managed contract."],[589.1,594.36,"P5 supplies and print: non-critical, close to leverage on volume print."],[594.36,598.2,"The direction: consolidate and channel through a catalogue."],[598.2,602.35,"The tail is channelled, not sourced event by event."],[602.35,607.52,"P2 consulting and P7 creative agencies: leverage moving to strategic."],[607.52,610.9,"Each contains standard work and scarce expertise."],[611.18,617.09,"The direction: structure the spend, through a panel of preferred suppliers with rate cards."],[617.09,623.28,"For creative work there is one more point to remember for later sessions: no auctions for creative work."],[623.78,629.69,"P3 Kelvar: bottleneck or strategic, with high supply power, and likely Exploitable."],[629.69,639.77,"The direction: change the nature of demand, with usage data, re-specification and a credible alternative, leading to a single-source negotiation."],[639.77,645.8,"The regulatory flag is DORA ICT, because core software is an ICT service."],[646.3,652.03,"P4 Telmo: leverage or bottleneck, depending on how specific the network is."],[652.03,653.17,"The direction: review it now."],[653.17,663.17,"The regulatory flag is DORA ICT, and because the claims systems use it, Risk must confirm whether it supports a critical function."],[663.45,670.5,"And P8 Northbyte: strategic, a DORA critical function, with Solvency II outsourcing to check."],[670.5,672.94,"The direction: partnership with a tested exit."],[672.94,676.4,"That may sound like a contradiction, but it is not."],[676.4,684.17,"We want a close relationship with a provider we depend on, and we need a credible, tested plan to leave if we must."],[684.45,690.03,"The exit plan is the gap, and it is also our insurance in the next negotiation."],[690.03,693.03,"Now compare this table with the starting point."],[693.03,698.81,"Of the eight projects, how many would an RFQ with three quotes have served well?"],[698.81,703.03,"Perhaps none of them fully, and certainly not P3, P4, P5 or P8."],[703.53,709.94,"Let me add the Chessboard and preferencing view for a few of these, because the table only shows Kraljic."],[709.94,722.52,"For P1 and P6, the Chessboard strategy is leverage competition among suppliers, and the levers are tendering, supplier pricing review and target pricing, using the wage floor as a check."],[722.8,727.56,"For P5, it is manage spend, with volume bundling and demand management."],[727.56,744.15,"For P2 and P7, the top firms probably see Nordvia as Core, but the smaller ones may see it as Nuisance, and the levers are volume bundling, supplier pricing review and demand management, such as stopping overlapping briefs to two agencies."],[744.65,753.89,"For P3, as we saw, the strategy is change the nature of demand, with technical data mining, re-specification and risk management."],[754.17,767.52,"For P8, the Chessboard strategy is closer to seek joint advantage with the supplier, with levers like value partnership and integrated operations planning, combined with risk management through the exit plan."],[767.8,779.07,"And for P4, the first lever is simply commercial data mining: find out what Telmo actually provides, at what price, and under which terms, before deciding whether to compete it."],[780.01,782.57,"Let's debrief the case with four questions."],[782.57,787.26,"First: which projects would three quotes have served badly?"],[787.26,792.8,"Kelvar, because there is only one supplier and an RFQ shows we have no strategy."],[792.8,796.61,"Northbyte, because the issue is dependency and exit, not price."],[796.61,801.83,"The print tail, because 1,900 small events cost more than they save."],[802.11,806.59,"And the agencies, because creative quality cannot be bought on three quotes."],[807.1,810.28,"Second: where did teams disagree, and on what evidence?"],[810.28,816.4,"Common disagreements are Telmo, leverage or bottleneck, and Kelvar, bottleneck or strategic."],[816.4,821.14,"That is fine, as long as each team can show the evidence."],[821.14,825.13,"Third: which projects did you start first, value, risk or timing?"],[825.41,829.72,"In practice, P4 and P8 usually come first on risk and timing."],[829.72,839.1,"Telmo's contract has already been extended past its original end, and Northbyte lacks a tested exit plan for a critical function."],[839.1,846.87,"P3 often comes first on value, and because a renewal with a nine-month notice period needs early preparation."],[847.15,852.05,"All three choices are defensible, as long as you say which criterion drove them."],[852.55,856.06,"And fourth: what does Telmo's rollover tell us about ownership?"],[856.06,857.13,"Think about it."],[857.13,862.17,"A service that the claims systems depend on was rolled over twice without review."],[862.17,864.58,"That usually means nobody owned it."],[864.58,871.47,"IT assumed Procurement would act, Procurement assumed IT was happy, and the contract simply continued."],[871.75,876.11,"The lesson for your own pipeline is to look for the contracts nobody owns."],[876.11,878.43,"They are often where the biggest risks hide."],[878.93,881.84,"There is a broader lesson in this debrief."],[881.84,885.54,"The three quotes habit is not wrong because RFQs are bad."],[885.54,890.88,"An RFQ is the right tool for a well-specified purchase in a competitive market."],[890.88,894.42,"It is wrong because it was applied without a decision."],[894.7,902.08,"The case shows that the same team, with the same tool set, can choose very different routes once it has positioned each project."],[903.03,904.81,"Time for a quick reflection."],[904.81,917.27,"The poll asks which Nordvia project the team should start first: P1 office cleaning, P2 IT consulting, P3 Kelvar licences, P4 Telmo network, or P8 Northbyte claims IT."],[917.27,924.26,"Choose your answer, and then say to yourself which criterion drove it: value, risk or timing."],[924.54,928.85,"If you can name the criterion and the evidence, your answer is defensible."],[929.8,932.24,"Now let's move from the case to your own work."],[932.24,936.07,"Between Session 1 and Session 2 you do Fieldwork 1."],[936.07,937.01,"Five steps."],[937.01,943.96,"First, meet: hold a 45-minute meeting with the business owner of your Sourcing Case to validate the need."],[943.96,949.21,"This is the most important item, and we will look at it in detail in a moment."],[949.71,960.98,"Second, collect: 24 months of spend by supplier, the current contracts with notice periods, indexation and auto-renewal clauses, and any Group framework for the category."],[960.98,966.9,"Remember the Kelvar auto-renewal: a notice period you do not know is a decision you will miss."],[967.18,975.91,"Third, confirm the regulatory triage with Risk, Compliance or Information Security, wherever you ticked a yes or a possible yes."],[975.91,978.85,"Do not wait until the contract stage to ask."],[979.36,987.84,"Fourth, update your fact base, your TCO baseline and your positioning in Practices 1.3 to 1.5 with what you learned."],[987.84,990.33,"Expect some positions to move."],[990.33,991.98,"That is a sign the fieldwork worked."],[991.98,998.24,"And fifth, share your one-page category profile with your trio, and collect one challenge."],[998.52,1001.93,"The full meeting guide is in the Fieldwork tab of your workbook."],[1002.43,1004.34,"A practical tip on timing."],[1004.34,1008.25,"Book the business owner meeting this week, even if the date is two weeks away."],[1008.25,1012.26,"Diaries fill quickly, and the meeting shapes everything else."],[1012.26,1020.76,"Do the data collection before the meeting if you can, so that you can show the business owner what you already know and ask about what you do not."],[1021.7,1024.74,"Here are the key questions for the need-validation meeting."],[1024.74,1028.63,"The full guide has eleven, and these six carry the most weight."],[1028.63,1032.52,"First: what outcome do you need over the next two to three years?"],[1032.52,1036.82,"You are asking about results, not about suppliers or products."],[1036.82,1038.75,"Second: what is not working today?"],[1038.75,1039.92,"Show me an example."],[1040.2,1041.53,"The words show me matter."],[1041.53,1045.04,"An example is evidence; a general complaint is not."],[1045.55,1049.32,"Third: what will change in volume, sites, users or scope?"],[1049.32,1054.83,"A new office, a merger of teams or a digital project can change the need completely."],[1054.83,1058.09,"Fourth: which requirements are truly fixed, and why?"],[1058.09,1064.73,"This is where you discover specification freedom, which, as we saw, is a source of demand power."],[1065.22,1068.18,"Fifth: have you already spoken to suppliers?"],[1068.18,1069.01,"What was promised?"],[1069.01,1071.92,"This question often reveals a pre-commitment."],[1071.92,1078.47,"Imagine the Head of Marketing says: yes, we told our favourite agency they would get the next campaign."],[1078.47,1081.67,"Better to know that now than after the tender is out."],[1081.95,1086.73,"And sixth: does the service touch a critical function or customer data?"],[1086.73,1092.4,"That checks your regulatory triage directly with the person who knows how the service is used."],[1092.68,1104.5,"The other questions in the guide cover who else depends on the service, who must approve, whether staff would move with the contract, the budget expectation, and the date the new arrangement must be live."],[1105.0,1107.53,"A word on how to use these questions."],[1107.53,1108.91,"Do not read them out like a survey."],[1108.91,1114.79,"Pick the ones that matter most for your project, and let the conversation flow."],[1114.79,1119.31,"Send the business owner two or three of them before the meeting, so they can prepare."],[1119.59,1126.1,"And end with the question about timing: when do you need the new arrangement live, and what happens if it is late?"],[1126.1,1131.22,"That answer tells you how much time you have for the route to market we design in Session 2."],[1132.18,1134.64,"How should you behave in the meeting?"],[1134.64,1135.65,"On the left, what to do."],[1135.65,1139.68,"Bring your fact base: the spend, the contracts, the triage."],[1139.68,1144.27,"It shows that you have done your homework and it anchors the conversation in facts."],[1144.27,1146.94,"Ask for the interest behind each position."],[1147.22,1152.34,"When the business owner says we need supplier X, ask what X gives them that matters."],[1152.34,1157.77,"Perhaps it is speed, perhaps it is one person they trust, perhaps it is a feature."],[1157.77,1160.74,"Note their words, not your interpretation."],[1160.74,1163.24,"And agree the next contact before you leave."],[1163.74,1165.52,"On the right, what not to do."],[1165.52,1167.82,"Do not propose a route to market yet."],[1167.82,1169.36,"That is Session 2."],[1169.36,1171.15,"Do not promise a saving."],[1171.15,1177.67,"Do not debate the incumbent: if the business owner loves or hates the current supplier, listen and note it."],[1177.67,1180.4,"And do not leave without the data you need."],[1180.68,1186.0,"The distinction between interests and positions comes from Fisher and Ury's Getting to Yes."],[1186.0,1191.54,"It starts here, with your internal client, and we use it fully in the negotiation session."],[1192.04,1194.32,"Here is what that looks like in practice."],[1194.32,1198.77,"Imagine you meet Nordvia's Head of Real Estate about the cleaning contracts."],[1198.77,1202.82,"She says: just get me a new cleaner, the current one is terrible."],[1202.82,1204.2,"That is a position."],[1204.2,1206.68,"You ask: what does terrible look like?"],[1206.68,1207.87,"Show me an example."],[1208.15,1215.06,"She describes washrooms that are not checked during the day and complaints from the executive floor."],[1215.06,1219.95,"Now you have interests: daytime presence in key areas and a fast response to complaints."],[1219.95,1224.92,"Those interests can go straight into an output specification and KPIs."],[1225.2,1230.83,"You did not propose a route, you did not promise a saving, and you left with exactly what you need."],[1231.78,1234.09,"One last reflection for this session."],[1234.09,1238.26,"How confident are you that your Sourcing Case project is the right one?"],[1238.26,1243.09,"Very, fairly, or not yet, because you need to check with the business owner."],[1243.09,1244.96,"Any answer is fine."],[1244.96,1249.42,"If it is not yet, the fieldwork meeting is exactly the place to find out."],[1249.7,1255.78,"A Sourcing Case that changes scope after a good conversation with the business is not a failure."],[1255.78,1258.49,"It is the need validation doing its job."],[1258.99,1271.28,"And if you discover that a different project would make a better Sourcing Case, talk to your trio and to the facilitator before Session 2, so that you arrive with a project you can carry through all four sessions."],[1272.23,1275.38,"Let's recap the whole of Session 1 in five lines."],[1275.38,1278.76,"One: Source to Contract is a decision chain."],[1278.76,1281.0,"Insight first, then strategy, then the tool."],[1281.0,1288.72,"We started with that sentence, and the case showed why it matters: the habit of three quotes jumps straight to the tool."],[1289.22,1293.17,"Two: validate the need and triage regulation at intake."],[1293.17,1295.36,"Is the demand justified?"],[1295.36,1304.99,"Does the service touch an outsourced critical function under Solvency II, an ICT service under DORA, personal data, or a staff transfer?"],[1305.27,1312.73,"Asking those questions at the start shapes the whole process; asking them at contract stage leaves few options."],[1312.73,1314.61,"Three: know the category."],[1314.61,1324.42,"Spend, contracts, demand drivers and total cost of ownership, using Ellram's three phases of pre-transaction, transaction and post-transaction costs."],[1324.7,1326.79,"The price is only part of the cost."],[1327.29,1330.36,"Four: know the market and how suppliers see us."],[1330.36,1341.81,"Five forces, PESTLE, cost drivers and capacity tell us about supply power, and Steele and Court's preferencing model tells us how attractive our account is."],[1341.81,1343.55,"And five: power decides strategy."],[1343.83,1351.8,"Kraljic gives our view, preferencing gives the supplier's view, and the Chessboard turns the balance of power into levers."],[1351.8,1356.39,"Demand power can be built, and the direction is named before any tool is chosen."],[1356.39,1363.0,"Before you close the session, try this: say the direction of your own project in one sentence, out loud."],[1363.28,1365.84,"If you can, Session 1 has done its job."],[1366.35,1368.68,"Look at how those five lines connect."],[1368.68,1370.06,"Each one feeds the next."],[1370.06,1374.3,"Without a validated need, the fact base describes the wrong scope."],[1374.3,1378.0,"Without a fact base, we cannot judge our demand power."],[1378.0,1382.78,"Without market analysis and preferencing, we cannot judge the supplier's power."],[1382.78,1385.38,"And without both, the direction is a guess."],[1385.66,1387.87,"That is what decision chain means."],[1387.87,1394.04,"If one link is weak, the decision at the end is weak too, no matter how good the RFx template is."],[1394.99,1398.55,"Next session asks the question: which route to market?"],[1398.55,1402.32,"Session 2 turns your direction into a sourcing strategy."],[1402.32,1403.82,"How many suppliers?"],[1403.82,1410.36,"A single supplier by tender, a panel of preferred suppliers, or a single-source negotiation?"],[1410.36,1414.77,"What contract architecture, what term, and what competitive mechanism?"],[1415.05,1418.35,"You will write a sourcing strategy paper for your own project."],[1418.85,1423.93,"So bring three things: your validated need, your fact base and your positioning."],[1423.93,1427.08,"And above all, hold the meeting with your business owner."],[1427.08,1432.41,"Of all the fieldwork items, it is the one that makes the biggest difference to Session 2."],[1432.41,1436.19,"Thank you for your work in this session, and see you in Session 2."],[1436.7,1441.59,"In Session 2 we will also return to several Nordvia projects from this case."],[1441.59,1445.34,"The IT consulting spend will become a panel design."],[1445.34,1448.72,"The cleaning contracts will become a tender strategy."],[1448.72,1452.44,"And Kelvar will become the start of a negotiation plan."],[1452.44,1456.35,"So the positioning you did today is not a one-off exercise."],[1456.63,1459.25,"It is the foundation for the rest of the course."]]}],"s2":[{"id":"s2-l1","session":"s2","title":"The six decisions of a sourcing strategy","summary":"Answer the six decisions of a sourcing strategy in the right order and use the strategy-selection matrix and five route-to-market questions to choose how a category goes to market.","from":0,"to":26,"audio":"audio/s2-l1.mp3","duration":1588.6,"segs":[[0,0.6,53.36,"Welcome to Session 2 of the Source to Contract course. I am glad you are back. Today's session is called WHICH route to market? and its subtitle says exactly what we will do: choose the sourcing strategy. This first lesson covers the opening of the session and module 2.1, the six decisions of a sourcing strategy. By the end of it you will be able to take any indirect category, look at its power position and at how demand arrives, and answer six questions in order: how many suppliers, what kind of relationship, what contract, what price model, what form of competition, and how long. You will also learn a simple matrix and five questions that point you to the right route to market. The next two lessons then take the two most common patterns one by one: one supplier chosen by tender, and a pool of preferred suppliers."],[1,54.31,66.24,"Let us start with the sentence on the screen. The tool follows the strategy. The strategy follows the power position. Never the reverse. Read it twice, because the whole session rests on it."],[1,66.74,105.33,"In Session 1 we did the analysis. We confirmed the need, we built a category profile, and we placed each category on the Kraljic matrix and on the Kearney Purchasing Chessboard. We know what we buy and who holds the power. Today that analysis becomes a decision. And notice the order. Many teams start with the tool. Someone says, let us run an RFP, or let us do an auction, and only afterwards asks what the market looks like. That is the reverse of the chain. Today we choose how each category goes to market, and, just as important, we write down why. Session 3 will then design the tool itself."],[2,106.28,137.28,"Here is where we are in the course. There are four questions, one per session. First, WHAT are we sourcing? That is done: the need, the category profile and the power position. Second, WHICH route to market? That is today, and its output is the sourcing strategy paper. Third, HOW do we run the event? That is Session 3: RFx documents, evaluation models and e-auctions in Coupa. And fourth, HOW do we close it well? Session 4: negotiation, the contract and the handover."],[2,137.78,162.15,"Each session adds one part to your Sourcing Case. Today's part is a special one. The strategy paper is the document your business owner must agree before any event is issued. Once a request for proposal is out in the market, many choices are locked in: the lots, the term, the weighting, even the number of suppliers you can award. So this is the last calm moment to get them right."],[3,163.1,191.52,"So what will you be able to do by the end of today? Six things. Answer the six decisions of a sourcing strategy. Choose a route with the strategy-selection matrix. Design a single-supplier tender for a site service like cleaning or security. Design a panel of preferred suppliers for professional and IT services. Prepare a sole-source negotiation with an incumbent vendor. And write a one-page strategy paper for your own project."],[3,192.02,213.97,"Here is a small exercise to keep in mind. Think about your own Sourcing Case. Which of the three big patterns do you think it belongs to? One supplier by tender, a panel of preferred suppliers, or a sole-source negotiation? Make a quick guess now. We will test that guess at the end of the session, and it is often interesting to see how the guess changes."],[4,214.92,253.39,"This is the plan of the full classroom day, so you can see where this lesson sits. We open with the debrief of Fieldwork 1. Then module 2.1, the six decisions, which is this lesson. Then 2.2, one supplier by tender, and 2.3, a pool of preferred suppliers. After that 2.4 on sole and single source, 2.5 on partnerships, the tail and the strategy paper, and finally Case 2. Module 2.3 is the longest block of the day, for a good reason: it carries the most common indirect categories, consulting, IT services and agencies."],[5,254.34,279.66,"In the classroom we would now run a short poll. The question is: did you validate the need with your business owner in Fieldwork 1? The options are: yes, need and scope confirmed; yes, but the scope changed; meeting booked, not yet held; or not yet. Take a moment and give your own honest answer. If the scope changed after that conversation, that is not a failure. It is the fieldwork doing its job."],[6,280.61,314.96,"Let us debrief Fieldwork 1 with four cards. First, need. Did the business owner confirm the need and the scope? Second, data. Which contract or spend fact surprised you? Very often it is the number of suppliers, or a contract that nobody knew had been rolled over for years. Third, triage. Did anything trigger outsourcing rules, DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act, personal data, or staff transfer? Those flags change the route to market, because they bring Risk and Compliance into the process and they add time."],[6,315.46,346.72,"And fourth, timing. When is the notice date, not only the expiry? This question is deliberate. Imagine a facilities contract that expires in June, with a six month notice period. If you only look at the expiry date, you think you have until spring. In reality, if you do nothing before December, the contract may roll over for another year. Every timeline we build today starts from the notice date and plans backwards. If you want, pause here and note your own notice date in your workbook."],[7,347.67,366.29,"Now to our case. Nordvia Insurance, our fictional insurer, runs about 60 sourcing events a year. Until now most of them were RFQs with three quotes, whatever the category. The same tool for cleaning, for consulting and for software licences. That is a habit, not a strategy."],[7,366.79,402.3,"Let us test the habit. What does three quotes achieve for the Kelvar renewal, Nordvia's core insurance software? Very little. There is only one vendor who can renew those licences, so the two other quotes are theatre. And for a consulting call-off? Three quotes for a two week assignment take weeks to collect, so in practice people skip them and call a favourite. The habit gives the appearance of competition without the substance. Probably this feels familiar from your own entity. The good news is that the fix is not more process. It is choosing the right process per category."],[8,403.25,432.82,"Another poll question for you to think about. How does your team usually choose the sourcing route today? Three quotes, whatever the category; the business proposes and we follow; we decide per category, but rarely write it down; or a documented strategy paper for every major project. There is no judgement here. Most teams pick the third answer. They do decide per category, but the reasoning stays in someone's head. That is exactly what the strategy paper fixes."],[9,433.77,450.77,"This brings us to module 2.1: the six decisions of a sourcing strategy. How many suppliers, what relationship, what contract, what price model, what competition, and how long? Six questions. Let us see where they sit in the chain and why their order matters."],[10,451.72,469.32,"This flow shows the chain from analysis to tool. On the left, the category profile. Then the power position. Then the six decisions. Then the route to market. And only at the end, the tool and the event. Sessions 1, 2 and 3 cover these boxes in that order."],[10,469.82,507.82,"Now look at the last box. The RFP, the RFQ or the auction is the output of the chain, not its start. Skipping a box is how three quotes became the default. If you go straight from the request to the tool, you skip the power position and the six decisions, and then the only tool you reach for is the one you used last time. Think of a marketing request for a new events agency. If you jump to an RFQ, you will compare day rates. If you first ask how demand arrives, you may find it is a stream of events across the year, which points to a small roster with a rate card, not a one-off quote."],[11,508.77,525.96,"This is not a local idea. The CIPS Procurement and Supply Cycle, in its stage 3, the Strategy stage, says you choose between competitive tendering and other approaches based on supply position. In other words, the market and the power balance decide the approach."],[11,526.46,551.7,"Paul O'Brien's 5i model says the same thing in its Innovation stage, where the category strategy is formed from the insight work before any implementation. Two different reference models, one message: the choice of approach rests on the market and power analysis. When your business owner asks why you are not simply running three quotes, you can say that this is how the profession's own standard models work."],[12,552.65,556.35,"Here are the six decisions, in order. Let me walk you through the table."],[12,556.85,600.54,"Decision one, the number of suppliers: single, dual, multi in the form of a panel, or sole. Decision two, the relationship type: transactional, preferred supplier, or strategic partner. Decision three, the contract architecture: a one-off contract, a term contract, a framework, a catalogue, or a rate card. Decision four, the commercial model: fixed price, unit rates, time and materials with a rate card, capped time and materials, an output price, or gain-share. Decision five, the competitive mechanism: RFQ, RFP, a multi-stage process, a mini-competition, an auction, or a negotiation. And decision six, the term: a base term plus options."],[12,601.04,644.5,"Why does the order matter? Because each decision constrains the next. The number of suppliers depends on how demand arrives and on supply risk. The relationship follows from the Kraljic position and the value at stake. The contract architecture follows the predictability of demand. The commercial model follows the clarity of scope. Only then can you choose a mechanism that fits all of that. Notice that the mechanism is decision five. If you choose it first, the other four are made by accident. Choose an e-auction first, and you have silently decided on a precise specification, a price-only award and a transactional relationship, perhaps for a service where none of that is true."],[13,645.45,656.72,"Decision one: how many suppliers? This follows Monczka and colleagues and Lysons and Farrington. Four options, each with a strength, a weakness and a typical fit for an insurer."],[13,657.22,686.81,"Single sourcing, chosen by competition, gives you leverage and one accountable party. The weakness is dependency during the term. It fits cleaning, security and a travel management company. Dual sourcing gives continuity and benchmark tension, because each supplier knows the other is there. The price is split volume and double management cost. It fits telecom, or regional temporary staffing where one agency cannot cover all locations."],[13,687.31,724.79,"Multi-sourcing, usually as a panel, brings specialists and competition for each job. The weakness is fragmentation if the panel grows too large. It fits consulting, IT services and agencies. And finally sole source. Look at the strength column: none. Sole source is not a choice we make. It is a market fact we have to manage. Proprietary core software is the typical example. Be careful with the words. Single source means we chose one supplier out of several. Sole source means there is only one. The difference matters for the whole negotiation strategy."],[14,725.74,750.34,"Why should we care about the number of suppliers? These figures give a direction. The Hackett Group reports that its Digital World Class procurement organisations, in its 2024 benchmark, work with 3.7 times fewer suppliers per billion dollars of spend, and have 23 percent shorter sourcing cycle times. Please treat these as consultancy benchmarks, not peer-reviewed results."],[14,750.84,782.1,"Now our own Group. Only about 2 percent of the Group's spend is under a Group contract today, and there are roughly 350 IT services suppliers for about 80 million euros of spend. These figures are anonymised and rounded. The point is direction, not a target number. Concentration is associated with better performance, and our Group is fragmented. That does not mean every category should go to one supplier. It means fragmentation should be a decision, not an accident."],[15,783.05,817.04,"Decisions two and three: the relationship and the contract. On the left, the relationship type. Transactional means we care about price and compliance, and little more. Preferred supplier means the supplier gets priority and agreed terms in exchange for performance. Strategic partner means joint plans and often open book costing. Most indirect categories sit in the first two. Strategic partnership is rare, expensive to run, and reserved for the few categories where both sides gain from working closely together."],[15,817.54,857.39,"On the right, the contract architecture. A one-off contract for a defined project. A term contract for a continuous service. A framework with call-offs for a stream of needs. And a catalogue or a rate card for repeat buys. The rule is simple: contract architecture follows the predictability of demand. A continuous service, like cleaning a building every night, needs a term contract. Unpredictable assignments, like legal advice or IT projects, need a framework. If you put a stream of assignments into a single term contract, you either lock in volume you cannot guarantee, or you end up negotiating every change."],[16,858.34,864.14,"Decision four: the commercial model. This is about who carries the risk. Let me go down the table."],[16,864.64,893.23,"Fixed price, when scope and deliverables are clear. Unit rates, when volume varies but the unit is clear: square metres, hours, devices. Time and materials with a rate card, when the scope is unclear, for short discovery work. Capped time and materials, when the scope is partly clear and you need to protect the budget. An output price, for a managed service with service levels. And gain-share, when the supplier controls the levers and a baseline is agreed."],[16,893.73,934.18,"The UK Consultancy Playbook, a public sector guide, gives the same logic for consulting. The principle behind it is this: whoever controls the outcome should carry the risk. Take an example. A consulting firm is asked to deliver a defined process redesign for claims handling, with clear deliverables. If you pay time and materials, every delay is your cost, although the firm controls the work. Fixed price per deliverable puts the risk where the control is. But if you ask a firm to explore a problem nobody understands yet, fixed price only buys a large risk premium. Then short, time-boxed time and materials is honest."],[17,935.13,941.87,"Decision five: two ways to compete. This distinction is the heart of the session, so let us take it slowly."],[17,942.37,963.13,"On the left, competition for the market. You run one tender per cycle for the whole contract. Then one supplier delivers and is measured. This fits continuous site services. The competitive tension is concentrated in one moment every few years, and during the term the tension comes from the KPIs and the options to extend."],[17,963.63,980.62,"On the right, competition in the market. Several pre-qualified suppliers stay in the game, and they compete again for every assignment. This fits streams of specialist work. The tension is spread across the whole term: every new job is a new chance to win or lose."],[17,981.12,1003.5,"So, cleaning is competition for the market. Consulting is competition in the market. And Kelvar, the core software vendor? Neither. There is no realistic alternative in the contract horizon, so we negotiate. Three answers: compete for the market, compete in the market, or negotiate. Keep them in mind; they reappear in every module today."],[18,1004.45,1030.18,"Decision six: the term. Four cards. On the supplier side, the term must be long enough to recover mobilisation and investment. A cleaning contractor buys equipment, recruits and trains staff, and sets up systems. If the term is too short, it prices that into the bid. On our side, the term must be long enough to recover our own switching cost: the transition effort, the service dip, the internal time."],[18,1030.68,1063.21,"Then options. Extensions should be earned by performance, not automatic. An option that is exercised by default is not leverage, it is just a longer contract. And exit. For ICT services, the term should be aligned to a tested exit plan, which DORA expects for important services. Lisa Ellram's total cost of ownership logic is useful here: mobilisation and transition are real post-transaction costs. So term length is a cost decision, not an administrative one."],[19,1064.16,1078.18,"Let us bring back a tool from Session 1, the Kearney Purchasing Chessboard, developed by Schuh and colleagues. The vertical axis is supply power, the horizontal axis is demand power. Four basic strategies."],[19,1078.68,1106.42,"When supply power is high and our demand power is low, we change the nature of demand: re-specify, mine technical data, open the door to alternatives. When both are high, we seek joint advantage: a value and cost partnership. When both are low, we manage spend: demand management, bundling, catalogues. And when our demand power is high, we leverage competition among suppliers: tenders, target pricing, auctions."],[19,1106.92,1131.24,"Each basic strategy points to a family of routes. Leverage competition points to tenders and auctions. Seek joint advantage points to partnerships. Manage spend points to channels like catalogues. And change the nature of demand points to negotiation combined with demand change. So the Chessboard position you wrote in your Sourcing Case part 1 already narrows the route."],[20,1132.19,1140.55,"Now the reference table of the day: the strategy-selection matrix. Six situations, each with a strategy and a primary tool."],[20,1141.05,1173.16,"A recurring site service, like cleaning or security: one supplier on a three to five year cycle, chosen by RFP with quality and price. A stream of specialist jobs, like consulting or IT services: a panel with a rate card, set up by a framework RFP, with mini-competitions for each job. A proprietary incumbent, like core software: a sole-source negotiation, built on a usage audit, a benchmark and your best alternative to a negotiated agreement, your BATNA."],[20,1173.66,1201.52,"A standard, price-driven purchase, like office supplies or standard laptops: competitive single or dual sourcing, by RFQ and possibly an English reverse auction. A complex transformation, like a managed IT service: a partnership, through a multi-stage process with dialogue and a best and final offer. And the tail, the many small purchases: channel and automate, with catalogues, punch-out and spot buys."],[20,1202.02,1213.54,"Keep this table close. It is the reference for Case 2 at the end of the session. When you are unsure about a category, find its row first, and then ask what is different about your situation."],[21,1214.49,1220.64,"The matrix tells you the patterns. These five questions, asked in order, tell you which pattern applies."],[21,1221.14,1244.84,"Question one: is there a real alternative within the contract horizon? If not, it is a sole-source negotiation, module 2.4, whatever the category. This question overrides everything else. Question two: how does demand arrive? A continuous service points to one supplier. A stream of assignments points to a panel. Many small orders point to a catalogue."],[21,1245.34,1281.93,"Question three: can we specify precisely and rank bids automatically? If yes, and you have four or five qualified bidders, then RFQ and an auction become possible. If not, use an RFP and negotiation. Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis showed in 2009 that auctions work best for simple, well specified work, and negotiation does better when the project is complex and the specification incomplete. Question four: is the solution itself unknown? Then a multi-stage negotiated process, which we see in module 2.5."],[21,1282.43,1300.1,"And question five: does an existing framework already cover the need? If yes, call off from it before designing anything new. This question is cheap and often forgotten. In our Group, a framework may already exist in another entity. A ten minute check can save months of tendering."],[22,1301.05,1305.78,"Let us run Nordvia's pipeline through the questions. This is fictional case data."],[22,1306.28,1339.07,"P1, office cleaning: demand is continuous, there are many real alternatives, so the route is one supplier per lot by RFP. P2, IT consulting: demand arrives as assignments, there are many alternatives, so a panel with a rate card. P3, Kelvar: the demand is continuous, but there is no real alternative for 18 to 24 months, because migrating a core insurance system takes that long. So question one overrides everything: sole-source negotiation."],[22,1339.57,1377.25,"P5, office supplies and print: many small orders, many alternatives, so RFQ, possibly an auction, and a catalogue. Remember, Nordvia raises about 1,900 purchase orders a year below 1,000 euros in this category. And P8, Northbyte, the claims IT managed service: a complex service where alternatives exist but are costly to reach, so a multi-stage negotiated process. Five lines, five different routes, from the same five questions. Later you will justify P1, P2, P3 and P5 in detail."],[23,1378.2,1396.74,"Here is a worked example: the six decisions for Nordvia P1, office cleaning. Three sites in two countries, 2.4 million euros a year, three local cleaners rolled over since 2019, quality complaints at HQ, expiry in June 2027."],[23,1397.24,1423.23,"Decision one: one supplier per country lot. Decision two: a preferred supplier, managed by KPIs. Decision three: a term contract per lot. Decision four: a fixed monthly fee plus unit rates for extras, with indexation by a wage index. Decision five: an RFP with quality and price, followed by a negotiation round. Decision six: three years, plus one, plus one."],[23,1423.73,1457.16,"Notice how each line follows from the facts. Continuous demand gives one supplier. A leverage position gives a preferred, KPI-managed relationship, not a partnership. Labour-heavy costs give a wage-index formula. Here is a question for you: which of these six would change if HQ were the only site? Probably decision one disappears into a single lot, and the bidder pool may widen to smaller local firms. The rest would stay. That is a good test of your own decisions: change one fact, and see which decision moves."],[24,1458.11,1485.44,"Now it is your turn. The exercise is the six decisions for your own project, about 15 minutes, in Module 2.1 of your workbook, on the Modules tab. Bring forward your Kraljic and Chessboard position from Session 1. Say how demand arrives. Answer the six decisions, one line each. Then, for each decision, write the option you rejected and why. If you work with a colleague, swap and challenge one decision."],[24,1485.94,1503.11,"The rejected column is the most important one. It is where you can see whether you made a decision or followed a habit. If you cannot name a rejected option, you probably did not consider one. Pause the lesson now if you want to do the exercise, and continue when you are ready."],[25,1504.06,1537.07,"Let us debrief with four questions. Which decision was hardest to answer? Often it is the commercial model, because it forces you to think about who controls the outcome. Where did your Session 1 position change your mind? Did anyone choose the mechanism before the number of suppliers? Be honest; most of us do at first. And which option did you reject that the business would have chosen? That last question prepares Fieldwork 2. The options you rejected are exactly what the business owner will ask about, so your reasons need to be ready."],[26,1538.02,1554.1,"Module 2.1 in three lines. First: six decisions, in order, and the mechanism is the fifth. Second: compete for the market, compete in the market, or negotiate. Third: write down the rejected options, because they are your argument."],[26,1554.6,1587.35,"To recap this lesson: we started from the sentence that the tool follows the strategy and the strategy follows the power position. We saw why three quotes for everything is a habit. We walked through the six decisions, the Chessboard, the strategy-selection matrix and the five route-to-market questions, and we applied them to Nordvia's pipeline and to the cleaning project. In the next lesson we take the first pattern in depth: one supplier, chosen by tender, for cleaning, security and soft facilities management. See you there."]],"questions":[{"after":6,"q":"Your entity's facilities contract expires on 30 June 2027 and has a six month notice period with automatic renewal. When does your sourcing timeline really start to bite?","options":["From the expiry date in June 2027","From the notice date at the end of December 2026","From the date the business owner signs the strategy paper","From the first quote received"],"answer":1,"explain":"If notice is not given by the notice date, the contract can roll over. Timelines are planned backwards from the notice date, not only from the expiry.","t":346.72},{"after":11,"q":"A colleague wants to start the Nordvia P5 office supplies project by booking an e-auction slot in Coupa. Using the chain from analysis to tool, what is the best response?","options":["Fine, auctions always give the best price for supplies","Run three quotes first, then decide on the auction","Ask the business owner which tool they prefer","First confirm the power position and the six decisions; the auction may fit, but it is the output of the chain"],"answer":3,"explain":"The tool is the last box of the chain. An auction may well fit a precise, price-driven category, but only after the power position and the earlier decisions confirm it, as CIPS and O'Brien both say.","t":551.7},{"after":13,"q":"Nordvia's core insurance software can only be renewed by Kelvar, and migration would take 18 to 24 months. How should the number of suppliers be described in the strategy?","options":["Sole source, a market fact to be managed","Single source, chosen for leverage","Dual source, to keep tension","A panel of one"],"answer":0,"explain":"Single source is a choice among several suppliers. Sole source means there is no realistic alternative in the horizon, so it is a fact to manage through negotiation and demand change, not a choice.","t":724.79},{"after":16,"q":"HR needs a firm to explore why staff turnover in claims is rising. Nobody yet knows what the deliverables will be. Which commercial model fits best for this first phase?","options":["Fixed price for the whole programme","Gain-share on reduced turnover","Short, time-boxed time and materials at rate card","Unit rates per interview"],"answer":2,"explain":"When the scope is unclear, a fixed price only buys a risk premium. Short discovery work fits time and materials at rate card; once deliverables are clear, the next phase can move to fixed price.","t":934.18},{"after":18,"q":"Marketing proposes to renew its events agency for one year at a time, every year, to keep the agency on its toes. The agency invests in a dedicated team and tools. What is the main weakness of this term design?","options":["One-year terms break the Chessboard logic","One-year terms are not allowed under DORA","It creates competition in the market","The supplier cannot recover its mobilisation and will price it in, and renewals become automatic rather than earned"],"answer":3,"explain":"The term must let the supplier recover its investment and let us recover our switching cost. Options should be earned by performance on top of a sensible base term, not a yearly roll-over.","t":1063.21},{"after":20,"q":"Nordvia's Legal team buys advice from external law firms on many different matters through the year. Which row of the strategy-selection matrix fits best?","options":["Recurring site service: one supplier on a three to five year cycle","Stream of specialist jobs: a panel with a rate card and mini-competitions","Standard, price-driven: RFQ and reverse auction","Tail: catalogue and spot buy"],"answer":1,"explain":"Legal advice arrives as a stream of different assignments needing specialists. That is competition in the market: a panel with agreed terms and competition for each job.","t":1213.54},{"after":22,"q":"A new travel management requirement arrives. Before designing any tender, which of the five route-to-market questions is the cheapest to answer and often forgotten?","options":["Does an existing framework, perhaps in another entity, already cover it?","Is the solution itself unknown?","Can we rank bids automatically?","How many bidders will join an auction?"],"answer":0,"explain":"Question five is cheap and easy to forget: if a Group or entity framework already exists, calling off from it avoids designing a new process at all.","t":1377.25},{"after":25,"q":"In the P1 cleaning example, suppose HQ were the only site. Which decision is most likely to change?","options":["Decision 5, which would become an e-auction","Decision 4, which would become gain-share","Decision 1, the lot structure, collapses into a single lot","Decision 2, which would become a strategic partnership"],"answer":2,"explain":"With one site there is nothing to split by country, so the lot structure becomes one lot. Demand is still continuous and the position is still leverage, so the other decisions stay largely the same.","t":1537.07}],"cues":[[0.6,3.91,"Welcome to Session 2 of the Source to Contract course."],[3.91,5.52,"I am glad you are back."],[5.52,8.48,"Today's session is called WHICH route to market?"],[8.48,14.16,"and its subtitle says exactly what we will do: choose the sourcing strategy."],[14.16,21.1,"This first lesson covers the opening of the session and module 2.1, the six decisions of a sourcing strategy."],[21.38,38.0,"By the end of it you will be able to take any indirect category, look at its power position and at how demand arrives, and answer six questions in order: how many suppliers, what kind of relationship, what contract, what price model, what form of competition, and how long."],[38.28,44.67,"You will also learn a simple matrix and five questions that point you to the right route to market."],[44.67,53.36,"The next two lessons then take the two most common patterns one by one: one supplier chosen by tender, and a pool of preferred suppliers."],[54.31,56.72,"Let us start with the sentence on the screen."],[56.72,59.07,"The tool follows the strategy."],[59.07,61.72,"The strategy follows the power position."],[61.72,63.11,"Never the reverse."],[63.11,66.24,"Read it twice, because the whole session rests on it."],[66.74,69.25,"In Session 1 we did the analysis."],[69.25,77.69,"We confirmed the need, we built a category profile, and we placed each category on the Kraljic matrix and on the Kearney Purchasing Chessboard."],[77.69,80.44,"We know what we buy and who holds the power."],[80.44,83.03,"Today that analysis becomes a decision."],[83.03,84.51,"And notice the order."],[84.51,86.54,"Many teams start with the tool."],[86.82,94.02,"Someone says, let us run an RFP, or let us do an auction, and only afterwards asks what the market looks like."],[94.02,96.27,"That is the reverse of the chain."],[96.27,102.28,"Today we choose how each category goes to market, and, just as important, we write down why."],[102.28,105.33,"Session 3 will then design the tool itself."],[106.28,108.71,"Here is where we are in the course."],[108.71,111.41,"There are four questions, one per session."],[111.41,113.12,"First, WHAT are we sourcing?"],[113.12,117.3,"That is done: the need, the category profile and the power position."],[117.3,118.87,"Second, WHICH route to market?"],[118.87,122.81,"That is today, and its output is the sourcing strategy paper."],[122.81,124.9,"Third, HOW do we run the event?"],[125.18,130.91,"That is Session 3: RFx documents, evaluation models and e-auctions in Coupa."],[130.91,133.15,"And fourth, HOW do we close it well?"],[133.15,137.28,"Session 4: negotiation, the contract and the handover."],[137.78,140.79,"Each session adds one part to your Sourcing Case."],[140.79,143.22,"Today's part is a special one."],[143.22,149.07,"The strategy paper is the document your business owner must agree before any event is issued."],[149.35,159.05,"Once a request for proposal is out in the market, many choices are locked in: the lots, the term, the weighting, even the number of suppliers you can award."],[159.05,162.15,"So this is the last calm moment to get them right."],[163.1,166.32,"So what will you be able to do by the end of today?"],[166.32,167.02,"Six things."],[167.02,170.23,"Answer the six decisions of a sourcing strategy."],[170.23,173.22,"Choose a route with the strategy-selection matrix."],[173.22,178.77,"Design a single-supplier tender for a site service like cleaning or security."],[178.77,183.58,"Design a panel of preferred suppliers for professional and IT services."],[183.86,187.95,"Prepare a sole-source negotiation with an incumbent vendor."],[187.95,191.52,"And write a one-page strategy paper for your own project."],[192.02,194.84,"Here is a small exercise to keep in mind."],[194.84,196.84,"Think about your own Sourcing Case."],[196.84,200.15,"Which of the three big patterns do you think it belongs to?"],[200.15,206.47,"One supplier by tender, a panel of preferred suppliers, or a sole-source negotiation?"],[206.47,207.83,"Make a quick guess now."],[208.11,213.97,"We will test that guess at the end of the session, and it is often interesting to see how the guess changes."],[214.92,220.54,"This is the plan of the full classroom day, so you can see where this lesson sits."],[220.54,223.52,"We open with the debrief of Fieldwork 1."],[223.52,228.58,"Then module 2.1, the six decisions, which is this lesson."],[228.58,233.29,"Then 2.2, one supplier by tender, and 2.3, a pool of preferred suppliers."],[233.57,242.06,"After that 2.4 on sole and single source, 2.5 on partnerships, the tail and the strategy paper, and finally Case 2."],[242.06,253.39,"Module 2.3 is the longest block of the day, for a good reason: it carries the most common indirect categories, consulting, IT services and agencies."],[254.34,256.92,"In the classroom we would now run a short poll."],[256.92,263.12,"The question is: did you validate the need with your business owner in Fieldwork 1?"],[263.12,270.0,"The options are: yes, need and scope confirmed; yes, but the scope changed; meeting booked, not yet held; or not yet."],[270.0,272.99,"Take a moment and give your own honest answer."],[273.27,277.42,"If the scope changed after that conversation, that is not a failure."],[277.42,279.67,"It is the fieldwork doing its job."],[280.61,283.39,"Let us debrief Fieldwork 1 with four cards."],[283.39,284.69,"First, need."],[284.69,288.01,"Did the business owner confirm the need and the scope?"],[288.01,288.96,"Second, data."],[288.96,291.73,"Which contract or spend fact surprised you?"],[291.73,298.69,"Very often it is the number of suppliers, or a contract that nobody knew had been rolled over for years."],[298.69,299.51,"Third, triage."],[299.79,307.79,"Did anything trigger outsourcing rules, DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act, personal data, or staff transfer?"],[307.79,314.96,"Those flags change the route to market, because they bring Risk and Compliance into the process and they add time."],[315.46,316.83,"And fourth, timing."],[316.83,319.83,"When is the notice date, not only the expiry?"],[319.83,321.6,"This question is deliberate."],[321.6,326.88,"Imagine a facilities contract that expires in June, with a six month notice period."],[326.88,331.76,"If you only look at the expiry date, you think you have until spring."],[332.04,337.62,"In reality, if you do nothing before December, the contract may roll over for another year."],[337.62,342.67,"Every timeline we build today starts from the notice date and plans backwards."],[342.67,346.72,"If you want, pause here and note your own notice date in your workbook."],[347.67,348.98,"Now to our case."],[348.98,354.5,"Nordvia Insurance, our fictional insurer, runs about 60 sourcing events a year."],[354.5,359.49,"Until now most of them were RFQs with three quotes, whatever the category."],[359.49,364.01,"The same tool for cleaning, for consulting and for software licences."],[364.01,366.29,"That is a habit, not a strategy."],[366.79,368.18,"Let us test the habit."],[368.18,373.65,"What does three quotes achieve for the Kelvar renewal, Nordvia's core insurance software?"],[373.65,374.84,"Very little."],[374.84,380.05,"There is only one vendor who can renew those licences, so the two other quotes are theatre."],[380.05,382.04,"And for a consulting call-off?"],[382.32,389.19,"Three quotes for a two week assignment take weeks to collect, so in practice people skip them and call a favourite."],[389.19,393.22,"The habit gives the appearance of competition without the substance."],[393.22,396.33,"Probably this feels familiar from your own entity."],[396.33,399.56,"The good news is that the fix is not more process."],[399.84,402.29,"It is choosing the right process per category."],[403.25,405.8,"Another poll question for you to think about."],[405.8,409.98,"How does your team usually choose the sourcing route today?"],[409.98,421.19,"Three quotes, whatever the category; the business proposes and we follow; we decide per category, but rarely write it down; or a documented strategy paper for every major project."],[421.19,422.86,"There is no judgement here."],[423.14,425.27,"Most teams pick the third answer."],[425.27,429.86,"They do decide per category, but the reasoning stays in someone's head."],[429.86,432.82,"That is exactly what the strategy paper fixes."],[433.77,438.87,"This brings us to module 2.1: the six decisions of a sourcing strategy."],[438.87,445.63,"How many suppliers, what relationship, what contract, what price model, what competition, and how long?"],[445.63,446.26,"Six questions."],[446.26,450.77,"Let us see where they sit in the chain and why their order matters."],[451.72,454.79,"This flow shows the chain from analysis to tool."],[454.79,457.22,"On the left, the category profile."],[457.22,458.97,"Then the power position."],[458.97,460.74,"Then the six decisions."],[460.74,462.4,"Then the route to market."],[462.4,465.41,"And only at the end, the tool and the event."],[465.41,469.32,"Sessions 1, 2 and 3 cover these boxes in that order."],[469.82,471.61,"Now look at the last box."],[471.61,477.25,"The RFP, the RFQ or the auction is the output of the chain, not its start."],[477.25,480.85,"Skipping a box is how three quotes became the default."],[481.13,491.75,"If you go straight from the request to the tool, you skip the power position and the six decisions, and then the only tool you reach for is the one you used last time."],[491.75,495.1,"Think of a marketing request for a new events agency."],[495.1,497.85,"If you jump to an RFQ, you will compare day rates."],[498.13,507.82,"If you first ask how demand arrives, you may find it is a stream of events across the year, which points to a small roster with a rate card, not a one-off quote."],[508.77,510.88,"This is not a local idea."],[510.88,521.01,"The CIPS Procurement and Supply Cycle, in its stage 3, the Strategy stage, says you choose between competitive tendering and other approaches based on supply position."],[521.01,525.96,"In other words, the market and the power balance decide the approach."],[526.46,536.55,"Paul O'Brien's 5i model says the same thing in its Innovation stage, where the category strategy is formed from the insight work before any implementation."],[536.55,543.44,"Two different reference models, one message: the choice of approach rests on the market and power analysis."],[543.72,551.7,"When your business owner asks why you are not simply running three quotes, you can say that this is how the profession's own standard models work."],[552.65,554.74,"Here are the six decisions, in order."],[554.74,556.34,"Let me walk you through the table."],[556.85,562.83,"Decision one, the number of suppliers: single, dual, multi in the form of a panel, or sole."],[562.83,569.02,"Decision two, the relationship type: transactional, preferred supplier, or strategic partner."],[569.02,576.78,"Decision three, the contract architecture: a one-off contract, a term contract, a framework, a catalogue, or a rate card."],[577.06,588.34,"Decision four, the commercial model: fixed price, unit rates, time and materials with a rate card, capped time and materials, an output price, or gain-share."],[588.34,596.83,"Decision five, the competitive mechanism: RFQ, RFP, a multi-stage process, a mini-competition, an auction, or a negotiation."],[597.11,600.55,"And decision six, the term: a base term plus options."],[601.04,602.87,"Why does the order matter?"],[602.87,605.25,"Because each decision constrains the next."],[605.25,610.21,"The number of suppliers depends on how demand arrives and on supply risk."],[610.21,615.08,"The relationship follows from the Kraljic position and the value at stake."],[615.08,619.0,"The contract architecture follows the predictability of demand."],[619.28,622.38,"The commercial model follows the clarity of scope."],[622.38,625.97,"Only then can you choose a mechanism that fits all of that."],[625.97,629.08,"Notice that the mechanism is decision five."],[629.08,632.57,"If you choose it first, the other four are made by accident."],[632.85,644.5,"Choose an e-auction first, and you have silently decided on a precise specification, a price-only award and a transactional relationship, perhaps for a service where none of that is true."],[645.45,647.57,"Decision one: how many suppliers?"],[647.57,651.5,"This follows Monczka and colleagues and Lysons and Farrington."],[651.5,656.71,"Four options, each with a strength, a weakness and a typical fit for an insurer."],[657.22,663.12,"Single sourcing, chosen by competition, gives you leverage and one accountable party."],[663.12,666.06,"The weakness is dependency during the term."],[666.06,670.13,"It fits cleaning, security and a travel management company."],[670.13,676.44,"Dual sourcing gives continuity and benchmark tension, because each supplier knows the other is there."],[676.72,680.12,"The price is split volume and double management cost."],[680.12,686.81,"It fits telecom, or regional temporary staffing where one agency cannot cover all locations."],[687.31,693.19,"Multi-sourcing, usually as a panel, brings specialists and competition for each job."],[693.19,697.08,"The weakness is fragmentation if the panel grows too large."],[697.08,700.28,"It fits consulting, IT services and agencies."],[700.28,702.21,"And finally sole source."],[702.21,704.58,"Look at the strength column: none."],[704.58,707.02,"Sole source is not a choice we make."],[707.3,709.65,"It is a market fact we have to manage."],[709.65,713.03,"Proprietary core software is the typical example."],[713.03,714.93,"Be careful with the words."],[714.93,718.64,"Single source means we chose one supplier out of several."],[718.64,721.34,"Sole source means there is only one."],[721.34,724.8,"The difference matters for the whole negotiation strategy."],[725.74,729.21,"Why should we care about the number of suppliers?"],[729.21,730.94,"These figures give a direction."],[730.94,745.73,"The Hackett Group reports that its Digital World Class procurement organisations, in its 2024 benchmark, work with 3.7 times fewer suppliers per billion dollars of spend, and have 23 percent shorter sourcing cycle times."],[746.01,750.34,"Please treat these as consultancy benchmarks, not peer-reviewed results."],[750.84,752.19,"Now our own Group."],[752.19,762.99,"Only about 2 percent of the Group's spend is under a Group contract today, and there are roughly 350 IT services suppliers for about 80 million euros of spend."],[762.99,766.08,"These figures are anonymised and rounded."],[766.08,769.12,"The point is direction, not a target number."],[769.4,774.58,"Concentration is associated with better performance, and our Group is fragmented."],[774.58,778.32,"That does not mean every category should go to one supplier."],[778.32,782.1,"It means fragmentation should be a decision, not an accident."],[783.05,787.03,"Decisions two and three: the relationship and the contract."],[787.03,789.25,"On the left, the relationship type."],[789.25,794.16,"Transactional means we care about price and compliance, and little more."],[794.16,800.18,"Preferred supplier means the supplier gets priority and agreed terms in exchange for performance."],[800.46,805.04,"Strategic partner means joint plans and often open book costing."],[805.04,808.19,"Most indirect categories sit in the first two."],[808.19,817.04,"Strategic partnership is rare, expensive to run, and reserved for the few categories where both sides gain from working closely together."],[817.54,820.54,"On the right, the contract architecture."],[820.54,822.84,"A one-off contract for a defined project."],[822.84,825.64,"A term contract for a continuous service."],[825.64,828.65,"A framework with call-offs for a stream of needs."],[828.65,831.93,"And a catalogue or a rate card for repeat buys."],[831.93,837.19,"The rule is simple: contract architecture follows the predictability of demand."],[837.47,843.08,"A continuous service, like cleaning a building every night, needs a term contract."],[843.08,847.69,"Unpredictable assignments, like legal advice or IT projects, need a framework."],[847.69,857.39,"If you put a stream of assignments into a single term contract, you either lock in volume you cannot guarantee, or you end up negotiating every change."],[858.34,860.72,"Decision four: the commercial model."],[860.72,862.56,"This is about who carries the risk."],[862.56,864.14,"Let me go down the table."],[864.64,867.38,"Fixed price, when scope and deliverables are clear."],[867.38,872.67,"Unit rates, when volume varies but the unit is clear: square metres, hours, devices."],[872.67,878.78,"Time and materials with a rate card, when the scope is unclear, for short discovery work."],[878.78,884.29,"Capped time and materials, when the scope is partly clear and you need to protect the budget."],[884.57,888.27,"An output price, for a managed service with service levels."],[888.27,893.23,"And gain-share, when the supplier controls the levers and a baseline is agreed."],[893.73,899.7,"The UK Consultancy Playbook, a public sector guide, gives the same logic for consulting."],[899.7,904.88,"The principle behind it is this: whoever controls the outcome should carry the risk."],[904.88,906.3,"Take an example."],[906.3,913.21,"A consulting firm is asked to deliver a defined process redesign for claims handling, with clear deliverables."],[913.49,919.31,"If you pay time and materials, every delay is your cost, although the firm controls the work."],[919.31,923.25,"Fixed price per deliverable puts the risk where the control is."],[923.25,930.49,"But if you ask a firm to explore a problem nobody understands yet, fixed price only buys a large risk premium."],[930.49,934.18,"Then short, time-boxed time and materials is honest."],[935.13,937.35,"Decision five: two ways to compete."],[937.35,941.87,"This distinction is the heart of the session, so let us take it slowly."],[942.37,944.72,"On the left, competition for the market."],[944.72,947.95,"You run one tender per cycle for the whole contract."],[947.95,950.72,"Then one supplier delivers and is measured."],[950.72,953.19,"This fits continuous site services."],[953.47,963.13,"The competitive tension is concentrated in one moment every few years, and during the term the tension comes from the KPIs and the options to extend."],[963.63,966.15,"On the right, competition in the market."],[966.15,972.58,"Several pre-qualified suppliers stay in the game, and they compete again for every assignment."],[972.58,974.5,"This fits streams of specialist work."],[974.5,980.61,"The tension is spread across the whole term: every new job is a new chance to win or lose."],[981.12,983.74,"So, cleaning is competition for the market."],[983.74,986.5,"Consulting is competition in the market."],[986.5,989.04,"And Kelvar, the core software vendor?"],[989.04,990.0,"Neither."],[990.0,994.69,"There is no realistic alternative in the contract horizon, so we negotiate."],[994.69,999.94,"Three answers: compete for the market, compete in the market, or negotiate."],[1000.22,1003.5,"Keep them in mind; they reappear in every module today."],[1004.45,1005.58,"Decision six: the term."],[1005.58,1006.81,"Four cards."],[1006.81,1012.72,"On the supplier side, the term must be long enough to recover mobilisation and investment."],[1012.72,1018.62,"A cleaning contractor buys equipment, recruits and trains staff, and sets up systems."],[1018.62,1021.94,"If the term is too short, it prices that into the bid."],[1022.22,1030.18,"On our side, the term must be long enough to recover our own switching cost: the transition effort, the service dip, the internal time."],[1030.68,1031.86,"Then options."],[1031.86,1035.75,"Extensions should be earned by performance, not automatic."],[1035.75,1041.51,"An option that is exercised by default is not leverage, it is just a longer contract."],[1041.51,1042.24,"And exit."],[1042.24,1050.07,"For ICT services, the term should be aligned to a tested exit plan, which DORA expects for important services."],[1050.35,1058.85,"Lisa Ellram's total cost of ownership logic is useful here: mobilisation and transition are real post-transaction costs."],[1058.85,1063.22,"So term length is a cost decision, not an administrative one."],[1064.16,1072.58,"Let us bring back a tool from Session 1, the Kearney Purchasing Chessboard, developed by Schuh and colleagues."],[1072.58,1076.38,"The vertical axis is supply power, the horizontal axis is demand power."],[1076.38,1078.18,"Four basic strategies."],[1078.68,1088.77,"When supply power is high and our demand power is low, we change the nature of demand: re-specify, mine technical data, open the door to alternatives."],[1088.77,1093.79,"When both are high, we seek joint advantage: a value and cost partnership."],[1093.79,1099.14,"When both are low, we manage spend: demand management, bundling, catalogues."],[1099.42,1106.42,"And when our demand power is high, we leverage competition among suppliers: tenders, target pricing, auctions."],[1106.92,1110.27,"Each basic strategy points to a family of routes."],[1110.27,1114.09,"Leverage competition points to tenders and auctions."],[1114.09,1116.76,"Seek joint advantage points to partnerships."],[1116.76,1120.52,"Manage spend points to channels like catalogues."],[1120.52,1125.84,"And change the nature of demand points to negotiation combined with demand change."],[1126.12,1131.24,"So the Chessboard position you wrote in your Sourcing Case part 1 already narrows the route."],[1132.19,1136.48,"Now the reference table of the day: the strategy-selection matrix."],[1136.48,1140.55,"Six situations, each with a strategy and a primary tool."],[1141.05,1151.03,"A recurring site service, like cleaning or security: one supplier on a three to five year cycle, chosen by RFP with quality and price."],[1151.03,1161.3,"A stream of specialist jobs, like consulting or IT services: a panel with a rate card, set up by a framework RFP, with mini-competitions for each job."],[1161.58,1173.16,"A proprietary incumbent, like core software: a sole-source negotiation, built on a usage audit, a benchmark and your best alternative to a negotiated agreement, your BATNA."],[1173.66,1184.93,"A standard, price-driven purchase, like office supplies or standard laptops: competitive single or dual sourcing, by RFQ and possibly an English reverse auction."],[1184.93,1194.8,"A complex transformation, like a managed IT service: a partnership, through a multi-stage process with dialogue and a best and final offer."],[1195.08,1201.52,"And the tail, the many small purchases: channel and automate, with catalogues, punch-out and spot buys."],[1202.02,1203.59,"Keep this table close."],[1203.59,1206.93,"It is the reference for Case 2 at the end of the session."],[1206.93,1213.54,"When you are unsure about a category, find its row first, and then ask what is different about your situation."],[1214.49,1216.4,"The matrix tells you the patterns."],[1216.4,1220.63,"These five questions, asked in order, tell you which pattern applies."],[1221.14,1226.38,"Question one: is there a real alternative within the contract horizon?"],[1226.38,1231.69,"If not, it is a sole-source negotiation, module 2.4, whatever the category."],[1231.69,1233.58,"This question overrides everything else."],[1233.58,1237.27,"Question two: how does demand arrive?"],[1237.27,1240.06,"A continuous service points to one supplier."],[1240.06,1242.37,"A stream of assignments points to a panel."],[1242.65,1244.84,"Many small orders point to a catalogue."],[1245.34,1250.85,"Question three: can we specify precisely and rank bids automatically?"],[1250.85,1257.57,"If yes, and you have four or five qualified bidders, then RFQ and an auction become possible."],[1257.57,1260.25,"If not, use an RFP and negotiation."],[1260.53,1273.9,"Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis showed in 2009 that auctions work best for simple, well specified work, and negotiation does better when the project is complex and the specification incomplete."],[1273.9,1276.62,"Question four: is the solution itself unknown?"],[1276.62,1281.93,"Then a multi-stage negotiated process, which we see in module 2.5."],[1282.43,1286.6,"And question five: does an existing framework already cover the need?"],[1286.6,1290.23,"If yes, call off from it before designing anything new."],[1290.23,1292.87,"This question is cheap and often forgotten."],[1292.87,1297.25,"In our Group, a framework may already exist in another entity."],[1297.25,1300.09,"A ten minute check can save months of tendering."],[1301.05,1303.95,"Let us run Nordvia's pipeline through the questions."],[1303.95,1305.79,"This is fictional case data."],[1306.28,1315.24,"P1, office cleaning: demand is continuous, there are many real alternatives, so the route is one supplier per lot by RFP."],[1315.24,1323.01,"P2, IT consulting: demand arrives as assignments, there are many alternatives, so a panel with a rate card."],[1323.29,1334.19,"P3, Kelvar: the demand is continuous, but there is no real alternative for 18 to 24 months, because migrating a core insurance system takes that long."],[1334.19,1339.07,"So question one overrides everything: sole-source negotiation."],[1339.57,1348.66,"P5, office supplies and print: many small orders, many alternatives, so RFQ, possibly an auction, and a catalogue."],[1348.66,1356.32,"Remember, Nordvia raises about 1,900 purchase orders a year below 1,000 euros in this category."],[1356.6,1368.8,"And P8, Northbyte, the claims IT managed service: a complex service where alternatives exist but are costly to reach, so a multi-stage negotiated process."],[1368.8,1373.35,"Five lines, five different routes, from the same five questions."],[1373.35,1377.25,"Later you will justify P1, P2, P3 and P5 in detail."],[1378.2,1383.91,"Here is a worked example: the six decisions for Nordvia P1, office cleaning."],[1383.91,1396.74,"Three sites in two countries, 2.4 million euros a year, three local cleaners rolled over since 2019, quality complaints at HQ, expiry in June 2027."],[1397.24,1400.02,"Decision one: one supplier per country lot."],[1400.02,1404.26,"Decision two: a preferred supplier, managed by KPIs."],[1404.26,1407.01,"Decision three: a term contract per lot."],[1407.01,1414.08,"Decision four: a fixed monthly fee plus unit rates for extras, with indexation by a wage index."],[1414.08,1419.9,"Decision five: an RFP with quality and price, followed by a negotiation round."],[1420.18,1423.23,"Decision six: three years, plus one, plus one."],[1423.73,1426.54,"Notice how each line follows from the facts."],[1426.54,1429.41,"Continuous demand gives one supplier."],[1429.41,1434.87,"A leverage position gives a preferred, KPI-managed relationship, not a partnership."],[1434.87,1437.45,"Labour-heavy costs give a wage-index formula."],[1437.45,1443.4,"Here is a question for you: which of these six would change if HQ were the only site?"],[1443.68,1450.14,"Probably decision one disappears into a single lot, and the bidder pool may widen to smaller local firms."],[1450.14,1451.63,"The rest would stay."],[1451.63,1457.16,"That is a good test of your own decisions: change one fact, and see which decision moves."],[1458.11,1459.72,"Now it is your turn."],[1459.72,1468.39,"The exercise is the six decisions for your own project, about 15 minutes, in Module 2.1 of your workbook, on the Modules tab."],[1468.39,1472.38,"Bring forward your Kraljic and Chessboard position from Session 1."],[1472.38,1474.38,"Say how demand arrives."],[1474.38,1477.27,"Answer the six decisions, one line each."],[1477.55,1481.66,"Then, for each decision, write the option you rejected and why."],[1481.66,1485.44,"If you work with a colleague, swap and challenge one decision."],[1485.94,1488.81,"The rejected column is the most important one."],[1488.81,1493.1,"It is where you can see whether you made a decision or followed a habit."],[1493.1,1497.85,"If you cannot name a rejected option, you probably did not consider one."],[1497.85,1503.11,"Pause the lesson now if you want to do the exercise, and continue when you are ready."],[1504.06,1506.29,"Let us debrief with four questions."],[1506.29,1508.72,"Which decision was hardest to answer?"],[1508.72,1514.45,"Often it is the commercial model, because it forces you to think about who controls the outcome."],[1514.45,1517.67,"Where did your Session 1 position change your mind?"],[1517.67,1521.16,"Did anyone choose the mechanism before the number of suppliers?"],[1521.16,1523.71,"Be honest; most of us do at first."],[1523.99,1527.43,"And which option did you reject that the business would have chosen?"],[1527.43,1530.43,"That last question prepares Fieldwork 2."],[1530.43,1537.07,"The options you rejected are exactly what the business owner will ask about, so your reasons need to be ready."],[1538.02,1539.72,"Module 2.1 in three lines."],[1539.72,1544.29,"First: six decisions, in order, and the mechanism is the fifth."],[1544.29,1549.3,"Second: compete for the market, compete in the market, or negotiate."],[1549.3,1554.11,"Third: write down the rejected options, because they are your argument."],[1554.6,1562.55,"To recap this lesson: we started from the sentence that the tool follows the strategy and the strategy follows the power position."],[1562.55,1565.59,"We saw why three quotes for everything is a habit."],[1565.87,1577.22,"We walked through the six decisions, the Chessboard, the strategy-selection matrix and the five route-to-market questions, and we applied them to Nordvia's pipeline and to the cleaning project."],[1577.5,1586.25,"In the next lesson we take the first pattern in depth: one supplier, chosen by tender, for cleaning, security and soft facilities management."],[1586.25,1587.35,"See you there."]]},{"id":"s2-l2","session":"s2","title":"One supplier, chosen by tender","summary":"Design a single-supplier tender strategy for cleaning, security and soft FM: lots, term, specification type, weights, should-cost floor, staff transfer, indexation and KPIs.","from":27,"to":46,"audio":"audio/s2-l2.mp3","duration":1504.2,"segs":[[27,0.6,46.41,"Welcome back. This lesson covers module 2.2: one supplier, chosen by tender. We will look at cleaning, security, reception and soft facilities management, the services that keep our buildings running every day. In the last lesson you learned the six decisions and the difference between competition for the market and competition in the market. Now we take the first pattern in depth. By the end of this lesson you will be able to design the tender strategy for a site service: the lot structure, the term, the type of specification, the quality and price weights, and the safeguards that protect both the service and our name. We will work with two Nordvia projects, P1 office cleaning and P6 security and reception."],[28,47.36,57.79,"Here is the core idea of the module. For cleaning and security, competition happens once every three years or so, for the whole contract. Make that moment count."],[28,58.29,93.86,"Think about what that means. During the term, there is no other cleaner waiting on the same floor. The supplier knows it has the contract until the next tender. So all the competitive tension we will ever get is concentrated in one event. After that, our leverage comes from two things only: the KPIs we wrote into the contract, and the options to extend that the supplier must earn. Everything in this module is about designing that one competitive moment well, and then holding one supplier to account. If the tender is weak, we live with the result for three to five years."],[28,94.36,135.2,"Let me give you a picture of what a weak competitive moment looks like. A facilities team, short of time, sends a two page request to four local cleaners with a floor plan and asks for a monthly price. No staff data, no quality criteria, no audit regime. The cheapest wins. Six months later the complaints start, and the buyer discovers there is nothing in the contract to measure against. The tender took two weeks. The consequences last three years. Compare that with a tender where the hours, the standards, the KPIs and the transfer data were all on the table. The extra effort at the start is small compared with the three years it protects."],[29,136.15,140.86,"Why one supplier per site or per cluster of sites? Four reasons on the cards."],[29,141.36,168.82,"First, accountability. One provider answers for the result in a building. If the meeting rooms are dirty on Monday morning, there is one number to call. Second, leverage. Labour is most of the cost in these services, so bundled hours mean better routes, better supervision and a better price. A supplier with forty hours a night in one building can afford a dedicated supervisor. Two suppliers with twenty hours each cannot."],[29,169.32,190.42,"Third, mobilisation. Each change of supplier costs recruitment, training, perhaps a staff transfer, and almost always a dip in service for a few weeks. Ellram would call these post-transaction costs, and they belong in the total cost of ownership. Fourth, governance. One set of KPIs, one review meeting, one invoice."],[29,190.92,210.38,"Some buyers split a site between two cleaners to keep them competing. It sounds clever, but it does not work. Two cleaners on one floor means two excuses. When the kitchen is not cleaned, each says it belonged to the other. Splitting a site does not add competitive tension during the term; it only adds interfaces."],[30,211.33,221.52,"This table is the playbook for site services, with cleaning in the middle column and security and reception on the right. The terms are common practice, not norms."],[30,222.02,246.18,"Position. Cleaning is a classic leverage category: many suppliers, a clear service, real buying power. Security moves from leverage towards bottleneck. Why? Because of licensing and vetting. Guards need licences, background checks and training, so fewer providers qualify, and the cost of a failure is higher. That is why the quality weighting goes up for security."],[30,246.68,284.84,"Suppliers. For cleaning, one per region or cluster. For security, one provider, possibly combined with reception. Tool. Cleaning uses an RFP with site visits and staff data. Security uses an RFP with presentations and references, because you want to meet the people who will run your front desk. Price model. Cleaning is a fixed fee plus unit rates. Security is hourly rates by role, such as guard, supervisor or receptionist, plus a management fee. And the term: three years plus one plus one for cleaning, and either the same or three plus two for security."],[30,285.34,314.36,"Notice that every row of this table is one of the six decisions from the last lesson, applied to site services. Position comes from Session 1. Suppliers is decision one. Tool is decision five. Price model is decision four. Term is decision six. So the playbook is not a new framework; it is the six decisions answered once for a whole family of categories, which saves you time every time one of these contracts comes up for renewal."],[31,315.31,326.02,"What do public tenders show? Public buyers publish their contracts, so they give us useful reference points, even though we as a private insurer are not bound by their rules."],[31,326.52,362.21,"First example: a UK university cleaning contract from 2024, with a term of three years plus two options of twelve months each, and a 50 50 split between quality and price. Second: an integrated facilities management call-off by HMRC, the UK tax authority, also 2024, with five years plus three plus one plus one. That is a much longer horizon. Third: the EU public procurement directive of 2014, in Article 33, sets a maximum of four years for frameworks as a benchmark."],[31,362.71,379.46,"The pattern is clear. The larger and more integrated the scope, the longer the term, because mobilisation and investment are larger. Office cleaning sits at the short end. If someone proposes a seven year cleaning contract, ask what investment would justify it."],[32,380.41,383.8,"So why three plus one plus one? Let us take each card."],[32,384.3,411.8,"The three year base. It lets the supplier recover its mobilisation: equipment, training, and the set-up of the computer aided facilities management system, the CAFM. And it lets us recover our own switching cost. The first plus one is earned by performance against the KPIs. The second plus one is earned again, which keeps tension until the very end of the contract. And then we re-tender, and competition for the market starts again."],[32,412.3,445.35,"Here is the important part. Options are leverage only if extension depends on performance. Write that condition into the contract: for example, the option may be exercised only if the average audit score over the last twelve months is above an agreed level. And put the option dates and the notice dates into the contract management system, so that a decision is taken in time. An option that nobody remembers becomes a roll-over, and a roll-over is exactly what Nordvia has had in cleaning since 2019."],[33,446.3,461.28,"Now the specification. Do we describe inputs, outputs, or a hybrid? This table draws on the CMS guidance by Hanson from 2000 and the outcome-based cleaning guide of Singapore's National Environment Agency."],[33,461.78,481.83,"An input specification describes hours, frequencies and headcount. For example: two cleaners, four hours a night, vacuum every office daily. Its strength is that it is easy to audit and compare. Its risk is that you pay for hours, not results, and there is no room for the supplier to propose a better method."],[33,482.33,506.01,"An output specification describes results: cleanliness standards, response times. For example: no visible dust on desks, washrooms restocked at all times, spills handled within thirty minutes. The supplier proposes the method, often at lower cost. The risk is that it is harder to draft and harder to assess, because bids are no longer directly comparable."],[33,506.51,530.54,"The hybrid specifies outputs for most areas and prescribes methods where we need control. It is usually the best fit, but it needs clear boundaries. Security is a good example of hybrid by design: you prescribe the procedures, the vetting and the licences, because those are non-negotiable, and you specify outputs everywhere else, such as response times to an alarm."],[33,531.04,561.76,"How do you choose in practice? Ask two questions per area of the building. First, do we care how it is done, or only that it is done? Second, could we measure the result objectively, with an audit or a sensor or a complaint log? If we only care about the result and we can measure it, use an output standard. If we must control the method, for safety, confidentiality or regulation, prescribe it. Most buildings end up with a mix, and that is why the hybrid is the usual best fit."],[34,562.71,568.11,"Here is a hybrid specification for Nordvia HQ. Fictional, but realistic."],[34,568.61,600.12,"Offices and meeting rooms: an output standard, audited monthly. Washrooms: an output standard plus a minimum check frequency, because a washroom that is clean at 8 and dirty at 11 is a complaint. Server rooms and archives: a prescribed method and vetted staff only, because these rooms contain equipment and confidential policyholder records. Complaints: a response time as a KPI. And the supplier proposes the method and hours, priced per site."],[34,600.62,622.4,"Remember that HQ has quality complaints in the case. An output standard lets us measure what the complaints are about. Today, with an old input contract, the cleaner can say: we did our four hours. With an output standard and a monthly audit, the conversation changes to: the standard was not met in these rooms, and here are the scores."],[35,623.35,646.18,"Quality and price weighting. On the left, the weights. For site services, quality should carry at least 50 percent. The weights are fixed and published before the tender is issued, never adjusted after you see the bids. And price is scored on the total annual cost, including the fixed fee and the expected unit-rate extras, not only on an hourly rate."],[35,646.68,682.59,"On the right, the quality criteria. The method and staffing plan. The mobilisation and staff transfer plan. The KPIs and reporting the supplier offers. And fair labour and ESG, environmental, social and governance criteria, which matter a lot in a sector where low wages and subcontracting chains are a known risk. Session 3 builds the full evaluation model and tests how sensitive the ranking is to the weights. Today we only set the principle in the strategy, so that the business owner agrees it before anyone has seen a price."],[35,683.09,706.05,"Why at least 50 percent for quality? Because in a labour service, the price differences between serious bidders are often small, while the differences in management, supervision and mobilisation are large, and they decide whether the service works. A weighting of, say, 30 percent quality would let a weak bidder win on price alone, and we would pay for it in complaints and re-work."],[36,707.0,712.2,"Now one of the most useful tools in this module: the should-cost floor. Five steps."],[36,712.7,735.51,"First, hours. From the specification, estimate the hours needed per site. Second, multiply by the collectively agreed wage, the legal minimum for that work. Third, add on-costs: statutory social charges, holidays, sickness cover. Fourth, add supervision, consumables and equipment. Fifth, add a reasonable overhead and margin."],[36,736.01,786.0,"Let me make this concrete with illustrative numbers, not real ones. Suppose a site needs 10,000 cleaning hours a year. Suppose the agreed wage is 15 euros an hour, and on-costs add about 40 percent, so 21 euros. That is already 210,000 euros of pure labour cost. Add supervision, materials and equipment, say another 30,000 euros, and a modest overhead and margin, and the floor lands somewhere around 260,000 euros. Now a bid comes in at 200,000 euros. How can that be paid? Labour is most of the cost in cleaning and security. A bid below this floor cannot be paid without breaking a rule somewhere: fewer hours than promised, wages below the agreement, or an unpaid subcontractor."],[36,786.5,805.66,"The floor is not a price you publish, and it is not a target. It is an internal reference you build before the bids arrive, so that you recognise an impossible price when you see one. Facilities and HR colleagues can usually help with the hours and the wage tables, and the current invoices give you a sanity check."],[37,806.61,816.18,"Which brings us to this statement. A bid below the wage floor is not a saving. It is a future service failure, or a legal exposure, with our name on it."],[37,816.68,854.32,"In an insurance group, the name matters. If a cleaning subcontractor at our headquarters is found paying below the legal wage, the headline will not mention the subcontractor. It will mention us. So the rule is: ask for an explanation of every abnormally low bid, and document it. Public guidance from the UK Crown Commercial Service asks buyers to do exactly that. Be honest about the evidence, though. No study quantifies how often auction-driven or low-bid awards lead to failures in cleaning. Present it as risk logic, not as a statistic. The logic is strong enough on its own."],[38,855.27,859.77,"Staff transfer changes the economics of the whole tender. Four cards."],[38,860.27,894.52,"The rule. The EU Acquired Rights Directive of 2001 protects employees when an undertaking or a business transfers to a new employer, and national rules differ in how they apply it. The case law. In the S\u00fczen case, the European Court of Justice held that in labour-intensive services, a transfer can occur if a major part of the workforce moves to the new contractor. National rules. In the UK the TUPE regulations go further, and in some countries collective agreements require the new contractor to take over the staff."],[38,895.02,929.11,"In the RFP, this means three things. Provide staff data so bidders can price correctly: headcount, hours, wages, length of service. Expect realistic savings. And allow enough mobilisation time. When staff transfer, the savings come from method and management, not from wages, because the wages travel with the people. If a business owner expects 20 percent savings from a cleaning tender where the whole team transfers, that expectation needs to be corrected early. Check national law with Legal for each country."],[38,929.61,960.35,"Staff transfer also affects the bidders. A new contractor taking over a team inherits its contracts, its length of service and sometimes its pension terms. If the RFP is silent, cautious bidders add a risk premium, and reckless ones ignore the cost and get into trouble later. Neither helps us. Clear staff data, given under a confidentiality agreement and in line with GDPR, the General Data Protection Regulation, lets every bidder price the same reality."],[39,961.3,965.23,"Price, indexation and KPIs. Let us go down the table."],[39,965.73,996.02,"The base price is a fixed monthly fee per site. Variations, like extra cleaning after an event or a deep clean, are priced at unit rates agreed in the tender. Indexation happens once a year, by a formula linked to a wage index, so that both sides know in advance what the change will be. The KPIs are the audit score, the response time and the complaints. And service credits are linked to those KPIs, and capped, so they motivate without becoming a penalty war."],[39,996.52,1026.12,"Now look at Nordvia P6, security and reception. Hourly rates were indexed twice in two years, and there are no KPIs. These are strategy failures, not contract details. Without an indexation formula, every increase is a negotiation the supplier starts when it suits them. Without KPIs, there is no basis for the options and no way to reward good work. Both problems should have been solved in the strategy, before the last contract was signed."],[39,1026.62,1056.17,"A word on service credits. They are not a source of income, and a supplier that pays service credits every month is a supplier you should be replacing, not billing. Their purpose is to make the KPIs matter in the monthly review. Keep the cap modest, typically a small share of the monthly fee, as an illustration, and link repeated failure to the options rather than to ever larger credits. The real reward for good performance is the next year of the contract."],[40,1057.12,1069.17,"What about an e-auction for cleaning? On the left, the never column. Never a price-only auction on the open market. Never before quality has been evaluated. And never without a should-cost floor."],[40,1069.67,1114.07,"On the right, possible, with care. Only among bidders who are already above a quality threshold. Only above the should-cost floor. And only with the rules disclosed in advance. The research brief rates the fit of e-negotiation for these services as medium. In practice most teams prefer a negotiation round or a best and final offer after the quality evaluation. Why so cautious? Because an auction pushes bidders to cut what is easiest to cut in a labour service, and that is hours or wages. Remember what Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis found: auctions suit simple, fully specified work. A site service with transfer issues and output standards is not that."],[41,1115.02,1129.48,"Let us apply this to P6, security and reception. 1.6 million euros a year, one provider, expiry December 2027. Hourly rates indexed twice in two years, no KPIs."],[41,1129.98,1148.71,"The route: one supplier by RFP, three plus one plus one, with a hybrid specification. A strong weight on vetting, licences, training and response times, because security is moving towards the bottleneck corner. Indexation by formula, and KPIs with service credits."],[41,1149.21,1180.73,"Same pattern as cleaning, different weights. Here is a question to think about: could P1 and P6 be one soft facilities management tender? Possibly, per country, if the market in that country offers providers who do both well. Bundling can give leverage and one point of contact. But it can also exclude strong specialists, and a weak security partner bundled with a good cleaner is still a weak security partner. If you bundle, consider allowing bids per service as well as for the bundle."],[41,1181.23,1212.89,"Look also at the timing. P6 expires in December 2027, six months after P1. If both are re-tendered together, the timeline is set by the earlier date, June 2027 for cleaning. That may be too tight for a combined tender. One way out is to align the two contracts: a decided short bridge for one of them, so that both can go to market as one soft facilities event. This is exactly the kind of choice the strategy paper should make explicit."],[42,1213.84,1247.1,"How does this look in Coupa? Four cards. The event: an RFP with one lot per country. In a Coupa lot, the items are awarded together, so a lot is the unit you award. The content: questionnaires for quality, a pricing template with hours, and the draft contract attached, so bidders price the real terms. Questions and answers: use the Message Center for site visit questions, so every bidder sees the same answers. And the contract: in contract lifecycle management, with option dates and notice alerts."],[42,1247.6,1267.22,"The lot rule matters. If Nordvia wants to allow one bidder to win one country only, the countries must be separate lots. If both countries sit in one lot, you can only award them together. Weighted scoring set-up and sealed opening of bids are features to confirm in your Coupa tenant, because configurations differ."],[42,1267.72,1286.11,"One practical tip. Attach the strategy paper to the sourcing project in Coupa before you create the event, so that anyone who later reviews the file can see why the lots, the weights and the term were chosen. How the paper links to the event depends on your configuration, so check it in your tenant."],[43,1287.06,1305.73,"Here is a poll to reflect on. For Nordvia P1 cleaning, what is the main risk in the re-tender? Too few bidders; abnormally low bids below the wage floor; staff transfer costs nobody priced; or the timeline to June 2027. Choose your answer before I continue."],[43,1306.23,1318.37,"All four are real risks. Most groups pick the low bids. But look at the timeline. It is the risk you control least, because it is already running. Every week without a decision makes it harder."],[44,1319.32,1344.94,"Now the exercise: the Nordvia cleaning re-tender, about 15 minutes, in Module 2.2 of your workbook. Choose the lot structure and the term. Choose the specification type. Set the quality and price weights. Tick the safeguards you would build in. And plan back from June 2027: when must the RFP be issued, when must the award be made, and how long does mobilisation take?"],[44,1345.44,1370.19,"A hint for the timeline. A standard tender like this needs nine to twelve months before expiry, plus mobilisation, which for cleaning with staff transfer can take two to three months. Start from June 2027 and work backwards. If your own project is a site service, add a line on how you would adapt the design. Pause the lesson now to do the exercise, and continue when you are ready."],[44,1370.69,1392.75,"While you work, think about who must be involved. Facilities owns the service. Legal must confirm the staff transfer rules in each country. HR may need to inform employee representatives. And the business owner must agree the weights before issue. Each of these adds time to the plan, so put them in your timeline as real steps, not as footnotes."],[45,1393.7,1422.66,"Let us debrief. One lot or one per country, and why? The expected answer is one lot per country, with an option for bidders to offer a bundle discount for both. That keeps local specialists in the game and still lets a strong regional provider show its value. Who chose output, and who chose hybrid? Hybrid is the usual best fit, with prescribed methods for server rooms and archives. What weight did you give quality? At least 50 percent."],[45,1423.16,1446.28,"And the hard one: is there still time before June 2027, and what is plan B? If the timeline is too tight, plan B is a short bridge extension with the current cleaners. But it must be decided, documented and limited in time, not rolled over by default. A decided bridge is a strategy; a silent roll-over is the problem we started with."],[46,1447.23,1467.18,"Module 2.2 in three lines. One supplier per site or cluster, chosen by tender, three plus one plus one. A hybrid specification, quality at least 50 percent, and a should-cost floor. And staff transfer, indexation and KPIs belong in the strategy, not only in the contract."],[46,1467.68,1503.04,"To recap: for site services, competition happens once per cycle, so the tender must be designed well. One accountable supplier per site, a term that recovers mobilisation, options earned by performance, a hybrid specification, quality weights fixed in advance, a should-cost floor that protects against abnormally low bids, and early attention to staff transfer and indexation. In the next lesson we look at the opposite case, where several suppliers are the right answer: a pool of preferred suppliers for professional and IT services."]],"questions":[{"after":29,"q":"To keep tension during the term, a Facilities manager proposes splitting the Nordvia HQ building between two cleaning contractors, one per half. What is the strongest objection?","options":["It breaks the Acquired Rights Directive","Coupa cannot handle two lots for one site","It adds interfaces and excuses without adding competition during the term, and loses the density that lowers cost","Two suppliers always cost exactly double"],"answer":2,"explain":"Splitting a site gives two excuses and two sets of governance, while neither supplier is really competing during the term. One accountable supplier with bundled hours gives better supervision and price.","t":210.38},{"after":32,"q":"Your current security contract has two one-year options that were always exercised automatically. What change makes the options real leverage?","options":["Make each extension conditional on KPI performance and put the option and notice dates in CLM","Shorten the base term to one year","Add a third option","Remove the options and sign for five years"],"answer":0,"explain":"Options are leverage only if extension depends on performance and a decision is actually taken in time. A condition in the contract and dates in the contract management system turn a roll-over into a choice.","t":445.35},{"after":34,"q":"For Nordvia's archive room, which holds confidential policyholder files, which approach to the cleaning specification fits best?","options":["Output standard only, the supplier decides how","Input specification for the whole building","Leave the archive out of the contract","Prescribed method and vetted staff only, inside a hybrid specification"],"answer":3,"explain":"Where we need control, as with confidential records or server rooms, a hybrid specification prescribes the method and the staff vetting, while other areas are specified by outputs.","t":622.4},{"after":36,"q":"Illustrative numbers: your should-cost floor for a site is about 260,000 euros a year, and one bid comes in at 200,000 euros. What should you do?","options":["Accept it, it is a 23 percent saving","Ask the bidder to explain the abnormally low price, check hours and wages, and document the outcome","Reject it without asking, it must be a mistake","Use it as the starting price for an e-auction"],"answer":1,"explain":"A bid below the floor usually means fewer hours, wages below the agreement or unpaid subcontractors. Ask for an explanation and document it, as public guidance recommends.","t":805.66},{"after":38,"q":"A business owner expects 20 percent savings from re-tendering cleaning, where the whole on-site team will transfer to the new contractor under national rules. How do you respond?","options":["Agree, competition always brings 20 percent","Exclude staff transfer from the RFP to protect the savings","Explain that wages transfer with the people, so savings come mainly from method and management, and set a realistic target","Ask bidders to price without the transferring staff"],"answer":2,"explain":"When staff transfer, wage costs follow them. Realistic savings come from better methods, routes and supervision, and staff data must be in the RFP so bidders can price correctly.","t":960.35},{"after":40,"q":"A colleague suggests a price-only reverse auction on the open market to re-tender Nordvia's security services. What is the best advice?","options":["Do not run a price-only open auction; if any auction is used, only among bidders above a quality threshold and the should-cost floor, with rules disclosed","Good idea, auctions are fastest","Run the auction first, then evaluate quality of the winner","Auctions are fine as long as there are three bidders"],"answer":0,"explain":"A price-only auction pushes bidders to cut hours or wages. In site services, any auction comes after quality, above the floor and with rules known in advance; most teams prefer a negotiation round or best and final offer.","t":1114.07},{"after":42,"q":"Nordvia wants to allow one bidder to win country A and a different bidder to win country B in the cleaning RFP in Coupa. How must the event be built?","options":["One lot containing both countries","Two separate RFP events with different weights","One lot, then split the award by email","Separate lots per country, because items in a lot are awarded together"],"answer":3,"explain":"In Coupa a lot is awarded together. To award countries separately, each country must be its own lot, optionally with a bundle offer allowed.","t":1286.11},{"after":45,"q":"It is now clear the P1 cleaning tender cannot be awarded and mobilised before June 2027. What is the right plan B?","options":["Let the current contracts roll over again","A short bridge extension, decided, documented and limited in time","Award directly to the cheapest current cleaner for five years","Cancel the tender and run three quotes"],"answer":1,"explain":"A bridge extension can be sound if it is a decision with an end date. A silent roll-over is the very habit the strategy is meant to break.","t":1446.28}],"cues":[[0.6,1.35,"Welcome back."],[1.35,5.5,"This lesson covers module 2.2: one supplier, chosen by tender."],[5.5,14.91,"We will look at cleaning, security, reception and soft facilities management, the services that keep our buildings running every day."],[15.19,22.98,"In the last lesson you learned the six decisions and the difference between competition for the market and competition in the market."],[22.98,25.56,"Now we take the first pattern in depth."],[25.84,40.14,"By the end of this lesson you will be able to design the tender strategy for a site service: the lot structure, the term, the type of specification, the quality and price weights, and the safeguards that protect both the service and our name."],[40.42,46.41,"We will work with two Nordvia projects, P1 office cleaning and P6 security and reception."],[47.36,49.88,"Here is the core idea of the module."],[49.88,56.23,"For cleaning and security, competition happens once every three years or so, for the whole contract."],[56.23,57.79,"Make that moment count."],[58.29,59.88,"Think about what that means."],[59.88,64.2,"During the term, there is no other cleaner waiting on the same floor."],[64.2,67.97,"The supplier knows it has the contract until the next tender."],[67.97,72.71,"So all the competitive tension we will ever get is concentrated in one event."],[72.99,82.38,"After that, our leverage comes from two things only: the KPIs we wrote into the contract, and the options to extend that the supplier must earn."],[82.38,89.65,"Everything in this module is about designing that one competitive moment well, and then holding one supplier to account."],[89.93,93.86,"If the tender is weak, we live with the result for three to five years."],[94.36,99.44,"Let me give you a picture of what a weak competitive moment looks like."],[99.44,107.96,"A facilities team, short of time, sends a two page request to four local cleaners with a floor plan and asks for a monthly price."],[107.96,111.13,"No staff data, no quality criteria, no audit regime."],[111.13,112.54,"The cheapest wins."],[112.82,119.87,"Six months later the complaints start, and the buyer discovers there is nothing in the contract to measure against."],[119.87,120.83,"The tender took two weeks."],[120.83,123.39,"The consequences last three years."],[123.39,130.35,"Compare that with a tender where the hours, the standards, the KPIs and the transfer data were all on the table."],[130.63,135.2,"The extra effort at the start is small compared with the three years it protects."],[136.15,139.17,"Why one supplier per site or per cluster of sites?"],[139.17,140.86,"Four reasons on the cards."],[141.36,143.08,"First, accountability."],[143.08,146.16,"One provider answers for the result in a building."],[146.16,151.18,"If the meeting rooms are dirty on Monday morning, there is one number to call."],[151.18,152.37,"Second, leverage."],[152.37,159.92,"Labour is most of the cost in these services, so bundled hours mean better routes, better supervision and a better price."],[160.2,165.76,"A supplier with forty hours a night in one building can afford a dedicated supervisor."],[165.76,168.82,"Two suppliers with twenty hours each cannot."],[169.32,171.1,"Third, mobilisation."],[171.1,179.79,"Each change of supplier costs recruitment, training, perhaps a staff transfer, and almost always a dip in service for a few weeks."],[179.79,185.54,"Ellram would call these post-transaction costs, and they belong in the total cost of ownership."],[185.54,187.54,"Fourth, governance."],[187.54,190.42,"One set of KPIs, one review meeting, one invoice."],[190.92,195.46,"Some buyers split a site between two cleaners to keep them competing."],[195.46,197.3,"It sounds clever, but it does not work."],[197.3,200.62,"Two cleaners on one floor means two excuses."],[200.62,204.68,"When the kitchen is not cleaned, each says it belonged to the other."],[204.68,210.38,"Splitting a site does not add competitive tension during the term; it only adds interfaces."],[211.33,219.43,"This table is the playbook for site services, with cleaning in the middle column and security and reception on the right."],[219.43,221.53,"The terms are common practice, not norms."],[222.02,222.81,"Position."],[222.81,229.17,"Cleaning is a classic leverage category: many suppliers, a clear service, real buying power."],[229.17,232.28,"Security moves from leverage towards bottleneck."],[232.28,233.19,"Why?"],[233.19,235.44,"Because of licensing and vetting."],[235.44,242.86,"Guards need licences, background checks and training, so fewer providers qualify, and the cost of a failure is higher."],[243.14,246.17,"That is why the quality weighting goes up for security."],[246.68,247.65,"Suppliers."],[247.65,250.39,"For cleaning, one per region or cluster."],[250.39,254.5,"For security, one provider, possibly combined with reception."],[254.5,255.46,"Tool."],[255.46,258.05,"Cleaning uses an RFP with site visits and staff data."],[258.05,266.66,"Security uses an RFP with presentations and references, because you want to meet the people who will run your front desk."],[266.66,267.65,"Price model."],[267.93,270.46,"Cleaning is a fixed fee plus unit rates."],[270.46,277.89,"Security is hourly rates by role, such as guard, supervisor or receptionist, plus a management fee."],[277.89,284.85,"And the term: three years plus one plus one for cleaning, and either the same or three plus two for security."],[285.34,294.27,"Notice that every row of this table is one of the six decisions from the last lesson, applied to site services."],[294.27,295.44,"Position comes from Session 1."],[295.44,297.34,"Suppliers is decision one."],[297.34,298.53,"Tool is decision five."],[298.53,300.69,"Price model is decision four."],[300.69,302.49,"Term is decision six."],[302.77,314.36,"So the playbook is not a new framework; it is the six decisions answered once for a whole family of categories, which saves you time every time one of these contracts comes up for renewal."],[315.31,317.07,"What do public tenders show?"],[317.07,326.02,"Public buyers publish their contracts, so they give us useful reference points, even though we as a private insurer are not bound by their rules."],[326.52,339.41,"First example: a UK university cleaning contract from 2024, with a term of three years plus two options of twelve months each, and a 50 50 split between quality and price."],[339.41,349.65,"Second: an integrated facilities management call-off by HMRC, the UK tax authority, also 2024, with five years plus three plus one plus one."],[349.93,352.24,"That is a much longer horizon."],[352.24,362.21,"Third: the EU public procurement directive of 2014, in Article 33, sets a maximum of four years for frameworks as a benchmark."],[362.71,364.15,"The pattern is clear."],[364.15,371.35,"The larger and more integrated the scope, the longer the term, because mobilisation and investment are larger."],[371.35,373.94,"Office cleaning sits at the short end."],[373.94,379.46,"If someone proposes a seven year cleaning contract, ask what investment would justify it."],[380.41,382.44,"So why three plus one plus one?"],[382.44,383.8,"Let us take each card."],[384.3,385.65,"The three year base."],[385.65,395.14,"It lets the supplier recover its mobilisation: equipment, training, and the set-up of the computer aided facilities management system, the CAFM."],[395.14,398.16,"And it lets us recover our own switching cost."],[398.16,402.22,"The first plus one is earned by performance against the KPIs."],[402.5,407.82,"The second plus one is earned again, which keeps tension until the very end of the contract."],[407.82,411.8,"And then we re-tender, and competition for the market starts again."],[412.3,414.16,"Here is the important part."],[414.16,418.61,"Options are leverage only if extension depends on performance."],[418.61,429.03,"Write that condition into the contract: for example, the option may be exercised only if the average audit score over the last twelve months is above an agreed level."],[429.31,436.99,"And put the option dates and the notice dates into the contract management system, so that a decision is taken in time."],[436.99,445.35,"An option that nobody remembers becomes a roll-over, and a roll-over is exactly what Nordvia has had in cleaning since 2019."],[446.3,447.99,"Now the specification."],[447.99,451.63,"Do we describe inputs, outputs, or a hybrid?"],[451.63,461.28,"This table draws on the CMS guidance by Hanson from 2000 and the outcome-based cleaning guide of Singapore's National Environment Agency."],[461.78,466.15,"An input specification describes hours, frequencies and headcount."],[466.15,471.53,"For example: two cleaners, four hours a night, vacuum every office daily."],[471.53,475.07,"Its strength is that it is easy to audit and compare."],[475.07,481.83,"Its risk is that you pay for hours, not results, and there is no room for the supplier to propose a better method."],[482.33,487.88,"An output specification describes results: cleanliness standards, response times."],[487.88,495.68,"For example: no visible dust on desks, washrooms restocked at all times, spills handled within thirty minutes."],[495.68,499.42,"The supplier proposes the method, often at lower cost."],[499.7,506.01,"The risk is that it is harder to draft and harder to assess, because bids are no longer directly comparable."],[506.51,512.13,"The hybrid specifies outputs for most areas and prescribes methods where we need control."],[512.13,515.75,"It is usually the best fit, but it needs clear boundaries."],[516.03,530.53,"Security is a good example of hybrid by design: you prescribe the procedures, the vetting and the licences, because those are non-negotiable, and you specify outputs everywhere else, such as response times to an alarm."],[531.04,533.11,"How do you choose in practice?"],[533.11,535.36,"Ask two questions per area of the building."],[535.36,539.39,"First, do we care how it is done, or only that it is done?"],[539.39,544.79,"Second, could we measure the result objectively, with an audit or a sensor or a complaint log?"],[544.79,549.96,"If we only care about the result and we can measure it, use an output standard."],[550.24,556.19,"If we must control the method, for safety, confidentiality or regulation, prescribe it."],[556.19,561.76,"Most buildings end up with a mix, and that is why the hybrid is the usual best fit."],[562.71,566.13,"Here is a hybrid specification for Nordvia HQ."],[566.13,568.11,"Fictional, but realistic."],[568.61,573.27,"Offices and meeting rooms: an output standard, audited monthly."],[573.27,582.16,"Washrooms: an output standard plus a minimum check frequency, because a washroom that is clean at 8 and dirty at 11 is a complaint."],[582.44,593.32,"Server rooms and archives: a prescribed method and vetted staff only, because these rooms contain equipment and confidential policyholder records."],[593.32,595.19,"Complaints: a response time as a KPI."],[595.19,600.12,"And the supplier proposes the method and hours, priced per site."],[600.62,604.18,"Remember that HQ has quality complaints in the case."],[604.18,607.95,"An output standard lets us measure what the complaints are about."],[607.95,613.29,"Today, with an old input contract, the cleaner can say: we did our four hours."],[613.57,622.4,"With an output standard and a monthly audit, the conversation changes to: the standard was not met in these rooms, and here are the scores."],[623.35,625.19,"Quality and price weighting."],[625.19,626.64,"On the left, the weights."],[626.64,630.28,"For site services, quality should carry at least 50 percent."],[630.28,637.28,"The weights are fixed and published before the tender is issued, never adjusted after you see the bids."],[637.56,646.18,"And price is scored on the total annual cost, including the fixed fee and the expected unit-rate extras, not only on an hourly rate."],[646.68,648.92,"On the right, the quality criteria."],[648.92,650.99,"The method and staffing plan."],[650.99,654.21,"The mobilisation and staff transfer plan."],[654.21,657.18,"The KPIs and reporting the supplier offers."],[657.18,668.7,"And fair labour and ESG, environmental, social and governance criteria, which matter a lot in a sector where low wages and subcontracting chains are a known risk."],[668.98,675.07,"Session 3 builds the full evaluation model and tests how sensitive the ranking is to the weights."],[675.07,682.59,"Today we only set the principle in the strategy, so that the business owner agrees it before anyone has seen a price."],[683.09,685.41,"Why at least 50 percent for quality?"],[685.41,698.13,"Because in a labour service, the price differences between serious bidders are often small, while the differences in management, supervision and mobilisation are large, and they decide whether the service works."],[698.41,706.05,"A weighting of, say, 30 percent quality would let a weak bidder win on price alone, and we would pay for it in complaints and re-work."],[707.0,711.11,"Now one of the most useful tools in this module: the should-cost floor."],[711.11,712.21,"Five steps."],[712.7,713.24,"First, hours."],[713.24,717.97,"From the specification, estimate the hours needed per site."],[717.97,723.79,"Second, multiply by the collectively agreed wage, the legal minimum for that work."],[723.79,729.06,"Third, add on-costs: statutory social charges, holidays, sickness cover."],[729.06,732.5,"Fourth, add supervision, consumables and equipment."],[732.78,735.51,"Fifth, add a reasonable overhead and margin."],[736.01,741.61,"Let me make this concrete with illustrative numbers, not real ones."],[741.61,745.79,"Suppose a site needs 10,000 cleaning hours a year."],[745.79,751.38,"Suppose the agreed wage is 15 euros an hour, and on-costs add about 40 percent, so 21 euros."],[751.38,755.57,"That is already 210,000 euros of pure labour cost."],[755.85,767.46,"Add supervision, materials and equipment, say another 30,000 euros, and a modest overhead and margin, and the floor lands somewhere around 260,000 euros."],[767.46,769.86,"Now a bid comes in at 200,000 euros."],[769.86,771.75,"How can that be paid?"],[771.75,775.76,"Labour is most of the cost in cleaning and security."],[776.04,786.0,"A bid below this floor cannot be paid without breaking a rule somewhere: fewer hours than promised, wages below the agreement, or an unpaid subcontractor."],[786.5,790.34,"The floor is not a price you publish, and it is not a target."],[790.34,797.42,"It is an internal reference you build before the bids arrive, so that you recognise an impossible price when you see one."],[797.42,805.66,"Facilities and HR colleagues can usually help with the hours and the wage tables, and the current invoices give you a sanity check."],[806.61,808.4,"Which brings us to this statement."],[808.4,811.47,"A bid below the wage floor is not a saving."],[811.47,816.19,"It is a future service failure, or a legal exposure, with our name on it."],[816.68,819.19,"In an insurance group, the name matters."],[819.19,828.12,"If a cleaning subcontractor at our headquarters is found paying below the legal wage, the headline will not mention the subcontractor."],[828.12,829.5,"It will mention us."],[829.5,834.02,"So the rule is: ask for an explanation of every abnormally low bid, and document it."],[834.3,839.8,"Public guidance from the UK Crown Commercial Service asks buyers to do exactly that."],[839.8,842.13,"Be honest about the evidence, though."],[842.13,848.33,"No study quantifies how often auction-driven or low-bid awards lead to failures in cleaning."],[848.33,851.72,"Present it as risk logic, not as a statistic."],[851.72,854.31,"The logic is strong enough on its own."],[855.27,859.19,"Staff transfer changes the economics of the whole tender."],[859.19,859.77,"Four cards."],[860.27,861.06,"The rule."],[861.06,872.75,"The EU Acquired Rights Directive of 2001 protects employees when an undertaking or a business transfers to a new employer, and national rules differ in how they apply it."],[872.75,873.94,"The case law."],[874.22,884.61,"In the S\u00fczen case, the European Court of Justice held that in labour-intensive services, a transfer can occur if a major part of the workforce moves to the new contractor."],[884.61,886.04,"National rules."],[886.32,894.52,"In the UK the TUPE regulations go further, and in some countries collective agreements require the new contractor to take over the staff."],[895.02,897.79,"In the RFP, this means three things."],[897.79,904.62,"Provide staff data so bidders can price correctly: headcount, hours, wages, length of service."],[904.62,905.31,"Expect realistic savings."],[905.31,907.77,"And allow enough mobilisation time."],[907.77,916.74,"When staff transfer, the savings come from method and management, not from wages, because the wages travel with the people."],[917.02,925.93,"If a business owner expects 20 percent savings from a cleaning tender where the whole team transfers, that expectation needs to be corrected early."],[925.93,929.11,"Check national law with Legal for each country."],[929.61,932.37,"Staff transfer also affects the bidders."],[932.37,939.51,"A new contractor taking over a team inherits its contracts, its length of service and sometimes its pension terms."],[939.51,947.74,"If the RFP is silent, cautious bidders add a risk premium, and reckless ones ignore the cost and get into trouble later."],[947.74,949.0,"Neither helps us."],[949.28,960.35,"Clear staff data, given under a confidentiality agreement and in line with GDPR, the General Data Protection Regulation, lets every bidder price the same reality."],[961.3,963.03,"Price, indexation and KPIs."],[963.03,965.23,"Let us go down the table."],[965.73,968.91,"The base price is a fixed monthly fee per site."],[968.91,976.53,"Variations, like extra cleaning after an event or a deep clean, are priced at unit rates agreed in the tender."],[976.53,984.38,"Indexation happens once a year, by a formula linked to a wage index, so that both sides know in advance what the change will be."],[984.66,989.35,"The KPIs are the audit score, the response time and the complaints."],[989.35,996.03,"And service credits are linked to those KPIs, and capped, so they motivate without becoming a penalty war."],[996.52,999.73,"Now look at Nordvia P6, security and reception."],[999.73,1004.42,"Hourly rates were indexed twice in two years, and there are no KPIs."],[1004.42,1008.04,"These are strategy failures, not contract details."],[1008.04,1014.87,"Without an indexation formula, every increase is a negotiation the supplier starts when it suits them."],[1015.15,1020.06,"Without KPIs, there is no basis for the options and no way to reward good work."],[1020.06,1026.11,"Both problems should have been solved in the strategy, before the last contract was signed."],[1026.62,1028.47,"A word on service credits."],[1028.47,1037.18,"They are not a source of income, and a supplier that pays service credits every month is a supplier you should be replacing, not billing."],[1037.18,1041.0,"Their purpose is to make the KPIs matter in the monthly review."],[1041.28,1051.65,"Keep the cap modest, typically a small share of the monthly fee, as an illustration, and link repeated failure to the options rather than to ever larger credits."],[1051.65,1056.17,"The real reward for good performance is the next year of the contract."],[1057.12,1059.85,"What about an e-auction for cleaning?"],[1059.85,1061.11,"On the left, the never column."],[1061.11,1064.28,"Never a price-only auction on the open market."],[1064.28,1066.92,"Never before quality has been evaluated."],[1066.92,1069.17,"And never without a should-cost floor."],[1069.67,1072.18,"On the right, possible, with care."],[1072.18,1075.95,"Only among bidders who are already above a quality threshold."],[1075.95,1078.2,"Only above the should-cost floor."],[1078.2,1081.33,"And only with the rules disclosed in advance."],[1081.33,1086.31,"The research brief rates the fit of e-negotiation for these services as medium."],[1086.59,1093.84,"In practice most teams prefer a negotiation round or a best and final offer after the quality evaluation."],[1093.84,1094.62,"Why so cautious?"],[1094.62,1102.68,"Because an auction pushes bidders to cut what is easiest to cut in a labour service, and that is hours or wages."],[1102.96,1109.28,"Remember what Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis found: auctions suit simple, fully specified work."],[1109.28,1114.07,"A site service with transfer issues and output standards is not that."],[1115.02,1118.8,"Let us apply this to P6, security and reception."],[1118.8,1125.36,"1.6 million euros a year, one provider, expiry December 2027."],[1125.36,1129.48,"Hourly rates indexed twice in two years, no KPIs."],[1129.98,1136.44,"The route: one supplier by RFP, three plus one plus one, with a hybrid specification."],[1136.44,1144.68,"A strong weight on vetting, licences, training and response times, because security is moving towards the bottleneck corner."],[1144.68,1148.71,"Indexation by formula, and KPIs with service credits."],[1149.21,1151.96,"Same pattern as cleaning, different weights."],[1151.96,1158.61,"Here is a question to think about: could P1 and P6 be one soft facilities management tender?"],[1158.61,1164.1,"Possibly, per country, if the market in that country offers providers who do both well."],[1164.1,1167.51,"Bundling can give leverage and one point of contact."],[1167.79,1176.05,"But it can also exclude strong specialists, and a weak security partner bundled with a good cleaner is still a weak security partner."],[1176.05,1180.72,"If you bundle, consider allowing bids per service as well as for the bundle."],[1181.23,1182.92,"Look also at the timing."],[1182.92,1186.81,"P6 expires in December 2027, six months after P1."],[1186.81,1195.15,"If both are re-tendered together, the timeline is set by the earlier date, June 2027 for cleaning."],[1195.15,1198.77,"That may be too tight for a combined tender."],[1199.05,1208.17,"One way out is to align the two contracts: a decided short bridge for one of them, so that both can go to market as one soft facilities event."],[1208.17,1212.89,"This is exactly the kind of choice the strategy paper should make explicit."],[1213.84,1215.76,"How does this look in Coupa?"],[1215.76,1216.52,"Four cards."],[1216.52,1219.32,"The event: an RFP with one lot per country."],[1219.32,1224.0,"In a Coupa lot, the items are awarded together, so a lot is the unit you award."],[1224.0,1233.57,"The content: questionnaires for quality, a pricing template with hours, and the draft contract attached, so bidders price the real terms."],[1233.85,1241.12,"Questions and answers: use the Message Center for site visit questions, so every bidder sees the same answers."],[1241.12,1247.1,"And the contract: in contract lifecycle management, with option dates and notice alerts."],[1247.6,1249.17,"The lot rule matters."],[1249.17,1255.28,"If Nordvia wants to allow one bidder to win one country only, the countries must be separate lots."],[1255.28,1259.54,"If both countries sit in one lot, you can only award them together."],[1259.54,1267.23,"Weighted scoring set-up and sealed opening of bids are features to confirm in your Coupa tenant, because configurations differ."],[1267.72,1268.89,"One practical tip."],[1268.89,1280.82,"Attach the strategy paper to the sourcing project in Coupa before you create the event, so that anyone who later reviews the file can see why the lots, the weights and the term were chosen."],[1280.82,1286.11,"How the paper links to the event depends on your configuration, so check it in your tenant."],[1287.06,1289.03,"Here is a poll to reflect on."],[1289.03,1293.45,"For Nordvia P1 cleaning, what is the main risk in the re-tender?"],[1293.45,1303.51,"Too few bidders; abnormally low bids below the wage floor; staff transfer costs nobody priced; or the timeline to June 2027."],[1303.51,1305.73,"Choose your answer before I continue."],[1306.23,1307.93,"All four are real risks."],[1307.93,1309.46,"Most groups pick the low bids."],[1309.46,1311.0,"But look at the timeline."],[1311.0,1315.89,"It is the risk you control least, because it is already running."],[1315.89,1318.37,"Every week without a decision makes it harder."],[1319.32,1325.88,"Now the exercise: the Nordvia cleaning re-tender, about 15 minutes, in Module 2.2 of your workbook."],[1325.88,1328.2,"Choose the lot structure and the term."],[1328.2,1330.57,"Choose the specification type."],[1330.57,1332.69,"Set the quality and price weights."],[1332.69,1335.34,"Tick the safeguards you would build in."],[1335.62,1344.94,"And plan back from June 2027: when must the RFP be issued, when must the award be made, and how long does mobilisation take?"],[1345.44,1347.08,"A hint for the timeline."],[1347.08,1356.91,"A standard tender like this needs nine to twelve months before expiry, plus mobilisation, which for cleaning with staff transfer can take two to three months."],[1356.91,1360.1,"Start from June 2027 and work backwards."],[1360.1,1365.71,"If your own project is a site service, add a line on how you would adapt the design."],[1365.99,1370.19,"Pause the lesson now to do the exercise, and continue when you are ready."],[1370.69,1374.29,"While you work, think about who must be involved."],[1374.29,1375.59,"Facilities owns the service."],[1375.59,1379.73,"Legal must confirm the staff transfer rules in each country."],[1379.73,1382.34,"HR may need to inform employee representatives."],[1382.34,1386.63,"And the business owner must agree the weights before issue."],[1386.91,1392.75,"Each of these adds time to the plan, so put them in your timeline as real steps, not as footnotes."],[1393.7,1394.9,"Let us debrief."],[1394.9,1396.9,"One lot or one per country, and why?"],[1396.9,1404.59,"The expected answer is one lot per country, with an option for bidders to offer a bundle discount for both."],[1404.59,1410.82,"That keeps local specialists in the game and still lets a strong regional provider show its value."],[1410.82,1413.45,"Who chose output, and who chose hybrid?"],[1413.73,1419.24,"Hybrid is the usual best fit, with prescribed methods for server rooms and archives."],[1419.24,1421.19,"What weight did you give quality?"],[1421.19,1422.65,"At least 50 percent."],[1423.16,1428.08,"And the hard one: is there still time before June 2027, and what is plan B?"],[1428.08,1434.9,"If the timeline is too tight, plan B is a short bridge extension with the current cleaners."],[1434.9,1440.5,"But it must be decided, documented and limited in time, not rolled over by default."],[1440.78,1446.29,"A decided bridge is a strategy; a silent roll-over is the problem we started with."],[1447.23,1448.84,"Module 2.2 in three lines."],[1448.84,1454.76,"One supplier per site or cluster, chosen by tender, three plus one plus one."],[1454.76,1460.09,"A hybrid specification, quality at least 50 percent, and a should-cost floor."],[1460.09,1467.18,"And staff transfer, indexation and KPIs belong in the strategy, not only in the contract."],[1467.68,1474.27,"To recap: for site services, competition happens once per cycle, so the tender must be designed well."],[1474.55,1493.05,"One accountable supplier per site, a term that recovers mobilisation, options earned by performance, a hybrid specification, quality weights fixed in advance, a should-cost floor that protects against abnormally low bids, and early attention to staff transfer and indexation."],[1493.33,1503.03,"In the next lesson we look at the opposite case, where several suppliers are the right answer: a pool of preferred suppliers for professional and IT services."]]},{"id":"s2-l3","session":"s2","title":"A pool of preferred suppliers","summary":"Design a panel of preferred suppliers for professional and IT services, with lots, panel size, a rate card, call-off rules and the labour-law and DORA safeguards built in once.","from":47,"to":69,"audio":"audio/s2-l3.mp3","duration":1537.7,"segs":[[47,0.6,46.96,"Welcome back. This lesson covers module 2.3: a pool of preferred suppliers, for professional services, consulting and IT services. It is the longest module of the session, because it carries the most common indirect categories in an insurer. In the last lesson we saw competition for the market: one supplier, chosen once per cycle. Now we look at the opposite case, competition in the market, where several suppliers are the right answer. By the end of this lesson you will be able to design a panel: its lots, the number of firms per lot, the rate card, the call-off rules, the pricing model per assignment, and the labour-law and DORA safeguards. Our case is Nordvia P2, IT consulting and project staff."],[48,47.91,64.87,"Here is the starting point. Professional and IT services arrive as a stream of different assignments. No single firm is best at all of them, and no RFP per assignment is fast enough. The answer is a panel: terms fixed once, competition every time."],[48,65.37,101.17,"Contrast this with cleaning. There, one continuous service: the same building, every night, for three years. Here, many different jobs. A data migration in March, a security architecture review in May, two testers for a release in September, a regulatory reporting project in the autumn. Each needs different skills, and each has its own deadline. That single difference, how demand arrives, changes the whole strategy. One supplier would be strong in some jobs and weak in others. A full tender for each job would take weeks, and the business would not wait."],[48,101.67,120.04,"This is also where van Weele's point about purchasing as a business function becomes very practical. The business does not want a procurement process. It wants the right expert, quickly, at a fair price. A panel is the structure that delivers all three at once, and that is how you should explain it."],[49,120.99,148.53,"Let us look at Nordvia's IT consulting today. Fictional case data, but you may recognise the pattern. 6.1 million euros, spent with 12 firms, through 38 call-offs. 61 percent of that spend goes to the top three firms. 29 of the 38 call-offs were awarded without any competition. And day rates for the same senior profile range from 820 to 1,450 euros."],[49,149.03,176.96,"Let those numbers land. The category is fragmented and concentrated at the same time. Fragmented, because there are twelve firms and no common terms. Concentrated, because most of the work goes to three favourites, without competition. So Nordvia has the disadvantages of both: no leverage from volume, and no tension from competition. And the rate spread tells you there is no shared reference for what a senior consultant should cost."],[50,177.91,199.93,"Here is a poll to think about. Nordvia IT consulting: twelve firms, thirty eight call-offs, rates from 820 to 1,450 euros. What would you do first? Award everything to the cheapest firm; run an e-auction on day rates; build a panel by lot with a rate card and call-off rules; or leave it to IT. Choose your answer."],[50,200.43,221.76,"Most people pick the panel. If you were tempted by the auction, ask yourself: what would you be ranking? Is a senior architect from one firm the same product as a senior architect from another? A day rate is only the price of an input. Without knowing the person, the method and the deliverable, a lower rate can easily mean a higher total cost."],[51,222.71,251.54,"Now the Group picture, anonymised and rounded. IT services: about 80 million euros with some 350 suppliers. Consulting and audit: about 49 million euros with some 360 suppliers. Advisory firms: about 12 million euros in 9 entities, through 60 supplier records, and no Group agreement. And only about 5 percent of IT services spend sits with a supplier shared by two entities."],[51,252.04,278.24,"It is the same pattern as Nordvia, at Group scale. Many entities buy similar services from overlapping firms, each on its own terms. That suggests an approach: a panel can be local first and Group later. Design your entity's panel so that other entities can join, for example by using common lot definitions and role families in the rate card. Then the second entity does not start from zero."],[51,278.74,301.76,"Imagine two entities each running their own small consulting panel with different role names, one calling a level senior consultant and the other manager, at different ceilings. Joining them later means renegotiating everything. If both use the same role families and seniority definitions from the start, merging them into a Group panel becomes a simple step."],[52,302.71,306.57,"Three wrong answers and one right one. Let us go down the table."],[52,307.07,331.74,"One supplier for everything. What happens: dependency, capability gaps, and no tension. That firm becomes the default for work it is not good at. Open market every time. Weeks per RFP, so in practice people skip it and pick a favourite. That is how good intentions turn into 29 direct awards. No rules, which is today. The same profile at very different rates."],[52,332.24,362.53,"And the right answer: a panel by lot, with a rate card. The ceilings are fixed once, and three to six pre-qualified firms compete for each job. The third row is where most organisations are. And here is the argument for your business owner: the panel is not more bureaucracy. It is less work per assignment. The legal terms, the security checks, the rates and the regulatory clauses are agreed once. Each new job needs only a short statement of work and a quick competition."],[53,363.48,383.66,"Where does the design of a panel come from? The reference model is Article 33 of the EU public procurement directive of 2014, which governs framework agreements. It is a public-sector rule, and a private insurer is not bound by it. But it is a useful benchmark, and auditors recognise its logic."],[53,384.16,419.26,"It offers two call-off routes. Direct award, when all terms are already fixed in the framework. Then the choice of firm must follow objective criteria, such as a cascade, where the top ranked firm is asked first, or rotation, or capability. Second, mini-competition, when not all terms are fixed. Then you invite all capable members of the lot in writing, set a deadline, and keep bids closed until it. And third, a mix of both, as long as the rules are stated upfront in the framework. The directive also caps frameworks at four years."],[53,419.76,435.63,"Notice the principle underneath. Whether you award directly or run a competition, the rule is decided in advance and applied the same way to everyone. That is what makes a panel fair, and what protects you when someone asks later why a firm was chosen."],[54,436.58,449.68,"For the details, the UK Crown Commercial Service consultancy framework, called RM6187, is the most detailed public model of a consulting panel. Let me read the structure."],[54,450.18,474.93,"Nine lots, and each further competition runs within one lot. Maximum day rates that call-offs may not exceed. At least ten working days for simple requirements, so suppliers can respond properly. Quality weighted between 50 and 90 percent, price between 10 and 50 percent. Three evaluators and a consensus meeting. And no negotiation after bids, only clarification."],[54,475.43,497.08,"Take the structure; adapt the thresholds to your own delegation of authority. An insurer with five million euros of consulting will not need nine lots, and the deadlines can be shorter for small, simple jobs. But maximum rates, quality-led evaluation, more than one evaluator and no post-bid haggling are principles worth keeping."],[55,498.03,500.53,"Now let us design the panel in six steps."],[55,501.03,534.87,"Step one, lots, organised by capability and perhaps by size band. Step two, panel size: enough firms to compete, few enough to engage. Step three, the rate card: maximum day rates, discounts, expenses, and a cap on indexation. Step four, call-off rules: thresholds for direct award and for mini-competition. Step five, a call-off template: the statement of work, deliverables, acceptance criteria and named key people. Step six, governance: scorecards, share of wallet, refresh and exit."],[55,535.37,566.56,"Each step answers a typical failure. No lots, and generalists win everything, because a large firm can always bid for every job. No size rule, and you have twenty members who never get work. No rate card, and the 1,450 euro day continues. No call-off rules, and favourites win everything. No template, and every call-off is drafted from scratch, slowly. No governance, and the panel decays: the same firms, the same complacency, five years later."],[55,567.06,601.58,"Let me say a little more about step six, governance, because it is the step most often skipped. Once a quarter, look at a simple scorecard per firm: quality of delivery, rates achieved against the ceiling, and share of wallet, meaning the share of the lot's spend each firm received. If one firm takes eighty percent of a lot, ask why. Perhaps it is simply the best, which is fine, but then the other members need to know whether they have a real chance. Plan a refresh point too, for example at the end of the base term, where weak members can leave and new ones can join."],[56,602.53,609.95,"Here is an illustrative lot structure for an insurer's IT and professional services. Adapt it to your own demand."],[56,610.45,646.38,"IT delivery: projects and programmes, typically priced at a fixed price per milestone. Technology advisory: architecture, cloud and security, priced at a fixed price per deliverable. IT staff augmentation: developers, testers and analysts, at time and materials under the rate card. Regulatory and actuarial advisory: Solvency II, IFRS 17 and DORA work, at a fixed price or capped time and materials. And strategy and transformation: operating model and programme work, at a fixed price per phase."],[56,646.88,670.78,"Notice that the pricing model differs per lot. That is decision four of the six, applied per lot. Delivery work has milestones, so fixed price fits. Staff augmentation is about people, so time and materials fits. Ask yourself which lots your own demand would need. In most entities, two or three lots cover most of the spend. Do not create a lot that nobody will use."],[57,671.73,689.14,"How many firms per lot? Too few, one or two, and there is no real competition for each assignment. Too many, ten or more, and members get little work, so they stop investing in you. The practice range is three to six per lot. Treat that as common practice, not a verified norm."],[57,689.64,723.54,"Here is the test: can each member expect meaningful work in a year? Imagine a lot with about 1.5 million euros of annual demand, an illustrative figure, and ten firms. If the work is spread evenly, each firm gets 150,000 euros, perhaps two small jobs. A firm in that position will not put its best people forward, and will not invest in learning your systems. A panel member that never wins anything stops trying. Size the panel to the demand, not to the number of firms that want to be on it."],[58,724.49,734.81,"Is there evidence from insurance? The best-documented cases are claims legal panels, which are an analogue rather than indirect spend, but the logic transfers."],[58,735.31,769.56,"Zurich cut its England and Wales claims legal panel from 28 to 14 firms in 2004, and replaced five separate panels with one central system. The Co-operative Insurance Society planned to go from 27 firms to possibly six, in 2005. The reasons cited, as reported by the trade journal The Lawyer, were unequal work distribution and quality. So, fewer firms, more work for each, more leverage and better quality. Exactly the argument we make for IT and consulting panels."],[59,770.51,802.64,"Now the rate card structure. Roles: role families per lot, for example developer, architect, project manager, business analyst. Seniority: junior, consultant, senior, manager, principal, with a short description of each level so firms cannot label a junior as a senior. Price: a maximum day rate, and call-offs may bid lower. Volume: discount tiers based on annual spend. Expenses: a fixed policy, or included in the rate. Indexation: an annual cap."],[59,803.14,828.95,"Why maximum rates and not fixed rates? Because the mini-competition can still bring the price down. The ceiling stops the 1,450 euro day. The competition finds the right price below the ceiling for each job. And the seniority definitions matter as much as the numbers. A rate card without them invites grade inflation, where the same person is billed one level higher on the next job."],[60,829.9,835.85,"The call-off rules. These thresholds are illustrative; align them to your delegation of authority."],[60,836.35,867.7,"Below 50,000 euros: direct award, at or below the rate card, with a written rationale. From 50,000 to 250,000 euros: a mini-competition, with at least three members invited. Above 250,000 euros, or for strategic work: a mini-competition with presentations, or a full RFP. Urgent needs: direct award, reviewed after the fact. And follow-on work: direct award if justified, and benchmarked against the rate card."],[60,868.2,896.21,"The 50,000 euro line echoes the Group's size bands. But the rule that matters most is this one: every direct award has a written rationale. Two or three sentences. Why this firm, why now, why no competition. That small habit changes behaviour, because people choose differently when they know they will have to write the reason down. And the after-the-fact review of urgent awards stops urgent from becoming the normal route."],[60,896.71,938.19,"Let us test the rules with a story. A Nordvia business analyst needs a data protection specialist for six weeks, estimated at about 40,000 euros. That is below the threshold, so a direct award at or below the rate card is allowed, with a short written rationale. Three months later the same firm is asked to extend the work, and the total now reaches 120,000 euros. Is that still a direct award? Only as justified follow-on work, benchmarked against the rate card, and the rationale must explain why a competition would not give a better result. Without that rule, a series of small awards quietly becomes a large one."],[61,939.14,974.13,"Fixed price or time and materials? On the left, fixed price per deliverable. It is the default for defined work. The supplier carries the delivery risk. And it needs a clear statement of work and acceptance criteria. On the right, capped or pure time and materials. Capped time and materials when the scope is partly clear. Pure time and materials only for discovery. And payment by results only if the supplier really controls the levers. This follows the three pricing models of the UK Consultancy Playbook from 2022."],[61,974.63,999.61,"Most of Nordvia's 38 call-offs were probably time and materials. Moving defined work to fixed price is a saving and a control at the same time. A saving, because the firm's own inefficiency is no longer our cost. A control, because we pay for accepted deliverables, not for days spent. The call-off template should make fixed price the default and require a reason for anything else."],[62,1000.56,1033.76,"Now an insurer-specific trap: labour law in staff augmentation. On the left, a service contract. The supplier owns the outcome and directs its own people. Fixed price deliverables fit well here. On the right, temporary agency work. We direct the people day to day. Then it must be labelled and licensed as agency work, and limits apply. In Germany, for example, the law on temporary agency work, the A\u00dcG, limits the assignment of the same worker to the same client to 18 consecutive months."],[62,1034.26,1064.64,"The danger is the grey zone in between. A contract called a service contract, but in practice our managers give daily instructions to the supplier's developers, who sit in our teams, use our tools and work our hours. That is a sham service contract, and it can create an employment relationship with the client. So this is a strategy decision, not a contract detail. Choose the model per lot and design the statement of work accordingly. Verify the current national law with Legal."],[62,1065.14,1083.06,"A simple way to decide per lot is to ask who gives the instructions. If the supplier's lead plans and directs the work and delivers an agreed result, it is a service. If our own team leader decides each day what the person does, it is labour supply, and it must be contracted as such."],[63,1084.01,1114.43,"The second trap: DORA and the IT panel. Four cards. Scope: IT services with access to our systems are likely to be ICT services under DORA. Register: every ICT arrangement goes into the register of information. Clauses: the contractual provisions of Article 30 go into the framework, so every call-off inherits them. Assessment: the pre-contract assessment required by Article 28, paragraph 4, is done at panel level."],[63,1114.93,1143.76,"Think about what that means for Nordvia. Today, 38 call-offs with 12 firms. Under DORA, each of those arrangements may need the right clauses, a register entry and a pre-contract assessment. Done one by one, that is a heavy load and a real compliance risk. Done once, at framework level, every call-off inherits the work. That is a strong argument for the panel when you talk to the CIO: it is not only cheaper, it makes DORA compliance manageable."],[63,1144.26,1163.09,"One caution. Whether a given service counts as supporting a critical or important function is a judgement for Risk and Compliance, not for procurement alone. Bring them into the panel design early, agree which lots are in scope, and let the framework carry the outcome of that assessment into every call-off."],[64,1164.04,1191.93,"Here is one possible panel for Nordvia P2. Lot 1, IT delivery. Lot 2, technology advisory. Lot 3, staff augmentation. Four firms per lot, chosen by a framework RFP. Maximum day rates by role and seniority. Mini-competition from 50,000 euros, direct award below with a rationale. DORA clauses and the labour-law model fixed per lot. And a framework of three years plus one."],[64,1192.43,1214.6,"This is one possible design, not the answer. Notice the incumbent favourites. They can join, but they must win a place on the panel. That message matters for the business. Nobody is being thrown out. The firms the IT teams like today are welcome to compete, and if they are as good as the teams believe, they will win their place and their share of the work."],[65,1215.55,1244.24,"The same logic works outside IT. Look at P7, creative agencies. 7.2 million euros with six agencies, overlapping briefs, no rate card, and every campaign briefed to a favourite. The proposed route: a lead creative agency chosen by pitch. A small specialist panel for production and digital. A rate card for fees; production at cost plus a transparent mark-up. And no auctions for creative work."],[65,1244.74,1277.56,"There is a twist compared with consulting. The lead agency relationship is closer to strategic, because the agency holds the brand, and continuity matters. So it is a roster: one lead, plus specialists. And on auctions, Article 35 of the same EU directive excludes electronic auctions for services whose subject is intellectual performance, such as design. It is a public rule again, but it reflects a sound logic: you cannot rank creative ideas automatically on price."],[65,1278.06,1299.95,"Agencies also show why the commercial model per lot matters. Creative fees are paid for thinking time, so a rate card fits. Production, such as printing or filming, is bought from third parties, so cost plus a transparent mark-up, with the right to see the invoices, removes the hidden margin that often sits in agency production bills."],[66,1300.9,1326.26,"How does a panel run in Coupa? The framework: the contract and the rate card live in contract lifecycle management. The mini-competition: a short RFP event created from a template and sent only to the lot's members. Questions and answers: the Message Center, which gives equal treatment and an audit trail. And buying: requisitions reference the framework, so off-panel spend becomes visible."],[66,1326.76,1347.03,"The template is the time saver. A mini-competition should be issued in an hour, not a week. If the template is ready, with the statement of work structure, the pricing sheet and the evaluation questions, the IT project manager has no reason to skip it. Confirm in your Coupa tenant how templates and supplier groups are set up."],[66,1347.53,1366.54,"Off-panel spend is the signal to watch. If requisitions for consulting keep arriving with suppliers outside the panel, either the panel is missing a capability, which means a lot or a member should be added at the next refresh, or the rules are being bypassed, which is a conversation with the budget holder."],[67,1367.49,1390.53,"Now the exercise: design the Nordvia IT services panel, about 20 minutes, in Module 2.3 of your workbook. First, check the two numbers from the case. Then define the lots and the number of firms per lot. Sketch the rate card by role and seniority. Write the call-off rules. And add the labour-law and DORA safeguards for the staff augmentation lot."],[67,1391.03,1412.11,"For the two checks: the first is the share of call-offs awarded without competition, 29 out of 38. The second is how much higher the top day rate is than the lowest, 1,450 against 820. Work them out yourself before you look at the answers. Pause the lesson now if you want to do the exercise."],[67,1412.61,1430.6,"Here are the answers. 29 divided by 38 is about 76 percent of call-offs without competition. And the difference of 630 euros, divided by 820, gives a top rate about 77 percent above the lowest, for the same senior profile."],[68,1431.55,1442.58,"Let us debrief. How many lots, and why those? How many firms per lot? Where did you set the direct-award threshold? And what will the CIO say to the rules, and how will you answer?"],[68,1443.08,1472.05,"Push yourself on the last question. The CIO will probably say two things: my teams need speed, and my favourite firm is good. The answer to both is in the design. The favourite firm can stay if it competes and wins its place. And speed comes from the template and the pre-agreed terms, not from skipping competition. A mini-competition from a ready template can run in days for small jobs, which is faster than negotiating a fresh contract with a favourite."],[69,1473.0,1489.34,"Module 2.3 in three lines. Streams of assignments lead to a panel by lot with a rate card. Mini-competition above a threshold, and a documented direct award below. And the labour-law model and DORA clauses are fixed once, in the framework."],[69,1489.84,1536.52,"To recap this lesson. Professional and IT services arrive as a stream of different jobs, so the answer is competition in the market. We saw Nordvia's fragmented and concentrated spend, and why one supplier, the open market and no rules all fail. We used the EU framework rules and the public consultancy framework as a benchmark, and designed a panel in six steps: lots, panel size, rate card, call-off rules, template and governance. We covered fixed price versus time and materials, the labour-law trap in staff augmentation, and why DORA is easier at panel level. In the next lesson we take the case where no competition is possible at all: sole and single source, with the Kelvar software renewal."]],"questions":[{"after":49,"q":"Nordvia's IT consulting shows 12 firms, 61 percent of spend with the top three, and 29 of 38 call-offs without competition. How is this category best described?","options":["Well consolidated, so no action is needed","A sole-source category","A tail category for catalogues","Fragmented and concentrated at once: many firms without common terms, but most work going to favourites without competition"],"answer":3,"explain":"Twelve firms on different terms means fragmentation, while most work going to three favourites without competition means concentration without tension. Nordvia gets neither leverage nor competition.","t":176.96},{"after":52,"q":"The IT director argues that a panel adds bureaucracy and slows projects down. What is the strongest answer?","options":["A panel fixes legal terms, rates and regulatory clauses once, so each assignment needs only a short statement of work and a quick competition","Procurement rules require a panel","Panels are always cheaper than direct awards","The CFO has decided"],"answer":0,"explain":"The panel moves the heavy work to the start. After that, each assignment is less work than today, not more, which is the argument that wins over the business.","t":362.53},{"after":54,"q":"Under the Article 33 logic, a direct award from a framework to a panel member is sound when","options":["the business owner prefers that firm","the firm offers the lowest day rate on the phone","all terms are fixed in the framework and the firm is chosen by objective criteria such as a cascade, rotation or capability","the job is urgent and no record is kept"],"answer":2,"explain":"Direct award fits when all terms are already fixed and the choice follows objective rules stated in advance. Otherwise a mini-competition among capable members is the route.","t":497.08},{"after":57,"q":"A colleague wants to put eleven firms on the staff augmentation lot, which has about 1.2 million euros of annual demand, illustrative, to keep everyone happy. What is the main risk?","options":["Too much competition drives rates too low","Each member can expect little work, so firms stop investing and stop putting their best people forward","Coupa cannot handle eleven suppliers","DORA limits panels to six firms"],"answer":1,"explain":"The test is whether each member can expect meaningful work in a year. A panel that is too large gives each firm too little, and quality and commitment fall.","t":723.54},{"after":59,"q":"Why does the Nordvia rate card set maximum day rates rather than fixed day rates?","options":["Fixed rates are not allowed by EU law","Maximum rates avoid the need for seniority definitions","It lets suppliers raise rates during the term","The ceiling stops extreme rates, while mini-competitions can still bring the price below it for each job"],"answer":3,"explain":"A maximum caps the price once for everyone, and competition below the ceiling finds the right price per job. Seniority definitions are still needed to prevent grade inflation.","t":828.95},{"after":61,"q":"A call-off asks a panel firm to deliver a defined set of regulatory reports with clear acceptance criteria. Which pricing model should the template default to?","options":["Fixed price per deliverable","Pure time and materials","Gain-share on regulatory fines avoided","Daily rate with no cap"],"answer":0,"explain":"Defined work with clear acceptance criteria fits fixed price per deliverable: the supplier controls the work, so it carries the delivery risk, and we pay for accepted results.","t":999.61},{"after":62,"q":"Under a service contract, three supplier developers sit in a Nordvia team, take daily instructions from a Nordvia manager and work Nordvia's hours. What is the issue?","options":["None, it is a normal service contract","It breaches the rate card","It may be a sham service contract that creates an employment relationship; choose and label the right model per lot and check with Legal","It only matters if the work lasts more than five years"],"answer":2,"explain":"If we direct the people, it looks like agency work, which must be labelled and licensed and has limits. Calling it a service contract does not change that, so the model must be chosen per lot.","t":1083.06},{"after":64,"q":"When presenting the panel to the CIO, which argument about DORA is most convincing?","options":["DORA does not apply to consulting","Article 30 clauses, register entries and the pre-contract assessment can be done once at framework level and inherited by every call-off","DORA requires exactly four firms per lot","DORA forbids direct awards"],"answer":1,"explain":"IT services with system access are likely ICT services. Doing the DORA work once in the framework, rather than for each of 38 call-offs, makes compliance manageable and is a strong reason to build the panel.","t":1214.6},{"after":68,"q":"The CIO says: my teams need speed, and my favourite firm is excellent. Which answer best reflects the panel design?","options":["Give the favourite firm a direct award for all jobs","Speed is not a procurement concern","Run a full RFP for each job to be safe","The favourite can stay if it wins a place and competes, and speed comes from the template and pre-agreed terms, not from skipping competition"],"answer":3,"explain":"The panel keeps good incumbents if they compete, and the ready template makes mini-competitions fast. That answers both concerns without returning to direct awards by habit.","t":1472.05}],"cues":[[0.6,1.38,"Welcome back."],[1.38,9.76,"This lesson covers module 2.3: a pool of preferred suppliers, for professional services, consulting and IT services."],[9.76,17.28,"It is the longest module of the session, because it carries the most common indirect categories in an insurer."],[17.56,23.14,"In the last lesson we saw competition for the market: one supplier, chosen once per cycle."],[23.14,29.85,"Now we look at the opposite case, competition in the market, where several suppliers are the right answer."],[30.13,43.78,"By the end of this lesson you will be able to design a panel: its lots, the number of firms per lot, the rate card, the call-off rules, the pricing model per assignment, and the labour-law and DORA safeguards."],[43.78,46.96,"Our case is Nordvia P2, IT consulting and project staff."],[47.91,49.46,"Here is the starting point."],[49.46,54.22,"Professional and IT services arrive as a stream of different assignments."],[54.22,60.02,"No single firm is best at all of them, and no RFP per assignment is fast enough."],[60.02,64.87,"The answer is a panel: terms fixed once, competition every time."],[65.37,67.16,"Contrast this with cleaning."],[67.16,72.54,"There, one continuous service: the same building, every night, for three years."],[72.54,74.59,"Here, many different jobs."],[74.59,84.12,"A data migration in March, a security architecture review in May, two testers for a release in September, a regulatory reporting project in the autumn."],[84.4,87.97,"Each needs different skills, and each has its own deadline."],[87.97,92.76,"That single difference, how demand arrives, changes the whole strategy."],[92.76,96.7,"One supplier would be strong in some jobs and weak in others."],[96.7,101.17,"A full tender for each job would take weeks, and the business would not wait."],[101.67,107.69,"This is also where van Weele's point about purchasing as a business function becomes very practical."],[107.69,110.59,"The business does not want a procurement process."],[110.59,114.37,"It wants the right expert, quickly, at a fair price."],[114.37,120.04,"A panel is the structure that delivers all three at once, and that is how you should explain it."],[120.99,124.02,"Let us look at Nordvia's IT consulting today."],[124.02,128.24,"Fictional case data, but you may recognise the pattern."],[128.24,132.85,"6.1 million euros, spent with 12 firms, through 38 call-offs."],[132.85,137.14,"61 percent of that spend goes to the top three firms."],[137.14,141.34,"29 of the 38 call-offs were awarded without any competition."],[141.62,148.53,"And day rates for the same senior profile range from 820 to 1,450 euros."],[149.03,150.59,"Let those numbers land."],[150.59,154.62,"The category is fragmented and concentrated at the same time."],[154.62,158.99,"Fragmented, because there are twelve firms and no common terms."],[158.99,164.07,"Concentrated, because most of the work goes to three favourites, without competition."],[164.35,170.63,"So Nordvia has the disadvantages of both: no leverage from volume, and no tension from competition."],[170.63,176.96,"And the rate spread tells you there is no shared reference for what a senior consultant should cost."],[177.91,179.56,"Here is a poll to think about."],[179.56,185.52,"Nordvia IT consulting: twelve firms, thirty eight call-offs, rates from 820 to 1,450 euros."],[185.52,187.94,"What would you do first?"],[187.94,198.78,"Award everything to the cheapest firm; run an e-auction on day rates; build a panel by lot with a rate card and call-off rules; or leave it to IT."],[198.78,199.93,"Choose your answer."],[200.43,202.12,"Most people pick the panel."],[202.12,206.68,"If you were tempted by the auction, ask yourself: what would you be ranking?"],[206.68,212.08,"Is a senior architect from one firm the same product as a senior architect from another?"],[212.08,214.96,"A day rate is only the price of an input."],[215.24,221.77,"Without knowing the person, the method and the deliverable, a lower rate can easily mean a higher total cost."],[222.71,225.72,"Now the Group picture, anonymised and rounded."],[225.72,231.31,"IT services: about 80 million euros with some 350 suppliers."],[231.31,236.69,"Consulting and audit: about 49 million euros with some 360 suppliers."],[236.69,245.22,"Advisory firms: about 12 million euros in 9 entities, through 60 supplier records, and no Group agreement."],[245.5,251.53,"And only about 5 percent of IT services spend sits with a supplier shared by two entities."],[252.04,255.35,"It is the same pattern as Nordvia, at Group scale."],[255.35,261.14,"Many entities buy similar services from overlapping firms, each on its own terms."],[261.14,265.69,"That suggests an approach: a panel can be local first and Group later."],[265.97,275.22,"Design your entity's panel so that other entities can join, for example by using common lot definitions and role families in the rate card."],[275.22,278.24,"Then the second entity does not start from zero."],[278.74,289.91,"Imagine two entities each running their own small consulting panel with different role names, one calling a level senior consultant and the other manager, at different ceilings."],[289.91,293.12,"Joining them later means renegotiating everything."],[293.4,301.76,"If both use the same role families and seniority definitions from the start, merging them into a Group panel becomes a simple step."],[302.71,305.13,"Three wrong answers and one right one."],[305.13,306.57,"Let us go down the table."],[307.07,308.88,"One supplier for everything."],[308.88,313.2,"What happens: dependency, capability gaps, and no tension."],[313.2,317.03,"That firm becomes the default for work it is not good at."],[317.03,318.58,"Open market every time."],[318.58,323.27,"Weeks per RFP, so in practice people skip it and pick a favourite."],[323.27,327.18,"That is how good intentions turn into 29 direct awards."],[327.18,329.09,"No rules, which is today."],[329.37,331.73,"The same profile at very different rates."],[332.24,335.81,"And the right answer: a panel by lot, with a rate card."],[335.81,341.62,"The ceilings are fixed once, and three to six pre-qualified firms compete for each job."],[341.62,344.72,"The third row is where most organisations are."],[344.72,349.67,"And here is the argument for your business owner: the panel is not more bureaucracy."],[349.67,351.57,"It is less work per assignment."],[351.85,357.89,"The legal terms, the security checks, the rates and the regulatory clauses are agreed once."],[357.89,362.54,"Each new job needs only a short statement of work and a quick competition."],[363.48,365.77,"Where does the design of a panel come from?"],[365.77,374.8,"The reference model is Article 33 of the EU public procurement directive of 2014, which governs framework agreements."],[374.8,379.38,"It is a public-sector rule, and a private insurer is not bound by it."],[379.38,383.66,"But it is a useful benchmark, and auditors recognise its logic."],[384.16,386.26,"It offers two call-off routes."],[386.26,389.98,"Direct award, when all terms are already fixed in the framework."],[389.98,399.76,"Then the choice of firm must follow objective criteria, such as a cascade, where the top ranked firm is asked first, or rotation, or capability."],[399.76,403.51,"Second, mini-competition, when not all terms are fixed."],[403.79,410.52,"Then you invite all capable members of the lot in writing, set a deadline, and keep bids closed until it."],[410.52,416.03,"And third, a mix of both, as long as the rules are stated upfront in the framework."],[416.03,419.26,"The directive also caps frameworks at four years."],[419.76,421.68,"Notice the principle underneath."],[421.68,429.55,"Whether you award directly or run a competition, the rule is decided in advance and applied the same way to everyone."],[429.55,435.63,"That is what makes a panel fair, and what protects you when someone asks later why a firm was chosen."],[436.58,448.11,"For the details, the UK Crown Commercial Service consultancy framework, called RM6187, is the most detailed public model of a consulting panel."],[448.11,449.68,"Let me read the structure."],[450.18,454.01,"Nine lots, and each further competition runs within one lot."],[454.01,457.33,"Maximum day rates that call-offs may not exceed."],[457.33,462.73,"At least ten working days for simple requirements, so suppliers can respond properly."],[462.73,468.38,"Quality weighted between 50 and 90 percent, price between 10 and 50 percent."],[468.38,471.09,"Three evaluators and a consensus meeting."],[471.37,474.93,"And no negotiation after bids, only clarification."],[475.43,480.24,"Take the structure; adapt the thresholds to your own delegation of authority."],[480.24,489.04,"An insurer with five million euros of consulting will not need nine lots, and the deadlines can be shorter for small, simple jobs."],[489.32,497.09,"But maximum rates, quality-led evaluation, more than one evaluator and no post-bid haggling are principles worth keeping."],[498.03,500.53,"Now let us design the panel in six steps."],[501.03,505.64,"Step one, lots, organised by capability and perhaps by size band."],[505.64,510.74,"Step two, panel size: enough firms to compete, few enough to engage."],[510.74,517.16,"Step three, the rate card: maximum day rates, discounts, expenses, and a cap on indexation."],[517.16,522.62,"Step four, call-off rules: thresholds for direct award and for mini-competition."],[522.9,530.21,"Step five, a call-off template: the statement of work, deliverables, acceptance criteria and named key people."],[530.21,534.87,"Step six, governance: scorecards, share of wallet, refresh and exit."],[535.37,537.76,"Each step answers a typical failure."],[537.76,543.77,"No lots, and generalists win everything, because a large firm can always bid for every job."],[543.77,549.03,"No size rule, and you have twenty members who never get work."],[549.03,552.47,"No rate card, and the 1,450 euro day continues."],[552.47,555.62,"No call-off rules, and favourites win everything."],[555.9,560.11,"No template, and every call-off is drafted from scratch, slowly."],[560.11,566.56,"No governance, and the panel decays: the same firms, the same complacency, five years later."],[567.06,572.57,"Let me say a little more about step six, governance, because it is the step most often skipped."],[572.57,583.68,"Once a quarter, look at a simple scorecard per firm: quality of delivery, rates achieved against the ceiling, and share of wallet, meaning the share of the lot's spend each firm received."],[583.96,587.36,"If one firm takes eighty percent of a lot, ask why."],[587.36,594.22,"Perhaps it is simply the best, which is fine, but then the other members need to know whether they have a real chance."],[594.22,601.58,"Plan a refresh point too, for example at the end of the base term, where weak members can leave and new ones can join."],[602.53,608.16,"Here is an illustrative lot structure for an insurer's IT and professional services."],[608.16,609.95,"Adapt it to your own demand."],[610.45,616.22,"IT delivery: projects and programmes, typically priced at a fixed price per milestone."],[616.22,622.91,"Technology advisory: architecture, cloud and security, priced at a fixed price per deliverable."],[622.91,629.93,"IT staff augmentation: developers, testers and analysts, at time and materials under the rate card."],[630.21,638.5,"Regulatory and actuarial advisory: Solvency II, IFRS 17 and DORA work, at a fixed price or capped time and materials."],[638.5,646.38,"And strategy and transformation: operating model and programme work, at a fixed price per phase."],[646.88,650.16,"Notice that the pricing model differs per lot."],[650.16,652.91,"That is decision four of the six, applied per lot."],[652.91,656.83,"Delivery work has milestones, so fixed price fits."],[656.83,660.9,"Staff augmentation is about people, so time and materials fits."],[660.9,664.25,"Ask yourself which lots your own demand would need."],[664.53,668.16,"In most entities, two or three lots cover most of the spend."],[668.16,670.78,"Do not create a lot that nobody will use."],[671.73,673.24,"How many firms per lot?"],[673.24,677.9,"Too few, one or two, and there is no real competition for each assignment."],[677.9,683.39,"Too many, ten or more, and members get little work, so they stop investing in you."],[683.39,685.66,"The practice range is three to six per lot."],[685.66,689.14,"Treat that as common practice, not a verified norm."],[689.64,695.19,"Here is the test: can each member expect meaningful work in a year?"],[695.19,702.37,"Imagine a lot with about 1.5 million euros of annual demand, an illustrative figure, and ten firms."],[702.37,708.29,"If the work is spread evenly, each firm gets 150,000 euros, perhaps two small jobs."],[708.57,715.43,"A firm in that position will not put its best people forward, and will not invest in learning your systems."],[715.43,718.79,"A panel member that never wins anything stops trying."],[718.79,723.54,"Size the panel to the demand, not to the number of firms that want to be on it."],[724.49,726.62,"Is there evidence from insurance?"],[726.62,734.82,"The best-documented cases are claims legal panels, which are an analogue rather than indirect spend, but the logic transfers."],[735.31,746.39,"Zurich cut its England and Wales claims legal panel from 28 to 14 firms in 2004, and replaced five separate panels with one central system."],[746.39,753.42,"The Co-operative Insurance Society planned to go from 27 firms to possibly six, in 2005."],[753.7,760.93,"The reasons cited, as reported by the trade journal The Lawyer, were unequal work distribution and quality."],[760.93,765.54,"So, fewer firms, more work for each, more leverage and better quality."],[765.54,769.55,"Exactly the argument we make for IT and consulting panels."],[770.51,772.41,"Now the rate card structure."],[772.41,779.54,"Roles: role families per lot, for example developer, architect, project manager, business analyst."],[779.54,789.18,"Seniority: junior, consultant, senior, manager, principal, with a short description of each level so firms cannot label a junior as a senior."],[789.46,793.86,"Price: a maximum day rate, and call-offs may bid lower."],[793.86,796.84,"Volume: discount tiers based on annual spend."],[796.84,800.34,"Expenses: a fixed policy, or included in the rate."],[800.34,802.64,"Indexation: an annual cap."],[803.14,805.91,"Why maximum rates and not fixed rates?"],[805.91,810.46,"Because the mini-competition can still bring the price down."],[810.46,812.23,"The ceiling stops the 1,450 euro day."],[812.23,817.15,"The competition finds the right price below the ceiling for each job."],[817.15,821.53,"And the seniority definitions matter as much as the numbers."],[821.81,828.96,"A rate card without them invites grade inflation, where the same person is billed one level higher on the next job."],[829.9,831.22,"The call-off rules."],[831.22,835.85,"These thresholds are illustrative; align them to your delegation of authority."],[836.35,843.33,"Below 50,000 euros: direct award, at or below the rate card, with a written rationale."],[843.33,850.31,"From 50,000 to 250,000 euros: a mini-competition, with at least three members invited."],[850.31,858.32,"Above 250,000 euros, or for strategic work: a mini-competition with presentations, or a full RFP."],[858.6,862.2,"Urgent needs: direct award, reviewed after the fact."],[862.2,867.69,"And follow-on work: direct award if justified, and benchmarked against the rate card."],[868.2,872.0,"The 50,000 euro line echoes the Group's size bands."],[872.0,878.38,"But the rule that matters most is this one: every direct award has a written rationale."],[878.38,879.57,"Two or three sentences."],[879.57,882.9,"Why this firm, why now, why no competition."],[883.18,890.54,"That small habit changes behaviour, because people choose differently when they know they will have to write the reason down."],[890.54,896.21,"And the after-the-fact review of urgent awards stops urgent from becoming the normal route."],[896.71,898.91,"Let us test the rules with a story."],[898.91,906.85,"A Nordvia business analyst needs a data protection specialist for six weeks, estimated at about 40,000 euros."],[906.85,914.08,"That is below the threshold, so a direct award at or below the rate card is allowed, with a short written rationale."],[914.36,921.7,"Three months later the same firm is asked to extend the work, and the total now reaches 120,000 euros."],[921.7,922.99,"Is that still a direct award?"],[922.99,933.36,"Only as justified follow-on work, benchmarked against the rate card, and the rationale must explain why a competition would not give a better result."],[933.64,938.19,"Without that rule, a series of small awards quietly becomes a large one."],[939.14,941.5,"Fixed price or time and materials?"],[941.5,944.24,"On the left, fixed price per deliverable."],[944.24,946.31,"It is the default for defined work."],[946.31,949.01,"The supplier carries the delivery risk."],[949.01,952.96,"And it needs a clear statement of work and acceptance criteria."],[952.96,956.21,"On the right, capped or pure time and materials."],[956.49,959.81,"Capped time and materials when the scope is partly clear."],[959.81,963.83,"Pure time and materials only for discovery."],[963.83,969.1,"And payment by results only if the supplier really controls the levers."],[969.1,974.13,"This follows the three pricing models of the UK Consultancy Playbook from 2022."],[974.63,978.91,"Most of Nordvia's 38 call-offs were probably time and materials."],[978.91,984.49,"Moving defined work to fixed price is a saving and a control at the same time."],[984.49,989.31,"A saving, because the firm's own inefficiency is no longer our cost."],[989.31,994.02,"A control, because we pay for accepted deliverables, not for days spent."],[994.3,999.61,"The call-off template should make fixed price the default and require a reason for anything else."],[1000.56,1005.31,"Now an insurer-specific trap: labour law in staff augmentation."],[1005.31,1007.38,"On the left, a service contract."],[1007.38,1010.79,"The supplier owns the outcome and directs its own people."],[1010.79,1013.81,"Fixed price deliverables fit well here."],[1013.81,1016.16,"On the right, temporary agency work."],[1016.16,1018.27,"We direct the people day to day."],[1018.55,1023.23,"Then it must be labelled and licensed as agency work, and limits apply."],[1023.23,1033.76,"In Germany, for example, the law on temporary agency work, the A\u00dcG, limits the assignment of the same worker to the same client to 18 consecutive months."],[1034.26,1037.02,"The danger is the grey zone in between."],[1037.02,1048.3,"A contract called a service contract, but in practice our managers give daily instructions to the supplier's developers, who sit in our teams, use our tools and work our hours."],[1048.3,1053.82,"That is a sham service contract, and it can create an employment relationship with the client."],[1054.1,1057.2,"So this is a strategy decision, not a contract detail."],[1057.2,1061.96,"Choose the model per lot and design the statement of work accordingly."],[1061.96,1064.64,"Verify the current national law with Legal."],[1065.14,1069.47,"A simple way to decide per lot is to ask who gives the instructions."],[1069.47,1075.77,"If the supplier's lead plans and directs the work and delivers an agreed result, it is a service."],[1075.77,1083.06,"If our own team leader decides each day what the person does, it is labour supply, and it must be contracted as such."],[1084.01,1086.88,"The second trap: DORA and the IT panel."],[1086.88,1087.34,"Four cards."],[1087.34,1094.82,"Scope: IT services with access to our systems are likely to be ICT services under DORA."],[1094.82,1100.02,"Register: every ICT arrangement goes into the register of information."],[1100.02,1107.03,"Clauses: the contractual provisions of Article 30 go into the framework, so every call-off inherits them."],[1107.31,1114.43,"Assessment: the pre-contract assessment required by Article 28, paragraph 4, is done at panel level."],[1114.93,1117.9,"Think about what that means for Nordvia."],[1117.9,1120.3,"Today, 38 call-offs with 12 firms."],[1120.3,1127.29,"Under DORA, each of those arrangements may need the right clauses, a register entry and a pre-contract assessment."],[1127.29,1131.5,"Done one by one, that is a heavy load and a real compliance risk."],[1131.5,1135.86,"Done once, at framework level, every call-off inherits the work."],[1136.14,1143.75,"That is a strong argument for the panel when you talk to the CIO: it is not only cheaper, it makes DORA compliance manageable."],[1144.26,1145.42,"One caution."],[1145.42,1154.39,"Whether a given service counts as supporting a critical or important function is a judgement for Risk and Compliance, not for procurement alone."],[1154.39,1163.1,"Bring them into the panel design early, agree which lots are in scope, and let the framework carry the outcome of that assessment into every call-off."],[1164.04,1167.0,"Here is one possible panel for Nordvia P2."],[1167.0,1169.23,"Lot 1, IT delivery."],[1169.23,1171.64,"Lot 2, technology advisory."],[1171.64,1172.56,"Lot 3, staff augmentation."],[1172.56,1176.81,"Four firms per lot, chosen by a framework RFP."],[1176.81,1181.01,"Maximum day rates by role and seniority."],[1181.01,1186.01,"Mini-competition from 50,000 euros, direct award below with a rationale."],[1186.29,1189.61,"DORA clauses and the labour-law model fixed per lot."],[1189.61,1191.93,"And a framework of three years plus one."],[1192.43,1195.48,"This is one possible design, not the answer."],[1195.48,1197.28,"Notice the incumbent favourites."],[1197.28,1200.87,"They can join, but they must win a place on the panel."],[1200.87,1203.24,"That message matters for the business."],[1203.24,1205.08,"Nobody is being thrown out."],[1205.36,1214.6,"The firms the IT teams like today are welcome to compete, and if they are as good as the teams believe, they will win their place and their share of the work."],[1215.55,1218.06,"The same logic works outside IT."],[1218.06,1219.49,"Look at P7, creative agencies."],[1219.49,1229.16,"7.2 million euros with six agencies, overlapping briefs, no rate card, and every campaign briefed to a favourite."],[1229.16,1233.38,"The proposed route: a lead creative agency chosen by pitch."],[1233.38,1237.33,"A small specialist panel for production and digital."],[1237.61,1242.12,"A rate card for fees; production at cost plus a transparent mark-up."],[1242.12,1244.25,"And no auctions for creative work."],[1244.74,1247.27,"There is a twist compared with consulting."],[1247.27,1254.9,"The lead agency relationship is closer to strategic, because the agency holds the brand, and continuity matters."],[1254.9,1258.71,"So it is a roster: one lead, plus specialists."],[1258.99,1269.96,"And on auctions, Article 35 of the same EU directive excludes electronic auctions for services whose subject is intellectual performance, such as design."],[1269.96,1277.55,"It is a public rule again, but it reflects a sound logic: you cannot rank creative ideas automatically on price."],[1278.06,1281.94,"Agencies also show why the commercial model per lot matters."],[1281.94,1286.27,"Creative fees are paid for thinking time, so a rate card fits."],[1286.55,1299.95,"Production, such as printing or filming, is bought from third parties, so cost plus a transparent mark-up, with the right to see the invoices, removes the hidden margin that often sits in agency production bills."],[1300.9,1302.59,"How does a panel run in Coupa?"],[1302.59,1308.19,"The framework: the contract and the rate card live in contract lifecycle management."],[1308.19,1315.13,"The mini-competition: a short RFP event created from a template and sent only to the lot's members."],[1315.13,1320.8,"Questions and answers: the Message Center, which gives equal treatment and an audit trail."],[1321.08,1326.27,"And buying: requisitions reference the framework, so off-panel spend becomes visible."],[1326.76,1328.73,"The template is the time saver."],[1328.73,1332.23,"A mini-competition should be issued in an hour, not a week."],[1332.23,1342.55,"If the template is ready, with the statement of work structure, the pricing sheet and the evaluation questions, the IT project manager has no reason to skip it."],[1342.83,1347.03,"Confirm in your Coupa tenant how templates and supplier groups are set up."],[1347.53,1349.88,"Off-panel spend is the signal to watch."],[1349.88,1366.54,"If requisitions for consulting keep arriving with suppliers outside the panel, either the panel is missing a capability, which means a lot or a member should be added at the next refresh, or the rules are being bypassed, which is a conversation with the budget holder."],[1367.49,1374.65,"Now the exercise: design the Nordvia IT services panel, about 20 minutes, in Module 2.3 of your workbook."],[1374.65,1377.4,"First, check the two numbers from the case."],[1377.4,1381.25,"Then define the lots and the number of firms per lot."],[1381.25,1384.4,"Sketch the rate card by role and seniority."],[1384.4,1386.05,"Write the call-off rules."],[1386.33,1390.53,"And add the labour-law and DORA safeguards for the staff augmentation lot."],[1391.03,1398.08,"For the two checks: the first is the share of call-offs awarded without competition, 29 out of 38."],[1398.08,1404.19,"The second is how much higher the top day rate is than the lowest, 1,450 against 820."],[1404.19,1407.97,"Work them out yourself before you look at the answers."],[1407.97,1412.11,"Pause the lesson now if you want to do the exercise."],[1412.61,1413.99,"Here are the answers."],[1413.99,1420.03,"29 divided by 38 is about 76 percent of call-offs without competition."],[1420.03,1430.59,"And the difference of 630 euros, divided by 820, gives a top rate about 77 percent above the lowest, for the same senior profile."],[1431.55,1432.74,"Let us debrief."],[1432.74,1434.74,"How many lots, and why those?"],[1434.74,1435.56,"How many firms per lot?"],[1435.56,1438.75,"Where did you set the direct-award threshold?"],[1438.75,1442.58,"And what will the CIO say to the rules, and how will you answer?"],[1443.08,1445.14,"Push yourself on the last question."],[1445.14,1450.99,"The CIO will probably say two things: my teams need speed, and my favourite firm is good."],[1450.99,1452.88,"The answer to both is in the design."],[1452.88,1457.16,"The favourite firm can stay if it competes and wins its place."],[1457.16,1463.03,"And speed comes from the template and the pre-agreed terms, not from skipping competition."],[1463.31,1472.05,"A mini-competition from a ready template can run in days for small jobs, which is faster than negotiating a fresh contract with a favourite."],[1473.0,1474.62,"Module 2.3 in three lines."],[1474.62,1479.28,"Streams of assignments lead to a panel by lot with a rate card."],[1479.28,1484.16,"Mini-competition above a threshold, and a documented direct award below."],[1484.16,1489.34,"And the labour-law model and DORA clauses are fixed once, in the framework."],[1489.84,1491.34,"To recap this lesson."],[1491.34,1498.72,"Professional and IT services arrive as a stream of different jobs, so the answer is competition in the market."],[1498.72,1506.46,"We saw Nordvia's fragmented and concentrated spend, and why one supplier, the open market and no rules all fail."],[1506.74,1519.55,"We used the EU framework rules and the public consultancy framework as a benchmark, and designed a panel in six steps: lots, panel size, rate card, call-off rules, template and governance."],[1519.55,1527.97,"We covered fixed price versus time and materials, the labour-law trap in staff augmentation, and why DORA is easier at panel level."],[1528.25,1536.52,"In the next lesson we take the case where no competition is possible at all: sole and single source, with the Kelvar software renewal."]]},{"id":"s2-l4","session":"s2","title":"When there is no real competition","summary":"Diagnose a sole or single source situation, build usage facts and a credible BATNA, and plan a renewal negotiation back from the notice date with the right protections for the next term.","from":70,"to":87,"audio":"audio/s2-l4.mp3","duration":1613.2,"segs":[[70,0.6,46.05,"Welcome back. This lesson covers module 2.4 of Session 2: what to do when there is no real competition. Up to now in this session, every route to market has had something in common. There was a market. There were several suppliers who could do the work, and our job was to make them compete in the right way, either for one contract by tender, or for a stream of assignments through a panel. But some of the biggest contracts in an insurance group do not look like that at all. Think of the core insurance platform, the policy administration system, the claims engine, or a specialist actuarial tool. One vendor, many years of history, data and processes built around its product, and a renewal date that keeps coming back."],[70,46.55,94.27,"Here is the plan for the next twenty-five minutes or so. First, a hard truth about competitive tools in a market without competition. Then the difference between sole source and single source, which matters more than it sounds. We will look at the situation from the vendor's side with Steele and Court's supplier preferencing model, and see how to get out of the danger zone. Then the heart of the module: a seven-step sole-source playbook, applied to Nordvia's project P3, the Kelvar software licences. We will work out what unused licences are worth, what a credible alternative looks like, which protections to buy for the next term, and how to plan backwards from the notice date. We finish with the do and never do list, the role of Coupa, and your own renewal outline."],[71,95.22,110.95,"Let's start with the sentence on this slide. An RFP sent to a vendor who knows it cannot lose is theatre. An auction with one realistic bidder is an expensive RFQ. And underneath: negotiate, and change the game before you sit down."],[71,111.45,173.49,"Why do I call it theatre? Because a request for proposal only creates pressure if the bidder believes it might lose. A software vendor whose platform runs your policies knows perfectly well that you cannot move in six months. So the RFP costs you weeks of work, it costs the vendor almost nothing, and the answer you receive is the same price list you would have received by email. Worse, it tells the vendor that you do not understand your own position. The same applies to auctions. A reverse auction needs several qualified bidders who can genuinely win. There is real evidence on what happens without them. A review by the US Government Accountability Office of federal reverse auctions in fiscal year 2012 found that over a third of them had no interactive bidding at all. One bid came in, nobody bid against it, and the agency often still paid the auction provider a fee. A competitive tool without competition gives you nothing except the appearance of process."],[71,173.99,198.1,"So what is the alternative? A prepared negotiation. And the second half of the sentence matters just as much: change the game before you sit down. The power in a sole-source negotiation is not created at the table. It is created months earlier, by collecting facts, building alternatives and reshaping what we actually need. That is what this whole module is about."],[72,199.05,205.39,"First, a diagnosis. Is this really sole source, or is it single source? Look at the two columns."],[72,205.89,238.64,"On the left, sole source. No alternative exists. The vendor owns proprietary intellectual property that nobody else can supply. A specialist actuarial model, perhaps, or a data feed that only one provider holds. Here the strategy is to protect yourself and, over time, reduce your dependency. On the right, single source. Alternatives do exist. Other platforms could do the job, but switching is slow, risky or expensive. Here the strategy is different: make the alternative credible."],[72,239.14,287.18,"Now, which column is highlighted? The right one, and that is deliberate. Most incumbent software is single source with high switching cost, not true sole source. And that is a weaker vendor position than it first looks. Why? Because switching cost is not a law of nature. It falls when you plan early, when you modularise the architecture, when you document your data, and when you give yourself a long enough runway. A vendor who thinks it is irreplaceable often behaves as if it were sole source. Our first job is to check that claim, honestly, and if it is single source, to start treating it that way. Ask yourself about your own biggest software contract: if you had three years and a budget, could you move? If the answer is yes, you are in the right-hand column."],[73,288.13,316.81,"Now let's turn the chair around, as we did in Session 1, and look at ourselves through the vendor's eyes. This is Steele and Court's supplier preferencing model from 1996. On the vertical axis, how attractive our account is to the supplier: do we pay on time, are we easy to work with, do we bring reference value or growth? On the horizontal axis, the relative value of our business to the supplier: how much of their revenue do we represent?"],[73,317.31,357.27,"That gives four boxes. Development, at the top left: low value to them today, but attractive. They want to grow us, so they will offer good terms to win more. Core, at the top right: high value and attractive. They will invest in us, protect the relationship and give us their best people. Nuisance, at the bottom left: low value and unattractive. They spend little effort on us, and that is often what we experience as poor service. And exploitable, at the bottom right, which is highlighted: high value, but unattractive. They want the revenue, but they do not care much about the relationship. In this box the supplier takes margin."],[73,357.77,409.34,"Here is the uncomfortable point for an insurer like Nordvia. A global software vendor serves hundreds of financial institutions. To that vendor, a mid-sized insurer is rarely core. We are often nuisance or, if the contract is large, exploitable. Put that next to our own Kraljic view. For us, the core platform is strategic or bottleneck: high impact, high supply risk. For them, we are exploitable. Strategic for us and exploitable for them is the danger zone. It explains a lot of what buyers experience: uplifts applied without discussion, account managers who change every year, and audit letters about licence compliance arriving just before renewal. None of that is personal. It is the rational behaviour of a supplier who sees our account in the bottom right box."],[73,409.84,435.83,"A small example from another category makes the point. Imagine a marketing analytics tool used by one department. The vendor is a small scale-up, and our Group is its biggest logo. There we are core, and the same procurement team gets fast answers and roadmap influence. Same buyer, different position. The model reminds us that power depends on both sides of the relationship, not only on how much we spend."],[74,436.78,440.66,"So how do we get out of the danger zone? The slide gives four moves."],[74,441.16,484.81,"The first is to raise attractiveness. Aggregate the volume across the Group so that the vendor sees one larger account instead of several small ones. Offer reference value: a case study, a site visit, participation in a product advisory board. And make payment easy and predictable. The second move is to reduce dependency: build a credible alternative for the next contract term. The third is to re-specify. Do we really need every module? Could a different architecture, for example a separate document management tool, take part of the scope away from the platform? The fourth is to mine the data: usage, entitlement and deployment. What have we bought, and what are we actually using?"],[74,485.31,527.88,"If you remember the Kearney Purchasing Chessboard from Session 1, you will recognise these. Where supply power is high, the Chessboard tells us to change the nature of demand: re-specification, technical data mining, innovation breakthrough. Combined with the preferencing logic, that gives us these four cards. One warning from the speaker notes is worth repeating. Paying late makes us less attractive, so fix that first. But paying on time alone does not make us a customer of choice. It is a hygiene factor. Attractiveness comes from growth potential, reference value and ease of doing business together. To move from exploitable towards core, we need more than good manners."],[75,528.83,542.22,"Now the heart of the module: the sole-source playbook in seven steps. This comes from section C.8 of our research brief, and it is the structure you will use in the exercise. Let's go through it step by step."],[75,542.72,576.41,"Step one: start early. The rule of thumb is twelve to eighteen months before renewal. That is practice, not a norm, but it reflects how long it takes to collect usage data, run an RFI to alternatives and align the executives. And here is the most important detail on the slide: the notice date, not the expiry date, is the deadline for step one. If the contract renews automatically unless you give notice nine months before expiry, then your real deadline is nine months earlier than the date everyone talks about."],[75,576.91,612.61,"Step two: know your usage. Entitlement versus deployment. How many named users have we paid for, and how many actually log in? Which modules have we licensed, and which are switched on? Unused licences are called shelfware, and we will put a value on them in a moment. Step three: build the BATNA, the best alternative to a negotiated agreement. An RFI to alternative vendors, an architecture study, or a reduced scope. Step four: benchmark. Price per user or per module, discount levels, and the caps that peers have obtained on annual uplifts."],[75,613.11,667.17,"Step five: unbundle. A software contract usually mixes licences, support and maintenance, cloud subscription and professional services. Each has its own logic and its own price, so separate them. Step six: protect. Uplift caps, the right to reduce volumes, exit terms, and the return of your data. And step seven: one voice. IT, Finance and Procurement aligned, with one mandate and one person speaking to the vendor. That last step sounds soft, but in practice it is where many negotiations are lost. A vendor's account team talks to many people in your organisation. If one of them says, we would never move, the renewal is just a formality, the BATNA you built over six months is worth nothing. Notice also that only one of the seven steps happens at the negotiating table. The other six are preparation."],[76,668.12,678.87,"Let's apply this to Nordvia. Remember, Nordvia is our fictional mid-sized insurer. Project P3 is the Kelvar core insurance software. Here are the facts."],[76,679.37,710.65,"Kelvar costs 11.2 million euros a year. At the last renewal, the vendor applied a 9 percent uplift. The renewal date is June 2027. There is a nine-month notice period and an auto-renewal clause. An estimated 20 to 25 percent of named-user licences are unused. There are two credible alternative platforms on the market, but a migration would take 18 to 24 months. And, crucially, no usage data was collected until now."],[76,711.15,754.31,"Before you go on, do two small calculations in your head. First: what is the real deadline? Nine months before June 2027 is September 2026. That is the date by which Nordvia must decide whether to give notice. Depending on where you are on your own calendar, that date may be very close or already behind you, and that is exactly the point the speaker notes want to land. Second: what does the auto-renewal clause do if nobody acts? It renews the contract on the existing terms, possibly with another uplift, and Nordvia loses the one moment in the cycle when it had leverage. Nobody decided to renew. The clause decided for them."],[76,754.81,771.41,"Now look at the last fact again. A team with no usage data cannot argue about shelfware, cannot right-size and cannot benchmark price per active user. The first action for P3 is therefore not a meeting with the vendor. It is an internal usage audit."],[77,772.36,779.31,"Let's talk about the BATNA, because this is where many buyers get it wrong. Is your alternative a bluff, or is it credible?"],[77,779.81,809.66,"On the left, the bluff. Someone says to the vendor: we will move to another platform. But there is no study, no budget and no timeline. And the vendor knows the migration takes two years, because it has seen many customers try. On the right, the credible version. An RFI answered by both alternative vendors. An architecture study and a migration plan. And a decision point for the next term: a date by which Nordvia will decide whether to stay or start migrating."],[77,810.16,845.17,"The footnote quotes the core idea of Fisher and Ury in Getting to Yes: your negotiating power depends on the attractiveness of your alternative, so improve it before you negotiate. For Kelvar, be honest. Can Nordvia leave by June 2027? No. Migration takes 18 to 24 months. So the alternatives are not a threat to walk away next summer. They are a BATNA for the next term. They shape the length of the new contract, the exit rights, and the vendor's willingness to cap uplifts. That is a quieter kind of power, but it is real."],[77,845.67,873.24,"Two lessons from the notes. A bluff that is called destroys the position for years: the vendor learns that your threats are empty, and the next renewal starts even weaker. And a credible BATNA does not need to be used to work. The vendor's account team will report internally that the customer has run an RFI, has a migration study, and has a decision date. That changes how their pricing committee behaves, even if you never migrate."],[78,874.19,886.99,"Now let's put a number on the cheapest lever of all: shelfware. This is an illustrative calculation, so read the footnote. The 50 percent licence share is an assumption for training, not a case fact."],[78,887.49,937.33,"Start with the total: 11.2 million euros a year. Assume half of that is named-user licences, so 5.6 million euros. The rest is support, hosting and other components. Now, 20 to 25 percent of those licences are unused. Twenty percent of 5.6 million is 1.12 million. Twenty-five percent is 1.4 million. So the value of unused licences is about 1.1 to 1.4 million euros a year. And there is a twist. The 9 percent uplift at the last renewal was applied to the unused licences too. Nordvia has been paying a price increase on software that nobody uses. On 1.12 million, that uplift alone is roughly one hundred thousand euros a year, again illustrative."],[78,937.83,984.49,"Why is this the cheapest lever? Because it costs nothing to the relationship. You are not threatening anybody. You are not asking the vendor to lower its list price. You are saying: here is what we deploy, here is what we pay for, let's align them. And it is objective. Fisher and Ury call this using objective criteria: a standard that both sides can accept as fair, independent of will. A vendor can argue about a discount. It is much harder to argue against your own login data. One practical caution: before you share usage data, check the licence metric in the contract and any compliance exposure, because a usage audit can also reveal under-licensing in another module. Know your full picture before you open it."],[79,985.44,993.89,"The fifth step of the playbook was unbundle. Let's look at it more closely, because it is where competition can come back into a sole-source deal."],[79,994.39,1027.14,"Four cards. Licences: right-size them to actual deployment, and negotiate a right to reduce volumes during the term. Support: cap the annual uplift, and consider whether every module needs the highest support level. Cloud subscription: look at price per user, the length of the term, and where the data is located, which matters for GDPR and for DORA. And services: implementation, upgrades, configuration and change requests. Separate them from the licence, and compete them on the panel."],[79,1027.64,1065.68,"That last card deserves a moment. The licence may be sole source. But the people who configure the platform, run the upgrade projects and build the interfaces are often not. Many vendors have certified partners, and independent integrators know the major insurance platforms well. If services are bundled into the vendor's contract, you pay vendor rates for work the market could do. Unbundle them, and you reopen competition on part of the spend. This connects directly to module 2.3: those services can sit in the IT consulting panel, with a rate card and mini-competitions."],[80,1066.63,1072.37,"Step six was protect the next term. This table lists six protections and why each matters."],[80,1072.87,1116.63,"First, a cap on renewal uplifts: no more 9 percent surprises. A cap might be tied to an inflation index, with a ceiling. Second, a price hold for the term, which gives budget certainty. Third, the right to reduce volumes, so that you pay for what is deployed. If the business shrinks or a module is retired, the bill follows. Fourth, transition assistance: the vendor's obligation to help you move to another provider at the end. It may look odd to negotiate your exit in a renewal, but this is what makes the BATNA real next time. Fifth, data portability and return: your data, in a usable format, within a defined time, so you can exit without hostage-taking."],[80,1117.13,1168.33,"And sixth, DORA exit and transition clauses. Under the Digital Operational Resilience Act, Article 30, paragraph 3, point f, contracts for ICT services that support critical or important functions must include exit strategies and transition periods. A core insurance platform almost always supports a critical or important function. So for Kelvar, this protection is not optional. It is a regulatory requirement, and Legal and Compliance own the interpretation of exactly what the clause must say. Notice the pattern in this table. Most of these protections are worth more over five years than a headline discount in year one. A vendor may offer an attractive discount precisely to avoid the uplift cap. Do the multi-year arithmetic before you accept."],[81,1169.28,1175.1,"Now let's turn the playbook into a calendar. Plan back from the notice date. T is the renewal date."],[81,1175.6,1207.42,"At T minus 18 months, the usage audit. At T minus 15 months, the RFI to alternatives. At T minus 12 months, benchmarking and the mandate: what we want, what we accept, and who speaks. At T minus 9 months, the notice decision. And from T minus 6 months to T, the negotiation and signature. The footnote is important: this is a practice timeline, not a norm. And notice that with a nine-month notice period, the notice decision falls exactly at T minus 9."],[81,1207.92,1264.48,"Let's apply it to Kelvar. T is June 2027. T minus 9 is September 2026. T minus 18 would have been December 2025. So if Nordvia is starting now, it is already behind the ideal timeline, and it has no usage data. What can it do? First, check whether a protective notice is still possible. A protective notice means giving notice in time to stop the automatic renewal, while stating that you intend to negotiate a new agreement. It keeps your options open. Legal must confirm what the clause allows. Second, compress the steps: run the usage audit and the RFI in parallel. And if the notice date has already passed, accept that the auto-renewal clause shapes the next term, and focus on what can still be negotiated inside it, such as volume reductions or services, while building the BATNA properly for the cycle after."],[81,1264.98,1280.93,"One more practical point. Put the notice date in the contract system as the critical date, with alerts well before it. Most companies track expiry dates. Far fewer track notice dates. That small difference is often the whole story of a bad renewal."],[82,1281.88,1304.29,"Here is the do and never do list. On the left, never. An auction with one realistic bidder. An RFP for show. IT pre-committing before the mandate. Long terms without exit rights. On the right, do. Facts first: usage and benchmarks. A credible BATNA for the next term. One voice, one mandate. And protections over headline discount."],[82,1304.79,1343.23,"The speaker notes say the most common failure is the third one on the left: a stakeholder tells the vendor that the renewal is a formality. It rarely happens out of bad intent. An IT manager has a good working relationship with the vendor, wants to keep a project on track, and says something reassuring at a steering meeting. The vendor's account manager writes it down. The cure is simple but requires discipline: agree the mandate early, explain to the stakeholders why silence matters, and give them one sentence they can use. For example: Procurement is leading the commercial discussion, please speak to them."],[83,1344.18,1384.21,"What role does Coupa play here? The honest answer is: data and documentation, not competition. No auction, and no RFP for show either. An RFI event in Coupa, sent to the alternative vendors, is how you build the BATNA, and it leaves a record. Contract lifecycle management should alert on the notice date, not only on expiry. And the workflow should record the justification for a sole-source award and the outcome of the negotiation, so that the next team, three years from now, starts with facts. The exact alert settings and workflow fields depend on your configuration, so confirm them in your Coupa tenant."],[84,1385.16,1404.17,"Time for a poll question. Think about Kelvar today. What is Nordvia's strongest lever? A reverse auction. The usage facts and a credible alternative for the next term. Threatening to leave by June 2027. Or accepting the uplift for a longer term. Take a moment to choose your answer."],[84,1404.67,1432.21,"The right answer is the second: the usage facts and a credible alternative for the next term. An auction has no second bidder. A threat to leave by June 2027 is a bluff, because migration takes 18 to 24 months. And accepting the uplift for a longer term just locks in the problem. The alternatives cannot replace Kelvar by June 2027, but they shape the next term and the protections."],[85,1433.16,1445.73,"Now it is your turn. This is the practice exercise for module 2.4: the sole-source renewal outline. It takes about twelve minutes and you will find it under Module 2.4 in your workbook."],[85,1446.23,1499.74,"First, check the illustrative shelfware value: if half of 11.2 million euros were named-user licences and 20 percent of them were unused, what is the annual value? You already know the answer from a few minutes ago. Then fill the eight boxes of the outline: usage facts, your BATNA and how you make it credible, benchmarks, what you unbundle, protections for the next term, the timeline back from the notice date, who speaks for you, and what you can give that is cheap for you. That last box matters: a reference visit, a longer term in exchange for caps, or predictable payment. Tick assumed wherever a fact is still missing. You can use Kelvar or your own sole-source project. If you want to do it now, pause the video, complete it in the Modules tab, and then come back. Session 4 will turn this outline into a full negotiation plan."],[86,1500.69,1512.96,"Let's debrief with four questions. What is your BATNA, honestly? Which protection matters most for the next term? Who speaks for Nordvia, and who must stay silent? And what would you unbundle and compete?"],[86,1513.46,1555.64,"If you wrote, leave Kelvar by June 2027, as your BATNA, prove it to yourself. Where is the migration plan? Where is the budget? If you cannot prove it, it is the bluff from the earlier slide. A stronger answer reads like this: two alternatives have answered an RFI, an architecture study is underway, and Nordvia will decide by a fixed date whether the next term is the last. For protections, many teams pick the uplift cap. That is fine, but for a platform supporting a critical function, the DORA exit clauses are not a choice. And on unbundling, the services around the platform are usually the best candidate for competition."],[87,1556.59,1575.36,"Let's recap module 2.4 in three lines. One: no real competition means negotiation, not an RFP for show or an auction. Two: usage facts, benchmarks and a credible BATNA create the power. Three: protect the next term and change the nature of demand."],[87,1575.86,1612.0,"Behind those three lines sits the playbook: start early from the notice date, know your usage, build a real alternative, benchmark, unbundle, protect, and speak with one voice. In the next lesson, module 2.5, we go to the two ends of the map. At one end, complex strategic partnerships like Northbyte, Nordvia's claims IT managed service, which need a multi-stage process. At the other, the tail of small purchases, which needs a channel rather than a tender. And we will put everything on one page: the strategy paper that records your decision."]],"questions":[{"after":71,"q":"Nordvia IT wants to run a reverse auction for the renewal of a niche actuarial tool that only one vendor can supply. What is the best response?","options":["Agree: an auction always produces the lowest price","Run the auction but invite two vendors who cannot meet the requirements","Decline the auction and prepare a negotiation, building facts and alternatives before sitting down","Send a full RFP to the vendor so the process looks competitive"],"answer":2,"explain":"An auction with one realistic bidder is just an expensive RFQ, and an RFP the vendor cannot lose is theatre. When there is no real competition, the power comes from preparation: facts, alternatives and a reshaped demand.","t":198.1},{"after":72,"q":"Nordvia's HR team uses a payroll platform. Three other vendors offer comparable products, but a migration would take about a year and needs careful testing. How should Procurement classify and treat it?","options":["Sole source: accept the vendor's terms and protect continuity","Single source: alternatives exist, so the strategy is to make an alternative credible","Leverage item: run a reverse auction among the four vendors now","Non-critical: move it to a catalogue"],"answer":1,"explain":"Alternatives exist but switching is slow, which is single source. The vendor's position is weaker than it looks, and the strategy is to make the alternative credible through early planning, not to accept the terms or auction a contract you cannot move quickly.","t":287.18},{"after":74,"q":"A global software vendor treats Nordvia's account as exploitable: high value to them, low attractiveness. Which move best helps Nordvia out of the danger zone?","options":["Pay invoices earlier than due to become a customer of choice","Send a strongly worded complaint to the vendor's CEO","Ask the vendor for a bigger discount in exchange for a longer term","Aggregate Group volume, offer reference value and build a credible alternative for the next term"],"answer":3,"explain":"Raising attractiveness and reducing dependency are the two ways out. Paying on time is a hygiene factor, not a reason to be preferred, and trading term for discount without protections can deepen the dependency.","t":527.88},{"after":76,"q":"The Kelvar contract renews in June 2027, with a nine-month notice period and an auto-renewal clause. What is the real deadline for Nordvia's first decision?","options":["June 2027, the renewal date","December 2026, six months before renewal","September 2026, the notice date","There is no deadline because the contract renews automatically"],"answer":2,"explain":"With nine months of notice, the decision falls in September 2026. If nobody acts by then, the auto-renewal clause renews the contract and Nordvia loses its moment of leverage.","t":771.41},{"after":78,"q":"Nordvia's IT manager wants to open the Kelvar renewal by demanding a 15 percent discount. Procurement suggests starting with the usage audit instead. Why is that the stronger opening?","options":["Usage data is objective and costs nothing to the relationship, so it is hard for the vendor to dispute","Usage data is confidential and the vendor cannot see it","A discount demand is illegal in a sole-source negotiation","The vendor is obliged to refund all unused licences"],"answer":0,"explain":"Entitlement versus deployment is an objective criterion in Fisher and Ury's sense. It shifts the conversation from will to facts, and in the illustrative case it is worth about 1.1 to 1.4 million euros a year.","t":984.49},{"after":80,"q":"In the Kelvar renewal, the vendor offers a 6 percent first-year discount if Nordvia drops its request for an uplift cap and transition assistance. What should Procurement do?","options":["Accept: a discount is worth more than clauses","Accept the discount and drop only the DORA exit clause","Decline: protections such as uplift caps and exit terms are usually worth more over the term, and DORA exit clauses are required for a critical function","Ask for a 10 percent discount instead"],"answer":2,"explain":"A one-off discount is easily lost to uncapped uplifts in later years, and transition assistance is what makes the next BATNA real. For a platform supporting a critical or important function, DORA Article 30 exit and transition clauses are mandatory anyway.","t":1168.33},{"after":81,"q":"Nordvia starts work on a different software renewal only eleven months before expiry, with a nine-month notice period and no usage data. What is the most sensible plan?","options":["Skip the usage audit and go straight to negotiation","Run the usage audit and the RFI in parallel, and ask Legal whether a protective notice can keep options open","Let the contract auto-renew and start preparing for the following cycle only","Launch a full RFP to replace the vendor within eleven months"],"answer":1,"explain":"When you are behind the practice timeline, compress the steps rather than skip them. A protective notice, confirmed by Legal, can stop the automatic renewal while you negotiate.","t":1280.93},{"after":83,"q":"Which use of Coupa fits a sole-source renewal like Kelvar?","options":["An English reverse auction with Kelvar as the only bidder","An RFP to Kelvar alone to create the appearance of competition","An RFI event to alternative vendors, CLM alerts on the notice date, and the justification recorded in the workflow","No use at all: sole-source deals should stay outside the system"],"answer":2,"explain":"In a sole-source case Coupa's honest role is data and documentation. The RFI builds the BATNA, alerts protect the notice date, and the record helps the next renewal team.","t":1384.21},{"after":86,"q":"A team's renewal outline lists the BATNA as: we will move to another platform by June 2027. What is the best feedback?","options":["It is a bluff unless they can show a migration plan, budget and timeline; reframe it as an alternative for the next term","Good: a strong threat creates pressure","Replace it with a reverse auction","Drop the BATNA box, since sole-source deals have none"],"answer":0,"explain":"A migration takes 18 to 24 months, so leaving by June 2027 is not credible and a called bluff weakens the position for years. A credible BATNA uses RFI answers, an architecture study and a decision point for the next term.","t":1555.64}],"cues":[[0.6,1.38,"Welcome back."],[1.38,7.12,"This lesson covers module 2.4 of Session 2: what to do when there is no real competition."],[7.12,12.79,"Up to now in this session, every route to market has had something in common."],[12.79,14.02,"There was a market."],[14.3,25.39,"There were several suppliers who could do the work, and our job was to make them compete in the right way, either for one contract by tender, or for a stream of assignments through a panel."],[25.39,30.02,"But some of the biggest contracts in an insurance group do not look like that at all."],[30.3,38.29,"Think of the core insurance platform, the policy administration system, the claims engine, or a specialist actuarial tool."],[38.29,46.05,"One vendor, many years of history, data and processes built around its product, and a renewal date that keeps coming back."],[46.55,49.68,"Here is the plan for the next twenty-five minutes or so."],[49.68,55.02,"First, a hard truth about competitive tools in a market without competition."],[55.02,60.46,"Then the difference between sole source and single source, which matters more than it sounds."],[60.74,70.67,"We will look at the situation from the vendor's side with Steele and Court's supplier preferencing model, and see how to get out of the danger zone."],[70.67,77.53,"Then the heart of the module: a seven-step sole-source playbook, applied to Nordvia's project P3, the Kelvar software licences."],[77.81,89.29,"We will work out what unused licences are worth, what a credible alternative looks like, which protections to buy for the next term, and how to plan backwards from the notice date."],[89.29,94.28,"We finish with the do and never do list, the role of Coupa, and your own renewal outline."],[95.22,98.43,"Let's start with the sentence on this slide."],[98.43,102.45,"An RFP sent to a vendor who knows it cannot lose is theatre."],[102.45,106.55,"An auction with one realistic bidder is an expensive RFQ."],[106.55,110.94,"And underneath: negotiate, and change the game before you sit down."],[111.45,112.84,"Why do I call it theatre?"],[112.84,118.25,"Because a request for proposal only creates pressure if the bidder believes it might lose."],[118.25,125.02,"A software vendor whose platform runs your policies knows perfectly well that you cannot move in six months."],[125.3,134.88,"So the RFP costs you weeks of work, it costs the vendor almost nothing, and the answer you receive is the same price list you would have received by email."],[134.88,139.44,"Worse, it tells the vendor that you do not understand your own position."],[139.44,141.51,"The same applies to auctions."],[141.79,146.46,"A reverse auction needs several qualified bidders who can genuinely win."],[146.46,149.79,"There is real evidence on what happens without them."],[149.79,160.84,"A review by the US Government Accountability Office of federal reverse auctions in fiscal year 2012 found that over a third of them had no interactive bidding at all."],[161.12,167.82,"One bid came in, nobody bid against it, and the agency often still paid the auction provider a fee."],[167.82,173.5,"A competitive tool without competition gives you nothing except the appearance of process."],[173.99,175.84,"So what is the alternative?"],[175.84,177.91,"A prepared negotiation."],[177.91,183.91,"And the second half of the sentence matters just as much: change the game before you sit down."],[183.91,188.45,"The power in a sole-source negotiation is not created at the table."],[188.73,195.64,"It is created months earlier, by collecting facts, building alternatives and reshaping what we actually need."],[195.64,198.1,"That is what this whole module is about."],[199.05,200.82,"First, a diagnosis."],[200.82,204.0,"Is this really sole source, or is it single source?"],[204.0,205.39,"Look at the two columns."],[205.89,207.61,"On the left, sole source."],[207.61,209.18,"No alternative exists."],[209.18,214.56,"The vendor owns proprietary intellectual property that nobody else can supply."],[214.56,220.52,"A specialist actuarial model, perhaps, or a data feed that only one provider holds."],[220.52,225.58,"Here the strategy is to protect yourself and, over time, reduce your dependency."],[225.58,227.65,"On the right, single source."],[227.93,229.33,"Alternatives do exist."],[229.33,234.2,"Other platforms could do the job, but switching is slow, risky or expensive."],[234.2,238.64,"Here the strategy is different: make the alternative credible."],[239.14,241.16,"Now, which column is highlighted?"],[241.16,243.51,"The right one, and that is deliberate."],[243.51,249.38,"Most incumbent software is single source with high switching cost, not true sole source."],[249.38,253.55,"And that is a weaker vendor position than it first looks."],[253.55,254.94,"Why?"],[254.94,256.42,"Because switching cost is not a law of nature."],[256.7,265.41,"It falls when you plan early, when you modularise the architecture, when you document your data, and when you give yourself a long enough runway."],[265.41,270.33,"A vendor who thinks it is irreplaceable often behaves as if it were sole source."],[270.61,276.92,"Our first job is to check that claim, honestly, and if it is single source, to start treating it that way."],[276.92,283.68,"Ask yourself about your own biggest software contract: if you had three years and a budget, could you move?"],[283.68,287.19,"If the answer is yes, you are in the right-hand column."],[288.13,295.57,"Now let's turn the chair around, as we did in Session 1, and look at ourselves through the vendor's eyes."],[295.57,299.76,"This is Steele and Court's supplier preferencing model from 1996."],[300.04,309.75,"On the vertical axis, how attractive our account is to the supplier: do we pay on time, are we easy to work with, do we bring reference value or growth?"],[309.75,316.8,"On the horizontal axis, the relative value of our business to the supplier: how much of their revenue do we represent?"],[317.31,319.03,"That gives four boxes."],[319.03,323.13,"Development, at the top left: low value to them today, but attractive."],[323.13,327.27,"They want to grow us, so they will offer good terms to win more."],[327.27,330.36,"Core, at the top right: high value and attractive."],[330.36,335.34,"They will invest in us, protect the relationship and give us their best people."],[335.62,339.2,"Nuisance, at the bottom left: low value and unattractive."],[339.2,344.96,"They spend little effort on us, and that is often what we experience as poor service."],[344.96,350.17,"And exploitable, at the bottom right, which is highlighted: high value, but unattractive."],[350.17,354.5,"They want the revenue, but they do not care much about the relationship."],[354.78,357.27,"In this box the supplier takes margin."],[357.77,361.56,"Here is the uncomfortable point for an insurer like Nordvia."],[361.56,367.02,"A global software vendor serves hundreds of financial institutions."],[367.02,369.85,"To that vendor, a mid-sized insurer is rarely core."],[369.85,374.72,"We are often nuisance or, if the contract is large, exploitable."],[374.72,377.08,"Put that next to our own Kraljic view."],[377.36,383.06,"For us, the core platform is strategic or bottleneck: high impact, high supply risk."],[383.06,385.5,"For them, we are exploitable."],[385.5,389.39,"Strategic for us and exploitable for them is the danger zone."],[389.67,402.07,"It explains a lot of what buyers experience: uplifts applied without discussion, account managers who change every year, and audit letters about licence compliance arriving just before renewal."],[402.07,403.31,"None of that is personal."],[403.31,409.34,"It is the rational behaviour of a supplier who sees our account in the bottom right box."],[409.84,412.88,"A small example from another category makes the point."],[412.88,416.8,"Imagine a marketing analytics tool used by one department."],[416.8,421.16,"The vendor is a small scale-up, and our Group is its biggest logo."],[421.16,426.8,"There we are core, and the same procurement team gets fast answers and roadmap influence."],[426.8,429.17,"Same buyer, different position."],[429.45,435.83,"The model reminds us that power depends on both sides of the relationship, not only on how much we spend."],[436.78,438.83,"So how do we get out of the danger zone?"],[438.83,440.66,"The slide gives four moves."],[441.16,443.57,"The first is to raise attractiveness."],[443.57,450.28,"Aggregate the volume across the Group so that the vendor sees one larger account instead of several small ones."],[450.28,457.04,"Offer reference value: a case study, a site visit, participation in a product advisory board."],[457.04,459.51,"And make payment easy and predictable."],[459.79,465.96,"The second move is to reduce dependency: build a credible alternative for the next contract term."],[465.96,466.89,"The third is to re-specify."],[466.89,469.39,"Do we really need every module?"],[469.39,477.28,"Could a different architecture, for example a separate document management tool, take part of the scope away from the platform?"],[477.56,481.5,"The fourth is to mine the data: usage, entitlement and deployment."],[481.5,484.81,"What have we bought, and what are we actually using?"],[485.31,491.14,"If you remember the Kearney Purchasing Chessboard from Session 1, you will recognise these."],[491.14,500.44,"Where supply power is high, the Chessboard tells us to change the nature of demand: re-specification, technical data mining, innovation breakthrough."],[500.44,504.7,"Combined with the preferencing logic, that gives us these four cards."],[504.98,508.9,"One warning from the speaker notes is worth repeating."],[508.9,511.97,"Paying late makes us less attractive, so fix that first."],[511.97,515.79,"But paying on time alone does not make us a customer of choice."],[515.79,517.67,"It is a hygiene factor."],[517.67,523.73,"Attractiveness comes from growth potential, reference value and ease of doing business together."],[524.01,527.88,"To move from exploitable towards core, we need more than good manners."],[528.83,533.32,"Now the heart of the module: the sole-source playbook in seven steps."],[533.32,540.07,"This comes from section C.8 of our research brief, and it is the structure you will use in the exercise."],[540.07,542.23,"Let's go through it step by step."],[542.72,544.47,"Step one: start early."],[544.47,548.24,"The rule of thumb is twelve to eighteen months before renewal."],[548.24,557.72,"That is practice, not a norm, but it reflects how long it takes to collect usage data, run an RFI to alternatives and align the executives."],[558.0,565.56,"And here is the most important detail on the slide: the notice date, not the expiry date, is the deadline for step one."],[565.56,576.41,"If the contract renews automatically unless you give notice nine months before expiry, then your real deadline is nine months earlier than the date everyone talks about."],[576.91,578.83,"Step two: know your usage."],[578.83,580.44,"Entitlement versus deployment."],[580.44,584.32,"How many named users have we paid for, and how many actually log in?"],[584.32,588.76,"Which modules have we licensed, and which are switched on?"],[588.76,593.68,"Unused licences are called shelfware, and we will put a value on them in a moment."],[593.96,598.63,"Step three: build the BATNA, the best alternative to a negotiated agreement."],[598.63,603.92,"An RFI to alternative vendors, an architecture study, or a reduced scope."],[603.92,605.83,"Step four: benchmark."],[605.83,612.6,"Price per user or per module, discount levels, and the caps that peers have obtained on annual uplifts."],[613.11,614.9,"Step five: unbundle."],[614.9,622.44,"A software contract usually mixes licences, support and maintenance, cloud subscription and professional services."],[622.44,626.22,"Each has its own logic and its own price, so separate them."],[626.22,627.91,"Step six: protect."],[627.91,633.42,"Uplift caps, the right to reduce volumes, exit terms, and the return of your data."],[633.7,635.77,"And step seven: one voice."],[635.77,642.2,"IT, Finance and Procurement aligned, with one mandate and one person speaking to the vendor."],[642.2,647.98,"That last step sounds soft, but in practice it is where many negotiations are lost."],[647.98,651.9,"A vendor's account team talks to many people in your organisation."],[652.18,659.95,"If one of them says, we would never move, the renewal is just a formality, the BATNA you built over six months is worth nothing."],[659.95,665.16,"Notice also that only one of the seven steps happens at the negotiating table."],[665.16,667.17,"The other six are preparation."],[668.12,669.84,"Let's apply this to Nordvia."],[669.84,673.73,"Remember, Nordvia is our fictional mid-sized insurer."],[673.73,677.71,"Project P3 is the Kelvar core insurance software."],[677.71,678.87,"Here are the facts."],[679.37,682.86,"Kelvar costs 11.2 million euros a year."],[682.86,687.11,"At the last renewal, the vendor applied a 9 percent uplift."],[687.11,689.04,"The renewal date is June 2027."],[689.04,694.72,"There is a nine-month notice period and an auto-renewal clause."],[694.72,699.68,"An estimated 20 to 25 percent of named-user licences are unused."],[699.96,706.67,"There are two credible alternative platforms on the market, but a migration would take 18 to 24 months."],[706.67,710.65,"And, crucially, no usage data was collected until now."],[711.15,715.67,"Before you go on, do two small calculations in your head."],[715.67,718.45,"First: what is the real deadline?"],[718.45,721.65,"Nine months before June 2027 is September 2026."],[721.65,727.28,"That is the date by which Nordvia must decide whether to give notice."],[727.56,737.63,"Depending on where you are on your own calendar, that date may be very close or already behind you, and that is exactly the point the speaker notes want to land."],[737.63,741.3,"Second: what does the auto-renewal clause do if nobody acts?"],[741.58,750.38,"It renews the contract on the existing terms, possibly with another uplift, and Nordvia loses the one moment in the cycle when it had leverage."],[750.38,752.5,"Nobody decided to renew."],[752.5,754.31,"The clause decided for them."],[754.81,757.09,"Now look at the last fact again."],[757.09,764.97,"A team with no usage data cannot argue about shelfware, cannot right-size and cannot benchmark price per active user."],[764.97,769.3,"The first action for P3 is therefore not a meeting with the vendor."],[769.3,771.41,"It is an internal usage audit."],[772.36,776.31,"Let's talk about the BATNA, because this is where many buyers get it wrong."],[776.31,779.31,"Is your alternative a bluff, or is it credible?"],[779.81,781.29,"On the left, the bluff."],[781.29,785.27,"Someone says to the vendor: we will move to another platform."],[785.27,788.58,"But there is no study, no budget and no timeline."],[788.58,794.92,"And the vendor knows the migration takes two years, because it has seen many customers try."],[794.92,796.79,"On the right, the credible version."],[796.79,799.76,"An RFI answered by both alternative vendors."],[800.04,802.96,"An architecture study and a migration plan."],[802.96,809.66,"And a decision point for the next term: a date by which Nordvia will decide whether to stay or start migrating."],[810.16,823.16,"The footnote quotes the core idea of Fisher and Ury in Getting to Yes: your negotiating power depends on the attractiveness of your alternative, so improve it before you negotiate."],[823.16,825.2,"For Kelvar, be honest."],[825.2,827.34,"Can Nordvia leave by June 2027?"],[827.34,828.54,"No."],[828.54,830.06,"Migration takes 18 to 24 months."],[830.34,833.76,"So the alternatives are not a threat to walk away next summer."],[833.76,835.9,"They are a BATNA for the next term."],[835.9,841.95,"They shape the length of the new contract, the exit rights, and the vendor's willingness to cap uplifts."],[841.95,845.17,"That is a quieter kind of power, but it is real."],[845.67,847.43,"Two lessons from the notes."],[847.43,856.52,"A bluff that is called destroys the position for years: the vendor learns that your threats are empty, and the next renewal starts even weaker."],[856.52,859.62,"And a credible BATNA does not need to be used to work."],[859.9,868.43,"The vendor's account team will report internally that the customer has run an RFI, has a migration study, and has a decision date."],[868.43,873.24,"That changes how their pricing committee behaves, even if you never migrate."],[874.19,878.19,"Now let's put a number on the cheapest lever of all: shelfware."],[878.19,882.23,"This is an illustrative calculation, so read the footnote."],[882.23,886.99,"The 50 percent licence share is an assumption for training, not a case fact."],[887.49,891.06,"Start with the total: 11.2 million euros a year."],[891.06,896.28,"Assume half of that is named-user licences, so 5.6 million euros."],[896.28,900.45,"The rest is support, hosting and other components."],[900.45,904.18,"Now, 20 to 25 percent of those licences are unused."],[904.18,907.87,"Twenty percent of 5.6 million is 1.12 million."],[907.87,910.87,"Twenty-five percent is 1.4 million."],[911.15,917.76,"So the value of unused licences is about 1.1 to 1.4 million euros a year."],[917.76,918.55,"And there is a twist."],[918.55,924.39,"The 9 percent uplift at the last renewal was applied to the unused licences too."],[924.39,929.71,"Nordvia has been paying a price increase on software that nobody uses."],[929.99,937.33,"On 1.12 million, that uplift alone is roughly one hundred thousand euros a year, again illustrative."],[937.83,939.65,"Why is this the cheapest lever?"],[939.65,942.93,"Because it costs nothing to the relationship."],[942.93,944.28,"You are not threatening anybody."],[944.28,947.49,"You are not asking the vendor to lower its list price."],[947.49,952.61,"You are saying: here is what we deploy, here is what we pay for, let's align them."],[952.61,954.3,"And it is objective."],[954.58,963.09,"Fisher and Ury call this using objective criteria: a standard that both sides can accept as fair, independent of will."],[963.09,965.28,"A vendor can argue about a discount."],[965.28,968.89,"It is much harder to argue against your own login data."],[969.17,981.77,"One practical caution: before you share usage data, check the licence metric in the contract and any compliance exposure, because a usage audit can also reveal under-licensing in another module."],[981.77,984.49,"Know your full picture before you open it."],[985.44,988.08,"The fifth step of the playbook was unbundle."],[988.08,993.89,"Let's look at it more closely, because it is where competition can come back into a sole-source deal."],[994.39,995.45,"Four cards."],[995.45,1002.75,"Licences: right-size them to actual deployment, and negotiate a right to reduce volumes during the term."],[1002.75,1009.07,"Support: cap the annual uplift, and consider whether every module needs the highest support level."],[1009.35,1018.16,"Cloud subscription: look at price per user, the length of the term, and where the data is located, which matters for GDPR and for DORA."],[1018.16,1022.98,"And services: implementation, upgrades, configuration and change requests."],[1022.98,1027.14,"Separate them from the licence, and compete them on the panel."],[1027.64,1029.81,"That last card deserves a moment."],[1029.81,1032.24,"The licence may be sole source."],[1032.24,1038.98,"But the people who configure the platform, run the upgrade projects and build the interfaces are often not."],[1038.98,1045.94,"Many vendors have certified partners, and independent integrators know the major insurance platforms well."],[1046.22,1053.32,"If services are bundled into the vendor's contract, you pay vendor rates for work the market could do."],[1053.32,1057.15,"Unbundle them, and you reopen competition on part of the spend."],[1057.15,1065.68,"This connects directly to module 2.3: those services can sit in the IT consulting panel, with a rate card and mini-competitions."],[1066.63,1068.89,"Step six was protect the next term."],[1068.89,1072.37,"This table lists six protections and why each matters."],[1072.87,1076.61,"First, a cap on renewal uplifts: no more 9 percent surprises."],[1076.61,1080.98,"A cap might be tied to an inflation index, with a ceiling."],[1080.98,1085.38,"Second, a price hold for the term, which gives budget certainty."],[1085.38,1089.98,"Third, the right to reduce volumes, so that you pay for what is deployed."],[1090.26,1094.58,"If the business shrinks or a module is retired, the bill follows."],[1094.58,1101.38,"Fourth, transition assistance: the vendor's obligation to help you move to another provider at the end."],[1101.38,1107.58,"It may look odd to negotiate your exit in a renewal, but this is what makes the BATNA real next time."],[1107.86,1116.63,"Fifth, data portability and return: your data, in a usable format, within a defined time, so you can exit without hostage-taking."],[1117.13,1120.47,"And sixth, DORA exit and transition clauses."],[1120.47,1134.96,"Under the Digital Operational Resilience Act, Article 30, paragraph 3, point f, contracts for ICT services that support critical or important functions must include exit strategies and transition periods."],[1135.24,1140.56,"A core insurance platform almost always supports a critical or important function."],[1140.56,1143.59,"So for Kelvar, this protection is not optional."],[1143.59,1151.76,"It is a regulatory requirement, and Legal and Compliance own the interpretation of exactly what the clause must say."],[1151.76,1153.78,"Notice the pattern in this table."],[1154.06,1159.97,"Most of these protections are worth more over five years than a headline discount in year one."],[1159.97,1165.3,"A vendor may offer an attractive discount precisely to avoid the uplift cap."],[1165.3,1168.34,"Do the multi-year arithmetic before you accept."],[1169.28,1172.25,"Now let's turn the playbook into a calendar."],[1172.25,1173.42,"Plan back from the notice date."],[1173.42,1175.1,"T is the renewal date."],[1175.6,1178.38,"At T minus 18 months, the usage audit."],[1178.38,1181.24,"At T minus 15 months, the RFI to alternatives."],[1181.24,1188.61,"At T minus 12 months, benchmarking and the mandate: what we want, what we accept, and who speaks."],[1188.61,1191.6,"At T minus 9 months, the notice decision."],[1191.6,1196.06,"And from T minus 6 months to T, the negotiation and signature."],[1196.34,1200.92,"The footnote is important: this is a practice timeline, not a norm."],[1200.92,1207.41,"And notice that with a nine-month notice period, the notice decision falls exactly at T minus 9."],[1207.92,1210.22,"Let's apply it to Kelvar."],[1210.22,1212.06,"T is June 2027."],[1212.06,1213.5,"T minus 9 is September 2026."],[1213.5,1216.56,"T minus 18 would have been December 2025."],[1216.56,1223.68,"So if Nordvia is starting now, it is already behind the ideal timeline, and it has no usage data."],[1223.68,1225.51,"What can it do?"],[1225.51,1230.43,"First, check whether a protective notice is still possible."],[1230.71,1240.13,"A protective notice means giving notice in time to stop the automatic renewal, while stating that you intend to negotiate a new agreement."],[1240.13,1241.55,"It keeps your options open."],[1241.55,1244.48,"Legal must confirm what the clause allows."],[1244.48,1249.07,"Second, compress the steps: run the usage audit and the RFI in parallel."],[1249.35,1264.48,"And if the notice date has already passed, accept that the auto-renewal clause shapes the next term, and focus on what can still be negotiated inside it, such as volume reductions or services, while building the BATNA properly for the cycle after."],[1264.98,1266.72,"One more practical point."],[1266.72,1272.14,"Put the notice date in the contract system as the critical date, with alerts well before it."],[1272.14,1274.86,"Most companies track expiry dates."],[1274.86,1276.77,"Far fewer track notice dates."],[1276.77,1280.94,"That small difference is often the whole story of a bad renewal."],[1281.88,1283.91,"Here is the do and never do list."],[1283.91,1285.61,"On the left, never."],[1285.61,1288.15,"An auction with one realistic bidder."],[1288.15,1289.18,"An RFP for show."],[1289.18,1291.57,"IT pre-committing before the mandate."],[1291.57,1293.81,"Long terms without exit rights."],[1293.81,1295.38,"On the right, do."],[1295.38,1297.75,"Facts first: usage and benchmarks."],[1297.75,1300.01,"A credible BATNA for the next term."],[1300.01,1301.78,"One voice, one mandate."],[1302.06,1304.28,"And protections over headline discount."],[1304.79,1313.15,"The speaker notes say the most common failure is the third one on the left: a stakeholder tells the vendor that the renewal is a formality."],[1313.15,1315.56,"It rarely happens out of bad intent."],[1315.84,1325.11,"An IT manager has a good working relationship with the vendor, wants to keep a project on track, and says something reassuring at a steering meeting."],[1325.11,1327.94,"The vendor's account manager writes it down."],[1328.22,1338.09,"The cure is simple but requires discipline: agree the mandate early, explain to the stakeholders why silence matters, and give them one sentence they can use."],[1338.09,1343.24,"For example: Procurement is leading the commercial discussion, please speak to them."],[1344.18,1345.98,"What role does Coupa play here?"],[1345.98,1350.74,"The honest answer is: data and documentation, not competition."],[1350.74,1354.04,"No auction, and no RFP for show either."],[1354.04,1360.93,"An RFI event in Coupa, sent to the alternative vendors, is how you build the BATNA, and it leaves a record."],[1361.21,1366.71,"Contract lifecycle management should alert on the notice date, not only on expiry."],[1366.71,1377.08,"And the workflow should record the justification for a sole-source award and the outcome of the negotiation, so that the next team, three years from now, starts with facts."],[1377.36,1384.21,"The exact alert settings and workflow fields depend on your configuration, so confirm them in your Coupa tenant."],[1385.16,1386.95,"Time for a poll question."],[1386.95,1388.62,"Think about Kelvar today."],[1388.62,1390.79,"What is Nordvia's strongest lever?"],[1390.79,1392.25,"A reverse auction."],[1392.25,1396.1,"The usage facts and a credible alternative for the next term."],[1396.1,1399.37,"Threatening to leave by June 2027."],[1399.37,1402.19,"Or accepting the uplift for a longer term."],[1402.19,1404.17,"Take a moment to choose your answer."],[1404.67,1411.03,"The right answer is the second: the usage facts and a credible alternative for the next term."],[1411.03,1413.53,"An auction has no second bidder."],[1413.53,1420.37,"A threat to leave by June 2027 is a bluff, because migration takes 18 to 24 months."],[1420.37,1424.6,"And accepting the uplift for a longer term just locks in the problem."],[1424.88,1432.21,"The alternatives cannot replace Kelvar by June 2027, but they shape the next term and the protections."],[1433.16,1434.76,"Now it is your turn."],[1434.76,1440.68,"This is the practice exercise for module 2.4: the sole-source renewal outline."],[1440.68,1445.73,"It takes about twelve minutes and you will find it under Module 2.4 in your workbook."],[1446.23,1456.51,"First, check the illustrative shelfware value: if half of 11.2 million euros were named-user licences and 20 percent of them were unused, what is the annual value?"],[1456.51,1460.2,"You already know the answer from a few minutes ago."],[1460.48,1476.08,"Then fill the eight boxes of the outline: usage facts, your BATNA and how you make it credible, benchmarks, what you unbundle, protections for the next term, the timeline back from the notice date, who speaks for you, and what you can give that is cheap for you."],[1476.36,1482.86,"That last box matters: a reference visit, a longer term in exchange for caps, or predictable payment."],[1482.86,1486.28,"Tick assumed wherever a fact is still missing."],[1486.28,1489.08,"You can use Kelvar or your own sole-source project."],[1489.08,1495.32,"If you want to do it now, pause the video, complete it in the Modules tab, and then come back."],[1495.6,1499.74,"Session 4 will turn this outline into a full negotiation plan."],[1500.69,1502.85,"Let's debrief with four questions."],[1502.85,1504.93,"What is your BATNA, honestly?"],[1504.93,1507.73,"Which protection matters most for the next term?"],[1507.73,1510.77,"Who speaks for Nordvia, and who must stay silent?"],[1510.77,1512.96,"And what would you unbundle and compete?"],[1513.46,1518.2,"If you wrote, leave Kelvar by June 2027, as your BATNA, prove it to yourself."],[1518.2,1521.54,"Where is the migration plan?"],[1521.54,1522.6,"Where is the budget?"],[1522.6,1526.39,"If you cannot prove it, it is the bluff from the earlier slide."],[1526.67,1538.73,"A stronger answer reads like this: two alternatives have answered an RFI, an architecture study is underway, and Nordvia will decide by a fixed date whether the next term is the last."],[1538.73,1542.18,"For protections, many teams pick the uplift cap."],[1542.46,1549.35,"That is fine, but for a platform supporting a critical function, the DORA exit clauses are not a choice."],[1549.35,1555.64,"And on unbundling, the services around the platform are usually the best candidate for competition."],[1556.59,1559.81,"Let's recap module 2.4 in three lines."],[1559.81,1565.26,"One: no real competition means negotiation, not an RFP for show or an auction."],[1565.26,1570.6,"Two: usage facts, benchmarks and a credible BATNA create the power."],[1570.6,1575.36,"Three: protect the next term and change the nature of demand."],[1575.86,1587.4,"Behind those three lines sits the playbook: start early from the notice date, know your usage, build a real alternative, benchmark, unbundle, protect, and speak with one voice."],[1587.4,1591.99,"In the next lesson, module 2.5, we go to the two ends of the map."],[1592.27,1600.41,"At one end, complex strategic partnerships like Northbyte, Nordvia's claims IT managed service, which need a multi-stage process."],[1600.41,1606.81,"At the other, the tail of small purchases, which needs a channel rather than a tender."],[1606.81,1612.0,"And we will put everything on one page: the strategy paper that records your decision."]]},{"id":"s2-l5","session":"s2","title":"Partnerships, the tail and the strategy paper","summary":"Run a multi-stage process for complex strategic deals, channel tail spend instead of sourcing it, and record your route to market on a one-page strategy paper.","from":88,"to":100,"audio":"audio/s2-l5.mp3","duration":1536.8,"segs":[[88,0.6,33.67,"Welcome back. This lesson covers module 2.5 of Session 2: strategic partnerships, the tail, and the strategy paper. So far in this session we have worked through three patterns. One supplier chosen by tender for a continuous site service, like cleaning. A panel with a rate card for a stream of professional assignments, like IT consulting. And, in the last lesson, a prepared negotiation when there is no real competition, like the Kelvar licences. Now we go to the two ends of the map."],[88,34.17,85.4,"At one end sit a handful of large, complex, high-risk deals. Managed IT services, claims IT, business process outsourcing. You cannot write a complete specification for these on day one, because the best solution is something you discover together with the bidders. They need a structured, multi-stage process. At the other end sits the tail: thousands of small orders, dozens or hundreds of suppliers, and very little value per transaction. Here the answer is not a better tender. It is a channel: a catalogue, a punch-out, a spot-buy process. Two very different problems, but both need a clear decision, and that decision needs to be written down. So the last part of this lesson is the one-page strategy paper, which records your route to market and goes to the business owner for agreement."],[88,85.9,108.73,"Here is the order. First the multi-stage process for complex deals, and how regulation shapes it. Then Nordvia's Northbyte contract as the partnership example. Then the tail in numbers, Nordvia's office supplies and print project, and the preconditions for an e-auction. And finally the strategy paper, with a seven-line example and your own draft."],[89,109.68,115.26,"Let's start with complex deals. This slide shows a multi-stage process in six steps."],[89,115.76,164.77,"Step one, the RFI, the request for information. Here you pre-qualify capability and compliance. Can this provider run a service of our size? Does it meet our information security standards? Can it support a function that is critical for an insurer? The RFI is a filter, not a beauty contest. Step two, RFP stage 1. You ask for the proposed solution and an indicative price. Indicative, because you do not yet know enough to ask for a binding one. Step three, the shortlist: three or four bidders. Why not more? Because the next step is expensive for everybody, and bidders invest more when they know they have a real chance. Why not fewer? Because with two bidders, competition becomes fragile: if one withdraws, you are back in a sole-source negotiation."],[89,165.27,210.72,"Step four, the dialogue. Structured workshops with each shortlisted bidder on the solution, on risk and on exit. This is where the solution gets better. A bidder might propose a different service model, or reveal a risk you had not seen, such as a dependency on a subcontractor. Treat every bidder equally, keep each bidder's ideas confidential, and document what was discussed. Step five, BAFO, the best and final offer. Each bidder submits a final offer against criteria that were fixed before the dialogue began. That is essential. If you change the criteria after you have seen the bids, you lose both fairness and credibility. Step six, the award, followed by contract and transition."],[89,211.22,268.25,"The footnote tells you where this structure comes from. It is borrowed from the EU public procurement procedures for complex requirements, the competitive procedure with negotiation and the competitive dialogue, in Articles 29 and 30 of the 2014 EU public procurement directive. As a private insurer, Nordvia is not bound by those procedures. We borrow the logic, staged reduction of bidders, dialogue sessions and final tenders against fixed criteria, without the legal constraints. In the Purchasing Chessboard, this is the territory of seek joint advantage: high supply power, high demand power, and value created together rather than squeezed out of the supplier. In Coupa, this can run as a sequence of events where responses from one stage carry over to the next; how exactly that is set up depends on your configuration, so confirm it in your Coupa tenant."],[89,268.75,340.14,"Let me give you a picture of how this feels in practice outside IT. Imagine an insurer outsourcing its document scanning and inbound mail handling, a typical business process outsourcing deal. In the RFI, eight providers answer, and three of them cannot show the security certifications you require, so they drop out. RFP stage 1 brings five solutions, from fully manual scanning centres to automated capture with machine reading. You shortlist three. In the dialogue workshops, one bidder points out that half of your inbound mail is already digital, and proposes a smaller physical operation with a digital intake. Nobody in your team had specified that. That is the value of the dialogue: the requirement improves before the price is fixed. Then, at BAFO, all three bidders price the improved scope against the same criteria, and you compare like with like. Notice what you did not do. You did not send a hundred-page specification on day one and pick the cheapest answer. And you did not sit down with one favourite provider and negotiate privately. You kept competition alive while you learned."],[90,341.09,358.82,"In insurance, the strategy for a complex deal is not only commercial. Regulation changes the timeline and the criteria. This table asks four questions. Remember, these come from the regulatory triage you did in Session 1. Here they become part of the plan."],[90,359.32,404.51,"First question: is this outsourcing of a critical or important function or activity, under Solvency II Article 49? If yes, the supervisor must be notified before the outsourcing takes place. That is a lead time, and it has to appear in your timeline. Second: provider selection, under the Delegated Regulation that implements Solvency II, Article 274. It requires a detailed examination of the provider's ability and capacity, any authorisation it needs, conflicts of interest, and confidentiality and security standards. That examination belongs in your RFI and your evaluation criteria, not in an annex nobody reads."],[90,405.01,437.97,"Third: is this an ICT service supporting a critical or important function, under DORA Article 28? If yes, you need a pre-contract risk assessment, an assessment of concentration risk, and an exit strategy that is actually tested. Fourth: is it any ICT service at all? Then it goes into the register of information, and the contract must contain the key clauses of DORA Article 30, paragraph 2, such as service descriptions, data location and access, and termination rights."],[90,438.47,480.97,"Two notes at the bottom of the slide. EIOPA's guidelines on outsourcing to cloud service providers were withdrawn from 17 January 2025, when DORA started to apply. If a colleague quotes them, gently point that out. And Legal and Compliance own the interpretation. Procurement's job is to ask the questions early and build the answers into the timeline and the criteria. Procurement does not decide alone whether something is a critical function. The practical effect is easy to remember: a yes to any of these questions makes the process longer and the criteria richer. Plan for it at the start, not when the contract is already being drafted."],[91,481.92,504.38,"Let's make it concrete with Nordvia project P8, the Northbyte partnership case. Northbyte runs Nordvia's claims IT managed service. It costs 19.4 million euros a year, and the contract expires in December 2027. It supports a critical function and it is in the DORA register. And there is no tested exit plan."],[91,504.88,553.47,"Look at the route on the slide: a multi-stage negotiated process, starting now. Why now, when the expiry is December 2027? Count backwards. An RFI, an RFP stage, a shortlist, several rounds of dialogue, BAFO, award. Then, if the service changes provider, a transition that for a claims platform could easily take a year or more, plus any supervisory notification. December 2027 is not far away at all. And look at the last line: exit feasibility as a selection criterion, not a contract annex. In other words, when you score the bidders, you score how realistically you could leave them. A provider that cannot describe a credible exit path should lose points, before you sign."],[91,553.97,599.97,"Now the subtle point from the speaker notes. Even if Northbyte stays, the process builds the exit plan that DORA requires. Think about it. A tested exit strategy needs somewhere to exit to. If Nordvia has never talked to alternative providers, it has no real exit, only a document. By running a genuine multi-stage process, Nordvia learns who could take over, how long it would take, and what data and knowledge would need to move. That is valuable whoever wins. And a final word on tone. This is a partnership process. Northbyte is not the enemy. If it competes well and wins, Nordvia gets a better contract, a better solution and a tested exit. That is seek joint advantage in practice."],[91,600.47,640.17,"How does Nordvia actually start? The first step is internal: confirm with Legal and Compliance that the service supports a critical or important function, which the register already suggests, and check whether a supervisory notification would be needed if the provider changed. In parallel, define what an exit would require: which data, which interfaces, which knowledge sits only in Northbyte's teams. Those answers become RFI questions. Then Nordvia can approach the market, including Northbyte itself, with a clear message: we are running a structured process, and the ability to exit is part of what we will score."],[92,641.12,645.39,"Now let's jump to the other end of the map: the tail. Here are four numbers."],[92,645.89,679.17,"The first three come from a McKinsey article published in 2018. Tail spend is 80 to 90 percent of purchased items, but only 10 to 20 percent of spend, with an estimated savings potential of 5 to 15 percent of that tail spend. The fourth number is our own Group: 86 percent of supplier records are below 50 thousand euros of spend, and together they account for 12 percent of spend. So the shape is the same. A huge number of suppliers and transactions, a small share of the money."],[92,679.67,701.22,"Read the footnote carefully. The McKinsey work addresses mainly direct industrial spend, so treat the numbers as an order of magnitude for us, not a precise benchmark. And many other tail statistics you will see in presentations come from vendors selling tail-spend tools, and could not be traced to a primary source. Use them with care."],[92,701.72,741.15,"The key message from the notes: the tail is an efficiency topic first. The savings on price are real, but they are secondary to the process cost. Think about what a single small purchase order costs: a requisition, an approval, a supplier set up in the system, a purchase order, a goods receipt, an invoice to match and pay. Multiply that by thousands of orders a year, and the process cost can rival the price savings. If you send a team to run a full tender for every small category, you spend expensive procurement time on the least valuable spend. That is why we say: channel the tail, do not source it."],[92,741.65,779.9,"It also helps to look at where the tail sits in the Group. The Group's spend data shows big differences between entities. In one entity, Entity F, about 30 percent of spend sits in the tail, and the entity works with around 1,780 suppliers, far more than entities of a similar size. In another, Entity I, the tail is around 26 percent of spend. Those are the places where a catalogue and guided buying would change daily life most, simply because so many small orders go to so many suppliers. When you hear tail, think first about where the transactions are, not only where the money is."],[93,780.85,799.35,"Here is Nordvia's tail example: project P5, office supplies and print. 2.3 million euros with 31 suppliers, and 1,900 purchase orders a year below 1,000 euros each. No catalogue. Let's follow the route on the slide, line by line."],[93,799.85,843.8,"First: is there a Group framework? This question always comes first. If the Group or another entity already has a framework for office supplies, the fastest and often cheapest route is to call off from it. Running your own tender would duplicate work and split volume. Second, if there is no framework: an RFQ on a basket of top items. You do not ask for prices on ten thousand products. You take the items that make up most of the volume, perhaps the top one hundred, with clear specifications and annual quantities, and you ask suppliers to price that basket. Third: an English reverse auction, if four to five qualified bidders compete. We will look at the preconditions on the next slide."],[93,844.3,886.5,"Fourth: a punch-out catalogue. Once the supplier is chosen, users order through Coupa from the supplier's catalogue, with negotiated prices, approvals already built in, and invoices that match automatically. Those 1,900 purchase orders become guided, low-effort transactions. And fifth: print as a separate lot. Why? Because print is a different market. Office supplies are standard products from distributors. Print, such as policy documents, marketing material or business cards, depends on paper, machines and make-ready costs, and different suppliers are good at it. Putting both in one lot would reduce the number of capable bidders."],[93,887.0,903.26,"The notes add something important: this is the one project of the day where an auction fits. Precise basket, many bidders, price decides, low switching cost. Keep that in mind, because in every other case we have discussed, an auction was on the never list."],[94,904.21,909.69,"So when does an e-auction fit? The slide gives four preconditions. All four must hold."],[94,910.19,960.63,"First, specification. It must be precise, so that bids can be ranked automatically. If you need a human to interpret each offer, an auction cannot work. Second, bidders: at least four to five qualified bidders. That figure comes from Smeltzer and Carr, 2003, and it is a guide, not a law. Remember the government example from the last lesson: auctions with no interactive bidding. Third, the decision: price, or a formula that combines price with a few measurable factors, decides. If quality judgements matter, an auction is the wrong tool. Fourth, the relationship. It must be able to bear the pressure, and there must be a credible intent to award. Suppliers who suspect that the auction is just a way to squeeze the incumbent will not bid seriously the next time."],[94,961.13,1020.51,"The footnote points to the evidence. Sandy Jap's research, in 2002 and 2007, found that open-bid auctions, where suppliers see prices, damage supplier trust more than sealed formats, without producing higher savings. And Article 35 of the same 2014 EU public procurement directive, sets the rules for public e-auctions, which are a useful model of discipline. Now, why do these four preconditions fit P5 and not the others? Office supplies have a precise basket, many distributors, price decides and switching is easy. Cleaning fails on precondition three, because quality and staff matter. Consulting fails on one and three, because every assignment is different. Kelvar fails on two, because there is one realistic bidder. Session 3 goes much deeper into auction design. Today the point is only where an auction fits on the map, and where it never fits."],[94,1021.01,1062.25,"Let me add a story that many buyers will recognise. A facilities team once ran an auction for a building maintenance contract, because the tool was available and the CFO wanted savings. The specification was a list of buildings with a few service lines, and quality was supposed to be checked afterwards. Three bidders took part. The winner bid aggressively, then started to send change requests for every job that was not literally in the list, and the quality complaints began in the second month. Within a year the contract cost more than the old one. None of the four preconditions had held. The tool did exactly what it was designed to do. It was simply the wrong tool."],[95,1063.2,1069.28,"Now let's bring the decision onto paper. This is the one-page strategy paper, in seven blocks."],[95,1069.78,1119.41,"Block one, need and scope: the validated need, the value, the expiry date and, yes, the notice date. Block two, position: the Kraljic quadrant, the Chessboard move, and how the incumbent sees us, from the Steele and Court model. Block three, route to market: the six decisions from module 2.1, one line each. Number of suppliers, lots, contract type, term, mechanism and timing. Block four, options rejected: what we will not do, and why. Block five, the timeline, planned back from the notice date. Block six, risks and regulation: outsourcing, DORA, personal data under GDPR, and labour law, for example staff transfer or the risk of hidden temporary agency work. And block seven, the decision requested: from whom, and by when."],[95,1119.91,1147.11,"Why only one page? The notes say it clearly: one page is a discipline. If the route needs two pages to justify, the analysis is not finished. A business owner, a CIO or a CFO will give your paper a few minutes. In those minutes they need to see the problem, the route, the alternatives you considered and the decision you need. Anything more belongs in the Sourcing Case behind the paper, as supporting evidence."],[95,1147.61,1168.86,"And notice block four. Many strategy documents list only the chosen route. That leaves the reader wondering whether you considered anything else. Writing down the rejected options shows your reasoning, and it pre-empts the obvious objections. If the business owner asks why not just keep the favourite supplier, the answer is already on the page."],[96,1169.81,1175.68,"Here is what that looks like for Nordvia's IT consulting project, P2, in seven lines."],[96,1176.18,1218.02,"Need: IT consulting, 6.1 million euros a year, 12 firms, no framework. Position: leverage, with real competition among suppliers. Route: a panel with three lots and four firms per lot, a rate card and call-off rules. Rejected: a single supplier, because of dependency, and an open RFP for every job, because it is too slow. Timeline: the framework RFP in the first quarter, the panel live by the third quarter. The quarters here are illustrative. Risks: the labour-law model for each lot, especially for staff augmentation, and the DORA clauses written into the framework. Decision: the CIO."],[96,1218.52,1248.41,"Notice how short it is. Every line carries a decision or a fact, nothing else. And the notes point to the line the CIO will read twice: the rejected options. Why? Because the CIO may well have a favourite firm, and the line, single supplier rejected because of dependency, speaks directly to that. It also explains why the business cannot just keep calling off without competition. If the CIO agrees with that line, the rest of the paper usually follows."],[96,1248.91,1278.11,"Try reading it from the CIO's point of view. In under a minute, the CIO knows what is being bought, why there is competition, how the panel will work, what was considered and rejected, when it will be live, which risks Legal and Compliance are watching, and what decision is being asked for. That is the test for your own paper: could a busy executive say yes or no after one read? If not, cut, sharpen, and move the detail into the Sourcing Case behind it."],[97,1279.06,1300.44,"A quick poll question for you. Which route will your own project take? One supplier by tender. A panel of preferred suppliers. A sole or single source negotiation. An RFQ and e-auction. A catalogue or Group framework call-off. Or a multi-stage negotiated process. Think about your own Sourcing Case, and choose."],[97,1300.94,1323.71,"In a classroom, we would collect the distribution. A mix of all patterns is a good sign: it shows the logic is being applied rather than one habit, such as three quotes for everything, being replaced by another habit. If you were unsure between two routes, go back to the first question of the route-to-market logic: is there a real alternative within the contract horizon?"],[98,1324.66,1344.79,"Now the exercise for module 2.5: your one-page strategy paper. It takes about twelve minutes, and you will find it under Module 2.5 in your workbook. Choose your route to market. Fill the seven blocks in short lines. Write the options you rejected. Plan back from the notice date. And name who must decide."],[98,1345.29,1365.01,"It will not be finished now, and that is fine. The aim is a draft that is good enough to take to the business owner in Fieldwork 2. Where you are missing a fact, such as the exact notice date, write what you assume and mark it to check. If you want to do it now, pause the video, write your draft in the Modules tab, and then come back."],[98,1365.51,1395.51,"A few tips as you write. Use the language of the business, not of procurement. Write the problem in the business owner's words, for example HQ complaints about cleaning, or day rates that vary widely for the same profile. Keep each block to one or two lines. In block three, the route, make sure the six decisions are consistent with each other: a panel with one supplier per lot is not a panel. And in block seven, be precise: not Management, but a named role and a date."],[99,1396.46,1407.17,"Let's debrief with three questions. Which route did you choose, and which did you reject? Is your timeline realistic from the notice date? And who outside Procurement must sign off?"],[99,1407.67,1450.98,"On the first question, check that your rejected options are real alternatives that someone might propose, not straw men. On the second, many first drafts plan from the expiry date and discover the notice date only later. If your project is a tender, remember that a standard tender often needs nine to twelve months before expiry, plus mobilisation, which is a practice estimate. On the third, think beyond the business owner. If your triage flagged outsourcing, DORA, personal data or staff transfer, Risk and Compliance, Legal or the Data Protection Officer need to be part of the decision. Better to name them now than to discover them at contract signature."],[100,1451.93,1470.68,"Let's recap module 2.5 in three lines. One: complex deals need a multi-stage process with dialogue and BAFO. Two: the tail is channelled through catalogues, punch-out and spot buy. Three: one page records the route, the rejected options and the decision."],[100,1471.18,1510.12,"Behind the first line sits the regulatory layer: Solvency II outsourcing, provider examination, and DORA, which together make exit feasibility a selection criterion. Behind the second sits the reminder that an e-auction fits only when all four preconditions hold, which is rare in indirect categories but true for a basket of office supplies. In the next lesson we bring it all together in Case 2. Four Nordvia projects, four routes to market, one logic. We will compare answers, look at what separated strong answers from weaker ones, and prepare you for Fieldwork 2 and Session 3."],[100,1510.62,1535.6,"Before you move on, take a minute with your own project. Which end of the map is it closer to? If it is a complex, high-risk service, have you planned the RFI, the shortlist, the dialogue and the BAFO, and have you asked the regulatory questions? If it is closer to the tail, could a catalogue or an existing framework solve it with far less effort than a tender? Either way, the paper is where you show your reasoning."]],"questions":[{"after":88,"q":"Which pairing of project and route best reflects the two ends of the map described in this lesson?","options":["Claims IT managed service: catalogue; office supplies: multi-stage dialogue","Claims IT managed service: multi-stage process with dialogue; office supplies: a channel such as a punch-out catalogue","Both: an RFQ with three quotes","Both: a sole-source negotiation"],"answer":1,"explain":"Complex, high-risk services cannot be fully specified upfront, so they need a staged process with dialogue. Thousands of small orders need a low-effort channel, not a tender.","t":108.73},{"after":89,"q":"Nordvia plans to outsource its customer contact centre. The solution is not fully known, and seven providers answered the RFI. What is the best next step after RFP stage 1?","options":["Award to the lowest indicative price straight away","Keep all seven bidders through every stage for maximum competition","Change the evaluation criteria after seeing the BAFO prices","Shortlist three or four bidders and run dialogue workshops before BAFO against criteria fixed in advance"],"answer":3,"explain":"A shortlist of three or four keeps competition real while making the dialogue affordable for everybody. Final offers must be judged against criteria fixed before the dialogue, or the process loses fairness and credibility.","t":340.14},{"after":90,"q":"Nordvia's sourcing team wants to replace the provider of an ICT service that supports a critical function. Which statement about the strategy is correct?","options":["Regulation only matters once the contract is drafted","The EIOPA cloud guidelines set the exit requirements","DORA requires a pre-contract assessment, concentration risk analysis and a tested exit, so the timeline and criteria must include them, with Legal and Compliance interpreting","Procurement decides alone whether the function is critical"],"answer":2,"explain":"DORA Article 28 shapes both timeline and criteria from the start. The EIOPA cloud guidelines were withdrawn from 17 January 2025, and Legal and Compliance own the interpretation.","t":480.97},{"after":91,"q":"The CIO says: Northbyte will win anyway, so why run a multi-stage process for the claims IT service? What is the strongest answer?","options":["Because policy requires three quotes for every contract","Because the process builds the alternatives a tested DORA exit plan needs, and improves terms and solution even if Northbyte stays","Because Northbyte must be replaced to comply with DORA","Because an auction would give a lower price"],"answer":1,"explain":"A tested exit needs somewhere to exit to. Running a real process gives Nordvia that knowledge and a better contract, whoever wins.","t":640.17},{"after":92,"q":"A colleague proposes running a full RFP for each of 40 small tail categories to capture the 5 to 15 percent savings potential. What is the main flaw?","options":["The tail is mainly an efficiency topic: process cost matters more than price, so channel it through catalogues and spot buy","Tail categories have no savings potential at all","RFPs are not allowed below 50 thousand euros","The McKinsey figures prove the savings will be exactly 15 percent"],"answer":0,"explain":"Tail spend is many items but little money, so tendering each category spends expensive procurement time on the least valuable spend. The savings figures are an order of magnitude from mainly direct spend, not a guarantee.","t":779.9},{"after":93,"q":"For P5 office supplies and print, what should Nordvia check first?","options":["Whether a Group or entity framework already covers the need","Whether an English reverse auction can be run next month","Whether all 31 suppliers can be kept with a catalogue each","Whether print can be added to the cleaning tender"],"answer":0,"explain":"If a framework already exists, calling off from it is usually faster and keeps Group volume together. Only if there is none does Nordvia move to an RFQ on a basket, a possible auction and a punch-out catalogue.","t":903.26},{"after":94,"q":"The Marketing team wants an e-auction to choose a creative agency for a brand campaign. Three agencies are interested. Which preconditions fail?","options":["Only the relationship precondition","None: an auction will work","The specification cannot be ranked automatically, quality judgement decides rather than price, and there are fewer than four to five bidders","Only the number of bidders"],"answer":2,"explain":"Creative work is judged on quality and ideas, not ranked automatically on price, and three bidders fall below the four to five that Smeltzer and Carr suggest. All four preconditions must hold for an auction to fit.","t":1062.25},{"after":95,"q":"A draft strategy paper runs to four pages and lists only the chosen route. What is the most useful advice?","options":["Add a fifth page on market background","Cut it to one page and add the options rejected and why, so the reasoning is visible and objections are pre-empted","Remove the timeline, as business owners do not read it","Send it to the supplier for comments first"],"answer":1,"explain":"One page is a discipline: if the route needs more, the analysis is not finished. The rejected options show your reasoning and answer the obvious objection before it is raised.","t":1168.86},{"after":96,"q":"In the P2 strategy paper, which line is the CIO most likely to read twice, and why?","options":["The need line, because the 6.1 million euros is surprising","The timeline, because quarters are always too short","The decision line, because the CIO dislikes approving things","The rejected options, because they speak directly to keeping a favourite firm without competition"],"answer":3,"explain":"The rejected options line explains why a single supplier and uncontrolled call-offs are not the route. If the CIO accepts that line, the rest of the paper usually follows.","t":1278.11},{"after":99,"q":"Your draft strategy paper for a facilities contract plans the tender from the expiry date in 10 months. The notice period is 6 months and the triage flagged staff transfer. What should you fix first?","options":["Nothing: the expiry date is the real deadline","Switch to an e-auction to save time","Replan from the notice date, which is only 4 months away, and involve Legal or HR on the staff transfer before the business owner meeting","Remove the staff transfer risk from the paper to keep it to one page"],"answer":2,"explain":"The notice date is the real deadline, and a standard tender usually needs more time than 4 months, so the timeline or a bridge decision must be addressed. Flagged regulatory or labour-law issues must be on the page with the right people involved.","t":1450.98}],"cues":[[0.6,1.35,"Welcome back."],[1.35,8.62,"This lesson covers module 2.5 of Session 2: strategic partnerships, the tail, and the strategy paper."],[8.62,12.72,"So far in this session we have worked through three patterns."],[12.72,17.58,"One supplier chosen by tender for a continuous site service, like cleaning."],[17.86,23.45,"A panel with a rate card for a stream of professional assignments, like IT consulting."],[23.45,31.23,"And, in the last lesson, a prepared negotiation when there is no real competition, like the Kelvar licences."],[31.23,33.67,"Now we go to the two ends of the map."],[34.17,38.55,"At one end sit a handful of large, complex, high-risk deals."],[38.55,42.27,"Managed IT services, claims IT, business process outsourcing."],[42.27,51.64,"You cannot write a complete specification for these on day one, because the best solution is something you discover together with the bidders."],[51.64,54.69,"They need a structured, multi-stage process."],[54.97,63.24,"At the other end sits the tail: thousands of small orders, dozens or hundreds of suppliers, and very little value per transaction."],[63.24,65.72,"Here the answer is not a better tender."],[65.72,70.31,"It is a channel: a catalogue, a punch-out, a spot-buy process."],[70.59,77.04,"Two very different problems, but both need a clear decision, and that decision needs to be written down."],[77.04,85.4,"So the last part of this lesson is the one-page strategy paper, which records your route to market and goes to the business owner for agreement."],[85.9,87.27,"Here is the order."],[87.27,92.22,"First the multi-stage process for complex deals, and how regulation shapes it."],[92.22,96.36,"Then Nordvia's Northbyte contract as the partnership example."],[96.36,103.59,"Then the tail in numbers, Nordvia's office supplies and print project, and the preconditions for an e-auction."],[103.87,108.73,"And finally the strategy paper, with a seven-line example and your own draft."],[109.68,111.64,"Let's start with complex deals."],[111.64,115.27,"This slide shows a multi-stage process in six steps."],[115.76,119.08,"Step one, the RFI, the request for information."],[119.08,122.13,"Here you pre-qualify capability and compliance."],[122.13,124.79,"Can this provider run a service of our size?"],[124.79,129.12,"Does it meet our information security standards?"],[129.12,132.6,"Can it support a function that is critical for an insurer?"],[132.6,135.43,"The RFI is a filter, not a beauty contest."],[135.43,137.07,"Step two, RFP stage 1."],[137.35,140.9,"You ask for the proposed solution and an indicative price."],[140.9,145.19,"Indicative, because you do not yet know enough to ask for a binding one."],[145.19,148.8,"Step three, the shortlist: three or four bidders."],[148.8,149.89,"Why not more?"],[149.89,156.17,"Because the next step is expensive for everybody, and bidders invest more when they know they have a real chance."],[156.45,157.51,"Why not fewer?"],[157.51,164.77,"Because with two bidders, competition becomes fragile: if one withdraws, you are back in a sole-source negotiation."],[165.27,167.03,"Step four, the dialogue."],[167.03,172.59,"Structured workshops with each shortlisted bidder on the solution, on risk and on exit."],[172.59,174.81,"This is where the solution gets better."],[174.81,183.23,"A bidder might propose a different service model, or reveal a risk you had not seen, such as a dependency on a subcontractor."],[183.51,190.36,"Treat every bidder equally, keep each bidder's ideas confidential, and document what was discussed."],[190.36,193.48,"Step five, BAFO, the best and final offer."],[193.48,199.28,"Each bidder submits a final offer against criteria that were fixed before the dialogue began."],[199.28,200.6,"That is essential."],[200.88,207.11,"If you change the criteria after you have seen the bids, you lose both fairness and credibility."],[207.11,210.72,"Step six, the award, followed by contract and transition."],[211.22,214.65,"The footnote tells you where this structure comes from."],[214.65,230.14,"It is borrowed from the EU public procurement procedures for complex requirements, the competitive procedure with negotiation and the competitive dialogue, in Articles 29 and 30 of the 2014 EU public procurement directive."],[230.42,234.7,"As a private insurer, Nordvia is not bound by those procedures."],[234.7,244.29,"We borrow the logic, staged reduction of bidders, dialogue sessions and final tenders against fixed criteria, without the legal constraints."],[244.57,255.75,"In the Purchasing Chessboard, this is the territory of seek joint advantage: high supply power, high demand power, and value created together rather than squeezed out of the supplier."],[256.03,268.25,"In Coupa, this can run as a sequence of events where responses from one stage carry over to the next; how exactly that is set up depends on your configuration, so confirm it in your Coupa tenant."],[268.75,272.71,"Let me give you a picture of how this feels in practice outside IT."],[272.71,280.9,"Imagine an insurer outsourcing its document scanning and inbound mail handling, a typical business process outsourcing deal."],[280.9,289.06,"In the RFI, eight providers answer, and three of them cannot show the security certifications you require, so they drop out."],[289.34,297.29,"RFP stage 1 brings five solutions, from fully manual scanning centres to automated capture with machine reading."],[297.29,298.3,"You shortlist three."],[298.3,309.07,"In the dialogue workshops, one bidder points out that half of your inbound mail is already digital, and proposes a smaller physical operation with a digital intake."],[309.35,311.8,"Nobody in your team had specified that."],[311.8,317.33,"That is the value of the dialogue: the requirement improves before the price is fixed."],[317.33,325.25,"Then, at BAFO, all three bidders price the improved scope against the same criteria, and you compare like with like."],[325.25,326.97,"Notice what you did not do."],[327.25,332.57,"You did not send a hundred-page specification on day one and pick the cheapest answer."],[332.57,337.46,"And you did not sit down with one favourite provider and negotiate privately."],[337.46,340.14,"You kept competition alive while you learned."],[341.09,345.61,"In insurance, the strategy for a complex deal is not only commercial."],[345.61,349.49,"Regulation changes the timeline and the criteria."],[349.49,351.18,"This table asks four questions."],[351.18,356.9,"Remember, these come from the regulatory triage you did in Session 1."],[356.9,358.82,"Here they become part of the plan."],[359.32,366.87,"First question: is this outsourcing of a critical or important function or activity, under Solvency II Article 49?"],[366.87,372.38,"If yes, the supervisor must be notified before the outsourcing takes place."],[372.38,376.66,"That is a lead time, and it has to appear in your timeline."],[376.94,384.0,"Second: provider selection, under the Delegated Regulation that implements Solvency II, Article 274."],[384.0,396.98,"It requires a detailed examination of the provider's ability and capacity, any authorisation it needs, conflicts of interest, and confidentiality and security standards."],[397.26,404.51,"That examination belongs in your RFI and your evaluation criteria, not in an annex nobody reads."],[405.01,412.13,"Third: is this an ICT service supporting a critical or important function, under DORA Article 28?"],[412.13,421.82,"If yes, you need a pre-contract risk assessment, an assessment of concentration risk, and an exit strategy that is actually tested."],[421.82,424.04,"Fourth: is it any ICT service at all?"],[424.32,437.97,"Then it goes into the register of information, and the contract must contain the key clauses of DORA Article 30, paragraph 2, such as service descriptions, data location and access, and termination rights."],[438.47,441.29,"Two notes at the bottom of the slide."],[441.29,450.17,"EIOPA's guidelines on outsourcing to cloud service providers were withdrawn from 17 January 2025, when DORA started to apply."],[450.17,453.35,"If a colleague quotes them, gently point that out."],[453.35,456.73,"And Legal and Compliance own the interpretation."],[457.01,463.72,"Procurement's job is to ask the questions early and build the answers into the timeline and the criteria."],[463.72,468.62,"Procurement does not decide alone whether something is a critical function."],[468.62,476.72,"The practical effect is easy to remember: a yes to any of these questions makes the process longer and the criteria richer."],[477.0,480.97,"Plan for it at the start, not when the contract is already being drafted."],[481.92,487.45,"Let's make it concrete with Nordvia project P8, the Northbyte partnership case."],[487.45,491.22,"Northbyte runs Nordvia's claims IT managed service."],[491.22,496.67,"It costs 19.4 million euros a year, and the contract expires in December 2027."],[496.67,502.1,"It supports a critical function and it is in the DORA register."],[502.1,504.38,"And there is no tested exit plan."],[504.88,511.48,"Look at the route on the slide: a multi-stage negotiated process, starting now."],[511.48,514.78,"Why now, when the expiry is December 2027?"],[514.78,515.62,"Count backwards."],[515.62,522.29,"An RFI, an RFP stage, a shortlist, several rounds of dialogue, BAFO, award."],[522.57,532.97,"Then, if the service changes provider, a transition that for a claims platform could easily take a year or more, plus any supervisory notification."],[532.97,535.98,"December 2027 is not far away at all."],[535.98,542.13,"And look at the last line: exit feasibility as a selection criterion, not a contract annex."],[542.41,547.7,"In other words, when you score the bidders, you score how realistically you could leave them."],[547.7,553.46,"A provider that cannot describe a credible exit path should lose points, before you sign."],[553.97,556.55,"Now the subtle point from the speaker notes."],[556.55,561.85,"Even if Northbyte stays, the process builds the exit plan that DORA requires."],[561.85,563.38,"Think about it."],[563.38,566.81,"A tested exit strategy needs somewhere to exit to."],[566.81,573.04,"If Nordvia has never talked to alternative providers, it has no real exit, only a document."],[573.32,582.23,"By running a genuine multi-stage process, Nordvia learns who could take over, how long it would take, and what data and knowledge would need to move."],[582.23,584.44,"That is valuable whoever wins."],[584.44,586.4,"And a final word on tone."],[586.4,588.57,"This is a partnership process."],[588.57,590.47,"Northbyte is not the enemy."],[590.75,597.21,"If it competes well and wins, Nordvia gets a better contract, a better solution and a tested exit."],[597.21,599.97,"That is seek joint advantage in practice."],[600.47,602.57,"How does Nordvia actually start?"],[602.57,617.64,"The first step is internal: confirm with Legal and Compliance that the service supports a critical or important function, which the register already suggests, and check whether a supervisory notification would be needed if the provider changed."],[617.92,626.31,"In parallel, define what an exit would require: which data, which interfaces, which knowledge sits only in Northbyte's teams."],[626.31,629.17,"Those answers become RFI questions."],[629.45,640.18,"Then Nordvia can approach the market, including Northbyte itself, with a clear message: we are running a structured process, and the ability to exit is part of what we will score."],[641.12,644.05,"Now let's jump to the other end of the map: the tail."],[644.05,645.39,"Here are four numbers."],[645.89,650.57,"The first three come from a McKinsey article published in 2018."],[650.57,662.17,"Tail spend is 80 to 90 percent of purchased items, but only 10 to 20 percent of spend, with an estimated savings potential of 5 to 15 percent of that tail spend."],[662.45,672.32,"The fourth number is our own Group: 86 percent of supplier records are below 50 thousand euros of spend, and together they account for 12 percent of spend."],[672.32,674.46,"So the shape is the same."],[674.46,679.17,"A huge number of suppliers and transactions, a small share of the money."],[679.67,681.47,"Read the footnote carefully."],[681.47,690.31,"The McKinsey work addresses mainly direct industrial spend, so treat the numbers as an order of magnitude for us, not a precise benchmark."],[690.31,699.79,"And many other tail statistics you will see in presentations come from vendors selling tail-spend tools, and could not be traced to a primary source."],[700.07,701.22,"Use them with care."],[701.72,706.36,"The key message from the notes: the tail is an efficiency topic first."],[706.36,711.21,"The savings on price are real, but they are secondary to the process cost."],[711.49,723.11,"Think about what a single small purchase order costs: a requisition, an approval, a supplier set up in the system, a purchase order, a goods receipt, an invoice to match and pay."],[723.11,729.01,"Multiply that by thousands of orders a year, and the process cost can rival the price savings."],[729.29,737.22,"If you send a team to run a full tender for every small category, you spend expensive procurement time on the least valuable spend."],[737.22,741.15,"That is why we say: channel the tail, do not source it."],[741.65,745.64,"It also helps to look at where the tail sits in the Group."],[745.64,750.05,"The Group's spend data shows big differences between entities."],[750.05,760.72,"In one entity, Entity F, about 30 percent of spend sits in the tail, and the entity works with around 1,780 suppliers, far more than entities of a similar size."],[761.0,764.92,"In another, Entity I, the tail is around 26 percent of spend."],[764.92,773.82,"Those are the places where a catalogue and guided buying would change daily life most, simply because so many small orders go to so many suppliers."],[773.82,779.9,"When you hear tail, think first about where the transactions are, not only where the money is."],[780.85,786.35,"Here is Nordvia's tail example: project P5, office supplies and print."],[786.35,794.25,"2.3 million euros with 31 suppliers, and 1,900 purchase orders a year below 1,000 euros each."],[794.25,795.22,"No catalogue."],[795.22,799.35,"Let's follow the route on the slide, line by line."],[799.85,801.87,"First: is there a Group framework?"],[801.87,804.32,"This question always comes first."],[804.32,812.72,"If the Group or another entity already has a framework for office supplies, the fastest and often cheapest route is to call off from it."],[812.72,816.53,"Running your own tender would duplicate work and split volume."],[816.81,821.03,"Second, if there is no framework: an RFQ on a basket of top items."],[821.03,824.81,"You do not ask for prices on ten thousand products."],[824.81,835.69,"You take the items that make up most of the volume, perhaps the top one hundred, with clear specifications and annual quantities, and you ask suppliers to price that basket."],[835.97,840.78,"Third: an English reverse auction, if four to five qualified bidders compete."],[840.78,843.8,"We will look at the preconditions on the next slide."],[844.3,846.47,"Fourth: a punch-out catalogue."],[846.47,859.54,"Once the supplier is chosen, users order through Coupa from the supplier's catalogue, with negotiated prices, approvals already built in, and invoices that match automatically."],[859.54,864.02,"Those 1,900 purchase orders become guided, low-effort transactions."],[864.02,866.35,"And fifth: print as a separate lot."],[866.35,866.4,"Why?"],[866.68,868.73,"Because print is a different market."],[868.73,872.39,"Office supplies are standard products from distributors."],[872.39,882.38,"Print, such as policy documents, marketing material or business cards, depends on paper, machines and make-ready costs, and different suppliers are good at it."],[882.38,886.5,"Putting both in one lot would reduce the number of capable bidders."],[887.0,893.18,"The notes add something important: this is the one project of the day where an auction fits."],[893.18,897.5,"Precise basket, many bidders, price decides, low switching cost."],[897.5,903.26,"Keep that in mind, because in every other case we have discussed, an auction was on the never list."],[904.21,905.89,"So when does an e-auction fit?"],[905.89,908.29,"The slide gives four preconditions."],[908.29,909.69,"All four must hold."],[910.19,911.84,"First, specification."],[911.84,915.55,"It must be precise, so that bids can be ranked automatically."],[915.55,920.5,"If you need a human to interpret each offer, an auction cannot work."],[920.5,924.64,"Second, bidders: at least four to five qualified bidders."],[924.64,929.48,"That figure comes from Smeltzer and Carr, 2003, and it is a guide, not a law."],[929.76,936.19,"Remember the government example from the last lesson: auctions with no interactive bidding."],[936.19,942.33,"Third, the decision: price, or a formula that combines price with a few measurable factors, decides."],[942.33,946.21,"If quality judgements matter, an auction is the wrong tool."],[946.21,948.12,"Fourth, the relationship."],[948.4,953.26,"It must be able to bear the pressure, and there must be a credible intent to award."],[953.26,960.63,"Suppliers who suspect that the auction is just a way to squeeze the incumbent will not bid seriously the next time."],[961.13,963.29,"The footnote points to the evidence."],[963.29,976.66,"Sandy Jap's research, in 2002 and 2007, found that open-bid auctions, where suppliers see prices, damage supplier trust more than sealed formats, without producing higher savings."],[976.94,987.06,"And Article 35 of the same 2014 EU public procurement directive, sets the rules for public e-auctions, which are a useful model of discipline."],[987.06,992.02,"Now, why do these four preconditions fit P5 and not the others?"],[992.02,998.57,"Office supplies have a precise basket, many distributors, price decides and switching is easy."],[998.85,1003.33,"Cleaning fails on precondition three, because quality and staff matter."],[1003.33,1008.17,"Consulting fails on one and three, because every assignment is different."],[1008.17,1012.65,"Kelvar fails on two, because there is one realistic bidder."],[1012.65,1015.79,"Session 3 goes much deeper into auction design."],[1016.07,1020.51,"Today the point is only where an auction fits on the map, and where it never fits."],[1021.01,1024.12,"Let me add a story that many buyers will recognise."],[1024.12,1032.93,"A facilities team once ran an auction for a building maintenance contract, because the tool was available and the CFO wanted savings."],[1032.93,1039.76,"The specification was a list of buildings with a few service lines, and quality was supposed to be checked afterwards."],[1040.04,1041.35,"Three bidders took part."],[1041.35,1051.66,"The winner bid aggressively, then started to send change requests for every job that was not literally in the list, and the quality complaints began in the second month."],[1051.66,1055.13,"Within a year the contract cost more than the old one."],[1055.13,1057.43,"None of the four preconditions had held."],[1057.71,1060.44,"The tool did exactly what it was designed to do."],[1060.44,1062.25,"It was simply the wrong tool."],[1063.2,1065.53,"Now let's bring the decision onto paper."],[1065.53,1069.28,"This is the one-page strategy paper, in seven blocks."],[1069.78,1076.12,"Block one, need and scope: the validated need, the value, the expiry date and, yes, the notice date."],[1076.12,1084.57,"Block two, position: the Kraljic quadrant, the Chessboard move, and how the incumbent sees us, from the Steele and Court model."],[1084.57,1089.51,"Block three, route to market: the six decisions from module 2.1, one line each."],[1089.79,1094.52,"Number of suppliers, lots, contract type, term, mechanism and timing."],[1094.52,1098.86,"Block four, options rejected: what we will not do, and why."],[1098.86,1102.44,"Block five, the timeline, planned back from the notice date."],[1102.72,1114.79,"Block six, risks and regulation: outsourcing, DORA, personal data under GDPR, and labour law, for example staff transfer or the risk of hidden temporary agency work."],[1114.79,1119.41,"And block seven, the decision requested: from whom, and by when."],[1119.91,1121.38,"Why only one page?"],[1121.38,1125.01,"The notes say it clearly: one page is a discipline."],[1125.01,1129.81,"If the route needs two pages to justify, the analysis is not finished."],[1129.81,1134.74,"A business owner, a CIO or a CFO will give your paper a few minutes."],[1135.02,1141.83,"In those minutes they need to see the problem, the route, the alternatives you considered and the decision you need."],[1141.83,1147.11,"Anything more belongs in the Sourcing Case behind the paper, as supporting evidence."],[1147.61,1149.22,"And notice block four."],[1149.22,1152.5,"Many strategy documents list only the chosen route."],[1152.5,1156.75,"That leaves the reader wondering whether you considered anything else."],[1156.75,1162.8,"Writing down the rejected options shows your reasoning, and it pre-empts the obvious objections."],[1163.08,1168.86,"If the business owner asks why not just keep the favourite supplier, the answer is already on the page."],[1169.81,1175.68,"Here is what that looks like for Nordvia's IT consulting project, P2, in seven lines."],[1176.18,1181.71,"Need: IT consulting, 6.1 million euros a year, 12 firms, no framework."],[1181.71,1186.3,"Position: leverage, with real competition among suppliers."],[1186.3,1192.16,"Route: a panel with three lots and four firms per lot, a rate card and call-off rules."],[1192.44,1199.92,"Rejected: a single supplier, because of dependency, and an open RFP for every job, because it is too slow."],[1199.92,1205.75,"Timeline: the framework RFP in the first quarter, the panel live by the third quarter."],[1205.75,1207.91,"The quarters here are illustrative."],[1208.19,1216.98,"Risks: the labour-law model for each lot, especially for staff augmentation, and the DORA clauses written into the framework."],[1216.98,1218.02,"Decision: the CIO."],[1218.52,1220.16,"Notice how short it is."],[1220.16,1223.83,"Every line carries a decision or a fact, nothing else."],[1223.83,1229.07,"And the notes point to the line the CIO will read twice: the rejected options."],[1229.07,1230.0,"Why?"],[1230.0,1238.4,"Because the CIO may well have a favourite firm, and the line, single supplier rejected because of dependency, speaks directly to that."],[1238.68,1243.69,"It also explains why the business cannot just keep calling off without competition."],[1243.69,1248.41,"If the CIO agrees with that line, the rest of the paper usually follows."],[1248.91,1251.82,"Try reading it from the CIO's point of view."],[1251.82,1266.87,"In under a minute, the CIO knows what is being bought, why there is competition, how the panel will work, what was considered and rejected, when it will be live, which risks Legal and Compliance are watching, and what decision is being asked for."],[1267.15,1272.99,"That is the test for your own paper: could a busy executive say yes or no after one read?"],[1272.99,1278.12,"If not, cut, sharpen, and move the detail into the Sourcing Case behind it."],[1279.06,1280.85,"A quick poll question for you."],[1280.85,1284.75,"Which route will your own project take?"],[1284.75,1285.64,"One supplier by tender."],[1285.64,1287.04,"A panel of preferred suppliers."],[1287.04,1290.11,"A sole or single source negotiation."],[1290.11,1291.68,"An RFQ and e-auction."],[1291.68,1294.9,"A catalogue or Group framework call-off."],[1294.9,1296.99,"Or a multi-stage negotiated process."],[1296.99,1300.44,"Think about your own Sourcing Case, and choose."],[1300.94,1303.73,"In a classroom, we would collect the distribution."],[1303.73,1314.08,"A mix of all patterns is a good sign: it shows the logic is being applied rather than one habit, such as three quotes for everything, being replaced by another habit."],[1314.36,1323.71,"If you were unsure between two routes, go back to the first question of the route-to-market logic: is there a real alternative within the contract horizon?"],[1324.66,1329.01,"Now the exercise for module 2.5: your one-page strategy paper."],[1329.01,1334.45,"It takes about twelve minutes, and you will find it under Module 2.5 in your workbook."],[1334.45,1336.52,"Choose your route to market."],[1336.52,1339.08,"Fill the seven blocks in short lines."],[1339.08,1341.14,"Write the options you rejected."],[1341.14,1343.09,"Plan back from the notice date."],[1343.09,1344.8,"And name who must decide."],[1345.29,1348.17,"It will not be finished now, and that is fine."],[1348.17,1353.17,"The aim is a draft that is good enough to take to the business owner in Fieldwork 2."],[1353.17,1359.22,"Where you are missing a fact, such as the exact notice date, write what you assume and mark it to check."],[1359.5,1365.0,"If you want to do it now, pause the video, write your draft in the Modules tab, and then come back."],[1365.51,1367.17,"A few tips as you write."],[1367.17,1370.34,"Use the language of the business, not of procurement."],[1370.34,1379.19,"Write the problem in the business owner's words, for example HQ complaints about cleaning, or day rates that vary widely for the same profile."],[1379.19,1381.68,"Keep each block to one or two lines."],[1381.96,1390.26,"In block three, the route, make sure the six decisions are consistent with each other: a panel with one supplier per lot is not a panel."],[1390.26,1395.51,"And in block seven, be precise: not Management, but a named role and a date."],[1396.46,1398.63,"Let's debrief with three questions."],[1398.63,1401.7,"Which route did you choose, and which did you reject?"],[1401.7,1404.65,"Is your timeline realistic from the notice date?"],[1404.65,1407.17,"And who outside Procurement must sign off?"],[1407.67,1415.62,"On the first question, check that your rejected options are real alternatives that someone might propose, not straw men."],[1415.62,1422.2,"On the second, many first drafts plan from the expiry date and discover the notice date only later."],[1422.48,1432.42,"If your project is a tender, remember that a standard tender often needs nine to twelve months before expiry, plus mobilisation, which is a practice estimate."],[1432.42,1435.36,"On the third, think beyond the business owner."],[1435.64,1445.94,"If your triage flagged outsourcing, DORA, personal data or staff transfer, Risk and Compliance, Legal or the Data Protection Officer need to be part of the decision."],[1445.94,1450.98,"Better to name them now than to discover them at contract signature."],[1451.93,1455.21,"Let's recap module 2.5 in three lines."],[1455.21,1460.45,"One: complex deals need a multi-stage process with dialogue and BAFO."],[1460.45,1465.54,"Two: the tail is channelled through catalogues, punch-out and spot buy."],[1465.54,1470.68,"Three: one page records the route, the rejected options and the decision."],[1471.18,1483.06,"Behind the first line sits the regulatory layer: Solvency II outsourcing, provider examination, and DORA, which together make exit feasibility a selection criterion."],[1483.34,1494.41,"Behind the second sits the reminder that an e-auction fits only when all four preconditions hold, which is rare in indirect categories but true for a basket of office supplies."],[1494.41,1499.04,"In the next lesson we bring it all together in Case 2."],[1499.04,1502.2,"Four Nordvia projects, four routes to market, one logic."],[1502.48,1510.12,"We will compare answers, look at what separated strong answers from weaker ones, and prepare you for Fieldwork 2 and Session 3."],[1510.62,1513.54,"Before you move on, take a minute with your own project."],[1513.54,1515.96,"Which end of the map is it closer to?"],[1515.96,1525.55,"If it is a complex, high-risk service, have you planned the RFI, the shortlist, the dialogue and the BAFO, and have you asked the regulatory questions?"],[1525.83,1532.31,"If it is closer to the tail, could a catalogue or an existing framework solve it with far less effort than a tender?"],[1532.31,1535.6,"Either way, the paper is where you show your reasoning."]]},{"id":"s2-l6","session":"s2","title":"Case 2: four projects, four routes","summary":"Choose and defend a route to market for four contrasting Nordvia projects, recognise the three patterns behind them, and prepare the strategy conversation of Fieldwork 2.","from":101,"to":113,"audio":"audio/s2-l6.mp3","duration":1413.5,"segs":[[101,0.6,18.37,"Welcome to the last lesson of Session 2. This lesson covers Case 2: four projects, four routes to market. It also closes the session, with Fieldwork 2, a short guide for your conversation with the business owner, a recap of the whole session, and a look ahead to Session 3."],[101,18.87,78.36,"Let me remind you why this case matters. Over the last five modules we have built a logic for choosing a route to market. We started with the six decisions: number of suppliers, lots, contract type, term, mechanism and timing, with the mechanism deliberately in fifth place. We saw that a continuous site service, like cleaning, calls for one supplier chosen by tender. That a stream of different assignments, like IT consulting, calls for a panel with a rate card and mini-competitions. That a contract with no real alternative, like the Kelvar licences, calls for a prepared negotiation. And, in the last lesson, that complex partnerships need a multi-stage process, while the tail needs a channel. The case asks a simple question: can you apply that logic to four real-looking projects at once, without falling back on one habit for all of them? The setting, as always, is Nordvia Insurance, our fictional mid-sized insurer."],[101,78.86,97.91,"One more thing before we start. A case like this is not a test with one right answer. It is practice in reasoning. When you compare your answer with the one I will show you, do not only ask whether you chose the same route. Ask whether you could explain your route to a sceptical business owner, using the facts on the table."],[102,98.86,126.36,"Here is the situation. Nordvia's Procurement team has nine people and runs about sixty sourcing events a year in Coupa. Until now, most of those were RFQs with three quotes, whatever the category. Office cleaning, consulting, software, paper: three quotes. The Head of Procurement wants to change that. She asks for a route to market for four projects in the pipeline, each one justified with the strategy-selection matrix."],[102,126.86,186.22,"Pause on that first fact, because it describes many procurement teams, not only fictional ones. Three quotes is a comfortable rule. It is easy to audit, easy to explain, and it feels like competition. But think about what it does in each of our patterns. For cleaning, it compares prices for a service that nobody has specified properly, so the cheapest quote wins and the complaints continue. For consulting, it creates a new mini-tender every time, which is slow, so the business learns to avoid it and call off without competition. For Kelvar, it is impossible, so someone writes a justification and the vendor sets the price. The rule is not wrong everywhere. It is wrong as a single rule for everything. That is what the Head of Procurement is trying to fix. In a classroom, you would work in teams of three or four, and each team would present one project in the debrief. Working alone, you can do all four, or pick two that are most like your own work."],[103,187.17,196.83,"Here are the four projects. Let's read the table row by row, and I will add the extra facts that are not on the slide but that you know from earlier modules."],[103,197.33,218.69,"P1, office cleaning. 2.4 million euros a year, three local suppliers, expiry June 2027. The contracts have been rolled over since 2019, and there are complaints at headquarters. Remember that cleaning covers three sites in two countries, which means two labour regimes and two local markets."],[103,219.19,250.78,"P2, IT consulting. 6.1 million euros a year, twelve firms and thirty-eight call-offs, and no framework. Day rates for the same senior profile range from 820 to 1,450 euros. That means the top rate is about 77 percent above the lowest. And two extra facts: the top three firms take 61 percent of the spend, and 29 of the 38 call-offs had no competition at all. That is about 76 percent."],[103,251.28,276.5,"P3, the Kelvar licences. 11.2 million euros, one vendor, renewal June 2027, a 9 percent uplift last time, and an auto-renewal clause. The extra facts: a nine-month notice period, an estimated 20 to 25 percent of named-user licences unused, and two credible alternative platforms with a migration time of 18 to 24 months."],[103,277.0,315.17,"And P5, office supplies and print. 2.3 million euros, thirty-one suppliers, various expiry dates, and 1,900 purchase orders a year below 1,000 euros. No catalogue. Look at the four rows together for a moment. Two projects expire in the same month, June 2027. One has a notice period that brings its real deadline much earlier. And the values range from 2.3 to 11.2 million euros. The biggest project is not necessarily the most competitive, and the smallest is not necessarily the simplest."],[104,316.12,343.73,"Now it is your team's turn. This is the case exercise, about fifteen minutes, in the Case tab of your workbook. For each project, choose the route. Decide the number of suppliers, the mechanism and the term. Justify each choice with the position and the facts: where does the project sit in Kraljic, how does the incumbent see Nordvia, and is there a real alternative? Name the first step. And add one thing you would never do on that project."],[104,344.23,385.11,"Work through the route-to-market questions in order. First: is there a real alternative within the contract horizon? If not, it is a negotiation, whatever the category. Second: how does demand arrive? One continuous service, a stream of different assignments, or many small orders? Third: is the solution itself unknown? Only then think about the mechanism. And remember the facilitator's challenge for the debrief: be ready to defend the project you found hardest. If you want to do the exercise now, pause the video, fill the table in the Case tab, and come back when you are ready. The next slide shows one possible answer, so it is worth trying first."],[105,386.06,400.29,"Welcome back. Here is one possible answer. I say possible, because other answers are defensible if the logic holds. Let's go row by row, and for each project I will explain the route, the term, and the never column."],[105,400.79,474.59,"P1, cleaning. Route: one supplier per country lot, chosen by RFP. Why per country? Because the two countries have different collective agreements and different local markets, so a lot per country lets the best local providers bid, with an option to bid for both lots as a bundle. Term: three plus one plus one years. That means a three-year initial term with two optional one-year extensions, a common practice pattern, not a norm. It gives the supplier time to invest in staff and equipment, and gives Nordvia two moments to decide whether to continue. The specification is hybrid: outputs such as cleanliness standards, plus some inputs such as frequencies for critical areas. Quality should weigh at least half of the evaluation. A should-cost floor, built from hours, collectively agreed wages and on-costs, flags bids that are too low to be lawful. And the bidders need staff transfer data, because staff may transfer to the new provider. The never column: a price-only auction. In cleaning, a price-only auction pushes bids towards the wage floor and quality down, which is exactly the problem at headquarters today."],[105,475.09,504.74,"Notice the timing issue on P1. A standard tender often needs nine to twelve months before expiry, plus mobilisation, which is a practice estimate. With expiry in June 2027, P1 is already tight. If the tender cannot finish in time, a short bridge extension of the current contracts is a defensible fallback, as long as it is a decision, written down with a reason and an end date, and not another silent roll-over."],[105,505.24,553.93,"P2, consulting. Route: a panel with three lots and a rate card. For example, delivery, advisory, and staff augmentation, with three to five firms in each lot. Maximum day rates by role and seniority, call-off rules with thresholds aligned to the delegation of authority, and fixed price per deliverable as the default for defined work. Term: three plus one. The never column: an auction on day rates. Why? Because a day rate says nothing about the quality of the person or the number of days a firm will need. Squeeze the rate and you may simply get a more junior consultant, or more days. The real problem in P2 is not the level of the rates. It is that 76 percent of call-offs had no competition. The panel fixes that."],[105,554.43,583.1,"P3, Kelvar. Route: a sole-source negotiation, with BATNA building. Term: aligned to the roadmap. That means the term should match Nordvia's own plan for the platform. If Nordvia might migrate in four years, a seven-year term with no exit is a mistake. The never column: an RFP for show. We covered why in module 2.4: the vendor knows it cannot lose, and the RFP tells it you have not understood your position."],[105,583.6,623.73,"P5, supplies and print. Route: check for a Group framework first, then an RFQ on a basket, an English reverse auction if enough qualified bidders compete, and a catalogue. Term: two to three years. The never column: tender every small order. The value is in the channel, not in 1,900 separate decisions. Now, the most important line from the speaker notes. Other answers are defensible, for example one lot for all cleaning sites, if a team argues for a single provider across both countries and explains how it would handle two labour regimes. What is not defensible is the same tool for all four."],[106,624.68,629.04,"What separated the strong answers from the weaker ones? Look at the two columns."],[106,629.54,654.01,"Weaker answers started from the tool. The first sentence was, we will run an RFP, or we will run an auction, before anyone had described the demand or the market. They used the same mechanism for all four projects. And they had no timeline from the notice date, so they missed that Kelvar's real deadline is September 2026, not June 2027."],[106,654.51,680.96,"Stronger answers started from the shape of demand and the alternatives. Is it one continuous service, a stream of jobs, a single vendor with no near-term substitute, or many small orders? They named the rejected options, which showed the reasoning. They planned back from the notice dates. And they saw the Group framework question for P5, which is the simplest saving of all: do not tender what the Group has already tendered."],[106,681.46,704.0,"The speaker notes give this slide a second life. Read the right-hand column as the checklist for Fieldwork 2. When you finish your own strategy paper, ask four questions. Did I start from the demand and the alternatives, or from a tool? Did I name what I rejected? Did I plan from the notice date? And did I check whether a Group or entity framework already exists?"],[106,704.5,747.47,"Let me give you an example of how a weaker answer sounds, so you can recognise it in your own drafts. Imagine a team writes for P2: we will run an RFP and select the cheapest three firms. It sounds decisive. But it starts from the tool, it says nothing about how assignments will be called off, and it does not explain how the 29 uncompeted call-offs will stop. A stronger version reads: demand arrives as a stream of different assignments, the market has many capable firms, so we will set up a panel with three lots and a rate card, and every call-off above a threshold will be competed among the panel firms. Same category, same facts, very different quality of thinking."],[107,748.42,763.82,"Let's debrief with four questions. Which project did your team argue about most? Did anyone choose an auction for P2 or P3, and if not, why not? What is the first step for Kelvar this month? And which project is most at risk on timing?"],[107,764.32,789.37,"Many teams argue most about P1, because of the lot question: one lot or two? That is a good argument to have, because both answers can work. Few teams choose an auction for P2 or P3 once they have walked through the preconditions, and that is the point. The first step for Kelvar this month is not a meeting with the vendor. It is the usage audit, together with a check of the notice clause with Legal."],[107,789.87,824.05,"And the timing question is the insight the notes want to land. P1 and P3 both expire in June 2027. On the surface, they look equally urgent. But P3 has a nine-month notice period and an auto-renewal clause, so its real deadline is September 2026. P3 is the most urgent project in the portfolio, even though its expiry date is the same as cleaning. In your own work, how many contracts in your pipeline are tracked by expiry date only? That is worth checking this week."],[107,824.55,846.69,"A useful habit for your own portfolio follows from this. Once a quarter, sort your contract list by notice date rather than expiry date, and look at the next eighteen months. The projects at the top of that list are the ones where your preparation has to start now. For Nordvia, Kelvar would sit at the very top, Northbyte would follow, and cleaning would already be pressing."],[108,847.64,858.31,"Now the slide that the notes call the slide to remember from today. Three patterns, one logic. Three rows, each with the demand and market, the route, and an example."],[108,858.81,883.79,"Row one. One continuous site service, with many suppliers in the market. Route: one supplier, chosen by tender, on a three plus one plus one term. Examples: cleaning and security. The logic is that the service needs one accountable provider on site, the market has plenty of capable bidders, and a well-designed tender with a strong quality weighting is how you get the best one."],[108,884.29,912.58,"Row two. A stream of different assignments. Route: a panel with a rate card and mini-competitions. Examples: consulting and IT services. The logic: you cannot tender each job separately because that is too slow, and you should not give every job to one firm because that creates dependency. So you pre-qualify a small group of firms, fix the commercial rules once, and let them compete for each assignment under simple call-off rules."],[108,913.08,935.14,"Row three. No real alternative in the horizon. Route: a sole-source negotiation. Example: incumbent software. The logic: competitive tools need competition, and here there is none within the contract term, so the power must come from preparation: usage facts, benchmarks, a credible BATNA for the next term, and protections."],[108,935.64,966.82,"You will notice that the multi-stage partnership process and the tail channel are not separate rows on this slide. They are the two ends of the map we added in module 2.5, and they follow the same questions: is there a real alternative, how does demand arrive, and is the solution known? The notes suggest one more step, and I suggest you do it now. Say out loud, or write down, which row your own project is in, and why. If you cannot say why in one sentence, go back to the questions."],[109,967.77,973.1,"Now, what happens between now and Session 3? This is Fieldwork 2, in five steps."],[109,973.6,1016.27,"Step one: finish the one-page strategy paper you drafted in module 2.5. Step two: present it to the business owner in a thirty-minute conversation. We will look at how to structure that on the next slide. Step three: involve Risk and Compliance where your triage flagged something, such as outsourcing, DORA, personal data or staff transfer. Ask them about the regulatory path and the lead times it adds. Step four: check whether a Group or entity framework already covers the need, and confirm the notice date of the current contract. Not the expiry date. The notice date. Step five: record the agreed paper in your Sourcing Case and bring it to Session 3."],[109,1016.77,1047.22,"Why does this matter so much? The notes explain it. Session 3 designs the RFx and the evaluation model. Without an agreed route, there is nothing to design. If you arrive at Session 3 with a route that the business owner has not agreed, you may design a beautiful tender for a project that should have been a panel call-off, or a negotiation. Remember too what the fieldwork guide says: you are not asking for permission to run a tender. You are agreeing how the business will buy, and why."],[109,1047.72,1072.45,"A practical tip on step four. Checking for a Group or entity framework often takes one email to the right colleague, but people skip it because they assume the answer is no. In the case, that check is the first line of the P5 route. In real life, it can save you a whole tender, or reveal that another entity has just negotiated better terms with the same supplier that you can join."],[110,1073.4,1078.11,"Here is a structure for that thirty-minute strategy conversation. Five blocks."],[110,1078.61,1099.33,"Minutes zero to five: the problem they recognise, in their words. Minutes five to fifteen: the route, and why it fits. Minutes fifteen to twenty-two: the options you rejected, and why. Minutes twenty-two to twenty-seven: the timeline from the notice date. And minutes twenty-seven to thirty: the decision you need, and by when."],[110,1099.83,1134.24,"The most important advice is in the notes. Lead with the problem the business owner already feels, not with procurement policy. For the Facilities manager, that is the complaints at headquarters. For the CIO on consulting, it might be that day rates for the same profile vary by 77 percent, or that projects wait for a firm to become available. For the IT director on Kelvar, it is the 9 percent uplift that landed on the budget last time. Start there, and the route becomes the answer to their problem rather than a rule you are imposing."],[110,1134.74,1170.06,"Expect the classic pushbacks, and prepare one sentence for each. We already have a favourite supplier: the panel and the direct-award rules answer that, because the favourite can join the panel if it competes. A tender takes too long: the timeline from the notice date answers that, and shows that starting now is the fastest option. The vendor will not negotiate: the BATNA and the usage facts answer that. And notice where the rejected options sit: in the middle, after the route. That is when the business owner is most likely to ask, why not just do it the old way?"],[111,1171.01,1190.14,"Here is a final poll question. What will be hardest when you present your strategy paper to the business owner? Explaining why their favourite supplier must compete. Getting a decision in time for the notice date. Getting Risk and Compliance involved. Or defending the options you rejected. Take a moment to choose."],[111,1190.64,1205.45,"In a classroom we would use the result to pair you with someone who expects the same difficulty, so you can rehearse together. You can do the same with a colleague. Pick the answer you chose, and practise your opening five minutes with them before the real meeting."],[111,1205.95,1238.63,"Whatever you chose, the preparation is similar. If the favourite supplier is the issue, prepare the sentence about the panel or the tender giving that supplier a fair chance to compete. If timing is the issue, bring the timeline from the notice date and show what happens if the decision slips by a month. If Risk and Compliance are the issue, contact them before the meeting, not after. And if it is the rejected options, make sure each one has a short, factual reason that the business owner can repeat to their own manager."],[112,1239.58,1248.61,"Let's close Session 2 with the five lines on this slide. Read them slowly with me, because these five lines are what we will ask back at the capstone."],[112,1249.11,1303.11,"One. The tool follows the strategy, and the strategy follows the power position. We do not start with RFQ, RFP or auction. We start with where we stand: Kraljic, the Chessboard, and how the supplier sees us. Two. Continuous site services: one supplier by tender, on a three plus one plus one term, with quality weighted strongly and a should-cost floor. Three. Streams of professional and IT work: a panel with a rate card and mini-competitions, so that every assignment is competed without a new tender each time. Four. No real competition: a prepared negotiation, never an auction. Usage facts, benchmarks, a credible BATNA for the next term, and protections such as uplift caps and DORA exit clauses. Five. One page records the route, the rejected options and the decision."],[112,1303.61,1347.07,"Around those five lines sit the ideas that make them work. The six decisions, with the mechanism in fifth place. The notice date as the real deadline. The two ends of the map: a multi-stage process with dialogue and BAFO for complex partnerships like Northbyte, where exit feasibility is a selection criterion, and a channel for the tail, where an e-auction fits only when all four preconditions hold. And the regulatory questions, Solvency II outsourcing and DORA, which change timelines and criteria, with Legal and Compliance owning the interpretation. If you can explain your own project with that logic, you have achieved what this session set out to do."],[113,1348.02,1373.41,"And that brings us to the next session, which asks a new question: how do we run the event? Session 2 was about what route to take. Session 3 is about running it well. We will design the RFx documents, build the evaluation model, including how to score price without distorting the result, and set up the e-negotiation in Coupa, including when and how to use the auction formats we only previewed today."],[113,1373.91,1391.32,"Please bring your agreed strategy paper. It is the input for everything we do next time. Check the pre-work for Session 3 in your workbook and the date of the next class. Thank you for your work in this session. Good luck with your conversation with the business owner, and see you in Session 3."],[113,1391.82,1412.34,"Between now and then, keep the three patterns in mind whenever a new request reaches your desk. Ask the questions in order: is there a real alternative, how does demand arrive, and is the solution known? You will be surprised how quickly the right route becomes obvious, and how much easier it is to explain to the people you work with."]],"questions":[{"after":102,"q":"Nordvia's team has used three quotes for almost every event. What is the main problem with that habit?","options":["Three quotes are always too few; five should be the rule","One rule for everything ignores the demand shape and the market, so it fails differently in cleaning, consulting and software","Three quotes are not allowed under Solvency II","Coupa cannot run RFQs with three suppliers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Three quotes can fit some purchases, but as a single rule it produces under-specified cleaning, uncompeted consulting call-offs and a vendor-set price for Kelvar. The route must follow the demand and the power position.","t":186.22},{"after":103,"q":"In P2 IT consulting, 29 of the 38 call-offs had no competition and day rates range from 820 to 1,450 euros. What is the core problem the route must fix?","options":["Most work is called off without competition, so a panel with a rate card and call-off rules is needed","The rates are too high, so an auction on day rates is needed","There are too many firms, so one supplier should get everything","The firms are underqualified, so a sole-source negotiation is needed"],"answer":0,"explain":"About 76 percent of call-offs had no competition. A panel with maximum rates and mini-competitions brings every assignment under competition without a new tender each time.","t":315.17},{"after":105,"q":"A team proposes one English reverse auction covering all four projects to save time. How would you respond?","options":["Agree: one event is more efficient","Agree for P1 and P2, but not for P3 and P5","Reject: auctions fit only where all preconditions hold, which is P5 at most; the other projects need a tender, a panel and a negotiation","Reject: auctions are never allowed in insurance"],"answer":2,"explain":"The same tool for all four is the one indefensible answer. Only P5 has a precise basket, many bidders, price deciding and low switching cost; cleaning, consulting and Kelvar each fail at least one precondition.","t":623.73},{"after":106,"q":"Which first sentence of a strategy paper signals a stronger answer?","options":["We will run an RFP in Coupa next month","We will run the same process as last year","We will ask three suppliers for quotes","Cleaning is one continuous service at three sites in two countries, with many capable local providers and a contract that expires in June 2027"],"answer":3,"explain":"Strong answers start from the shape of demand and the alternatives, not from a tool. The mechanism comes later, as the fifth of the six decisions.","t":747.47},{"after":107,"q":"P1 cleaning and P3 Kelvar both expire in June 2027. Which statement about urgency is correct?","options":["They are equally urgent because the expiry date is the same","P1 is more urgent because cleaning has staff transfer","P3 is more urgent because its nine-month notice period and auto-renewal clause put the real deadline in September 2026","Neither is urgent until 2027"],"answer":2,"explain":"The notice date, not the expiry date, is the real deadline. Missing it lets the auto-renewal clause decide the next term for Kelvar.","t":846.69},{"after":108,"q":"Nordvia Legal uses many law firms for different matters, each assignment different, with no framework. Which pattern fits best?","options":["One supplier by tender on a 3 plus 1 plus 1 term","A panel with a rate card and mini-competitions","A sole-source negotiation","An English reverse auction on hourly rates"],"answer":1,"explain":"A stream of different assignments with many capable firms is the panel pattern. It avoids both dependency on one firm and a slow tender for every matter.","t":966.82},{"after":109,"q":"A participant plans to skip the business owner conversation and go straight to designing the RFx in Session 3. What is the risk?","options":["None: the business owner only needs to sign the contract","Coupa will block the event without an approval","The supplier will refuse to bid","The RFx may be designed for a route the business has not agreed, for example a tender where a panel call-off or negotiation was needed"],"answer":3,"explain":"Session 3 designs the event for the agreed route. Without agreement, the design may answer the wrong question, and the business may not support the outcome.","t":1072.45},{"after":110,"q":"You meet the Facilities manager to agree the P1 cleaning strategy. How should you open the conversation?","options":["With the complaints at headquarters, the problem the manager already feels","With the procurement policy that requires a tender above a threshold","With the evaluation weights for the RFP","With the list of options you rejected"],"answer":0,"explain":"Leading with the problem the business owner recognises makes the route the answer to their problem rather than a rule imposed on them. The route, rejected options, timeline and decision follow.","t":1170.06},{"after":112,"q":"Which statement best captures the main lesson of Session 2?","options":["The best tool is the e-auction, used wherever possible","Every contract should be tendered every three years","The tool follows the strategy and the strategy follows the power position, so different demand shapes and markets lead to different routes","Procurement should let the business choose the route"],"answer":2,"explain":"This is the first of the five lines to remember. Continuous site services, streams of work and sole-source situations each lead to a different route, recorded on one page with the rejected options.","t":1347.07}],"cues":[[0.6,3.18,"Welcome to the last lesson of Session 2."],[3.18,7.49,"This lesson covers Case 2: four projects, four routes to market."],[7.49,18.37,"It also closes the session, with Fieldwork 2, a short guide for your conversation with the business owner, a recap of the whole session, and a look ahead to Session 3."],[18.87,21.26,"Let me remind you why this case matters."],[21.26,26.96,"Over the last five modules we have built a logic for choosing a route to market."],[26.96,36.21,"We started with the six decisions: number of suppliers, lots, contract type, term, mechanism and timing, with the mechanism deliberately in fifth place."],[36.49,42.53,"We saw that a continuous site service, like cleaning, calls for one supplier chosen by tender."],[42.53,49.9,"That a stream of different assignments, like IT consulting, calls for a panel with a rate card and mini-competitions."],[49.9,56.42,"That a contract with no real alternative, like the Kelvar licences, calls for a prepared negotiation."],[56.7,63.72,"And, in the last lesson, that complex partnerships need a multi-stage process, while the tail needs a channel."],[63.72,73.04,"The case asks a simple question: can you apply that logic to four real-looking projects at once, without falling back on one habit for all of them?"],[73.32,78.36,"The setting, as always, is Nordvia Insurance, our fictional mid-sized insurer."],[78.86,80.83,"One more thing before we start."],[80.83,84.39,"A case like this is not a test with one right answer."],[84.39,85.52,"It is practice in reasoning."],[85.52,92.28,"When you compare your answer with the one I will show you, do not only ask whether you chose the same route."],[92.56,97.91,"Ask whether you could explain your route to a sceptical business owner, using the facts on the table."],[98.86,100.5,"Here is the situation."],[100.5,106.64,"Nordvia's Procurement team has nine people and runs about sixty sourcing events a year in Coupa."],[106.64,112.06,"Until now, most of those were RFQs with three quotes, whatever the category."],[112.06,115.99,"Office cleaning, consulting, software, paper: three quotes."],[115.99,118.98,"The Head of Procurement wants to change that."],[119.26,126.36,"She asks for a route to market for four projects in the pipeline, each one justified with the strategy-selection matrix."],[126.86,132.53,"Pause on that first fact, because it describes many procurement teams, not only fictional ones."],[132.53,134.84,"Three quotes is a comfortable rule."],[134.84,139.63,"It is easy to audit, easy to explain, and it feels like competition."],[139.63,142.37,"But think about what it does in each of our patterns."],[142.65,150.92,"For cleaning, it compares prices for a service that nobody has specified properly, so the cheapest quote wins and the complaints continue."],[150.92,159.91,"For consulting, it creates a new mini-tender every time, which is slow, so the business learns to avoid it and call off without competition."],[160.19,166.14,"For Kelvar, it is impossible, so someone writes a justification and the vendor sets the price."],[166.14,168.49,"The rule is not wrong everywhere."],[168.49,170.96,"It is wrong as a single rule for everything."],[170.96,174.48,"That is what the Head of Procurement is trying to fix."],[174.76,181.23,"In a classroom, you would work in teams of three or four, and each team would present one project in the debrief."],[181.23,186.22,"Working alone, you can do all four, or pick two that are most like your own work."],[187.17,188.84,"Here are the four projects."],[188.84,196.83,"Let's read the table row by row, and I will add the extra facts that are not on the slide but that you know from earlier modules."],[197.33,198.62,"P1, office cleaning."],[198.62,203.82,"2.4 million euros a year, three local suppliers, expiry June 2027."],[203.82,210.26,"The contracts have been rolled over since 2019, and there are complaints at headquarters."],[210.26,218.68,"Remember that cleaning covers three sites in two countries, which means two labour regimes and two local markets."],[219.19,220.58,"P2, IT consulting."],[220.58,227.7,"6.1 million euros a year, twelve firms and thirty-eight call-offs, and no framework."],[227.7,234.57,"Day rates for the same senior profile range from 820 to 1,450 euros."],[234.57,238.75,"That means the top rate is about 77 percent above the lowest."],[239.03,248.94,"And two extra facts: the top three firms take 61 percent of the spend, and 29 of the 38 call-offs had no competition at all."],[248.94,250.79,"That is about 76 percent."],[251.28,253.57,"P3, the Kelvar licences."],[253.57,261.66,"11.2 million euros, one vendor, renewal June 2027, a 9 percent uplift last time, and an auto-renewal clause."],[261.66,276.5,"The extra facts: a nine-month notice period, an estimated 20 to 25 percent of named-user licences unused, and two credible alternative platforms with a migration time of 18 to 24 months."],[277.0,279.79,"And P5, office supplies and print."],[279.79,289.7,"2.3 million euros, thirty-one suppliers, various expiry dates, and 1,900 purchase orders a year below 1,000 euros."],[289.7,290.23,"No catalogue."],[290.23,294.2,"Look at the four rows together for a moment."],[294.2,298.18,"Two projects expire in the same month, June 2027."],[298.46,302.86,"One has a notice period that brings its real deadline much earlier."],[302.86,306.7,"And the values range from 2.3 to 11.2 million euros."],[306.7,315.17,"The biggest project is not necessarily the most competitive, and the smallest is not necessarily the simplest."],[316.12,318.09,"Now it is your team's turn."],[318.09,323.04,"This is the case exercise, about fifteen minutes, in the Case tab of your workbook."],[323.04,325.19,"For each project, choose the route."],[325.19,328.86,"Decide the number of suppliers, the mechanism and the term."],[329.14,339.33,"Justify each choice with the position and the facts: where does the project sit in Kraljic, how does the incumbent see Nordvia, and is there a real alternative?"],[339.33,340.55,"Name the first step."],[340.55,343.73,"And add one thing you would never do on that project."],[344.23,347.6,"Work through the route-to-market questions in order."],[347.6,352.06,"First: is there a real alternative within the contract horizon?"],[352.06,354.94,"If not, it is a negotiation, whatever the category."],[354.94,357.44,"Second: how does demand arrive?"],[357.44,362.02,"One continuous service, a stream of different assignments, or many small orders?"],[362.02,364.88,"Third: is the solution itself unknown?"],[365.16,367.24,"Only then think about the mechanism."],[367.24,373.65,"And remember the facilitator's challenge for the debrief: be ready to defend the project you found hardest."],[373.65,380.73,"If you want to do the exercise now, pause the video, fill the table in the Case tab, and come back when you are ready."],[381.01,385.11,"The next slide shows one possible answer, so it is worth trying first."],[386.06,386.87,"Welcome back."],[386.87,389.22,"Here is one possible answer."],[389.22,394.02,"I say possible, because other answers are defensible if the logic holds."],[394.02,400.29,"Let's go row by row, and for each project I will explain the route, the term, and the never column."],[400.79,401.55,"P1, cleaning."],[401.55,404.95,"Route: one supplier per country lot, chosen by RFP."],[404.95,406.3,"Why per country?"],[406.3,419.95,"Because the two countries have different collective agreements and different local markets, so a lot per country lets the best local providers bid, with an option to bid for both lots as a bundle."],[419.95,421.97,"Term: three plus one plus one years."],[422.25,430.1,"That means a three-year initial term with two optional one-year extensions, a common practice pattern, not a norm."],[430.1,437.36,"It gives the supplier time to invest in staff and equipment, and gives Nordvia two moments to decide whether to continue."],[437.64,445.42,"The specification is hybrid: outputs such as cleanliness standards, plus some inputs such as frequencies for critical areas."],[445.42,449.48,"Quality should weigh at least half of the evaluation."],[449.48,457.37,"A should-cost floor, built from hours, collectively agreed wages and on-costs, flags bids that are too low to be lawful."],[457.65,463.32,"And the bidders need staff transfer data, because staff may transfer to the new provider."],[463.32,465.78,"The never column: a price-only auction."],[465.78,474.59,"In cleaning, a price-only auction pushes bids towards the wage floor and quality down, which is exactly the problem at headquarters today."],[475.09,477.78,"Notice the timing issue on P1."],[477.78,486.27,"A standard tender often needs nine to twelve months before expiry, plus mobilisation, which is a practice estimate."],[486.27,490.58,"With expiry in June 2027, P1 is already tight."],[490.86,504.75,"If the tender cannot finish in time, a short bridge extension of the current contracts is a defensible fallback, as long as it is a decision, written down with a reason and an end date, and not another silent roll-over."],[505.24,505.91,"P2, consulting."],[505.91,509.73,"Route: a panel with three lots and a rate card."],[509.73,516.31,"For example, delivery, advisory, and staff augmentation, with three to five firms in each lot."],[516.59,528.65,"Maximum day rates by role and seniority, call-off rules with thresholds aligned to the delegation of authority, and fixed price per deliverable as the default for defined work."],[528.65,530.13,"Term: three plus one."],[530.13,533.26,"The never column: an auction on day rates."],[533.26,533.32,"Why?"],[533.6,539.86,"Because a day rate says nothing about the quality of the person or the number of days a firm will need."],[539.86,545.26,"Squeeze the rate and you may simply get a more junior consultant, or more days."],[545.26,549.04,"The real problem in P2 is not the level of the rates."],[549.04,552.42,"It is that 76 percent of call-offs had no competition."],[552.42,553.93,"The panel fixes that."],[554.43,555.09,"P3, Kelvar."],[555.09,558.75,"Route: a sole-source negotiation, with BATNA building."],[558.75,560.67,"Term: aligned to the roadmap."],[560.67,566.18,"That means the term should match Nordvia's own plan for the platform."],[566.18,572.41,"If Nordvia might migrate in four years, a seven-year term with no exit is a mistake."],[572.41,574.68,"The never column: an RFP for show."],[574.96,583.1,"We covered why in module 2.4: the vendor knows it cannot lose, and the RFP tells it you have not understood your position."],[583.6,585.38,"P5, supplies and print."],[585.38,595.6,"Route: check for a Group framework first, then an RFQ on a basket, an English reverse auction if enough qualified bidders compete, and a catalogue."],[595.6,597.46,"Term: two to three years."],[597.46,599.92,"The never column: tender every small order."],[599.92,604.57,"The value is in the channel, not in 1,900 separate decisions."],[604.85,608.05,"Now, the most important line from the speaker notes."],[608.05,620.1,"Other answers are defensible, for example one lot for all cleaning sites, if a team argues for a single provider across both countries and explains how it would handle two labour regimes."],[620.1,623.73,"What is not defensible is the same tool for all four."],[624.68,627.88,"What separated the strong answers from the weaker ones?"],[627.88,629.03,"Look at the two columns."],[629.54,631.8,"Weaker answers started from the tool."],[631.8,640.64,"The first sentence was, we will run an RFP, or we will run an auction, before anyone had described the demand or the market."],[640.64,643.92,"They used the same mechanism for all four projects."],[644.2,654.01,"And they had no timeline from the notice date, so they missed that Kelvar's real deadline is September 2026, not June 2027."],[654.51,658.77,"Stronger answers started from the shape of demand and the alternatives."],[658.77,666.76,"Is it one continuous service, a stream of jobs, a single vendor with no near-term substitute, or many small orders?"],[666.76,670.33,"They named the rejected options, which showed the reasoning."],[670.33,672.47,"They planned back from the notice dates."],[672.75,680.97,"And they saw the Group framework question for P5, which is the simplest saving of all: do not tender what the Group has already tendered."],[681.46,684.38,"The speaker notes give this slide a second life."],[684.38,688.36,"Read the right-hand column as the checklist for Fieldwork 2."],[688.36,692.29,"When you finish your own strategy paper, ask four questions."],[692.29,696.3,"Did I start from the demand and the alternatives, or from a tool?"],[696.3,698.16,"Did I name what I rejected?"],[698.16,699.98,"Did I plan from the notice date?"],[700.26,703.99,"And did I check whether a Group or entity framework already exists?"],[704.5,711.21,"Let me give you an example of how a weaker answer sounds, so you can recognise it in your own drafts."],[711.21,716.77,"Imagine a team writes for P2: we will run an RFP and select the cheapest three firms."],[716.77,718.39,"It sounds decisive."],[718.67,727.76,"But it starts from the tool, it says nothing about how assignments will be called off, and it does not explain how the 29 uncompeted call-offs will stop."],[728.04,743.26,"A stronger version reads: demand arrives as a stream of different assignments, the market has many capable firms, so we will set up a panel with three lots and a rate card, and every call-off above a threshold will be competed among the panel firms."],[743.26,747.47,"Same category, same facts, very different quality of thinking."],[748.42,750.57,"Let's debrief with four questions."],[750.57,753.29,"Which project did your team argue about most?"],[753.29,758.11,"Did anyone choose an auction for P2 or P3, and if not, why not?"],[758.11,760.93,"What is the first step for Kelvar this month?"],[760.93,763.82,"And which project is most at risk on timing?"],[764.32,769.15,"Many teams argue most about P1, because of the lot question: one lot or two?"],[769.15,773.5,"That is a good argument to have, because both answers can work."],[773.5,780.6,"Few teams choose an auction for P2 or P3 once they have walked through the preconditions, and that is the point."],[780.88,784.92,"The first step for Kelvar this month is not a meeting with the vendor."],[784.92,789.37,"It is the usage audit, together with a check of the notice clause with Legal."],[789.87,794.94,"And the timing question is the insight the notes want to land."],[794.94,798.33,"P1 and P3 both expire in June 2027."],[798.33,801.09,"On the surface, they look equally urgent."],[801.09,809.69,"But P3 has a nine-month notice period and an auto-renewal clause, so its real deadline is September 2026."],[809.97,816.09,"P3 is the most urgent project in the portfolio, even though its expiry date is the same as cleaning."],[816.09,822.13,"In your own work, how many contracts in your pipeline are tracked by expiry date only?"],[822.13,824.05,"That is worth checking this week."],[824.55,828.13,"A useful habit for your own portfolio follows from this."],[828.13,835.38,"Once a quarter, sort your contract list by notice date rather than expiry date, and look at the next eighteen months."],[835.38,840.32,"The projects at the top of that list are the ones where your preparation has to start now."],[840.6,846.7,"For Nordvia, Kelvar would sit at the very top, Northbyte would follow, and cleaning would already be pressing."],[847.64,852.04,"Now the slide that the notes call the slide to remember from today."],[852.04,853.56,"Three patterns, one logic."],[853.56,858.31,"Three rows, each with the demand and market, the route, and an example."],[858.81,859.77,"Row one."],[859.77,864.09,"One continuous site service, with many suppliers in the market."],[864.09,868.93,"Route: one supplier, chosen by tender, on a three plus one plus one term."],[868.93,871.55,"Examples: cleaning and security."],[871.83,883.79,"The logic is that the service needs one accountable provider on site, the market has plenty of capable bidders, and a well-designed tender with a strong quality weighting is how you get the best one."],[884.29,884.6,"Row two."],[884.6,887.23,"A stream of different assignments."],[887.23,891.24,"Route: a panel with a rate card and mini-competitions."],[891.24,892.94,"Examples: consulting and IT services."],[892.94,903.96,"The logic: you cannot tender each job separately because that is too slow, and you should not give every job to one firm because that creates dependency."],[904.24,912.58,"So you pre-qualify a small group of firms, fix the commercial rules once, and let them compete for each assignment under simple call-off rules."],[913.08,913.88,"Row three."],[913.88,916.31,"No real alternative in the horizon."],[916.31,919.24,"Route: a sole-source negotiation."],[919.24,921.36,"Example: incumbent software."],[921.64,935.14,"The logic: competitive tools need competition, and here there is none within the contract term, so the power must come from preparation: usage facts, benchmarks, a credible BATNA for the next term, and protections."],[935.64,942.73,"You will notice that the multi-stage partnership process and the tail channel are not separate rows on this slide."],[942.73,953.77,"They are the two ends of the map we added in module 2.5, and they follow the same questions: is there a real alternative, how does demand arrive, and is the solution known?"],[954.05,957.72,"The notes suggest one more step, and I suggest you do it now."],[957.72,963.02,"Say out loud, or write down, which row your own project is in, and why."],[963.02,966.81,"If you cannot say why in one sentence, go back to the questions."],[967.77,970.49,"Now, what happens between now and Session 3?"],[970.49,973.1,"This is Fieldwork 2, in five steps."],[973.6,977.76,"Step one: finish the one-page strategy paper you drafted in module 2.5."],[977.76,982.92,"Step two: present it to the business owner in a thirty-minute conversation."],[982.92,986.91,"We will look at how to structure that on the next slide."],[987.19,996.23,"Step three: involve Risk and Compliance where your triage flagged something, such as outsourcing, DORA, personal data or staff transfer."],[996.23,1000.29,"Ask them about the regulatory path and the lead times it adds."],[1000.57,1008.75,"Step four: check whether a Group or entity framework already covers the need, and confirm the notice date of the current contract."],[1008.75,1009.69,"Not the expiry date."],[1009.69,1010.98,"The notice date."],[1010.98,1016.27,"Step five: record the agreed paper in your Sourcing Case and bring it to Session 3."],[1016.77,1018.41,"Why does this matter so much?"],[1018.41,1019.97,"The notes explain it."],[1019.97,1024.06,"Session 3 designs the RFx and the evaluation model."],[1024.06,1027.12,"Without an agreed route, there is nothing to design."],[1027.4,1038.63,"If you arrive at Session 3 with a route that the business owner has not agreed, you may design a beautiful tender for a project that should have been a panel call-off, or a negotiation."],[1038.63,1044.16,"Remember too what the fieldwork guide says: you are not asking for permission to run a tender."],[1044.44,1047.22,"You are agreeing how the business will buy, and why."],[1047.72,1049.79,"A practical tip on step four."],[1049.79,1058.46,"Checking for a Group or entity framework often takes one email to the right colleague, but people skip it because they assume the answer is no."],[1058.46,1062.55,"In the case, that check is the first line of the P5 route."],[1062.83,1072.45,"In real life, it can save you a whole tender, or reveal that another entity has just negotiated better terms with the same supplier that you can join."],[1073.4,1077.51,"Here is a structure for that thirty-minute strategy conversation."],[1077.51,1078.11,"Five blocks."],[1078.61,1082.71,"Minutes zero to five: the problem they recognise, in their words."],[1082.71,1086.41,"Minutes five to fifteen: the route, and why it fits."],[1086.41,1089.98,"Minutes fifteen to twenty-two: the options you rejected, and why."],[1089.98,1094.91,"Minutes twenty-two to twenty-seven: the timeline from the notice date."],[1095.19,1099.33,"And minutes twenty-seven to thirty: the decision you need, and by when."],[1099.83,1102.46,"The most important advice is in the notes."],[1102.46,1107.67,"Lead with the problem the business owner already feels, not with procurement policy."],[1107.67,1111.61,"For the Facilities manager, that is the complaints at headquarters."],[1111.89,1122.14,"For the CIO on consulting, it might be that day rates for the same profile vary by 77 percent, or that projects wait for a firm to become available."],[1122.14,1128.46,"For the IT director on Kelvar, it is the 9 percent uplift that landed on the budget last time."],[1128.74,1134.25,"Start there, and the route becomes the answer to their problem rather than a rule you are imposing."],[1134.74,1138.34,"Expect the classic pushbacks, and prepare one sentence for each."],[1138.34,1146.96,"We already have a favourite supplier: the panel and the direct-award rules answer that, because the favourite can join the panel if it competes."],[1147.24,1155.22,"A tender takes too long: the timeline from the notice date answers that, and shows that starting now is the fastest option."],[1155.22,1159.88,"The vendor will not negotiate: the BATNA and the usage facts answer that."],[1159.88,1164.76,"And notice where the rejected options sit: in the middle, after the route."],[1165.04,1170.05,"That is when the business owner is most likely to ask, why not just do it the old way?"],[1171.01,1173.08,"Here is a final poll question."],[1173.08,1177.57,"What will be hardest when you present your strategy paper to the business owner?"],[1177.57,1180.54,"Explaining why their favourite supplier must compete."],[1180.54,1184.31,"Getting a decision in time for the notice date."],[1184.31,1185.98,"Getting Risk and Compliance involved."],[1185.98,1188.59,"Or defending the options you rejected."],[1188.59,1190.15,"Take a moment to choose."],[1190.64,1197.49,"In a classroom we would use the result to pair you with someone who expects the same difficulty, so you can rehearse together."],[1197.49,1199.72,"You can do the same with a colleague."],[1199.72,1205.45,"Pick the answer you chose, and practise your opening five minutes with them before the real meeting."],[1205.95,1208.88,"Whatever you chose, the preparation is similar."],[1208.88,1217.22,"If the favourite supplier is the issue, prepare the sentence about the panel or the tender giving that supplier a fair chance to compete."],[1217.22,1224.6,"If timing is the issue, bring the timeline from the notice date and show what happens if the decision slips by a month."],[1224.88,1230.1,"If Risk and Compliance are the issue, contact them before the meeting, not after."],[1230.1,1238.64,"And if it is the rejected options, make sure each one has a short, factual reason that the business owner can repeat to their own manager."],[1239.58,1243.1,"Let's close Session 2 with the five lines on this slide."],[1243.1,1248.6,"Read them slowly with me, because these five lines are what we will ask back at the capstone."],[1249.11,1249.59,"One."],[1249.59,1255.25,"The tool follows the strategy, and the strategy follows the power position."],[1255.25,1257.51,"We do not start with RFQ, RFP or auction."],[1257.51,1264.04,"We start with where we stand: Kraljic, the Chessboard, and how the supplier sees us."],[1264.04,1264.09,"Two."],[1264.37,1274.3,"Continuous site services: one supplier by tender, on a three plus one plus one term, with quality weighted strongly and a should-cost floor."],[1274.3,1275.22,"Three."],[1275.22,1284.95,"Streams of professional and IT work: a panel with a rate card and mini-competitions, so that every assignment is competed without a new tender each time."],[1284.95,1285.0,"Four."],[1285.28,1289.88,"No real competition: a prepared negotiation, never an auction."],[1289.88,1297.75,"Usage facts, benchmarks, a credible BATNA for the next term, and protections such as uplift caps and DORA exit clauses."],[1297.75,1298.8,"Five."],[1298.8,1303.11,"One page records the route, the rejected options and the decision."],[1303.61,1307.38,"Around those five lines sit the ideas that make them work."],[1307.38,1311.08,"The six decisions, with the mechanism in fifth place."],[1311.08,1313.47,"The notice date as the real deadline."],[1313.75,1329.75,"The two ends of the map: a multi-stage process with dialogue and BAFO for complex partnerships like Northbyte, where exit feasibility is a selection criterion, and a channel for the tail, where an e-auction fits only when all four preconditions hold."],[1330.03,1340.14,"And the regulatory questions, Solvency II outsourcing and DORA, which change timelines and criteria, with Legal and Compliance owning the interpretation."],[1340.14,1347.07,"If you can explain your own project with that logic, you have achieved what this session set out to do."],[1348.02,1353.08,"And that brings us to the next session, which asks a new question: how do we run the event?"],[1353.08,1355.76,"Session 2 was about what route to take."],[1355.76,1358.17,"Session 3 is about running it well."],[1358.45,1373.41,"We will design the RFx documents, build the evaluation model, including how to score price without distorting the result, and set up the e-negotiation in Coupa, including when and how to use the auction formats we only previewed today."],[1373.91,1376.08,"Please bring your agreed strategy paper."],[1376.08,1378.72,"It is the input for everything we do next time."],[1378.72,1384.35,"Check the pre-work for Session 3 in your workbook and the date of the next class."],[1384.35,1386.54,"Thank you for your work in this session."],[1386.54,1391.32,"Good luck with your conversation with the business owner, and see you in Session 3."],[1391.82,1398.14,"Between now and then, keep the three patterns in mind whenever a new request reaches your desk."],[1398.14,1404.64,"Ask the questions in order: is there a real alternative, how does demand arrive, and is the solution known?"],[1404.92,1412.35,"You will be surprised how quickly the right route becomes obvious, and how much easier it is to explain to the people you work with."]]}],"s3":[{"id":"s3-l1","session":"s3","title":"The RFx toolbox and the event sequence","summary":"Choose the event sequence that follows from your route to market, plan it backwards from the notice deadline and assemble a complete RFx pack.","from":0,"to":26,"audio":"audio/s3-l1.mp3","duration":1501.2,"segs":[[0,0.6,20.5,"Welcome to Session 3 of the Source to Contract course. In the first session we asked what we are sourcing. In the second we chose the route to market. Today we move to a very practical question: how do we run the event? The subtitle tells you the territory: RFx, evaluation and e-auctions in Coupa."],[0,21.0,59.39,"This first video lesson covers three things. First, the opening of the session: where we are in the course and what you will produce today. Second, a short debrief of your fieldwork, the strategy paper you took to your business owner. Third, module 3.1, the RFx toolbox. We will look at what each tool is for, how the event sequence follows from your route to market, how to plan the timeline backwards, and what belongs in every RFx pack. As always, the video will pause now and then with a question. Take your time with each one. They are there to help you apply the ideas to real situations."],[1,60.34,67.83,"Let me start with the sentence that frames the whole session. The event cannot fix a wrong strategy. It can ruin a right one."],[1,68.33,115.29,"Think about what that means. If you chose the wrong route to market in Session 2, for example a tender for a software vendor you cannot realistically replace, then no amount of clever event design will save you. The best RFP in the world will not create competition that does not exist. But the reverse is also true. You can have a sound strategy, a panel of consulting firms, a well argued tender for cleaning, and then destroy it with a badly designed event: weights decided after the bids arrive, a pricing schedule nobody can compare, a timeline that runs out before the notice date. So today's rule is simple. Nothing is designed until the route to market is stated. Keep your strategy paper next to you while you watch."],[2,116.24,147.21,"Here is where we are in the course. The course follows four questions. WHAT are we sourcing? That was Session 1: the need, the category profile and your power position. WHICH route to market? That was Session 2: the sourcing strategy paper. Today is the third question: HOW do we run the event? That means the RFx plan, the evaluation model and the design of any e-negotiation. And in Session 4 we ask HOW do we close it well: negotiation, contract and handover."],[2,147.71,169.43,"Your Sourcing Case follows the same path. Parts 1 and 2 are done. Part 3 is built today, in the workbook, module by module. Part 4 comes in Session 4, and the capstone presentation follows about four weeks later. So everything you write today is not an exercise for its own sake. It becomes a section of the case you will defend."],[3,170.38,210.07,"Today you will do five things, one per module. First, choose the event sequence that follows from your strategy, and plan its timeline. That is module 3.1, in this lesson. Second, build a weighted evaluation model and fix it before issue. That is module 3.2. Third, evaluate real looking bids fairly, including one that is abnormally low. That is module 3.3. Fourth, decide when an e-auction helps and when it hurts. And fifth, write the Coupa set-up sheet for your own event. Each module ends with a practice block in the workbook, and each practice block becomes part of your Sourcing Case."],[4,211.02,246.24,"Here is the plan of the live session, which these video lessons follow. We open with a fifteen minute debrief of Fieldwork 2. Then module 3.1, the RFx toolbox, and module 3.2, designing the request. After a short break comes module 3.3, running it fairly, which is the heaviest calculation block. It sits right after the break on purpose, when energy is back. Then e-auctions, a second break, Coupa Sourcing in practice, and finally Case 3 and the close. This lesson takes you through the debrief and module 3.1."],[5,247.19,265.6,"So, the debrief. Fieldwork 2 asked you to take your one page strategy paper to the business owner. The question on the slide is the one that matters: what changed when the business owner read your route to market? A strategy that nobody has challenged is not yet a strategy. It is a draft."],[6,266.55,293.82,"In the live session we run a quick poll here. What did the business owner change in your strategy paper? Nothing, because it was approved as written? The number of suppliers? The term or the contract model? The scope, or even the need itself? Or have you not met them yet? Take a moment and answer it for yourself. If the honest answer is that you have not met them yet, write down now when you will, because the event you design today depends on their support."],[7,294.77,328.93,"In the classroom this is a trio debrief in breakout rooms, ten minutes, strictly on time. Each person gets two minutes on the business owner conversation. Which of the six strategy decisions did they challenge? Did Risk or Compliance add a gate, for example a DORA assessment or an outsourcing review? And finally, one sentence for the plenary: what will your event now look like? If you are watching alone, pause the video and write that one sentence in your workbook. The purpose is simply to bring the strategy back to mind before you design the event."],[8,329.88,339.41,"Across many projects, business owners tend to push back on the same four things. Let us look at them, because each one has a design answer in today's session."],[8,339.91,356.43,"The first is the number of suppliers. You hear two opposite complaints. Why only one? Or, why a panel, I already know who I want. The design answer is lots and published award rules. You decide in advance how many suppliers can win, and on what basis."],[8,356.93,374.68,"The second is the term. For the business owner it often feels too long, because they fear being locked in. For the supplier it may be too short to recover the cost of mobilisation. The design answer is term options, for example a base term plus extensions at your discretion."],[8,375.18,393.01,"The third is the incumbent. There is a real fear of losing a supplier who knows the building or the systems. The answer is not to protect the incumbent. It is a fair evaluation model that gives honest credit for continuity and transition risk, scored the same way for everyone."],[8,393.51,406.55,"And the fourth is the timeline. Many business owners discover in this conversation that the notice deadline is much closer than they thought. The answer is a backwards timeline, which we build later in this lesson."],[9,407.5,418.12,"Now we start module 3.1, the RFx toolbox. The subtitle is the key idea: every tool answers a different question, so choose the question first."],[10,419.07,429.89,"The tool follows the strategy, never the reverse. And the line underneath is worth saying out loud: we always do an RFQ with three quotes is a habit, not a strategy."],[10,430.39,482.59,"In our fictional case, Nordvia Insurance's procurement team of nine ran about sixty events a year in Coupa. Most of them were three quote RFQs, whatever the category. Cleaning, consulting, software, print, all through the same funnel. Does that sound familiar? It is very common, and it is understandable. Three quotes feel safe. They tick the policy box. But think about what they do in practice. For a consulting assignment, three quotes compare day rates and ignore the quality of the team. For a software renewal with one realistic vendor, two of the three quotes are decoration. And for office supplies, three quotes leave money on the table that a proper competitive event would find. The habit is not wrong because RFQs are bad. It is wrong because it skips the step where you ask what question you need the market to answer."],[11,483.54,491.95,"So here are four tools and the four questions they answer. These distinctions also match the way Coupa describes its own event types."],[11,492.45,508.38,"First, the RFI, the request for information. Its question is: who can do this, and how? It is for market discovery and pre-qualification. In Coupa an RFI collects qualitative information, forms and attachments, with no pricing."],[11,508.88,524.76,"Second, the RFP, the request for proposal. Its question is: what would you do, and at what price? You are asking for a solution plus a price, because the how matters. In Coupa an RFP collects prices and information in the same event."],[11,525.26,542.0,"Third, the RFQ, the request for quotation. Its question is: what is your price for this exact thing? It works only when the requirement is defined precisely enough that price is the main difference. In Coupa an RFQ prices items and lots."],[11,542.5,568.83,"And fourth, the e-auction. Its question is: how low will you go, live? It is price discovery under rules, with suppliers seeing their rank or the leading price and improving their bids in real time. We spend a whole module on auctions later today, because it is the sharpest tool in the box and the easiest to misuse. For now, remember the pairing. Discovery, solution, price, live price. Four questions, four tools."],[12,569.78,576.22,"This table tells you when to use each of the three RFx tools, and, just as important, when not to."],[12,576.72,618.58,"Start with the RFI. Use it when the market is new or changing, before a framework tender to understand who is out there, or to build a BATNA, your best alternative to a negotiated agreement, against an incumbent. Do not use it when the market is well known. Then it just adds weeks. There are three classic RFI pitfalls. It gets too long, with sixty questions nobody will read. Prices given in an RFI get treated as binding, which they are not. And it gets used as free consulting, asking suppliers to design your solution with no intention of buying from them. Suppliers remember buyers who do that, and they bid less seriously next time."],[12,619.08,642.15,"Next, the RFP. Use it when quality differs between suppliers: soft facility management such as cleaning and security, consulting, marketing agencies, IT services, software as a service. Do not use it for pure commodities, where it adds effort without adding insight, or in a sole source situation, where there is nothing to choose between."],[12,642.65,661.74,"And the RFQ. Use it for standard items, unchanged renewals, or as the pre-bid phase before an auction. Do not use it when the specification is unclear. If suppliers have to guess what you mean, they will price their guesses, and you will be comparing different things that happen to share a line item."],[13,662.69,667.17,"Real events are often combinations. This slide shows four patterns."],[13,667.67,680.19,"First, RFx plus auction. Coupa can run an RFx as the pre-bidding phase of an auction. Suppliers qualify and submit initial prices, and only then does the live auction start."],[13,680.69,713.59,"Second, multi-stage. An RFI, then an RFP, then a shortlist, then dialogue, then revised offers. You reduce the number of bidders step by step as the solution becomes clearer. This borrows the structure of the EU competitive procedure with negotiation, in Articles 29 and 30 of the 2014 EU public procurement directive, without its legal constraints. As a private insurer you are free to design the stages, but you must tell bidders in advance what the stages are."],[13,714.09,723.41,"Third, follow-on events. In Coupa, responses can carry over to the next event as drafts, so suppliers do not retype everything in round two."],[13,723.91,743.52,"And fourth, BAFO, the best and final offer. This is a final sealed improvement from the shortlist, judged against the criteria you already published. One warning: if you run a BAFO in every event, suppliers learn to hold something back for it. Use it when there is a reason, not as a routine extra round."],[14,744.47,750.72,"Here is a typical multi-stage pattern for complex services. Follow the steps from left to right."],[14,751.22,785.53,"It starts with an RFI to pre-qualify: who has the capability, the certifications and the scale? Then RFP stage one, where qualified suppliers propose a solution with an indicative price. Then a shortlist of three or four. Why three or four? Because dialogue is expensive, for you and for them, and more than four rarely adds real competition. Then dialogue workshops, where you test the solutions, clarify your requirement and let suppliers improve their proposals. Then a BAFO, and finally the award."],[14,786.03,815.13,"The sentence under the flow matters. An auction on price may follow the BAFO, but only among bidders above the quality threshold, and only if the preconditions we discuss in module 3.4 hold. Use this pattern for managed services and business process outsourcing, like Nordvia's claims IT managed service with Northbyte. Never use it for a stationery catalogue. A six month multi-stage process for pens would cost more than it could ever save."],[15,816.08,830.56,"This is the bridge from Session 2, and probably the most useful table in the module. On the left, the route to market from your strategy paper. On the right, the event sequence that follows from it. Find your own row as we go."],[15,831.06,843.99,"If your route is one supplier chosen by tender, for example cleaning or security, the sequence is: an optional RFI, then an RFP, site visits, and then negotiation or a BAFO."],[15,844.49,855.8,"If your route is a panel, for consulting, IT services or agencies, you run a framework RFP with a rate card, and afterwards mini-competitions for each assignment."],[15,856.3,869.16,"If your route is sole or single source, typically incumbent software, there is no RFP. The sequence is usage analysis, benchmark, an RFI to the alternatives, and negotiation."],[15,869.66,881.18,"If your category is a commodity, office supplies or print volumes, you go RFQ, then an English reverse auction, then a catalogue so that people can actually buy from the result."],[15,881.68,900.88,"And for a strategic partnership: RFI, RFP, dialogue and BAFO, the multi-stage pattern we just saw. Pause here if you need to, and write your row in your workbook. If your project does not fit any row, that is a signal to go back to your strategy paper, not to invent a new sequence."],[16,901.83,911.62,"Let us make it concrete with three worked examples from the fictional Nordvia case. First, project P1, office cleaning at three sites in two countries."],[16,912.12,956.4,"The route from the strategy paper is one supplier per lot, chosen by tender, with a term of three years plus one plus one. Is there an RFI? No. The local market is known, and there are four to six capable cleaners. An RFI would only add weeks. So the event is an RFP with a hybrid specification, staff data and the draft contract. Staff data matters in cleaning, because bidders need to know how many people may transfer and on what terms to price correctly. Then site visits, clarification, and negotiation or a BAFO with the best two. Then award, three months of mobilisation, and a start before the contracts expire in June 2027."],[16,956.9,970.46,"Now notice what is missing. There is no auction. Cleaning is labour intensive, and a live price war can push bids below what the wage floor allows. Module 3.4 explains why that matters so much."],[17,971.41,989.91,"Project P2 is IT consulting and project staff. Today Nordvia uses twelve firms through thirty eight call-offs, and senior day rates for the same profile range from 820 to 1,450 euros. That spread alone tells you there is no market discipline."],[17,990.41,1023.33,"The answer is a panel, not a tender for each project. Event one is a framework RFP, split into lots, with a rate card of maximum day rates. Nordvia awards three to five firms per lot. Then, during the life of the framework, assignments above a threshold go to mini-competitions in Coupa RFP events, and below the threshold to a documented direct award. And here is a clever detail: labour law checks and the DORA screening are done once, in the framework, not repeated in every call-off."],[17,1023.83,1041.79,"The design lesson is that the framework event happens once, and mini-competitions happen many times. So you must design both before you issue the first. If the mini-competition rules are vague, the business will route every assignment to its favourite firm, and the framework becomes a list of names."],[18,1042.74,1049.36,"Project P3 is Kelvar, the core insurance software. The title says it: no RFP for show."],[18,1049.86,1069.23,"The facts: one vendor, 11.2 million euros a year, a 9 percent uplift at the last renewal, an auto-renewal clause and nine months' notice. There are two credible alternatives, but migration would take eighteen to twenty four months. So realistically, Kelvar is the supplier for the next cycle."],[18,1069.73,1111.46,"What is the event, then? An RFI to the two alternatives. Its purpose is not to replace Kelvar. It is to make the BATNA real, to know what moving would cost and how long it would take, so that the negotiation is grounded in facts. Then a usage audit, because the team estimates twenty to twenty five percent shelfware, licences paid for and not used. Then a benchmark, and negotiation rounds, which we run in Session 4. And never an auction. There is one realistic supplier. An RFP that everyone knows will not change the supplier destroys credibility, with the vendor, who sees through it, and with the market, who wasted effort bidding."],[19,1112.41,1145.39,"In the live session we poll the room here. Think about Nordvia's P2, IT consulting: twelve firms, thirty eight call-offs, no framework. Which first event would you run? An e-auction on day rates? A framework RFP with a rate card, then mini-competitions? An RFQ for the cheapest firm? Or a negotiation with Brightline only? Make your choice before you read on. The expected answer is the framework RFP with a rate card. An auction on day rates would buy cheap profiles, not capability."],[20,1146.34,1159.53,"Now to the timeline. This table gives indicative durations, and the footnote is important. These are rules of thumb from practice, not norms. Replace them with your own team's data as soon as you have it."],[20,1160.03,1207.88,"An RFI response window is typically two to three weeks. An RFP response window for services, three to six weeks. A simple mini-competition, at least ten working days, which is the minimum in the UK Crown Commercial Service consultancy framework. That is the only figure on this slide with a documented reference. Evaluation and consensus, two to four weeks. Negotiation, BAFO and approvals, two to six weeks. And mobilisation for a labour intensive service, often around three months. Everything except the ten working days is illustrative. Look at the total, though. Add the middle of each range and a services tender easily takes five to seven months from issue to start, before you even count the time to prepare the pack."],[21,1208.83,1233.6,"Which brings us to the most practical habit in this module: plan backwards from the notice deadline. Read the flow from left to right. You start at the notice deadline. Before it, the contract must be signed. Before that, the award approved. Before that, evaluation and negotiation. Before that, bids received. Before that, the RFx issued. And before that, the pack and the weights agreed."],[21,1234.1,1257.06,"Take Kelvar. The licence renews in June 2027, with nine months' notice. So the real deadline is nine months before the expiry, around September 2026, not June 2027. If you have not secured a better deal or given notice by then, the auto-renewal takes effect and your leverage is gone for another cycle."],[21,1257.56,1274.54,"Most urgent events are missed notice dates in disguise. Nobody planned to be in a hurry. They simply counted from the expiry. So, a small action for you now: pause the video and write down the notice date of your own project. Not the expiry. The notice date."],[22,1275.49,1278.67,"What goes into every RFx pack? Six blocks."],[22,1279.17,1328.54,"First, instructions: the timetable, the rules, the Q and A channel, and how bids will be evaluated. Second, the specification: input, output or hybrid, with volumes and sites. Third, the pricing schedule: one template for everyone, with recurring prices, one-off costs and unit rates kept separate. Fourth, the questionnaire, with knock-out questions first and the scored questions after them. Fifth, the draft contract, with service levels, KPIs and service credits. And sixth, the regulatory annexes: the DORA Article 30 clauses for ICT services, the Solvency II Article 274 terms where you outsource a critical or important function, data protection terms, and staff data where people may transfer."],[22,1329.04,1343.93,"In practice, the two blocks most often missing are the draft contract and the regulatory annex. Both are painful to add later. A supplier who has already won is not in a hurry to accept audit and access rights it never priced."],[23,1344.88,1349.19,"So, one instruction worth its own slide. Attach the draft contract."],[23,1349.69,1389.42,"With the contract in the pack, suppliers bid on your terms. Each bidder must say which clauses they accept and which they want to change, and those deviations become part of the evaluation. That is negotiation through the bids, while you still have competition. Without the contract, you negotiate the terms after award, when the winner knows it has won and your leverage has gone. Picture a security services tender where liability caps, service credits and the exit clause are only discussed after the award. Every point becomes a concession you have to buy back. Attach the contract, and those points are priced into the bids instead."],[24,1390.37,1421.6,"Time to apply this. Practice 3.1 in your workbook takes about fifteen minutes. State your route to market from your strategy paper. Write the notice deadline, not the expiry. List the stages and the tool for each one. Plan the start and the duration of each stage backwards. And tick what your RFx pack will contain. The most common error is a sequence that starts from today rather than from the notice deadline. Pause the video now and do it in the Modules tab if you want, then continue."],[25,1422.55,1450.67,"Let us debrief with four questions. Whose sequence starts later than it should, and what will you do about it? Be honest: that is the most valuable finding of the exercise. Who removed an RFI, and who added one, and why? Who has a sole source project, and what exactly is your RFI for? And what will your draft contract need that it does not have today? In the classroom we put two timelines on screen, and we praise the backward plan, not the long one."],[26,1451.62,1473.77,"Here is module 3.1 in three lines. First, choose the question, then the tool: RFI, RFP, RFQ, BAFO or auction. Second, the sequence follows the route to market, and a sole source gets a negotiation, not an RFP for show. Third, plan backwards from the notice deadline, and attach the draft contract."],[26,1474.27,1500.0,"Try saying those three lines in your own words before you move on. Then think about what comes next. You now know which events you will run and when. But an RFP is only as good as what it asks for. In the next lesson, module 3.2, we design the request itself: the specification, the lots, the pricing schedule, and the evaluation model that you must fix before anything goes out. See you there."]],"questions":[{"after":3,"q":"A colleague wants to start designing the cleaning RFP before the route to market is agreed, to save time. What is the main risk?","options":["None: the event design is independent of the strategy","A well-built event can still deliver the wrong strategy, and a weak one can ruin a right strategy","Coupa will reject the event","The business owner will not need to be involved"],"answer":1,"explain":"The event cannot fix a wrong strategy, and it can ruin a right one. That is why nothing is designed until the route to market is stated.","t":210.07},{"after":8,"q":"Nordvia's Head of Real Estate worries that switching cleaning supplier at HQ will lose people who know the building. What is the best design answer?","options":["Exclude new entrants so the incumbent keeps the contract","Give the incumbent a fixed bonus of points for being the incumbent","Use a fair evaluation model that scores transition and continuity the same way for every bidder","Ignore the concern, because price is what matters"],"answer":2,"explain":"Fear of losing the incumbent is answered by a fair model, not by protection. Scoring transition risk and continuity for all bidders gives honest credit without breaking equal treatment.","t":406.55},{"after":11,"q":"Nordvia's General Services team wants to know which suppliers could run a print management service it has never bought before. Which tool fits first?","options":["An RFI","An RFQ","An e-auction","A BAFO"],"answer":0,"explain":"The question is who can do this and how, which is market discovery. An RFI answers it; pricing tools need a defined requirement that does not exist yet.","t":568.83},{"after":13,"q":"In a security services RFP, a colleague suggests adding a BAFO round to every event as standard. What is the risk?","options":["Coupa cannot run follow-on events","Bidders learn to hold back their best price for the BAFO","A BAFO breaks equal treatment by definition","There is no risk; more rounds always mean lower prices"],"answer":1,"explain":"A routine BAFO teaches suppliers to keep something in reserve, so first-round bids get worse. Use a BAFO when there is a reason, against the published criteria.","t":743.52},{"after":15,"q":"Your strategy paper sets a panel of three to five creative agencies. Which event sequence follows?","options":["An English reverse auction on agency fees","Usage analysis, benchmark and negotiation","An RFQ with three quotes for each campaign","A framework RFP with a rate card, then mini-competitions for campaigns"],"answer":3,"explain":"A panel route leads to a framework RFP with a rate card, then mini-competitions or documented direct awards. Auctions and RFQs would compare price on work where quality differs.","t":900.88},{"after":17,"q":"Nordvia is designing the IT consulting framework. Where should the DORA screening and labour law checks sit?","options":["In every mini-competition, repeated each time","Only after a problem appears","In the framework award, once, so call-offs can run quickly","Nowhere: consulting is never in scope"],"answer":2,"explain":"Gates that apply to every assignment are best checked once in the framework. Mini-competitions then focus on the specific assignment, and the framework rules must be designed before the first event is issued.","t":1041.79},{"after":18,"q":"A stakeholder proposes a full RFP to all core software vendors for the Kelvar renewal, knowing migration would take two years. What do you recommend?","options":["Run the RFP anyway, it creates pressure","Run an e-auction among the vendors","An RFI to the two alternatives to make the BATNA real, then usage audit, benchmark and negotiation","Renew automatically and save the effort"],"answer":2,"explain":"With one realistic supplier, an RFP for show destroys credibility with the vendor and the market. A targeted RFI builds a real alternative, and the leverage comes from usage data and benchmarks in negotiation.","t":1111.46},{"after":21,"q":"A facilities contract expires on 31 December 2027 with six months' notice. When does your plan really have to be finished?","options":["Around 30 June 2027, the notice deadline","31 December 2027","Whenever the RFP closes","Three months after the expiry, during mobilisation"],"answer":0,"explain":"The real deadline is the notice date, six months before expiry. Planning backwards from it, rather than from the expiry or from today, avoids the classic urgent event caused by a missed notice.","t":1274.54},{"after":23,"q":"Why should the draft contract be attached to the RFx pack?","options":["Because Coupa will not publish an event without it","To make the pack longer and discourage weak bidders","So legal can skip the contract review","So suppliers bid on your terms and their deviations become part of the evaluation"],"answer":3,"explain":"With the contract in the pack, terms are negotiated through the bids while competition still exists. Without it, terms are negotiated after award, when leverage has gone.","t":1389.42},{"after":26,"q":"Which statement best sums up module 3.1?","options":["Always run an RFQ with three quotes; it is the safest habit","Use an e-auction whenever there are more than two suppliers","Choose the event from the route to market, plan backwards from the notice date and attach the draft contract","Plan the timeline from today, then add buffer"],"answer":2,"explain":"The tool follows the strategy, the timeline starts from the notice deadline, and the draft contract belongs in the pack. Habits and forward planning are the common failure modes.","t":1500.0}],"cues":[[0.6,4.06,"Welcome to Session 3 of the Source to Contract course."],[4.06,7.97,"In the first session we asked what we are sourcing."],[7.97,10.07,"In the second we chose the route to market."],[10.07,14.61,"Today we move to a very practical question: how do we run the event?"],[14.61,20.5,"The subtitle tells you the territory: RFx, evaluation and e-auctions in Coupa."],[21.0,23.71,"This first video lesson covers three things."],[23.71,29.86,"First, the opening of the session: where we are in the course and what you will produce today."],[29.86,36.27,"Second, a short debrief of your fieldwork, the strategy paper you took to your business owner."],[36.27,38.81,"Third, module 3.1, the RFx toolbox."],[39.09,49.36,"We will look at what each tool is for, how the event sequence follows from your route to market, how to plan the timeline backwards, and what belongs in every RFx pack."],[49.36,53.42,"As always, the video will pause now and then with a question."],[53.42,55.26,"Take your time with each one."],[55.54,59.38,"They are there to help you apply the ideas to real situations."],[60.34,64.33,"Let me start with the sentence that frames the whole session."],[64.33,66.27,"The event cannot fix a wrong strategy."],[66.27,67.83,"It can ruin a right one."],[68.33,69.86,"Think about what that means."],[69.86,81.91,"If you chose the wrong route to market in Session 2, for example a tender for a software vendor you cannot realistically replace, then no amount of clever event design will save you."],[81.91,86.74,"The best RFP in the world will not create competition that does not exist."],[86.74,88.49,"But the reverse is also true."],[88.77,106.22,"You can have a sound strategy, a panel of consulting firms, a well argued tender for cleaning, and then destroy it with a badly designed event: weights decided after the bids arrive, a pricing schedule nobody can compare, a timeline that runs out before the notice date."],[106.22,108.38,"So today's rule is simple."],[108.66,112.23,"Nothing is designed until the route to market is stated."],[112.23,115.29,"Keep your strategy paper next to you while you watch."],[116.24,118.61,"Here is where we are in the course."],[118.61,120.21,"The course follows four questions."],[120.21,121.59,"WHAT are we sourcing?"],[121.59,126.21,"That was Session 1: the need, the category profile and your power position."],[126.21,127.8,"WHICH route to market?"],[127.8,131.21,"That was Session 2: the sourcing strategy paper."],[131.21,134.52,"Today is the third question: HOW do we run the event?"],[134.8,140.82,"That means the RFx plan, the evaluation model and the design of any e-negotiation."],[140.82,147.22,"And in Session 4 we ask HOW do we close it well: negotiation, contract and handover."],[147.71,150.77,"Your Sourcing Case follows the same path."],[150.77,152.16,"Parts 1 and 2 are done."],[152.16,156.84,"Part 3 is built today, in the workbook, module by module."],[156.84,161.54,"Part 4 comes in Session 4, and the capstone presentation follows about four weeks later."],[161.54,166.65,"So everything you write today is not an exercise for its own sake."],[166.93,169.43,"It becomes a section of the case you will defend."],[170.38,173.38,"Today you will do five things, one per module."],[173.38,179.81,"First, choose the event sequence that follows from your strategy, and plan its timeline."],[179.81,182.28,"That is module 3.1, in this lesson."],[182.28,187.53,"Second, build a weighted evaluation model and fix it before issue."],[187.53,188.19,"That is module 3.2."],[188.47,193.76,"Third, evaluate real looking bids fairly, including one that is abnormally low."],[193.76,195.27,"That is module 3.3."],[195.27,199.53,"Fourth, decide when an e-auction helps and when it hurts."],[199.53,203.32,"And fifth, write the Coupa set-up sheet for your own event."],[203.6,210.07,"Each module ends with a practice block in the workbook, and each practice block becomes part of your Sourcing Case."],[211.02,215.7,"Here is the plan of the live session, which these video lessons follow."],[215.7,219.43,"We open with a fifteen minute debrief of Fieldwork 2."],[219.43,225.36,"Then module 3.1, the RFx toolbox, and module 3.2, designing the request."],[225.36,231.86,"After a short break comes module 3.3, running it fairly, which is the heaviest calculation block."],[232.14,236.47,"It sits right after the break on purpose, when energy is back."],[236.47,242.16,"Then e-auctions, a second break, Coupa Sourcing in practice, and finally Case 3 and the close."],[242.16,246.24,"This lesson takes you through the debrief and module 3.1."],[247.19,248.6,"So, the debrief."],[248.6,253.63,"Fieldwork 2 asked you to take your one page strategy paper to the business owner."],[253.63,260.19,"The question on the slide is the one that matters: what changed when the business owner read your route to market?"],[260.19,264.42,"A strategy that nobody has challenged is not yet a strategy."],[264.42,265.6,"It is a draft."],[266.55,269.04,"In the live session we run a quick poll here."],[269.04,272.88,"What did the business owner change in your strategy paper?"],[272.88,275.62,"Nothing, because it was approved as written?"],[275.62,276.74,"The number of suppliers?"],[276.74,278.48,"The term or the contract model?"],[278.48,280.89,"The scope, or even the need itself?"],[280.89,282.57,"Or have you not met them yet?"],[282.57,284.98,"Take a moment and answer it for yourself."],[285.26,293.82,"If the honest answer is that you have not met them yet, write down now when you will, because the event you design today depends on their support."],[294.77,300.54,"In the classroom this is a trio debrief in breakout rooms, ten minutes, strictly on time."],[300.54,304.34,"Each person gets two minutes on the business owner conversation."],[304.34,307.64,"Which of the six strategy decisions did they challenge?"],[307.64,313.74,"Did Risk or Compliance add a gate, for example a DORA assessment or an outsourcing review?"],[314.02,318.54,"And finally, one sentence for the plenary: what will your event now look like?"],[318.54,323.75,"If you are watching alone, pause the video and write that one sentence in your workbook."],[323.75,328.93,"The purpose is simply to bring the strategy back to mind before you design the event."],[329.88,334.82,"Across many projects, business owners tend to push back on the same four things."],[334.82,339.42,"Let us look at them, because each one has a design answer in today's session."],[339.91,342.53,"The first is the number of suppliers."],[342.53,344.05,"You hear two opposite complaints."],[344.05,345.29,"Why only one?"],[345.29,348.52,"Or, why a panel, I already know who I want."],[348.52,352.21,"The design answer is lots and published award rules."],[352.21,356.42,"You decide in advance how many suppliers can win, and on what basis."],[356.93,358.48,"The second is the term."],[358.48,363.57,"For the business owner it often feels too long, because they fear being locked in."],[363.57,368.2,"For the supplier it may be too short to recover the cost of mobilisation."],[368.2,374.68,"The design answer is term options, for example a base term plus extensions at your discretion."],[375.18,377.0,"The third is the incumbent."],[377.0,382.23,"There is a real fear of losing a supplier who knows the building or the systems."],[382.23,385.05,"The answer is not to protect the incumbent."],[385.05,393.01,"It is a fair evaluation model that gives honest credit for continuity and transition risk, scored the same way for everyone."],[393.51,395.49,"And the fourth is the timeline."],[395.49,402.07,"Many business owners discover in this conversation that the notice deadline is much closer than they thought."],[402.07,406.54,"The answer is a backwards timeline, which we build later in this lesson."],[407.5,410.44,"Now we start module 3.1, the RFx toolbox."],[410.44,418.12,"The subtitle is the key idea: every tool answers a different question, so choose the question first."],[419.07,422.38,"The tool follows the strategy, never the reverse."],[422.38,429.89,"And the line underneath is worth saying out loud: we always do an RFQ with three quotes is a habit, not a strategy."],[430.39,437.0,"In our fictional case, Nordvia Insurance's procurement team of nine ran about sixty events a year in Coupa."],[437.0,441.25,"Most of them were three quote RFQs, whatever the category."],[441.25,445.81,"Cleaning, consulting, software, print, all through the same funnel."],[445.81,447.51,"Does that sound familiar?"],[447.51,450.42,"It is very common, and it is understandable."],[450.7,452.32,"Three quotes feel safe."],[452.32,453.8,"They tick the policy box."],[453.8,455.97,"But think about what they do in practice."],[455.97,462.27,"For a consulting assignment, three quotes compare day rates and ignore the quality of the team."],[462.27,467.53,"For a software renewal with one realistic vendor, two of the three quotes are decoration."],[467.81,473.91,"And for office supplies, three quotes leave money on the table that a proper competitive event would find."],[473.91,476.49,"The habit is not wrong because RFQs are bad."],[476.49,482.6,"It is wrong because it skips the step where you ask what question you need the market to answer."],[483.54,487.39,"So here are four tools and the four questions they answer."],[487.39,491.95,"These distinctions also match the way Coupa describes its own event types."],[492.45,495.6,"First, the RFI, the request for information."],[495.6,498.76,"Its question is: who can do this, and how?"],[498.76,502.0,"It is for market discovery and pre-qualification."],[502.0,508.39,"In Coupa an RFI collects qualitative information, forms and attachments, with no pricing."],[508.88,512.06,"Second, the RFP, the request for proposal."],[512.06,515.75,"Its question is: what would you do, and at what price?"],[515.75,519.88,"You are asking for a solution plus a price, because the how matters."],[519.88,524.75,"In Coupa an RFP collects prices and information in the same event."],[525.26,528.73,"Third, the RFQ, the request for quotation."],[528.73,532.44,"Its question is: what is your price for this exact thing?"],[532.44,539.31,"It works only when the requirement is defined precisely enough that price is the main difference."],[539.31,542.01,"In Coupa an RFQ prices items and lots."],[542.5,544.2,"And fourth, the e-auction."],[544.2,547.45,"Its question is: how low will you go, live?"],[547.45,555.24,"It is price discovery under rules, with suppliers seeing their rank or the leading price and improving their bids in real time."],[555.24,562.19,"We spend a whole module on auctions later today, because it is the sharpest tool in the box and the easiest to misuse."],[562.47,564.1,"For now, remember the pairing."],[564.1,566.99,"Discovery, solution, price, live price."],[566.99,568.83,"Four questions, four tools."],[569.78,576.22,"This table tells you when to use each of the three RFx tools, and, just as important, when not to."],[576.72,578.39,"Start with the RFI."],[578.39,590.31,"Use it when the market is new or changing, before a framework tender to understand who is out there, or to build a BATNA, your best alternative to a negotiated agreement, against an incumbent."],[590.31,593.63,"Do not use it when the market is well known."],[593.63,595.24,"Then it just adds weeks."],[595.24,597.39,"There are three classic RFI pitfalls."],[597.67,601.51,"It gets too long, with sixty questions nobody will read."],[601.51,605.94,"Prices given in an RFI get treated as binding, which they are not."],[605.94,613.65,"And it gets used as free consulting, asking suppliers to design your solution with no intention of buying from them."],[613.65,618.58,"Suppliers remember buyers who do that, and they bid less seriously next time."],[619.08,620.21,"Next, the RFP."],[620.21,632.56,"Use it when quality differs between suppliers: soft facility management such as cleaning and security, consulting, marketing agencies, IT services, software as a service."],[632.84,642.14,"Do not use it for pure commodities, where it adds effort without adding insight, or in a sole source situation, where there is nothing to choose between."],[642.65,643.41,"And the RFQ."],[643.41,649.38,"Use it for standard items, unchanged renewals, or as the pre-bid phase before an auction."],[649.38,653.76,"Do not use it when the specification is unclear."],[653.76,661.74,"If suppliers have to guess what you mean, they will price their guesses, and you will be comparing different things that happen to share a line item."],[662.69,665.09,"Real events are often combinations."],[665.09,667.17,"This slide shows four patterns."],[667.67,669.44,"First, RFx plus auction."],[669.44,673.61,"Coupa can run an RFx as the pre-bidding phase of an auction."],[673.61,680.19,"Suppliers qualify and submit initial prices, and only then does the live auction start."],[680.69,682.48,"Second, multi-stage."],[682.48,687.03,"An RFI, then an RFP, then a shortlist, then dialogue, then revised offers."],[687.03,693.32,"You reduce the number of bidders step by step as the solution becomes clearer."],[693.6,705.75,"This borrows the structure of the EU competitive procedure with negotiation, in Articles 29 and 30 of the 2014 EU public procurement directive, without its legal constraints."],[705.75,713.59,"As a private insurer you are free to design the stages, but you must tell bidders in advance what the stages are."],[714.09,716.1,"Third, follow-on events."],[716.1,723.41,"In Coupa, responses can carry over to the next event as drafts, so suppliers do not retype everything in round two."],[723.91,727.02,"And fourth, BAFO, the best and final offer."],[727.02,733.53,"This is a final sealed improvement from the shortlist, judged against the criteria you already published."],[733.53,739.44,"One warning: if you run a BAFO in every event, suppliers learn to hold something back for it."],[739.44,743.52,"Use it when there is a reason, not as a routine extra round."],[744.47,748.56,"Here is a typical multi-stage pattern for complex services."],[748.56,750.72,"Follow the steps from left to right."],[751.22,758.13,"It starts with an RFI to pre-qualify: who has the capability, the certifications and the scale?"],[758.13,764.08,"Then RFP stage one, where qualified suppliers propose a solution with an indicative price."],[764.08,766.48,"Then a shortlist of three or four."],[766.48,767.73,"Why three or four?"],[768.01,774.86,"Because dialogue is expensive, for you and for them, and more than four rarely adds real competition."],[774.86,782.81,"Then dialogue workshops, where you test the solutions, clarify your requirement and let suppliers improve their proposals."],[782.81,785.53,"Then a BAFO, and finally the award."],[786.03,788.09,"The sentence under the flow matters."],[788.09,798.7,"An auction on price may follow the BAFO, but only among bidders above the quality threshold, and only if the preconditions we discuss in module 3.4 hold."],[798.7,806.89,"Use this pattern for managed services and business process outsourcing, like Nordvia's claims IT managed service with Northbyte."],[807.17,809.8,"Never use it for a stationery catalogue."],[809.8,815.13,"A six month multi-stage process for pens would cost more than it could ever save."],[816.08,821.38,"This is the bridge from Session 2, and probably the most useful table in the module."],[821.38,825.0,"On the left, the route to market from your strategy paper."],[825.0,828.36,"On the right, the event sequence that follows from it."],[828.36,830.57,"Find your own row as we go."],[831.06,843.99,"If your route is one supplier chosen by tender, for example cleaning or security, the sequence is: an optional RFI, then an RFP, site visits, and then negotiation or a BAFO."],[844.49,855.8,"If your route is a panel, for consulting, IT services or agencies, you run a framework RFP with a rate card, and afterwards mini-competitions for each assignment."],[856.3,862.54,"If your route is sole or single source, typically incumbent software, there is no RFP."],[862.54,869.16,"The sequence is usage analysis, benchmark, an RFI to the alternatives, and negotiation."],[869.66,881.18,"If your category is a commodity, office supplies or print volumes, you go RFQ, then an English reverse auction, then a catalogue so that people can actually buy from the result."],[881.68,888.24,"And for a strategic partnership: RFI, RFP, dialogue and BAFO, the multi-stage pattern we just saw."],[888.24,893.12,"Pause here if you need to, and write your row in your workbook."],[893.12,900.88,"If your project does not fit any row, that is a signal to go back to your strategy paper, not to invent a new sequence."],[901.83,907.35,"Let us make it concrete with three worked examples from the fictional Nordvia case."],[907.35,911.62,"First, project P1, office cleaning at three sites in two countries."],[912.12,921.6,"The route from the strategy paper is one supplier per lot, chosen by tender, with a term of three years plus one plus one."],[921.6,922.38,"Is there an RFI?"],[922.38,922.83,"No."],[922.83,926.58,"The local market is known, and there are four to six capable cleaners."],[926.58,928.97,"An RFI would only add weeks."],[929.25,935.31,"So the event is an RFP with a hybrid specification, staff data and the draft contract."],[935.31,944.43,"Staff data matters in cleaning, because bidders need to know how many people may transfer and on what terms to price correctly."],[944.43,949.05,"Then site visits, clarification, and negotiation or a BAFO with the best two."],[949.33,956.39,"Then award, three months of mobilisation, and a start before the contracts expire in June 2027."],[956.9,958.61,"Now notice what is missing."],[958.61,960.12,"There is no auction."],[960.12,967.43,"Cleaning is labour intensive, and a live price war can push bids below what the wage floor allows."],[967.43,970.47,"Module 3.4 explains why that matters so much."],[971.41,974.62,"Project P2 is IT consulting and project staff."],[974.62,985.47,"Today Nordvia uses twelve firms through thirty eight call-offs, and senior day rates for the same profile range from 820 to 1,450 euros."],[985.47,989.91,"That spread alone tells you there is no market discipline."],[990.41,994.0,"The answer is a panel, not a tender for each project."],[994.0,1000.67,"Event one is a framework RFP, split into lots, with a rate card of maximum day rates."],[1000.67,1003.55,"Nordvia awards three to five firms per lot."],[1003.83,1014.67,"Then, during the life of the framework, assignments above a threshold go to mini-competitions in Coupa RFP events, and below the threshold to a documented direct award."],[1014.67,1023.33,"And here is a clever detail: labour law checks and the DORA screening are done once, in the framework, not repeated in every call-off."],[1023.83,1030.03,"The design lesson is that the framework event happens once, and mini-competitions happen many times."],[1030.03,1033.47,"So you must design both before you issue the first."],[1033.47,1041.79,"If the mini-competition rules are vague, the business will route every assignment to its favourite firm, and the framework becomes a list of names."],[1042.74,1046.46,"Project P3 is Kelvar, the core insurance software."],[1046.46,1049.35,"The title says it: no RFP for show."],[1049.86,1058.38,"The facts: one vendor, 11.2 million euros a year, a 9 percent uplift at the last renewal, an auto-renewal clause and nine months' notice."],[1058.38,1065.43,"There are two credible alternatives, but migration would take eighteen to twenty four months."],[1065.43,1069.23,"So realistically, Kelvar is the supplier for the next cycle."],[1069.73,1071.24,"What is the event, then?"],[1071.24,1073.84,"An RFI to the two alternatives."],[1073.84,1075.52,"Its purpose is not to replace Kelvar."],[1075.52,1084.58,"It is to make the BATNA real, to know what moving would cost and how long it would take, so that the negotiation is grounded in facts."],[1084.86,1092.37,"Then a usage audit, because the team estimates twenty to twenty five percent shelfware, licences paid for and not used."],[1092.37,1097.17,"Then a benchmark, and negotiation rounds, which we run in Session 4."],[1097.17,1098.7,"And never an auction."],[1098.7,1101.07,"There is one realistic supplier."],[1101.35,1111.46,"An RFP that everyone knows will not change the supplier destroys credibility, with the vendor, who sees through it, and with the market, who wasted effort bidding."],[1112.41,1114.73,"In the live session we poll the room here."],[1114.73,1121.36,"Think about Nordvia's P2, IT consulting: twelve firms, thirty eight call-offs, no framework."],[1121.36,1123.49,"Which first event would you run?"],[1123.49,1126.06,"An e-auction on day rates?"],[1126.06,1129.38,"A framework RFP with a rate card, then mini-competitions?"],[1129.38,1131.55,"An RFQ for the cheapest firm?"],[1131.83,1134.49,"Or a negotiation with Brightline only?"],[1134.49,1136.8,"Make your choice before you read on."],[1136.8,1141.09,"The expected answer is the framework RFP with a rate card."],[1141.09,1145.39,"An auction on day rates would buy cheap profiles, not capability."],[1146.34,1147.81,"Now to the timeline."],[1147.81,1152.46,"This table gives indicative durations, and the footnote is important."],[1152.46,1155.14,"These are rules of thumb from practice, not norms."],[1155.14,1159.52,"Replace them with your own team's data as soon as you have it."],[1160.03,1163.95,"An RFI response window is typically two to three weeks."],[1163.95,1167.79,"An RFP response window for services, three to six weeks."],[1167.79,1176.63,"A simple mini-competition, at least ten working days, which is the minimum in the UK Crown Commercial Service consultancy framework."],[1176.63,1180.83,"That is the only figure on this slide with a documented reference."],[1181.11,1184.11,"Evaluation and consensus, two to four weeks."],[1184.11,1187.37,"Negotiation, BAFO and approvals, two to six weeks."],[1187.37,1192.64,"And mobilisation for a labour intensive service, often around three months."],[1192.64,1197.04,"Everything except the ten working days is illustrative."],[1197.04,1198.39,"Look at the total, though."],[1198.67,1207.89,"Add the middle of each range and a services tender easily takes five to seven months from issue to start, before you even count the time to prepare the pack."],[1208.83,1216.17,"Which brings us to the most practical habit in this module: plan backwards from the notice deadline."],[1216.17,1217.2,"Read the flow from left to right."],[1217.2,1219.62,"You start at the notice deadline."],[1219.62,1222.28,"Before it, the contract must be signed."],[1222.28,1223.97,"Before that, the award approved."],[1223.97,1226.38,"Before that, evaluation and negotiation."],[1226.38,1228.5,"Before that, bids received."],[1228.78,1230.9,"Before that, the RFx issued."],[1230.9,1233.6,"And before that, the pack and the weights agreed."],[1234.1,1235.23,"Take Kelvar."],[1235.23,1240.34,"The licence renews in June 2027, with nine months' notice."],[1240.34,1246.38,"So the real deadline is nine months before the expiry, around September 2026, not June 2027."],[1246.38,1257.05,"If you have not secured a better deal or given notice by then, the auto-renewal takes effect and your leverage is gone for another cycle."],[1257.56,1261.06,"Most urgent events are missed notice dates in disguise."],[1261.06,1263.2,"Nobody planned to be in a hurry."],[1263.2,1265.58,"They simply counted from the expiry."],[1265.58,1272.64,"So, a small action for you now: pause the video and write down the notice date of your own project."],[1272.64,1273.38,"Not the expiry."],[1273.38,1274.54,"The notice date."],[1275.49,1277.62,"What goes into every RFx pack?"],[1277.62,1278.67,"Six blocks."],[1279.17,1285.89,"First, instructions: the timetable, the rules, the Q and A channel, and how bids will be evaluated."],[1285.89,1291.06,"Second, the specification: input, output or hybrid, with volumes and sites."],[1291.06,1298.97,"Third, the pricing schedule: one template for everyone, with recurring prices, one-off costs and unit rates kept separate."],[1299.25,1305.62,"Fourth, the questionnaire, with knock-out questions first and the scored questions after them."],[1305.62,1310.21,"Fifth, the draft contract, with service levels, KPIs and service credits."],[1310.49,1328.54,"And sixth, the regulatory annexes: the DORA Article 30 clauses for ICT services, the Solvency II Article 274 terms where you outsource a critical or important function, data protection terms, and staff data where people may transfer."],[1329.04,1335.03,"In practice, the two blocks most often missing are the draft contract and the regulatory annex."],[1335.03,1337.36,"Both are painful to add later."],[1337.36,1343.93,"A supplier who has already won is not in a hurry to accept audit and access rights it never priced."],[1344.88,1347.43,"So, one instruction worth its own slide."],[1347.43,1349.19,"Attach the draft contract."],[1349.69,1353.49,"With the contract in the pack, suppliers bid on your terms."],[1353.49,1361.63,"Each bidder must say which clauses they accept and which they want to change, and those deviations become part of the evaluation."],[1361.63,1366.07,"That is negotiation through the bids, while you still have competition."],[1366.35,1373.65,"Without the contract, you negotiate the terms after award, when the winner knows it has won and your leverage has gone."],[1373.65,1381.99,"Picture a security services tender where liability caps, service credits and the exit clause are only discussed after the award."],[1381.99,1385.26,"Every point becomes a concession you have to buy back."],[1385.54,1389.42,"Attach the contract, and those points are priced into the bids instead."],[1390.37,1391.82,"Time to apply this."],[1391.82,1395.36,"Practice 3.1 in your workbook takes about fifteen minutes."],[1395.36,1398.78,"State your route to market from your strategy paper."],[1398.78,1401.68,"Write the notice deadline, not the expiry."],[1401.68,1404.49,"List the stages and the tool for each one."],[1404.49,1408.08,"Plan the start and the duration of each stage backwards."],[1408.08,1410.72,"And tick what your RFx pack will contain."],[1411.0,1416.45,"The most common error is a sequence that starts from today rather than from the notice deadline."],[1416.45,1421.6,"Pause the video now and do it in the Modules tab if you want, then continue."],[1422.55,1424.79,"Let us debrief with four questions."],[1424.79,1429.71,"Whose sequence starts later than it should, and what will you do about it?"],[1429.71,1432.94,"Be honest: that is the most valuable finding of the exercise."],[1432.94,1436.68,"Who removed an RFI, and who added one, and why?"],[1436.68,1440.85,"Who has a sole source project, and what exactly is your RFI for?"],[1441.13,1444.42,"And what will your draft contract need that it does not have today?"],[1444.42,1450.67,"In the classroom we put two timelines on screen, and we praise the backward plan, not the long one."],[1451.62,1454.51,"Here is module 3.1 in three lines."],[1454.51,1460.02,"First, choose the question, then the tool: RFI, RFP, RFQ, BAFO or auction."],[1460.02,1467.89,"Second, the sequence follows the route to market, and a sole source gets a negotiation, not an RFP for show."],[1467.89,1473.76,"Third, plan backwards from the notice deadline, and attach the draft contract."],[1474.27,1478.23,"Try saying those three lines in your own words before you move on."],[1478.23,1480.38,"Then think about what comes next."],[1480.38,1483.31,"You now know which events you will run and when."],[1483.31,1486.49,"But an RFP is only as good as what it asks for."],[1486.77,1498.88,"In the next lesson, module 3.2, we design the request itself: the specification, the lots, the pricing schedule, and the evaluation model that you must fix before anything goes out."],[1498.88,1500.0,"See you there."]]},{"id":"s3-l2","session":"s3","title":"Designing the request and the evaluation model","summary":"Design a request that makes bids comparable and build a weighted evaluation model, with a tested price formula, fixed before issue.","from":27,"to":45,"audio":"audio/s3-l2.mp3","duration":1431.5,"segs":[[27,0.6,17.67,"Welcome back. This is the second video lesson of Session 3, and it covers module 3.2, designing the request. In the last lesson you chose your event sequence and planned it backwards from the notice deadline. Now we decide what goes into the request itself."],[27,18.17,57.17,"Here is what we will cover. First, the specification: input, output or a hybrid of both. Then lots, and why you should design them the way you will award. Then the pricing schedule and total cost of ownership. Then the difference between knock-out criteria and weighted criteria. And then the heart of the module: building the evaluation model, with a scoring scale, weights and a price formula. We will finish with a worked example that shows how the choice of formula alone can change the winner. The subtitle on the slide says it all: the request decides what you can compare, and therefore what you can award."],[28,58.12,72.84,"Decisions made early lock in most of the cost and risk. This is one of the central lessons of Arjan van Weele's purchasing process model, in the eighth edition of Procurement and Supply Chain Management, written with Frank Rozemeijer."],[28,73.34,111.83,"In van Weele's model, the tactical part of purchasing runs in three steps: determine the specification, select the supplier, and contract. The specification comes first, and it weighs most. Why? Because everything downstream depends on it. If the specification asks for the wrong thing, the evaluation will faithfully select the best supplier of the wrong thing. If it is vague, suppliers price different interpretations, and your evaluation compares apples with pears. And no scoring model, however careful, can repair that later. Mistakes in the specification cannot be fixed in the evaluation."],[28,112.33,132.81,"Think of a marketing team that asks for an events agency able to handle up to two hundred events a year, when it actually runs about forty. Every bidder prices capacity for two hundred. The evaluation is perfectly fair, and the result is perfectly expensive. The problem was decided on day one, in the specification."],[29,133.76,137.26,"So the first specification choice: input or output?"],[29,137.76,170.25,"Look at the left-hand column, input specifications. You prescribe the inputs: frequencies, hours, headcount, methods. Clean the offices every evening, five people, four hours each. The advantages are real. Input specifications are easy to audit and easy to compare, because every bidder prices the same thing. But they stifle innovation, because the supplier cannot propose a better method. And they often produce higher bids, because you pay for the hours you prescribed, whether or not the work needs them."],[29,170.75,200.0,"Now the right-hand column, output specifications. You describe results: clean, safe, available, on time. The meeting rooms are clean and ready by eight in the morning. The supplier proposes the method, which may well be cheaper. Perhaps robot floor care at night, perhaps a different shift pattern. But output specifications are harder to draft and harder to assess, and they need inspection and KPIs, because you can only manage a result you measure."],[29,200.5,243.15,"In practice the usual answer is a hybrid. Guidance on facility management contracts, for example from the law firm CMS in 2000, makes the point that both have merits. You specify outputs for most areas, and you prescribe methods where the insurer needs control: security procedures, access to secure areas, data handling, the clean desk policy in claims. Singapore's outcome-based cleaning guide is a good public example of how to specify results and back them with inspection templates. For an insurer, that hybrid logic is natural. You do not need to control how the carpet is cleaned. You do need to control who enters the server room."],[30,244.1,248.71,"Next, lots. The title gives the rule: design them the way you will award."],[30,249.21,265.17,"Start with the Coupa rule. Items in a lot are always awarded together and ranked together. So a lot is not a formatting decision. It is a commercial decision. If you put three sites into one lot, you are saying in advance that one supplier will win all three."],[30,265.67,305.78,"Then the main options. Lots by site or by country let local suppliers bid, and they spread mobilisation risk: if one transition goes badly, the others are not affected. Lots by service separate, for example, cleaning from security, or combine them into one soft facility management lot. And bundles let suppliers price combinations. A supplier may be willing to give a discount if it wins two or three lots together. You can capture that with a bundle discount in the pricing schedule, or, for larger and more complex awards, with Coupa Sourcing Optimization, which lets you compare package bids across many lots."],[30,306.28,312.25,"The test is simple. If you would never award it to a single supplier, it should not be a single lot."],[30,312.75,348.15,"Lots work the same way outside facilities. For an IT services framework, you might create one lot for application development, one for infrastructure support and one for project management, because different firms are strong in each, and you intend to award each to a different group. For a marketing panel, you might separate creative work from media buying, because combining them in one lot would favour full-service networks and exclude specialists who may be better at one of them. In every case, ask yourself first how you intend to award, and then draw the lots to match."],[31,349.1,356.54,"Here is how Nordvia applied this to P1, office cleaning. It is a worked example, and the countries are anonymised."],[31,357.04,386.55,"Lot 1 is the headquarters, the site with the quality complaints. Lot 2 is the second site in Country 1. Lot 3 is the site in Country 2. Why separate lots? Because the headquarters has a specific quality problem that deserves its own focus, and because the site in Country 2 has a different labour market, different collective wage rules, and probably different local suppliers. Bidders may bid for one, two or all three lots, with an optional bundle discount."],[31,387.05,413.04,"And the award rule is published in advance: best score per lot, and bundles accepted only if they are cheaper in total. That last part is important. Without it, a large supplier could win all three lots with a bundle that looks attractive but costs more than the best combination of individual winners. With three sites you can check that in a spreadsheet. With thirty, that is where Sourcing Optimization earns its place."],[32,413.99,420.0,"Now the pricing schedule. Its single purpose is to make bids comparable. Four design rules."],[32,420.5,438.42,"One template. You fill in your volumes, sites and assumptions, and everyone prices the same thing. Square metres, opening hours, number of desks, expected extra cleans. If each bidder makes its own assumptions, you are no longer comparing prices, you are comparing assumptions."],[32,438.92,458.96,"Three blocks. The recurring price, for example the monthly fee. One-off costs, such as mobilisation and transition. And unit rates for extras, such as an extra deep clean after an event. Keeping them separate stops a bidder hiding a high transition cost inside a low monthly fee, or the reverse."],[32,459.46,477.63,"Indexation. The mechanism is part of the bid. For cleaning, that is usually a wage index linked to the collective agreement. Remember Nordvia's security contract, where hourly rates were indexed twice in two years with no clear rule. Ask for the mechanism in the bid, and evaluate it."],[32,478.13,492.19,"And no free text. Prices go in cells, conditions go in the questionnaire. The test on this slide is a good one to remember: if two bids cannot be put in the same spreadsheet without your interpretation, the schedule failed."],[32,492.69,529.03,"Here is what that looks like for the Nordvia cleaning tender. Nordvia fills in the floor area per site, the opening hours, the number of workstations and an estimate of twenty extra cleans a year. Each bidder then gives a monthly fee per lot, a one-off mobilisation cost, unit rates for an extra clean and a window clean, and the index it proposes, linked to the collective cleaning wage. Because every number sits in the same cell for every bidder, the procurement lead can compare the four bids in minutes, and nobody has to guess what a phrase like price subject to site survey means."],[33,529.98,542.46,"This leads naturally to total cost of ownership, using the framework of Lisa Ellram. Her point is that the price on the invoice is only part of the cost. She groups costs into three stages."],[33,542.96,574.3,"Pre-transaction costs come before you buy. In indirect procurement that means tender effort, due diligence, and onboarding the new supplier in Coupa Supplier Information Management. Transaction costs are what most people think of as the price: the annual price, the indexation, the unit rates for extras. And post-transaction costs come after the award: mobilisation, staff transfer, the full-time equivalent you need to manage the contract, and at the end, exit and migration."],[33,574.8,625.23,"Why does this matter in evaluation? Because two bids with the same annual price can have very different total costs. For a cleaning switch, transition cost is real: new staff, new training, perhaps a dip in quality for a few months. For software as a service, exit and migration are often the largest hidden item, the cost of getting your data out and moving to the next provider. Let me give an illustrative example. Suppose two SaaS bids are both about 400 thousand euros a year over four years. One allows data export in a standard format at no charge. The other charges for export and uses a proprietary format. On the first year's price, they are equal. Over the term, including exit, they are not. Evaluate on the total over the term, not on year one."],[34,626.18,633.25,"Next, a distinction that many evaluation models get wrong: knock-out criteria versus weighted criteria."],[34,633.75,664.02,"On the left, knock-out criteria. These are pass or fail. Licences and vetting for a security provider. A collective wage compliance declaration for cleaning. ISO 27001 or an equivalent for an ICT supplier. Acceptance of audit and access rights, as required by DORA Article 30 and Solvency II Article 274. And financial standing. If a bidder fails one of these, it is out, whatever its price."],[34,664.52,683.12,"On the right, highlighted, are the weighted criteria. These are the ones that differentiate between compliant bidders: the service plan and staffing model, KPIs, reporting and quality control, transition and mobilisation, ESG and innovation, and price or total cost of ownership."],[34,683.62,711.91,"The rule is in the speaker's note: regulatory requirements are gates, not points. Imagine you score audit rights as a weighted criterion worth five percent. A bidder refuses supervisor access, loses five points, and wins anyway on price. Now the insurer has a contract that does not meet its regulatory obligations. A bidder who refuses supervisor access cannot be allowed to win on price. That is why it must be a gate."],[35,712.86,719.29,"Now we build the evaluation model. These six steps are in the order of work, so follow them in sequence."],[35,719.79,734.87,"Step one, criteria. They come from the specification and from the objectives in your strategy paper. If the strategy says the main problem is quality at the headquarters, quality control and KPIs should be visible criteria."],[35,735.37,751.79,"Step two, weights. Decide quality versus price first, then split the quality weight into sub-criteria. Be explicit about whether sub-criterion weights are shares of one hundred or shares of the quality weight. Either works, as long as everyone knows which."],[35,752.29,757.86,"Step three, the scale. Zero to five, with written descriptors, which we look at in a moment."],[35,758.36,772.95,"Step four, evidence. For each criterion, what will bidders have to show? A staffing plan, a sample report, a reference site, a mobilisation plan with named people. If you do not ask for evidence, evaluators score promises."],[35,773.45,779.11,"Step five, the formula: how price becomes a score. We will see that this choice is not neutral."],[35,779.61,803.91,"And step six, fix and disclose. The model is fixed before issue and disclosed in the Coupa event. This is the single most important governance rule of the session. Weights set after the bids arrive are an equal-treatment and governance failure. If you can see the bids when you choose the weights, you are no longer choosing the best bid. You are choosing the weights that make your preferred bid win."],[36,804.86,829.99,"In the live session we pause for a poll. Think about your last sourcing event. When were the evaluation weights fixed? Before issue, and disclosed to bidders? Before issue, but not disclosed? After the bids arrived? Were there no weights at all? Or do you not know? Answer honestly for yourself. If your answer is anything other than the first option, ask yourself why, and what it would take to change it in your next event."],[36,830.49,848.86,"Why does this matter so much? Because the moment the weights are set after opening, every losing bidder has a legitimate question: did you choose the best bid, or did you choose the weights that suited the bid you already preferred? You will not be able to answer that question convincingly, however good your intentions were."],[37,849.81,871.73,"Here is a scoring scale with descriptors. Five is excellent: fully meets the requirement with evidence, and adds value beyond it. Four is good: fully meets it, with evidence. Three is acceptable: meets it, with minor gaps. Two is weak: significant gaps. One is poor: barely addresses it. And zero means not met, or no answer."],[37,872.23,912.81,"Why bother with descriptors? Because they make individual scores comparable before the consensus meeting. Without them, one evaluator's three is another evaluator's four, and the consensus meeting becomes a negotiation between moods. With them, the discussion becomes: does this answer show evidence, yes or no? Does it add value beyond the requirement? Notice the difference between four and five. Five is not for a very good answer. It is for an answer that fully meets the requirement and adds something you did not ask for, like a cleaning supplier proposing a sensor-based system that cleans meeting rooms only after they have been used."],[37,913.31,947.57,"Let me show you how this works in a consensus meeting. Three evaluators score the transition plan of a cleaning bidder. Two give a four, one gives a two. With descriptors, the conversation is concrete. The evaluator who gave a two points out that the plan has no named transition manager and no timeline for staff transfer, which is a significant gap. The others look again, agree that the evidence is missing, and the team settles on a three, with that rationale written down. Without descriptors, they would simply have averaged their opinions."],[38,948.52,955.12,"How should you set the weights between quality and price? There is no universal answer, but there are reference points."],[38,955.62,994.19,"In the UK Crown Commercial Service consultancy framework, further competitions must give quality at least 50 percent, in a range from 50 to 90 percent. A 2024 UK university cleaning tender was awarded on a 50 50 split between quality and price. Those two are documented public references. For quality-led services, common practice puts quality at 50 to 70 percent and price at 30 to 50. Note the word practice: that is a range many teams use, not a norm. And for a well specified commodity, price, or a total cost formula, decides."],[38,994.69,1013.29,"The point is that the weight should reflect the category and the strategy. For Nordvia's consulting framework, where the problem is wildly different capability for the same day rate, quality should weigh heavily. For office supplies with a fixed item list, quality differences are small, and price should decide."],[39,1014.24,1039.5,"In the live session we run another poll here. Which price scoring formula does your team use today? Lowest price divided by bid price times one hundred? A linear deviation from the lowest? Ranking points, for first, second and third? No formula, just judgement? Or does it change every time? Think about your answer. Most teams use the first formula without knowing its side effect. The next slides show it."],[40,1040.45,1044.02,"Here are two common price scoring formulas side by side."],[40,1044.52,1077.76,"On the left, the relative formula. The price score equals the lowest price divided by the bid price, times one hundred. The lowest bid gets one hundred, and a bid twice as expensive gets fifty. It is simple and common. But look at the last two lines. Every score moves when the lowest bid changes. And one very low bid compresses the others. If one bidder comes in far below the rest, everyone else's price score drops, and the gaps between them shrink. The whole contest gets reshaped by a single bid."],[40,1078.26,1104.9,"On the right, the linear deviation formula. The price score equals one hundred times one minus the difference between the bid and the lowest, divided by the lowest, with a floor at zero. In words: you lose one point for every percent you are above the lowest bid. A bid 20 percent above the lowest scores eighty. Compared with the relative formula, this one punishes price gaps harder, and so favours cheaper bids more strongly."],[40,1105.4,1112.0,"Neither formula is right or wrong. What is wrong is not knowing what your formula does before you publish it."],[41,1112.95,1125.66,"This worked example shows why. It uses the fictional Nordvia headquarters cleaning bids that we evaluate in detail in module 3.3. There are four bidders, A to D, weighted 50 50."],[41,1126.16,1151.38,"With the relative formula and all bids in, the ranking is D with 80.2, B with 79.6 and C with 79.0. With the linear formula, same bids, same weights, the ranking flips: C with 79.0, B with 78.3 and D with 77.5. Same bids, same weights, a different winner. Nothing about the quality or the prices changed. Only the formula."],[41,1151.88,1173.08,"Now remove Bidder C, which, as we will see in the next lesson, may be an abnormally low bid. With the relative formula, D scores 87.1 and B 87.0. With the linear formula, B scores 87.0 and D 86.8. Practically a tie either way, with the order depending on the formula."],[41,1173.58,1200.3,"So the lesson is: test the formula on fictional bid sets before issue. Run three invented sets through your model. A normal one, one with a very low outlier, and one with two bids very close together. If the ranking flips with the formula, or with a small change of weights, then the model is deciding, not the bids. Better to find that out on a Tuesday afternoon with invented numbers than in front of a losing bidder's lawyer."],[42,1201.25,1210.1,"Let us gather the common design mistakes. Each one is a reason an award can be challenged by a losing bidder or questioned by Internal Audit."],[42,1210.6,1224.76,"The gold-plated specification: asking for more than the business needs, and paying for it. The events agency example from earlier is one. Twenty four by seven support for a system used in office hours is another."],[42,1225.26,1233.16,"Weights after opening: changing the model to fit the preferred bidder. We have said it already, and we will say it again in the next lesson."],[42,1233.66,1244.73,"First-year price only: ignoring indexation, mobilisation and exit. A low first year with an aggressive index can be the most expensive bid over five years."],[42,1245.23,1251.42,"And regulation as points: scoring audit rights instead of requiring them. Gates, not points."],[42,1251.92,1271.37,"Notice that all four mistakes share a root cause. They happen when the design is rushed, or when it is done after people have already formed a view about who should win. That is why the order of work matters: specification, lots, pricing schedule, criteria, weights, formula, test, and only then issue."],[43,1272.32,1307.76,"Now it is your turn. Practice 3.2 in your workbook takes about twenty minutes. Choose the specification type and the lots for your project. Set the split between quality and price, then the quality sub-criteria. Write the knock-out criteria, including the regulatory gates. Choose the price formula. And list the total cost elements beyond the annual price. One tip: state whether your sub-criterion weights are shares of one hundred or shares of the quality weight. Pause the video now and work in the Modules tab if you want, then continue."],[44,1308.71,1344.4,"Let us debrief. Who has a knock-out criterion that is really a weighted one, or the reverse? A common example is ESG: a code of conduct signature is a gate, but the quality of a carbon reduction plan is a weighted criterion. Whose quality weight is below 50 percent for a service? Why? Which total cost element would change your ranking? And the last question: would your business owner sign these weights today? That is the bridge to Fieldwork 3. The weights must be validated with the business before issue, because after issue they cannot change."],[44,1344.9,1359.79,"If you are watching alone, take two of these questions and answer them in writing in your workbook. The question about the business owner is the one I would choose first. If you hesitate, find out now what they would change, because next week it will be too late."],[45,1360.74,1379.47,"Here is module 3.2 in three lines. First, the specification and the lots decide what you can compare and what you can award. Second, knock-outs for gates, weights for differences, and total cost over the term. Third, fix, test and disclose the model and the formula before issue."],[45,1379.97,1413.73,"Let me recap the journey of this lesson. We started with van Weele: early decisions lock in cost and risk. We chose between input, output and hybrid specifications. We designed lots the way we will award, and a pricing schedule that puts every bid into the same spreadsheet. We used Ellram's total cost of ownership to look beyond the first-year price. We separated gates from weighted criteria. We built the model in six steps, with descriptors and reference weights. And we saw that the formula alone can change the winner."],[45,1414.23,1430.29,"In the next lesson, module 3.3, we use a model on real looking bids. You will meet the four Nordvia cleaning bids in full, deal with one that looks abnormally low, test the sensitivity of the result, and decide what to recommend. See you there."]],"questions":[{"after":29,"q":"Nordvia's claims department wants a cleaning specification for its open-plan floors and a secure archive room. Which specification approach fits best?","options":["Input only: prescribe hours and headcount everywhere","Output only: describe results everywhere and let the supplier decide all methods","A hybrid: outputs for the open-plan floors, prescribed methods and access rules for the secure archive","No specification: let suppliers propose freely"],"answer":2,"explain":"A hybrid lets suppliers innovate where results are what matter, while the insurer keeps control where it needs it, such as secure areas and data handling.","t":243.15},{"after":31,"q":"A large cleaner offers a bundle for all three Nordvia sites. When should the bundle be accepted under the published rule?","options":["Only if it is cheaper in total than the best winners per lot","Always, because one supplier is easier to manage","Only if the incumbent does not bid","Never: bundles break equal treatment"],"answer":0,"explain":"The award rule was published as best score per lot, with bundles accepted only if cheaper in total. That protects the buyer from an attractive-looking bundle that costs more than the best combination.","t":413.04},{"after":33,"q":"Two SaaS bids for an HR tool have the same annual price. One charges for data export in a proprietary format at exit. What should the evaluation do?","options":["Treat them as equal, since the annual price is the same","Ignore exit costs because exit may never happen","Exclude the second bidder automatically","Include exit and migration costs in a TCO over the term"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ellram's TCO includes post-transaction costs such as exit and migration, often the largest hidden item for SaaS. Evaluating over the term makes the difference visible.","t":625.23},{"after":34,"q":"An ICT bidder refuses to accept audit and access rights for the supervisor. In a well designed model, what happens?","options":["It loses a few points on a weighted criterion","It can still win if its price is low enough","It fails a knock-out criterion and cannot be awarded","The weights are adjusted to compensate"],"answer":2,"explain":"Regulatory requirements such as DORA Article 30 and Solvency II Article 274 rights are gates, not points. A bidder who refuses them cannot win on price.","t":711.91},{"after":35,"q":"After the bids arrive, a stakeholder asks to raise the quality weight from 50 to 60 percent because the favourite supplier would then win. What do you do?","options":["Agree, because quality matters","Agree only if Internal Audit is informed","Refuse: the weights were fixed and disclosed before issue and stay as published","Run both versions and pick the one the business prefers"],"answer":2,"explain":"Weights set or changed after bids arrive are an equal-treatment and governance failure. The model is fixed and disclosed before issue, and it stays.","t":803.91},{"after":38,"q":"Which weighting is easiest to defend for Nordvia's IT consulting framework, where the same senior day rate buys very different capability?","options":["Quality around 60 to 70, price 30 to 40","Quality 20, price 80","Price only, lowest compliant wins","No weights, judgement after seeing the bids"],"answer":0,"explain":"Quality-led services commonly sit at 50 to 70 percent quality, and the CCS consultancy reference requires at least 50. Where capability differs widely, quality should weigh heavily.","t":1013.29},{"after":40,"q":"Using the relative formula, a new bidder enters far below everyone else. What happens to the other bidders' price scores?","options":["Nothing changes for them","They all fall and the gaps between them shrink","They all rise","Only the second-lowest bidder is affected"],"answer":1,"explain":"In the relative formula every score depends on the lowest bid. One very low bid lowers and compresses all the others, which can reorder the contest.","t":1112.0},{"after":41,"q":"With the Nordvia bids at 50 50, the relative formula ranks D first and the linear formula ranks C first. What is the right lesson?","options":["Always use the linear formula","Always use the relative formula","Choose the formula after seeing the bids","Test the formula on fictional bid sets before issue and know its effect"],"answer":3,"explain":"Neither formula is right or wrong, but they can produce different winners. Testing the model on invented bid sets before issue shows whether the formula, rather than the bids, would decide.","t":1200.3},{"after":45,"q":"Which pair best describes how to treat a collective wage compliance declaration and a cleaning service plan?","options":["Both weighted","The declaration is a knock-out gate; the service plan is a weighted criterion","Both knock-out","The service plan is a knock-out; the declaration is weighted"],"answer":1,"explain":"Compliance requirements are pass or fail gates. Differentiating features like the service plan are weighted so that compliant bidders can be ranked.","t":1430.29}],"cues":[[0.6,1.41,"Welcome back."],[1.41,7.93,"This is the second video lesson of Session 3, and it covers module 3.2, designing the request."],[7.93,14.34,"In the last lesson you chose your event sequence and planned it backwards from the notice deadline."],[14.34,17.67,"Now we decide what goes into the request itself."],[18.17,19.91,"Here is what we will cover."],[19.91,24.45,"First, the specification: input, output or a hybrid of both."],[24.45,28.81,"Then lots, and why you should design them the way you will award."],[28.81,32.07,"Then the pricing schedule and total cost of ownership."],[32.07,36.79,"Then the difference between knock-out criteria and weighted criteria."],[37.07,44.33,"And then the heart of the module: building the evaluation model, with a scoring scale, weights and a price formula."],[44.33,50.09,"We will finish with a worked example that shows how the choice of formula alone can change the winner."],[50.37,57.17,"The subtitle on the slide says it all: the request decides what you can compare, and therefore what you can award."],[58.12,61.57,"Decisions made early lock in most of the cost and risk."],[61.57,72.84,"This is one of the central lessons of Arjan van Weele's purchasing process model, in the eighth edition of Procurement and Supply Chain Management, written with Frank Rozemeijer."],[73.34,82.08,"In van Weele's model, the tactical part of purchasing runs in three steps: determine the specification, select the supplier, and contract."],[82.08,85.43,"The specification comes first, and it weighs most."],[85.43,86.3,"Why?"],[86.3,88.72,"Because everything downstream depends on it."],[89.0,96.14,"If the specification asks for the wrong thing, the evaluation will faithfully select the best supplier of the wrong thing."],[96.14,103.44,"If it is vague, suppliers price different interpretations, and your evaluation compares apples with pears."],[103.44,107.37,"And no scoring model, however careful, can repair that later."],[107.65,111.83,"Mistakes in the specification cannot be fixed in the evaluation."],[112.33,121.46,"Think of a marketing team that asks for an events agency able to handle up to two hundred events a year, when it actually runs about forty."],[121.46,123.67,"Every bidder prices capacity for two hundred."],[123.67,128.96,"The evaluation is perfectly fair, and the result is perfectly expensive."],[128.96,132.81,"The problem was decided on day one, in the specification."],[133.76,137.26,"So the first specification choice: input or output?"],[137.76,140.89,"Look at the left-hand column, input specifications."],[140.89,145.72,"You prescribe the inputs: frequencies, hours, headcount, methods."],[145.72,149.57,"Clean the offices every evening, five people, four hours each."],[149.57,151.86,"The advantages are real."],[151.86,158.15,"Input specifications are easy to audit and easy to compare, because every bidder prices the same thing."],[158.43,163.32,"But they stifle innovation, because the supplier cannot propose a better method."],[163.32,170.25,"And they often produce higher bids, because you pay for the hours you prescribed, whether or not the work needs them."],[170.75,174.19,"Now the right-hand column, output specifications."],[174.19,176.96,"You describe results: clean, safe, available, on time."],[176.96,181.12,"The meeting rooms are clean and ready by eight in the morning."],[181.12,185.34,"The supplier proposes the method, which may well be cheaper."],[185.34,189.91,"Perhaps robot floor care at night, perhaps a different shift pattern."],[190.19,200.0,"But output specifications are harder to draft and harder to assess, and they need inspection and KPIs, because you can only manage a result you measure."],[200.5,203.4,"In practice the usual answer is a hybrid."],[203.4,212.08,"Guidance on facility management contracts, for example from the law firm CMS in 2000, makes the point that both have merits."],[212.36,225.28,"You specify outputs for most areas, and you prescribe methods where the insurer needs control: security procedures, access to secure areas, data handling, the clean desk policy in claims."],[225.28,233.55,"Singapore's outcome-based cleaning guide is a good public example of how to specify results and back them with inspection templates."],[233.83,236.2,"For an insurer, that hybrid logic is natural."],[236.2,240.21,"You do not need to control how the carpet is cleaned."],[240.21,243.15,"You do need to control who enters the server room."],[244.1,244.94,"Next, lots."],[244.94,248.71,"The title gives the rule: design them the way you will award."],[249.21,250.78,"Start with the Coupa rule."],[250.78,254.58,"Items in a lot are always awarded together and ranked together."],[254.58,257.39,"So a lot is not a formatting decision."],[257.39,259.36,"It is a commercial decision."],[259.36,265.17,"If you put three sites into one lot, you are saying in advance that one supplier will win all three."],[265.67,267.16,"Then the main options."],[267.16,277.16,"Lots by site or by country let local suppliers bid, and they spread mobilisation risk: if one transition goes badly, the others are not affected."],[277.16,285.02,"Lots by service separate, for example, cleaning from security, or combine them into one soft facility management lot."],[285.3,288.35,"And bundles let suppliers price combinations."],[288.35,293.15,"A supplier may be willing to give a discount if it wins two or three lots together."],[293.43,305.78,"You can capture that with a bundle discount in the pricing schedule, or, for larger and more complex awards, with Coupa Sourcing Optimization, which lets you compare package bids across many lots."],[306.28,307.75,"The test is simple."],[307.75,312.25,"If you would never award it to a single supplier, it should not be a single lot."],[312.75,315.67,"Lots work the same way outside facilities."],[315.67,330.39,"For an IT services framework, you might create one lot for application development, one for infrastructure support and one for project management, because different firms are strong in each, and you intend to award each to a different group."],[330.67,342.1,"For a marketing panel, you might separate creative work from media buying, because combining them in one lot would favour full-service networks and exclude specialists who may be better at one of them."],[342.1,348.14,"In every case, ask yourself first how you intend to award, and then draw the lots to match."],[349.1,353.08,"Here is how Nordvia applied this to P1, office cleaning."],[353.08,356.55,"It is a worked example, and the countries are anonymised."],[357.04,361.44,"Lot 1 is the headquarters, the site with the quality complaints."],[361.44,364.29,"Lot 2 is the second site in Country 1."],[364.29,366.33,"Lot 3 is the site in Country 2."],[366.33,367.71,"Why separate lots?"],[367.99,380.88,"Because the headquarters has a specific quality problem that deserves its own focus, and because the site in Country 2 has a different labour market, different collective wage rules, and probably different local suppliers."],[380.88,386.55,"Bidders may bid for one, two or all three lots, with an optional bundle discount."],[387.05,394.69,"And the award rule is published in advance: best score per lot, and bundles accepted only if they are cheaper in total."],[394.69,395.86,"That last part is important."],[395.86,406.1,"Without it, a large supplier could win all three lots with a bundle that looks attractive but costs more than the best combination of individual winners."],[406.38,409.47,"With three sites you can check that in a spreadsheet."],[409.47,413.04,"With thirty, that is where Sourcing Optimization earns its place."],[413.99,415.65,"Now the pricing schedule."],[415.65,418.53,"Its single purpose is to make bids comparable."],[418.53,420.01,"Four design rules."],[420.5,421.49,"One template."],[421.49,426.75,"You fill in your volumes, sites and assumptions, and everyone prices the same thing."],[426.75,431.19,"Square metres, opening hours, number of desks, expected extra cleans."],[431.19,438.42,"If each bidder makes its own assumptions, you are no longer comparing prices, you are comparing assumptions."],[438.92,439.99,"Three blocks."],[439.99,443.02,"The recurring price, for example the monthly fee."],[443.02,446.88,"One-off costs, such as mobilisation and transition."],[446.88,451.6,"And unit rates for extras, such as an extra deep clean after an event."],[451.6,458.95,"Keeping them separate stops a bidder hiding a high transition cost inside a low monthly fee, or the reverse."],[459.46,460.5,"Indexation."],[460.5,462.67,"The mechanism is part of the bid."],[462.67,467.5,"For cleaning, that is usually a wage index linked to the collective agreement."],[467.5,474.4,"Remember Nordvia's security contract, where hourly rates were indexed twice in two years with no clear rule."],[474.4,477.64,"Ask for the mechanism in the bid, and evaluate it."],[478.13,479.04,"And no free text."],[479.04,482.37,"Prices go in cells, conditions go in the questionnaire."],[482.37,492.19,"The test on this slide is a good one to remember: if two bids cannot be put in the same spreadsheet without your interpretation, the schedule failed."],[492.69,496.01,"Here is what that looks like for the Nordvia cleaning tender."],[496.01,504.89,"Nordvia fills in the floor area per site, the opening hours, the number of workstations and an estimate of twenty extra cleans a year."],[505.17,517.27,"Each bidder then gives a monthly fee per lot, a one-off mobilisation cost, unit rates for an extra clean and a window clean, and the index it proposes, linked to the collective cleaning wage."],[517.55,529.03,"Because every number sits in the same cell for every bidder, the procurement lead can compare the four bids in minutes, and nobody has to guess what a phrase like price subject to site survey means."],[529.98,535.47,"This leads naturally to total cost of ownership, using the framework of Lisa Ellram."],[535.47,539.97,"Her point is that the price on the invoice is only part of the cost."],[539.97,542.46,"She groups costs into three stages."],[542.96,545.67,"Pre-transaction costs come before you buy."],[545.67,554.43,"In indirect procurement that means tender effort, due diligence, and onboarding the new supplier in Coupa Supplier Information Management."],[554.43,562.57,"Transaction costs are what most people think of as the price: the annual price, the indexation, the unit rates for extras."],[562.85,574.3,"And post-transaction costs come after the award: mobilisation, staff transfer, the full-time equivalent you need to manage the contract, and at the end, exit and migration."],[574.8,576.9,"Why does this matter in evaluation?"],[576.9,582.27,"Because two bids with the same annual price can have very different total costs."],[582.27,589.63,"For a cleaning switch, transition cost is real: new staff, new training, perhaps a dip in quality for a few months."],[589.91,600.21,"For software as a service, exit and migration are often the largest hidden item, the cost of getting your data out and moving to the next provider."],[600.21,602.55,"Let me give an illustrative example."],[602.55,607.85,"Suppose two SaaS bids are both about 400 thousand euros a year over four years."],[608.13,612.05,"One allows data export in a standard format at no charge."],[612.05,615.73,"The other charges for export and uses a proprietary format."],[615.73,618.72,"On the first year's price, they are equal."],[618.72,621.54,"Over the term, including exit, they are not."],[621.54,625.24,"Evaluate on the total over the term, not on year one."],[626.18,633.24,"Next, a distinction that many evaluation models get wrong: knock-out criteria versus weighted criteria."],[633.75,636.1,"On the left, knock-out criteria."],[636.1,638.13,"These are pass or fail."],[638.13,642.02,"Licences and vetting for a security provider."],[642.02,647.2,"A collective wage compliance declaration for cleaning."],[647.2,650.48,"ISO 27001 or an equivalent for an ICT supplier."],[650.48,658.45,"Acceptance of audit and access rights, as required by DORA Article 30 and Solvency II Article 274."],[658.73,660.33,"And financial standing."],[660.33,664.02,"If a bidder fails one of these, it is out, whatever its price."],[664.52,667.75,"On the right, highlighted, are the weighted criteria."],[667.75,683.12,"These are the ones that differentiate between compliant bidders: the service plan and staffing model, KPIs, reporting and quality control, transition and mobilisation, ESG and innovation, and price or total cost of ownership."],[683.62,688.88,"The rule is in the speaker's note: regulatory requirements are gates, not points."],[688.88,694.23,"Imagine you score audit rights as a weighted criterion worth five percent."],[694.23,700.02,"A bidder refuses supervisor access, loses five points, and wins anyway on price."],[700.02,704.91,"Now the insurer has a contract that does not meet its regulatory obligations."],[705.19,709.98,"A bidder who refuses supervisor access cannot be allowed to win on price."],[709.98,711.91,"That is why it must be a gate."],[712.86,715.12,"Now we build the evaluation model."],[715.12,719.28,"These six steps are in the order of work, so follow them in sequence."],[719.79,720.62,"Step one, criteria."],[720.62,726.72,"They come from the specification and from the objectives in your strategy paper."],[726.72,734.87,"If the strategy says the main problem is quality at the headquarters, quality control and KPIs should be visible criteria."],[735.37,736.5,"Step two, weights."],[736.5,742.51,"Decide quality versus price first, then split the quality weight into sub-criteria."],[742.51,748.88,"Be explicit about whether sub-criterion weights are shares of one hundred or shares of the quality weight."],[748.88,751.8,"Either works, as long as everyone knows which."],[752.29,753.86,"Step three, the scale."],[753.86,757.86,"Zero to five, with written descriptors, which we look at in a moment."],[758.36,759.93,"Step four, evidence."],[759.93,763.03,"For each criterion, what will bidders have to show?"],[763.03,769.16,"A staffing plan, a sample report, a reference site, a mobilisation plan with named people."],[769.16,772.95,"If you do not ask for evidence, evaluators score promises."],[773.45,776.22,"Step five, the formula: how price becomes a score."],[776.22,779.1,"We will see that this choice is not neutral."],[779.61,782.18,"And step six, fix and disclose."],[782.18,785.61,"The model is fixed before issue and disclosed in the Coupa event."],[785.61,790.71,"This is the single most important governance rule of the session."],[790.71,795.48,"Weights set after the bids arrive are an equal-treatment and governance failure."],[795.76,800.75,"If you can see the bids when you choose the weights, you are no longer choosing the best bid."],[800.75,803.91,"You are choosing the weights that make your preferred bid win."],[804.86,807.12,"In the live session we pause for a poll."],[807.12,809.31,"Think about your last sourcing event."],[809.31,811.83,"When were the evaluation weights fixed?"],[811.83,814.5,"Before issue, and disclosed to bidders?"],[814.5,816.76,"Before issue, but not disclosed?"],[816.76,818.07,"After the bids arrived?"],[818.07,819.72,"Were there no weights at all?"],[819.72,820.84,"Or do you not know?"],[820.84,822.67,"Answer honestly for yourself."],[822.95,829.99,"If your answer is anything other than the first option, ask yourself why, and what it would take to change it in your next event."],[830.49,832.25,"Why does this matter so much?"],[832.25,843.78,"Because the moment the weights are set after opening, every losing bidder has a legitimate question: did you choose the best bid, or did you choose the weights that suited the bid you already preferred?"],[844.06,848.86,"You will not be able to answer that question convincingly, however good your intentions were."],[849.81,852.27,"Here is a scoring scale with descriptors."],[852.27,858.65,"Five is excellent: fully meets the requirement with evidence, and adds value beyond it."],[858.65,861.76,"Four is good: fully meets it, with evidence."],[861.76,865.13,"Three is acceptable: meets it, with minor gaps."],[865.13,866.62,"Two is weak: significant gaps."],[866.62,868.97,"One is poor: barely addresses it."],[869.25,871.72,"And zero means not met, or no answer."],[872.23,874.05,"Why bother with descriptors?"],[874.05,879.04,"Because they make individual scores comparable before the consensus meeting."],[879.04,887.23,"Without them, one evaluator's three is another evaluator's four, and the consensus meeting becomes a negotiation between moods."],[887.23,892.5,"With them, the discussion becomes: does this answer show evidence, yes or no?"],[892.78,895.63,"Does it add value beyond the requirement?"],[895.63,897.81,"Notice the difference between four and five."],[897.81,900.18,"Five is not for a very good answer."],[900.46,912.81,"It is for an answer that fully meets the requirement and adds something you did not ask for, like a cleaning supplier proposing a sensor-based system that cleans meeting rooms only after they have been used."],[913.31,916.38,"Let me show you how this works in a consensus meeting."],[916.38,920.36,"Three evaluators score the transition plan of a cleaning bidder."],[920.36,923.24,"Two give a four, one gives a two."],[923.24,926.3,"With descriptors, the conversation is concrete."],[926.58,935.75,"The evaluator who gave a two points out that the plan has no named transition manager and no timeline for staff transfer, which is a significant gap."],[935.75,943.71,"The others look again, agree that the evidence is missing, and the team settles on a three, with that rationale written down."],[943.99,947.58,"Without descriptors, they would simply have averaged their opinions."],[948.52,951.45,"How should you set the weights between quality and price?"],[951.45,955.11,"There is no universal answer, but there are reference points."],[955.62,966.21,"In the UK Crown Commercial Service consultancy framework, further competitions must give quality at least 50 percent, in a range from 50 to 90 percent."],[966.21,973.57,"A 2024 UK university cleaning tender was awarded on a 50 50 split between quality and price."],[973.57,976.53,"Those two are documented public references."],[976.81,983.73,"For quality-led services, common practice puts quality at 50 to 70 percent and price at 30 to 50."],[983.73,988.69,"Note the word practice: that is a range many teams use, not a norm."],[988.69,994.19,"And for a well specified commodity, price, or a total cost formula, decides."],[994.69,998.57,"The point is that the weight should reflect the category and the strategy."],[998.57,1006.91,"For Nordvia's consulting framework, where the problem is wildly different capability for the same day rate, quality should weigh heavily."],[1006.91,1013.29,"For office supplies with a fixed item list, quality differences are small, and price should decide."],[1014.24,1017.19,"In the live session we run another poll here."],[1017.19,1020.47,"Which price scoring formula does your team use today?"],[1020.47,1023.2,"Lowest price divided by bid price times one hundred?"],[1023.2,1025.44,"A linear deviation from the lowest?"],[1025.44,1028.43,"Ranking points, for first, second and third?"],[1028.43,1030.62,"No formula, just judgement?"],[1030.62,1032.56,"Or does it change every time?"],[1032.56,1033.78,"Think about your answer."],[1034.06,1038.01,"Most teams use the first formula without knowing its side effect."],[1038.01,1039.5,"The next slides show it."],[1040.45,1044.01,"Here are two common price scoring formulas side by side."],[1044.52,1046.7,"On the left, the relative formula."],[1046.7,1052.85,"The price score equals the lowest price divided by the bid price, times one hundred."],[1052.85,1057.22,"The lowest bid gets one hundred, and a bid twice as expensive gets fifty."],[1057.22,1059.22,"It is simple and common."],[1059.22,1061.22,"But look at the last two lines."],[1061.22,1064.19,"Every score moves when the lowest bid changes."],[1064.47,1067.29,"And one very low bid compresses the others."],[1067.29,1074.39,"If one bidder comes in far below the rest, everyone else's price score drops, and the gaps between them shrink."],[1074.39,1077.76,"The whole contest gets reshaped by a single bid."],[1078.26,1081.02,"On the right, the linear deviation formula."],[1081.02,1089.59,"The price score equals one hundred times one minus the difference between the bid and the lowest, divided by the lowest, with a floor at zero."],[1089.59,1094.6,"In words: you lose one point for every percent you are above the lowest bid."],[1094.6,1097.67,"A bid 20 percent above the lowest scores eighty."],[1097.95,1104.91,"Compared with the relative formula, this one punishes price gaps harder, and so favours cheaper bids more strongly."],[1105.4,1107.22,"Neither formula is right or wrong."],[1107.22,1111.99,"What is wrong is not knowing what your formula does before you publish it."],[1112.95,1115.12,"This worked example shows why."],[1115.12,1121.91,"It uses the fictional Nordvia headquarters cleaning bids that we evaluate in detail in module 3.3."],[1121.91,1125.66,"There are four bidders, A to D, weighted 50 50."],[1126.16,1134.33,"With the relative formula and all bids in, the ranking is D with 80.2, B with 79.6 and C with 79.0."],[1134.33,1141.69,"With the linear formula, same bids, same weights, the ranking flips: C with 79.0, B with 78.3 and D with 77.5."],[1141.69,1145.86,"Same bids, same weights, a different winner."],[1145.86,1149.8,"Nothing about the quality or the prices changed."],[1150.08,1151.38,"Only the formula."],[1151.88,1159.37,"Now remove Bidder C, which, as we will see in the next lesson, may be an abnormally low bid."],[1159.37,1163.47,"With the relative formula, D scores 87.1 and B 87.0."],[1163.47,1167.68,"With the linear formula, B scores 87.0 and D 86.8."],[1167.68,1173.09,"Practically a tie either way, with the order depending on the formula."],[1173.58,1178.06,"So the lesson is: test the formula on fictional bid sets before issue."],[1178.06,1181.02,"Run three invented sets through your model."],[1181.02,1185.76,"A normal one, one with a very low outlier, and one with two bids very close together."],[1185.76,1193.57,"If the ranking flips with the formula, or with a small change of weights, then the model is deciding, not the bids."],[1193.85,1200.29,"Better to find that out on a Tuesday afternoon with invented numbers than in front of a losing bidder's lawyer."],[1201.25,1203.82,"Let us gather the common design mistakes."],[1203.82,1210.1,"Each one is a reason an award can be challenged by a losing bidder or questioned by Internal Audit."],[1210.6,1216.27,"The gold-plated specification: asking for more than the business needs, and paying for it."],[1216.27,1219.79,"The events agency example from earlier is one."],[1219.79,1224.77,"Twenty four by seven support for a system used in office hours is another."],[1225.26,1229.54,"Weights after opening: changing the model to fit the preferred bidder."],[1229.54,1233.15,"We have said it already, and we will say it again in the next lesson."],[1233.66,1238.78,"First-year price only: ignoring indexation, mobilisation and exit."],[1238.78,1244.73,"A low first year with an aggressive index can be the most expensive bid over five years."],[1245.23,1250.32,"And regulation as points: scoring audit rights instead of requiring them."],[1250.32,1251.42,"Gates, not points."],[1251.92,1255.08,"Notice that all four mistakes share a root cause."],[1255.08,1263.15,"They happen when the design is rushed, or when it is done after people have already formed a view about who should win."],[1263.15,1271.38,"That is why the order of work matters: specification, lots, pricing schedule, criteria, weights, formula, test, and only then issue."],[1272.32,1273.93,"Now it is your turn."],[1273.93,1277.35,"Practice 3.2 in your workbook takes about twenty minutes."],[1277.35,1281.44,"Choose the specification type and the lots for your project."],[1281.44,1286.21,"Set the split between quality and price, then the quality sub-criteria."],[1286.21,1290.51,"Write the knock-out criteria, including the regulatory gates."],[1290.51,1292.1,"Choose the price formula."],[1292.38,1295.9,"And list the total cost elements beyond the annual price."],[1295.9,1302.88,"One tip: state whether your sub-criterion weights are shares of one hundred or shares of the quality weight."],[1302.88,1307.76,"Pause the video now and work in the Modules tab if you want, then continue."],[1308.71,1309.9,"Let us debrief."],[1309.9,1315.22,"Who has a knock-out criterion that is really a weighted one, or the reverse?"],[1315.22,1324.2,"A common example is ESG: a code of conduct signature is a gate, but the quality of a carbon reduction plan is a weighted criterion."],[1324.2,1328.23,"Whose quality weight is below 50 percent for a service?"],[1328.23,1328.27,"Why?"],[1328.55,1331.44,"Which total cost element would change your ranking?"],[1331.44,1336.3,"And the last question: would your business owner sign these weights today?"],[1336.3,1338.4,"That is the bridge to Fieldwork 3."],[1338.4,1344.4,"The weights must be validated with the business before issue, because after issue they cannot change."],[1344.9,1350.43,"If you are watching alone, take two of these questions and answer them in writing in your workbook."],[1350.43,1354.48,"The question about the business owner is the one I would choose first."],[1354.48,1359.79,"If you hesitate, find out now what they would change, because next week it will be too late."],[1360.74,1362.72,"Here is module 3.2 in three lines."],[1362.72,1368.98,"First, the specification and the lots decide what you can compare and what you can award."],[1368.98,1374.69,"Second, knock-outs for gates, weights for differences, and total cost over the term."],[1374.69,1379.47,"Third, fix, test and disclose the model and the formula before issue."],[1379.97,1382.16,"Let me recap the journey of this lesson."],[1382.16,1386.94,"We started with van Weele: early decisions lock in cost and risk."],[1386.94,1391.25,"We chose between input, output and hybrid specifications."],[1391.25,1397.66,"We designed lots the way we will award, and a pricing schedule that puts every bid into the same spreadsheet."],[1397.94,1403.08,"We used Ellram's total cost of ownership to look beyond the first-year price."],[1403.08,1405.56,"We separated gates from weighted criteria."],[1405.56,1410.35,"We built the model in six steps, with descriptors and reference weights."],[1410.35,1413.72,"And we saw that the formula alone can change the winner."],[1414.23,1418.66,"In the next lesson, module 3.3, we use a model on real looking bids."],[1418.66,1429.15,"You will meet the four Nordvia cleaning bids in full, deal with one that looks abnormally low, test the sensitivity of the result, and decide what to recommend."],[1429.15,1430.29,"See you there."]]},{"id":"s3-l3","session":"s3","title":"Running the event fairly: evaluation and award","summary":"Run a fair evaluation, score bids with the published model, handle an abnormally low bid, test sensitivity and write a defensible award recommendation.","from":46,"to":63,"audio":"audio/s3-l3.mp3","duration":1450.9,"segs":[[46,0.6,19.31,"Welcome to the third video lesson of Session 3. This one covers module 3.3, running it fairly: Q and A, evaluation and the award decision. In the last lesson you built an evaluation model and fixed it before issue. Now the bids are in, and we use the model for real."],[46,19.81,61.01,"Here is the plan. First, why fairness matters, commercially and not only ethically. Then equal treatment in practice, including how to use the Coupa Message Center. Then the six steps of the evaluation process. After that, the heart of this lesson: the four fictional Nordvia bids for the headquarters cleaning lot. We will compute the price scores together, total them, deal with a bid that looks abnormally low, and test how sensitive the result is. We finish with due diligence, the regulatory gates for an insurer, and the evaluation report. Have a pen and paper or a calculator ready. There are numbers in this lesson, and it helps to follow them yourself."],[47,61.96,72.78,"Fairness is what keeps good suppliers bidding next time. And, as the slide says, it is what lets you defend the award to an auditor, a supervisor or a losing incumbent."],[47,73.28,116.33,"In public procurement, equal treatment is a legal duty. In private procurement, which is where an insurer usually sits for its indirect spend, it is not imposed in the same way. So why should we care? Because it is a commercial asset. Suppliers price the risk of an unfair buyer. If a good cleaning company believes that the incumbent always wins, or that the decision is made over lunch, it will either not bid at all, or it will put in a quick, unambitious bid. Over a few cycles you end up with fewer bidders and weaker offers, and you will never see the figures that tell you why. A reputation for fair events is something you build one event at a time, and you can lose it in one event too."],[47,116.83,130.02,"There is also the internal audience. Internal Audit, the risk function and, for some services, the supervisor may ask you to show how a decision was made. Fairness that you cannot evidence does not count."],[47,130.52,162.35,"Here is a small story that happens in many organisations. A facilities team runs a security tender. The incumbent is invited to a private walk-through of the new building that the other bidders never see, and wins. Nobody intended anything improper. But the next time the team tenders security, two of the three strong challengers decline to bid. They have concluded, reasonably, that the game is fixed. The cost of that one shortcut shows up years later, in prices nobody can benchmark."],[48,163.3,166.8,"What does equal treatment look like in practice? Four rules."],[48,167.3,177.64,"First, same information. The same documents, at the same time, with the same deadline for everyone. If you extend the deadline for one bidder, you extend it for all."],[48,178.14,195.25,"Second, one channel. All questions go through the Coupa Message Center. Every exchange there is logged and triggers email notifications, and that log is your equal-treatment evidence. If a question is asked by phone, ask the supplier to put it in the Message Center."],[48,195.75,210.54,"Third, shared answers. When an answer matters to everyone, send it to everyone, anonymised. If one bidder asks whether the headquarters lot includes the underground car park, every bidder needs that answer, without knowing who asked."],[48,211.04,230.28,"Fourth, no side channels, above all with the incumbent. The most common breach is not the procurement team. It is the incumbent calling the business owner directly, over coffee, to share a few thoughts about the tender. So brief your stakeholders before issue: during the event, all contact goes through procurement."],[48,230.78,259.73,"And one more principle from the note: clarifications explain a bid, they do not let a bidder change it. You may ask a bidder what it meant by its staffing table. You may not let it send a new staffing table because it has heard its price is high. In the UK Crown Commercial Service consultancy framework there is no negotiation after bids, only transparent clarification. In a private event you may plan negotiation rounds, but only if the RFx said so."],[49,260.68,263.97,"Now the evaluation process itself, in six steps."],[49,264.47,275.26,"Step one, the compliance check. Is the bid complete, and does it pass every knock-out criterion? A bid without the wage compliance declaration does not go further."],[49,275.76,298.12,"Step two, individual scoring. About three evaluators score the quality responses on their own, using the descriptors, and without seeing prices. Why without prices? Because once you know a bid is cheap, it is very hard not to read its quality answers more kindly, or more harshly. Before they start, evaluators declare any conflicts of interest."],[49,298.62,313.36,"Step three, the consensus meeting. The evaluators discuss the gaps between their scores and agree one score for each criterion, with a written rationale. The rationale is what you will use later when a losing bidder asks for a debrief."],[49,313.86,319.62,"Step four, the commercial evaluation, using the published formula on a total cost basis."],[49,320.12,327.99,"Step five, the sensitivity test. Would reasonable changes alter the winner? We do this in a few minutes with the Nordvia bids."],[49,328.49,333.49,"And step six, the report: the evaluation report and the award recommendation."],[49,333.99,369.38,"Individual scoring by about three evaluators followed by consensus is the CCS consultancy guidance, and it is good private-sector practice too. One practical note on Coupa. Whether Coupa offers a formal sealed envelope setting, where technical and commercial parts are opened separately, is not confirmed in its public documentation. Confirm it in your Coupa tenant. If it is not available, keep prices apart by role: the evaluators who score quality simply do not get access to the pricing section until the quality scores are agreed."],[50,370.33,385.03,"Here are the four bids for the Nordvia headquarters cleaning lot. They are fictional. Quality is scored from zero to one hundred after consensus of three evaluators, and the published model is 50 percent quality and 50 percent price."],[50,385.53,421.72,"Bidder A is the incumbent. Annual price 690 thousand euros, quality 62. Same method as today, and staff continuity. Bidder B: 610 thousand, quality 74. An output-based plan, with robot floor care at night. Bidder C: 520 thousand, quality 58. And a note that its hours look below the wage floor. Bidder D: 655 thousand, quality 81. The strongest quality and ESG plan, but it requires a four-year term, while the published structure is three years plus one plus one."],[50,422.22,429.36,"Before we calculate anything, make a guess. Which bidder do you think wins? Write your guess down. We will come back to it at the end."],[50,429.86,463.66,"Before you guess, read the notes column carefully, because each bid tells a story. A is the comfortable choice: nothing changes, including the quality complaints that started this project. B proposes a different way of working, which could fix the quality problem and lower the price, but brings change risk. C is very cheap, with the weakest plan and a warning sign. D is the best plan, at a moderate price, but it asks for a term that differs from what was published. In a real event, each of those notes becomes a question for clarification."],[51,464.61,477.81,"Step one, price scores. We use the published formula, the relative one: price score equals lowest price divided by bid price, times one hundred. The lowest price is 520, from Bidder C."],[51,478.31,509.15,"Let us do the first one together. Bidder A: 520 divided by 690 is about 0.754. Times one hundred, 75.4. Now try Bidder B yourself before I say it. 520 divided by 610. The answer is 85.2. Bidder C is the lowest, so it scores exactly 100. And Bidder D: 520 divided by 655, which gives 79.4."],[51,509.65,526.83,"So the price scores are A 75.4, B 85.2, C 100 and D 79.4. Notice how much C's low price shapes this. Because C sets the reference, every other score is measured against 520. Keep that in mind."],[52,527.78,533.99,"Step two, total scores at 50 50. The total is half the quality score plus half the price score."],[52,534.49,558.83,"For D: half of 81 is 40.5, half of 79.4 is 39.7, total 80.2. For B: half of 74 is 37, half of 85.2 is 42.6, total 79.6. For C: half of 58 is 29, plus 50, total 79.0. And A: 31 plus 37.7, total 68.7."],[52,559.33,583.35,"Look at the bars. D, B and C are within 1.2 points of each other. A is clearly behind. So is D the winner? On paper, yes, by a small margin. But a gap of 1.2 points on a hundred-point scale, when each quality score comes from human judgement, is not a clear winner. And one of the three leading bids has a warning note. Before we go further, we have to deal with C."],[52,583.85,611.46,"And notice where the incumbent sits. Bidder A, with 68.7, is more than ten points behind. That is a useful message for the business owner who feared losing the incumbent. The fair model gave A full credit for its quality, and A still did not come close. Its price was the highest and its plan did not address the complaints. When the business owner asks why the incumbent lost, the model gives you a clear, documented answer."],[53,612.41,619.15,"Let us talk about abnormally low bids. Four things to know: the signal, the duty, the test and the decision."],[53,619.65,633.22,"The signal: a bid far below the others, or below a should-cost model. One bid much cheaper than the rest is not proof of anything. It may be a genuinely more efficient supplier. But it is a signal you must follow up."],[53,633.72,645.05,"The duty: ask the bidder in writing to explain, as CCS guidance does. In writing, through the Message Center, so it is on the record and so the bidder has a fair chance to answer."],[53,645.55,666.63,"The test: build or use a should-cost model. For cleaning, that means hours, times the collective wage, plus statutory on-costs such as holidays and sickness, plus supervision, plus consumables and equipment, plus overhead, plus margin. If the bid price cannot cover that sum for the hours the work needs, something does not add up."],[53,667.13,691.28,"And the decision: accept with evidence, or reject and record why. Never reject automatically, and never award blindly. In cleaning and security, a bid below the wage floor is both a service-failure risk and a legal risk. Staff are underpaid or hours are cut, turnover rises, quality falls, and the insurer's name ends up attached to a labour-law problem."],[53,691.78,736.88,"Let me give you an illustrative calculation, with invented round numbers, to show how the test works. Suppose a site needs about 200 productive cleaning hours a week. Suppose the collective wage plus statutory on-costs comes to about 20 euros an hour. That is about 4,000 euros a week, or roughly 208 thousand euros a year, for labour alone, before supervision, consumables, overhead and margin. If a bid for that site is 180 thousand euros a year, it cannot cover the wage floor for the hours needed. Either the bidder plans fewer hours, or it plans to pay less than the agreement requires. Both are problems you want to find before award, not after."],[54,737.83,740.24,"So let us put Bidder C under the microscope."],[54,740.74,762.97,"Its price is 520. That is about 15 percent below the next bid, B at 610, and about 20 percent below the average of the other three, which is about 652. The data note in the case says 18 percent below the others. Depending on the comparison it is 15 or 20 percent. Either way, it is a red flag."],[54,763.47,779.43,"Its quality score is 58, the weakest plan of the four. And its staffing hours look below what the wage floor allows for the area to be cleaned. So we have three signals pointing the same way: very low price, weakest plan, implausible hours."],[54,779.93,812.34,"The action: a written request to explain its hours, wage rates and supervision. For example: please explain how many productive hours per week your price assumes for the headquarters, the hourly wage and on-costs you have applied, and how supervision is staffed. Then compare the answer with the should-cost model. If the explanation holds, perhaps C uses a smarter shift pattern, you may accept the bid, and record why. If the explanation fails, you reject it as abnormally low and record the evidence."],[55,813.29,823.31,"Step three, sensitivity. This table shows what happens to the totals under different scenarios. All figures are fictional, using the relative price formula."],[55,823.81,834.25,"The first row is our base case: 50 50 with all bids. D 80.2, B 79.6, C 79.0, A 68.7."],[55,834.75,849.06,"Second row, 60 percent quality and 40 percent price. D rises to 80.4, B falls to 78.5, and C drops to 74.8. With more weight on quality, D leads more clearly."],[55,849.56,864.79,"Third row, 40 percent quality and 60 percent price. Now C wins with 83.2, ahead of B with 80.7 and D with 80.0. In other words, if we cared a bit more about price, the doubtful bid would win."],[55,865.29,886.35,"Now the last two rows, without C. Remember the footnote: without C, the lowest price becomes 610, B's price, and every price score moves. At 50 50, D scores 87.1 and B 87.0. Almost a perfect tie. At 60 40, D 85.9 and B 84.4."],[55,886.85,903.96,"So what have we learned? Two things. First, C, the doubtful bid, moves the whole table. Its presence shifts every price score, and under a price-heavy weighting it would win. Second, once C is resolved, the real contest is between B and D, and it is very close."],[56,904.91,910.86,"So what does the sensitivity test tell you, and what does it not tell you? Look at the left-hand column first."],[56,911.36,933.55,"It does not tell you to change the weights now. The weights were published at 50 50, and they stay at 50 50. It does not tell you that the highest number wins regardless. And it does not tell you that C should be ignored silently. If C is excluded, that must be a documented decision based on its explanation, not a quiet deletion from the spreadsheet."],[56,934.05,971.92,"Now the right-hand column, highlighted. It tells you that the decision is close between B and D. It tells you that C must be resolved first, because it moves every price score. It tells you to clarify, then negotiate or ask for a BAFO with B and D, if your RFx allowed for that step. And it tells you to say in the report that the result is sensitive. That is a finding, not a failure. And there is one more open point: D's request for a four-year term, against the published three plus one plus one. That has to be clarified with D too, on the same terms you would apply to anyone."],[56,972.42,978.12,"The principle from the note: sensitivity informs the next step. It never rewrites the model."],[57,979.07,1018.9,"In the live session we poll the room here. Bidder C is about 20 percent below the average of the others, and its hours look below the wage floor. What is your first step? Award it? Exclude it at once? Ask for a written explanation and check it against a should-cost model? Or tell the others to match it? Think about your answer, and also about why immediate exclusion is risky. The expected answer is the written explanation, checked against a should-cost model. Excluding C at once, without asking, could exclude a genuinely efficient supplier and expose you to a challenge. And telling the others to match it would break equal treatment."],[58,1019.85,1024.69,"Before any award, you run due diligence on the preferred bidders. Four areas."],[58,1025.19,1060.59,"Financial: the supplier's standing, its dependency on your contract, and its insurance cover. If your contract would be half of a small cleaner's revenue, that is a risk for both of you. Information security: ISO 27001 or a SOC 2 report, access control, and where data is located. Even a cleaner gets badges for secure floors. Labour and ESG: wage compliance, subcontractors, and the code of conduct. And references: similar sites or services, checked by phone, not only collected as a list."],[58,1061.09,1078.07,"The note makes an important point. Due diligence runs on the preferred bidders, before award, as gates. It is not a weighted criterion. You do not give B three extra points for good references. You check that B passes, and if it does not, it cannot be awarded."],[58,1078.57,1109.16,"For the Nordvia cleaning lot, due diligence would run on B and D, the two preferred bidders. For B, the robot floor care at night raises a practical question: who has access to the building at night, and how are those people vetted? For D, the request for a four-year term invites a financial question: why does it need the longer term? Perhaps it plans a large investment in equipment that it needs to recover. That is a legitimate reason, but you want to understand it before the BAFO."],[59,1110.11,1117.45,"For an insurer, some services also pass through regulatory gates before award. This table shows the main ones."],[59,1117.95,1139.46,"If the service is an ICT service, DORA applies. Article 28, paragraph 4, requires a pre-contract assessment. Does the service support a critical or important function? Have all relevant risks been identified, including concentration risk? Has due diligence on the provider been done? Are there conflicts of interest?"],[59,1139.96,1156.64,"If the ICT service supports a critical or important function, Article 30, paragraph 3, adds requirements. The specific contract clauses must be accepted, an exit strategy must be feasible, and the supervisor must be informed in time."],[59,1157.14,1175.23,"If you outsource a critical or important function under Solvency II, Article 274 of the Delegated Regulation requires a detailed examination of the provider's ability, capacity and any authorisation required, and sets terms for the written agreement."],[59,1175.73,1182.02,"And if the supplier processes personal data, you need a data processing agreement and a security review."],[59,1182.52,1203.15,"Most soft facility management categories, like our cleaning lot, are usually not critical outsourcing. But Nordvia's network provider Telmo, whose services are used by the claims systems, and Northbyte, which runs the claims IT managed service, may well be. The assessment is the insurer's own, and it must be documented."],[59,1203.65,1222.55,"In practice, this means the regulatory gate has to be planned into your timeline from module 3.1. A DORA assessment and supervisor notification take time, and they cannot start the day before signature. If your project touches one of these services, put the gate in your backwards plan now."],[60,1223.5,1227.39,"All of this ends up in the evaluation report. Four sections."],[60,1227.89,1253.49,"Process: who evaluated, the conflicts declared, the timeline, and the clarifications asked and answered. Results: the scores per criterion with their rationale, the price scores and the totals. Tests: the sensitivity analysis, how the abnormally low bid was handled, and the due diligence results. And the recommendation: the next step or the award, with conditions, risks and the approvals needed."],[60,1253.99,1280.91,"For the Nordvia cleaning lot, the recommendation is probably not yet an award. It might read: Bidder C asked to explain its price; subject to that, B and D are within a fraction of a point and are invited to a BAFO under the published criteria, with D asked to confirm the published term. Write the report as if a losing bidder will ask for a debrief, because one will. Session 4 covers the award and the debriefing."],[61,1281.86,1317.34,"Now it is your turn to do the full calculation. Practice 3.3 in your workbook takes about twenty minutes. Compute the price scores with lowest divided by bid times one hundred. Compute the totals at 50 50 and at 60 40. Recompute without Bidder C. Name the abnormally low bid and write what you will ask it. And write what the sensitivity tells you. The workbook checks the numbers automatically. If you finish early, compute the scores with the linear formula as a bonus. Pause the video now and do it in the Modules tab if you want, then continue."],[61,1317.84,1342.81,"A word on why this exercise matters beyond cleaning. The same steps apply to an IT services lot, a marketing agency selection or a legal panel: price scores from the published formula, totals at the published weights, a check of any outlier, and a sensitivity test before you recommend anything. Once you have done it by hand once, you will read every evaluation spreadsheet differently."],[62,1343.76,1381.33,"Let us debrief. Who guessed D at the start? Who guessed C? Go back to the guess you wrote down at the beginning. How did excluding C change the gap between B and D? It closed to a tenth of a point at 50 50. Would you tell the business owner who is winning at this stage? I would be careful. With a gap that small and a BAFO to come, saying D is winning creates expectations, and it can leak to bidders. And what would you put in the evaluation report about sensitivity? The point of this debrief is simple: intuition and the model often differ, and the model is the one that must be defensible."],[63,1382.28,1401.24,"Here is module 3.3 in three lines. First, same information, one channel, no side doors: fairness is designed. Second, score individually, agree by consensus, keep prices apart. Third, resolve abnormally low bids and test sensitivity before you recommend."],[63,1401.74,1433.19,"Let me recap the three lessons of this session so far. In module 3.1 you chose the event sequence from your route to market and planned it backwards from the notice deadline. In module 3.2 you designed the request: specification, lots, pricing schedule, gates, weights and a tested formula, all fixed before issue. And in this module you ran the event fairly, scored the bids, handled an abnormally low one, and saw that the honest result was a close contest between B and D."],[63,1433.69,1449.73,"Which leaves a question for the next module. Would an e-auction help us choose between B and D? Or would it hurt? Module 3.4 looks at the evidence on e-auctions, when they work, when they damage relationships, and which format to choose. See you there."]],"questions":[{"after":48,"q":"During the cleaning RFP, the incumbent calls Nordvia's Head of Real Estate to discuss the tender. What should have been done to prevent this?","options":["Brief stakeholders before issue that all contact goes through procurement and the Message Center","Exclude the incumbent from the event","Let the call happen but take notes","Share the incumbent's thoughts with all bidders afterwards"],"answer":0,"explain":"Side channels, above all with the incumbent, are the most common breach of equal treatment. Briefing the business owner before issue and using one channel prevents it and creates the evidence log.","t":259.73},{"after":49,"q":"Why do quality evaluators score without seeing prices?","options":["Because Coupa always hides prices automatically","So that knowledge of the price does not colour their reading of the quality answers","Because price is not part of the evaluation","To save time in the consensus meeting"],"answer":1,"explain":"Knowing a bid is cheap or expensive biases how quality answers are read. Keeping prices apart, by a sealed setting confirmed in your tenant or by role, protects the independence of the quality scores.","t":369.38},{"after":51,"q":"If Bidder B's price had been 580 thousand euros instead of 610, with C still at 520, what would B's price score be under the relative formula?","options":["About 85.2","About 100","About 89.7","About 78.0"],"answer":2,"explain":"Price score equals lowest divided by bid times one hundred: 520 divided by 580 is about 0.897, so about 89.7.","t":526.83},{"after":53,"q":"A security services bid for Nordvia is far below the others. Which test tells you whether the price can be sustainable?","options":["Comparing it with last year's invoice only","A should-cost model: hours times the collective wage plus on-costs, supervision, equipment, overhead and margin","Asking the other bidders whether it is realistic","Checking whether the bidder is a large company"],"answer":1,"explain":"A should-cost model built from the wage floor and the hours needed shows whether the price can cover lawful wages and the service. It turns a suspicion into evidence for the decision.","t":736.88},{"after":54,"q":"Bidder C answers your written request with a credible staffing plan showing fewer but properly paid hours thanks to new equipment, which fits the should-cost model. What do you do?","options":["Accept the bid as valid and record the evidence for the decision","Reject it anyway, because it is far below the others","Ask the other bidders to match its price","Increase the quality weight to reduce its chances"],"answer":0,"explain":"An abnormally low bid is never rejected automatically. If the explanation holds against the should-cost model, the bid stays in, and the reasons are recorded.","t":812.34},{"after":56,"q":"The sensitivity test shows that at 40 60 Bidder C would win. A colleague suggests moving to 60 40 to be safe. What is the correct response?","options":["Agree, since quality matters more in cleaning","Run the event again with new weights","Keep the published 50 50; use the sensitivity to plan the next step and report that the result is sensitive","Award to whoever wins most scenarios"],"answer":2,"explain":"Sensitivity informs the next step, it never rewrites the model. The weights were published and stay; the finding is reported and leads to resolving C and a BAFO with B and D.","t":978.12},{"after":58,"q":"Bidder B's references are excellent. How should that enter the decision?","options":["As extra points on its quality score","As a due diligence gate that B passes before award","It should be ignored","As a reason to skip the financial check"],"answer":1,"explain":"Due diligence runs on preferred bidders as pass or fail gates before award. It is not a weighted criterion and does not add points.","t":1109.16},{"after":60,"q":"For the Nordvia cleaning lot, which recommendation fits the evidence at this stage?","options":["Award to D, the highest score at 50 50","Award to C, the lowest price","Resolve C's explanation, then invite B and D to a BAFO under the published criteria, with D asked to confirm the published term","Re-tender with new weights"],"answer":2,"explain":"The model shows a close contest that depends on how C is resolved. The report should say so and recommend the next step, not force an award or change the model.","t":1280.91},{"after":62,"q":"A losing bidder will ask for a debrief. What makes the evaluation report defensible?","options":["Process, scores with rationale, sensitivity and abnormally low bid handling, due diligence and a clear recommendation","A short statement of the winner and the total score","The business owner's preference in writing","The incumbent's performance history only"],"answer":0,"explain":"A defensible report shows how the decision was made: who evaluated, the rationale for scores, the tests run and the conditions of the recommendation.","t":1381.33}],"cues":[[0.6,3.59,"Welcome to the third video lesson of Session 3."],[3.59,9.77,"This one covers module 3.3, running it fairly: Q and A, evaluation and the award decision."],[9.77,15.88,"In the last lesson you built an evaluation model and fixed it before issue."],[15.88,19.31,"Now the bids are in, and we use the model for real."],[19.81,21.05,"Here is the plan."],[21.05,24.7,"First, why fairness matters, commercially and not only ethically."],[24.7,30.32,"Then equal treatment in practice, including how to use the Coupa Message Center."],[30.32,33.5,"Then the six steps of the evaluation process."],[33.5,39.37,"After that, the heart of this lesson: the four fictional Nordvia bids for the headquarters cleaning lot."],[39.65,47.88,"We will compute the price scores together, total them, deal with a bid that looks abnormally low, and test how sensitive the result is."],[47.88,54.65,"We finish with due diligence, the regulatory gates for an insurer, and the evaluation report."],[54.65,57.17,"Have a pen and paper or a calculator ready."],[57.45,61.01,"There are numbers in this lesson, and it helps to follow them yourself."],[61.96,65.31,"Fairness is what keeps good suppliers bidding next time."],[65.31,72.78,"And, as the slide says, it is what lets you defend the award to an auditor, a supervisor or a losing incumbent."],[73.28,76.9,"In public procurement, equal treatment is a legal duty."],[76.9,84.31,"In private procurement, which is where an insurer usually sits for its indirect spend, it is not imposed in the same way."],[84.31,85.82,"So why should we care?"],[85.82,87.96,"Because it is a commercial asset."],[87.96,91.03,"Suppliers price the risk of an unfair buyer."],[91.31,102.22,"If a good cleaning company believes that the incumbent always wins, or that the decision is made over lunch, it will either not bid at all, or it will put in a quick, unambitious bid."],[102.22,109.44,"Over a few cycles you end up with fewer bidders and weaker offers, and you will never see the figures that tell you why."],[109.72,116.34,"A reputation for fair events is something you build one event at a time, and you can lose it in one event too."],[116.83,119.08,"There is also the internal audience."],[119.08,126.95,"Internal Audit, the risk function and, for some services, the supervisor may ask you to show how a decision was made."],[126.95,130.01,"Fairness that you cannot evidence does not count."],[130.52,134.41,"Here is a small story that happens in many organisations."],[134.41,137.56,"A facilities team runs a security tender."],[137.56,144.73,"The incumbent is invited to a private walk-through of the new building that the other bidders never see, and wins."],[144.73,147.22,"Nobody intended anything improper."],[147.5,153.7,"But the next time the team tenders security, two of the three strong challengers decline to bid."],[153.7,157.26,"They have concluded, reasonably, that the game is fixed."],[157.26,162.35,"The cost of that one shortcut shows up years later, in prices nobody can benchmark."],[163.3,165.71,"What does equal treatment look like in practice?"],[165.71,166.8,"Four rules."],[167.3,169.26,"First, same information."],[169.26,173.11,"The same documents, at the same time, with the same deadline for everyone."],[173.11,177.65,"If you extend the deadline for one bidder, you extend it for all."],[178.14,179.72,"Second, one channel."],[179.72,182.8,"All questions go through the Coupa Message Center."],[182.8,189.73,"Every exchange there is logged and triggers email notifications, and that log is your equal-treatment evidence."],[189.73,195.25,"If a question is asked by phone, ask the supplier to put it in the Message Center."],[195.75,197.56,"Third, shared answers."],[197.56,201.12,"When an answer matters to everyone, send it to everyone, anonymised."],[201.12,210.53,"If one bidder asks whether the headquarters lot includes the underground car park, every bidder needs that answer, without knowing who asked."],[211.04,214.42,"Fourth, no side channels, above all with the incumbent."],[214.42,217.56,"The most common breach is not the procurement team."],[217.56,224.13,"It is the incumbent calling the business owner directly, over coffee, to share a few thoughts about the tender."],[224.13,230.28,"So brief your stakeholders before issue: during the event, all contact goes through procurement."],[230.78,237.06,"And one more principle from the note: clarifications explain a bid, they do not let a bidder change it."],[237.06,240.56,"You may ask a bidder what it meant by its staffing table."],[240.56,245.27,"You may not let it send a new staffing table because it has heard its price is high."],[245.55,254.38,"In the UK Crown Commercial Service consultancy framework there is no negotiation after bids, only transparent clarification."],[254.38,259.73,"In a private event you may plan negotiation rounds, but only if the RFx said so."],[260.68,263.97,"Now the evaluation process itself, in six steps."],[264.47,266.61,"Step one, the compliance check."],[266.61,270.94,"Is the bid complete, and does it pass every knock-out criterion?"],[270.94,275.26,"A bid without the wage compliance declaration does not go further."],[275.76,278.02,"Step two, individual scoring."],[278.02,285.12,"About three evaluators score the quality responses on their own, using the descriptors, and without seeing prices."],[285.12,286.03,"Why without prices?"],[286.03,294.02,"Because once you know a bid is cheap, it is very hard not to read its quality answers more kindly, or more harshly."],[294.3,298.12,"Before they start, evaluators declare any conflicts of interest."],[298.62,300.98,"Step three, the consensus meeting."],[300.98,308.39,"The evaluators discuss the gaps between their scores and agree one score for each criterion, with a written rationale."],[308.39,313.36,"The rationale is what you will use later when a losing bidder asks for a debrief."],[313.86,319.62,"Step four, the commercial evaluation, using the published formula on a total cost basis."],[320.12,322.58,"Step five, the sensitivity test."],[322.58,324.49,"Would reasonable changes alter the winner?"],[324.49,327.99,"We do this in a few minutes with the Nordvia bids."],[328.49,333.48,"And step six, the report: the evaluation report and the award recommendation."],[333.99,343.91,"Individual scoring by about three evaluators followed by consensus is the CCS consultancy guidance, and it is good private-sector practice too."],[343.91,346.17,"One practical note on Coupa."],[346.45,356.24,"Whether Coupa offers a formal sealed envelope setting, where technical and commercial parts are opened separately, is not confirmed in its public documentation."],[356.24,358.36,"Confirm it in your Coupa tenant."],[358.64,369.39,"If it is not available, keep prices apart by role: the evaluators who score quality simply do not get access to the pricing section until the quality scores are agreed."],[370.33,373.87,"Here are the four bids for the Nordvia headquarters cleaning lot."],[373.87,375.23,"They are fictional."],[375.23,385.03,"Quality is scored from zero to one hundred after consensus of three evaluators, and the published model is 50 percent quality and 50 percent price."],[385.53,387.7,"Bidder A is the incumbent."],[387.7,391.27,"Annual price 690 thousand euros, quality 62."],[391.27,394.22,"Same method as today, and staff continuity."],[394.22,396.87,"Bidder B: 610 thousand, quality 74."],[396.87,401.78,"An output-based plan, with robot floor care at night."],[401.78,404.48,"Bidder C: 520 thousand, quality 58."],[404.48,408.61,"And a note that its hours look below the wage floor."],[408.89,411.74,"Bidder D: 655 thousand, quality 81."],[411.74,421.71,"The strongest quality and ESG plan, but it requires a four-year term, while the published structure is three years plus one plus one."],[422.22,424.6,"Before we calculate anything, make a guess."],[424.6,426.36,"Which bidder do you think wins?"],[426.36,427.72,"Write your guess down."],[427.72,429.37,"We will come back to it at the end."],[429.86,434.55,"Before you guess, read the notes column carefully, because each bid tells a story."],[434.55,441.06,"A is the comfortable choice: nothing changes, including the quality complaints that started this project."],[441.06,448.4,"B proposes a different way of working, which could fix the quality problem and lower the price, but brings change risk."],[448.68,452.74,"C is very cheap, with the weakest plan and a warning sign."],[452.74,458.79,"D is the best plan, at a moderate price, but it asks for a term that differs from what was published."],[458.79,463.65,"In a real event, each of those notes becomes a question for clarification."],[464.61,466.42,"Step one, price scores."],[466.42,475.06,"We use the published formula, the relative one: price score equals lowest price divided by bid price, times one hundred."],[475.06,477.82,"The lowest price is 520, from Bidder C."],[478.31,481.08,"Let us do the first one together."],[481.08,486.89,"Bidder A: 520 divided by 690 is about 0.754."],[486.89,489.06,"Times one hundred, 75.4."],[489.06,494.48,"Now try Bidder B yourself before I say it."],[494.48,495.97,"520 divided by 610."],[495.97,498.42,"The answer is 85.2."],[498.42,503.54,"Bidder C is the lowest, so it scores exactly 100."],[503.54,509.15,"And Bidder D: 520 divided by 655, which gives 79.4."],[509.65,515.14,"So the price scores are A 75.4, B 85.2, C 100 and D 79.4."],[515.14,518.96,"Notice how much C's low price shapes this."],[518.96,525.35,"Because C sets the reference, every other score is measured against 520."],[525.35,526.82,"Keep that in mind."],[527.78,530.1,"Step two, total scores at 50 50."],[530.1,533.99,"The total is half the quality score plus half the price score."],[534.49,541.56,"For D: half of 81 is 40.5, half of 79.4 is 39.7, total 80.2."],[541.56,548.86,"For B: half of 74 is 37, half of 85.2 is 42.6, total 79.6."],[548.86,554.69,"For C: half of 58 is 29, plus 50, total 79.0."],[554.69,558.83,"And A: 31 plus 37.7, total 68.7."],[559.33,560.38,"Look at the bars."],[560.38,563.96,"D, B and C are within 1.2 points of each other."],[563.96,565.58,"A is clearly behind."],[565.58,566.17,"So is D the winner?"],[566.17,568.52,"On paper, yes, by a small margin."],[568.52,576.86,"But a gap of 1.2 points on a hundred-point scale, when each quality score comes from human judgement, is not a clear winner."],[576.86,580.49,"And one of the three leading bids has a warning note."],[580.77,583.35,"Before we go further, we have to deal with C."],[583.85,586.07,"And notice where the incumbent sits."],[586.07,590.14,"Bidder A, with 68.7, is more than ten points behind."],[590.14,595.47,"That is a useful message for the business owner who feared losing the incumbent."],[595.47,600.72,"The fair model gave A full credit for its quality, and A still did not come close."],[601.0,605.1,"Its price was the highest and its plan did not address the complaints."],[605.1,611.46,"When the business owner asks why the incumbent lost, the model gives you a clear, documented answer."],[612.41,614.8,"Let us talk about abnormally low bids."],[614.8,619.15,"Four things to know: the signal, the duty, the test and the decision."],[619.65,624.4,"The signal: a bid far below the others, or below a should-cost model."],[624.4,628.08,"One bid much cheaper than the rest is not proof of anything."],[628.08,630.92,"It may be a genuinely more efficient supplier."],[630.92,633.22,"But it is a signal you must follow up."],[633.72,638.21,"The duty: ask the bidder in writing to explain, as CCS guidance does."],[638.21,645.05,"In writing, through the Message Center, so it is on the record and so the bidder has a fair chance to answer."],[645.55,648.68,"The test: build or use a should-cost model."],[648.68,661.14,"For cleaning, that means hours, times the collective wage, plus statutory on-costs such as holidays and sickness, plus supervision, plus consumables and equipment, plus overhead, plus margin."],[661.42,666.63,"If the bid price cannot cover that sum for the hours the work needs, something does not add up."],[667.13,671.76,"And the decision: accept with evidence, or reject and record why."],[671.76,675.52,"Never reject automatically, and never award blindly."],[675.52,682.15,"In cleaning and security, a bid below the wage floor is both a service-failure risk and a legal risk."],[682.43,691.28,"Staff are underpaid or hours are cut, turnover rises, quality falls, and the insurer's name ends up attached to a labour-law problem."],[691.78,697.95,"Let me give you an illustrative calculation, with invented round numbers, to show how the test works."],[697.95,702.79,"Suppose a site needs about 200 productive cleaning hours a week."],[702.79,708.76,"Suppose the collective wage plus statutory on-costs comes to about 20 euros an hour."],[709.04,719.03,"That is about 4,000 euros a week, or roughly 208 thousand euros a year, for labour alone, before supervision, consumables, overhead and margin."],[719.03,727.17,"If a bid for that site is 180 thousand euros a year, it cannot cover the wage floor for the hours needed."],[727.45,733.0,"Either the bidder plans fewer hours, or it plans to pay less than the agreement requires."],[733.0,736.88,"Both are problems you want to find before award, not after."],[737.83,740.24,"So let us put Bidder C under the microscope."],[740.74,741.62,"Its price is 520."],[741.62,752.0,"That is about 15 percent below the next bid, B at 610, and about 20 percent below the average of the other three, which is about 652."],[752.0,756.85,"The data note in the case says 18 percent below the others."],[756.85,760.65,"Depending on the comparison it is 15 or 20 percent."],[760.65,762.97,"Either way, it is a red flag."],[763.47,767.37,"Its quality score is 58, the weakest plan of the four."],[767.37,773.39,"And its staffing hours look below what the wage floor allows for the area to be cleaned."],[773.39,779.43,"So we have three signals pointing the same way: very low price, weakest plan, implausible hours."],[779.93,785.44,"The action: a written request to explain its hours, wage rates and supervision."],[785.44,796.42,"For example: please explain how many productive hours per week your price assumes for the headquarters, the hourly wage and on-costs you have applied, and how supervision is staffed."],[796.42,799.73,"Then compare the answer with the should-cost model."],[800.01,806.79,"If the explanation holds, perhaps C uses a smarter shift pattern, you may accept the bid, and record why."],[806.79,812.34,"If the explanation fails, you reject it as abnormally low and record the evidence."],[813.29,815.29,"Step three, sensitivity."],[815.29,819.38,"This table shows what happens to the totals under different scenarios."],[819.38,823.32,"All figures are fictional, using the relative price formula."],[823.81,830.34,"The first row is our base case: 50 50 with all bids."],[830.34,834.24,"D 80.2, B 79.6, C 79.0, A 68.7."],[834.75,839.86,"Second row, 60 percent quality and 40 percent price."],[839.86,844.19,"D rises to 80.4, B falls to 78.5, and C drops to 74.8."],[844.19,849.06,"With more weight on quality, D leads more clearly."],[849.56,853.46,"Third row, 40 percent quality and 60 percent price."],[853.46,858.74,"Now C wins with 83.2, ahead of B with 80.7 and D with 80.0."],[858.74,864.79,"In other words, if we cared a bit more about price, the doubtful bid would win."],[865.29,868.76,"Now the last two rows, without C."],[868.76,878.46,"Remember the footnote: without C, the lowest price becomes 610, B's price, and every price score moves."],[878.46,881.75,"At 50 50, D scores 87.1 and B 87.0."],[881.75,884.17,"Almost a perfect tie."],[884.17,886.35,"At 60 40, D 85.9 and B 84.4."],[886.85,887.95,"So what have we learned?"],[887.95,888.99,"Two things."],[888.99,892.48,"First, C, the doubtful bid, moves the whole table."],[892.48,898.32,"Its presence shifts every price score, and under a price-heavy weighting it would win."],[898.32,903.96,"Second, once C is resolved, the real contest is between B and D, and it is very close."],[904.91,908.75,"So what does the sensitivity test tell you, and what does it not tell you?"],[908.75,910.86,"Look at the left-hand column first."],[911.36,913.87,"It does not tell you to change the weights now."],[913.87,917.99,"The weights were published at 50 50, and they stay at 50 50."],[917.99,922.35,"It does not tell you that the highest number wins regardless."],[922.35,925.53,"And it does not tell you that C should be ignored silently."],[925.81,933.55,"If C is excluded, that must be a documented decision based on its explanation, not a quiet deletion from the spreadsheet."],[934.05,936.67,"Now the right-hand column, highlighted."],[936.67,940.42,"It tells you that the decision is close between B and D."],[940.42,946.09,"It tells you that C must be resolved first, because it moves every price score."],[946.09,953.25,"It tells you to clarify, then negotiate or ask for a BAFO with B and D, if your RFx allowed for that step."],[953.53,958.35,"And it tells you to say in the report that the result is sensitive."],[958.35,959.53,"That is a finding, not a failure."],[959.53,967.04,"And there is one more open point: D's request for a four-year term, against the published three plus one plus one."],[967.04,971.92,"That has to be clarified with D too, on the same terms you would apply to anyone."],[972.42,976.42,"The principle from the note: sensitivity informs the next step."],[976.42,978.12,"It never rewrites the model."],[979.07,981.44,"In the live session we poll the room here."],[981.44,987.69,"Bidder C is about 20 percent below the average of the others, and its hours look below the wage floor."],[987.69,989.12,"What is your first step?"],[989.12,989.95,"Award it?"],[989.95,991.36,"Exclude it at once?"],[991.36,995.56,"Ask for a written explanation and check it against a should-cost model?"],[995.56,997.27,"Or tell the others to match it?"],[997.55,1002.22,"Think about your answer, and also about why immediate exclusion is risky."],[1002.22,1007.87,"The expected answer is the written explanation, checked against a should-cost model."],[1007.87,1015.36,"Excluding C at once, without asking, could exclude a genuinely efficient supplier and expose you to a challenge."],[1015.64,1018.9,"And telling the others to match it would break equal treatment."],[1019.85,1023.53,"Before any award, you run due diligence on the preferred bidders."],[1023.53,1024.69,"Four areas."],[1025.19,1032.18,"Financial: the supplier's standing, its dependency on your contract, and its insurance cover."],[1032.18,1038.86,"If your contract would be half of a small cleaner's revenue, that is a risk for both of you."],[1038.86,1045.26,"Information security: ISO 27001 or a SOC 2 report, access control, and where data is located."],[1045.54,1048.44,"Even a cleaner gets badges for secure floors."],[1048.44,1054.18,"Labour and ESG: wage compliance, subcontractors, and the code of conduct."],[1054.18,1060.58,"And references: similar sites or services, checked by phone, not only collected as a list."],[1061.09,1063.12,"The note makes an important point."],[1063.12,1068.01,"Due diligence runs on the preferred bidders, before award, as gates."],[1068.01,1070.21,"It is not a weighted criterion."],[1070.21,1073.89,"You do not give B three extra points for good references."],[1073.89,1078.07,"You check that B passes, and if it does not, it cannot be awarded."],[1078.57,1084.46,"For the Nordvia cleaning lot, due diligence would run on B and D, the two preferred bidders."],[1084.46,1093.16,"For B, the robot floor care at night raises a practical question: who has access to the building at night, and how are those people vetted?"],[1093.44,1099.95,"For D, the request for a four-year term invites a financial question: why does it need the longer term?"],[1099.95,1104.71,"Perhaps it plans a large investment in equipment that it needs to recover."],[1104.71,1109.16,"That is a legitimate reason, but you want to understand it before the BAFO."],[1110.11,1115.31,"For an insurer, some services also pass through regulatory gates before award."],[1115.31,1117.45,"This table shows the main ones."],[1117.95,1121.62,"If the service is an ICT service, DORA applies."],[1121.62,1125.63,"Article 28, paragraph 4, requires a pre-contract assessment."],[1125.63,1130.11,"Does the service support a critical or important function?"],[1130.11,1134.65,"Have all relevant risks been identified, including concentration risk?"],[1134.65,1137.64,"Has due diligence on the provider been done?"],[1137.64,1139.45,"Are there conflicts of interest?"],[1139.96,1147.24,"If the ICT service supports a critical or important function, Article 30, paragraph 3, adds requirements."],[1147.24,1156.64,"The specific contract clauses must be accepted, an exit strategy must be feasible, and the supervisor must be informed in time."],[1157.14,1175.23,"If you outsource a critical or important function under Solvency II, Article 274 of the Delegated Regulation requires a detailed examination of the provider's ability, capacity and any authorisation required, and sets terms for the written agreement."],[1175.73,1182.02,"And if the supplier processes personal data, you need a data processing agreement and a security review."],[1182.52,1188.91,"Most soft facility management categories, like our cleaning lot, are usually not critical outsourcing."],[1188.91,1199.06,"But Nordvia's network provider Telmo, whose services are used by the claims systems, and Northbyte, which runs the claims IT managed service, may well be."],[1199.06,1203.15,"The assessment is the insurer's own, and it must be documented."],[1203.65,1210.41,"In practice, this means the regulatory gate has to be planned into your timeline from module 3.1."],[1210.41,1216.84,"A DORA assessment and supervisor notification take time, and they cannot start the day before signature."],[1216.84,1222.55,"If your project touches one of these services, put the gate in your backwards plan now."],[1223.5,1226.18,"All of this ends up in the evaluation report."],[1226.18,1227.38,"Four sections."],[1227.89,1234.48,"Process: who evaluated, the conflicts declared, the timeline, and the clarifications asked and answered."],[1234.48,1240.46,"Results: the scores per criterion with their rationale, the price scores and the totals."],[1240.46,1247.26,"Tests: the sensitivity analysis, how the abnormally low bid was handled, and the due diligence results."],[1247.54,1253.49,"And the recommendation: the next step or the award, with conditions, risks and the approvals needed."],[1253.99,1258.62,"For the Nordvia cleaning lot, the recommendation is probably not yet an award."],[1258.62,1272.55,"It might read: Bidder C asked to explain its price; subject to that, B and D are within a fraction of a point and are invited to a BAFO under the published criteria, with D asked to confirm the published term."],[1272.83,1277.55,"Write the report as if a losing bidder will ask for a debrief, because one will."],[1277.55,1280.92,"Session 4 covers the award and the debriefing."],[1281.86,1285.28,"Now it is your turn to do the full calculation."],[1285.28,1288.76,"Practice 3.3 in your workbook takes about twenty minutes."],[1288.76,1293.85,"Compute the price scores with lowest divided by bid times one hundred."],[1293.85,1296.8,"Compute the totals at 50 50 and at 60 40."],[1296.8,1298.14,"Recompute without Bidder C."],[1298.14,1302.55,"Name the abnormally low bid and write what you will ask it."],[1302.83,1305.08,"And write what the sensitivity tells you."],[1305.08,1307.95,"The workbook checks the numbers automatically."],[1307.95,1312.6,"If you finish early, compute the scores with the linear formula as a bonus."],[1312.6,1317.34,"Pause the video now and do it in the Modules tab if you want, then continue."],[1317.84,1321.42,"A word on why this exercise matters beyond cleaning."],[1321.42,1337.57,"The same steps apply to an IT services lot, a marketing agency selection or a legal panel: price scores from the published formula, totals at the published weights, a check of any outlier, and a sensitivity test before you recommend anything."],[1337.85,1342.8,"Once you have done it by hand once, you will read every evaluation spreadsheet differently."],[1343.76,1344.91,"Let us debrief."],[1344.91,1346.31,"Who guessed D at the start?"],[1346.31,1347.7,"Who guessed C?"],[1347.7,1350.45,"Go back to the guess you wrote down at the beginning."],[1350.45,1354.14,"How did excluding C change the gap between B and D?"],[1354.14,1356.34,"It closed to a tenth of a point at 50 50."],[1356.34,1360.36,"Would you tell the business owner who is winning at this stage?"],[1360.36,1361.47,"I would be careful."],[1361.75,1369.16,"With a gap that small and a BAFO to come, saying D is winning creates expectations, and it can leak to bidders."],[1369.16,1373.32,"And what would you put in the evaluation report about sensitivity?"],[1373.32,1381.33,"The point of this debrief is simple: intuition and the model often differ, and the model is the one that must be defensible."],[1382.28,1385.25,"Here is module 3.3 in three lines."],[1385.25,1390.83,"First, same information, one channel, no side doors: fairness is designed."],[1390.83,1395.51,"Second, score individually, agree by consensus, keep prices apart."],[1395.51,1401.25,"Third, resolve abnormally low bids and test sensitivity before you recommend."],[1401.74,1405.6,"Let me recap the three lessons of this session so far."],[1405.6,1413.16,"In module 3.1 you chose the event sequence from your route to market and planned it backwards from the notice deadline."],[1413.16,1422.41,"In module 3.2 you designed the request: specification, lots, pricing schedule, gates, weights and a tested formula, all fixed before issue."],[1422.69,1433.19,"And in this module you ran the event fairly, scored the bids, handled an abnormally low one, and saw that the honest result was a close contest between B and D."],[1433.69,1436.89,"Which leaves a question for the next module."],[1436.89,1439.32,"Would an e-auction help us choose between B and D?"],[1439.32,1440.63,"Or would it hurt?"],[1440.63,1448.75,"Module 3.4 looks at the evidence on e-auctions, when they work, when they damage relationships, and which format to choose."],[1448.75,1449.73,"See you there."]]},{"id":"s3-l4","session":"s3","title":"E-auctions: when they work and when they hurt","summary":"Decide whether a category passes the e-auction preconditions, choose the right Coupa format, and write the rules you would publish.","from":64,"to":85,"audio":"audio/s3-l4.mp3","duration":1542.7,"segs":[[64,0.6,18.9,"Welcome back. This lesson covers module 3.4 of Session 3: e-auctions, when they work and when they hurt. The subtitle on this slide is the whole argument in one line. An e-auction is a sharp tool for well-specified leverage categories, and a blunt one everywhere else."],[64,19.4,59.84,"Here is the plan. We start with what an auction actually is, in negotiation terms. Then we walk through the formats, with special attention to the three that Coupa documents, because one of them works the opposite way to what most people expect. After that we look at the research: what Jap found about open and sealed bids, the preconditions checklist, the numbers, and three warnings from Emiliani and Stec, from Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis, and from European public procurement law. We finish with misuse cases, the rules to publish, auction ethics, and a suitability check on four Nordvia categories, which you will then do yourself."],[65,60.79,65.42,"Let's start with the statement. An e-auction automates distributive bargaining."],[65,65.92,102.29,"Distributive bargaining is the term Walton and McKersie used for negotiating over a fixed pie. Whatever the supplier gains, we lose, and the other way round. Price is the classic example. In a traditional negotiation, the buyer and the supplier haggle: offer, counter-offer, pause, phone call, another offer. An auction replaces all of that with real-time feedback. Every supplier sees where it stands and decides, in minutes, whether to move. That is its strength. It is fast, it is transparent, and it reveals the market price for a well-defined object."],[65,102.79,129.31,"And it is also its limit. An auction cannot create value. It cannot trade a longer term for a better service level, or a change in our specification for a lower cost to serve. Those multi-issue trade-offs are integrative bargaining, and they need conversation. So the first question for any auction is simple. Is this a fixed pie? If the honest answer is no, an auction will squeeze the pie rather than grow it."],[66,130.26,154.11,"Now a quick poll. How many e-auctions has your team run in the last year? None, one to three, four to ten, more than ten, or you don't know. Take a moment to think about your own answer. If it is none, this lesson will give you the mechanics. If it is many, pay particular attention to the misuse cases later, because experienced teams are the ones most tempted to use the tool everywhere."],[67,155.06,162.98,"This table shows the auction formats. Three columns: the format, the mechanics, and what it is best for. Let's go row by row."],[67,163.48,179.33,"First, the English reverse auction. Suppliers bid down, and the lowest bid at the close wins. This is what most people picture when they hear the word auction. It is best for leverage categories with four or more suppliers and a fixed specification."],[67,179.83,197.84,"Second, the Dutch reverse auction, as Coupa defines it. Here the price starts low and rises, and the first supplier to accept wins. It is good for speed, for simple bundles, and for testing where the floor of the market really is. We will come back to this one, because it surprises people."],[67,198.34,210.32,"Third, the reverse Japanese auction. The price falls by steps, every supplier must accept each step to stay in, and the last active bidder wins. It suits incumbent-heavy markets."],[67,210.82,253.16,"Fourth, the sealed bid. One blind bid each, all opened together. This is the classic tender, and strictly speaking it is not a live auction at all. It is best when the relationship must survive the process. And fifth, the multi-attribute auction, where a formula re-ranks price and other attributes live. It only works when quality has been scored first. And notice the last words in that cell: confirm in tenant. The first three formats are documented by Coupa. Multi-attribute live auctions are not clearly documented in core Coupa Sourcing, so please do not promise them to a business owner until your Coupa administrator has confirmed what your tenant does."],[68,254.11,257.84,"Let's look closer at the English reverse auction, in four cards."],[68,258.34,278.43,"The mechanics: suppliers bid down, and the lowest bid at close wins. The Coupa rules: no price increases, and no very small improvements. That second rule matters. Without it, a supplier could win by undercutting the leader by one cent, again and again, and the auction would drag on without discovering anything."],[68,278.93,306.2,"Feedback: the buyer chooses what suppliers see. Either their rank, or the difference between their bid and the best bid. And the last card is the warning. Full price visibility fuels price wars. Jap's research in 2007 is the reason many buyers show only rank. When a supplier sees a competitor's exact price, the event becomes emotional. People bid to beat a rival, not to win a contract they can deliver profitably."],[68,306.7,330.98,"One more design choice is how the auction closes. The literature describes a hard close, where the auction simply ends at a fixed time, and a soft close, where a late bid extends the auction by a few minutes so that everyone can respond. A soft close removes the incentive to snipe in the last second. Coupa's exact extension parameters are something you must confirm in your tenant."],[69,331.93,351.22,"Now the format that surprises people. Here is the definition from Coupa's supplier documentation of the Dutch reverse auction: a buyer-controlled clock auction. The buyer sets a low starting price, which rises over time by a pre-set amount. The first supplier to accept the call-out price wins."],[69,351.72,386.71,"Why does this surprise people? Because many web sources describe a Dutch auction as a falling price. That is true, but it is the selling version, the one used to sell flowers. A seller starts high and lowers the price until a buyer accepts. In a reverse auction, we are the buyer, so the logic flips. Coupa starts low, at a price no supplier would accept, and raises it step by step. The first supplier who decides that price is acceptable presses accept and wins. In Coupa, bidding is on total cost, and all items are awarded together."],[69,387.21,431.44,"The whole format depends on one number: the start price. It needs to be well researched. Set it too low and the clock may run for a long time, or nobody accepts at all. Set it too high and the first supplier accepts immediately, and you overpay. So a Dutch reverse auction only makes sense when you have a good should-cost model or a recent benchmark. Imagine a simple bundle of printer toner for all Nordvia offices. You know the market roughly from last year's RFQ. A Dutch reverse auction gives you speed and certainty, and it tells you where the first supplier is willing to stop. It does not tell you how low the second supplier might have gone. That is the money it can leave on the table."],[70,432.39,435.8,"The third Coupa format is the reverse Japanese auction."],[70,436.3,453.28,"The start is above the expected buying price, so everyone can accept the first step. Then the price falls by steps. At each step, suppliers must actively accept the new price to stay in. When a supplier stops accepting, it drops out. The last active bidder wins."],[70,453.78,492.21,"Where does it fit? In incumbent-heavy markets, and wherever you want less visible price-war emotion. Nobody sees a rival's price. Each supplier only answers one question at every step: am I still in at this price? Practitioners tend to use it with roughly five to eight suppliers, and that is an indicative range, not a rule. There is one caution in the speaker notes. Exit points are revealed, so suppliers learn when rivals drop out. In a concentrated market with a few players who know each other, that information can ease tacit coordination. Keep that in mind in categories such as telecom."],[71,493.16,526.4,"Here is another poll to check the definition. In Coupa, a Dutch reverse auction starts high and the price falls, starts low and the price rises until a supplier accepts, is the same as an English reverse auction, or is not available. Answer it in your head before I tell you. The correct answer is the second one. It starts low and rises until a supplier accepts. If you picked the first option, you are in good company, and now you know why: the falling version is the selling auction, not Coupa's reverse one."],[72,527.35,532.96,"Now the research that should shape your format choice. Open or sealed? What did Jap find?"],[72,533.46,558.78,"Sandy Jap studied six auctions covering about 100 million US dollars of contracts. She compared open-bid auctions, where suppliers see prices live, with sealed-bid formats. Look at the left-hand column first. Open bidding creates live price pressure. But it raises supplier suspicion that the buyer is being opportunistic, and if it is overused it erodes cooperation."],[72,559.28,600.29,"Now the right-hand column, which is highlighted for a reason. A sealed bid gives you no live price discovery, and it does less damage to trust. And here is the key finding: buyer savings did not differ between the formats. Read that again. The format that damages trust more did not produce more savings. So when the relationship matters after the award, the visible price war buys you nothing extra. Jap also observed that auctions used sparingly can act as a wake-up call to a complacent supply base. Used constantly, they teach suppliers to protect themselves: higher opening prices, less investment, less sharing of ideas."],[73,601.24,610.84,"This is the slide to photograph: the preconditions checklist. Six conditions. And the rule in the speaker notes is strict. One no is enough to stop."],[73,611.34,638.92,"First, a precise specification, so precise that bids can be ranked automatically. If two bids at the same price would need a human to say which is better, the specification is not precise enough for an auction. Second, at least four to five qualified bidders. That figure comes from Smeltzer and Carr in 2003. Qualified means they have passed your knock-out questions and quality gates, not that they have downloaded the documents."],[73,639.42,661.78,"Third, a market with spare capacity, and an account suppliers want. If suppliers are full, they will not chase your volume. If your account is small or difficult, they will not fight for it. Fourth, price, or a published formula, decides. All non-price elements must be evaluated before the auction starts. The auction is not the place to discover quality."],[73,662.28,686.39,"Fifth, low switching cost, and a relationship that can tolerate an auction. If changing supplier would take eighteen months and a data migration, the incumbent knows it, and the auction is theatre. Sixth, a credible BATNA, your best alternative to a negotiated agreement, and a real intention to award as announced. If you would never actually move the business, do not pretend."],[73,686.89,719.9,"Let's test it quickly on a familiar example. Standard laptops for a refresh programme across several entities. Precise specification? Yes, a model and configuration. Bidders? Several resellers. Spare capacity? Usually. Price decides? After the technical gate, yes. Switching cost? Low for a reseller. Intention to award? Yes. Six yeses: a good candidate. Now try the same check on a strategy consulting engagement. It fails at the first line, and you can stop there."],[74,720.85,723.69,"What does the evidence say in numbers? Four figures."],[74,724.19,742.19,"First, five to fifteen percent. That is the typical claimed price reduction, from Jap in 2002 and Smeltzer and Carr in 2003. Notice the word claimed. Second, four to five, the minimum number of qualified bidders to start, again from Smeltzer and Carr."],[74,742.69,775.65,"Third, more than one third. That is the share of United States federal reverse auctions in fiscal year 2012 that had no interactive bidding at all, according to the Government Accountability Office report GAO-14-108. Nobody bid against anybody. And the fourth figure explains why that hurts: the auction provider charged up to three percent of the winning bid. The lesson in the speaker notes is worth repeating. An auction with one bidder is just an expensive RFQ."],[74,776.15,793.48,"And look at the footnote. The academic figures are as summarised by Mora Cortez and colleagues in 2023, and they are a range, not a promise. If someone puts fifteen percent into a business case before the preconditions are checked, that is a hope, not a forecast."],[75,794.43,810.06,"Here is the first of three warnings. Emiliani and Stec, writing in 2002 in Supply Chain Management, an International Journal: the savings actually achieved are less than that reported by suppliers of online auction services."],[75,810.56,845.53,"Why the gap? Because the price drop on the auction screen is a gross figure. The net saving is what remains after switching costs, quality problems, extra management time, and the internal effort to run the event. Picture a facilities buyer who auctions a courier contract and reports twelve percent. Six months later, missed deliveries require a second courier for urgent items, and the contract manager spends a day a week on complaints. The real saving is far smaller. So the practical rule is: report auction results net, not gross."],[76,846.48,863.17,"The second warning comes from economists. Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis, in 2009, studied private building contracts. Their conclusion: auctions perform poorly when projects are complex, contractual design is incomplete and there are few bidders."],[76,863.67,899.06,"They found something else too. Auctions stifle the communication between buyer and supplier that you need in order to use the contractor's expertise. In complex work, the supplier often knows a better way to do the job. A negotiation lets that knowledge reach you. An auction asks only for a number. That is the economic case for negotiation in complex services: IT development, transformation programmes, outsourcing. Three warning signs, then. Complexity, an incomplete contract, and few bidders. Any one of them should make you pause."],[77,900.01,921.28,"The third reference is the law. The European Union public procurement Directive of 2014, number 24, in Article 35, sets the rules for electronic auctions in public procurement. The Group is a private insurer, so this is reference practice rather than a rule we must follow. But it is an excellent heuristic."],[77,921.78,957.66,"Four cards. Public e-auctions require precisely established specifications. Non-price elements must be fully evaluated before the auction starts. The mathematical formula used to re-rank bids must be published in advance. And services with intellectual performances as their subject, such as design, are excluded entirely. Apply that test to our own categories. Creative agencies fail it. Consulting fails it. If a public authority would not be allowed to auction it, a private insurer should think very carefully before doing so."],[78,958.61,964.99,"With those three warnings in mind, here are the misuse cases. Five of them, and you will recognise most."],[78,965.49,1008.01,"One: intellectual services. Consulting, creative work and IT development cannot be ranked automatically. The cheapest strategy deck is rarely the best one, and the outcome cost of a weak consultant dwarfs the day rate you saved. Two: price-only labour services. In cleaning or security, the price is mostly wages. A price-only auction can push bids below sustainable wages, and the buyer then owns the consequences: high staff turnover, poor service, and possibly a reputational problem if the supplier breaks wage rules. Never run a price-only auction for these services without a quality threshold and a should-cost floor."],[78,1008.51,1024.53,"Three: one or two bidders. There is no competition, only a fee. Four: the auction as a bluff. Running an auction with no intention to award, or with rules kept hidden, to scare the incumbent into a discount. Suppliers see through this quickly."],[78,1025.03,1063.22,"And five: overuse with partners. Liker and Choi, in the Harvard Business Review in 2004, described how Ford's use of reverse auctions and GM's contracts allowing a switch to a cheaper supplier at any moment left the US automakers' supplier relations worse than before the quality movement of the 1980s. Suppliers responded by sharing less and investing less with those customers. The lesson for us is not that auctions are bad. It is that a buyer who treats every supplier as a commodity eventually gets commodity behaviour from strategic partners too."],[79,1064.17,1071.3,"To be fair to the tool, here is the best case: aggregated commodities. A public example from the United Kingdom."],[79,1071.8,1098.66,"Thirty-four councils aggregated about 48,000 IT devices under a Crown Commercial Service framework. The further competition was concluded by an e-auction. The published result was 8.8 million pounds saved over two years, against a pre-auction value of 24.6 million pounds. That is about 36 percent. And the savings per basket ranged from 2 percent to 44 percent."],[79,1099.16,1145.4,"Why did it work so well? Look at the conditions. Huge aggregated volume, which makes the account very attractive. Standard devices with precise specifications. A framework of pre-qualified suppliers. And price as the deciding factor. Every precondition holds. That is why the last line matters most: these are best-case conditions, so do not transfer these percentages to services. The case study was published in 2016 and has since been withdrawn as older than five years, so treat it as an illustration of what aggregation plus an auction can do for commodities. Within the Group, the equivalent would be combining the laptop or mobile phone demand of several entities into one event."],[80,1146.35,1153.47,"If an auction passes the checklist, the next job is to publish the rules before it starts. This table lists five."],[80,1153.97,1188.79,"The start or reserve price, based on a should-cost model or a benchmark. The decrement, which is the minimum improvement per bid, and you must confirm how that setting works in your tenant. The closing rule: hard close or extensions, again confirm in your tenant. The feedback: rank, or difference to best bid. And the award rule, which is the one buyers most often forget. Is the best bid guaranteed to win? On which formula? If quality was scored in the RFP stage and the auction only moves price, say exactly how the two are combined."],[80,1189.29,1225.39,"Why all this effort? Because procedural fairness reduces perceived opportunism. That finding comes from Daly and Nath in 2005, as summarised by Mora Cortez and colleagues. Suppliers who understand the rules bid with more confidence and less padding. And one practical tip from the speaker notes: offer a mock auction to every supplier. A supplier who has never used the Coupa Supplier Portal for live bidding should not be learning the interface while the clock is running. A failed login during the real event is a fairness problem, not just a technical one."],[81,1226.34,1229.86,"Closely linked to the rules is auction ethics. Four cards."],[81,1230.36,1267.84,"No phantom bidders. Never fake competition, for example by inventing bids or inviting a supplier you know will not bid just to make the room look full. No bluff. Run an auction only if you will award as announced. No post-auction haggling. The auction result is the price. If you call the winner the next morning to ask for another two percent, every supplier will hold back next time, because they learn that the auction is not the final round. And codes of conduct: Emiliani in 2005 described codes covering transparency, security, confidentiality and auditing."],[81,1268.34,1275.13,"The speaker note says it best. Suppliers talk to each other. One unethical auction makes the next ten harder."],[82,1276.08,1283.86,"Now let's bring it home to Nordvia. This table checks four categories against everything we have discussed. The case is fictional."],[82,1284.36,1320.06,"First row, P5, office supplies and print. Verdict: an English reverse auction after an RFQ. Why? Precise items, many suppliers, and price decides. The RFQ qualifies the suppliers and validates the item list, then the auction discovers the price. Second row, P1, office cleaning. Verdict: no price-only auction. It is labour-intensive, quality comes first, and there are few strong bidders. You will see in the case at the end of this session that only two strong bids remain for the HQ lot once the doubtful one is dealt with."],[82,1320.56,1342.91,"Third row, P7, creative agencies. Verdict: no auction. These are intellectual services, and the Article 35 logic applies directly. Fourth row, P4, network and telecom with Telmo. Verdict: possible on standard tariffs, with care. Only with four or more qualified providers, and only after the DORA assessment is done."],[82,1343.41,1378.07,"That last row is deliberately open. Nordvia's claims systems use Telmo's network, so the service may support a critical or important function under DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act. If it does, the regulatory gates, exit planning and contract clauses come first, and price competition sits on top of them, not instead of them. Standard mobile tariffs might pass. The core network that claims handling depends on might not. Reasonable teams can disagree here, and that disagreement is exactly what you should explore in the practice."],[83,1379.02,1385.01,"Which brings us to Practice 3.4, the auction suitability check. Fifteen minutes in your workbook."],[83,1385.51,1424.04,"Check P5, P1, P7 and P4 against the preconditions. Then add your own project as row five. For each row, choose a format, or write no auction. And if you chose an auction for your own project, write the rules you would publish: feedback, closing and the award rule. One instruction from the facilitator notes: write the number of qualified bidders as a number. Not several. Not a few. A number. If you cannot write a number, you do not know whether the auction will work. Pause the lesson now if you would like to do the exercise in the Modules tab of your workbook, and continue when you are ready."],[84,1424.99,1430.05,"Welcome back. Here are the debrief questions. Think about your own answers as I go."],[84,1430.55,1480.33,"Where did you hesitate, and on which precondition? Most people hesitate on Telmo, and usually on the number of qualified providers or on switching cost. Did you choose an auction for your own project? If so, defend it against all six lines of the checklist. Would an auction between B and D in the cleaning case help? The answer in the facilitator notes is no. Two strong bidders, a labour-intensive service, and a relationship you need after the award. A best and final offer, a BAFO, does the job without the price-war risk. And finally: which format would your incumbent hate most, and why? Often it is the English reverse auction with visible prices, because the incumbent's margin is exposed in public. That tells you something about how the relationship would look the morning after."],[85,1481.28,1484.35,"Let's recap module 3.4 in three lines."],[85,1484.85,1516.68,"One. Auctions work for precise specifications, enough bidders and price-led decisions. Check all six preconditions, and remember that one no is enough to stop. Two. Expect five to fifteen percent at best, and report savings net. That is the lesson from Jap and from Emiliani and Stec. Three. Know Coupa's formats: the English reverse auction, the Dutch reverse auction that rises from a low start, and the reverse Japanese auction, where suppliers drop out as the price falls."],[85,1517.18,1541.54,"And underneath all three: the auction is a tool that follows the strategy, never the other way round. In the next lesson, we put all of this into a Coupa event. We will look at the event types, the life of an event from template to contract, the pre-bidding phase, what Coupa documents and what you must confirm in your tenant, and we will build a set-up sheet for the Nordvia cleaning tender. See you there."]],"questions":[{"after":67,"q":"A Nordvia buyer has a fixed specification for toner and paper, six interested suppliers and a decision driven purely by price. Which format from the table fits best?","options":["Sealed bid, to protect the relationship","English reverse auction","Multi-attribute auction, as configured in core Coupa","No auction, because price-only events always damage trust"],"answer":1,"explain":"A fixed specification, four or more suppliers and a price-led decision are exactly what the English reverse auction is best for. Multi-attribute live auctions are not clearly documented in core Coupa Sourcing and would need confirming in the tenant.","t":253.16},{"after":69,"q":"You plan a Coupa Dutch reverse auction for a simple stationery bundle. Which input matters most to get right before you launch?","options":["The minimum decrement between bids","The number of rounds after the auction","A well-researched start price, because the price rises from it until a supplier accepts","The rank feedback setting"],"answer":2,"explain":"In Coupa's Dutch reverse format the buyer's clock starts low and rises. Too low a start and nobody accepts; too high and the first supplier accepts at once and you overpay, so a should-cost or benchmark start price is essential.","t":431.44},{"after":72,"q":"The Facilities team wants an open-price English auction for a service whose supplier must work closely with Nordvia afterwards, arguing that visible prices maximise savings. What does Jap's research suggest?","options":["Open bidding raised supplier suspicion while buyer savings did not differ from sealed formats, so a sealed or rank-only design is safer","Open bidding produced clearly higher savings, so it is worth the trust cost","Sealed bids always produce lower prices than open auctions","Jap found that auctions improve cooperation when used often"],"answer":0,"explain":"Jap found that open-bid auctions damaged trust more than sealed ones while buyer savings did not differ. When the relationship matters after award, visible price wars add risk without extra savings.","t":600.29},{"after":73,"q":"An HR buyer proposes an auction for a recruitment platform. The specification is precise, price would decide, and the market has spare capacity, but only two providers passed the knock-out questions. What should she do?","options":["Run the auction anyway, because five of six conditions hold","Invite unqualified providers to make the auction look competitive","Run a Dutch reverse auction, which works with any number of bidders","Stop: with fewer than four to five qualified bidders the checklist fails, so use an RFQ or a negotiation"],"answer":3,"explain":"One no on the checklist is enough to stop. With two qualified bidders there is no real competition, only an auction fee, and inviting unqualified bidders would create meaningless bids.","t":719.9},{"after":76,"q":"A business case for an IT development tender assumes 15 percent savings from an e-auction on day rates. Which challenge is best supported by the evidence in this module?","options":["Fifteen percent is too low; aggregated commodity cases show 36 percent","The work is complex and incompletely specified, where auctions perform poorly and block the dialogue needed to use supplier expertise; and claimed reductions are a range, reported gross","Auctions are forbidden for IT in private companies","Day rates cannot be entered in Coupa"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis found auctions perform poorly for complex work with incomplete contracts and few bidders. The five to fifteen percent range is claimed and gross, and Emiliani and Stec showed realised savings are lower.","t":899.06},{"after":78,"q":"A colleague suggests a price-only reverse auction for security guarding across three Nordvia sites, with eight interested suppliers. What is the main risk to address first?","options":["Too many bidders will slow the auction","Security services are intellectual performances under Article 35","The incumbent will refuse to use the Supplier Portal","Bids can fall below sustainable wages, so a quality threshold and a should-cost floor are needed, or no price-only auction at all"],"answer":3,"explain":"In labour-intensive services the price is mostly wages. A price-only auction can drive bids below sustainable wage levels, which the buyer then owns as poor service, turnover and reputational risk.","t":1063.22},{"after":81,"q":"An English reverse auction for mobile handsets closes. The next morning, a stakeholder asks you to phone the winner for another two percent. What is the right response?","options":["Call the winner, because the auction only set a starting point","Call all bidders for a final round to be fair","Decline: the published rule is that the auction result is the price, and post-auction haggling damages every future event","Re-run the auction with a smaller decrement"],"answer":2,"explain":"Auction ethics say no post-auction haggling. If suppliers learn the auction is not the final round, they hold back in the next one, and one unethical auction makes the next ten harder.","t":1275.13},{"after":82,"q":"For Nordvia's P4 network and telecom services with Telmo, what must be in place before any auction on standard tariffs is considered?","options":["At least four qualified providers and the DORA assessment completed, since the service may support a critical function","Only a precise list of tariffs","Approval from the creative agency panel","A Dutch reverse auction start price set at the incumbent's current rate"],"answer":0,"explain":"Telmo's network supports claims systems, so it may support a critical or important function. Regulatory gates come first, and an auction is only possible on standard tariffs with four or more qualified providers.","t":1378.07},{"after":85,"q":"Which statement best summarises module 3.4?","options":["Auctions are the default route for all indirect categories in Coupa","Auctions work when the preconditions hold, deliver a modest net saving at best, and must use the right Coupa format with published rules","Auctions should be avoided because they always damage relationships","Auctions are only useful for public bodies under Article 35"],"answer":1,"explain":"The module's line is that auctions are a sharp tool for well-specified leverage categories: check the six preconditions, expect five to fifteen percent at best reported net, and choose and publish the format and rules in advance.","t":1541.54}],"cues":[[0.6,1.35,"Welcome back."],[1.35,8.11,"This lesson covers module 3.4 of Session 3: e-auctions, when they work and when they hurt."],[8.11,12.44,"The subtitle on this slide is the whole argument in one line."],[12.44,18.9,"An e-auction is a sharp tool for well-specified leverage categories, and a blunt one everywhere else."],[19.4,20.8,"Here is the plan."],[20.8,25.42,"We start with what an auction actually is, in negotiation terms."],[25.42,34.99,"Then we walk through the formats, with special attention to the three that Coupa documents, because one of them works the opposite way to what most people expect."],[35.27,50.21,"After that we look at the research: what Jap found about open and sealed bids, the preconditions checklist, the numbers, and three warnings from Emiliani and Stec, from Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis, and from European public procurement law."],[50.49,59.83,"We finish with misuse cases, the rules to publish, auction ethics, and a suitability check on four Nordvia categories, which you will then do yourself."],[60.79,62.45,"Let's start with the statement."],[62.45,65.42,"An e-auction automates distributive bargaining."],[65.92,71.72,"Distributive bargaining is the term Walton and McKersie used for negotiating over a fixed pie."],[71.72,76.49,"Whatever the supplier gains, we lose, and the other way round."],[76.49,77.97,"Price is the classic example."],[77.97,86.08,"In a traditional negotiation, the buyer and the supplier haggle: offer, counter-offer, pause, phone call, another offer."],[86.36,89.98,"An auction replaces all of that with real-time feedback."],[89.98,94.92,"Every supplier sees where it stands and decides, in minutes, whether to move."],[94.92,96.35,"That is its strength."],[96.35,102.3,"It is fast, it is transparent, and it reveals the market price for a well-defined object."],[102.79,104.44,"And it is also its limit."],[104.44,106.94,"An auction cannot create value."],[106.94,114.59,"It cannot trade a longer term for a better service level, or a change in our specification for a lower cost to serve."],[114.59,119.92,"Those multi-issue trade-offs are integrative bargaining, and they need conversation."],[119.92,122.95,"So the first question for any auction is simple."],[123.23,124.66,"Is this a fixed pie?"],[124.66,129.31,"If the honest answer is no, an auction will squeeze the pie rather than grow it."],[130.26,131.0,"Now a quick poll."],[131.0,134.77,"How many e-auctions has your team run in the last year?"],[134.77,138.2,"None, one to three, four to ten, more than ten, or you don't know."],[138.2,141.81,"Take a moment to think about your own answer."],[141.81,144.87,"If it is none, this lesson will give you the mechanics."],[145.15,154.11,"If it is many, pay particular attention to the misuse cases later, because experienced teams are the ones most tempted to use the tool everywhere."],[155.06,157.38,"This table shows the auction formats."],[157.38,161.8,"Three columns: the format, the mechanics, and what it is best for."],[161.8,162.97,"Let's go row by row."],[163.48,165.76,"First, the English reverse auction."],[165.76,168.81,"Suppliers bid down, and the lowest bid at the close wins."],[168.81,173.79,"This is what most people picture when they hear the word auction."],[173.79,179.33,"It is best for leverage categories with four or more suppliers and a fixed specification."],[179.83,183.43,"Second, the Dutch reverse auction, as Coupa defines it."],[183.43,187.77,"Here the price starts low and rises, and the first supplier to accept wins."],[187.77,194.68,"It is good for speed, for simple bundles, and for testing where the floor of the market really is."],[194.68,197.84,"We will come back to this one, because it surprises people."],[198.34,200.94,"Third, the reverse Japanese auction."],[200.94,208.16,"The price falls by steps, every supplier must accept each step to stay in, and the last active bidder wins."],[208.16,210.33,"It suits incumbent-heavy markets."],[210.82,212.37,"Fourth, the sealed bid."],[212.37,214.58,"One blind bid each, all opened together."],[214.58,220.56,"This is the classic tender, and strictly speaking it is not a live auction at all."],[220.56,224.09,"It is best when the relationship must survive the process."],[224.09,230.4,"And fifth, the multi-attribute auction, where a formula re-ranks price and other attributes live."],[230.68,233.59,"It only works when quality has been scored first."],[233.59,237.77,"And notice the last words in that cell: confirm in tenant."],[237.77,240.8,"The first three formats are documented by Coupa."],[241.08,253.15,"Multi-attribute live auctions are not clearly documented in core Coupa Sourcing, so please do not promise them to a business owner until your Coupa administrator has confirmed what your tenant does."],[254.11,257.84,"Let's look closer at the English reverse auction, in four cards."],[258.34,262.95,"The mechanics: suppliers bid down, and the lowest bid at close wins."],[262.95,267.94,"The Coupa rules: no price increases, and no very small improvements."],[267.94,268.65,"That second rule matters."],[268.65,278.44,"Without it, a supplier could win by undercutting the leader by one cent, again and again, and the auction would drag on without discovering anything."],[278.93,281.87,"Feedback: the buyer chooses what suppliers see."],[281.87,287.96,"Either their rank, or the difference between their bid and the best bid."],[287.96,288.53,"And the last card is the warning."],[288.53,291.01,"Full price visibility fuels price wars."],[291.01,296.08,"Jap's research in 2007 is the reason many buyers show only rank."],[296.36,301.4,"When a supplier sees a competitor's exact price, the event becomes emotional."],[301.4,306.2,"People bid to beat a rival, not to win a contract they can deliver profitably."],[306.7,309.81,"One more design choice is how the auction closes."],[309.81,321.64,"The literature describes a hard close, where the auction simply ends at a fixed time, and a soft close, where a late bid extends the auction by a few minutes so that everyone can respond."],[321.64,325.81,"A soft close removes the incentive to snipe in the last second."],[326.09,330.98,"Coupa's exact extension parameters are something you must confirm in your tenant."],[331.93,334.35,"Now the format that surprises people."],[334.35,342.09,"Here is the definition from Coupa's supplier documentation of the Dutch reverse auction: a buyer-controlled clock auction."],[342.09,347.8,"The buyer sets a low starting price, which rises over time by a pre-set amount."],[347.8,351.22,"The first supplier to accept the call-out price wins."],[351.72,353.35,"Why does this surprise people?"],[353.35,357.74,"Because many web sources describe a Dutch auction as a falling price."],[357.74,362.46,"That is true, but it is the selling version, the one used to sell flowers."],[362.46,366.68,"A seller starts high and lowers the price until a buyer accepts."],[366.68,370.6,"In a reverse auction, we are the buyer, so the logic flips."],[370.88,376.47,"Coupa starts low, at a price no supplier would accept, and raises it step by step."],[376.47,381.89,"The first supplier who decides that price is acceptable presses accept and wins."],[381.89,386.71,"In Coupa, bidding is on total cost, and all items are awarded together."],[387.21,390.56,"The whole format depends on one number: the start price."],[390.56,392.55,"It needs to be well researched."],[392.55,398.01,"Set it too low and the clock may run for a long time, or nobody accepts at all."],[398.01,402.8,"Set it too high and the first supplier accepts immediately, and you overpay."],[403.08,409.94,"So a Dutch reverse auction only makes sense when you have a good should-cost model or a recent benchmark."],[409.94,414.64,"Imagine a simple bundle of printer toner for all Nordvia offices."],[414.64,417.85,"You know the market roughly from last year's RFQ."],[418.13,425.06,"A Dutch reverse auction gives you speed and certainty, and it tells you where the first supplier is willing to stop."],[425.06,428.92,"It does not tell you how low the second supplier might have gone."],[428.92,431.44,"That is the money it can leave on the table."],[432.39,435.8,"The third Coupa format is the reverse Japanese auction."],[436.3,441.31,"The start is above the expected buying price, so everyone can accept the first step."],[441.31,443.5,"Then the price falls by steps."],[443.5,448.03,"At each step, suppliers must actively accept the new price to stay in."],[448.03,451.25,"When a supplier stops accepting, it drops out."],[451.25,453.28,"The last active bidder wins."],[453.78,454.73,"Where does it fit?"],[454.73,459.98,"In incumbent-heavy markets, and wherever you want less visible price-war emotion."],[459.98,462.35,"Nobody sees a rival's price."],[462.35,467.56,"Each supplier only answers one question at every step: am I still in at this price?"],[467.84,474.73,"Practitioners tend to use it with roughly five to eight suppliers, and that is an indicative range, not a rule."],[474.73,477.37,"There is one caution in the speaker notes."],[477.37,481.64,"Exit points are revealed, so suppliers learn when rivals drop out."],[481.92,488.96,"In a concentrated market with a few players who know each other, that information can ease tacit coordination."],[488.96,492.21,"Keep that in mind in categories such as telecom."],[493.16,496.42,"Here is another poll to check the definition."],[496.42,508.61,"In Coupa, a Dutch reverse auction starts high and the price falls, starts low and the price rises until a supplier accepts, is the same as an English reverse auction, or is not available."],[508.61,511.23,"Answer it in your head before I tell you."],[511.23,513.47,"The correct answer is the second one."],[513.75,516.8,"It starts low and rises until a supplier accepts."],[516.8,526.4,"If you picked the first option, you are in good company, and now you know why: the falling version is the selling auction, not Coupa's reverse one."],[527.35,530.59,"Now the research that should shape your format choice."],[530.59,531.71,"Open or sealed?"],[531.71,532.96,"What did Jap find?"],[533.46,539.36,"Sandy Jap studied six auctions covering about 100 million US dollars of contracts."],[539.36,545.56,"She compared open-bid auctions, where suppliers see prices live, with sealed-bid formats."],[545.56,547.88,"Look at the left-hand column first."],[547.88,550.7,"Open bidding creates live price pressure."],[550.98,558.79,"But it raises supplier suspicion that the buyer is being opportunistic, and if it is overused it erodes cooperation."],[559.28,562.95,"Now the right-hand column, which is highlighted for a reason."],[562.95,568.25,"A sealed bid gives you no live price discovery, and it does less damage to trust."],[568.25,573.84,"And here is the key finding: buyer savings did not differ between the formats."],[573.84,574.7,"Read that again."],[574.7,578.8,"The format that damages trust more did not produce more savings."],[579.08,585.43,"So when the relationship matters after the award, the visible price war buys you nothing extra."],[585.43,591.74,"Jap also observed that auctions used sparingly can act as a wake-up call to a complacent supply base."],[591.74,600.29,"Used constantly, they teach suppliers to protect themselves: higher opening prices, less investment, less sharing of ideas."],[601.24,605.01,"This is the slide to photograph: the preconditions checklist."],[605.01,606.49,"Six conditions."],[606.49,609.0,"And the rule in the speaker notes is strict."],[609.0,610.84,"One no is enough to stop."],[611.34,616.62,"First, a precise specification, so precise that bids can be ranked automatically."],[616.62,625.08,"If two bids at the same price would need a human to say which is better, the specification is not precise enough for an auction."],[625.08,628.75,"Second, at least four to five qualified bidders."],[628.75,631.54,"That figure comes from Smeltzer and Carr in 2003."],[631.82,638.93,"Qualified means they have passed your knock-out questions and quality gates, not that they have downloaded the documents."],[639.42,643.59,"Third, a market with spare capacity, and an account suppliers want."],[643.59,646.95,"If suppliers are full, they will not chase your volume."],[646.95,651.63,"If your account is small or difficult, they will not fight for it."],[651.63,654.62,"Fourth, price, or a published formula, decides."],[654.62,658.68,"All non-price elements must be evaluated before the auction starts."],[658.96,661.78,"The auction is not the place to discover quality."],[662.28,667.23,"Fifth, low switching cost, and a relationship that can tolerate an auction."],[667.23,674.83,"If changing supplier would take eighteen months and a data migration, the incumbent knows it, and the auction is theatre."],[674.83,682.8,"Sixth, a credible BATNA, your best alternative to a negotiated agreement, and a real intention to award as announced."],[683.08,686.39,"If you would never actually move the business, do not pretend."],[686.89,690.34,"Let's test it quickly on a familiar example."],[690.34,695.1,"Standard laptops for a refresh programme across several entities."],[695.1,697.53,"Precise specification?"],[697.53,698.37,"Yes, a model and configuration."],[698.37,699.78,"Bidders?"],[699.78,701.04,"Several resellers."],[701.04,702.03,"Spare capacity?"],[702.03,703.3,"Usually."],[703.3,704.05,"Price decides?"],[704.05,705.35,"After the technical gate, yes."],[705.35,706.55,"Switching cost?"],[706.55,708.12,"Low for a reseller."],[708.12,709.67,"Intention to award?"],[709.95,710.49,"Yes."],[710.49,711.62,"Six yeses: a good candidate."],[711.62,715.84,"Now try the same check on a strategy consulting engagement."],[715.84,719.9,"It fails at the first line, and you can stop there."],[720.85,722.6,"What does the evidence say in numbers?"],[722.6,723.69,"Four figures."],[724.19,726.39,"First, five to fifteen percent."],[726.39,733.75,"That is the typical claimed price reduction, from Jap in 2002 and Smeltzer and Carr in 2003."],[733.75,734.89,"Notice the word claimed."],[734.89,742.2,"Second, four to five, the minimum number of qualified bidders to start, again from Smeltzer and Carr."],[742.69,744.67,"Third, more than one third."],[744.67,759.34,"That is the share of United States federal reverse auctions in fiscal year 2012 that had no interactive bidding at all, according to the Government Accountability Office report GAO-14-108."],[759.34,761.48,"Nobody bid against anybody."],[761.76,768.61,"And the fourth figure explains why that hurts: the auction provider charged up to three percent of the winning bid."],[768.61,771.8,"The lesson in the speaker notes is worth repeating."],[771.8,775.65,"An auction with one bidder is just an expensive RFQ."],[776.15,777.46,"And look at the footnote."],[777.46,785.41,"The academic figures are as summarised by Mora Cortez and colleagues in 2023, and they are a range, not a promise."],[785.41,793.47,"If someone puts fifteen percent into a business case before the preconditions are checked, that is a hope, not a forecast."],[794.43,796.72,"Here is the first of three warnings."],[796.72,810.07,"Emiliani and Stec, writing in 2002 in Supply Chain Management, an International Journal: the savings actually achieved are less than that reported by suppliers of online auction services."],[810.56,811.38,"Why the gap?"],[811.38,815.07,"Because the price drop on the auction screen is a gross figure."],[815.07,824.28,"The net saving is what remains after switching costs, quality problems, extra management time, and the internal effort to run the event."],[824.28,829.74,"Picture a facilities buyer who auctions a courier contract and reports twelve percent."],[830.02,839.76,"Six months later, missed deliveries require a second courier for urgent items, and the contract manager spends a day a week on complaints."],[839.76,841.19,"The real saving is far smaller."],[841.19,845.53,"So the practical rule is: report auction results net, not gross."],[846.48,848.99,"The second warning comes from economists."],[848.99,854.18,"Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis, in 2009, studied private building contracts."],[854.18,863.16,"Their conclusion: auctions perform poorly when projects are complex, contractual design is incomplete and there are few bidders."],[863.67,865.78,"They found something else too."],[865.78,873.83,"Auctions stifle the communication between buyer and supplier that you need in order to use the contractor's expertise."],[873.83,877.33,"In complex work, the supplier often knows a better way to do the job."],[877.33,880.85,"A negotiation lets that knowledge reach you."],[880.85,882.87,"An auction asks only for a number."],[883.15,891.49,"That is the economic case for negotiation in complex services: IT development, transformation programmes, outsourcing."],[891.49,892.91,"Three warning signs, then."],[892.91,896.04,"Complexity, an incomplete contract, and few bidders."],[896.04,899.06,"Any one of them should make you pause."],[900.01,902.1,"The third reference is the law."],[902.1,912.29,"The European Union public procurement Directive of 2014, number 24, in Article 35, sets the rules for electronic auctions in public procurement."],[912.29,919.13,"The Group is a private insurer, so this is reference practice rather than a rule we must follow."],[919.13,921.28,"But it is an excellent heuristic."],[921.78,922.83,"Four cards."],[922.83,927.45,"Public e-auctions require precisely established specifications."],[927.45,932.06,"Non-price elements must be fully evaluated before the auction starts."],[932.06,937.2,"The mathematical formula used to re-rank bids must be published in advance."],[937.48,944.19,"And services with intellectual performances as their subject, such as design, are excluded entirely."],[944.19,946.91,"Apply that test to our own categories."],[946.91,948.46,"Creative agencies fail it."],[948.46,949.78,"Consulting fails it."],[949.78,957.67,"If a public authority would not be allowed to auction it, a private insurer should think very carefully before doing so."],[958.61,962.37,"With those three warnings in mind, here are the misuse cases."],[962.37,964.99,"Five of them, and you will recognise most."],[965.49,967.52,"One: intellectual services."],[967.52,972.69,"Consulting, creative work and IT development cannot be ranked automatically."],[972.69,981.15,"The cheapest strategy deck is rarely the best one, and the outcome cost of a weak consultant dwarfs the day rate you saved."],[981.15,983.2,"Two: price-only labour services."],[983.2,987.06,"In cleaning or security, the price is mostly wages."],[987.34,1001.6,"A price-only auction can push bids below sustainable wages, and the buyer then owns the consequences: high staff turnover, poor service, and possibly a reputational problem if the supplier breaks wage rules."],[1001.6,1008.01,"Never run a price-only auction for these services without a quality threshold and a should-cost floor."],[1008.51,1010.26,"Three: one or two bidders."],[1010.26,1012.86,"There is no competition, only a fee."],[1012.86,1015.29,"Four: the auction as a bluff."],[1015.29,1022.25,"Running an auction with no intention to award, or with rules kept hidden, to scare the incumbent into a discount."],[1022.25,1024.53,"Suppliers see through this quickly."],[1025.03,1027.61,"And five: overuse with partners."],[1027.61,1046.68,"Liker and Choi, in the Harvard Business Review in 2004, described how Ford's use of reverse auctions and GM's contracts allowing a switch to a cheaper supplier at any moment left the US automakers' supplier relations worse than before the quality movement of the 1980s."],[1046.96,1052.06,"Suppliers responded by sharing less and investing less with those customers."],[1052.06,1055.44,"The lesson for us is not that auctions are bad."],[1055.44,1063.22,"It is that a buyer who treats every supplier as a commodity eventually gets commodity behaviour from strategic partners too."],[1064.17,1068.79,"To be fair to the tool, here is the best case: aggregated commodities."],[1068.79,1071.3,"A public example from the United Kingdom."],[1071.8,1079.08,"Thirty-four councils aggregated about 48,000 IT devices under a Crown Commercial Service framework."],[1079.08,1083.84,"The further competition was concluded by an e-auction."],[1083.84,1092.37,"The published result was 8.8 million pounds saved over two years, against a pre-auction value of 24.6 million pounds."],[1092.37,1094.11,"That is about 36 percent."],[1094.39,1098.66,"And the savings per basket ranged from 2 percent to 44 percent."],[1099.16,1100.98,"Why did it work so well?"],[1100.98,1102.79,"Look at the conditions."],[1102.79,1106.56,"Huge aggregated volume, which makes the account very attractive."],[1106.56,1109.96,"Standard devices with precise specifications."],[1109.96,1112.75,"A framework of pre-qualified suppliers."],[1112.75,1115.14,"And price as the deciding factor."],[1115.14,1116.87,"Every precondition holds."],[1117.15,1125.41,"That is why the last line matters most: these are best-case conditions, so do not transfer these percentages to services."],[1125.41,1137.84,"The case study was published in 2016 and has since been withdrawn as older than five years, so treat it as an illustration of what aggregation plus an auction can do for commodities."],[1138.12,1145.39,"Within the Group, the equivalent would be combining the laptop or mobile phone demand of several entities into one event."],[1146.35,1151.71,"If an auction passes the checklist, the next job is to publish the rules before it starts."],[1151.71,1153.48,"This table lists five."],[1153.97,1158.77,"The start or reserve price, based on a should-cost model or a benchmark."],[1158.77,1166.57,"The decrement, which is the minimum improvement per bid, and you must confirm how that setting works in your tenant."],[1166.57,1170.49,"The closing rule: hard close or extensions, again confirm in your tenant."],[1170.49,1173.79,"The feedback: rank, or difference to best bid."],[1174.07,1178.25,"And the award rule, which is the one buyers most often forget."],[1178.25,1180.04,"Is the best bid guaranteed to win?"],[1180.04,1181.5,"On which formula?"],[1181.5,1188.79,"If quality was scored in the RFP stage and the auction only moves price, say exactly how the two are combined."],[1189.29,1190.6,"Why all this effort?"],[1190.6,1194.59,"Because procedural fairness reduces perceived opportunism."],[1194.59,1201.41,"That finding comes from Daly and Nath in 2005, as summarised by Mora Cortez and colleagues."],[1201.41,1206.61,"Suppliers who understand the rules bid with more confidence and less padding."],[1206.89,1211.71,"And one practical tip from the speaker notes: offer a mock auction to every supplier."],[1211.71,1220.07,"A supplier who has never used the Coupa Supplier Portal for live bidding should not be learning the interface while the clock is running."],[1220.07,1225.39,"A failed login during the real event is a fairness problem, not just a technical one."],[1226.34,1229.29,"Closely linked to the rules is auction ethics."],[1229.29,1229.86,"Four cards."],[1230.36,1231.85,"No phantom bidders."],[1231.85,1239.83,"Never fake competition, for example by inventing bids or inviting a supplier you know will not bid just to make the room look full."],[1239.83,1241.03,"No bluff."],[1241.03,1244.34,"Run an auction only if you will award as announced."],[1244.34,1246.25,"No post-auction haggling."],[1246.25,1248.24,"The auction result is the price."],[1248.52,1259.85,"If you call the winner the next morning to ask for another two percent, every supplier will hold back next time, because they learn that the auction is not the final round."],[1259.85,1267.85,"And codes of conduct: Emiliani in 2005 described codes covering transparency, security, confidentiality and auditing."],[1268.34,1270.14,"The speaker note says it best."],[1270.14,1272.12,"Suppliers talk to each other."],[1272.12,1275.12,"One unethical auction makes the next ten harder."],[1276.08,1278.15,"Now let's bring it home to Nordvia."],[1278.15,1282.21,"This table checks four categories against everything we have discussed."],[1282.21,1283.87,"The case is fictional."],[1284.36,1287.57,"First row, P5, office supplies and print."],[1287.57,1291.22,"Verdict: an English reverse auction after an RFQ."],[1291.22,1291.89,"Why?"],[1291.89,1295.12,"Precise items, many suppliers, and price decides."],[1295.12,1302.22,"The RFQ qualifies the suppliers and validates the item list, then the auction discovers the price."],[1302.22,1304.13,"Second row, P1, office cleaning."],[1304.13,1306.57,"Verdict: no price-only auction."],[1306.85,1312.02,"It is labour-intensive, quality comes first, and there are few strong bidders."],[1312.02,1320.05,"You will see in the case at the end of this session that only two strong bids remain for the HQ lot once the doubtful one is dealt with."],[1320.56,1323.43,"Third row, P7, creative agencies."],[1323.43,1324.32,"Verdict: no auction."],[1324.32,1329.98,"These are intellectual services, and the Article 35 logic applies directly."],[1329.98,1332.62,"Fourth row, P4, network and telecom with Telmo."],[1332.62,1337.13,"Verdict: possible on standard tariffs, with care."],[1337.13,1342.92,"Only with four or more qualified providers, and only after the DORA assessment is done."],[1343.41,1345.46,"That last row is deliberately open."],[1345.46,1356.0,"Nordvia's claims systems use Telmo's network, so the service may support a critical or important function under DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act."],[1356.28,1365.26,"If it does, the regulatory gates, exit planning and contract clauses come first, and price competition sits on top of them, not instead of them."],[1365.26,1368.07,"Standard mobile tariffs might pass."],[1368.07,1371.49,"The core network that claims handling depends on might not."],[1371.77,1378.06,"Reasonable teams can disagree here, and that disagreement is exactly what you should explore in the practice."],[1379.02,1382.91,"Which brings us to Practice 3.4, the auction suitability check."],[1382.91,1385.01,"Fifteen minutes in your workbook."],[1385.51,1389.36,"Check P5, P1, P7 and P4 against the preconditions."],[1389.36,1392.26,"Then add your own project as row five."],[1392.26,1395.49,"For each row, choose a format, or write no auction."],[1395.49,1404.01,"And if you chose an auction for your own project, write the rules you would publish: feedback, closing and the award rule."],[1404.29,1410.41,"One instruction from the facilitator notes: write the number of qualified bidders as a number."],[1410.41,1411.1,"Not several."],[1411.1,1412.12,"Not a few."],[1412.12,1413.02,"A number."],[1413.02,1416.85,"If you cannot write a number, you do not know whether the auction will work."],[1417.13,1424.04,"Pause the lesson now if you would like to do the exercise in the Modules tab of your workbook, and continue when you are ready."],[1424.99,1425.79,"Welcome back."],[1425.79,1427.87,"Here are the debrief questions."],[1427.87,1430.05,"Think about your own answers as I go."],[1430.55,1433.66,"Where did you hesitate, and on which precondition?"],[1433.66,1439.89,"Most people hesitate on Telmo, and usually on the number of qualified providers or on switching cost."],[1439.89,1442.63,"Did you choose an auction for your own project?"],[1442.63,1446.52,"If so, defend it against all six lines of the checklist."],[1446.52,1450.01,"Would an auction between B and D in the cleaning case help?"],[1450.29,1452.94,"The answer in the facilitator notes is no."],[1452.94,1459.21,"Two strong bidders, a labour-intensive service, and a relationship you need after the award."],[1459.21,1464.0,"A best and final offer, a BAFO, does the job without the price-war risk."],[1464.0,1468.21,"And finally: which format would your incumbent hate most, and why?"],[1468.49,1475.24,"Often it is the English reverse auction with visible prices, because the incumbent's margin is exposed in public."],[1475.24,1480.33,"That tells you something about how the relationship would look the morning after."],[1481.28,1484.35,"Let's recap module 3.4 in three lines."],[1484.85,1484.87,"One."],[1484.87,1490.63,"Auctions work for precise specifications, enough bidders and price-led decisions."],[1490.63,1496.06,"Check all six preconditions, and remember that one no is enough to stop."],[1496.06,1496.49,"Two."],[1496.49,1500.22,"Expect five to fifteen percent at best, and report savings net."],[1500.22,1503.91,"That is the lesson from Jap and from Emiliani and Stec."],[1503.91,1504.69,"Three."],[1504.97,1516.68,"Know Coupa's formats: the English reverse auction, the Dutch reverse auction that rises from a low start, and the reverse Japanese auction, where suppliers drop out as the price falls."],[1517.18,1523.74,"And underneath all three: the auction is a tool that follows the strategy, never the other way round."],[1523.74,1527.4,"In the next lesson, we put all of this into a Coupa event."],[1527.68,1540.39,"We will look at the event types, the life of an event from template to contract, the pre-bidding phase, what Coupa documents and what you must confirm in your tenant, and we will build a set-up sheet for the Nordvia cleaning tender."],[1540.39,1541.55,"See you there."]]},{"id":"s3-l5","session":"s3","title":"Coupa Sourcing in practice","summary":"Translate a sourcing plan into a Coupa event set-up sheet, using documented features with confidence and flagging the settings to confirm in your tenant.","from":86,"to":103,"audio":"audio/s3-l5.mp3","duration":1517.0,"segs":[[86,0.6,33.73,"Welcome back. This lesson covers module 3.5 of Session 3: Coupa Sourcing in practice. So far in this session we have chosen the sourcing question and the event sequence, designed the request and the evaluation model, run the evaluation fairly, and decided when an auction makes sense. Now we need a place where all of that actually happens. That place is the Coupa event. As the subtitle says, it is where the event lives: invitations, questions and answers, bids, scoring, award and the audit trail."],[86,34.23,73.56,"Here is the plan. We start with a principle about what to trust in the documentation. Then we look at Coupa's event types and the life of an event from template to contract. We go deeper into the pre-bidding phase, questionnaires, lots and items, and then the most important slide of the lesson: what Coupa documents versus what you must confirm in your tenant. After that, scoring in practice, Coupa Sourcing Optimization with a Nordvia cleaning example, Coupa's role by negotiation type, the handover from award to buying channel, good practice on templates, and a model set-up sheet. You will finish by writing your own."],[87,74.51,81.93,"Let's start with the principle on this slide. Use what Coupa documents with confidence. Confirm the rest before you promise it."],[87,82.43,119.92,"Why does this matter so much? Look at the line underneath. A supplier briefing that promises a setting your tenant does not have is a credibility problem. Imagine you tell six cleaning suppliers in a briefing that technical and commercial envelopes will be opened separately, on a fixed date, by the system. Then you discover your tenant has no such setting, and you have to improvise. Every supplier now wonders what else you promised that is not true. Or you tell your business owner that the auction will extend automatically by five minutes after each late bid, and on the day it closes abruptly at the stated time."],[87,120.42,155.68,"There is also a practical reason. Coupa's documentation has moved. The older site, compass dot coupa dot com, is legacy, and docs dot coupa dot com is current. And the public pages describe the supplier side best, because they are written to help suppliers respond. The buyer-side configuration of your own tenant depends on what your organisation has licensed and switched on. So throughout this lesson, I will be precise about which features are documented by Coupa and which ones you should treat as a question for your Coupa administrator."],[88,156.63,163.35,"This table lists the Coupa event types, as described in Coupa's supplier documentation. Five rows."],[88,163.85,211.09,"Spot Buy is for urgent or short-term needs, with no contract. Think of a one-off purchase of event furniture for a conference next month. The RFI, request for information, collects qualitative information through forms and attachments, with no pricing. You use it to understand the market and to pre-qualify suppliers. The RFQ, request for quotation, asks for prices for items and lots. It fits when the specification is fully defined and you mainly need the price. The RFP, request for proposal, asks for prices plus information in the same event. This is the workhorse for services, where you score quality and price together. And the auction can run stand-alone, or after an RFx as a pre-bidding phase."],[88,211.59,253.0,"The speaker note gives you the exercise. Map each of the sequences we designed in module 3.1 onto these event types. For example, for Nordvia's P2 IT consulting, where the poll earlier suggested a framework RFP with a rate card followed by mini-competitions, you might use an RFI to understand the twelve firms, an RFP to build the framework and the rate card, and then a smaller RFP event for each mini-competition among panel members. For P5 office supplies, an RFQ followed by an English reverse auction. The event types are the building blocks. The strategy decides how you combine them."],[89,253.95,257.73,"Now the life of an event in Coupa, as a flow of eight steps."],[89,258.23,298.7,"It starts with a template. Then you build the items, lots and questionnaires. Then you invite suppliers. The slide says Private, and for good reason. Coupa offers three privacy options. Public means any supplier can see the event. Private means only invited suppliers can see it, and this is the usual choice for a tender. Hidden restricts it further, to specific supplier users. Next, the questions and answers run through the Message Center. Then the bids come in, possibly as a pre-bidding phase before an auction. Then scoring, then award, and finally the contract in CLM, contract lifecycle management."],[89,299.2,337.4,"There is one detail in the speaker notes that you should mention in every supplier briefing. Suppliers respond through the Coupa Supplier Portal, and before they can see the event documents they must accept the event terms. That yes cannot be reversed. A supplier who clicks it quickly without reading, and then discovers a confidentiality clause or a liability term it cannot accept, has a problem. So tell suppliers in advance what the terms say, and give them time to read them. This is also a fairness point. If one supplier's legal team needs a week to approve the terms, make sure your timeline allows for it."],[90,338.35,343.7,"Let's zoom in on one step that Coupa documents well: the pre-bidding phase. Four cards."],[90,344.2,363.7,"What it is: the RFx serves as the pre-bidding phase of an auction. Updates: suppliers can update their submission without limit. Rules: the bidding rules do not apply yet. And feedback: submissions are collected without feedback. Suppliers do not see their rank or anyone else's price at this stage."],[90,364.2,391.29,"Why is this so useful? Because it gives you a clean place to do the work that must happen before any auction. Remember the Article 35 practice from the last lesson: non-price elements fully evaluated before the auction starts. The pre-bidding phase is where you do that. You collect the technical answers, run the knock-out questions, score quality, and decide who is qualified to enter the live auction. Only then do you open the bidding."],[90,391.79,427.62,"Here is a short example. Nordvia wants to buy standard laptops for three entities. The RFQ asks for the item prices and a technical questionnaire on warranty, delivery lead times and the security configuration. Eight resellers respond. Two fail the security questionnaire. During the pre-bidding phase, the remaining six can update their prices as often as they like, without seeing anyone else. When you open the English reverse auction, only qualified bidders take part, each with a sensible opening price. No meaningless bids, no surprises."],[91,428.57,432.62,"Next, the content of the event: questionnaires, lots and items."],[91,433.12,478.27,"Questionnaires: you can attach several per event. A typical structure has three. A knock-out questionnaire, a quality questionnaire, and a regulatory one covering, for example, DORA and information security. Lots: items in a lot are always awarded together and ranked together. That is a design decision, not just a technical one. If you put all three Nordvia cleaning sites in one lot, you must award them to one supplier. If you make three lots, you can split them. Participation: partial participation, meaning a supplier bids on some lots but not others, where it is configured. And Excel: bulk import and export of supplier responses, which saves a lot of time on long price schedules."],[91,478.77,518.62,"The speaker note has a practical rule. Build the knock-out questionnaire first. A failed gate should stop the bidder before anyone reads the quality answers. Why? Because evaluators are human. If an evaluator reads a brilliant quality proposal and only then discovers that the supplier fails the insurance requirement, there is pressure to find a way round the gate. If the gate comes first, the decision is clean. Knock-out questions should be yes or no, and they should be things you would genuinely exclude a supplier for: wage compliance, a mandatory certification, minimum insurance cover, acceptance of key contract terms."],[91,519.12,559.53,"Lots deserve one more thought, because the lot structure is one of the most powerful levers in the whole event. Many small lots invite specialist and local suppliers, and they let you split the award to reduce dependency on one supplier. One large lot attracts suppliers who want scale, and it simplifies contract management. There is no universal answer. The category strategy from Session 2 tells you whether Nordvia wants one partner or several, and the lots should follow that choice, not the other way round. Decide it before you build the event, because changing lots after suppliers have started pricing is confusing and unfair."],[92,560.48,566.71,"This is the most important slide of the lesson. Settings: documented versus confirm in your tenant."],[92,567.21,591.04,"Look at the left-hand column first, documented by Coupa. The three auction formats: English reverse, Dutch reverse and reverse Japanese. Rank or difference-to-best feedback. The rules preventing price increases and very small improvements. The pre-bidding phase without feedback. And lots awarded together. You can use all of these with confidence and describe them to suppliers."],[92,591.54,613.95,"Now the right-hand column, which is highlighted. Confirm in your Coupa tenant. Five items. Overtime or soft-close extensions. Minimum decrement parameters. A sealed-envelope opening, where technical and commercial bids are opened separately at a set date. Multi-attribute live auctions. And how weighted scoring is set up in core Sourcing."],[92,614.45,649.59,"The speaker note is clear. Do not present the right-hand column as fact. These features may well exist in your configuration. But they are not described clearly in the public documentation reviewed for this course, and they depend on your licence and your set-up. So agree with your Coupa administrator how each one is handled, and write the answer into your set-up sheet. Then the sheet becomes a record of what is true for your tenant, not what someone remembers from a demo. And if the answer is that a setting does not exist, you still have options. We will see one on the next slide."],[93,650.54,654.1,"Scoring in practice. Four cards, and they form a sequence."],[93,654.6,673.01,"First, confirm. Find out how weighted scoring is configured and who sees which scores. Third-party sources describe weights multiplied by scores in Coupa, but the exact core configuration is not verified in Coupa's public documentation. So treat it as a tenant question."],[93,673.51,699.46,"Second, the fallback. If the configuration does not do what your published model says, run the published model in the evaluation template, and attach that template to the event. The model you published to suppliers, with its weights and its price formula, is what matters. The tool that does the arithmetic is secondary. What you must avoid is a mismatch between the model you published and the calculation that is actually used."],[93,699.96,740.02,"Third, keep prices apart. In module 3.3 we saw that quality evaluators should score without seeing prices, because knowing the price biases the quality judgement. If your tenant has no sealed-envelope setting, you can still keep prices apart by role, giving quality evaluators no access to the commercial response, or by stage, scoring quality before the commercial part is opened. And fourth, record. Scores and their rationale are stored with the event. If a losing bidder asks for a debrief, or an auditor asks why the contract went to supplier B, the answer is in the event, not in somebody's inbox."],[93,740.52,777.35,"A short story shows why the record matters. A losing bidder in a facilities tender asks for a debrief and challenges its quality score on mobilisation. If the evaluators' individual scores, the consensus score and a one-line rationale per criterion sit in the event, the buyer can answer in an afternoon, calmly and consistently. If the scores live in three personal spreadsheets and an email thread, the same question takes a week, and the answers may not match. The first buyer looks professional. The second looks as if the decision was made first and justified later."],[94,778.3,782.87,"Now a more advanced tool: Coupa Sourcing Optimization. Four cards."],[94,783.37,818.55,"Origin: it was Trade Extensions, which Coupa acquired in May 2017. Expressive bidding: suppliers can bid on packages and bundles, offer volume discounts, and propose alternatives. Instead of forcing every supplier to price every lot separately, you let them say, for example, I will do sites one and two together for this price, or all three for that price. Scenarios: you run what-if comparisons of different awards under constraints. And rounds: several bidding rounds, with the award flowing into contracts and catalogues."],[94,819.05,850.71,"A note of caution from the speaker notes. These are vendor descriptions, and the savings figures in Coupa's case studies are vendor claims. Use the tool for what it does well. In indirect procurement that typically means multi-site facility management, telecom lines, hotel programmes, print, courier services, and temporary staffing by region. The common factor is many lots that interact. When winning one lot changes what a supplier can offer on another, optimisation helps you see the trade-offs."],[95,851.66,857.51,"Let's see it on a worked example: optimisation for Nordvia cleaning. The example is fictional."],[95,858.01,894.9,"Three sites, in two countries, and four to six bidders. Bidders price each lot, and any bundle they like. One supplier might offer a discount if it wins both sites in the same country. Another might offer all three sites together. There is a constraint: at least one supplier per country with local capacity. Now look at the three scenarios. Scenario one awards the best bid per lot. Scenario two finds the lowest total cost, which may mean accepting a bundle. Scenario three limits the result to a maximum of two suppliers, to keep contract management simple."],[95,895.4,923.86,"Then the buyer compares cost against risk before choosing. Scenario two might be cheapest, but if it puts all three sites with one supplier, the Group carries concentration risk. Scenario one might mean three suppliers and three contracts to manage. Scenario three might cost a little more than the lowest total, but it balances cost, risk and management effort. Optimisation does not make the decision. It makes the cost of each choice visible."],[95,924.36,946.42,"And here is the honest point in the speaker notes. With three sites, a spreadsheet can do this. With thirty sites across several entities, optimisation earns its place. That is the Group-level case. If the entities of the Group pooled their cleaning demand into one event, the number of possible combinations would explode, and a spreadsheet would no longer be reliable."],[96,947.37,953.96,"This table shows Coupa's role by negotiation type. It connects the strategy to the tool. Six rows."],[96,954.46,1004.86,"Price-only, with many suppliers: an RFQ plus an English reverse auction. Few suppliers, or an incumbent-heavy market: a reverse Japanese auction or a sealed BAFO, best and final offer. Quality-led services: an RFP, then negotiation rounds, then a BAFO, using follow-on events. Coupa documents that follow-on events can carry over supplier responses, so suppliers do not have to re-enter everything. Panel call-offs: a mini-competition as an RFP event sent to the panel members. Multi-lot with bundles: Sourcing Optimization. And sole source: Coupa's role is data preparation and the CLM workflow, not competition. There is no one to compete with, so the tool supports the negotiation instead of replacing it."],[96,1005.36,1023.53,"Notice what is common to every row. The Message Center and the audit trail serve every negotiation type. Even in a sole-source negotiation, keeping the formal exchanges in one place protects you. Session 4 deepens this matrix, when we look at negotiation planning in detail."],[96,1024.03,1059.79,"Let's make that concrete with a Nordvia example. Take P4, network and telecom. Standard mobile tariffs, with several qualified operators, might sit in the first row: an RFQ and then an English reverse auction. The core network that claims systems depend on sits closer to the third row: an RFP, negotiation rounds on service levels and exit terms, then a BAFO. The same supplier, Telmo, can appear in two rows at once, because the rows describe the negotiation, not the supplier. That is why you design the route lot by lot."],[97,1060.74,1066.18,"Now, what happens after the award? This flow shows the path from award to buying channel."],[97,1066.68,1087.01,"Award. Then the contract in CLM. Then the clause library and the approvals. Then the supplier is onboarded in SIM, supplier information management, where InfoSec, DORA and ESG questionnaires sit. Then catalogue or contract-backed buying. And finally, invoices matched to the contract."],[97,1087.51,1124.91,"Why does this flow belong in a sourcing lesson? Because a saving that never reaches the buying channel is a saving on paper only. Picture a successful office supplies auction. The new prices are excellent. But the old catalogue stays live for three months, and users keep ordering from it. The savings report says one thing, and the invoices say another. Coupa describes winning terms flowing into draft contracts and catalogues, and that is a vendor description, so check how it works in your tenant. The principle holds regardless. Close the loop. Session 4 covers the handover in depth."],[98,1125.86,1129.13,"Good practice: templates and audit trail. Four cards."],[98,1129.63,1163.48,"Templates by route: a soft facility management tender template, a panel mini-competition template, an RFQ plus auction template. Everything in the event: the draft contract, the weights, the annexes. Not in emails. Message Center only: every clarification is logged, and every answer goes to all bidders when it concerns all of them. That is your equal-treatment evidence. And regulatory questions: DORA and outsourcing questionnaires reused from SIM, so that you do not reinvent them for every event."],[98,1163.98,1190.35,"The speaker note captures the idea. Templates turn good practice into the default. A buyer under time pressure will reuse whatever is easiest. If the easiest thing is a well-designed template with the knock-out questionnaire, the scoring sheet and the draft contract already attached, good practice happens automatically. So ask yourself: who owns the templates in your team today? If the answer is nobody, that is a quick win."],[98,1190.85,1227.32,"Think about the audit trail from the other side, too. Under Solvency II, an insurer's outsourcing of critical or important functions is subject to governance requirements, and DORA adds requirements for ICT third-party arrangements. When Internal Audit or a supervisor asks how a supplier was selected, the strongest answer is a complete event: the invitation list, every clarification, the scores, the award rationale and the approvals, all in one place. A buyer who has to reconstruct that from email is exposed, however good the decision was."],[99,1228.27,1239.04,"Let's bring it all together in a worked example: the set-up sheet for Nordvia P1 cleaning. The example is fictional, and it is the model answer for the practice you will do next."],[99,1239.54,1264.05,"Event: an RFP, Private, three lots, with a bundle option. Questionnaires: a knock-out questionnaire on wage compliance and insurance, a quality questionnaire, and an ESG questionnaire. Attachments: the hybrid specification, which combines outputs with a few essential inputs, the staff data for any transfer of employees, and the draft contract with its KPIs."],[99,1264.55,1292.27,"Scoring: fifty percent quality and fifty percent price, with the relative price formula, where the lowest price divided by each bid price, times one hundred, gives the price score. Quality is scored without prices. Follow-on: a BAFO event to the best two. And notice the last words: no auction. Everything we said in module 3.4 applies. A labour-intensive service, few strong bidders, and a relationship that matters."],[99,1292.77,1318.65,"Now, what would you confirm in your tenant for this event? The speaker note gives two answers. First, the scoring set-up: can the fifty-fifty model with the relative price formula be configured, or do you run it in the evaluation template? Second, how prices are kept from the quality evaluators: by a sealed setting, by role, or by stage. If you can answer those two questions before issue, the event is ready."],[100,1319.6,1339.21,"A short poll. Which part of your RFx plan are you least sure about? The event sequence, the evaluation model, auction or not, the Coupa set-up, or the regulatory gates? Think about your own project for a moment. Whatever you choose, that is the line on your set-up sheet where you should spend the most time in the practice."],[100,1339.71,1361.73,"If you chose the Coupa set-up, you are not alone, and the rest of this lesson is designed for you. If you chose the evaluation model, go back to the sensitivity analysis from module 3.3 and test your weights before you build the event. If you chose the regulatory gates, that is a conversation with Risk or Compliance, and it belongs in your fieldwork."],[101,1362.68,1369.35,"Now it is your turn. Practice 3.5: your Coupa event set-up sheet. Fifteen minutes in your workbook."],[101,1369.85,1404.2,"Fill the ten lines of the set-up sheet: event types and sequence, privacy and invited suppliers, lots and items, questionnaires and knock-out questions, attachments, the pre-bidding phase and timeline, the auction format with feedback and closing if there is one, the scoring set-up and who sees prices, the Message Center rules, and the award, CLM and catalogue handover. Mark each setting you must confirm in your tenant. Write who will build the event and who approves it. And then the quality check: give your sheet to a colleague. Could they build the event from it?"],[101,1404.7,1418.91,"That neighbour test is the standard. If a colleague cannot build the event from your sheet, it is not finished. Pause the lesson now if you would like to complete the set-up sheet in the Modules tab of your workbook, and continue when you are ready."],[102,1419.86,1422.48,"Welcome back. Here are the debrief questions."],[102,1422.98,1465.36,"Which settings did you mark confirm in tenant? Most people mark the scoring set-up and the separation of prices, and many mark the auction extensions. Who needs Sourcing Optimization, and who only thinks so? If you have three lots and no bundles, you probably do not. If you have many interacting lots across entities, you probably do. Where will your draft contract and weights live? The right answer is in the event, attached before issue. And what will you ask your Coupa administrator this week? Write the questions down now. In the live session, the facilitator collects the tenant questions from the whole cohort and sends them to the Coupa administrator, so that everyone benefits from the answers."],[103,1466.31,1469.46,"Let's recap module 3.5 in three lines."],[103,1469.96,1493.82,"One. Coupa documents the event types, the auction formats, the pre-bidding phase, lots and feedback. Use them with confidence. Two. Confirm overtime, decrements, sealed opening and scoring in your tenant before you promise them to anyone. Three. Put everything in the event, and close the loop to CLM and catalogues, so that people actually buy against the new terms."],[103,1494.32,1515.8,"In the next lesson, we put the whole chain to work on one lot. Case 3 takes the Nordvia HQ cleaning lot end to end: an abnormally low bid, a bidder that wants to change the term, a close score, and a Head of Real Estate who wants a decision this month. Then we close the session with Fieldwork 3 and a bridge to Session 4. See you there."]],"questions":[{"after":88,"q":"Nordvia's Marketing team needs a stand for a trade fair in three weeks, a one-off purchase with no ongoing contract. Which Coupa event type fits best?","options":["RFI","RFP followed by an auction","Sourcing Optimization","Spot Buy"],"answer":3,"explain":"Coupa describes Spot Buy for urgent or short-term needs with no contract. An RFI collects information without prices, and an RFP plus auction would be far too heavy for a one-off need.","t":253.0},{"after":89,"q":"In the supplier briefing for a Private RFP, what should you tell suppliers about the event terms in the Coupa Supplier Portal?","options":["That they can accept now and withdraw acceptance later","That the terms are optional","That they must accept the terms before seeing the event, the acceptance cannot be reversed, so their legal teams should review them in time","That the terms only apply to the winner"],"answer":2,"explain":"Suppliers accept the event terms before access, and that acceptance cannot be reversed. Telling them in advance and allowing review time is both good practice and a fairness point.","t":337.4},{"after":91,"q":"During the evaluation of a security services RFP, an evaluator finds an excellent quality proposal from a bidder that does not meet the minimum insurance cover. What design choice would have prevented the pressure to find a way round the gate?","options":["Building the knock-out questionnaire first, so a failed gate stops the bidder before anyone reads quality answers","Putting all sites in one lot","Showing prices to the quality evaluators","Using a Dutch reverse auction"],"answer":0,"explain":"A knock-out questionnaire evaluated first keeps the decision clean. If evaluators read a strong proposal before the gate, there is pressure to bend the rule.","t":559.53},{"after":92,"q":"A colleague's draft supplier briefing says the auction will automatically extend by five minutes after every late bid. What should you do before it goes out?","options":["Send it, because all Coupa auctions extend automatically","Confirm the overtime or soft-close setting with your Coupa administrator and record it in the set-up sheet before promising it","Remove the auction and run an RFI instead","Replace it with a promise of a sealed-envelope opening"],"answer":1,"explain":"Overtime and soft-close extensions are on the confirm in your tenant list. Promising an unconfirmed setting is a credibility problem when the auction behaves differently.","t":649.59},{"after":93,"q":"Your tenant turns out to have no formal sealed-envelope setting. How can you still keep prices away from the quality evaluators?","options":["You cannot, so drop the quality criteria","Ask the evaluators to promise not to look","Switch to a price-only RFQ","Keep prices apart by role or by stage, and run the published model in the evaluation template attached to the event"],"answer":3,"explain":"The fallback is to separate by role, giving quality evaluators no commercial access, or by stage, scoring quality before prices are opened, and to run the published model in an attached evaluation template.","t":777.35},{"after":95,"q":"A Group category manager wants to pool cleaning demand of six entities, thirty sites and several countries into one event, letting suppliers bid bundles. Why is Sourcing Optimization worth considering here but not for a single three-site lot?","options":["It is mandatory for all cleaning tenders","It guarantees the savings shown in Coupa case studies","With many interacting lots and bundles, the combinations exceed what a spreadsheet handles reliably, and scenarios make cost versus risk visible","It replaces the need for quality evaluation"],"answer":2,"explain":"With three sites a spreadsheet can do it; with thirty sites across entities, optimisation earns its place. It makes the trade-offs between scenarios visible, while the buyer still decides.","t":946.42},{"after":97,"q":"Three months after an excellent office supplies auction, invoices show users still buying at old prices. Which step in the flow from award to buying channel was most likely missed?","options":["Activating catalogue or contract-backed buying on the new terms and retiring the old channel","The pre-bidding phase","The knock-out questionnaire","The Message Center rules"],"answer":0,"explain":"A saving that never reaches the buying channel stays on paper. Closing the loop means the new catalogue or contract-backed buying replaces the old one, so invoices match the contract.","t":1124.91},{"after":99,"q":"For the Nordvia P1 cleaning set-up sheet, which follow-on step matches the model answer?","options":["An English reverse auction among all four bidders","A BAFO event to the best two, with no auction","A Dutch reverse auction with a start price at the incumbent's rate","A Spot Buy to the lowest bidder"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cleaning is labour-intensive with few strong bidders and a relationship that matters, so the preconditions for an auction fail. A BAFO event to the best two does the job on the published model.","t":1318.65},{"after":102,"q":"You hand your set-up sheet to a colleague, who says they could not build the event from it because the scoring line just says weighted scoring as usual. What is the best fix?","options":["Leave it, because the Coupa administrator will know","Delete the scoring line","State the weights, the price formula, who sees prices, and mark the scoring configuration as confirm in tenant with the agreed fallback","Replace it with a link to last year's event"],"answer":2,"explain":"The neighbour test is the quality check: the sheet must be specific enough to build from. Writing the model and flagging the tenant question turns an assumption into a record.","t":1465.36}],"cues":[[0.6,1.35,"Welcome back."],[1.35,5.5,"This lesson covers module 3.5 of Session 3: Coupa Sourcing in practice."],[5.5,19.25,"So far in this session we have chosen the sourcing question and the event sequence, designed the request and the evaluation model, run the evaluation fairly, and decided when an auction makes sense."],[19.53,23.34,"Now we need a place where all of that actually happens."],[23.34,24.66,"That place is the Coupa event."],[24.66,33.73,"As the subtitle says, it is where the event lives: invitations, questions and answers, bids, scoring, award and the audit trail."],[34.23,35.65,"Here is the plan."],[35.65,39.68,"We start with a principle about what to trust in the documentation."],[39.68,45.07,"Then we look at Coupa's event types and the life of an event from template to contract."],[45.35,56.72,"We go deeper into the pre-bidding phase, questionnaires, lots and items, and then the most important slide of the lesson: what Coupa documents versus what you must confirm in your tenant."],[57.0,71.45,"After that, scoring in practice, Coupa Sourcing Optimization with a Nordvia cleaning example, Coupa's role by negotiation type, the handover from award to buying channel, good practice on templates, and a model set-up sheet."],[71.45,73.55,"You will finish by writing your own."],[74.51,77.5,"Let's start with the principle on this slide."],[77.5,79.57,"Use what Coupa documents with confidence."],[79.57,81.93,"Confirm the rest before you promise it."],[82.43,84.09,"Why does this matter so much?"],[84.09,85.9,"Look at the line underneath."],[85.9,91.92,"A supplier briefing that promises a setting your tenant does not have is a credibility problem."],[91.92,100.88,"Imagine you tell six cleaning suppliers in a briefing that technical and commercial envelopes will be opened separately, on a fixed date, by the system."],[101.16,105.75,"Then you discover your tenant has no such setting, and you have to improvise."],[105.75,109.94,"Every supplier now wonders what else you promised that is not true."],[109.94,119.92,"Or you tell your business owner that the auction will extend automatically by five minutes after each late bid, and on the day it closes abruptly at the stated time."],[120.42,122.94,"There is also a practical reason."],[122.94,125.03,"Coupa's documentation has moved."],[125.03,131.59,"The older site, compass dot coupa dot com, is legacy, and docs dot coupa dot com is current."],[131.59,138.7,"And the public pages describe the supplier side best, because they are written to help suppliers respond."],[138.98,146.01,"The buyer-side configuration of your own tenant depends on what your organisation has licensed and switched on."],[146.01,155.69,"So throughout this lesson, I will be precise about which features are documented by Coupa and which ones you should treat as a question for your Coupa administrator."],[156.63,162.09,"This table lists the Coupa event types, as described in Coupa's supplier documentation."],[162.09,163.35,"Five rows."],[163.85,167.98,"Spot Buy is for urgent or short-term needs, with no contract."],[167.98,172.76,"Think of a one-off purchase of event furniture for a conference next month."],[172.76,179.95,"The RFI, request for information, collects qualitative information through forms and attachments, with no pricing."],[179.95,184.61,"You use it to understand the market and to pre-qualify suppliers."],[184.89,189.81,"The RFQ, request for quotation, asks for prices for items and lots."],[189.81,195.04,"It fits when the specification is fully defined and you mainly need the price."],[195.04,200.91,"The RFP, request for proposal, asks for prices plus information in the same event."],[200.91,205.92,"This is the workhorse for services, where you score quality and price together."],[206.2,211.09,"And the auction can run stand-alone, or after an RFx as a pre-bidding phase."],[211.59,214.59,"The speaker note gives you the exercise."],[214.59,219.72,"Map each of the sequences we designed in module 3.1 onto these event types."],[220.0,242.06,"For example, for Nordvia's P2 IT consulting, where the poll earlier suggested a framework RFP with a rate card followed by mini-competitions, you might use an RFI to understand the twelve firms, an RFP to build the framework and the rate card, and then a smaller RFP event for each mini-competition among panel members."],[242.34,247.34,"For P5 office supplies, an RFQ followed by an English reverse auction."],[247.34,250.05,"The event types are the building blocks."],[250.05,253.0,"The strategy decides how you combine them."],[253.95,257.73,"Now the life of an event in Coupa, as a flow of eight steps."],[258.23,259.7,"It starts with a template."],[259.7,262.91,"Then you build the items, lots and questionnaires."],[262.91,264.97,"Then you invite suppliers."],[264.97,267.94,"The slide says Private, and for good reason."],[267.94,270.68,"Coupa offers three privacy options."],[270.68,273.48,"Public means any supplier can see the event."],[273.76,279.61,"Private means only invited suppliers can see it, and this is the usual choice for a tender."],[279.61,283.53,"Hidden restricts it further, to specific supplier users."],[283.53,287.58,"Next, the questions and answers run through the Message Center."],[287.58,292.13,"Then the bids come in, possibly as a pre-bidding phase before an auction."],[292.41,298.7,"Then scoring, then award, and finally the contract in CLM, contract lifecycle management."],[299.2,304.56,"There is one detail in the speaker notes that you should mention in every supplier briefing."],[304.56,312.49,"Suppliers respond through the Coupa Supplier Portal, and before they can see the event documents they must accept the event terms."],[312.49,314.52,"That yes cannot be reversed."],[314.8,324.11,"A supplier who clicks it quickly without reading, and then discovers a confidentiality clause or a liability term it cannot accept, has a problem."],[324.11,329.25,"So tell suppliers in advance what the terms say, and give them time to read them."],[329.25,331.35,"This is also a fairness point."],[331.63,337.39,"If one supplier's legal team needs a week to approve the terms, make sure your timeline allows for it."],[338.35,343.12,"Let's zoom in on one step that Coupa documents well: the pre-bidding phase."],[343.12,343.7,"Four cards."],[344.2,348.51,"What it is: the RFx serves as the pre-bidding phase of an auction."],[348.51,352.68,"Updates: suppliers can update their submission without limit."],[352.68,355.45,"Rules: the bidding rules do not apply yet."],[355.45,359.1,"And feedback: submissions are collected without feedback."],[359.1,363.7,"Suppliers do not see their rank or anyone else's price at this stage."],[364.2,365.84,"Why is this so useful?"],[365.84,370.43,"Because it gives you a clean place to do the work that must happen before any auction."],[370.43,378.42,"Remember the Article 35 practice from the last lesson: non-price elements fully evaluated before the auction starts."],[378.42,380.88,"The pre-bidding phase is where you do that."],[381.16,389.19,"You collect the technical answers, run the knock-out questions, score quality, and decide who is qualified to enter the live auction."],[389.19,391.3,"Only then do you open the bidding."],[391.79,393.42,"Here is a short example."],[393.42,397.25,"Nordvia wants to buy standard laptops for three entities."],[397.25,406.17,"The RFQ asks for the item prices and a technical questionnaire on warranty, delivery lead times and the security configuration."],[406.17,408.19,"Eight resellers respond."],[408.19,410.41,"Two fail the security questionnaire."],[410.69,418.4,"During the pre-bidding phase, the remaining six can update their prices as often as they like, without seeing anyone else."],[418.4,425.96,"When you open the English reverse auction, only qualified bidders take part, each with a sensible opening price."],[425.96,427.61,"No meaningless bids, no surprises."],[428.57,432.62,"Next, the content of the event: questionnaires, lots and items."],[433.12,436.42,"Questionnaires: you can attach several per event."],[436.42,438.23,"A typical structure has three."],[438.23,446.44,"A knock-out questionnaire, a quality questionnaire, and a regulatory one covering, for example, DORA and information security."],[446.44,451.25,"Lots: items in a lot are always awarded together and ranked together."],[451.53,454.81,"That is a design decision, not just a technical one."],[454.81,461.11,"If you put all three Nordvia cleaning sites in one lot, you must award them to one supplier."],[461.11,463.0,"If you make three lots, you can split them."],[463.0,470.86,"Participation: partial participation, meaning a supplier bids on some lots but not others, where it is configured."],[471.14,478.27,"And Excel: bulk import and export of supplier responses, which saves a lot of time on long price schedules."],[478.77,480.98,"The speaker note has a practical rule."],[480.98,483.54,"Build the knock-out questionnaire first."],[483.54,489.13,"A failed gate should stop the bidder before anyone reads the quality answers."],[489.13,489.13,"Why?"],[489.13,491.02,"Because evaluators are human."],[491.3,501.99,"If an evaluator reads a brilliant quality proposal and only then discovers that the supplier fails the insurance requirement, there is pressure to find a way round the gate."],[501.99,504.76,"If the gate comes first, the decision is clean."],[505.04,518.63,"Knock-out questions should be yes or no, and they should be things you would genuinely exclude a supplier for: wage compliance, a mandatory certification, minimum insurance cover, acceptance of key contract terms."],[519.12,525.85,"Lots deserve one more thought, because the lot structure is one of the most powerful levers in the whole event."],[525.85,534.01,"Many small lots invite specialist and local suppliers, and they let you split the award to reduce dependency on one supplier."],[534.29,539.86,"One large lot attracts suppliers who want scale, and it simplifies contract management."],[539.86,542.16,"There is no universal answer."],[542.16,551.7,"The category strategy from Session 2 tells you whether Nordvia wants one partner or several, and the lots should follow that choice, not the other way round."],[551.98,559.53,"Decide it before you build the event, because changing lots after suppliers have started pricing is confusing and unfair."],[560.48,563.14,"This is the most important slide of the lesson."],[563.14,566.71,"Settings: documented versus confirm in your tenant."],[567.21,570.72,"Look at the left-hand column first, documented by Coupa."],[570.72,576.05,"The three auction formats: English reverse, Dutch reverse and reverse Japanese."],[576.05,578.25,"Rank or difference-to-best feedback."],[578.25,582.81,"The rules preventing price increases and very small improvements."],[582.81,585.24,"The pre-bidding phase without feedback."],[585.24,586.82,"And lots awarded together."],[587.1,591.04,"You can use all of these with confidence and describe them to suppliers."],[591.54,595.34,"Now the right-hand column, which is highlighted."],[595.34,596.34,"Confirm in your Coupa tenant."],[596.34,597.83,"Five items."],[597.83,599.33,"Overtime or soft-close extensions."],[599.33,601.78,"Minimum decrement parameters."],[601.78,608.71,"A sealed-envelope opening, where technical and commercial bids are opened separately at a set date."],[608.71,610.91,"Multi-attribute live auctions."],[611.19,613.94,"And how weighted scoring is set up in core Sourcing."],[614.45,616.21,"The speaker note is clear."],[616.21,618.88,"Do not present the right-hand column as fact."],[618.88,622.77,"These features may well exist in your configuration."],[622.77,630.34,"But they are not described clearly in the public documentation reviewed for this course, and they depend on your licence and your set-up."],[630.62,636.8,"So agree with your Coupa administrator how each one is handled, and write the answer into your set-up sheet."],[636.8,642.94,"Then the sheet becomes a record of what is true for your tenant, not what someone remembers from a demo."],[642.94,647.46,"And if the answer is that a setting does not exist, you still have options."],[647.74,649.59,"We will see one on the next slide."],[650.54,651.9,"Scoring in practice."],[651.9,654.1,"Four cards, and they form a sequence."],[654.6,656.03,"First, confirm."],[656.03,660.84,"Find out how weighted scoring is configured and who sees which scores."],[660.84,670.7,"Third-party sources describe weights multiplied by scores in Coupa, but the exact core configuration is not verified in Coupa's public documentation."],[670.7,673.01,"So treat it as a tenant question."],[673.51,674.72,"Second, the fallback."],[674.72,684.16,"If the configuration does not do what your published model says, run the published model in the evaluation template, and attach that template to the event."],[684.16,690.25,"The model you published to suppliers, with its weights and its price formula, is what matters."],[690.25,693.03,"The tool that does the arithmetic is secondary."],[693.31,699.45,"What you must avoid is a mismatch between the model you published and the calculation that is actually used."],[699.96,701.76,"Third, keep prices apart."],[701.76,711.27,"In module 3.3 we saw that quality evaluators should score without seeing prices, because knowing the price biases the quality judgement."],[711.55,725.88,"If your tenant has no sealed-envelope setting, you can still keep prices apart by role, giving quality evaluators no access to the commercial response, or by stage, scoring quality before the commercial part is opened."],[725.88,726.79,"And fourth, record."],[726.79,730.21,"Scores and their rationale are stored with the event."],[730.49,740.03,"If a losing bidder asks for a debrief, or an auditor asks why the contract went to supplier B, the answer is in the event, not in somebody's inbox."],[740.52,743.38,"A short story shows why the record matters."],[743.38,750.25,"A losing bidder in a facilities tender asks for a debrief and challenges its quality score on mobilisation."],[750.53,762.27,"If the evaluators' individual scores, the consensus score and a one-line rationale per criterion sit in the event, the buyer can answer in an afternoon, calmly and consistently."],[762.27,770.45,"If the scores live in three personal spreadsheets and an email thread, the same question takes a week, and the answers may not match."],[770.73,772.8,"The first buyer looks professional."],[772.8,777.35,"The second looks as if the decision was made first and justified later."],[778.3,782.25,"Now a more advanced tool: Coupa Sourcing Optimization."],[782.25,782.87,"Four cards."],[783.37,789.1,"Origin: it was Trade Extensions, which Coupa acquired in May 2017."],[789.1,797.0,"Expressive bidding: suppliers can bid on packages and bundles, offer volume discounts, and propose alternatives."],[797.28,808.26,"Instead of forcing every supplier to price every lot separately, you let them say, for example, I will do sites one and two together for this price, or all three for that price."],[808.26,813.11,"Scenarios: you run what-if comparisons of different awards under constraints."],[813.39,818.55,"And rounds: several bidding rounds, with the award flowing into contracts and catalogues."],[819.05,821.35,"A note of caution from the speaker notes."],[821.35,827.42,"These are vendor descriptions, and the savings figures in Coupa's case studies are vendor claims."],[827.42,829.46,"Use the tool for what it does well."],[829.74,841.21,"In indirect procurement that typically means multi-site facility management, telecom lines, hotel programmes, print, courier services, and temporary staffing by region."],[841.21,844.2,"The common factor is many lots that interact."],[844.48,850.71,"When winning one lot changes what a supplier can offer on another, optimisation helps you see the trade-offs."],[851.66,855.84,"Let's see it on a worked example: optimisation for Nordvia cleaning."],[855.84,857.51,"The example is fictional."],[858.01,861.49,"Three sites, in two countries, and four to six bidders."],[861.49,864.56,"Bidders price each lot, and any bundle they like."],[864.56,869.77,"One supplier might offer a discount if it wins both sites in the same country."],[869.77,872.72,"Another might offer all three sites together."],[872.72,877.53,"There is a constraint: at least one supplier per country with local capacity."],[877.81,879.78,"Now look at the three scenarios."],[879.78,882.7,"Scenario one awards the best bid per lot."],[882.7,888.36,"Scenario two finds the lowest total cost, which may mean accepting a bundle."],[888.36,894.9,"Scenario three limits the result to a maximum of two suppliers, to keep contract management simple."],[895.4,898.89,"Then the buyer compares cost against risk before choosing."],[898.89,906.56,"Scenario two might be cheapest, but if it puts all three sites with one supplier, the Group carries concentration risk."],[906.56,911.25,"Scenario one might mean three suppliers and three contracts to manage."],[911.53,918.84,"Scenario three might cost a little more than the lowest total, but it balances cost, risk and management effort."],[918.84,921.56,"Optimisation does not make the decision."],[921.56,923.86,"It makes the cost of each choice visible."],[924.36,927.54,"And here is the honest point in the speaker notes."],[927.54,930.12,"With three sites, a spreadsheet can do this."],[930.12,934.78,"With thirty sites across several entities, optimisation earns its place."],[934.78,936.65,"That is the Group-level case."],[936.93,946.42,"If the entities of the Group pooled their cleaning demand into one event, the number of possible combinations would explode, and a spreadsheet would no longer be reliable."],[947.37,950.95,"This table shows Coupa's role by negotiation type."],[950.95,952.82,"It connects the strategy to the tool."],[952.82,953.96,"Six rows."],[954.46,959.67,"Price-only, with many suppliers: an RFQ plus an English reverse auction."],[959.67,967.7,"Few suppliers, or an incumbent-heavy market: a reverse Japanese auction or a sealed BAFO, best and final offer."],[967.7,974.75,"Quality-led services: an RFP, then negotiation rounds, then a BAFO, using follow-on events."],[975.03,983.08,"Coupa documents that follow-on events can carry over supplier responses, so suppliers do not have to re-enter everything."],[983.08,988.57,"Panel call-offs: a mini-competition as an RFP event sent to the panel members."],[988.57,992.12,"Multi-lot with bundles: Sourcing Optimization."],[992.4,998.29,"And sole source: Coupa's role is data preparation and the CLM workflow, not competition."],[998.29,1004.85,"There is no one to compete with, so the tool supports the negotiation instead of replacing it."],[1005.36,1007.74,"Notice what is common to every row."],[1007.74,1012.11,"The Message Center and the audit trail serve every negotiation type."],[1012.11,1018.19,"Even in a sole-source negotiation, keeping the formal exchanges in one place protects you."],[1018.19,1023.54,"Session 4 deepens this matrix, when we look at negotiation planning in detail."],[1024.03,1027.34,"Let's make that concrete with a Nordvia example."],[1027.34,1029.86,"Take P4, network and telecom."],[1029.86,1038.3,"Standard mobile tariffs, with several qualified operators, might sit in the first row: an RFQ and then an English reverse auction."],[1038.58,1049.1,"The core network that claims systems depend on sits closer to the third row: an RFP, negotiation rounds on service levels and exit terms, then a BAFO."],[1049.1,1056.46,"The same supplier, Telmo, can appear in two rows at once, because the rows describe the negotiation, not the supplier."],[1056.46,1059.79,"That is why you design the route lot by lot."],[1060.74,1062.91,"Now, what happens after the award?"],[1062.91,1066.18,"This flow shows the path from award to buying channel."],[1066.68,1067.39,"Award."],[1067.39,1068.4,"Then the contract in CLM."],[1068.4,1072.42,"Then the clause library and the approvals."],[1072.42,1080.91,"Then the supplier is onboarded in SIM, supplier information management, where InfoSec, DORA and ESG questionnaires sit."],[1080.91,1083.46,"Then catalogue or contract-backed buying."],[1083.46,1087.01,"And finally, invoices matched to the contract."],[1087.51,1090.32,"Why does this flow belong in a sourcing lesson?"],[1090.32,1095.45,"Because a saving that never reaches the buying channel is a saving on paper only."],[1095.45,1098.23,"Picture a successful office supplies auction."],[1098.23,1100.14,"The new prices are excellent."],[1100.14,1105.6,"But the old catalogue stays live for three months, and users keep ordering from it."],[1105.88,1109.94,"The savings report says one thing, and the invoices say another."],[1109.94,1118.66,"Coupa describes winning terms flowing into draft contracts and catalogues, and that is a vendor description, so check how it works in your tenant."],[1118.66,1121.8,"The principle holds regardless."],[1121.8,1122.13,"Close the loop."],[1122.13,1124.91,"Session 4 covers the handover in depth."],[1125.86,1128.15,"Good practice: templates and audit trail."],[1128.15,1129.12,"Four cards."],[1129.63,1138.42,"Templates by route: a soft facility management tender template, a panel mini-competition template, an RFQ plus auction template."],[1138.42,1142.97,"Everything in the event: the draft contract, the weights, the annexes."],[1142.97,1144.18,"Not in emails."],[1144.46,1152.1,"Message Center only: every clarification is logged, and every answer goes to all bidders when it concerns all of them."],[1152.1,1154.6,"That is your equal-treatment evidence."],[1154.6,1163.49,"And regulatory questions: DORA and outsourcing questionnaires reused from SIM, so that you do not reinvent them for every event."],[1163.98,1166.41,"The speaker note captures the idea."],[1166.41,1168.99,"Templates turn good practice into the default."],[1168.99,1173.42,"A buyer under time pressure will reuse whatever is easiest."],[1173.42,1183.8,"If the easiest thing is a well-designed template with the knock-out questionnaire, the scoring sheet and the draft contract already attached, good practice happens automatically."],[1184.08,1187.59,"So ask yourself: who owns the templates in your team today?"],[1187.59,1190.35,"If the answer is nobody, that is a quick win."],[1190.85,1194.0,"Think about the audit trail from the other side, too."],[1194.0,1206.4,"Under Solvency II, an insurer's outsourcing of critical or important functions is subject to governance requirements, and DORA adds requirements for ICT third-party arrangements."],[1206.68,1221.6,"When Internal Audit or a supervisor asks how a supplier was selected, the strongest answer is a complete event: the invitation list, every clarification, the scores, the award rationale and the approvals, all in one place."],[1221.6,1227.31,"A buyer who has to reconstruct that from email is exposed, however good the decision was."],[1228.27,1233.97,"Let's bring it all together in a worked example: the set-up sheet for Nordvia P1 cleaning."],[1233.97,1239.04,"The example is fictional, and it is the model answer for the practice you will do next."],[1239.54,1243.43,"Event: an RFP, Private, three lots, with a bundle option."],[1243.43,1252.25,"Questionnaires: a knock-out questionnaire on wage compliance and insurance, a quality questionnaire, and an ESG questionnaire."],[1252.53,1264.05,"Attachments: the hybrid specification, which combines outputs with a few essential inputs, the staff data for any transfer of employees, and the draft contract with its KPIs."],[1264.55,1276.25,"Scoring: fifty percent quality and fifty percent price, with the relative price formula, where the lowest price divided by each bid price, times one hundred, gives the price score."],[1276.25,1278.89,"Quality is scored without prices."],[1278.89,1280.83,"Follow-on: a BAFO event to the best two."],[1280.83,1283.54,"And notice the last words: no auction."],[1283.82,1286.39,"Everything we said in module 3.4 applies."],[1286.39,1292.26,"A labour-intensive service, few strong bidders, and a relationship that matters."],[1292.77,1296.15,"Now, what would you confirm in your tenant for this event?"],[1296.15,1298.16,"The speaker note gives two answers."],[1298.16,1307.21,"First, the scoring set-up: can the fifty-fifty model with the relative price formula be configured, or do you run it in the evaluation template?"],[1307.49,1314.4,"Second, how prices are kept from the quality evaluators: by a sealed setting, by role, or by stage."],[1314.4,1318.65,"If you can answer those two questions before issue, the event is ready."],[1319.6,1320.69,"A short poll."],[1320.69,1324.03,"Which part of your RFx plan are you least sure about?"],[1324.03,1330.65,"The event sequence, the evaluation model, auction or not, the Coupa set-up, or the regulatory gates?"],[1330.65,1333.34,"Think about your own project for a moment."],[1333.62,1339.21,"Whatever you choose, that is the line on your set-up sheet where you should spend the most time in the practice."],[1339.71,1345.39,"If you chose the Coupa set-up, you are not alone, and the rest of this lesson is designed for you."],[1345.39,1354.52,"If you chose the evaluation model, go back to the sensitivity analysis from module 3.3 and test your weights before you build the event."],[1354.8,1361.73,"If you chose the regulatory gates, that is a conversation with Risk or Compliance, and it belongs in your fieldwork."],[1362.68,1364.11,"Now it is your turn."],[1364.11,1367.17,"Practice 3.5: your Coupa event set-up sheet."],[1367.17,1369.36,"Fifteen minutes in your workbook."],[1369.85,1393.08,"Fill the ten lines of the set-up sheet: event types and sequence, privacy and invited suppliers, lots and items, questionnaires and knock-out questions, attachments, the pre-bidding phase and timeline, the auction format with feedback and closing if there is one, the scoring set-up and who sees prices, the Message Center rules, and the award, CLM and catalogue handover."],[1393.36,1396.2,"Mark each setting you must confirm in your tenant."],[1396.2,1398.88,"Write who will build the event and who approves it."],[1398.88,1402.47,"And then the quality check: give your sheet to a colleague."],[1402.47,1404.2,"Could they build the event from it?"],[1404.7,1406.96,"That neighbour test is the standard."],[1406.96,1411.24,"If a colleague cannot build the event from your sheet, it is not finished."],[1411.24,1418.91,"Pause the lesson now if you would like to complete the set-up sheet in the Modules tab of your workbook, and continue when you are ready."],[1419.86,1420.62,"Welcome back."],[1420.62,1422.48,"Here are the debrief questions."],[1422.98,1425.77,"Which settings did you mark confirm in tenant?"],[1425.77,1432.3,"Most people mark the scoring set-up and the separation of prices, and many mark the auction extensions."],[1432.3,1436.03,"Who needs Sourcing Optimization, and who only thinks so?"],[1436.03,1439.58,"If you have three lots and no bundles, you probably do not."],[1439.86,1443.84,"If you have many interacting lots across entities, you probably do."],[1443.84,1446.55,"Where will your draft contract and weights live?"],[1446.55,1450.27,"The right answer is in the event, attached before issue."],[1450.27,1453.29,"And what will you ask your Coupa administrator this week?"],[1453.29,1455.07,"Write the questions down now."],[1455.35,1465.35,"In the live session, the facilitator collects the tenant questions from the whole cohort and sends them to the Coupa administrator, so that everyone benefits from the answers."],[1466.31,1469.47,"Let's recap module 3.5 in three lines."],[1469.96,1470.47,"One."],[1470.47,1476.04,"Coupa documents the event types, the auction formats, the pre-bidding phase, lots and feedback."],[1476.04,1478.73,"Use them with confidence."],[1478.73,1479.45,"Two."],[1479.45,1486.13,"Confirm overtime, decrements, sealed opening and scoring in your tenant before you promise them to anyone."],[1486.13,1486.19,"Three."],[1486.47,1493.81,"Put everything in the event, and close the loop to CLM and catalogues, so that people actually buy against the new terms."],[1494.32,1498.34,"In the next lesson, we put the whole chain to work on one lot."],[1498.34,1510.45,"Case 3 takes the Nordvia HQ cleaning lot end to end: an abnormally low bid, a bidder that wants to change the term, a close score, and a Head of Real Estate who wants a decision this month."],[1510.73,1514.76,"Then we close the session with Fieldwork 3 and a bridge to Session 4."],[1514.76,1515.81,"See you there."]]},{"id":"s3-l6","session":"s3","title":"Case 3, fieldwork and close","summary":"Make and defend an award recommendation on a close, contested tender, and prepare a validated evaluation model for Session 4.","from":104,"to":113,"audio":"audio/s3-l6.mp3","duration":1416.5,"segs":[[104,0.6,13.36,"Welcome back. This is the last lesson of Session 3. It covers Case 3, the HQ cleaning lot end to end, then Fieldwork 3, and then a recap of the whole session and a bridge to Session 4."],[104,13.86,60.43,"Why a case at this point? Because every module in this session gave you one tool. Module 3.1 chose the sourcing question and the event. Module 3.2 designed the request. Module 3.3 built and ran the evaluation. Module 3.4 told us when an auction helps and when it hurts. And module 3.5 put it all into Coupa. A real tender does not arrive in modules. It arrives as one messy situation, with a deadline, a stakeholder who wants an answer, and bids that do not behave the way the model expected. That is what Nordvia Facilities gives us now. The case is fictional, and it uses the same numbers you saw in module 3.3, so the focus is on decisions, not arithmetic."],[104,60.93,78.83,"As you work through it, keep one question in mind. If this tender were challenged tomorrow, by a losing bidder, by Internal Audit, or by the Head of Real Estate herself, could you explain every step from the published rules? That question is the thread that runs through the whole of Session 3."],[105,79.78,82.41,"Here is the situation. Read along with me."],[105,82.91,121.48,"Office cleaning at Nordvia is worth 2.4 million euros, across three sites in two countries. The contract has been rolled over since 2019, without a competition. There are quality complaints at headquarters, and the Head of Real Estate wants a decision this month. Following the strategy from Session 2, Procurement issued an RFP with a hybrid specification, meaning outputs for most areas with a few essential inputs, a term of three years plus one plus one, and a published evaluation model of fifty percent quality and fifty percent price. Four bids arrived for the HQ lot."],[105,121.98,172.63,"Let me give you the four bids, as annual prices. Bidder A, the incumbent, at 690 thousand euros, with a quality score of 62. A proposes the same method as today, and staff continuity. Bidder B at 610 thousand euros, with a quality score of 74. B offers an output-based plan, with robot floor care at night. Bidder C at 520 thousand euros, with a quality score of 58. Its price is far below the others, and its staffing hours look below the wage floor. And Bidder D at 655 thousand euros, with the highest quality score, 81. D has the strongest quality and ESG plan, but it requires a four-year term. The quality scores were given by three evaluators after a consensus meeting."],[105,173.13,212.36,"Now let's run the published model, as we did in module 3.3. The price formula is relative: the lowest price divided by each bid, times one hundred. With all four bids, C is the lowest, so C gets 100 price points. B gets about 85, D about 79, and A about 75. Combine with quality at fifty-fifty. D comes first with about 80.2 points. B is second with about 79.6. C is third with about 79.0. A is last with about 68.7. Notice how close the top three are. Less than one and a half points separate them."],[105,212.86,243.76,"And notice something more uncomfortable. C, the bid that looks too cheap to be real, is distorting everyone else's price score. Because the relative formula anchors on the lowest bid, C's low price pulls down the price points of every other bidder. C is about 20 percent below the average of the other three bids. If you remember the poll in module 3.3, this is exactly the situation it described. So the first question of the case is not who wins. It is what to do about C."],[105,244.26,277.78,"Before we move on, what about the incumbent? Bidder A scores last on every version of the model. Its price is the highest and its quality score of 62 reflects the complaints at headquarters. That is useful information in itself. The rollover since 2019 was not protecting Nordvia from a bad deal. It was hiding one. The incumbent is not entitled to a second chance because it is the incumbent. But it is entitled to the same treatment as everyone else, which means a clear, respectful debrief when the result is known."],[106,278.73,284.27,"Here is your team's task. Twenty-five minutes, on the Case tab of your workbook. Five questions."],[106,284.77,306.36,"First, how do you handle Bidder C? Second, whom do you negotiate with, or ask for a BAFO, a best and final offer? Third, auction or not, and why? Fourth, how do you treat D's condition of a four-year term? And fifth, your award recommendation and its conditions: due diligence, the sensitivity of the result, and mobilisation."],[106,306.86,349.71,"A few hints before you start, without giving the answer away. For C, think about what the evidence says about abnormally low bids, and what a should-cost model for cleaning looks like: hours, multiplied by the collectively agreed wage, plus on-costs, supervision, consumables, overhead and margin. For the auction question, take out the preconditions checklist from module 3.4 and go through it line by line. For D, ask yourself what the other bidders were told about the term, and whether they would have bid differently if a four-year term had been allowed. And for the recommendation, remember that the Head of Real Estate wants a decision, but what she needs is a decision she can defend."],[106,350.21,367.55,"In the live session, teams often want to run an auction between B and D. If that is your instinct, hold on to it, and test it honestly against the checklist. Pause the lesson now if you would like to write your team answer in the Case tab of your workbook, and continue when you are ready."],[106,368.05,402.38,"One more hint about C. A should-cost check does not need to be complicated. Take the cleaning area and frequencies in the specification, estimate the productive hours a competent supplier needs, multiply by the collective wage plus on-costs, and add a reasonable margin for supervision, consumables and overhead. If C's price only works with far fewer hours than that, the bid is not cheaper. It is a different, smaller service, or it relies on paying people less than the floor. Either way, it is not comparable with the others."],[106,402.88,426.35,"And a reminder on the timeline. The Head of Real Estate wants a decision this month. That pressure is real, and it is legitimate. But a decision taken in a hurry on a doubtful bid can cost far more time later, if the contract fails or the award is challenged. Part of your answer is how you manage her expectations: what you can decide this month, and what must still be checked."],[107,427.3,430.48,"Welcome back. Let's debrief, using four questions."],[107,430.98,473.65,"First: who would auction, and which precondition fails? Let's go through the list. Precise specification? Partly. It is a hybrid specification for a service, and quality differences matter. Four to five qualified bidders? No. Once C is dealt with, you have two strong bidders, B and D, and a weaker incumbent. Price decides? No, quality is half the model. Low switching cost, and a relationship that tolerates it? Cleaning involves staff on site every day, possibly staff transfer, and a supplier you need to work with closely for three years or more. Several lines fail, and one no is enough. So, no auction. A BAFO on the published model does the job."],[107,474.15,513.4,"Second: who accepted D's four-year term, and is that fair to B? This is where many teams slip. D's bid is attractive, and it is tempting to just say yes to the condition. But the RFP published three plus one plus one. Bidders A, B and C priced that structure. A longer guaranteed term has value to a supplier. It spreads mobilisation and equipment costs, like B's floor robots, over more years. If you accept the longer term for D alone, you are comparing D's price on one basis with B's price on another. That is not equal treatment, and it would be hard to defend if B challenged the award."],[107,513.9,565.58,"Third: how did you write the sensitivity into the recommendation? Let's look at the numbers without C. At fifty-fifty with the relative formula, D scores about 87.1 and B about 87.0. D leads by one tenth of a point. At sixty percent quality and forty percent price, D leads more clearly, about 85.9 against 84.4. At forty percent quality and sixty percent price, B leads, about 89.6 against 88.3. And with a linear price formula instead of the relative one, B also leads, by a fraction. In other words, the winner depends on choices that were made before the bids arrived. That is exactly why weights and formulas must be fixed and published before issue. And it is why the recommendation must say, honestly, that the decision is close."],[107,566.08,590.76,"Fourth: what would you design differently next time? The speaker note gives the answer. A published quality threshold of 60 would have excluded C automatically, because C scored 58. Many tender designs set a minimum quality score that a bid must reach before its price is even considered. That protects the model from exactly this distortion. It is the design lesson for Fieldwork 3."],[107,591.26,619.98,"There is a fifth question worth asking, even though it is not on the slide. What would you tell the incumbent? A transition from A to a new supplier means mobilisation, possibly staff transfer, and a period where A is still cleaning the building while knowing it has lost. Good practice is to plan that handover in the contract and in the communication, not to leave it to chance. The relationship with an outgoing supplier still matters for the last months of service."],[108,620.93,630.21,"Here is one strong answer. Note the word one. There is more than one good answer, and this slide is shown only after the teams have presented theirs."],[108,630.71,673.45,"Bidder C. Send a written request asking C to explain its hours and its price, and check the explanation against a should-cost model built on the collective wage. Public procurement guidance explicitly asks buyers to seek explanations for abnormally low bids, and that is good practice for a private insurer too. If C's explanation does not convince, reject the bid as abnormally low and record why. Do not exclude C at once without asking, and do not award it without checking. Either shortcut creates risk: the first a fairness challenge, the second a contract that fails within months, with staff paid below the floor and Nordvia's name attached."],[108,673.95,689.22,"Next step. A BAFO with B and D, on the published fifty-fifty model, and no auction. Both are invited to improve their offer, under the same rules, in a follow-on event in Coupa, with every clarification through the Message Center."],[108,689.72,712.08,"D's term. Ask D to price the compliant three plus one plus one term. You can let D explain what it would offer for a longer term, but the comparison is made on the structure everyone bid against. Do not change the structure for one bidder. If Nordvia genuinely wants a four-year term, that is a decision to make for all bidders, not a favour to one."],[108,712.58,764.17,"And the recommendation. Award to the best BAFO score, subject to due diligence. Due diligence here means checking references, wage compliance, insurance, and the mobilisation plan, and for B, checking that night-time robot floor care actually works in the HQ building. And state clearly that the decision is close. A good recommendation to the Head of Real Estate might read like this. On the published model, D and B are within a fraction of a point after clarification of C. We have invited both to a final offer under the same rules. We will recommend the higher score, subject to checks, and we will tell you before the end of the month. That is fast, fair and honest about sensitivity. The logic behind it, in the words of the speaker note, is fairness, published rules and honesty about sensitivity."],[108,764.67,798.19,"How do you run the BAFO in practice? Send both bidders the same letter, in the same follow-on event, on the same day. Tell them which elements they may improve, for example price, mobilisation plan or KPIs, and confirm that the published fifty-fifty model and the relative price formula still apply. Set a single deadline. Do not tell B what D offered, or D what B offered. And do not hold a second, informal round afterwards. The BAFO is the final round, just as the auction result would have been the price."],[109,799.14,810.15,"That brings us to the close of the session. Fieldwork 3 and next steps. And the subtitle is the most important instruction of all: validate the weights before anyone sees a bid."],[109,810.65,845.55,"Why repeat it so strongly? Because you have just seen what happens without it. In Case 3, the ranking between B and D flips with a change of ten points in the weighting, or a change in the price formula. If those choices had been left open until after the bids arrived, every stakeholder with a favourite supplier would have an argument for the weights that suits them. Once the bids are visible, any change to the model looks like manipulation, even when it is made in good faith. Fixing the model first is not bureaucracy. It is what makes the result defensible."],[109,846.05,881.62,"Think about the alternative from the business owner's point of view. Suppose the Head of Real Estate had told you before issue that service quality at headquarters mattered more than anything else, and you had agreed sixty percent quality. D would lead clearly, and the decision would be easier. Or suppose Finance had insisted on sixty percent price. B would lead. Neither choice is wrong. What would be wrong is making that choice after seeing the bids. The weights are a statement of what the business values, and they only mean something if they are made before anyone knows who benefits."],[110,882.57,886.81,"Here is Fieldwork 3, to complete before Session 4. Five steps."],[110,887.31,905.92,"First, the RFx pack outline for your own project: instructions to bidders, the specification type, the lots, the pricing schedule, the questionnaire, the draft contract, and the regulatory annexes. An outline, not the full documents. But every heading should be there, with a line on what goes in it."],[110,906.42,915.23,"Second, the weights validated. With the business owner, before issue, and recorded. We will look at how to hold that conversation on the next slide."],[110,915.73,942.76,"Third, test bid sets. Run three fictional sets of bids through your model. Make one set where a cheap bid has weak quality, one where a strong bid is expensive, and one where two bids are very close. Watch what your price formula does. If a single low bid squeezes everyone else's price score, as C did in the case, you will see it now rather than after issue. And check whether a quality threshold would change the outcome."],[110,943.26,975.41,"Fourth, tenant settings. Confirm with your Coupa administrator the settings you flagged in Practice 3.5: scoring, sealed opening, and auction extensions if you plan an auction. And fifth, regulatory gates. Where your triage in Session 1 flagged ICT services, or a critical or important function, agree the DORA and outsourcing gate criteria with Risk or Compliance. Knock-out questions on those topics belong in the event, so they must be settled before you build it."],[110,975.91,993.36,"The speaker note explains why this matters for what comes next. The validated evaluation model is the core of part three of your Sourcing Case. Session 4 builds the negotiation plan on top of it. You cannot plan a negotiation well if you do not know what the model rewards."],[110,993.86,1023.92,"A word on the RFx pack outline. The most common gap is the draft contract. Buyers plan to write it after the award, when they know the winner. But the draft contract is where you negotiate through the bids: service levels, service credits, indexation, exit and data return. If it is attached to the RFP, every bidder prices the same obligations. If it comes later, you negotiate it with one supplier who already knows it has won, and your leverage has gone."],[111,1024.87,1033.43,"Now, the conversation that many buyers avoid: the weights conversation with the business owner. Two columns, what to ask and what to show."],[111,1033.93,1072.63,"Look at the left-hand column first, the questions to ask. If the bids come in close, these weights decide. Are you comfortable? That one question changes the conversation. The weights stop being a procurement formality and become the business owner's decision. Next: what would make you unhappy with the winner? This often reveals a criterion that is missing from the model, or one that is really a knock-out, not a weighted score. And third: which criterion would you never trade? If the answer is, for example, data security for an IT service, that criterion probably belongs in the knock-out gates rather than in the weights."],[111,1073.13,1087.68,"Now the right-hand column, what to show. A test bid set where the ranking flips. The knock-out gates that are not negotiable. And where the model will live in Coupa, so the business owner knows it is written into the event and cannot drift."],[111,1088.18,1131.9,"Let me give you a short example of how this plays out. A Nordvia HR director owns a tender for an employee assistance programme. She asks for seventy percent quality, because she cares about service. The buyer shows her a test bid set. At seventy-thirty, a provider that is 40 percent more expensive wins because of a slightly better quality score. The figures are illustrative. She pauses, and says that is not what she meant. They agree sixty-forty, plus a quality threshold, so that weak providers are excluded and price then decides among the good ones. As the speaker note says, a business owner who sees the model reverse a ranking understands why the weights must be agreed before issue."],[111,1132.4,1166.49,"What if the business owner pushes back and wants to keep the weights open until the bids arrive? Explain the risk calmly. Without fixed weights, every evaluator and every stakeholder can argue afterwards for the model that favours their preferred supplier, and the losing bidders can argue that the rules changed. Then offer the reassurance she is really looking for: the test bid sets let her see the consequences now, and the quality threshold protects her from a weak, cheap winner. Record what she pushed back on and what you agreed. That record is part of the fieldwork."],[112,1167.44,1177.17,"Let's recap the whole of Session 3 in five lines. This session was about one question: how do we run the competition well, once the strategy is clear?"],[112,1177.67,1197.53,"One. The tool follows the strategy. Choose the question, then the event. An RFI, an RFQ, an RFP, a framework with mini-competitions, an auction, or a negotiation: each answers a different question, and the category strategy from Session 2 tells you which question you are asking."],[112,1198.03,1214.43,"Two. Design the request so bids are comparable, and fix the model before issue. A clear specification, a pricing schedule that forces like-for-like answers, a draft contract attached, and weights and price formula published before anyone sees a bid."],[112,1214.93,1234.56,"Three. Run it fairly. One channel for clarifications, individual scores before consensus, and a sensitivity check before the recommendation. Fairness is not only ethics. It is what lets you defend the result, to a losing bidder, to an auditor, or to a stakeholder who preferred another supplier."],[112,1235.06,1255.9,"Four. Auction only when the preconditions hold, and in the right format. Precise specification, four to five qualified bidders, spare capacity, price or a formula decides, low switching cost, and a credible alternative with a real intention to award. And expect five to fifteen percent at best, reported net."],[112,1256.4,1268.59,"Five. Build it in Coupa, and confirm what your tenant really does. Use the documented features with confidence, and turn everything else into a question for your Coupa administrator before you promise it."],[112,1269.09,1282.74,"The speaker note suggests one last step, and I invite you to do it now. Write down the one line from these five that you will apply first in your own project. Just one. That is the line you will be held to when we meet again."],[112,1283.24,1305.04,"Notice how the five lines connect. The strategy chooses the event. The event design makes the bids comparable. The fair process makes the result defensible. The auction checklist stops you using the sharpest tool in the wrong place. And Coupa holds it all together with an audit trail. Case 3 used every one of those lines on a single lot."],[113,1305.99,1310.11,"And that brings us to the bridge. Next session: how do we close it well?"],[113,1310.61,1350.36,"Session 3 ended with a recommendation and a BAFO. Session 4 picks up from there. We will negotiate, contract and hand over. Not every category ends with a competition. Some have only one realistic supplier, and that changes everything about the negotiation. So Session 4 opens with a role-play: the Kelvar sole-source negotiation, for Nordvia's core insurance software licences. There is no competitor to point to, the last renewal brought an uplift, and there is an auto-renewal clause. Please reread the Kelvar data in the case materials before we meet, so that you arrive ready to plan."],[113,1350.86,1376.38,"Please bring three things. Your RFx plan. Your validated evaluation model, with the weights agreed with your business owner. And your e-negotiation design, meaning your choice of BAFO, negotiation rounds or auction, and the rules you would publish. Session 4 will build the negotiation plan on top of them, then turn the result into a contract, and hand it over to the people who will manage it."],[113,1376.88,1401.2,"Before you close this lesson, take two minutes for a personal check. Look back at your own project. Do you know which event sequence you will use? Is your evaluation model written down, with a price formula you have tested? Have you decided, honestly, whether an auction passes the checklist? Do you know which Coupa settings you still need to confirm? If any answer is no, that is your first task for the fieldwork."],[113,1401.7,1415.33,"Thank you for your work in this session. You have gone from a strategy to a fair, defensible, well-built competition. Complete Fieldwork 3, have the weights conversation, and I look forward to seeing you in Session 4."]],"questions":[{"after":105,"q":"With all four bids and the relative price formula, Bidder C at 520 thousand euros sets the lowest price. What effect does that have on the other bidders?","options":["None, because each bid is scored on its own","It lowers every other bidder's price score, because the formula anchors on the lowest bid","It raises the quality scores of the other bidders","It automatically excludes C from the evaluation"],"answer":1,"explain":"The relative formula divides the lowest price by each bid. A very low outlier pulls down every other bidder's price points, which is why an abnormally low bid must be dealt with before ranking.","t":277.78},{"after":106,"q":"Your team is tempted to exclude Bidder C immediately because its price looks impossible. What is the better first step?","options":["Award to C, because it scores well on price","Tell the other bidders to match C's price","Exclude C without recording a reason to avoid a dispute","Ask C for a written explanation of its hours and price, check it against a should-cost model on the collective wage, and reject and record only if it fails"],"answer":3,"explain":"Good practice for abnormally low bids is to seek an explanation and test it. Excluding without asking risks a fairness challenge; awarding without checking risks a failing contract below the wage floor.","t":426.35},{"after":107,"q":"Without C, D leads B by about one tenth of a point at fifty fifty, but B leads at forty sixty or with a linear price formula. What should the recommendation to the Head of Real Estate say?","options":["That the decision is close and sensitive to the model, with a BAFO on the published model before the award","That D clearly wins and no further step is needed","That the weights should now be changed to forty sixty so B wins","That the tender must be cancelled"],"answer":0,"explain":"The published model stays fixed, and the recommendation must be honest that the result is sensitive. A BAFO under the same rules gives both bidders a fair final chance.","t":619.98},{"after":108,"q":"D says it will only sign if the term is four years, while the RFP published three plus one plus one. What is the fairest response?","options":["Accept the four-year term for D, as it has the best quality","Offer all bidders a four-year term only after D wins","Ask D to price the compliant three plus one plus one term, so all bids are compared on the same structure","Disqualify D without asking"],"answer":2,"explain":"A longer term has value to a supplier, so accepting it for one bidder compares bids on different bases. If Nordvia truly wants a four-year term, that is a decision for all bidders, not a favour to one.","t":798.19},{"after":109,"q":"Why does this module insist on validating the weights before anyone sees a bid?","options":["Because Coupa will not accept weight changes after issue","Because suppliers set the weights","Because weights do not affect the ranking","Because once bids are visible, any change looks like manipulation and lets stakeholders argue for weights that suit their favourite"],"answer":3,"explain":"Case 3 showed the ranking flipping with the weights. Fixing them first is what makes the result defensible, even when a later change would be made in good faith.","t":881.62},{"after":110,"q":"In your Fieldwork 3 test bid sets, a single cheap, low-quality bid squeezes every other bidder's price score. Which design change from the case would best protect your model?","options":["Removing the price criterion","A published quality threshold that bids must reach before price is considered","Running an English reverse auction among all bidders","Keeping the weights secret from bidders"],"answer":1,"explain":"A published quality threshold of 60 would have excluded C automatically in the case. Testing bid sets before issue shows the distortion while you can still fix the design.","t":1023.92},{"after":111,"q":"A business owner asks for seventy percent quality. What is the most effective way to validate that choice with her?","options":["Show a test bid set where the ranking flips, and ask whether she is comfortable that these weights decide if bids come in close","Accept it, since the business owner decides","Refuse and impose fifty fifty","Wait for the bids and adjust if the result looks wrong"],"answer":0,"explain":"Seeing the model reverse a ranking makes the consequences concrete. The question about close bids turns the weights into the business owner's own, informed decision before issue.","t":1166.49},{"after":113,"q":"Which statement best links Session 3 to Session 4?","options":["Session 4 replaces the evaluation model with a negotiation","Session 4 only covers auctions","Session 4 builds a negotiation plan, contract and handover on top of the RFx plan, the validated evaluation model and the e-negotiation design from Session 3","Session 4 repeats the market analysis from Session 1"],"answer":2,"explain":"Participants bring the RFx plan, validated model and e-negotiation design to Session 4, which opens with the Kelvar sole-source role-play and then moves to contracting and handover.","t":1415.33}],"cues":[[0.6,1.38,"Welcome back."],[1.38,4.34,"This is the last lesson of Session 3."],[4.34,13.36,"It covers Case 3, the HQ cleaning lot end to end, then Fieldwork 3, and then a recap of the whole session and a bridge to Session 4."],[13.86,15.57,"Why a case at this point?"],[15.57,18.86,"Because every module in this session gave you one tool."],[18.86,23.3,"Module 3.1 chose the sourcing question and the event."],[23.3,25.88,"Module 3.2 designed the request."],[25.88,28.66,"Module 3.3 built and ran the evaluation."],[28.66,33.47,"Module 3.4 told us when an auction helps and when it hurts."],[33.47,35.81,"And module 3.5 put it all into Coupa."],[36.09,38.87,"A real tender does not arrive in modules."],[38.87,48.75,"It arrives as one messy situation, with a deadline, a stakeholder who wants an answer, and bids that do not behave the way the model expected."],[48.75,51.69,"That is what Nordvia Facilities gives us now."],[51.97,60.44,"The case is fictional, and it uses the same numbers you saw in module 3.3, so the focus is on decisions, not arithmetic."],[60.93,63.95,"As you work through it, keep one question in mind."],[63.95,74.83,"If this tender were challenged tomorrow, by a losing bidder, by Internal Audit, or by the Head of Real Estate herself, could you explain every step from the published rules?"],[74.83,78.83,"That question is the thread that runs through the whole of Session 3."],[79.78,81.21,"Here is the situation."],[81.21,82.4,"Read along with me."],[82.91,89.53,"Office cleaning at Nordvia is worth 2.4 million euros, across three sites in two countries."],[89.53,93.67,"The contract has been rolled over since 2019, without a competition."],[93.67,100.34,"There are quality complaints at headquarters, and the Head of Real Estate wants a decision this month."],[100.62,118.93,"Following the strategy from Session 2, Procurement issued an RFP with a hybrid specification, meaning outputs for most areas with a few essential inputs, a term of three years plus one plus one, and a published evaluation model of fifty percent quality and fifty percent price."],[118.93,121.48,"Four bids arrived for the HQ lot."],[121.98,125.8,"Let me give you the four bids, as annual prices."],[125.8,130.97,"Bidder A, the incumbent, at 690 thousand euros, with a quality score of 62."],[130.97,136.37,"A proposes the same method as today, and staff continuity."],[136.37,140.41,"Bidder B at 610 thousand euros, with a quality score of 74."],[140.41,144.91,"B offers an output-based plan, with robot floor care at night."],[145.19,149.93,"Bidder C at 520 thousand euros, with a quality score of 58."],[149.93,155.64,"Its price is far below the others, and its staffing hours look below the wage floor."],[155.64,162.12,"And Bidder D at 655 thousand euros, with the highest quality score, 81."],[162.12,167.68,"D has the strongest quality and ESG plan, but it requires a four-year term."],[167.96,172.63,"The quality scores were given by three evaluators after a consensus meeting."],[173.13,177.33,"Now let's run the published model, as we did in module 3.3."],[177.33,184.12,"The price formula is relative: the lowest price divided by each bid, times one hundred."],[184.12,188.48,"With all four bids, C is the lowest, so C gets 100 price points."],[188.48,192.72,"B gets about 85, D about 79, and A about 75."],[192.72,195.02,"Combine with quality at fifty-fifty."],[195.3,198.3,"D comes first with about 80.2 points."],[198.3,201.08,"B is second with about 79.6."],[201.08,202.29,"C is third with about 79.0."],[202.29,204.8,"A is last with about 68.7."],[204.8,208.56,"Notice how close the top three are."],[208.56,212.36,"Less than one and a half points separate them."],[212.86,215.16,"And notice something more uncomfortable."],[215.16,220.61,"C, the bid that looks too cheap to be real, is distorting everyone else's price score."],[220.61,228.69,"Because the relative formula anchors on the lowest bid, C's low price pulls down the price points of every other bidder."],[228.69,232.64,"C is about 20 percent below the average of the other three bids."],[232.92,238.9,"If you remember the poll in module 3.3, this is exactly the situation it described."],[238.9,242.11,"So the first question of the case is not who wins."],[242.11,243.75,"It is what to do about C."],[244.26,246.58,"Before we move on, what about the incumbent?"],[246.58,250.47,"Bidder A scores last on every version of the model."],[250.47,256.02,"Its price is the highest and its quality score of 62 reflects the complaints at headquarters."],[256.02,259.96,"That is useful information in itself."],[259.96,263.84,"The rollover since 2019 was not protecting Nordvia from a bad deal."],[263.84,265.08,"It was hiding one."],[265.36,270.13,"The incumbent is not entitled to a second chance because it is the incumbent."],[270.13,277.78,"But it is entitled to the same treatment as everyone else, which means a clear, respectful debrief when the result is known."],[278.73,280.31,"Here is your team's task."],[280.31,283.54,"Twenty-five minutes, on the Case tab of your workbook."],[283.54,284.28,"Five questions."],[284.77,287.16,"First, how do you handle Bidder C?"],[287.16,292.04,"Second, whom do you negotiate with, or ask for a BAFO, a best and final offer?"],[292.04,294.02,"Third, auction or not, and why?"],[294.02,298.3,"Fourth, how do you treat D's condition of a four-year term?"],[298.58,306.36,"And fifth, your award recommendation and its conditions: due diligence, the sensitivity of the result, and mobilisation."],[306.86,310.29,"A few hints before you start, without giving the answer away."],[310.29,325.61,"For C, think about what the evidence says about abnormally low bids, and what a should-cost model for cleaning looks like: hours, multiplied by the collectively agreed wage, plus on-costs, supervision, consumables, overhead and margin."],[325.89,333.42,"For the auction question, take out the preconditions checklist from module 3.4 and go through it line by line."],[333.42,341.47,"For D, ask yourself what the other bidders were told about the term, and whether they would have bid differently if a four-year term had been allowed."],[341.75,349.7,"And for the recommendation, remember that the Head of Real Estate wants a decision, but what she needs is a decision she can defend."],[350.21,355.1,"In the live session, teams often want to run an auction between B and D."],[355.1,359.82,"If that is your instinct, hold on to it, and test it honestly against the checklist."],[359.82,367.55,"Pause the lesson now if you would like to write your team answer in the Case tab of your workbook, and continue when you are ready."],[368.05,369.74,"One more hint about C."],[369.74,373.22,"A should-cost check does not need to be complicated."],[373.22,388.57,"Take the cleaning area and frequencies in the specification, estimate the productive hours a competent supplier needs, multiply by the collective wage plus on-costs, and add a reasonable margin for supervision, consumables and overhead."],[388.85,393.97,"If C's price only works with far fewer hours than that, the bid is not cheaper."],[393.97,399.41,"It is a different, smaller service, or it relies on paying people less than the floor."],[399.41,402.38,"Either way, it is not comparable with the others."],[402.88,404.96,"And a reminder on the timeline."],[404.96,408.07,"The Head of Real Estate wants a decision this month."],[408.07,411.02,"That pressure is real, and it is legitimate."],[411.02,419.58,"But a decision taken in a hurry on a doubtful bid can cost far more time later, if the contract fails or the award is challenged."],[419.86,426.35,"Part of your answer is how you manage her expectations: what you can decide this month, and what must still be checked."],[427.3,428.05,"Welcome back."],[428.05,430.48,"Let's debrief, using four questions."],[430.98,434.52,"First: who would auction, and which precondition fails?"],[434.52,436.33,"Let's go through the list."],[436.33,437.38,"Precise specification?"],[437.38,438.31,"Partly."],[438.31,443.56,"It is a hybrid specification for a service, and quality differences matter."],[443.56,445.75,"Four to five qualified bidders?"],[445.75,446.72,"No."],[446.72,451.03,"Once C is dealt with, you have two strong bidders, B and D, and a weaker incumbent."],[451.31,452.33,"Price decides?"],[452.33,454.4,"No, quality is half the model."],[454.4,458.16,"Low switching cost, and a relationship that tolerates it?"],[458.16,467.92,"Cleaning involves staff on site every day, possibly staff transfer, and a supplier you need to work with closely for three years or more."],[467.92,470.01,"Several lines fail, and one no is enough."],[470.01,470.88,"So, no auction."],[471.16,473.65,"A BAFO on the published model does the job."],[474.15,477.88,"Second: who accepted D's four-year term, and is that fair to B?"],[477.88,480.49,"This is where many teams slip."],[480.49,485.67,"D's bid is attractive, and it is tempting to just say yes to the condition."],[485.67,488.96,"But the RFP published three plus one plus one."],[488.96,491.78,"Bidders A, B and C priced that structure."],[491.78,494.93,"A longer guaranteed term has value to a supplier."],[495.21,501.03,"It spreads mobilisation and equipment costs, like B's floor robots, over more years."],[501.03,508.15,"If you accept the longer term for D alone, you are comparing D's price on one basis with B's price on another."],[508.15,513.41,"That is not equal treatment, and it would be hard to defend if B challenged the award."],[513.9,518.62,"Third: how did you write the sensitivity into the recommendation?"],[518.62,521.88,"Let's look at the numbers without C."],[521.88,527.27,"At fifty-fifty with the relative formula, D scores about 87.1 and B about 87.0."],[527.27,529.54,"D leads by one tenth of a point."],[529.54,536.79,"At sixty percent quality and forty percent price, D leads more clearly, about 85.9 against 84.4."],[537.07,542.65,"At forty percent quality and sixty percent price, B leads, about 89.6 against 88.3."],[542.65,549.42,"And with a linear price formula instead of the relative one, B also leads, by a fraction."],[549.42,555.12,"In other words, the winner depends on choices that were made before the bids arrived."],[555.4,560.32,"That is exactly why weights and formulas must be fixed and published before issue."],[560.32,565.57,"And it is why the recommendation must say, honestly, that the decision is close."],[566.08,569.33,"Fourth: what would you design differently next time?"],[569.33,571.57,"The speaker note gives the answer."],[571.57,577.88,"A published quality threshold of 60 would have excluded C automatically, because C scored 58."],[577.88,584.55,"Many tender designs set a minimum quality score that a bid must reach before its price is even considered."],[584.83,588.04,"That protects the model from exactly this distortion."],[588.04,590.77,"It is the design lesson for Fieldwork 3."],[591.26,595.79,"There is a fifth question worth asking, even though it is not on the slide."],[595.79,597.75,"What would you tell the incumbent?"],[597.75,607.75,"A transition from A to a new supplier means mobilisation, possibly staff transfer, and a period where A is still cleaning the building while knowing it has lost."],[608.03,614.95,"Good practice is to plan that handover in the contract and in the communication, not to leave it to chance."],[614.95,619.98,"The relationship with an outgoing supplier still matters for the last months of service."],[620.93,622.93,"Here is one strong answer."],[622.93,623.46,"Note the word one."],[623.46,630.21,"There is more than one good answer, and this slide is shown only after the teams have presented theirs."],[630.71,631.68,"Bidder C."],[631.68,641.69,"Send a written request asking C to explain its hours and its price, and check the explanation against a should-cost model built on the collective wage."],[641.69,651.23,"Public procurement guidance explicitly asks buyers to seek explanations for abnormally low bids, and that is good practice for a private insurer too."],[651.51,657.67,"If C's explanation does not convince, reject the bid as abnormally low and record why."],[657.67,662.52,"Do not exclude C at once without asking, and do not award it without checking."],[662.8,673.45,"Either shortcut creates risk: the first a fairness challenge, the second a contract that fails within months, with staff paid below the floor and Nordvia's name attached."],[673.95,674.61,"Next step."],[674.61,679.55,"A BAFO with B and D, on the published fifty-fifty model, and no auction."],[679.55,689.22,"Both are invited to improve their offer, under the same rules, in a follow-on event in Coupa, with every clarification through the Message Center."],[689.72,690.7,"D's term."],[690.7,694.98,"Ask D to price the compliant three plus one plus one term."],[694.98,702.75,"You can let D explain what it would offer for a longer term, but the comparison is made on the structure everyone bid against."],[702.75,705.27,"Do not change the structure for one bidder."],[705.55,712.08,"If Nordvia genuinely wants a four-year term, that is a decision to make for all bidders, not a favour to one."],[712.58,713.96,"And the recommendation."],[713.96,717.91,"Award to the best BAFO score, subject to due diligence."],[717.91,730.3,"Due diligence here means checking references, wage compliance, insurance, and the mobilisation plan, and for B, checking that night-time robot floor care actually works in the HQ building."],[730.3,732.95,"And state clearly that the decision is close."],[733.23,737.46,"A good recommendation to the Head of Real Estate might read like this."],[737.46,743.6,"On the published model, D and B are within a fraction of a point after clarification of C."],[743.6,747.36,"We have invited both to a final offer under the same rules."],[747.64,754.52,"We will recommend the higher score, subject to checks, and we will tell you before the end of the month."],[754.52,757.12,"That is fast, fair and honest about sensitivity."],[757.12,764.17,"The logic behind it, in the words of the speaker note, is fairness, published rules and honesty about sensitivity."],[764.67,766.83,"How do you run the BAFO in practice?"],[766.83,772.2,"Send both bidders the same letter, in the same follow-on event, on the same day."],[772.2,784.51,"Tell them which elements they may improve, for example price, mobilisation plan or KPIs, and confirm that the published fifty-fifty model and the relative price formula still apply."],[784.79,786.34,"Set a single deadline."],[786.34,789.84,"Do not tell B what D offered, or D what B offered."],[789.84,793.48,"And do not hold a second, informal round afterwards."],[793.48,798.19,"The BAFO is the final round, just as the auction result would have been the price."],[799.14,802.01,"That brings us to the close of the session."],[802.01,803.38,"Fieldwork 3 and next steps."],[803.38,810.15,"And the subtitle is the most important instruction of all: validate the weights before anyone sees a bid."],[810.65,812.42,"Why repeat it so strongly?"],[812.42,815.86,"Because you have just seen what happens without it."],[815.86,823.26,"In Case 3, the ranking between B and D flips with a change of ten points in the weighting, or a change in the price formula."],[823.54,832.92,"If those choices had been left open until after the bids arrived, every stakeholder with a favourite supplier would have an argument for the weights that suits them."],[832.92,840.38,"Once the bids are visible, any change to the model looks like manipulation, even when it is made in good faith."],[840.38,842.99,"Fixing the model first is not bureaucracy."],[843.27,845.56,"It is what makes the result defensible."],[846.05,849.46,"Think about the alternative from the business owner's point of view."],[849.46,859.54,"Suppose the Head of Real Estate had told you before issue that service quality at headquarters mattered more than anything else, and you had agreed sixty percent quality."],[859.54,863.07,"D would lead clearly, and the decision would be easier."],[863.35,866.75,"Or suppose Finance had insisted on sixty percent price."],[866.75,867.49,"B would lead."],[867.49,868.84,"Neither choice is wrong."],[868.84,872.85,"What would be wrong is making that choice after seeing the bids."],[872.85,881.62,"The weights are a statement of what the business values, and they only mean something if they are made before anyone knows who benefits."],[882.57,885.7,"Here is Fieldwork 3, to complete before Session 4."],[885.7,886.82,"Five steps."],[887.31,899.35,"First, the RFx pack outline for your own project: instructions to bidders, the specification type, the lots, the pricing schedule, the questionnaire, the draft contract, and the regulatory annexes."],[899.35,901.93,"An outline, not the full documents."],[901.93,905.91,"But every heading should be there, with a line on what goes in it."],[906.42,908.39,"Second, the weights validated."],[908.39,911.09,"With the business owner, before issue, and recorded."],[911.09,915.23,"We will look at how to hold that conversation on the next slide."],[915.73,917.14,"Third, test bid sets."],[917.14,920.61,"Run three fictional sets of bids through your model."],[920.61,928.75,"Make one set where a cheap bid has weak quality, one where a strong bid is expensive, and one where two bids are very close."],[928.75,930.94,"Watch what your price formula does."],[931.22,939.23,"If a single low bid squeezes everyone else's price score, as C did in the case, you will see it now rather than after issue."],[939.23,942.76,"And check whether a quality threshold would change the outcome."],[943.26,944.96,"Fourth, tenant settings."],[944.96,955.38,"Confirm with your Coupa administrator the settings you flagged in Practice 3.5: scoring, sealed opening, and auction extensions if you plan an auction."],[955.38,957.72,"And fifth, regulatory gates."],[958.0,968.71,"Where your triage in Session 1 flagged ICT services, or a critical or important function, agree the DORA and outsourcing gate criteria with Risk or Compliance."],[968.71,975.41,"Knock-out questions on those topics belong in the event, so they must be settled before you build it."],[975.91,979.66,"The speaker note explains why this matters for what comes next."],[979.66,984.78,"The validated evaluation model is the core of part three of your Sourcing Case."],[984.78,988.3,"Session 4 builds the negotiation plan on top of it."],[988.3,993.36,"You cannot plan a negotiation well if you do not know what the model rewards."],[993.86,995.54,"A word on the RFx pack outline."],[995.54,998.79,"The most common gap is the draft contract."],[998.79,1003.04,"Buyers plan to write it after the award, when they know the winner."],[1003.04,1011.8,"But the draft contract is where you negotiate through the bids: service levels, service credits, indexation, exit and data return."],[1012.08,1017.17,"If it is attached to the RFP, every bidder prices the same obligations."],[1017.17,1023.92,"If it comes later, you negotiate it with one supplier who already knows it has won, and your leverage has gone."],[1024.87,1030.45,"Now, the conversation that many buyers avoid: the weights conversation with the business owner."],[1030.45,1033.42,"Two columns, what to ask and what to show."],[1033.93,1037.39,"Look at the left-hand column first, the questions to ask."],[1037.39,1040.35,"If the bids come in close, these weights decide."],[1040.35,1041.62,"Are you comfortable?"],[1041.62,1044.57,"That one question changes the conversation."],[1044.57,1049.38,"The weights stop being a procurement formality and become the business owner's decision."],[1049.38,1052.32,"Next: what would make you unhappy with the winner?"],[1052.6,1060.27,"This often reveals a criterion that is missing from the model, or one that is really a knock-out, not a weighted score."],[1060.27,1063.27,"And third: which criterion would you never trade?"],[1063.27,1072.63,"If the answer is, for example, data security for an IT service, that criterion probably belongs in the knock-out gates rather than in the weights."],[1073.13,1076.0,"Now the right-hand column, what to show."],[1076.0,1078.21,"A test bid set where the ranking flips."],[1078.21,1080.06,"The knock-out gates that are not negotiable."],[1080.06,1087.68,"And where the model will live in Coupa, so the business owner knows it is written into the event and cannot drift."],[1088.18,1091.17,"Let me give you a short example of how this plays out."],[1091.17,1096.13,"A Nordvia HR director owns a tender for an employee assistance programme."],[1096.13,1100.59,"She asks for seventy percent quality, because she cares about service."],[1100.59,1102.92,"The buyer shows her a test bid set."],[1103.2,1110.14,"At seventy-thirty, a provider that is 40 percent more expensive wins because of a slightly better quality score."],[1110.14,1112.32,"The figures are illustrative."],[1112.32,1115.5,"She pauses, and says that is not what she meant."],[1115.5,1123.32,"They agree sixty-forty, plus a quality threshold, so that weak providers are excluded and price then decides among the good ones."],[1123.6,1131.9,"As the speaker note says, a business owner who sees the model reverse a ranking understands why the weights must be agreed before issue."],[1132.4,1138.16,"What if the business owner pushes back and wants to keep the weights open until the bids arrive?"],[1138.16,1139.69,"Explain the risk calmly."],[1139.69,1150.98,"Without fixed weights, every evaluator and every stakeholder can argue afterwards for the model that favours their preferred supplier, and the losing bidders can argue that the rules changed."],[1151.26,1161.12,"Then offer the reassurance she is really looking for: the test bid sets let her see the consequences now, and the quality threshold protects her from a weak, cheap winner."],[1161.12,1164.29,"Record what she pushed back on and what you agreed."],[1164.29,1166.49,"That record is part of the fieldwork."],[1167.44,1170.84,"Let's recap the whole of Session 3 in five lines."],[1170.84,1177.17,"This session was about one question: how do we run the competition well, once the strategy is clear?"],[1177.67,1178.13,"One."],[1178.13,1180.23,"The tool follows the strategy."],[1180.23,1182.73,"Choose the question, then the event."],[1182.73,1197.53,"An RFI, an RFQ, an RFP, a framework with mini-competitions, an auction, or a negotiation: each answers a different question, and the category strategy from Session 2 tells you which question you are asking."],[1198.03,1198.61,"Two."],[1198.61,1203.34,"Design the request so bids are comparable, and fix the model before issue."],[1203.34,1214.44,"A clear specification, a pricing schedule that forces like-for-like answers, a draft contract attached, and weights and price formula published before anyone sees a bid."],[1214.93,1215.57,"Three."],[1215.57,1216.96,"Run it fairly."],[1216.96,1223.78,"One channel for clarifications, individual scores before consensus, and a sensitivity check before the recommendation."],[1223.78,1226.41,"Fairness is not only ethics."],[1226.41,1234.56,"It is what lets you defend the result, to a losing bidder, to an auditor, or to a stakeholder who preferred another supplier."],[1235.06,1235.1,"Four."],[1235.1,1239.73,"Auction only when the preconditions hold, and in the right format."],[1239.73,1252.04,"Precise specification, four to five qualified bidders, spare capacity, price or a formula decides, low switching cost, and a credible alternative with a real intention to award."],[1252.04,1255.9,"And expect five to fifteen percent at best, reported net."],[1256.4,1256.45,"Five."],[1256.45,1260.81,"Build it in Coupa, and confirm what your tenant really does."],[1260.81,1268.58,"Use the documented features with confidence, and turn everything else into a question for your Coupa administrator before you promise it."],[1269.09,1273.63,"The speaker note suggests one last step, and I invite you to do it now."],[1273.63,1278.5,"Write down the one line from these five that you will apply first in your own project."],[1278.5,1279.57,"Just one."],[1279.57,1282.74,"That is the line you will be held to when we meet again."],[1283.24,1285.53,"Notice how the five lines connect."],[1285.53,1287.49,"The strategy chooses the event."],[1287.49,1290.57,"The event design makes the bids comparable."],[1290.57,1292.68,"The fair process makes the result defensible."],[1292.68,1297.74,"The auction checklist stops you using the sharpest tool in the wrong place."],[1297.74,1301.2,"And Coupa holds it all together with an audit trail."],[1301.48,1305.05,"Case 3 used every one of those lines on a single lot."],[1305.99,1307.72,"And that brings us to the bridge."],[1307.72,1310.11,"Next session: how do we close it well?"],[1310.61,1314.19,"Session 3 ended with a recommendation and a BAFO."],[1314.19,1316.44,"Session 4 picks up from there."],[1316.44,1319.58,"We will negotiate, contract and hand over."],[1319.58,1322.58,"Not every category ends with a competition."],[1322.58,1328.25,"Some have only one realistic supplier, and that changes everything about the negotiation."],[1328.53,1337.28,"So Session 4 opens with a role-play: the Kelvar sole-source negotiation, for Nordvia's core insurance software licences."],[1337.28,1344.0,"There is no competitor to point to, the last renewal brought an uplift, and there is an auto-renewal clause."],[1344.28,1350.36,"Please reread the Kelvar data in the case materials before we meet, so that you arrive ready to plan."],[1350.86,1352.56,"Please bring three things."],[1352.56,1353.55,"Your RFx plan."],[1353.55,1359.05,"Your validated evaluation model, with the weights agreed with your business owner."],[1359.05,1367.12,"And your e-negotiation design, meaning your choice of BAFO, negotiation rounds or auction, and the rules you would publish."],[1367.4,1376.38,"Session 4 will build the negotiation plan on top of them, then turn the result into a contract, and hand it over to the people who will manage it."],[1376.88,1381.02,"Before you close this lesson, take two minutes for a personal check."],[1381.02,1382.66,"Look back at your own project."],[1382.66,1385.32,"Do you know which event sequence you will use?"],[1385.32,1390.05,"Is your evaluation model written down, with a price formula you have tested?"],[1390.05,1394.1,"Have you decided, honestly, whether an auction passes the checklist?"],[1394.38,1397.92,"Do you know which Coupa settings you still need to confirm?"],[1397.92,1401.2,"If any answer is no, that is your first task for the fieldwork."],[1401.7,1403.7,"Thank you for your work in this session."],[1403.7,1408.9,"You have gone from a strategy to a fair, defensible, well-built competition."],[1408.9,1415.33,"Complete Fieldwork 3, have the weights conversation, and I look forward to seeing you in Session 4."]]}],"s4":[{"id":"s4-l1","session":"s4","title":"Opening and negotiation planning","summary":"Plan a negotiation on one page, from the power position to interests, BATNAs, the four numbers of every issue, conditional concessions and team roles.","from":0,"to":28,"audio":"audio/s4-l1.mp3","duration":1496.2,"segs":[[0,0.6,14.83,"Welcome to Session 4 of the Source to Contract course. This is the last session, and its question is: HOW do we close it well? The subtitle tells you what that means in practice: negotiation, contract and handover."],[0,15.33,47.29,"In this first lesson we do three things. We open the session and look at where we are in the course. We debrief the third piece of fieldwork, where you took your evaluation model to the business owner. And then we work through module 4.1, negotiation planning: how to prepare a negotiation on one page, from the power position all the way to the concessions you are willing to make. By the end of this lesson you should be able to sit down with your own Sourcing Case and write a negotiation plan that a business owner can approve."],[1,48.24,63.37,"Let us start with a hard truth. A good strategy and a fair event can still end in a weak deal, a weak contract, or a contract nobody uses. You can do everything right in Sessions 1 to 3, and still lose the value in the last few weeks."],[1,63.87,96.57,"Remember the big idea of this course. Source to Contract is a decision chain, not a document chain. Every step makes a decision that the next step depends on. Today covers the last three links: negotiate, contract and hand over. These are the links where value is most often lost. A price agreed verbally and never written down. A contract signed without the protections we needed. A new agreement that sits in a folder while the business keeps buying from the old supplier. Today is about closing those leaks."],[2,97.52,121.04,"Here is where we are. In Session 1 we asked WHAT are we sourcing? You defined the need, built a category profile and worked out your power position. In Session 2 we asked WHICH route to market? and you wrote the sourcing strategy paper. In Session 3 we asked HOW do we run the event? You built the RFx plan, the evaluation model and the e-negotiation design."],[2,121.54,141.98,"Today is the fourth question: HOW do we close it well? You will add three pieces to your Sourcing Case: a negotiation plan, a contract checklist and a handover plan. That completes the fourth and final part of your case, and the capstone presentation will bring all four parts together. So everything we do today has a direct use."],[3,142.93,179.37,"Today you will plan a negotiation on one page, from power to concessions. You will match each negotiation type to the right e-negotiation mode in Coupa. You will negotiate a sole-source software renewal in a role-play, the Kelvar case. Then, in later lessons, you will write an award recommendation and structure the contract, build Solvency II and DORA requirements into the contract, and plan the handover and the first ninety days. Notice that every module ends with a practice block on your own Sourcing Case. Theory first, then straight into your own project."],[4,180.32,217.16,"Here is the plan for the live session, which these video lessons follow. We open with the Fieldwork 3 debrief. Then module 4.1 on negotiation planning, the subject of this lesson. Then 4.2, e-negotiation by negotiation type. Then Case 4, the Kelvar renewal role-play. After a break come 4.3 on award and contract, and 4.4 on contracts in a regulated insurer. After a second break, 4.5 on handover, and finally the capstone briefing and close. It is a full session, so let us use the time well."],[5,218.11,247.49,"Before we debrief, a quick reflection. The question is: did your business owner validate the evaluation weights before issue? Possible answers are: yes, unchanged; yes, with changes; not yet; or there is no competitive event in my case. Take a moment and answer it honestly for yourself. If your answer is not yet, that is useful information. It means the weights are still your weights, not the business's weights, and that is a risk you can still fix."],[6,248.44,267.69,"So, the debrief of Fieldwork 3. You took your RFx pack and your evaluation model to the business owner before issue. What happened? This step matters because a model that the business owner has not seen will be challenged the moment the scores come in, usually by the person whose favourite supplier lost."],[7,268.64,296.24,"In the live session we debrief in trios for ten minutes, three minutes each. If you are working alone, use the same four prompts. First: what did the business owner change in your model? Second, the weights: did the quality and price split move, and why? Third, knock-outs: which criterion became a pass or fail gate instead of a scored criterion? Fourth, a surprise: one thing you learned about the need that you did not know before."],[7,296.74,332.27,"Two kinds of example are especially useful. One where the weights moved, for example a Facilities owner who pushed quality up from 50 to 60 percent because complaints at headquarters had damaged their credibility. And one where a regulatory criterion became a knock-out, for example an IT owner who insisted that data location inside the agreed region is a gate, not a score. Both kinds of example feed straight into today's modules. Weights shape the award recommendation, and knock-outs often come back as non-negotiable contract clauses."],[8,333.22,343.39,"Now module 4.1, negotiation planning. The subtitle says it plainly: negotiations are won or lost before anyone sits at the table."],[9,344.34,361.62,"Here is the central claim of this module. Most of the outcome of a negotiation is fixed by the power position and by the preparation, not by talent in the room. That is good news. You cannot change your personality before Tuesday's meeting, but you can change your preparation."],[9,362.12,386.62,"Ask yourself: when did you last prepare a negotiation on paper? Not in your head, not in the taxi, but written down, with numbers, and shared with your team. In most procurement groups, few people can say they did this in the last year. That gap is the opportunity. A buyer with a written plan, a known walk-away and a credible alternative will usually beat a more charming buyer who improvises."],[10,387.57,401.09,"This is the negotiation process: Prepare, Open, Explore, Bargain, Close and Review. It is the standard sequence you will find in procurement negotiation texts, including Jonathan O'Brien's work."],[10,401.59,428.07,"Prepare is where you collect the facts, set objectives per issue, work out both sides' alternatives and agree the team. Open is the agenda, the relationship and your first anchor. Explore is where you ask questions and find interests and priorities. Bargain is the exchange of conditional concessions. Close means summarising what was agreed. And Review means learning the lessons and following up on the relationship."],[10,428.57,455.28,"Two points to remember. First, most of the time goes into Prepare. A good rule is 60 to 70 percent of your total negotiation time on preparation. Second, Close means in writing, the same day, and then into the contract. Send the summary by email that afternoon, with the agreed points issue by issue. If you wait a week, memories diverge, and the supplier's version usually becomes the official one."],[11,456.23,474.56,"Where does preparation start? With power. This table brings forward the positioning you did in Session 1. The first column is our position on the Kraljic matrix. The second is how the supplier sees us, using Steele and Court's supplier preferencing. The third is the stance that follows."],[11,475.06,512.5,"Let us read it row by row. Leverage, and the supplier sees us as core or development: use competition, firm and fair. They want our business, so we can ask for their best. Leverage, but they see us as a nuisance: we are small to them, so we aggregate volume, for example across Group entities, to become attractive. Bottleneck, and they see us as exploitable or a nuisance: here the stance is to secure supply, protect the future with contract terms, and quietly build alternatives. Strategic, and they see us as core: this is partnership territory, joint value and open book."],[11,513.0,541.23,"And the last row: strategic for us, but exploitable or a nuisance for them. This is the danger zone. We depend on them, and they do not care much about us. The stance is to raise our attractiveness, or to change the nature of the demand so that we depend on them less. This last row is the typical situation of a mid-sized insurer with an incumbent core software vendor. Keep it in mind, because it is exactly the Kelvar case later today."],[12,542.18,559.27,"Let us look more closely at the supplier's view, because buyers often forget that the supplier has a matrix too. Steele and Court, in 1996, put the attractiveness of our account on the vertical axis and its value to the supplier on the horizontal axis."],[12,559.77,586.31,"Bottom left is nuisance: small and unattractive. They spend little effort on us and show little flexibility. Top left is development: attractive but small today, so they invest to grow us. Top right is core: attractive and valuable, so they defend us. And bottom right, highlighted here, is exploitable: valuable but unattractive. We pay them a lot, but we are not interesting, so they maximise margin."],[12,586.81,616.23,"Why is exploitable highlighted? Because that is where a vendor like Kelvar is likely to put Nordvia. Nordvia pays a lot, costs little to serve and has bought nothing new in years. What can you do? Raise attractiveness. Bring Group volume to the table. Offer reference value, for example a case study. Share a clear roadmap so they see future business. And pay on time, which sounds small but matters to account managers. Attractiveness is something you can manage."],[13,617.18,629.17,"The third lens is the Kearney Purchasing Chessboard, by Schuh and colleagues. At its simplest level it has four basic strategies, depending on the balance of demand power and supply power."],[13,629.67,654.59,"When both demand power and supply power are low, the strategy is manage spend: demand management and bundling. When our power is high, leverage competition: tendering and target pricing. When both are high, seek joint advantage: value and cost partnerships. And when their power is high, change the nature of demand: re-specification, innovation and risk management."],[13,655.09,679.82,"Why does this matter for negotiation? Because the strategy tells you what kind of negotiation you are in before you choose a tactic. If you are in leverage competition, a firm price negotiation backed by bids is right. If you are in change the nature of demand, haggling on price is mostly wasted effort. Your energy should go into reducing what you need from them and building alternatives."],[14,680.77,695.55,"Now the method for the negotiation itself. Fisher and Ury, in Getting to Yes, first published in 1981, with a third edition with Patton in 2011, describe principled negotiation in four steps."],[14,696.05,717.19,"First, people and problem: separate the relationship from the issue. You can be warm with the account manager and firm on the numbers. Second, interests: focus on interests, not positions. Third, options: invent options for mutual gain before you decide. Fourth, criteria: insist on objective criteria."],[14,717.69,743.04,"In procurement, objective criteria have familiar names: benchmarks, should-cost models and total cost of ownership, in Lisa Ellram's sense. When a supplier says the market rate is X, you do not answer with your opinion. You answer with a benchmark or a cost model. The conversation then moves from who is stronger to what is fair, and that is a conversation a well-prepared buyer usually wins."],[15,743.99,751.71,"Let us make the difference between a position and an interest concrete. On the left, what people say. On the right, why they say it."],[15,752.21,781.12,"Bidder D, in the headquarters cleaning tender, says: we need a four-year term. That is a position. The interest behind it is to pay back the floor-care robots they will buy for our site. Kelvar says: the uplift is nine percent, as last time. The interest is to hit the year-end forecast and protect the price level across their customer base. And our own business owner says: just renew, no hassle. The interest is no disruption during a busy year."],[15,781.62,807.48,"Here is the key insight. Interests can be met in several ways; positions only in one. If Bidder D's real interest is payback on the robots, we do not have to give four years. We can offer three years plus option years tied to KPIs, or an amortisation schedule that compensates them if we exit early. And how do you find the interest? You ask. What makes four years important to you? Then you listen."],[16,808.43,841.31,"For every issue in a negotiation, you prepare four numbers. The opening is your first offer: ambitious, defensible, and it anchors the discussion. The target is what you realistically aim for, based on objective criteria. The walk-away is the worst you will accept, and it is derived from your BATNA, your Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement. And the ZOPA, the Zone Of Possible Agreement, a term associated with Howard Raiffa in 1982, is the space between your walk-away and theirs."],[16,841.81,864.23,"One rule here is non-negotiable. If there is no ZOPA, there is no deal at that scope. Then you change the scope or the alternatives. You never change your walk-away in the room. A walk-away that moves under pressure was never a walk-away. If new facts genuinely appear, you take a break, go back to your mandate holder and agree a new position outside the room."],[17,865.18,877.62,"Here is an illustrative example from the Nordvia headquarters cleaning lot: the best and final offer round with Bidder D. The numbers are annual prices in thousands of euros, and they are illustrative."],[17,878.12,893.54,"Read from the top. Our opening is 600 thousand euros. Our target is 630. Their estimated floor is about 620. Our walk-away is 655. And their bid is also 655."],[17,894.04,927.69,"Why is our walk-away the same as their bid? Because Bidder B offers comparable value at a lower price, so Bidder B is part of our BATNA. We would not pay Bidder D more than its current bid, because we have a good alternative. And where does their estimated floor come from? From a should-cost model: the hours needed, multiplied by the collectively agreed wage floor, plus supervision, consumables, overhead and a reasonable margin. Below about 620, Bidder D would either lose money or cut hours, and we do not want either."],[17,928.19,953.64,"So the ZOPA here runs from roughly 620 to 655. Our target of 630 sits inside it, close to their floor but not below it. And our opening at 600 is below their floor on purpose: it anchors, and it leaves room for one or two conditional moves. Notice how every number on this slide has a source. That is what makes it defensible when Bidder D pushes back."],[18,954.59,969.39,"BATNA: build it, do not assume it. Fisher and Ury make the point that your power depends on the attractiveness of your alternative. So the best way to improve a negotiation is often to work on your alternative, weeks before the meeting."],[18,969.89,999.19,"How do you strengthen it? Run an RFI to alternative suppliers. Qualify a second supplier or a bridge solution. Re-specify the need so that more suppliers can bid, for example by splitting a lot or accepting a standard product. Price an insourcing or stay-as-is option honestly, with real numbers. And estimate their BATNA too: what happens to them if they lose us? A supplier with a half-empty order book has a weak BATNA, and you should know that."],[18,999.69,1020.68,"Two warnings. A bluff is not a BATNA. Experienced account managers can usually tell when an alternative has never been tested. And only use alternatives that the business has approved. If you tell a vendor you will move to another platform and your CIO has never agreed to that, you lose credibility on both sides of the table."],[19,1021.63,1046.4,"A reflection question now. How strong is the BATNA in your Sourcing Case today? Strong: a qualified alternative is ready. Medium: alternatives exist but are not tested. Weak: we depend on the incumbent. Or: I do not know yet. Think about your answer. If you chose medium or weak, write down one action from the previous slide that would make it stronger before you negotiate."],[20,1047.35,1058.02,"Next, a powerful idea: many issues create room to trade. This table lists six issues, with their value to us and our estimate of their value to the supplier."],[20,1058.52,1077.17,"Annual price: high for us, high for them. Term: medium for us, high for them. Indexation: high for us, medium for them. Payment terms: low for us, medium for them. Service credits: high for us, medium for them. And a reference and case study: low for us, medium for them."],[20,1077.67,1111.8,"Now look for the rows where the values differ. Those are your trading opportunities. Term matters more to them than to us, and so do payment terms and a reference. Indexation and service credits matter more to us. So a smart package might be: we accept a longer term and faster payment, and in exchange we get a lower price, capped indexation and real service credits. Lax and Sebenius call this creating value before claiming it. If you negotiate only on price, you are fighting over a fixed pie. When you add issues, the pie can grow."],[21,1112.75,1117.19,"Once you know what you can trade, you need rules for how to concede. There are four."],[21,1117.69,1142.63,"First, conditional. If you do this, then we do that. Never give without getting. Second, decreasing. Each concession is smaller than the last, which signals that you are approaching your limit. Third, cheap for us. Give what costs us little and matters to them, like the reference or the payment terms we just saw. Fourth, recorded. The analyst notes every move and who made it, so there is no confusion at the end."],[21,1143.13,1161.93,"Why are unconditional concessions so dangerous? Because a concession made without condition teaches the other side that pressure works. They will simply push again. And prepare your concession list before the meeting, not during it. Under pressure in the room, people give away things they would never have given at their desk."],[22,1162.88,1169.26,"All of this comes together in one principle: one page, prepared as a team, approved before the meeting."],[22,1169.76,1193.44,"This is in the spirit of Jonathan O'Brien's Red Sheet, from his book Negotiation for Procurement and Supply Chain Professionals, fourth edition, 2025. Red Sheet is a registered method of Positive Purchasing. What we use in this course is a one-pager inspired by it, not the official template. If you negotiate often, the book is worth reading in full."],[23,1194.39,1198.64,"Here is the structure of the negotiation one-pager. Six sections."],[23,1199.14,1228.3,"Objectives: what a good outcome is, in the business owner's words. Not procurement's words. Facts and power: spend, benchmark, should-cost, the Kraljic position and the supplier preferencing view. Alternatives: our BATNA and their estimated BATNA. Issues: for each one, the opening, target, walk-away and priority. Concessions: what we can give, and only in exchange for what. And team and mandate: the roles, who can agree what, and the escalation route."],[23,1228.8,1261.81,"Imagine you are preparing the renewal of a facilities management contract worth, illustratively, 1.6 million euros a year. On one page you would have the business owner's goal, say fewer complaints and stable costs, the benchmark rate per hour, your alternative bidder, five issues with their four numbers, three conditional concessions and the names of the people in the room. That page is what you take to the business owner for approval. This structure is Practice 4.1, and it is also the negotiation slide of your capstone."],[24,1262.76,1288.66,"Who is in the room? Four roles. The lead negotiator speaks, proposes and concedes. Only one person does that. The analyst handles numbers, takes notes and watches for signals, like the moment the supplier's tone changes on one issue. The business owner confirms needs, and never concedes in the room. And the executive sponsor handles escalation and any top-to-top meeting, if that is planned."],[24,1289.16,1314.68,"Walton and McKersie, in 1965, called the work of aligning your own side intra-organisational bargaining. Without it, the supplier negotiates with your stakeholders instead of with you. A classic example: the IT manager tells the vendor over lunch that the renewal is basically agreed. From that moment your walk-away is worthless. Align your side first, and agree that one voice speaks."],[25,1315.63,1322.26,"Finally in this module, tactics to recognise. On the left, what you may hear. On the right, how to respond."],[25,1322.76,1351.67,"A very high first anchor: re-anchor with your objective criteria, the benchmark or the should-cost. Take it or leave it: test it. Ask what would change their view. Often the answer reveals an interest. An artificial deadline: check the real deadline. It is often yours, for example your notice period, so manage it early. Small extras added at the end, the famous one more thing: package everything. Nothing is agreed until everything is agreed."],[25,1352.17,1370.24,"Naming a tactic calmly often neutralises it. You might simply say: I notice we are being given a deadline. Can we look at what really has to happen by Friday? And the best defence of all is a prepared walk-away. If you know your limit and your alternative, pressure loses most of its force."],[26,1371.19,1397.09,"Now it is your turn. This is Practice 4.1, your negotiation one-pager, in your workbook. Plan about 15 minutes. Choose the power position of your Sourcing Case. Write both sides' interests and both sides' BATNAs. List four issues, each with an opening, a target and a walk-away. Plan three conditional concessions. And name the team roles. In the live session, trios then challenge each other's BATNA."],[26,1397.59,1410.91,"Pay most attention to the BATNA line. Weak or bluffed BATNAs are the most common gap. If you want to, pause the video now, open the Modules tab and complete the one-pager. Then come back and continue."],[27,1411.86,1428.96,"Let us debrief with four questions. Whose BATNA turned out weaker than expected? Which issue has a big value difference between the sides? Who found a concession that is cheap for us and valuable to them? And who is the business owner in the room, and can they stay silent on price?"],[27,1429.46,1445.06,"If your BATNA felt weak, you are in good company, and you will see in the Kelvar case how a weak BATNA can still be made credible with honest work. And if you are not sure your business owner can stay silent on price, talk to them before the meeting, not after."],[28,1446.01,1461.13,"Here is module 4.1 in three lines. Power and preparation decide most of the outcome. Know the interests, the BATNAs and the four numbers of every issue. And one page, one team, one voice, with conditional concessions."],[28,1461.63,1495.0,"To recap the whole lesson: we placed today's session in the decision chain, debriefed your fieldwork on the weights, and then built the logic of a negotiation plan, from Kraljic, preferencing and the Chessboard, through Fisher and Ury's interests and objective criteria, to the four numbers, the BATNA, tradable issues, concession rules, the one-pager and team roles. Keep your one-pager open. In the next lesson we look at e-negotiation: how to choose the right mechanism, and the right role for Coupa, for each type of negotiation."]],"questions":[{"after":7,"q":"Your Facilities business owner reviewed your cleaning evaluation model and insisted that a valid wage-floor check becomes a pass or fail gate rather than a scored criterion. What is the best way to treat this change?","options":["Resist it, because knock-outs reduce competition","Accept it and record it, because it reflects a real requirement and will also shape the contract","Keep it as a scored criterion but give it a higher weight","Ignore it until the scores come in"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fieldwork 3 exists so the business owner owns the model before issue. A legitimate requirement such as the wage floor works best as a gate, and gates often return later as contract clauses.","t":332.27},{"after":11,"q":"Nordvia is strategic for a supplier category, but the supplier sees Nordvia as exploitable. Which stance fits best?","options":["Run an English reverse auction to force the price down","Partnership and open book, because the category is strategic","Raise Nordvia's attractiveness or change the nature of demand to reduce dependency","Accept the supplier's terms, because there is no alternative"],"answer":2,"explain":"Strategic for us and exploitable for them is the danger zone. The stance is to raise attractiveness, for example with Group volume or a reference, or to change the nature of demand so dependency falls.","t":541.23},{"after":13,"q":"The Chessboard places a category in change the nature of demand. Where should most of your negotiation effort go?","options":["Haggling hard on the unit price","Re-specifying the need and building alternatives so you depend less on the supplier","Tendering with as many bidders as possible","Bundling the category with unrelated spend"],"answer":1,"explain":"When supply power is high, price haggling achieves little. Re-specification, innovation and risk management reduce dependency and change the balance of power.","t":679.82},{"after":15,"q":"Bidder D insists on a four-year term for the HQ cleaning contract. What is the most useful next move?","options":["Ask what makes four years important to them","Refuse, because the policy is three years","Accept, because Bidder D has the best quality","Threaten to award to Bidder B"],"answer":0,"explain":"Four years is a position. Asking about it reveals the interest, payback on the floor-care robots, which can be met in other ways, such as KPI-based option years or an amortisation schedule on early exit.","t":807.48},{"after":17,"q":"In the illustrative BAFO with Bidder D, why is Nordvia's walk-away set at 655 thousand euros?","options":["It is the budget approved by Finance","It is the midpoint of the ZOPA","Bidder B offers comparable value, so paying more than D's current bid makes no sense","It is Bidder D's estimated floor"],"answer":2,"explain":"The walk-away comes from the BATNA. Bidder B is a comparable alternative, so Nordvia will not pay D more than its current bid of 655.","t":953.64},{"after":20,"q":"Looking at the issues table, which package creates the most value for Nordvia?","options":["Concede on price in exchange for a reference","Concede on service credits in exchange for a longer term","Concede on indexation in exchange for faster payment","Offer a longer term and faster payment in exchange for a lower price and capped indexation"],"answer":3,"explain":"Trade issues where the values differ. Term and payment terms matter more to the supplier, indexation and price matter more to Nordvia, so trading one for the other creates value before claiming it.","t":1111.8},{"after":21,"q":"Late in a negotiation, the supplier asks for 60-day payment terms. Which response follows the concession rules?","options":["Agree, because payment terms cost us little","If you cap indexation at the labour share, then we can move to 60 days","Refuse, because every concession weakens our position","Agree, and ask for something in return at the next meeting"],"answer":1,"explain":"Concessions should be conditional: if you, then we. Giving something cheap for us is fine, but only in exchange for something we value, decided now and recorded.","t":1161.93},{"after":24,"q":"During preparation, the IT business owner says he will tell the vendor at lunch that the renewal is basically done, to keep the mood positive. What should you do?","options":["Let him, because a good relationship helps","Ask the executive sponsor to attend the lunch instead","Align beforehand: the business owner confirms needs but makes no commitments, and one voice speaks for Nordvia","Cancel the negotiation"],"answer":2,"explain":"Walton and McKersie's intra-organisational bargaining: align your own side first. A pre-commitment by a stakeholder destroys the walk-away and lets the supplier negotiate around you.","t":1314.68},{"after":27,"q":"The supplier says the offer is only valid until Friday. What is the best first response?","options":["Check what really has to happen by Friday and whose deadline it is","Sign before Friday to secure the price","Ignore the deadline completely","Raise your walk-away to close quickly"],"answer":0,"explain":"Artificial deadlines are a classic tactic. Test them calmly; the real deadline is often your own, such as a notice period, and a prepared walk-away removes most of the pressure.","t":1445.06}],"cues":[[0.6,4.04,"Welcome to Session 4 of the Source to Contract course."],[4.04,8.68,"This is the last session, and its question is: HOW do we close it well?"],[8.68,14.83,"The subtitle tells you what that means in practice: negotiation, contract and handover."],[15.33,17.39,"In this first lesson we do three things."],[17.39,21.41,"We open the session and look at where we are in the course."],[21.41,26.87,"We debrief the third piece of fieldwork, where you took your evaluation model to the business owner."],[27.15,38.78,"And then we work through module 4.1, negotiation planning: how to prepare a negotiation on one page, from the power position all the way to the concessions you are willing to make."],[39.06,47.29,"By the end of this lesson you should be able to sit down with your own Sourcing Case and write a negotiation plan that a business owner can approve."],[48.24,50.08,"Let us start with a hard truth."],[50.08,57.73,"A good strategy and a fair event can still end in a weak deal, a weak contract, or a contract nobody uses."],[57.73,63.37,"You can do everything right in Sessions 1 to 3, and still lose the value in the last few weeks."],[63.87,66.25,"Remember the big idea of this course."],[66.25,70.47,"Source to Contract is a decision chain, not a document chain."],[70.47,73.98,"Every step makes a decision that the next step depends on."],[73.98,79.07,"Today covers the last three links: negotiate, contract and hand over."],[79.07,82.22,"These are the links where value is most often lost."],[82.5,85.45,"A price agreed verbally and never written down."],[85.45,88.7,"A contract signed without the protections we needed."],[88.7,94.2,"A new agreement that sits in a folder while the business keeps buying from the old supplier."],[94.2,96.58,"Today is about closing those leaks."],[97.52,98.93,"Here is where we are."],[98.93,101.81,"In Session 1 we asked WHAT are we sourcing?"],[101.81,106.58,"You defined the need, built a category profile and worked out your power position."],[106.58,109.8,"In Session 2 we asked WHICH route to market?"],[109.8,112.44,"and you wrote the sourcing strategy paper."],[112.44,115.7,"In Session 3 we asked HOW do we run the event?"],[115.98,121.03,"You built the RFx plan, the evaluation model and the e-negotiation design."],[121.54,125.05,"Today is the fourth question: HOW do we close it well?"],[125.05,131.58,"You will add three pieces to your Sourcing Case: a negotiation plan, a contract checklist and a handover plan."],[131.58,139.1,"That completes the fourth and final part of your case, and the capstone presentation will bring all four parts together."],[139.38,141.98,"So everything we do today has a direct use."],[142.93,147.84,"Today you will plan a negotiation on one page, from power to concessions."],[147.84,153.32,"You will match each negotiation type to the right e-negotiation mode in Coupa."],[153.32,158.63,"You will negotiate a sole-source software renewal in a role-play, the Kelvar case."],[158.91,171.78,"Then, in later lessons, you will write an award recommendation and structure the contract, build Solvency II and DORA requirements into the contract, and plan the handover and the first ninety days."],[171.78,176.34,"Notice that every module ends with a practice block on your own Sourcing Case."],[176.62,179.37,"Theory first, then straight into your own project."],[180.32,185.43,"Here is the plan for the live session, which these video lessons follow."],[185.43,188.56,"We open with the Fieldwork 3 debrief."],[188.56,193.56,"Then module 4.1 on negotiation planning, the subject of this lesson."],[193.56,196.95,"Then 4.2, e-negotiation by negotiation type."],[196.95,200.05,"Then Case 4, the Kelvar renewal role-play."],[200.33,207.62,"After a break come 4.3 on award and contract, and 4.4 on contracts in a regulated insurer."],[207.62,214.04,"After a second break, 4.5 on handover, and finally the capstone briefing and close."],[214.04,217.17,"It is a full session, so let us use the time well."],[218.11,220.53,"Before we debrief, a quick reflection."],[220.53,225.82,"The question is: did your business owner validate the evaluation weights before issue?"],[225.82,234.3,"Possible answers are: yes, unchanged; yes, with changes; not yet; or there is no competitive event in my case."],[234.3,237.35,"Take a moment and answer it honestly for yourself."],[237.63,241.2,"If your answer is not yet, that is useful information."],[241.2,247.49,"It means the weights are still your weights, not the business's weights, and that is a risk you can still fix."],[248.44,250.14,"So, the debrief of Fieldwork 3."],[250.14,256.24,"You took your RFx pack and your evaluation model to the business owner before issue."],[256.24,256.89,"What happened?"],[256.89,267.68,"This step matters because a model that the business owner has not seen will be challenged the moment the scores come in, usually by the person whose favourite supplier lost."],[268.64,273.01,"In the live session we debrief in trios for ten minutes, three minutes each."],[273.01,276.28,"If you are working alone, use the same four prompts."],[276.28,279.72,"First: what did the business owner change in your model?"],[279.72,284.06,"Second, the weights: did the quality and price split move, and why?"],[284.34,291.14,"Third, knock-outs: which criterion became a pass or fail gate instead of a scored criterion?"],[291.14,296.25,"Fourth, a surprise: one thing you learned about the need that you did not know before."],[296.74,299.45,"Two kinds of example are especially useful."],[299.45,309.84,"One where the weights moved, for example a Facilities owner who pushed quality up from 50 to 60 percent because complaints at headquarters had damaged their credibility."],[310.12,321.84,"And one where a regulatory criterion became a knock-out, for example an IT owner who insisted that data location inside the agreed region is a gate, not a score."],[321.84,325.41,"Both kinds of example feed straight into today's modules."],[325.69,332.27,"Weights shape the award recommendation, and knock-outs often come back as non-negotiable contract clauses."],[333.22,336.88,"Now module 4.1, negotiation planning."],[336.88,343.4,"The subtitle says it plainly: negotiations are won or lost before anyone sits at the table."],[344.34,346.91,"Here is the central claim of this module."],[346.91,354.69,"Most of the outcome of a negotiation is fixed by the power position and by the preparation, not by talent in the room."],[354.69,355.36,"That is good news."],[355.36,361.62,"You cannot change your personality before Tuesday's meeting, but you can change your preparation."],[362.12,365.77,"Ask yourself: when did you last prepare a negotiation on paper?"],[365.77,372.0,"Not in your head, not in the taxi, but written down, with numbers, and shared with your team."],[372.0,376.45,"In most procurement groups, few people can say they did this in the last year."],[376.45,378.27,"That gap is the opportunity."],[378.55,386.61,"A buyer with a written plan, a known walk-away and a credible alternative will usually beat a more charming buyer who improvises."],[387.57,392.8,"This is the negotiation process: Prepare, Open, Explore, Bargain, Close and Review."],[392.8,401.1,"It is the standard sequence you will find in procurement negotiation texts, including Jonathan O'Brien's work."],[401.59,409.21,"Prepare is where you collect the facts, set objectives per issue, work out both sides' alternatives and agree the team."],[409.21,413.0,"Open is the agenda, the relationship and your first anchor."],[413.0,417.5,"Explore is where you ask questions and find interests and priorities."],[417.5,420.81,"Bargain is the exchange of conditional concessions."],[421.09,423.73,"Close means summarising what was agreed."],[423.73,428.07,"And Review means learning the lessons and following up on the relationship."],[428.57,430.0,"Two points to remember."],[430.0,432.75,"First, most of the time goes into Prepare."],[432.75,438.39,"A good rule is 60 to 70 percent of your total negotiation time on preparation."],[438.39,443.47,"Second, Close means in writing, the same day, and then into the contract."],[443.47,449.07,"Send the summary by email that afternoon, with the agreed points issue by issue."],[449.35,455.28,"If you wait a week, memories diverge, and the supplier's version usually becomes the official one."],[456.23,458.17,"Where does preparation start?"],[458.17,458.91,"With power."],[458.91,462.93,"This table brings forward the positioning you did in Session 1."],[462.93,466.56,"The first column is our position on the Kraljic matrix."],[466.56,472.16,"The second is how the supplier sees us, using Steele and Court's supplier preferencing."],[472.16,474.56,"The third is the stance that follows."],[475.06,476.92,"Let us read it row by row."],[476.92,482.12,"Leverage, and the supplier sees us as core or development: use competition, firm and fair."],[482.12,486.48,"They want our business, so we can ask for their best."],[486.48,495.09,"Leverage, but they see us as a nuisance: we are small to them, so we aggregate volume, for example across Group entities, to become attractive."],[495.37,506.09,"Bottleneck, and they see us as exploitable or a nuisance: here the stance is to secure supply, protect the future with contract terms, and quietly build alternatives."],[506.09,512.5,"Strategic, and they see us as core: this is partnership territory, joint value and open book."],[513.0,517.86,"And the last row: strategic for us, but exploitable or a nuisance for them."],[517.86,518.87,"This is the danger zone."],[518.87,523.12,"We depend on them, and they do not care much about us."],[523.12,529.68,"The stance is to raise our attractiveness, or to change the nature of the demand so that we depend on them less."],[529.96,536.7,"This last row is the typical situation of a mid-sized insurer with an incumbent core software vendor."],[536.7,541.23,"Keep it in mind, because it is exactly the Kelvar case later today."],[542.18,549.49,"Let us look more closely at the supplier's view, because buyers often forget that the supplier has a matrix too."],[549.49,559.27,"Steele and Court, in 1996, put the attractiveness of our account on the vertical axis and its value to the supplier on the horizontal axis."],[559.77,562.98,"Bottom left is nuisance: small and unattractive."],[562.98,566.86,"They spend little effort on us and show little flexibility."],[566.86,572.3,"Top left is development: attractive but small today, so they invest to grow us."],[572.3,576.39,"Top right is core: attractive and valuable, so they defend us."],[576.67,581.94,"And bottom right, highlighted here, is exploitable: valuable but unattractive."],[581.94,586.31,"We pay them a lot, but we are not interesting, so they maximise margin."],[586.81,588.77,"Why is exploitable highlighted?"],[588.77,593.52,"Because that is where a vendor like Kelvar is likely to put Nordvia."],[593.52,598.38,"Nordvia pays a lot, costs little to serve and has bought nothing new in years."],[598.38,599.11,"What can you do?"],[599.11,600.68,"Raise attractiveness."],[600.68,602.86,"Bring Group volume to the table."],[602.86,605.86,"Offer reference value, for example a case study."],[606.14,609.17,"Share a clear roadmap so they see future business."],[609.17,613.61,"And pay on time, which sounds small but matters to account managers."],[613.61,616.23,"Attractiveness is something you can manage."],[617.18,622.05,"The third lens is the Kearney Purchasing Chessboard, by Schuh and colleagues."],[622.05,629.17,"At its simplest level it has four basic strategies, depending on the balance of demand power and supply power."],[629.67,636.93,"When both demand power and supply power are low, the strategy is manage spend: demand management and bundling."],[636.93,642.22,"When our power is high, leverage competition: tendering and target pricing."],[642.22,647.33,"When both are high, seek joint advantage: value and cost partnerships."],[647.61,654.59,"And when their power is high, change the nature of demand: re-specification, innovation and risk management."],[655.09,657.31,"Why does this matter for negotiation?"],[657.31,662.82,"Because the strategy tells you what kind of negotiation you are in before you choose a tactic."],[662.82,669.3,"If you are in leverage competition, a firm price negotiation backed by bids is right."],[669.3,674.8,"If you are in change the nature of demand, haggling on price is mostly wasted effort."],[675.08,679.82,"Your energy should go into reducing what you need from them and building alternatives."],[680.77,683.62,"Now the method for the negotiation itself."],[683.62,695.55,"Fisher and Ury, in Getting to Yes, first published in 1981, with a third edition with Patton in 2011, describe principled negotiation in four steps."],[696.05,701.23,"First, people and problem: separate the relationship from the issue."],[701.23,705.53,"You can be warm with the account manager and firm on the numbers."],[705.53,709.1,"Second, interests: focus on interests, not positions."],[709.1,713.82,"Third, options: invent options for mutual gain before you decide."],[713.82,717.19,"Fourth, criteria: insist on objective criteria."],[717.69,727.31,"In procurement, objective criteria have familiar names: benchmarks, should-cost models and total cost of ownership, in Lisa Ellram's sense."],[727.31,732.05,"When a supplier says the market rate is X, you do not answer with your opinion."],[732.05,734.82,"You answer with a benchmark or a cost model."],[735.1,743.04,"The conversation then moves from who is stronger to what is fair, and that is a conversation a well-prepared buyer usually wins."],[743.99,748.35,"Let us make the difference between a position and an interest concrete."],[748.35,749.66,"On the left, what people say."],[749.66,751.71,"On the right, why they say it."],[752.21,757.01,"Bidder D, in the headquarters cleaning tender, says: we need a four-year term."],[757.01,758.53,"That is a position."],[758.53,763.6,"The interest behind it is to pay back the floor-care robots they will buy for our site."],[763.6,767.78,"Kelvar says: the uplift is nine percent, as last time."],[768.06,773.84,"The interest is to hit the year-end forecast and protect the price level across their customer base."],[773.84,778.14,"And our own business owner says: just renew, no hassle."],[778.14,781.12,"The interest is no disruption during a busy year."],[781.62,783.28,"Here is the key insight."],[783.28,787.63,"Interests can be met in several ways; positions only in one."],[787.63,793.38,"If Bidder D's real interest is payback on the robots, we do not have to give four years."],[793.38,801.8,"We can offer three years plus option years tied to KPIs, or an amortisation schedule that compensates them if we exit early."],[802.08,803.65,"And how do you find the interest?"],[803.65,804.43,"You ask."],[804.43,806.43,"What makes four years important to you?"],[806.43,807.48,"Then you listen."],[808.43,812.25,"For every issue in a negotiation, you prepare four numbers."],[812.25,818.16,"The opening is your first offer: ambitious, defensible, and it anchors the discussion."],[818.16,823.15,"The target is what you realistically aim for, based on objective criteria."],[823.43,832.1,"The walk-away is the worst you will accept, and it is derived from your BATNA, your Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement."],[832.1,841.31,"And the ZOPA, the Zone Of Possible Agreement, a term associated with Howard Raiffa in 1982, is the space between your walk-away and theirs."],[841.81,844.16,"One rule here is non-negotiable."],[844.16,847.23,"If there is no ZOPA, there is no deal at that scope."],[847.23,849.67,"Then you change the scope or the alternatives."],[849.67,853.05,"You never change your walk-away in the room."],[853.05,856.74,"A walk-away that moves under pressure was never a walk-away."],[857.02,864.23,"If new facts genuinely appear, you take a break, go back to your mandate holder and agree a new position outside the room."],[865.18,872.61,"Here is an illustrative example from the Nordvia headquarters cleaning lot: the best and final offer round with Bidder D."],[872.61,877.62,"The numbers are annual prices in thousands of euros, and they are illustrative."],[878.12,879.9,"Read from the top."],[879.9,883.5,"Our opening is 600 thousand euros."],[883.5,885.09,"Our target is 630."],[885.09,888.59,"Their estimated floor is about 620."],[888.59,890.8,"Our walk-away is 655."],[890.8,893.54,"And their bid is also 655."],[894.04,896.66,"Why is our walk-away the same as their bid?"],[896.66,902.62,"Because Bidder B offers comparable value at a lower price, so Bidder B is part of our BATNA."],[902.62,907.67,"We would not pay Bidder D more than its current bid, because we have a good alternative."],[907.67,910.21,"And where does their estimated floor come from?"],[910.49,921.19,"From a should-cost model: the hours needed, multiplied by the collectively agreed wage floor, plus supervision, consumables, overhead and a reasonable margin."],[921.19,927.69,"Below about 620, Bidder D would either lose money or cut hours, and we do not want either."],[928.19,931.67,"So the ZOPA here runs from roughly 620 to 655."],[931.67,937.6,"Our target of 630 sits inside it, close to their floor but not below it."],[937.6,945.48,"And our opening at 600 is below their floor on purpose: it anchors, and it leaves room for one or two conditional moves."],[945.48,949.89,"Notice how every number on this slide has a source."],[950.17,953.64,"That is what makes it defensible when Bidder D pushes back."],[954.59,956.72,"BATNA: build it, do not assume it."],[956.72,962.68,"Fisher and Ury make the point that your power depends on the attractiveness of your alternative."],[962.68,969.4,"So the best way to improve a negotiation is often to work on your alternative, weeks before the meeting."],[969.89,971.08,"How do you strengthen it?"],[971.08,974.25,"Run an RFI to alternative suppliers."],[974.25,977.74,"Qualify a second supplier or a bridge solution."],[977.74,985.46,"Re-specify the need so that more suppliers can bid, for example by splitting a lot or accepting a standard product."],[985.46,990.24,"Price an insourcing or stay-as-is option honestly, with real numbers."],[990.52,994.54,"And estimate their BATNA too: what happens to them if they lose us?"],[994.54,999.18,"A supplier with a half-empty order book has a weak BATNA, and you should know that."],[999.69,1000.72,"Two warnings."],[1000.72,1002.62,"A bluff is not a BATNA."],[1002.62,1007.86,"Experienced account managers can usually tell when an alternative has never been tested."],[1007.86,1011.87,"And only use alternatives that the business has approved."],[1012.15,1020.68,"If you tell a vendor you will move to another platform and your CIO has never agreed to that, you lose credibility on both sides of the table."],[1021.63,1023.38,"A reflection question now."],[1023.38,1027.35,"How strong is the BATNA in your Sourcing Case today?"],[1027.35,1029.64,"Strong: a qualified alternative is ready."],[1029.64,1033.28,"Medium: alternatives exist but are not tested."],[1033.28,1035.84,"Weak: we depend on the incumbent."],[1035.84,1037.28,"Or: I do not know yet."],[1037.28,1038.74,"Think about your answer."],[1039.02,1046.4,"If you chose medium or weak, write down one action from the previous slide that would make it stronger before you negotiate."],[1047.35,1050.44,"Next, a powerful idea: many issues create room to trade."],[1050.44,1058.02,"This table lists six issues, with their value to us and our estimate of their value to the supplier."],[1058.52,1061.09,"Annual price: high for us, high for them."],[1061.09,1063.71,"Term: medium for us, high for them."],[1063.71,1066.76,"Indexation: high for us, medium for them."],[1066.76,1070.07,"Payment terms: low for us, medium for them."],[1070.07,1073.29,"Service credits: high for us, medium for them."],[1073.29,1077.17,"And a reference and case study: low for us, medium for them."],[1077.67,1080.3,"Now look for the rows where the values differ."],[1080.3,1082.69,"Those are your trading opportunities."],[1082.69,1087.64,"Term matters more to them than to us, and so do payment terms and a reference."],[1087.64,1090.81,"Indexation and service credits matter more to us."],[1091.09,1101.05,"So a smart package might be: we accept a longer term and faster payment, and in exchange we get a lower price, capped indexation and real service credits."],[1101.05,1105.19,"Lax and Sebenius call this creating value before claiming it."],[1105.19,1109.4,"If you negotiate only on price, you are fighting over a fixed pie."],[1109.68,1111.81,"When you add issues, the pie can grow."],[1112.75,1116.19,"Once you know what you can trade, you need rules for how to concede."],[1116.19,1117.19,"There are four."],[1117.69,1118.99,"First, conditional."],[1118.99,1120.86,"If you do this, then we do that."],[1120.86,1122.55,"Never give without getting."],[1122.55,1123.38,"Second, decreasing."],[1123.38,1129.29,"Each concession is smaller than the last, which signals that you are approaching your limit."],[1129.29,1130.19,"Third, cheap for us."],[1130.19,1136.29,"Give what costs us little and matters to them, like the reference or the payment terms we just saw."],[1136.57,1137.3,"Fourth, recorded."],[1137.3,1142.63,"The analyst notes every move and who made it, so there is no confusion at the end."],[1143.13,1146.47,"Why are unconditional concessions so dangerous?"],[1146.47,1151.03,"Because a concession made without condition teaches the other side that pressure works."],[1151.03,1153.26,"They will simply push again."],[1153.26,1156.91,"And prepare your concession list before the meeting, not during it."],[1157.19,1161.93,"Under pressure in the room, people give away things they would never have given at their desk."],[1162.88,1169.26,"All of this comes together in one principle: one page, prepared as a team, approved before the meeting."],[1169.76,1180.51,"This is in the spirit of Jonathan O'Brien's Red Sheet, from his book Negotiation for Procurement and Supply Chain Professionals, fourth edition, 2025."],[1180.51,1184.39,"Red Sheet is a registered method of Positive Purchasing."],[1184.39,1189.83,"What we use in this course is a one-pager inspired by it, not the official template."],[1190.11,1193.44,"If you negotiate often, the book is worth reading in full."],[1194.39,1197.86,"Here is the structure of the negotiation one-pager."],[1197.86,1198.64,"Six sections."],[1199.14,1203.25,"Objectives: what a good outcome is, in the business owner's words."],[1203.25,1204.97,"Not procurement's words."],[1204.97,1212.3,"Facts and power: spend, benchmark, should-cost, the Kraljic position and the supplier preferencing view."],[1212.3,1214.69,"Alternatives: our BATNA and their estimated BATNA."],[1214.69,1219.3,"Issues: for each one, the opening, target, walk-away and priority."],[1219.58,1223.45,"Concessions: what we can give, and only in exchange for what."],[1223.45,1228.31,"And team and mandate: the roles, who can agree what, and the escalation route."],[1228.8,1237.01,"Imagine you are preparing the renewal of a facilities management contract worth, illustratively, 1.6 million euros a year."],[1237.29,1252.36,"On one page you would have the business owner's goal, say fewer complaints and stable costs, the benchmark rate per hour, your alternative bidder, five issues with their four numbers, three conditional concessions and the names of the people in the room."],[1252.36,1255.66,"That page is what you take to the business owner for approval."],[1255.94,1261.81,"This structure is Practice 4.1, and it is also the negotiation slide of your capstone."],[1262.76,1263.83,"Who is in the room?"],[1263.83,1264.97,"Four roles."],[1264.97,1268.78,"The lead negotiator speaks, proposes and concedes."],[1268.78,1270.33,"Only one person does that."],[1270.33,1278.74,"The analyst handles numbers, takes notes and watches for signals, like the moment the supplier's tone changes on one issue."],[1278.74,1282.69,"The business owner confirms needs, and never concedes in the room."],[1282.97,1288.66,"And the executive sponsor handles escalation and any top-to-top meeting, if that is planned."],[1289.16,1296.92,"Walton and McKersie, in 1965, called the work of aligning your own side intra-organisational bargaining."],[1296.92,1301.67,"Without it, the supplier negotiates with your stakeholders instead of with you."],[1301.67,1308.4,"A classic example: the IT manager tells the vendor over lunch that the renewal is basically agreed."],[1308.68,1311.33,"From that moment your walk-away is worthless."],[1311.33,1314.68,"Align your side first, and agree that one voice speaks."],[1315.63,1318.62,"Finally in this module, tactics to recognise."],[1318.62,1320.43,"On the left, what you may hear."],[1320.43,1322.26,"On the right, how to respond."],[1322.76,1329.06,"A very high first anchor: re-anchor with your objective criteria, the benchmark or the should-cost."],[1329.06,1331.24,"Take it or leave it: test it."],[1331.24,1333.36,"Ask what would change their view."],[1333.36,1335.95,"Often the answer reveals an interest."],[1335.95,1339.2,"An artificial deadline: check the real deadline."],[1339.2,1343.5,"It is often yours, for example your notice period, so manage it early."],[1343.78,1348.8,"Small extras added at the end, the famous one more thing: package everything."],[1348.8,1351.67,"Nothing is agreed until everything is agreed."],[1352.17,1355.12,"Naming a tactic calmly often neutralises it."],[1355.12,1359.2,"You might simply say: I notice we are being given a deadline."],[1359.2,1362.33,"Can we look at what really has to happen by Friday?"],[1362.33,1365.63,"And the best defence of all is a prepared walk-away."],[1365.63,1370.24,"If you know your limit and your alternative, pressure loses most of its force."],[1371.19,1372.63,"Now it is your turn."],[1372.63,1376.85,"This is Practice 4.1, your negotiation one-pager, in your workbook."],[1376.85,1378.57,"Plan about 15 minutes."],[1378.57,1381.61,"Choose the power position of your Sourcing Case."],[1381.61,1384.86,"Write both sides' interests and both sides' BATNAs."],[1384.86,1389.17,"List four issues, each with an opening, a target and a walk-away."],[1389.17,1391.54,"Plan three conditional concessions."],[1391.82,1393.47,"And name the team roles."],[1393.47,1397.09,"In the live session, trios then challenge each other's BATNA."],[1397.59,1399.82,"Pay most attention to the BATNA line."],[1399.82,1403.18,"Weak or bluffed BATNAs are the most common gap."],[1403.18,1408.89,"If you want to, pause the video now, open the Modules tab and complete the one-pager."],[1408.89,1410.9,"Then come back and continue."],[1411.86,1414.07,"Let us debrief with four questions."],[1414.07,1416.94,"Whose BATNA turned out weaker than expected?"],[1416.94,1420.45,"Which issue has a big value difference between the sides?"],[1420.45,1424.31,"Who found a concession that is cheap for us and valuable to them?"],[1424.31,1428.97,"And who is the business owner in the room, and can they stay silent on price?"],[1429.46,1438.26,"If your BATNA felt weak, you are in good company, and you will see in the Kelvar case how a weak BATNA can still be made credible with honest work."],[1438.26,1445.05,"And if you are not sure your business owner can stay silent on price, talk to them before the meeting, not after."],[1446.01,1448.99,"Here is module 4.1 in three lines."],[1448.99,1451.82,"Power and preparation decide most of the outcome."],[1451.82,1456.58,"Know the interests, the BATNAs and the four numbers of every issue."],[1456.58,1461.14,"And one page, one team, one voice, with conditional concessions."],[1461.63,1483.97,"To recap the whole lesson: we placed today's session in the decision chain, debriefed your fieldwork on the weights, and then built the logic of a negotiation plan, from Kraljic, preferencing and the Chessboard, through Fisher and Ury's interests and objective criteria, to the four numbers, the BATNA, tradable issues, concession rules, the one-pager and team roles."],[1484.25,1485.62,"Keep your one-pager open."],[1485.62,1495.0,"In the next lesson we look at e-negotiation: how to choose the right mechanism, and the right role for Coupa, for each type of negotiation."]]},{"id":"s4-l2","session":"s4","title":"E-negotiation by negotiation type","summary":"Choose the right negotiation mechanism and Coupa set-up for each negotiation type, and recognise when an e-auction helps and when it harms.","from":29,"to":47,"audio":"audio/s4-l2.mp3","duration":1523.3,"segs":[[29,0.6,38.28,"Welcome back. This lesson covers module 4.2: e-negotiation by negotiation type. In the last lesson you prepared a negotiation on one page. Now we ask a practical question: once you know what kind of negotiation you are in, which mechanism should you use, and what role should Coupa play? By the end of this lesson you should be able to look at any sourcing project and say: this is the negotiation type, this is the mechanism, and this is how I set it up in Coupa. The subtitle of the module is the message in one line: the tool serves the negotiation, not the reverse."],[30,39.23,51.26,"Let us start with a statement that may sound obvious, but is ignored surprisingly often. An e-auction is a mechanism for one kind of negotiation. It is not a negotiation strategy."],[30,51.76,83.51,"The right order is: first decide what kind of negotiation this is, then the mechanism, then configure Coupa. This is the course's big idea again: strategy first, tool second. The mistake we see in practice goes the other way. Someone has a licence for auctions, likes the savings report at the end, and starts looking for categories to auction. That is how you end up auctioning a consulting call-off or a cleaning contract, and paying for it later in quality, trust and staff turnover."],[30,84.01,96.02,"So think of the auction as one instrument in a toolbox. A good procurement team uses it often where it fits, for example on hardware, supplies or mobile tariffs, and never where it does not."],[31,96.97,103.92,"Here is the first half of the matrix. Three columns: the negotiation type, the mechanism, and Coupa's role."],[31,104.42,138.49,"Row one: competitive leverage. Typical categories are supplies, print, hardware and mobile. The mechanism is an RFQ, followed by an English reverse auction if the preconditions hold. If the market is incumbent-heavy, a reverse Japanese auction can work better. Coupa's role is to run the RFQ as a pre-bidding phase, then the auction, with rank or gap-to-best feedback to the bidders. The RFQ phase matters: it qualifies bidders and fixes the specification before anyone sees a live price."],[31,138.99,170.31,"Row two: quality-led services, such as cleaning, security, or the selection of a SaaS tool. Here the mechanism is an RFP with an evaluation model fixed before issue, then a shortlist, negotiation rounds and a best and final offer, a BAFO. Coupa supports this with an RFP event with questionnaires and lots, a follow-on event for the BAFO, and the Message Center for clarifications. The point is that price matters, but quality decides who stays in the race."],[31,170.81,189.78,"Row three: panels, for example consulting, IT services and agencies. The mechanism is a mini-competition per call-off, or a documented direct award below a threshold. Coupa's role is a short RFP event created from a template, with the rate card and a statement of work."],[31,190.28,202.44,"One important note. Everything in the Coupa column on this slide is in Coupa's public documentation. Settings that are not documented come on the next slides, marked: confirm in your tenant."],[31,202.94,247.74,"Let me add a short story to make row two real. Imagine Nordvia's security and reception contract, 1.6 million euros a year, with hourly rates indexed twice in two years and no KPIs. It is tempting to run it as a price competition, because the current rates feel high. But security is a quality-led service. The guard at reception is the first person a customer or a supervisor meets. So the right design is an RFP with a fixed evaluation model, a shortlist of the strongest two or three, a negotiation round on the KPIs and the indexation formula, and then a BAFO. You still get a sharp price, because the BAFO is competitive. But you get it inside a quality frame."],[31,248.24,271.88,"And row three, panels, deserves one more sentence. A panel is negotiated once, at the framework level, with rate ceilings and terms. After that, each call-off is a small, fast competition inside that frame. The heavy negotiation is behind you. What remains is discipline: inviting enough panel members, using the template, and recording the decision."],[32,272.83,276.31,"Here is the second half of the matrix, with three more types."],[32,276.81,306.98,"Row four: sole or single source, typically incumbent software. The mechanism is face-to-face, data-driven and executive, with an RFI to alternatives to make the BATNA credible. Coupa's role is spend and contract data, the RFI event, and redlines and approvals in contract lifecycle management, CLM. And notice the last words: never an auction. With one realistic supplier, an auction is either a bluff or an expensive formality."],[32,307.48,336.43,"Row five: strategic partnership, for example managed services or business process outsourcing. The mechanism is an RFI, then an RFP, then dialogue workshops, then a BAFO against fixed criteria. In Coupa you run staged follow-on events, use the Message Center, and keep an audit trail of every step. This matters because these contracts are large, long and often regulated, and you will need to show how the decision was made."],[32,336.93,366.59,"Row six: multi-lot deals and bundles, such as multi-site facilities management or telecom lines. The mechanism is combinatorial bids and scenario analysis. In Coupa, that is Sourcing Optimization: package bids, constraints and what-if scenarios. Sourcing Optimization comes from Coupa's acquisition of Trade Extensions in 2017. Before you plan an event around it, check whether the Group actually licenses that module."],[32,367.09,395.93,"Look at the pattern across all six rows. As we move from competitive leverage to sole source and strategic partnership, the mechanism shifts from automated price competition to human conversation, and Coupa's role shifts from running the competition to providing structure, data and records. But Coupa never disappears. Even in the most human negotiation, someone needs the spend data, the contract dates, the approval workflow and the audit trail."],[33,396.88,406.16,"Now an important discipline for anyone who designs events in Coupa: separating what is documented from what you must confirm in your own tenant."],[33,406.66,429.15,"On the left, documented by Coupa. RFI, RFQ, RFP and Spot Buy events. English reverse, Dutch reverse and reverse Japanese auctions. An RFx as a pre-bidding phase before an auction. Rank or difference-to-best feedback, and lots awarded together. And the Message Center, follow-on events and Sourcing Optimization."],[33,429.65,452.29,"On the right, confirm in your Coupa tenant. Overtime or soft-close rules, which extend an auction when a late bid arrives. Minimum decrement settings. A formal sealed-envelope opening. Multi-attribute live auctions. And how weighted scoring is configured. These may exist in your tenant, or behave differently from what you expect, so do not assume."],[33,452.79,476.53,"The practical rule: never promise a supplier an auction rule you have not checked in the tenant. Write the rules into the event documents, and test them in a mock auction with colleagues playing bidders. Imagine telling six bidders that the auction extends by two minutes after every late bid, and then the event simply closes on time. You have just created a dispute, and possibly a complaint."],[34,477.48,482.64,"Let us look at the auction formats as Coupa defines them, because the names cause confusion."],[34,483.14,519.71,"English reverse: suppliers bid down, the lowest wins. Bids must improve, so no increases and no tiny improvements. Reverse Japanese: the price starts high and falls by steps. Suppliers accept each step, and the last one still active wins. This format hides the competitors' prices, which can reduce the visible price war. Dutch reverse: here it is a buyer's clock. The price starts low and rises, and the first supplier to accept wins. This is fast. And finally, sealed RFx: one bid each, no feedback. That is the classic tender."],[34,520.21,540.29,"A warning about the Dutch format. Many web sources describe the Dutch auction as descending. That is the selling version, where a seller's price falls until a buyer accepts. In a reverse auction, it works the other way. Use Coupa's definition in your event documents, so that suppliers and colleagues share the same picture."],[34,540.79,576.64,"How do you choose between the three live formats? A simple way to think about it. Use English reverse when you have many qualified bidders and want full transparency on rank. Use reverse Japanese when an incumbent is strong and you want every bidder to decide, step by step, whether it is still in, without watching competitors undercut each other. And use Dutch reverse when speed matters and the market price is well known. Whatever you choose, write the format into the event rules, with a plain explanation, and run a briefing call so that every bidder understands it before the event opens."],[35,577.59,584.56,"When is an auction the right choice? There are six preconditions, and all must be true. Not most of them. All of them."],[35,585.06,618.41,"One: the specification is precise and bids can be ranked automatically. Two: at least four to five qualified, interested bidders. That guidance comes from Smeltzer and Carr, 2003, as summarised by Mora Cortez and colleagues in 2023. Three: spare capacity in the market and low switching cost. Four: price, or a formula, decides after quality has been assessed. Five: a credible BATNA, and the intention to award. And six: the post-award relationship can tolerate it."],[35,618.91,651.85,"You will recognise this checklist from Session 3. Let us test it quickly on laptops for the Group's offices. A precise specification: yes, a defined model. Six qualified resellers: yes. Spare capacity: yes. Price decides after technical compliance: yes. We intend to award: yes. And the relationship can tolerate it: a reseller relationship, yes. Now test it on the Kelvar renewal. It fails at the second line. One realistic supplier. The checklist stops you there."],[35,652.35,686.83,"Why does each condition matter? Without a precise specification, bidders are not bidding for the same thing, and the lowest price may simply be the thinnest offer. Without enough bidders, there is no real dynamic. Without spare capacity, suppliers do not need to fight for the volume. If quality has not been assessed before the price event, you may be forced to award to a bid you do not want. Without the intention to award, you are bluffing. And if the relationship cannot tolerate a public price fight, you may win the auction and lose the supplier's goodwill for years."],[36,687.78,690.72,"What does the evidence say about results? Four points."],[36,691.22,748.12,"First, five to fifteen percent. That is the typical range of price reductions described in the auction literature, for example Jap in 2002 and Smeltzer and Carr in 2003. Treat it as the literature's typical range, not a promise. Second: less. Emiliani and Stec, in 2002, found that realised savings were lower than providers reported. Vendor savings claims are not independent evidence. Third: over one third. A report by the US Government Accountability Office, GAO-14-108, found that in fiscal year 2012 over one third of US federal reverse auctions had no interactive bidding at all. Fourth: no gain. Sandy Jap's research in 2003 and 2007 found that open-bid formats hurt trust more than sealed formats, without producing higher savings."],[36,748.62,767.29,"What does this mean for you? Auctions can work, in the right conditions. But the savings are often smaller than the brochure says, many auctions do not produce real competition, and visible bidding has a relationship cost. So use them where the conditions hold, and be honest in your savings reports."],[36,767.79,786.56,"There is one more lesson here. Incumbents tend to bid less aggressively than challengers, because they know their switching-cost advantage. So the savings in an auction often come from the challengers pushing the incumbent, which only happens if the challengers are genuinely qualified and you would genuinely switch."],[37,787.51,795.39,"Now four worked examples from Nordvia, our fictional insurer. First, quality-led services: the headquarters cleaning lot."],[37,795.89,829.25,"The RFP used a fifty-fifty split between quality and price, fixed before issue. Bidder C was excluded: after clarification, its staffing hours were below what the wage floor allows. That left two bidders close together, Bidder B and Bidder D. Should Nordvia run an auction? No. There are only two bidders, and it is a labour-intensive service. A price-only auction here would push bids towards the wage floor, and the people who clean the building at night would pay the price, followed by Nordvia in service quality."],[37,829.75,850.41,"Instead, Nordvia runs a BAFO round as a Coupa follow-on event, with the responses carried over. Both bidders get a fair last chance, under the same rules, on price and on the points still open, such as Bidder D's request for a four-year term. That is a negotiation mechanism designed for the negotiation type."],[38,851.36,859.12,"The second example is a panel: a call-off under the IT services panel. The need is a senior developer for six months."],[38,859.62,886.7,"The value is above the threshold, so it is a mini-competition, and at least three panel members are invited. The buyer creates a short RFP event from the call-off template, asking for the role, CVs and a day rate within the rate card ceiling. Evaluation follows the framework criteria, and every bidder gets written feedback. Below the threshold, the route would be a documented direct award, with a rate-card check."],[38,887.2,906.35,"This is fast, fair and auditable. And it is how Nordvia closes the spread you saw in Session 2, where the same senior profile was bought at day rates from 820 to 1,450 euros. Every call-off now competes within a ceiling, so the high end of that spread disappears."],[38,906.85,935.83,"Notice what made this possible. The hard negotiation happened earlier, when the panel was set up: who is on it, the rate card ceilings, the call-off terms. Each call-off then only competes on what is left open, the person and the rate within the ceiling. That is why panels give speed without giving up competition. And Coupa's templates make sure every buyer runs the call-off the same way, which matters when an auditor later asks why one firm received most of the work."],[39,936.78,941.75,"The third example is sole source: Kelvar, the core insurance software vendor."],[39,942.25,982.48,"No auction, and no RFP for show. There is one realistic supplier for this cycle, and everyone knows it, including Kelvar. Running a fake tender would waste suppliers' time and damage Nordvia's credibility. So what is Coupa's role? It provides usage and spend data, the contract terms and the notice dates. An RFI event to two alternative platforms makes the BATNA credible, not for this renewal, but for the next one. The negotiation itself is face-to-face, with executive alignment. And the redlines and approvals go through the CLM workflow, so every change to the contract is visible and approved."],[39,982.98,994.99,"Notice that Coupa adds real value here even though there is no competition in the event. Data, alternatives, documentation. This is the set-up for the role-play in the next lesson."],[39,995.49,1023.19,"There is a broader point. In a sole-source negotiation, your power does not come from the event. It comes from information. How many named users do we really use? What did we pay per user last time, and what do benchmarks suggest? When is the notice deadline? What do the alternatives cost, and how long would migration take? Coupa holds part of that information, and procurement's job is to bring it together before the vendor brings its own version."],[40,1024.14,1029.32,"The fourth example is illustrative: bundles, with telecom lines for 40 sites."],[40,1029.82,1077.06,"The scope spans three countries and covers fixed, mobile and data lines. Suppliers offer package discounts across sites and services: a lower price if they win all mobile lines, or everything in one country. A normal RFQ cannot capture that. Sourcing Optimization lets suppliers bid packages and alternatives. The buyer then compares scenarios. The lowest total cost. A maximum of two suppliers. One supplier per country. Each scenario has a price, and you can see what a constraint really costs. If a maximum of two suppliers adds, for example, an illustrative two percent to the total, the business can decide whether simpler management is worth it. The chosen scenario then flows into contracts."],[40,1077.56,1096.1,"Expressive bidding plus scenario analysis is integrative negotiation run through a machine. One more point: telecom services are ICT services, so check the DORA scope before you contract, especially if any lines support critical functions such as claims systems."],[41,1097.05,1100.55,"Now, what never to do. Five misuses, and why they hurt."],[41,1101.05,1146.0,"An auction as a bluff, with no intention to award. Trust and future participation collapse, as Jap's 2003 work warns. Renegotiating after the auction. Bidders learn to hold back next time, and the rules lose meaning. Phantom bidders, invented to create pressure. That is unethical, and fatal to trust when found, and it usually is found. One or two bidders. As the GAO report showed, that is just an expensive RFQ. And intellectual or complex work. In EU public procurement, electronic auctions are excluded for intellectual performances, under Directive 2014 24, Article 35. We are not a public buyer, but the logic transfers."],[41,1146.5,1163.72,"Add a sixth case from our own categories: price-only auctions for cleaning or security without a quality threshold and a wage-floor check. The cheapest bid often means fewer hours or underpaid staff. That leads to service failure and legal exposure for the buyer."],[41,1164.22,1193.25,"Why are these misuses so tempting? Because each one produces a good number in the short term. The bluff auction scares the incumbent into a discount. The post-auction call extracts an extra percent. But the cost appears later, and it lands on someone else: the next buyer, who finds that suppliers no longer bid seriously, or no longer bid at all. A reputation for fair events is an asset. It takes years to build, and one bad event to lose."],[42,1194.2,1212.81,"Here is a sentence from research worth remembering. Auctions perform poorly when projects are complex, contractual design is incomplete and there are few bidders. That is from Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis, 2009, who studied private building contracts in Northern California."],[42,1213.31,1236.8,"Their point is that auctions stifle the communication needed to use the supplier's expertise. When the work is complex, the best supplier often helps you shape the solution. An auction closes that conversation. Negotiation does better there. Think of a claims IT transformation or a new marketing agency relationship. You want dialogue, not a ticking clock."],[42,1237.3,1258.53,"So when you face a complex project, the question is not how to get the lowest price in a live event. It is how to structure a conversation where the best supplier shares its knowledge, and the competition still keeps everyone honest. That is what RFP rounds, dialogue workshops and a BAFO against fixed criteria are designed to do."],[43,1259.48,1285.19,"A reflection question. Which e-auction misuse have you seen? An auction with one or two bidders. An auction for services that cannot be specified. Renegotiation after the auction. An auction run without intention to award. Or none of these. Think about it for a moment. If you have seen one, ask yourself what it cost: in money, in trust, or in the number of suppliers willing to bid the next time."],[44,1286.14,1309.86,"Now Practice 4.2: choose the negotiation mode. Plan about 10 minutes, in your workbook. You have six Nordvia scenarios plus your own project. For each one, choose the mode from the matrix, and describe the Coupa set-up in one line. In the live session, pairs compare and defend their choice for scenarios 2, 4 and 5, because those produce the best discussion."],[44,1310.36,1343.73,"The six scenarios are: office supplies with a fixed basket and six qualified suppliers; headquarters cleaning with four bids, two above the quality threshold; a senior developer for six months under the IT services panel; the Kelvar core licence renewal; the Northbyte claims IT managed service at the end of its term; and telecom lines for 40 sites in three countries with bundle discounts. If you want to, pause the video now, open the Modules tab and complete it. Then continue to compare with the suggested answers."],[45,1344.68,1346.43,"Here are the suggested answers."],[45,1346.93,1388.77,"Scenario 1, office supplies: an RFQ, then an English reverse auction. In Coupa, the RFQ as pre-bidding, then the auction, and the result loaded as a catalogue so people actually buy from it. Scenario 2, headquarters cleaning: an RFP with rounds and a BAFO, run as a follow-on event. Scenario 3, the senior developer: a panel mini-competition, an RFP from the call-off template. Scenario 4, Kelvar: a sole-source negotiation, supported by data, an RFI to alternatives and CLM. Scenario 6, telecom lines: Sourcing Optimization, with package bids and scenarios."],[45,1389.27,1421.38,"Scenario 5, Northbyte, deserves a moment. The suggested answer is a multi-stage dialogue and a BAFO, using staged events and including exit and DORA criteria. Northbyte runs a claims IT managed service of 19.4 million euros a year that supports a critical function. But it can also be a sole-source renegotiation, if no exit is feasible in time before the contract ends. Either answer needs a tested exit plan. We return to that in module 4.4."],[46,1422.33,1454.82,"Let us debrief. Where was the auction tempting but wrong? For many people it is the cleaning lot, because the price gap looks attractive. In which scenario does Coupa add most value without any auction? Often Kelvar or the panel call-off. What did you choose for your own project, and does it follow from your power position? If it does not, look again at your Session 1 positioning. And finally: which Coupa setting must you confirm in the tenant before your event? Write that one down. It is a concrete action for Fieldwork 4."],[46,1455.32,1480.11,"Let me give you one example of a good final action. A buyer planning an office supplies auction writes: before the event, confirm with the Coupa administrator whether overtime rules and minimum decrements are available, and run a mock auction with two colleagues. That is specific, owned and testable. Compare it with: check Coupa settings. The first one will happen; the second one probably will not."],[47,1481.06,1497.49,"Here is module 4.2 in three lines. Negotiation type first, mechanism second, Coupa third. Auctions only when every precondition holds, and never as a bluff. And Coupa adds value in every type: structure, data and an audit trail."],[47,1497.99,1522.08,"To recap the lesson: we built the six-row matrix from competitive leverage to bundles, separated what Coupa documents from what you must confirm in your tenant, clarified the auction formats, tested the six preconditions, looked honestly at the evidence, and walked through four Nordvia examples. In the next lesson we negotiate where no auction is possible: the Kelvar sole-source renewal."]],"questions":[{"after":31,"q":"A colleague says: we have auction licences in Coupa, so let us auction the creative agency roster this year. What is the main flaw in this reasoning?","options":["It starts from the tool instead of the negotiation type","Agencies cannot log into Coupa","Auctions are not allowed in the private sector","Creative agencies always charge fixed fees"],"answer":0,"explain":"Strategy first, tool second. Creative work is quality-led and hard to specify, so the negotiation type points to an RFP or panel, not an auction.","t":271.88},{"after":33,"q":"You plan an English reverse auction and want a two-minute extension after every late bid. What should you do first?","options":["Announce the rule to bidders now","Assume it works, because English auctions always have it","Drop the auction and use a sealed RFQ","Confirm the overtime setting in your Coupa tenant and test it in a mock auction"],"answer":3,"explain":"Overtime or soft-close rules are not documented publicly, so they must be confirmed in the tenant. Never promise suppliers a rule you have not tested.","t":476.53},{"after":35,"q":"For a printing tender, the specification is precise, price decides, and you intend to award, but only two qualified printers are interested. What does the checklist tell you?","options":["Run the auction, most preconditions hold","Run a reverse Japanese auction instead","Do not run an auction: all preconditions must be true, and two bidders is too few","Run the auction and invite unqualified printers to make up the numbers"],"answer":2,"explain":"Every precondition must hold. With two bidders an auction becomes an expensive RFQ, so use an RFQ with negotiation instead.","t":686.83},{"after":37,"q":"In the HQ cleaning tender, Bidders B and D are close after evaluation. Why does Nordvia choose a BAFO rather than an auction?","options":["Coupa cannot run auctions for services","With two bidders in a labour-intensive service, an auction would push bids towards the wage floor","BAFO rounds always produce larger savings","Bidder D refused to take part in an auction"],"answer":1,"explain":"Two bidders and labour-intensive work fail the preconditions. A BAFO in a follow-on event gives both a fair last chance under the same rules without driving wages down.","t":850.41},{"after":40,"q":"For telecom lines across 40 sites, the business wants at most two suppliers. How does Sourcing Optimization help with that decision?","options":["It forces suppliers to bid only on single lines","It automatically awards to the lowest bidder","It replaces the need for a DORA check","It shows the cost of the constraint by comparing the scenario with the lowest-cost scenario"],"answer":3,"explain":"Scenario analysis makes the price of each constraint visible, so the business can judge whether simpler supplier management is worth it. DORA scope still has to be checked.","t":1096.1},{"after":42,"q":"After an auction for office supplies, the winner's account manager calls to offer a further 3 percent if Nordvia extends the term. What is the right response?","options":["Decline: renegotiating after the auction undermines the rules and future bidder trust","Accept, since it is extra savings","Re-run the auction with the new offer","Accept, but do not tell the other bidders"],"answer":0,"explain":"Post-auction renegotiation teaches bidders to hold back next time and makes the rules meaningless. Award as the rules promised.","t":1258.53},{"after":45,"q":"Northbyte's claims IT managed service ends in December 2027 and no exit is feasible in time. Which statement is right?","options":["Run an auction to create pressure","No exit plan is needed if the contract is renewed","A sole-source renegotiation can be acceptable, but a tested exit plan is still required","Award a one-year extension without negotiation"],"answer":2,"explain":"Scenario 5 can be either multi-stage dialogue or a sole-source renegotiation. Either way, a critical ICT service needs a tested exit plan, which module 4.4 covers.","t":1421.38}],"cues":[[0.6,1.38,"Welcome back."],[1.38,7.01,"This lesson covers module 4.2: e-negotiation by negotiation type."],[7.01,8.97,"In the last lesson you prepared a negotiation on one page."],[8.97,20.35,"Now we ask a practical question: once you know what kind of negotiation you are in, which mechanism should you use, and what role should Coupa play?"],[20.63,31.21,"By the end of this lesson you should be able to look at any sourcing project and say: this is the negotiation type, this is the mechanism, and this is how I set it up in Coupa."],[31.21,38.28,"The subtitle of the module is the message in one line: the tool serves the negotiation, not the reverse."],[39.23,45.27,"Let us start with a statement that may sound obvious, but is ignored surprisingly often."],[45.27,48.88,"An e-auction is a mechanism for one kind of negotiation."],[48.88,51.26,"It is not a negotiation strategy."],[51.76,59.02,"The right order is: first decide what kind of negotiation this is, then the mechanism, then configure Coupa."],[59.02,63.6,"This is the course's big idea again: strategy first, tool second."],[63.6,66.93,"The mistake we see in practice goes the other way."],[67.21,74.42,"Someone has a licence for auctions, likes the savings report at the end, and starts looking for categories to auction."],[74.42,83.51,"That is how you end up auctioning a consulting call-off or a cleaning contract, and paying for it later in quality, trust and staff turnover."],[84.01,87.47,"So think of the auction as one instrument in a toolbox."],[87.47,96.02,"A good procurement team uses it often where it fits, for example on hardware, supplies or mobile tariffs, and never where it does not."],[96.97,99.2,"Here is the first half of the matrix."],[99.2,103.92,"Three columns: the negotiation type, the mechanism, and Coupa's role."],[104.42,106.75,"Row one: competitive leverage."],[106.75,111.11,"Typical categories are supplies, print, hardware and mobile."],[111.11,117.44,"The mechanism is an RFQ, followed by an English reverse auction if the preconditions hold."],[117.44,122.38,"If the market is incumbent-heavy, a reverse Japanese auction can work better."],[122.66,130.99,"Coupa's role is to run the RFQ as a pre-bidding phase, then the auction, with rank or gap-to-best feedback to the bidders."],[130.99,138.49,"The RFQ phase matters: it qualifies bidders and fixes the specification before anyone sees a live price."],[138.99,145.61,"Row two: quality-led services, such as cleaning, security, or the selection of a SaaS tool."],[145.61,155.93,"Here the mechanism is an RFP with an evaluation model fixed before issue, then a shortlist, negotiation rounds and a best and final offer, a BAFO."],[156.21,165.53,"Coupa supports this with an RFP event with questionnaires and lots, a follow-on event for the BAFO, and the Message Center for clarifications."],[165.53,170.31,"The point is that price matters, but quality decides who stays in the race."],[170.81,176.44,"Row three: panels, for example consulting, IT services and agencies."],[176.44,182.09,"The mechanism is a mini-competition per call-off, or a documented direct award below a threshold."],[182.09,189.78,"Coupa's role is a short RFP event created from a template, with the rate card and a statement of work."],[190.28,191.47,"One important note."],[191.47,196.78,"Everything in the Coupa column on this slide is in Coupa's public documentation."],[196.78,202.44,"Settings that are not documented come on the next slides, marked: confirm in your tenant."],[202.94,205.7,"Let me add a short story to make row two real."],[205.7,215.17,"Imagine Nordvia's security and reception contract, 1.6 million euros a year, with hourly rates indexed twice in two years and no KPIs."],[215.17,221.15,"It is tempting to run it as a price competition, because the current rates feel high."],[221.15,223.78,"But security is a quality-led service."],[224.06,228.81,"The guard at reception is the first person a customer or a supervisor meets."],[228.81,241.44,"So the right design is an RFP with a fixed evaluation model, a shortlist of the strongest two or three, a negotiation round on the KPIs and the indexation formula, and then a BAFO."],[241.44,245.27,"You still get a sharp price, because the BAFO is competitive."],[245.55,247.75,"But you get it inside a quality frame."],[248.24,251.77,"And row three, panels, deserves one more sentence."],[251.77,257.39,"A panel is negotiated once, at the framework level, with rate ceilings and terms."],[257.39,262.74,"After that, each call-off is a small, fast competition inside that frame."],[262.74,265.2,"The heavy negotiation is behind you."],[265.48,271.88,"What remains is discipline: inviting enough panel members, using the template, and recording the decision."],[272.83,276.31,"Here is the second half of the matrix, with three more types."],[276.81,281.37,"Row four: sole or single source, typically incumbent software."],[281.37,289.01,"The mechanism is face-to-face, data-driven and executive, with an RFI to alternatives to make the BATNA credible."],[289.01,298.25,"Coupa's role is spend and contract data, the RFI event, and redlines and approvals in contract lifecycle management, CLM."],[298.53,301.52,"And notice the last words: never an auction."],[301.52,306.98,"With one realistic supplier, an auction is either a bluff or an expensive formality."],[307.48,314.45,"Row five: strategic partnership, for example managed services or business process outsourcing."],[314.45,320.7,"The mechanism is an RFI, then an RFP, then dialogue workshops, then a BAFO against fixed criteria."],[320.7,328.64,"In Coupa you run staged follow-on events, use the Message Center, and keep an audit trail of every step."],[328.92,336.43,"This matters because these contracts are large, long and often regulated, and you will need to show how the decision was made."],[336.93,344.12,"Row six: multi-lot deals and bundles, such as multi-site facilities management or telecom lines."],[344.12,348.45,"The mechanism is combinatorial bids and scenario analysis."],[348.45,354.53,"In Coupa, that is Sourcing Optimization: package bids, constraints and what-if scenarios."],[354.81,360.04,"Sourcing Optimization comes from Coupa's acquisition of Trade Extensions in 2017."],[360.04,366.59,"Before you plan an event around it, check whether the Group actually licenses that module."],[367.09,369.66,"Look at the pattern across all six rows."],[369.66,385.85,"As we move from competitive leverage to sole source and strategic partnership, the mechanism shifts from automated price competition to human conversation, and Coupa's role shifts from running the competition to providing structure, data and records."],[385.85,387.8,"But Coupa never disappears."],[388.08,395.94,"Even in the most human negotiation, someone needs the spend data, the contract dates, the approval workflow and the audit trail."],[396.88,406.16,"Now an important discipline for anyone who designs events in Coupa: separating what is documented from what you must confirm in your own tenant."],[406.66,408.9,"On the left, documented by Coupa."],[408.9,411.45,"RFI, RFQ, RFP and Spot Buy events."],[411.45,416.27,"English reverse, Dutch reverse and reverse Japanese auctions."],[416.27,419.79,"An RFx as a pre-bidding phase before an auction."],[419.79,423.77,"Rank or difference-to-best feedback, and lots awarded together."],[423.77,429.15,"And the Message Center, follow-on events and Sourcing Optimization."],[429.65,432.17,"On the right, confirm in your Coupa tenant."],[432.17,437.72,"Overtime or soft-close rules, which extend an auction when a late bid arrives."],[437.72,439.69,"Minimum decrement settings."],[439.69,442.03,"A formal sealed-envelope opening."],[442.03,444.27,"Multi-attribute live auctions."],[444.27,446.57,"And how weighted scoring is configured."],[446.85,452.29,"These may exist in your tenant, or behave differently from what you expect, so do not assume."],[452.79,458.66,"The practical rule: never promise a supplier an auction rule you have not checked in the tenant."],[458.66,464.82,"Write the rules into the event documents, and test them in a mock auction with colleagues playing bidders."],[465.1,473.18,"Imagine telling six bidders that the auction extends by two minutes after every late bid, and then the event simply closes on time."],[473.18,476.54,"You have just created a dispute, and possibly a complaint."],[477.48,482.64,"Let us look at the auction formats as Coupa defines them, because the names cause confusion."],[483.14,486.81,"English reverse: suppliers bid down, the lowest wins."],[486.81,491.18,"Bids must improve, so no increases and no tiny improvements."],[491.18,495.13,"Reverse Japanese: the price starts high and falls by steps."],[495.13,499.11,"Suppliers accept each step, and the last one still active wins."],[499.11,505.09,"This format hides the competitors' prices, which can reduce the visible price war."],[505.37,508.02,"Dutch reverse: here it is a buyer's clock."],[508.02,513.78,"The price starts low and rises, and the first supplier to accept wins."],[513.78,515.68,"This is fast."],[515.68,517.88,"And finally, sealed RFx: one bid each, no feedback."],[517.88,519.71,"That is the classic tender."],[520.21,522.16,"A warning about the Dutch format."],[522.16,525.97,"Many web sources describe the Dutch auction as descending."],[525.97,530.99,"That is the selling version, where a seller's price falls until a buyer accepts."],[530.99,533.82,"In a reverse auction, it works the other way."],[534.1,540.29,"Use Coupa's definition in your event documents, so that suppliers and colleagues share the same picture."],[540.79,543.41,"How do you choose between the three live formats?"],[543.41,545.32,"A simple way to think about it."],[545.32,550.99,"Use English reverse when you have many qualified bidders and want full transparency on rank."],[551.27,561.83,"Use reverse Japanese when an incumbent is strong and you want every bidder to decide, step by step, whether it is still in, without watching competitors undercut each other."],[561.83,566.48,"And use Dutch reverse when speed matters and the market price is well known."],[566.76,576.64,"Whatever you choose, write the format into the event rules, with a plain explanation, and run a briefing call so that every bidder understands it before the event opens."],[577.59,579.62,"When is an auction the right choice?"],[579.62,582.6,"There are six preconditions, and all must be true."],[582.6,583.84,"Not most of them."],[583.84,584.57,"All of them."],[585.06,590.26,"One: the specification is precise and bids can be ranked automatically."],[590.26,593.58,"Two: at least four to five qualified, interested bidders."],[593.58,601.71,"That guidance comes from Smeltzer and Carr, 2003, as summarised by Mora Cortez and colleagues in 2023."],[601.71,605.97,"Three: spare capacity in the market and low switching cost."],[606.25,610.69,"Four: price, or a formula, decides after quality has been assessed."],[610.69,614.59,"Five: a credible BATNA, and the intention to award."],[614.59,618.41,"And six: the post-award relationship can tolerate it."],[618.91,622.62,"You will recognise this checklist from Session 3."],[622.62,626.88,"Let us test it quickly on laptops for the Group's offices."],[626.88,630.68,"A precise specification: yes, a defined model."],[630.68,632.77,"Six qualified resellers: yes."],[632.77,633.55,"Spare capacity: yes."],[633.55,636.88,"Price decides after technical compliance: yes."],[636.88,638.98,"We intend to award: yes."],[639.26,643.86,"And the relationship can tolerate it: a reseller relationship, yes."],[643.86,646.14,"Now test it on the Kelvar renewal."],[646.14,647.99,"It fails at the second line."],[647.99,650.07,"One realistic supplier."],[650.07,651.85,"The checklist stops you there."],[652.35,653.95,"Why does each condition matter?"],[653.95,662.59,"Without a precise specification, bidders are not bidding for the same thing, and the lowest price may simply be the thinnest offer."],[662.59,665.17,"Without enough bidders, there is no real dynamic."],[665.17,669.37,"Without spare capacity, suppliers do not need to fight for the volume."],[669.65,676.37,"If quality has not been assessed before the price event, you may be forced to award to a bid you do not want."],[676.37,679.09,"Without the intention to award, you are bluffing."],[679.09,686.83,"And if the relationship cannot tolerate a public price fight, you may win the auction and lose the supplier's goodwill for years."],[687.78,690.16,"What does the evidence say about results?"],[690.16,690.72,"Four points."],[691.22,693.39,"First, five to fifteen percent."],[693.39,703.46,"That is the typical range of price reductions described in the auction literature, for example Jap in 2002 and Smeltzer and Carr in 2003."],[703.46,707.89,"Treat it as the literature's typical range, not a promise."],[707.89,709.05,"Second: less."],[709.33,715.8,"Emiliani and Stec, in 2002, found that realised savings were lower than providers reported."],[715.8,719.52,"Vendor savings claims are not independent evidence."],[719.52,721.45,"Third: over one third."],[721.73,736.47,"A report by the US Government Accountability Office, GAO-14-108, found that in fiscal year 2012 over one third of US federal reverse auctions had no interactive bidding at all."],[736.47,737.52,"Fourth: no gain."],[737.8,748.12,"Sandy Jap's research in 2003 and 2007 found that open-bid formats hurt trust more than sealed formats, without producing higher savings."],[748.62,750.03,"What does this mean for you?"],[750.03,752.94,"Auctions can work, in the right conditions."],[752.94,762.61,"But the savings are often smaller than the brochure says, many auctions do not produce real competition, and visible bidding has a relationship cost."],[762.61,767.29,"So use them where the conditions hold, and be honest in your savings reports."],[767.79,769.43,"There is one more lesson here."],[769.43,776.07,"Incumbents tend to bid less aggressively than challengers, because they know their switching-cost advantage."],[776.07,786.56,"So the savings in an auction often come from the challengers pushing the incumbent, which only happens if the challengers are genuinely qualified and you would genuinely switch."],[787.51,791.43,"Now four worked examples from Nordvia, our fictional insurer."],[791.43,795.38,"First, quality-led services: the headquarters cleaning lot."],[795.89,801.27,"The RFP used a fifty-fifty split between quality and price, fixed before issue."],[801.27,808.37,"Bidder C was excluded: after clarification, its staffing hours were below what the wage floor allows."],[808.37,812.18,"That left two bidders close together, Bidder B and Bidder D."],[812.18,814.7,"Should Nordvia run an auction?"],[814.7,814.75,"No."],[815.03,819.0,"There are only two bidders, and it is a labour-intensive service."],[819.0,829.26,"A price-only auction here would push bids towards the wage floor, and the people who clean the building at night would pay the price, followed by Nordvia in service quality."],[829.75,836.07,"Instead, Nordvia runs a BAFO round as a Coupa follow-on event, with the responses carried over."],[836.07,845.83,"Both bidders get a fair last chance, under the same rules, on price and on the points still open, such as Bidder D's request for a four-year term."],[845.83,850.4,"That is a negotiation mechanism designed for the negotiation type."],[851.36,856.24,"The second example is a panel: a call-off under the IT services panel."],[856.24,859.13,"The need is a senior developer for six months."],[859.62,866.25,"The value is above the threshold, so it is a mini-competition, and at least three panel members are invited."],[866.25,875.26,"The buyer creates a short RFP event from the call-off template, asking for the role, CVs and a day rate within the rate card ceiling."],[875.54,880.74,"Evaluation follows the framework criteria, and every bidder gets written feedback."],[880.74,886.69,"Below the threshold, the route would be a documented direct award, with a rate-card check."],[887.2,889.95,"This is fast, fair and auditable."],[889.95,899.98,"And it is how Nordvia closes the spread you saw in Session 2, where the same senior profile was bought at day rates from 820 to 1,450 euros."],[899.98,906.36,"Every call-off now competes within a ceiling, so the high end of that spread disappears."],[906.85,908.89,"Notice what made this possible."],[908.89,916.71,"The hard negotiation happened earlier, when the panel was set up: who is on it, the rate card ceilings, the call-off terms."],[916.71,922.62,"Each call-off then only competes on what is left open, the person and the rate within the ceiling."],[922.62,926.22,"That is why panels give speed without giving up competition."],[926.5,935.82,"And Coupa's templates make sure every buyer runs the call-off the same way, which matters when an auditor later asks why one firm received most of the work."],[936.78,941.75,"The third example is sole source: Kelvar, the core insurance software vendor."],[942.25,944.0,"No auction, and no RFP for show."],[944.0,950.83,"There is one realistic supplier for this cycle, and everyone knows it, including Kelvar."],[950.83,956.03,"Running a fake tender would waste suppliers' time and damage Nordvia's credibility."],[956.03,957.87,"So what is Coupa's role?"],[957.87,962.5,"It provides usage and spend data, the contract terms and the notice dates."],[962.78,970.14,"An RFI event to two alternative platforms makes the BATNA credible, not for this renewal, but for the next one."],[970.14,974.55,"The negotiation itself is face-to-face, with executive alignment."],[974.55,982.49,"And the redlines and approvals go through the CLM workflow, so every change to the contract is visible and approved."],[982.98,988.54,"Notice that Coupa adds real value here even though there is no competition in the event."],[988.54,990.6,"Data, alternatives, documentation."],[990.6,994.99,"This is the set-up for the role-play in the next lesson."],[995.49,997.02,"There is a broader point."],[997.02,1001.64,"In a sole-source negotiation, your power does not come from the event."],[1001.64,1003.43,"It comes from information."],[1003.43,1005.97,"How many named users do we really use?"],[1005.97,1010.0,"What did we pay per user last time, and what do benchmarks suggest?"],[1010.0,1011.66,"When is the notice deadline?"],[1011.94,1015.95,"What do the alternatives cost, and how long would migration take?"],[1015.95,1023.18,"Coupa holds part of that information, and procurement's job is to bring it together before the vendor brings its own version."],[1024.14,1029.32,"The fourth example is illustrative: bundles, with telecom lines for 40 sites."],[1029.82,1034.95,"The scope spans three countries and covers fixed, mobile and data lines."],[1034.95,1044.85,"Suppliers offer package discounts across sites and services: a lower price if they win all mobile lines, or everything in one country."],[1044.85,1046.67,"A normal RFQ cannot capture that."],[1046.67,1051.22,"Sourcing Optimization lets suppliers bid packages and alternatives."],[1051.5,1053.72,"The buyer then compares scenarios."],[1053.72,1055.48,"The lowest total cost."],[1055.48,1057.55,"A maximum of two suppliers."],[1057.55,1058.71,"One supplier per country."],[1058.71,1063.98,"Each scenario has a price, and you can see what a constraint really costs."],[1064.26,1073.9,"If a maximum of two suppliers adds, for example, an illustrative two percent to the total, the business can decide whether simpler management is worth it."],[1073.9,1077.06,"The chosen scenario then flows into contracts."],[1077.56,1083.97,"Expressive bidding plus scenario analysis is integrative negotiation run through a machine."],[1083.97,1096.1,"One more point: telecom services are ICT services, so check the DORA scope before you contract, especially if any lines support critical functions such as claims systems."],[1097.05,1098.42,"Now, what never to do."],[1098.42,1100.55,"Five misuses, and why they hurt."],[1101.05,1104.4,"An auction as a bluff, with no intention to award."],[1104.4,1109.02,"Trust and future participation collapse, as Jap's 2003 work warns."],[1109.02,1111.52,"Renegotiating after the auction."],[1111.52,1116.31,"Bidders learn to hold back next time, and the rules lose meaning."],[1116.31,1119.33,"Phantom bidders, invented to create pressure."],[1119.61,1124.78,"That is unethical, and fatal to trust when found, and it usually is found."],[1124.78,1126.98,"One or two bidders."],[1126.98,1130.28,"As the GAO report showed, that is just an expensive RFQ."],[1130.28,1132.85,"And intellectual or complex work."],[1132.85,1142.63,"In EU public procurement, electronic auctions are excluded for intellectual performances, under Directive 2014 24, Article 35."],[1142.91,1146.0,"We are not a public buyer, but the logic transfers."],[1146.5,1155.3,"Add a sixth case from our own categories: price-only auctions for cleaning or security without a quality threshold and a wage-floor check."],[1155.3,1159.87,"The cheapest bid often means fewer hours or underpaid staff."],[1159.87,1163.72,"That leads to service failure and legal exposure for the buyer."],[1164.22,1166.6,"Why are these misuses so tempting?"],[1166.6,1170.1,"Because each one produces a good number in the short term."],[1170.1,1173.73,"The bluff auction scares the incumbent into a discount."],[1173.73,1177.08,"The post-auction call extracts an extra percent."],[1177.36,1187.36,"But the cost appears later, and it lands on someone else: the next buyer, who finds that suppliers no longer bid seriously, or no longer bid at all."],[1187.36,1189.89,"A reputation for fair events is an asset."],[1189.89,1193.26,"It takes years to build, and one bad event to lose."],[1194.2,1198.04,"Here is a sentence from research worth remembering."],[1198.04,1204.76,"Auctions perform poorly when projects are complex, contractual design is incomplete and there are few bidders."],[1204.76,1212.8,"That is from Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis, 2009, who studied private building contracts in Northern California."],[1213.31,1219.48,"Their point is that auctions stifle the communication needed to use the supplier's expertise."],[1219.48,1224.54,"When the work is complex, the best supplier often helps you shape the solution."],[1224.54,1226.62,"An auction closes that conversation."],[1226.62,1229.01,"Negotiation does better there."],[1229.01,1234.28,"Think of a claims IT transformation or a new marketing agency relationship."],[1234.56,1236.8,"You want dialogue, not a ticking clock."],[1237.3,1243.39,"So when you face a complex project, the question is not how to get the lowest price in a live event."],[1243.39,1251.21,"It is how to structure a conversation where the best supplier shares its knowledge, and the competition still keeps everyone honest."],[1251.49,1258.53,"That is what RFP rounds, dialogue workshops and a BAFO against fixed criteria are designed to do."],[1259.48,1260.88,"A reflection question."],[1260.88,1263.59,"Which e-auction misuse have you seen?"],[1263.59,1265.61,"An auction with one or two bidders."],[1265.61,1268.81,"An auction for services that cannot be specified."],[1268.81,1271.39,"Renegotiation after the auction."],[1271.39,1274.4,"An auction run without intention to award."],[1274.4,1275.72,"Or none of these."],[1275.72,1277.25,"Think about it for a moment."],[1277.53,1285.19,"If you have seen one, ask yourself what it cost: in money, in trust, or in the number of suppliers willing to bid the next time."],[1286.14,1290.08,"Now Practice 4.2: choose the negotiation mode."],[1290.08,1291.98,"Plan about 10 minutes, in your workbook."],[1291.98,1295.76,"You have six Nordvia scenarios plus your own project."],[1295.76,1301.41,"For each one, choose the mode from the matrix, and describe the Coupa set-up in one line."],[1301.69,1309.87,"In the live session, pairs compare and defend their choice for scenarios 2, 4 and 5, because those produce the best discussion."],[1310.36,1335.26,"The six scenarios are: office supplies with a fixed basket and six qualified suppliers; headquarters cleaning with four bids, two above the quality threshold; a senior developer for six months under the IT services panel; the Kelvar core licence renewal; the Northbyte claims IT managed service at the end of its term; and telecom lines for 40 sites in three countries with bundle discounts."],[1335.54,1340.26,"If you want to, pause the video now, open the Modules tab and complete it."],[1340.26,1343.73,"Then continue to compare with the suggested answers."],[1344.68,1346.43,"Here are the suggested answers."],[1346.93,1351.61,"Scenario 1, office supplies: an RFQ, then an English reverse auction."],[1351.61,1360.25,"In Coupa, the RFQ as pre-bidding, then the auction, and the result loaded as a catalogue so people actually buy from it."],[1360.25,1367.09,"Scenario 2, headquarters cleaning: an RFP with rounds and a BAFO, run as a follow-on event."],[1367.37,1375.34,"Scenario 3, the senior developer: a panel mini-competition, an RFP from the call-off template."],[1375.34,1382.4,"Scenario 4, Kelvar: a sole-source negotiation, supported by data, an RFI to alternatives and CLM."],[1382.4,1388.77,"Scenario 6, telecom lines: Sourcing Optimization, with package bids and scenarios."],[1389.27,1392.21,"Scenario 5, Northbyte, deserves a moment."],[1392.21,1400.97,"The suggested answer is a multi-stage dialogue and a BAFO, using staged events and including exit and DORA criteria."],[1400.97,1408.85,"Northbyte runs a claims IT managed service of 19.4 million euros a year that supports a critical function."],[1409.13,1417.03,"But it can also be a sole-source renegotiation, if no exit is feasible in time before the contract ends."],[1417.03,1418.5,"Either answer needs a tested exit plan."],[1418.5,1421.38,"We return to that in module 4.4."],[1422.33,1423.54,"Let us debrief."],[1423.54,1425.98,"Where was the auction tempting but wrong?"],[1425.98,1430.42,"For many people it is the cleaning lot, because the price gap looks attractive."],[1430.42,1434.35,"In which scenario does Coupa add most value without any auction?"],[1434.35,1437.07,"Often Kelvar or the panel call-off."],[1437.35,1442.89,"What did you choose for your own project, and does it follow from your power position?"],[1442.89,1446.65,"If it does not, look again at your Session 1 positioning."],[1446.65,1451.24,"And finally: which Coupa setting must you confirm in the tenant before your event?"],[1451.24,1452.25,"Write that one down."],[1452.25,1454.82,"It is a concrete action for Fieldwork 4."],[1455.32,1458.4,"Let me give you one example of a good final action."],[1458.4,1472.0,"A buyer planning an office supplies auction writes: before the event, confirm with the Coupa administrator whether overtime rules and minimum decrements are available, and run a mock auction with two colleagues."],[1472.0,1474.2,"That is specific, owned and testable."],[1474.48,1476.82,"Compare it with: check Coupa settings."],[1476.82,1480.11,"The first one will happen; the second one probably will not."],[1481.06,1483.96,"Here is module 4.2 in three lines."],[1483.96,1487.55,"Negotiation type first, mechanism second, Coupa third."],[1487.55,1492.59,"Auctions only when every precondition holds, and never as a bluff."],[1492.59,1497.49,"And Coupa adds value in every type: structure, data and an audit trail."],[1497.99,1516.12,"To recap the lesson: we built the six-row matrix from competitive leverage to bundles, separated what Coupa documents from what you must confirm in your tenant, clarified the auction formats, tested the six preconditions, looked honestly at the evidence, and walked through four Nordvia examples."],[1516.4,1522.08,"In the next lesson we negotiate where no auction is possible: the Kelvar sole-source renewal."]]},{"id":"s4-l3","session":"s4","title":"Case 4: the Kelvar renewal","summary":"Prepare and run a sole-source renewal negotiation: use timing, an honest BATNA, rightsizing and conditional concessions to reach a strong deal without giving up DORA clauses.","from":48,"to":58,"audio":"audio/s4-l3.mp3","duration":1435.4,"segs":[[48,0.6,38.96,"Welcome to Case 4, part 1: the Kelvar renewal. This is a sole-source negotiation role-play, set at Nordvia Insurance, our fictional insurer. In this lesson we go deep into one negotiation. We look at the facts, work out the power position honestly, build the BATNA, choose the levers, plan the concessions, and then look at what a good outcome looked like and why. If you are following the live session, you will play this case in a breakout room. If you are studying alone, you can still prepare the buyer side in your workbook, and this lesson will walk you through the logic so you can compare your plan with a strong one."],[48,39.46,62.44,"Why spend a whole lesson on one negotiation? Because sole-source renewals are where many insurers lose the most money with the least noise. There is no tender, no committee, no savings report. The contract simply rolls on, a little more expensive each time. Kelvar is the kind of case almost every procurement team in the Group has somewhere in its portfolio."],[49,63.39,97.34,"Let us start with the facts, which are fictional. The annual spend is 11.2 million euros, for core insurance software licences. The last renewal brought a nine percent uplift, under an auto-renewal clause. The current term runs to June 2027, and notice must be given nine months before. Usage data suggests that an estimated 20 to 25 percent of named-user licences are unused. And there are two credible alternative platforms, but migration would take 18 to 24 months."],[49,97.84,149.62,"Read these facts as a negotiator, one by one. The spend of 11.2 million euros makes this the largest licence line in Nordvia's IT budget. Every percent is worth about 112 thousand euros a year. The nine percent uplift last time tells you what happens when nobody prepares: an auto-renewal clause did the negotiating, and Kelvar won by default. The notice period tells you that time is a resource. Nine months before June 2027 is the last moment Nordvia can stop the contract from rolling over. The unused licences are your strongest fact. Nordvia is paying for something it does not use, and that is not a matter of opinion. And the alternatives tell you two things at once: there is a credible future without Kelvar, but not a quick one."],[49,150.12,182.72,"Now the most important line, which is in the speaker notes rather than on the table. A protective notice was served before the deadline. That means the auto-renewal no longer applies. Without a new agreement, the contract simply ends in June 2027. That single action changed the power balance. Before the notice, Kelvar could wait: doing nothing meant renewal at its price. After the notice, doing nothing means Kelvar loses an 11 million euro customer. Kelvar now needs an agreement too."],[49,183.22,209.72,"A protective notice is not a threat to leave. It is a formal step that keeps your options open. You write to the vendor, within the notice period, that you do not wish the contract to renew automatically, and that you look forward to negotiating new terms. Legal should check the wording against the contract. The lesson for your own portfolio is simple: every auto-renewal clause needs a date in the pipeline well before the notice deadline."],[50,210.67,215.17,"Now the power position, using the four lenses from module 4.1."],[50,215.67,253.77,"Kraljic: strategic, with high supply risk for this cycle. The software runs core insurance processes, the spend is high, and switching is slow. Preferencing: Kelvar likely sees Nordvia as exploitable. Nordvia pays a lot, costs little to serve, and has bought nothing new in years. From Kelvar's side, Nordvia is a reliable source of margin, not a growth account. The Chessboard: change the nature of demand. That means fewer users, a re-specified scope, and alternatives prepared for the future. Price haggling alone will not move a vendor that knows you cannot leave quickly."],[50,254.27,286.81,"And the BATNA: weak now, real in two years. Let us be honest about it, because honesty is the whole point. No migration is possible by June 2027. If Nordvia walked away from Kelvar tomorrow, it would have no core system. So the credible threat is not this cycle. It is the next one. What makes the BATNA real is work: the CIO has approved an RFI to both alternative platforms, and a plan for a bridge of 12 to 24 months on reduced users, followed by migration."],[50,287.31,329.91,"So what is Nordvia's alternative to a negotiated agreement, stated precisely? A short bridge contract with Kelvar on a reduced user base, at roughly today's price, while preparing a migration. That alternative is not pleasant, but it is real and approved. It sets the walk-away: Nordvia will not sign a long contract on worse terms than its bridge. And Kelvar's BATNA? If Nordvia signs only a bridge and migrates, Kelvar loses the customer in two or three years. If there is no deal before Kelvar's year end, it also misses its forecast. Kelvar's alternative is worse than it looks from its side of the table, as long as Nordvia's preparation is visible."],[50,330.41,354.48,"This is where many buyers go wrong. They either bluff, saying they will move platforms by next summer, which the account manager knows is impossible. Or they give up, assuming a sole source means no power. Both are mistakes. The honest position is stronger than both: we cannot leave this year, we have started preparing to leave next time, and the terms we sign now will decide whether we do."],[50,354.98,383.66,"One more point on preferencing. Remember that attractiveness can be managed. Nordvia can become more interesting to Kelvar without paying more: a clear three-year roadmap, a willingness to look at new products, a reference if the service is good, and reliable, on-time payment. None of these costs much. All of them move Nordvia, in Kelvar's eyes, away from exploitable and towards core. And a core customer gets better terms and more flexibility."],[51,384.61,389.02,"With the power position clear, here are the levers for the buyers. Six of them."],[51,389.52,407.48,"Rightsizing: remove unused named users before pricing anything. This is the single biggest lever, and the order matters. If you negotiate a discount first and then remove users, the vendor will try to reprice the smaller volume. Agree the volume first, then the price per unit."],[51,407.98,439.9,"Let us do the arithmetic, with illustrative numbers. If the price were purely per named user, removing 20 percent of users would take 11.2 million euros down to about 9 million, and removing 25 percent would take it to 8.4 million. In reality, licence prices are not perfectly linear. There are platform fees, support charges and minimum volumes. That is why the buyer brief sets an illustrative target of about 10 million euros a year after rightsizing, not 9."],[51,440.4,493.95,"Protect the future: an uplift cap, a price hold and the right to reduce volumes. This is how you make sure the nine percent never happens again. Term: trade the length of the term for price and flexibility. Kelvar wants five years. Nordvia wants three, because the alternatives will be ready by then. Regulation: the software supports critical functions, so the clauses required by DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act, Article 30, paragraph 3, are not negotiable. What you negotiate is how they are met, not whether. Group volume: check whether other Group entities license Kelvar. If Entity C or Entity F, for example, also run it, the conversation changes from one mid-sized customer to a Group relationship. And one voice: IT must not pre-commit in parallel conversations."],[51,494.45,550.16,"Let me say more about two of these levers, because they are often misunderstood. First, term. Many buyers think a longer term is always a gift to the vendor, to be given only for a big discount. But here the term has a strategic value for Nordvia too. Three years ends just as the alternatives could be ready. Five years would lock Nordvia in past the point where it could leave, and destroy the next BATNA before it exists. So the term is not just a price lever. It is how Nordvia protects its future power. Second, one voice. Imagine the head of claims IT meets Kelvar's product team at a user conference and says: of course we are renewing, we could never move. That sentence, said in good faith, is worth millions to Kelvar. So before the negotiation, the CIO, the business owner and procurement agree a single message, and every contact with Kelvar is logged."],[51,550.66,571.89,"These levers come from the sole-source playbook in Session 2. There is a seventh lever if time allows: unbundling. Separate the licence, the support and the services, and look at each one. Support is often charged as a percentage of licence value, so rightsizing the licences should reduce support too, but only if the contract says so."],[52,572.84,594.24,"Now the roles in each breakout room. The lead buyer runs the negotiation from the one-pager. The analyst handles numbers, notes and signals. The Kelvar account manager has a private brief from the teacher, which the buyers do not see. And the observer records the first offer, every concession, and every interest that is discovered."],[52,594.74,642.92,"If you are studying alone, take the buyer side and imagine the account manager sitting across from you. Before we go further, here is the buyer brief in full, because your one-pager depends on it. Kelvar opened at plus nine percent, about 12.2 million euros a year, for five years. The illustrative targets are about 10 million euros a year after removing unused users, an uplift cap of three percent, three years with the right to reduce volume by 10 percent a year, and transition assistance. The illustrative walk-away is 11.2 million euros flat for three years, with the reduction rights and the DORA clauses. In other words: no increase at all, a shorter term, flexibility and compliance. Anything worse, and Nordvia prefers its bridge."],[52,643.42,672.03,"Let me put the four numbers for price side by side. Opening: Nordvia could open close to 9.5 million euros, below its target, defensible with the usage data. Target: about 10 million. Walk-away: 11.2 million, flat. Kelvar's opening: 12.2 million. The gap between the two openings is 2.7 million euros a year. Over three years that is more than 8 million euros. That is what this conversation is worth."],[52,672.53,717.63,"One more preparation step before the room: agree the concession list. Here is a sensible one for Nordvia, written as conditional moves. If Kelvar accepts three years with reduction rights, then Nordvia can sign quickly, at a date that suits Kelvar. If Kelvar caps any uplift at three percent, then Nordvia can consider a reference, provided the service meets its levels. If Kelvar accepts pooled audits and certifications that satisfy DORA, then Nordvia can agree a sensible schedule for on-site visits. Notice that each move gives something cheap for Nordvia and asks for something Nordvia values. And notice what is not on the list: the walk-away price, the DORA clauses themselves, and a five-year term."],[53,718.58,730.96,"Here is the hint for the role-play. Kelvar's opening is firm. Its real interests are not in the opening. Your job is to find out what the seller needs, not to argue about the nine percent."],[53,731.46,760.64,"Think back to Fisher and Ury. The nine percent is a position. Behind it there are interests, and they are not on the table yet. They only come out with good questions about the seller's goals. Not questions about price, but questions like these. What does a successful year look like for your team? How does this renewal fit your plans for our account? Where is your product going in the next three years? What would make this deal easy to approve on your side? Is there any timing that matters to you?"],[53,761.14,787.43,"Each of those questions invites the account manager to talk about their world, their targets, their product strategy and their internal approvals. A seller who feels heard often reveals more than they intended. And notice what you do not do: you do not attack the nine percent, you do not argue about whether it is fair, and you do not reveal your walk-away. You listen, you summarise, and you let the analyst write down every hint."],[53,787.93,816.82,"Also prepare your own opening statement. A good one is calm and factual. For example: we value the platform and want to find an agreement before the contract ends. Our usage review shows that a significant share of named users are not active, so we want to base the new agreement on actual use. We are looking for a shorter term with predictable pricing, and we need the DORA requirements for a critical service built in. Then stop talking, and ask your first question."],[54,817.77,837.2,"Now the role-play itself, which takes 35 minutes in the live session. Ten minutes for the buyers to prepare the one-pager while the seller reads the brief. Twenty minutes to negotiate. Five minutes of observer feedback. And then record the outcome per issue, against target and walk-away, in Case 4 of your workbook."],[54,837.7,867.29,"A few practical tips for those twenty minutes. Use the first five to explore, not to bargain. Put rightsizing on the table early, as a fact, not as a concession. Use packages: if you give us three years with a three percent cap and reduction rights, then we can discuss what matters to you. Keep the analyst writing every number. And if the room does not close, record where you stopped and why. A clean no deal, with a clear record, is better than a deal beyond your walk-away."],[54,867.79,903.79,"Your workbook has a negotiation record with five issues: annual price, uplift cap, term, volume flexibility, and exit and DORA clauses. For each, write what was agreed and whether it was better than target, at target, between target and walk-away, or beyond walk-away. Then write down the seller interests you found, and the question that revealed each one. That last field is the most valuable learning in the exercise. If you are working alone, pause the video now, open your workbook, prepare the buyer one-pager and imagine the negotiation. Then continue."],[54,904.29,931.7,"And a word for the observer, if you play that role. Your notes are the mirror for the room. Write down who made the first offer and what it was. Note every concession, who made it, and whether it was conditional. Mark the moment any interest appeared, and the question that triggered it. In the feedback, start with facts, not judgements: at minute twelve, the buyer moved from three to four years without asking for anything. Facts like that teach more than any lecture."],[55,932.65,957.89,"A reflection question. Where did the Kelvar annual price land in your room? Below 10.3 million euros. Between 10.3 and 10.8 million. Between 10.8 and 11.2 million. Or above 11.2 million, or no deal. If you prepared alone, ask yourself where your plan would have landed. Hold that number in your mind, because the next slides reveal the seller's limits."],[56,958.84,980.26,"Now the debrief, and the reveal. Five questions. Who discovered the seller's year-end deadline, and with which question? Who learned about the SaaS strategy, and what did it let you trade? Did anyone concede on the DORA clauses, and what should have happened? Where did the price land against your target and walk-away? And what would you do differently 18 months before renewal?"],[56,980.76,1027.49,"Here is the seller's private brief. Kelvar's fiscal year ends in May, and this deal is in its forecast. Signing before year end matters more to the account manager than the last euro. Kelvar also wants customers to move to its SaaS edition, and an insurance reference in this region would help its sales. Nordvia costs little to serve and has bought nothing new in years. The seller's illustrative limits: down to about 10.3 million euros a year for three years, in exchange for a joint SaaS evaluation in 2028 and a reference, with an uplift cap of three percent. The seller dislikes unlimited on-site audits, but accepts the DORA clauses if they can be met through certifications and pooled audits."],[56,1027.99,1062.04,"Now you can see the ZOPA on price. Nordvia's walk-away is 11.2 million euros. Kelvar's floor is about 10.3 million, but only if Kelvar gets its interests met: the year-end signature, the SaaS evaluation and the reference. So the zone of agreement runs from about 10.3 to 11.2 million euros. Nordvia's target of 10 million was just below the seller's floor. That is fine. A target should be ambitious. But it explains why the best rooms landed near 10.3, not below it."],[56,1062.54,1098.74,"Notice also that the seller's floor depends on the interests. A room that never asked about goals saw only the nine percent and a seller defending it. That room usually landed between 10.8 and 11.2 million, or failed to close. A room that discovered the year end and the SaaS strategy could offer something cheap for Nordvia and valuable to Kelvar, and in return reach the seller's floor. Rooms that found the interests usually landed near the seller's floor. That is the integrative logic of Lax and Sebenius at work: create value, then claim it."],[56,1099.24,1131.82,"And the DORA question. Some buyers, under pressure, offered to drop the audit clause in exchange for price. That is a mistake. For a service supporting a critical function, the Article 30, paragraph 3 clauses, including audit and access rights and exit with an adequate transition period, are required. You cannot trade away a legal requirement. What you can negotiate is how it is met, and that is exactly the seller's real concern: not the principle of audit, but the cost of unlimited on-site visits."],[56,1132.32,1167.81,"And the last debrief question: what would you do differently 18 months before renewal? This is where the real lesson lies. With 18 months, Nordvia could have run the usage review calmly, sent the RFI to both alternatives, checked Group volume across the entities, and aligned the CIO and the business owner on one message. It could even have started a proof of concept with one alternative, which would turn a weak BATNA into a medium one. Most of the value in a sole-source renewal is created long before the first meeting. The negotiation in the room only collects it."],[57,1168.76,1173.17,"Here is what a good outcome looked like. It is illustrative, after the reveal."],[57,1173.67,1201.75,"First, unused named users removed before pricing. Second, about 10.3 million euros a year for three years, with any uplift capped at three percent. Third, the right to reduce volume each year. Fourth, the DORA clauses met through certifications and pooled audits, not dropped. And fifth, in exchange: a joint SaaS evaluation in 2028 and a reference, signed before Kelvar's year end."],[57,1202.25,1236.02,"Let us value it, again with illustrative numbers. Kelvar's opening, 12.2 million euros a year over three years, totals about 36.6 million. The outcome, 10.3 million a year, totals about 30.9 million. That is roughly 5.7 million euros less over the term. Compared with simply staying at today's 11.2 million, the saving is about 2.7 million over three years. And compared with another nine percent uplift at the next renewal, the cap protects much more than that."],[57,1236.52,1278.26,"But the value is not only in the price. The three-year term ends just as the alternatives could be ready, so Nordvia keeps its next BATNA alive. The reduction right means Nordvia pays for actual use as it changes. The pooled audits and certifications satisfy the regulator at a cost Kelvar accepts. And look at what Nordvia gave: a joint evaluation of the SaaS edition, which Nordvia would want to look at anyway, a reference, if the service is good, and a signature date that suited Kelvar. Each of those cost Nordvia little and mattered a lot to Kelvar. Concessions that are cheap for us and valuable to them, given only in exchange."],[57,1278.76,1304.81,"Notice one detail in the SaaS evaluation. It is an evaluation, not a commitment to migrate. Nordvia keeps the option to compare the SaaS edition with the two alternative platforms in 2028. If you had promised to move to SaaS, you would have handed Kelvar the next negotiation. Both sides got what mattered most to them. That is integrative negotiation in a sole-source setting."],[58,1305.76,1317.6,"Here is Case 4 in three lines. Serve the protective notice: timing is power. An honest BATNA beats a bluff. And ask about their goals; trade what is cheap for you and valuable to them."],[58,1318.1,1353.92,"Which of the three applies to your own project? If you have an incumbent contract with an auto-renewal clause, the first line is your action this week: find the notice date and put it in the pipeline. Ideally, start preparing 12 to 18 months before renewal. That is a practitioner rule of thumb, not a law, but it gives you time for a usage review, an RFI and executive alignment. If your alternative is weak, the second line asks you to build it honestly. And for every negotiation, the third line is the habit that separates good negotiators from loud ones."],[58,1354.42,1399.91,"One last reflection on the poll. If your room landed between 10.8 and 11.2 million euros, do not see it as a failure. It was still inside the walk-away, and you protected the term and the clauses. The lesson is simply that better questions about the seller's goals would have opened more value. If your room landed below 10.3 million, check whether the seller gave up something that was not in the brief, or whether Nordvia quietly gave away a DORA clause or a long term to get there. A low price bought with a bad contract is not a good outcome. And if your room did not close, look at the record. Did you run out of time because you bargained on price before exploring interests? That is the most common reason."],[58,1400.41,1434.2,"To recap the lesson: we read the Kelvar facts as a negotiator, saw how a protective notice shifted the power, positioned the case with Kraljic, preferencing and the Chessboard, built an honest BATNA, chose the levers, planned the four numbers on price, explored the seller's interests, and saw how a package of conditional concessions reached the seller's floor while keeping the DORA clauses intact. In the next lesson we move from the deal to the paper: the award recommendation and the structure of a contract that protects the value you have just negotiated."]],"questions":[{"after":49,"q":"Why did serving the protective notice change the power balance in the Kelvar renewal?","options":["It allowed Nordvia to migrate to another platform before June 2027","Without a new agreement the contract now ends, so Kelvar also needs a deal","It forced Kelvar to drop the nine percent uplift","It made the DORA clauses optional"],"answer":1,"explain":"Before the notice, doing nothing meant renewal at Kelvar's price. After it, doing nothing means Kelvar loses the customer, so both sides need an agreement.","t":209.72},{"after":50,"q":"In the first meeting, the IT director wants to tell Kelvar that Nordvia will move to another platform by next summer. What do you advise?","options":["Do it, a strong threat improves the price","Say nothing about alternatives at all","State the honest position: no migration this cycle, but an approved RFI and bridge plan for the next one","Ask Kelvar to propose its own alternative"],"answer":2,"explain":"Migration takes 18 to 24 months, so the threat would be seen as a bluff. An honest, approved BATNA for the next cycle is more credible and therefore more powerful.","t":383.66},{"after":51,"q":"Kelvar offers a 6 percent discount on the current price if Nordvia agrees now, before the user review is finished. What is the best response?","options":["Accept, a discount is a discount","Ask for 8 percent instead","Accept and remove unused users later","Agree the volume first by removing unused named users, then negotiate the unit price"],"answer":3,"explain":"Rightsizing comes before pricing. If you accept a discount first, the vendor will try to reprice the smaller volume later, and the value of removing 20 to 25 percent of unused users is lost.","t":571.89},{"after":52,"q":"Kelvar's account manager proposes 11.4 million euros a year for three years with a three percent cap and reduction rights. Using the buyer brief, how should Nordvia treat this offer?","options":["It is beyond the walk-away of 11.2 million flat, so Nordvia cannot accept it as it stands","It is at target, so sign","It is between target and walk-away, so accept","It is irrelevant because only the term matters"],"answer":0,"explain":"The illustrative walk-away is 11.2 million euros flat for three years with reduction rights and DORA clauses. Anything worse is beyond walk-away, so Nordvia keeps negotiating or prefers its bridge.","t":717.63},{"after":53,"q":"Which question is most likely to reveal Kelvar's real interests?","options":["Why is your uplift so high?","What does a successful year look like for your team, and does any timing matter to you?","Can you match your competitors' prices?","What is your lowest possible price?"],"answer":1,"explain":"Open questions about the seller's goals bring out interests such as the year-end forecast and the SaaS strategy. Questions about price only invite the seller to defend its position.","t":816.82},{"after":54,"q":"With two minutes left, the seller offers 11.0 million euros a year for five years. Your target is three years. What should you do?","options":["Accept, because the price is below the walk-away","Accept five years to close before time runs out","Do not trade the term under time pressure: record where you stopped, or offer a conditional package on three years","Give up the reduction rights to get three years"],"answer":2,"explain":"A five-year term would remove Nordvia's next BATNA. A clean no deal with a clear record beats a deal that breaks the plan, and any move should be conditional.","t":931.7},{"after":56,"q":"The seller says Kelvar cannot accept unlimited on-site audits. What is the right response under DORA?","options":["Drop the audit clause in exchange for a lower price","Agree on how audit rights are met, for example through certifications and pooled audits, without removing them","Refuse to discuss audit at all","Move the audit clause to a side letter that is not signed"],"answer":1,"explain":"For a critical ICT service the Article 30, paragraph 3 clauses are required. Negotiate how they are met, never whether, which also answers the seller's real concern about cost.","t":1167.81},{"after":57,"q":"In the good outcome, Nordvia agreed to a joint SaaS evaluation in 2028. Why is this a well-designed concession?","options":["It commits Nordvia to migrate to SaaS, which pleases Kelvar","It replaces the need for the DORA exit clause","It is cheap for Nordvia, valuable to Kelvar, and keeps Nordvia free to compare alternatives","It allows Kelvar to raise prices in 2028"],"answer":2,"explain":"An evaluation is not a commitment. It matters to Kelvar's SaaS strategy, costs Nordvia little, and preserves Nordvia's BATNA for the next cycle.","t":1304.81},{"after":58,"q":"You find that the facilities management contract in your Sourcing Case has an auto-renewal clause with a six-month notice period. What is your first action?","options":["Wait for the supplier's renewal proposal","Ask for a discount at the next quarterly review","Find the notice date, put a decision date well before it in the pipeline, and prepare to serve a protective notice","Terminate the contract immediately"],"answer":2,"explain":"Timing is power. Knowing and acting on the notice date keeps your options open; a protective notice stops the contract from renewing by default without being a threat to leave.","t":1434.2}],"cues":[[0.6,4.33,"Welcome to Case 4, part 1: the Kelvar renewal."],[4.33,10.88,"This is a sole-source negotiation role-play, set at Nordvia Insurance, our fictional insurer."],[10.88,14.1,"In this lesson we go deep into one negotiation."],[14.38,24.85,"We look at the facts, work out the power position honestly, build the BATNA, choose the levers, plan the concessions, and then look at what a good outcome looked like and why."],[24.85,29.23,"If you are following the live session, you will play this case in a breakout room."],[29.51,38.96,"If you are studying alone, you can still prepare the buyer side in your workbook, and this lesson will walk you through the logic so you can compare your plan with a strong one."],[39.46,42.38,"Why spend a whole lesson on one negotiation?"],[42.38,48.72,"Because sole-source renewals are where many insurers lose the most money with the least noise."],[48.72,51.9,"There is no tender, no committee, no savings report."],[51.9,56.16,"The contract simply rolls on, a little more expensive each time."],[56.44,62.44,"Kelvar is the kind of case almost every procurement team in the Group has somewhere in its portfolio."],[63.39,66.99,"Let us start with the facts, which are fictional."],[66.99,72.29,"The annual spend is 11.2 million euros, for core insurance software licences."],[72.29,77.88,"The last renewal brought a nine percent uplift, under an auto-renewal clause."],[77.88,83.7,"The current term runs to June 2027, and notice must be given nine months before."],[83.98,91.04,"Usage data suggests that an estimated 20 to 25 percent of named-user licences are unused."],[91.04,97.33,"And there are two credible alternative platforms, but migration would take 18 to 24 months."],[97.84,101.45,"Read these facts as a negotiator, one by one."],[101.45,108.91,"The spend of 11.2 million euros makes this the largest licence line in Nordvia's IT budget."],[108.91,113.18,"Every percent is worth about 112 thousand euros a year."],[113.46,123.95,"The nine percent uplift last time tells you what happens when nobody prepares: an auto-renewal clause did the negotiating, and Kelvar won by default."],[123.95,126.78,"The notice period tells you that time is a resource."],[126.78,133.77,"Nine months before June 2027 is the last moment Nordvia can stop the contract from rolling over."],[134.05,136.86,"The unused licences are your strongest fact."],[136.86,142.73,"Nordvia is paying for something it does not use, and that is not a matter of opinion."],[142.73,149.62,"And the alternatives tell you two things at once: there is a credible future without Kelvar, but not a quick one."],[150.12,155.71,"Now the most important line, which is in the speaker notes rather than on the table."],[155.71,159.18,"A protective notice was served before the deadline."],[159.18,162.82,"That means the auto-renewal no longer applies."],[162.82,166.68,"Without a new agreement, the contract simply ends in June 2027."],[166.68,169.66,"That single action changed the power balance."],[169.94,174.79,"Before the notice, Kelvar could wait: doing nothing meant renewal at its price."],[174.79,180.33,"After the notice, doing nothing means Kelvar loses an 11 million euro customer."],[180.33,182.72,"Kelvar now needs an agreement too."],[183.22,185.91,"A protective notice is not a threat to leave."],[185.91,188.54,"It is a formal step that keeps your options open."],[188.54,198.5,"You write to the vendor, within the notice period, that you do not wish the contract to renew automatically, and that you look forward to negotiating new terms."],[198.5,201.35,"Legal should check the wording against the contract."],[201.63,209.72,"The lesson for your own portfolio is simple: every auto-renewal clause needs a date in the pipeline well before the notice deadline."],[210.67,215.17,"Now the power position, using the four lenses from module 4.1."],[215.67,219.32,"Kraljic: strategic, with high supply risk for this cycle."],[219.32,225.07,"The software runs core insurance processes, the spend is high, and switching is slow."],[225.07,229.44,"Preferencing: Kelvar likely sees Nordvia as exploitable."],[229.44,234.51,"Nordvia pays a lot, costs little to serve, and has bought nothing new in years."],[234.79,240.12,"From Kelvar's side, Nordvia is a reliable source of margin, not a growth account."],[240.12,243.31,"The Chessboard: change the nature of demand."],[243.31,248.7,"That means fewer users, a re-specified scope, and alternatives prepared for the future."],[248.7,253.77,"Price haggling alone will not move a vendor that knows you cannot leave quickly."],[254.27,257.5,"And the BATNA: weak now, real in two years."],[257.5,261.25,"Let us be honest about it, because honesty is the whole point."],[261.25,263.97,"No migration is possible by June 2027."],[263.97,268.75,"If Nordvia walked away from Kelvar tomorrow, it would have no core system."],[268.75,272.2,"So the credible threat is not this cycle."],[272.2,273.36,"It is the next one."],[273.64,286.81,"What makes the BATNA real is work: the CIO has approved an RFI to both alternative platforms, and a plan for a bridge of 12 to 24 months on reduced users, followed by migration."],[287.31,292.49,"So what is Nordvia's alternative to a negotiated agreement, stated precisely?"],[292.49,300.07,"A short bridge contract with Kelvar on a reduced user base, at roughly today's price, while preparing a migration."],[300.07,304.29,"That alternative is not pleasant, but it is real and approved."],[304.57,310.33,"It sets the walk-away: Nordvia will not sign a long contract on worse terms than its bridge."],[310.33,311.92,"And Kelvar's BATNA?"],[311.92,318.23,"If Nordvia signs only a bridge and migrates, Kelvar loses the customer in two or three years."],[318.23,322.9,"If there is no deal before Kelvar's year end, it also misses its forecast."],[323.18,329.92,"Kelvar's alternative is worse than it looks from its side of the table, as long as Nordvia's preparation is visible."],[330.41,332.6,"This is where many buyers go wrong."],[332.6,340.26,"They either bluff, saying they will move platforms by next summer, which the account manager knows is impossible."],[340.26,343.6,"Or they give up, assuming a sole source means no power."],[343.6,344.51,"Both are mistakes."],[344.79,354.48,"The honest position is stronger than both: we cannot leave this year, we have started preparing to leave next time, and the terms we sign now will decide whether we do."],[354.98,357.02,"One more point on preferencing."],[357.02,359.67,"Remember that attractiveness can be managed."],[359.67,372.11,"Nordvia can become more interesting to Kelvar without paying more: a clear three-year roadmap, a willingness to look at new products, a reference if the service is good, and reliable, on-time payment."],[372.11,373.82,"None of these costs much."],[374.1,380.01,"All of them move Nordvia, in Kelvar's eyes, away from exploitable and towards core."],[380.01,383.65,"And a core customer gets better terms and more flexibility."],[384.61,388.0,"With the power position clear, here are the levers for the buyers."],[388.0,389.03,"Six of them."],[389.52,393.6,"Rightsizing: remove unused named users before pricing anything."],[393.6,397.89,"This is the single biggest lever, and the order matters."],[397.89,404.51,"If you negotiate a discount first and then remove users, the vendor will try to reprice the smaller volume."],[404.51,407.48,"Agree the volume first, then the price per unit."],[407.98,411.78,"Let us do the arithmetic, with illustrative numbers."],[411.78,423.96,"If the price were purely per named user, removing 20 percent of users would take 11.2 million euros down to about 9 million, and removing 25 percent would take it to 8.4 million."],[423.96,427.91,"In reality, licence prices are not perfectly linear."],[428.19,432.46,"There are platform fees, support charges and minimum volumes."],[432.46,439.9,"That is why the buyer brief sets an illustrative target of about 10 million euros a year after rightsizing, not 9."],[440.4,445.68,"Protect the future: an uplift cap, a price hold and the right to reduce volumes."],[445.68,449.26,"This is how you make sure the nine percent never happens again."],[449.26,453.45,"Term: trade the length of the term for price and flexibility."],[453.45,455.59,"Kelvar wants five years."],[455.59,459.51,"Nordvia wants three, because the alternatives will be ready by then."],[459.79,472.01,"Regulation: the software supports critical functions, so the clauses required by DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act, Article 30, paragraph 3, are not negotiable."],[472.01,475.49,"What you negotiate is how they are met, not whether."],[475.49,479.76,"Group volume: check whether other Group entities license Kelvar."],[480.04,489.32,"If Entity C or Entity F, for example, also run it, the conversation changes from one mid-sized customer to a Group relationship."],[489.32,493.95,"And one voice: IT must not pre-commit in parallel conversations."],[494.45,499.72,"Let me say more about two of these levers, because they are often misunderstood."],[499.72,500.65,"First, term."],[500.65,506.64,"Many buyers think a longer term is always a gift to the vendor, to be given only for a big discount."],[506.64,510.07,"But here the term has a strategic value for Nordvia too."],[510.07,513.93,"Three years ends just as the alternatives could be ready."],[514.21,520.96,"Five years would lock Nordvia in past the point where it could leave, and destroy the next BATNA before it exists."],[520.96,524.44,"So the term is not just a price lever."],[524.44,526.71,"It is how Nordvia protects its future power."],[526.71,527.95,"Second, one voice."],[528.23,537.2,"Imagine the head of claims IT meets Kelvar's product team at a user conference and says: of course we are renewing, we could never move."],[537.2,541.05,"That sentence, said in good faith, is worth millions to Kelvar."],[541.33,550.16,"So before the negotiation, the CIO, the business owner and procurement agree a single message, and every contact with Kelvar is logged."],[550.66,554.07,"These levers come from the sole-source playbook in Session 2."],[554.07,557.76,"There is a seventh lever if time allows: unbundling."],[557.76,562.24,"Separate the licence, the support and the services, and look at each one."],[562.52,571.89,"Support is often charged as a percentage of licence value, so rightsizing the licences should reduce support too, but only if the contract says so."],[572.84,575.31,"Now the roles in each breakout room."],[575.31,578.98,"The lead buyer runs the negotiation from the one-pager."],[578.98,581.61,"The analyst handles numbers, notes and signals."],[581.61,588.22,"The Kelvar account manager has a private brief from the teacher, which the buyers do not see."],[588.5,594.24,"And the observer records the first offer, every concession, and every interest that is discovered."],[594.74,602.02,"If you are studying alone, take the buyer side and imagine the account manager sitting across from you."],[602.02,607.3,"Before we go further, here is the buyer brief in full, because your one-pager depends on it."],[607.3,612.6,"Kelvar opened at plus nine percent, about 12.2 million euros a year, for five years."],[612.88,626.47,"The illustrative targets are about 10 million euros a year after removing unused users, an uplift cap of three percent, three years with the right to reduce volume by 10 percent a year, and transition assistance."],[626.75,634.32,"The illustrative walk-away is 11.2 million euros flat for three years, with the reduction rights and the DORA clauses."],[634.32,639.98,"In other words: no increase at all, a shorter term, flexibility and compliance."],[639.98,642.92,"Anything worse, and Nordvia prefers its bridge."],[643.42,647.06,"Let me put the four numbers for price side by side."],[647.06,655.31,"Opening: Nordvia could open close to 9.5 million euros, below its target, defensible with the usage data."],[655.31,657.16,"Target: about 10 million."],[657.16,659.42,"Walk-away: 11.2 million, flat."],[659.42,661.62,"Kelvar's opening: 12.2 million."],[661.62,665.95,"The gap between the two openings is 2.7 million euros a year."],[666.23,669.6,"Over three years that is more than 8 million euros."],[669.6,672.03,"That is what this conversation is worth."],[672.53,676.8,"One more preparation step before the room: agree the concession list."],[676.8,681.18,"Here is a sensible one for Nordvia, written as conditional moves."],[681.18,688.08,"If Kelvar accepts three years with reduction rights, then Nordvia can sign quickly, at a date that suits Kelvar."],[688.36,696.0,"If Kelvar caps any uplift at three percent, then Nordvia can consider a reference, provided the service meets its levels."],[696.0,704.79,"If Kelvar accepts pooled audits and certifications that satisfy DORA, then Nordvia can agree a sensible schedule for on-site visits."],[705.07,710.75,"Notice that each move gives something cheap for Nordvia and asks for something Nordvia values."],[710.75,717.63,"And notice what is not on the list: the walk-away price, the DORA clauses themselves, and a five-year term."],[718.58,720.71,"Here is the hint for the role-play."],[720.71,722.9,"Kelvar's opening is firm."],[722.9,725.2,"Its real interests are not in the opening."],[725.2,730.95,"Your job is to find out what the seller needs, not to argue about the nine percent."],[731.46,733.41,"Think back to Fisher and Ury."],[733.41,734.85,"The nine percent is a position."],[734.85,739.2,"Behind it there are interests, and they are not on the table yet."],[739.2,743.08,"They only come out with good questions about the seller's goals."],[743.08,745.92,"Not questions about price, but questions like these."],[745.92,749.25,"What does a successful year look like for your team?"],[749.53,752.49,"How does this renewal fit your plans for our account?"],[752.49,755.36,"Where is your product going in the next three years?"],[755.36,758.4,"What would make this deal easy to approve on your side?"],[758.4,760.65,"Is there any timing that matters to you?"],[761.14,769.96,"Each of those questions invites the account manager to talk about their world, their targets, their product strategy and their internal approvals."],[769.96,773.92,"A seller who feels heard often reveals more than they intended."],[774.2,782.68,"And notice what you do not do: you do not attack the nine percent, you do not argue about whether it is fair, and you do not reveal your walk-away."],[782.68,787.43,"You listen, you summarise, and you let the analyst write down every hint."],[787.93,790.48,"Also prepare your own opening statement."],[790.48,792.45,"A good one is calm and factual."],[792.45,797.68,"For example: we value the platform and want to find an agreement before the contract ends."],[797.68,806.28,"Our usage review shows that a significant share of named users are not active, so we want to base the new agreement on actual use."],[806.56,813.58,"We are looking for a shorter term with predictable pricing, and we need the DORA requirements for a critical service built in."],[813.58,816.82,"Then stop talking, and ask your first question."],[817.77,822.27,"Now the role-play itself, which takes 35 minutes in the live session."],[822.27,827.09,"Ten minutes for the buyers to prepare the one-pager while the seller reads the brief."],[827.09,829.07,"Twenty minutes to negotiate."],[829.07,831.37,"Five minutes of observer feedback."],[831.37,837.2,"And then record the outcome per issue, against target and walk-away, in Case 4 of your workbook."],[837.7,840.46,"A few practical tips for those twenty minutes."],[840.46,843.73,"Use the first five to explore, not to bargain."],[843.73,848.4,"Put rightsizing on the table early, as a fact, not as a concession."],[848.4,855.73,"Use packages: if you give us three years with a three percent cap and reduction rights, then we can discuss what matters to you."],[856.01,858.24,"Keep the analyst writing every number."],[858.24,862.37,"And if the room does not close, record where you stopped and why."],[862.37,867.29,"A clean no deal, with a clear record, is better than a deal beyond your walk-away."],[867.79,876.39,"Your workbook has a negotiation record with five issues: annual price, uplift cap, term, volume flexibility, and exit and DORA clauses."],[876.39,885.16,"For each, write what was agreed and whether it was better than target, at target, between target and walk-away, or beyond walk-away."],[885.44,890.16,"Then write down the seller interests you found, and the question that revealed each one."],[890.16,895.05,"That last field is the most valuable learning in the exercise."],[895.05,903.08,"If you are working alone, pause the video now, open your workbook, prepare the buyer one-pager and imagine the negotiation."],[903.08,903.78,"Then continue."],[904.29,907.12,"And a word for the observer, if you play that role."],[907.12,909.55,"Your notes are the mirror for the room."],[909.55,912.75,"Write down who made the first offer and what it was."],[912.75,916.8,"Note every concession, who made it, and whether it was conditional."],[916.8,920.84,"Mark the moment any interest appeared, and the question that triggered it."],[921.12,929.34,"In the feedback, start with facts, not judgements: at minute twelve, the buyer moved from three to four years without asking for anything."],[929.34,931.71,"Facts like that teach more than any lecture."],[932.65,934.06,"A reflection question."],[934.06,938.08,"Where did the Kelvar annual price land in your room?"],[938.08,940.71,"Below 10.3 million euros."],[940.71,942.83,"Between 10.3 and 10.8 million."],[942.83,945.17,"Between 10.8 and 11.2 million."],[945.17,947.55,"Or above 11.2 million, or no deal."],[947.55,953.22,"If you prepared alone, ask yourself where your plan would have landed."],[953.5,957.89,"Hold that number in your mind, because the next slides reveal the seller's limits."],[958.84,960.91,"Now the debrief, and the reveal."],[960.91,961.48,"Five questions."],[961.48,965.54,"Who discovered the seller's year-end deadline, and with which question?"],[965.54,969.01,"Who learned about the SaaS strategy, and what did it let you trade?"],[969.01,973.55,"Did anyone concede on the DORA clauses, and what should have happened?"],[973.55,976.99,"Where did the price land against your target and walk-away?"],[977.27,980.26,"And what would you do differently 18 months before renewal?"],[980.76,982.86,"Here is the seller's private brief."],[982.86,987.02,"Kelvar's fiscal year ends in May, and this deal is in its forecast."],[987.02,992.37,"Signing before year end matters more to the account manager than the last euro."],[992.37,999.73,"Kelvar also wants customers to move to its SaaS edition, and an insurance reference in this region would help its sales."],[1000.01,1005.15,"Nordvia costs little to serve and has bought nothing new in years."],[1005.15,1018.52,"The seller's illustrative limits: down to about 10.3 million euros a year for three years, in exchange for a joint SaaS evaluation in 2028 and a reference, with an uplift cap of three percent."],[1018.8,1027.49,"The seller dislikes unlimited on-site audits, but accepts the DORA clauses if they can be met through certifications and pooled audits."],[1027.99,1030.17,"Now you can see the ZOPA on price."],[1030.17,1033.87,"Nordvia's walk-away is 11.2 million euros."],[1033.87,1044.11,"Kelvar's floor is about 10.3 million, but only if Kelvar gets its interests met: the year-end signature, the SaaS evaluation and the reference."],[1044.11,1049.88,"So the zone of agreement runs from about 10.3 to 11.2 million euros."],[1050.16,1054.55,"Nordvia's target of 10 million was just below the seller's floor."],[1054.55,1055.49,"That is fine."],[1055.49,1057.19,"A target should be ambitious."],[1057.19,1062.04,"But it explains why the best rooms landed near 10.3, not below it."],[1062.54,1066.28,"Notice also that the seller's floor depends on the interests."],[1066.28,1073.92,"A room that never asked about goals saw only the nine percent and a seller defending it."],[1073.92,1078.65,"That room usually landed between 10.8 and 11.2 million, or failed to close."],[1078.93,1088.49,"A room that discovered the year end and the SaaS strategy could offer something cheap for Nordvia and valuable to Kelvar, and in return reach the seller's floor."],[1088.49,1093.43,"Rooms that found the interests usually landed near the seller's floor."],[1093.43,1098.75,"That is the integrative logic of Lax and Sebenius at work: create value, then claim it."],[1099.24,1100.67,"And the DORA question."],[1100.67,1105.86,"Some buyers, under pressure, offered to drop the audit clause in exchange for price."],[1105.86,1107.08,"That is a mistake."],[1107.08,1118.57,"For a service supporting a critical function, the Article 30, paragraph 3 clauses, including audit and access rights and exit with an adequate transition period, are required."],[1118.85,1121.49,"You cannot trade away a legal requirement."],[1121.49,1131.82,"What you can negotiate is how it is met, and that is exactly the seller's real concern: not the principle of audit, but the cost of unlimited on-site visits."],[1132.32,1137.22,"And the last debrief question: what would you do differently 18 months before renewal?"],[1137.22,1139.55,"This is where the real lesson lies."],[1139.83,1153.02,"With 18 months, Nordvia could have run the usage review calmly, sent the RFI to both alternatives, checked Group volume across the entities, and aligned the CIO and the business owner on one message."],[1153.02,1159.54,"It could even have started a proof of concept with one alternative, which would turn a weak BATNA into a medium one."],[1159.82,1164.9,"Most of the value in a sole-source renewal is created long before the first meeting."],[1164.9,1167.8,"The negotiation in the room only collects it."],[1168.76,1170.92,"Here is what a good outcome looked like."],[1170.92,1173.18,"It is illustrative, after the reveal."],[1173.67,1177.33,"First, unused named users removed before pricing."],[1177.33,1183.86,"Second, about 10.3 million euros a year for three years, with any uplift capped at three percent."],[1183.86,1187.51,"Third, the right to reduce volume each year."],[1187.51,1192.87,"Fourth, the DORA clauses met through certifications and pooled audits, not dropped."],[1193.15,1201.75,"And fifth, in exchange: a joint SaaS evaluation in 2028 and a reference, signed before Kelvar's year end."],[1202.25,1206.15,"Let us value it, again with illustrative numbers."],[1206.15,1213.37,"Kelvar's opening, 12.2 million euros a year over three years, totals about 36.6 million."],[1213.37,1218.06,"The outcome, 10.3 million a year, totals about 30.9 million."],[1218.06,1222.05,"That is roughly 5.7 million euros less over the term."],[1222.33,1229.01,"Compared with simply staying at today's 11.2 million, the saving is about 2.7 million over three years."],[1229.01,1236.02,"And compared with another nine percent uplift at the next renewal, the cap protects much more than that."],[1236.52,1239.21,"But the value is not only in the price."],[1239.21,1246.16,"The three-year term ends just as the alternatives could be ready, so Nordvia keeps its next BATNA alive."],[1246.16,1250.93,"The reduction right means Nordvia pays for actual use as it changes."],[1250.93,1256.49,"The pooled audits and certifications satisfy the regulator at a cost Kelvar accepts."],[1256.77,1269.17,"And look at what Nordvia gave: a joint evaluation of the SaaS edition, which Nordvia would want to look at anyway, a reference, if the service is good, and a signature date that suited Kelvar."],[1269.17,1273.19,"Each of those cost Nordvia little and mattered a lot to Kelvar."],[1273.47,1278.25,"Concessions that are cheap for us and valuable to them, given only in exchange."],[1278.76,1282.07,"Notice one detail in the SaaS evaluation."],[1282.07,1285.31,"It is an evaluation, not a commitment to migrate."],[1285.31,1291.76,"Nordvia keeps the option to compare the SaaS edition with the two alternative platforms in 2028."],[1291.76,1298.39,"If you had promised to move to SaaS, you would have handed Kelvar the next negotiation."],[1298.39,1301.03,"Both sides got what mattered most to them."],[1301.31,1304.81,"That is integrative negotiation in a sole-source setting."],[1305.76,1307.95,"Here is Case 4 in three lines."],[1307.95,1310.49,"Serve the protective notice: timing is power."],[1310.49,1312.23,"An honest BATNA beats a bluff."],[1312.23,1317.6,"And ask about their goals; trade what is cheap for you and valuable to them."],[1318.1,1320.74,"Which of the three applies to your own project?"],[1320.74,1330.94,"If you have an incumbent contract with an auto-renewal clause, the first line is your action this week: find the notice date and put it in the pipeline."],[1330.94,1333.99,"Ideally, start preparing 12 to 18 months before renewal."],[1334.27,1342.29,"That is a practitioner rule of thumb, not a law, but it gives you time for a usage review, an RFI and executive alignment."],[1342.29,1347.41,"If your alternative is weak, the second line asks you to build it honestly."],[1347.41,1353.92,"And for every negotiation, the third line is the habit that separates good negotiators from loud ones."],[1354.42,1356.57,"One last reflection on the poll."],[1356.57,1362.07,"If your room landed between 10.8 and 11.2 million euros, do not see it as a failure."],[1362.07,1367.43,"It was still inside the walk-away, and you protected the term and the clauses."],[1367.43,1373.88,"The lesson is simply that better questions about the seller's goals would have opened more value."],[1374.16,1385.77,"If your room landed below 10.3 million, check whether the seller gave up something that was not in the brief, or whether Nordvia quietly gave away a DORA clause or a long term to get there."],[1385.77,1389.92,"A low price bought with a bad contract is not a good outcome."],[1389.92,1392.87,"And if your room did not close, look at the record."],[1393.15,1397.87,"Did you run out of time because you bargained on price before exploring interests?"],[1397.87,1399.91,"That is the most common reason."],[1400.41,1424.35,"To recap the lesson: we read the Kelvar facts as a negotiator, saw how a protective notice shifted the power, positioned the case with Kraljic, preferencing and the Chessboard, built an honest BATNA, chose the levers, planned the four numbers on price, explored the seller's interests, and saw how a package of conditional concessions reached the seller's floor while keeping the DORA clauses intact."],[1424.63,1434.2,"In the next lesson we move from the deal to the paper: the award recommendation and the structure of a contract that protects the value you have just negotiated."]]},{"id":"s4-l4","session":"s4","title":"Award and contract","summary":"Write an award recommendation approvers can sign without reopening the file, and structure a contract whose price mechanism, KPIs, term and exit protect what the negotiation won.","from":59,"to":73,"audio":"audio/s4-l4.mp3","duration":1549.8,"segs":[[59,0.6,38.02,"Welcome back. This lesson covers module 4.3 of Session 4: award and contract. In the previous lessons of this session we prepared and ran the negotiation. Now we turn the result into a decision that approvers can sign, and into a contract that protects what we won. The subtitle on this slide says it plainly. Leverage is highest before signature. Once the contract is signed, every change becomes a request, and the supplier no longer has to compete for your business. So whatever you want the supplier to do over the next three, four or five years has to be written down now."],[59,38.52,77.91,"Here is the plan. First, the path from evaluation to signature, step by step. Second, the award recommendation: what goes into it, and why approvers should never need to reopen the file. Third, we return to the Nordvia headquarters cleaning tender and look at the scores, which turn out to be a tie. Then debriefing unsuccessful bidders. After that, the contract itself: its architecture, its schedules, price mechanisms, a worked example on indexation, and service levels with service credits. We close with Coupa contract lifecycle management, an exercise on the cleaning award, and the debrief."],[60,78.86,96.18,"Let's start with the path from evaluation to signature. There are six steps on the slide. Evaluation. Negotiation or best and final offer, which we call BAFO. Award recommendation. Approval. Courtesy period and debriefs. And finally, signature."],[60,96.68,140.72,"Why spell this out? Because each step has an owner, and each step is a place where value can leak. Evaluation belongs to the evaluation panel, scoring individually first and then agreeing in a consensus meeting. Negotiation or BAFO belongs to the sourcing lead, inside the mandate agreed with the business owner. The award recommendation is written by Procurement, but the business owner co-signs it, because the business owner has to live with the supplier. Approval follows the delegation of authority: the higher the contract value, the more senior the approvers. The courtesy period and the debriefs belong to Procurement again. And signature belongs to whoever holds signing authority in the entity."],[60,141.22,184.76,"Now look at the line at the bottom of the slide. What is not in the contract will not happen. That sounds obvious, yet most of us have seen it go wrong. The winning bid promised a dedicated account manager, a quarterly innovation workshop and a free transition. The contract mentioned none of it. Eighteen months later, the account manager covers six clients, the workshops never happened, and the transition was invoiced as extra work. Nobody lied. The promise simply never travelled from the bid into the contract. So here is a good habit. Before signature, go through the winning bid line by line, and for every promise that influenced the score, ask one question: where is this in the contract?"],[60,185.26,205.17,"In Coupa, the approvals run through the contract lifecycle management workflow, CLM for short, with an audit trail. That matters in a regulated insurer. When internal audit asks who approved this contract, on what basis and when, the answer should be one click away, not buried in somebody's mailbox."],[61,206.12,226.75,"Now the award recommendation. This is the single document that carries the decision from Procurement to the approvers. The test is simple. Approvers should be able to sign without reopening the file. If a CFO has to ask for the evaluation spreadsheet to understand why we chose this supplier, the recommendation has failed."],[61,227.25,297.87,"Look at the six sections in the table. First, scope and value: what we are buying, for whom, for how long, and the total value over the full term, including options. Not the annual value. The total. Second, process: the route to market, which bidders were invited and why, and the timeline. This shows that the competition was fair. Third, evaluation: the scores, their sensitivity to the weights, and how any abnormally low bids were treated. Fourth, commercials: the outcome against the target and against the baseline, and the total cost of ownership. Ellram's point about TCO applies here. The cheapest price is not the lowest cost if transition, contract management and exit are expensive. Fifth, risk and regulation: the due diligence results, financial, information security and regulatory, the classification of the contract, for example whether it is an ICT service under DORA, and the clauses that classification requires. Sixth, the decision: the recommendation itself, the conditions attached to it, and the approvals required under the delegation of authority."],[61,298.37,338.46,"Let me stress sensitivity, because it is the part most often left out. Suppose your weights were 50 percent quality and 50 percent price, and your preferred bidder wins by one point. What happens at 60 to 40? Or at 40 to 60? If the recommendation flips when the weights move by ten points, the approvers need to know. It does not make the decision wrong. The weights were agreed before issue, and that is what protects the fairness of the event. But it tells the approvers that the bids are close, and that the conditions in the contract matter a great deal. A recommendation that flips at 60 to 40 needs to say so, in writing, on the first page."],[61,338.96,357.29,"One more point, on conditions. A recommendation can say: award to this bidder, on condition that the KPIs, the indexation clause and the exit schedule are agreed as drafted. That keeps your leverage in the final contract talks, because the approval itself depends on those terms."],[62,358.24,383.22,"Let's make this concrete with the Nordvia headquarters cleaning tender, project P1 in the pipeline. These bids are fictional. Bidder C has already been excluded. Its staffing hours failed the wage-floor check, a classic abnormally low bid in a labour-intensive service. If the hours cannot cover the cleaning, either the service fails or the staff are underpaid, and neither is acceptable."],[62,383.72,399.44,"Three bidders remain. Look at the columns: price in thousands of euros, a quality score, and the total at 50 to 50. The price score uses the formula in the footnote: the lowest compliant price divided by the bid price, times 100."],[62,399.94,457.48,"Bidder A is the incumbent, at 690 thousand euros, with a quality score of 62. Its price score is 610 divided by 690, times 100, which is about 88.4. Half of 62 plus half of 88.4 gives 75.2. The incumbent is clearly third. Bidder B is the cheapest, at 610 thousand euros, so it gets the full 100 points for price. With quality at 74, its total is half of 74 plus half of 100, which is 87.0. Bidder D bids 655 thousand euros, with the best quality, 81. Its price score is 610 divided by 655, times 100, about 93.1. Half of 81 plus half of 93.1 gives 87.1. But D wants a four-year contract, which is not the term we asked for."],[62,457.98,514.71,"So B scores 87.0 and D scores 87.1. A difference of a tenth of a point. Is that a winner? No. That is a statistical tie. Scoring has a margin of error. One evaluator marking one criterion one point differently could flip it. So what do you do? This is the moment for a BAFO round, not for an auction. Why not an auction? Because this is a quality-sensitive labour service. The two bidders differ on quality and on terms, not only on price, and a price-only auction would push both of them toward the very wage floor we just used to exclude Bidder C. A BAFO lets both bidders improve their price and terms once, in writing, under the same rules. And it lets you answer D's four-year request directly. The term is ours to set. We can say what we would accept, for example three years plus two optional one-year extensions tied to performance."],[63,515.66,546.57,"After the decision come the debriefs. There are four cards on the slide. Always: debrief in writing, before or soon after signature. Share: their scores, and their strengths and weaknesses against each criterion. Never share: another bidder's prices or confidential solution. And the courtesy period: a short pause before signing. A mandatory standstill period exists only in public procurement. In a private insurer, the courtesy period is good practice, not law."],[63,547.07,586.25,"Why bother? Three reasons. First, fairness. Bidders invested real time and money in their proposals, and a clear explanation respects that. Second, market health. Good suppliers who lose without understanding why often stop bidding, and in a narrow market that weakens your next tender. Third, risk. A short courtesy period gives a losing bidder the chance to point out a genuine error in the evaluation before the contract is signed, while it is still cheap to fix. In the public sector, the Crown Commercial Service requires written feedback in further competitions. We are not bound by that, but it is a good benchmark."],[63,586.75,617.98,"What does a good debrief sound like? For the Nordvia incumbent, something like this. Your quality score was 62, against a best score of 81. The main gaps were your staffing method and your KPI reporting proposal. Your price was competitive but did not close the quality gap. What it never says is: Bidder B was 80 thousand euros cheaper. That is someone else's confidential information. Give feedback on their own bid against the criteria, never a comparison with a competitor's price."],[64,618.93,630.13,"Now to the contract itself, starting with its architecture. Before you draft a single clause, decide what shape the contract should have. The table shows four typical situations."],[64,630.63,689.16,"For panel or project work, for example consulting, IT services or marketing agencies, the architecture is a master agreement plus statements of work, or call-offs. The master agreement fixes the legal terms, the rate card and the governance once. Each project then gets a short statement of work with scope, deliverables, price and timeline. That way you do not renegotiate liability every time someone needs two weeks of work. For cleaning or security, it is a single-supplier term contract with options: one supplier, one scope, a base term long enough to recover mobilisation, and extension options the buyer can exercise if performance is good. For tail and catalogue items, it is a catalogue contract with a price list. Office supplies, promotional items, small facilities consumables. Here the value lies in easy buying and fixed prices, not in heavy governance."],[64,689.66,724.35,"For software, it is a licence or subscription, with support and services as separate schedules. The note explains why. When licence, support and services are bundled into one price, you cannot see what each costs, and at renewal you cannot negotiate them separately. Keeping them separate keeps the unbundling lever alive for the next negotiation. Think of the Kelvar case from earlier in this session. The strongest arguments were about unused licences and the uplift cap. You can only make those arguments if the licence line is visible."],[65,725.3,735.92,"Inside a services contract, most of the value sits in the schedules, not in the general terms. Let's go through the six on the slide, and what you must get right in each."],[65,736.42,789.65,"Scope and specification: output-based or hybrid, and a clear statement of what is in and what is out. Disputes in services contracts are very often scope disputes. Is window cleaning above the second floor included? Is the deep clean after an office move included? Write it down. Service levels: a few measurable KPIs, with the method and the reporting. We come back to these shortly. Pricing: the price mechanism, rates, indexation and invoicing. If the invoicing rules are vague, invoice checking becomes a monthly argument. Term and termination: the base term, the options, termination for cause, termination for convenience, and change of control. Change of control matters more than people think. If your cleaning supplier is bought by a company you excluded on quality, do you still want the contract?"],[65,790.15,825.21,"Exit and transition: the supplier's assistance at the end, the return of data and assets, and staff data. In cleaning and security, staff may transfer to the next supplier, and the next supplier needs accurate staff data to price fairly. If the exit schedule does not oblige the outgoing supplier to provide it, your next tender starts blind. And protection: the data processing agreement under the GDPR, the General Data Protection Regulation, plus information security, liability, insurance, audit rights and subcontracting."],[65,825.71,854.87,"Now the note on this slide, which is one of the key points of the module. If the draft contract was attached to the RFP, bidders have already marked their deviations. The negotiation happened through the bids. That is far better than negotiating the contract after award, when the winner knows it has won and your leverage is gone. So attach the draft contract, with these schedules, to every RFP you can, and evaluate the deviations as part of the bid."],[66,855.82,893.56,"Let's look at price mechanisms. There are five in the table, each with what it fits and what to watch. A fixed periodic price fits a stable scope, such as cleaning or a managed service. Watch two things: a clear scope, and change control. A fixed price on a vague scope is an invitation to invoice extras. Unit rates fit variations and extras, for example an hourly rate for additional guards at an event, or a price per square metre for deep cleaning. Watch the volume forecasts. Bidders price unit rates against the volumes you gave them. If those volumes were wrong, the rates may not hold."],[66,894.06,943.86,"Capped time and materials fits an unclear scope, for example a discovery phase in an IT project, when nobody yet knows exactly what needs doing. Watch the cap and the reporting. Without a cap, time and materials rewards the supplier for taking longer. Indexation fits multi-year labour services. Watch four parameters: which index, what share of the price it applies to, how often, and with what cap. The next slide is a worked example. Gain-share fits productivity and energy. For example, the supplier proposes changes that cut energy use in a building, and both sides share the savings. Watch the baseline. It must be agreed upfront, before anything changes. Otherwise every gain-share discussion becomes an argument about what would have happened anyway."],[66,944.36,960.74,"The note brings back Ellram's total cost of ownership view. Transition, contract management and exit costs belong in the comparison, not only the price. A mechanism that looks cheap but needs someone checking complex invoices every month is not cheap."],[67,961.69,994.32,"Here is a worked example on indexation, using Nordvia security, project P6 in the pipeline. It is fictional. The situation today: hourly rates indexed twice in two years, and no KPIs. So the price went up twice, and nobody can say whether the service got better or worse. How common is that? Very. Indexation is often where labour-service contracts lose their savings. You run a good tender, you win a good rate, and within three years the indexation clause has quietly given most of it back."],[67,994.82,1045.85,"Look at the five lines of indexation done right. Once a year, on a fixed date. Not whenever the supplier asks. On the labour share of the price only. In security, labour is most of the cost, but not all of it. Equipment, management overhead and margin do not rise with a wage agreement. Linked to a named, published wage index. Not to the supplier's own cost statement, and not to a vague phrase like market conditions, but a named index both sides can look up. With a cap, and only if the KPIs were met. The cap protects the budget in an unusual year, and the KPI condition links price to performance. And recorded as a contract amendment in Coupa CLM, so that the new rates are the contracted rates, invoices are checked against them, and the history is visible at the next renewal."],[67,1046.35,1080.12,"Let me give you a simple illustration, with illustrative numbers. Suppose the annual price is one million euros, labour is 80 percent of it, and the named wage index rises 4 percent. Applied to the whole price, the increase would be 40 thousand euros. Applied only to the labour share, it is 32 thousand euros. With a cap of 3 percent on the labour share, it is 24 thousand euros. And if the KPIs were missed, it is zero. Same index, very different outcomes. That is exactly what your clause should do."],[68,1081.07,1113.48,"Next, service levels, KPIs and service credits. Four cards. Few: five to eight KPIs that matter to the business. Not thirty. When there are thirty KPIs, nobody reads the report, and the supplier learns that none of them matter. Measurable: the source of the data, the method and the frequency, agreed in the contract. A KPI like user satisfaction means nothing until you say: measured by a quarterly survey of named users, on a five-point scale, with a target average of four."],[68,1113.98,1151.87,"At risk: service credits as a share of the monthly fee, with a cap. If a KPI is missed, the supplier credits a defined share of the fee. The cap keeps the commercial risk proportionate, which keeps the price reasonable. Fair: earn-back for sustained recovery, and termination for persistent failure. Earn-back means that if the supplier misses a KPI one month but then performs above target for the next few months, it can recover the credit. That rewards fixing the problem. And if the failure persists, you need a clear right to terminate, because credits alone never fix a broken service."],[68,1152.37,1194.64,"The note makes an important point. The exact percentages are commercial choices, not norms. No standard says credits must be a particular share of the fee. And service credits are a lever for behaviour, not a revenue line. If someone starts budgeting service credit income, something is wrong with the supplier or with the contract. Think of Nordvia security again. No KPIs today. A good start would be five: shift coverage, response time to alarms, incident reports within an agreed time, staff turnover on site, and a quarterly user satisfaction score. Each with a source and a method. And the yearly indexation applies only if they are met."],[69,1195.59,1227.76,"Time for a short reflection. The poll asks: which contract area is weakest in your entity today? The options are KPIs and service credits, indexation, exit and transition, regulatory clauses, and renewal management. Take a moment to pick your own answer, and be honest. If you are not sure, think of the last three services contracts you saw. Which of these areas would you least like an auditor to look at? Keep that answer in mind. Several of these weaknesses come back in the next two modules."],[70,1228.71,1276.82,"Now, where does all this live? In Coupa contract lifecycle management. Four cards. Templates: start from the Group template, not the supplier's paper. When you start from the supplier's paper, you spend your time removing what favours them. When you start from yours, they spend their time asking for changes, and you decide. Clause library: approved clauses available inside Microsoft Word, so that a sourcing lead in any entity can insert the approved indexation or data processing clause without redrafting it. Workflow: review, negotiation and approval with an audit trail. And alerts: milestones, options, notice periods and expiry. Those alerts are what prevents the silent roll-over we discuss in module 4.5."],[70,1277.32,1290.51,"All four features are in Coupa's public product description. Which templates actually exist for each category in your entity is a different question. Ask your Coupa administrators, and confirm it in your Coupa tenant."],[71,1291.46,1319.92,"Now it is your turn. This is Practice 4.3 in your workbook, about twelve minutes. We go back to the headquarters cleaning tender after the BAFO round. The results are illustrative. Bidder B now bids 600 thousand euros, with quality still at 74. Bidder D bids 632 thousand euros, with quality 81, and now accepts a term of three years plus one plus one, with options based on KPIs."],[71,1320.42,1351.78,"Your tasks. First, calculate both totals at 50 to 50, with the same price formula: lowest price divided by bid price, times 100. Second, test the result at 60 to 40, quality to price. Third, write your recommendation, with conditions for the contract. Fourth, draft two lines of debrief for the incumbent. The workbook has check fields that confirm your numbers. If you want to do the exercise, pause the video now, open the Modules tab, and come back when you are done."],[72,1352.73,1408.2,"Welcome back. Let's go through the numbers. After the BAFO, Bidder B has the lowest price, 600 thousand euros, so it scores 100 on price. Half of 74 plus half of 100 is 87.0. Bidder D's price score is 600 divided by 632, times 100, about 94.9. Half of 81 plus half of 94.9 is about 88.0. So at 50 to 50, D leads by one point. At 60 to 40, quality to price, B gets 0.6 times 74, which is 44.4, plus 0.4 times 100, which is 40, for 84.4. D gets 0.6 times 81, which is 48.6, plus 0.4 times 94.9, about 38.0, for 86.6. So D wins more clearly."],[72,1408.7,1436.32,"Now the slide adds one more test, and it is the point of the whole exercise. At 40 to 60, quality to price, the order flips. B scores 89.6 and D scores 89.4. So in the recommendation you write: D is recommended at the agreed weights, its lead grows when quality weighs more, and the result would reverse if price weighed 60 percent. That one sentence protects the approvers, and it protects you."],[72,1436.82,1474.57,"Why does this matter? Because it shows why weights are fixed before issue. If they could change after the bids were opened, anyone who preferred B could argue for 40 to 60, and anyone who preferred D for 60 to 40. Fixing the weights first removes that temptation. And sensitivity belongs in every recommendation, so that approvers see how robust the decision is. The conditions for the contract are the KPIs, indexation linked to a wage index, staff transfer data, and exit. And the incumbent debrief covers the quality score gap and its reasons, never the other bidders' prices."],[73,1475.52,1520.09,"Let's recap module 4.3 in three lines. First, an award recommendation can be signed without reopening the file: scope and value, process, evaluation with sensitivity, commercials against target and baseline, risk and regulation, and a clear decision with conditions. Second, debrief every bidder in writing: their own scores, strengths and weaknesses, never another bidder's prices. It keeps the market healthy and good suppliers coming back. Third, the price mechanism, the KPIs, the term and the exit protect what the negotiation won. A great price with a loose indexation clause, no KPIs and no exit plan is a great price for about eighteen months."],[73,1520.59,1548.58,"And here is the bridge to the next lesson. In an insurer, some of the contract is not ours to negotiate at all. It is set by regulation: the Solvency II outsourcing rules and DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act. Module 4.4 shows which clauses those are, how to classify a contract early, and how to build the clauses into the draft so that they never become a negotiation issue. See you there."]],"questions":[{"after":60,"q":"The winning Nordvia facilities bid promised a quarterly innovation workshop, and that promise lifted its quality score. The draft contract does not mention it. What should you do before signature?","options":["Nothing, because the bid is binding anyway","Write the workshop into the contract schedules as an obligation","Ask the supplier to confirm it by email after signature","Lower the supplier's quality score"],"answer":1,"explain":"What is not in the contract will not happen. Every promise that influenced the score should be traced from the bid into the contract before signature, while your leverage is highest.","t":205.17},{"after":61,"q":"Your preferred bidder wins at the agreed 50 to 50 weights, but loses if quality falls to 40 percent. How do you handle this in the award recommendation?","options":["Change the weights so the result is clearer","Leave the sensitivity out, because the weights were agreed","Keep the agreed weights and state the flip clearly in the recommendation","Re-tender the whole requirement"],"answer":2,"explain":"Weights fixed before issue protect fairness, so you do not change them. But approvers must see how robust the decision is, so a recommendation that flips with the weights says so in writing.","t":357.29},{"after":62,"q":"In the cleaning tender, Bidder B scores 87.0 and Bidder D scores 87.1, and D asks for a four-year term. Which next step fits best?","options":["Award to D, because it has the highest score","Run a price-only e-auction between B and D","Award to B, because it is cheaper","Run a BAFO round with both, setting the term you would accept"],"answer":3,"explain":"A tenth of a point is a statistical tie. A BAFO lets both bidders improve price and terms once under the same rules, while an auction would push a labour service toward the wage floor.","t":514.71},{"after":63,"q":"The losing incumbent asks for a call to understand why it lost and what the winner's price was. What is the right response?","options":["Share its scores, strengths and weaknesses per criterion in writing, without other prices","Share the winner's price so it can improve next time","Decline any debrief, because this is not public procurement","Share the full evaluation spreadsheet"],"answer":0,"explain":"A written debrief on its own scores against each criterion is good practice and keeps good suppliers bidding. Another bidder's prices and solution are confidential and are never shared.","t":617.98},{"after":65,"q":"You are renewing a core software contract where licences, support and implementation services are priced as one bundle. Which contract architecture protects you at the next renewal?","options":["One bundled price, because it is simpler to manage","Licence or subscription, with support and services in separate schedules","A catalogue contract with a price list","A master agreement with no pricing schedule"],"answer":1,"explain":"Separate schedules make each cost visible, so at renewal you can challenge unused licences, support uplifts and services independently. Bundling removes that unbundling lever.","t":854.87},{"after":66,"q":"Nordvia IT needs a supplier for a discovery phase where nobody yet knows the exact scope. Which price mechanism fits?","options":["Fixed periodic price","Gain-share on productivity","Capped time and materials with reporting","Indexation to a wage index"],"answer":2,"explain":"Capped time and materials fits unclear scope. The cap and regular reporting stop the mechanism from rewarding the supplier for taking longer.","t":960.74},{"after":67,"q":"The security supplier proposes indexing the whole price twice a year to its own cost statement. Which counter-proposal follows good practice?","options":["Accept, provided the increases stay small","Index the whole price once a year to general inflation","Refuse any indexation for the full term","Index once a year, labour share only, to a named wage index, with a cap and only if KPIs were met"],"answer":3,"explain":"Indexation done right is yearly, on the labour share only, linked to a named published index, capped and conditional on performance. That is where labour-service contracts usually keep or lose their savings.","t":1080.12},{"after":68,"q":"A business owner wants 25 KPIs and large service credits, saying the credits will help fund next year's budget. What do you advise?","options":["Five to eight measurable KPIs, with capped credits used as a lever for behaviour","Keep all 25 KPIs, but drop the credits","Accept, because more KPIs give more control","Replace KPIs with a yearly satisfaction survey"],"answer":0,"explain":"A few measurable KPIs that matter get read and managed. Service credits are capped and exist to change behaviour, with earn-back and termination for persistent failure, not to generate revenue.","t":1194.64},{"after":72,"q":"After the BAFO, D leads at 50 to 50 and at 60 to 40 quality to price, but B would win at 40 to 60. What belongs in your recommendation?","options":["Recommend B, because price is safer","Recommend D, show the sensitivity including the flip, and attach contract conditions","Recommend D and leave out the 40 to 60 result to avoid confusion","Ask approvers to choose the weights"],"answer":1,"explain":"D wins at the agreed weights, so it is recommended. Showing the flip at 40 to 60 tells approvers the decision is close, and conditions on KPIs, indexation, staff data and exit protect the value.","t":1474.57}],"cues":[[0.6,1.38,"Welcome back."],[1.38,5.16,"This lesson covers module 4.3 of Session 4: award and contract."],[5.16,11.8,"In the previous lessons of this session we prepared and ran the negotiation."],[11.8,18.63,"Now we turn the result into a decision that approvers can sign, and into a contract that protects what we won."],[18.63,21.29,"The subtitle on this slide says it plainly."],[21.57,24.24,"Leverage is highest before signature."],[24.24,30.89,"Once the contract is signed, every change becomes a request, and the supplier no longer has to compete for your business."],[30.89,38.02,"So whatever you want the supplier to do over the next three, four or five years has to be written down now."],[38.52,39.78,"Here is the plan."],[39.78,43.31,"First, the path from evaluation to signature, step by step."],[43.31,50.13,"Second, the award recommendation: what goes into it, and why approvers should never need to reopen the file."],[50.13,57.76,"Third, we return to the Nordvia headquarters cleaning tender and look at the scores, which turn out to be a tie."],[58.04,60.59,"Then debriefing unsuccessful bidders."],[60.59,71.01,"After that, the contract itself: its architecture, its schedules, price mechanisms, a worked example on indexation, and service levels with service credits."],[71.01,77.9,"We close with Coupa contract lifecycle management, an exercise on the cleaning award, and the debrief."],[78.86,82.72,"Let's start with the path from evaluation to signature."],[82.72,85.58,"There are six steps on the slide."],[85.58,86.1,"Evaluation."],[86.1,89.88,"Negotiation or best and final offer, which we call BAFO."],[89.88,91.61,"Award recommendation."],[91.61,92.5,"Approval."],[92.5,94.53,"Courtesy period and debriefs."],[94.53,96.18,"And finally, signature."],[96.68,97.84,"Why spell this out?"],[97.84,102.48,"Because each step has an owner, and each step is a place where value can leak."],[102.48,110.04,"Evaluation belongs to the evaluation panel, scoring individually first and then agreeing in a consensus meeting."],[110.04,116.8,"Negotiation or BAFO belongs to the sourcing lead, inside the mandate agreed with the business owner."],[117.08,126.09,"The award recommendation is written by Procurement, but the business owner co-signs it, because the business owner has to live with the supplier."],[126.09,132.68,"Approval follows the delegation of authority: the higher the contract value, the more senior the approvers."],[132.68,136.34,"The courtesy period and the debriefs belong to Procurement again."],[136.62,140.72,"And signature belongs to whoever holds signing authority in the entity."],[141.22,143.79,"Now look at the line at the bottom of the slide."],[143.79,146.36,"What is not in the contract will not happen."],[146.36,150.31,"That sounds obvious, yet most of us have seen it go wrong."],[150.31,157.19,"The winning bid promised a dedicated account manager, a quarterly innovation workshop and a free transition."],[157.19,159.25,"The contract mentioned none of it."],[159.53,168.24,"Eighteen months later, the account manager covers six clients, the workshops never happened, and the transition was invoiced as extra work."],[168.24,169.62,"Nobody lied."],[169.62,173.14,"The promise simply never travelled from the bid into the contract."],[173.14,174.93,"So here is a good habit."],[175.21,184.77,"Before signature, go through the winning bid line by line, and for every promise that influenced the score, ask one question: where is this in the contract?"],[185.26,192.84,"In Coupa, the approvals run through the contract lifecycle management workflow, CLM for short, with an audit trail."],[192.84,195.64,"That matters in a regulated insurer."],[195.64,205.16,"When internal audit asks who approved this contract, on what basis and when, the answer should be one click away, not buried in somebody's mailbox."],[206.12,208.34,"Now the award recommendation."],[208.34,213.53,"This is the single document that carries the decision from Procurement to the approvers."],[213.53,214.4,"The test is simple."],[214.4,218.66,"Approvers should be able to sign without reopening the file."],[218.94,226.75,"If a CFO has to ask for the evaluation spreadsheet to understand why we chose this supplier, the recommendation has failed."],[227.25,229.41,"Look at the six sections in the table."],[229.41,237.66,"First, scope and value: what we are buying, for whom, for how long, and the total value over the full term, including options."],[237.66,238.82,"Not the annual value."],[238.82,239.78,"The total."],[239.78,245.64,"Second, process: the route to market, which bidders were invited and why, and the timeline."],[245.92,248.45,"This shows that the competition was fair."],[248.45,255.56,"Third, evaluation: the scores, their sensitivity to the weights, and how any abnormally low bids were treated."],[255.56,262.47,"Fourth, commercials: the outcome against the target and against the baseline, and the total cost of ownership."],[262.47,265.27,"Ellram's point about TCO applies here."],[265.55,271.99,"The cheapest price is not the lowest cost if transition, contract management and exit are expensive."],[272.27,288.83,"Fifth, risk and regulation: the due diligence results, financial, information security and regulatory, the classification of the contract, for example whether it is an ICT service under DORA, and the clauses that classification requires."],[289.11,297.87,"Sixth, the decision: the recommendation itself, the conditions attached to it, and the approvals required under the delegation of authority."],[298.37,302.44,"Let me stress sensitivity, because it is the part most often left out."],[302.44,309.87,"Suppose your weights were 50 percent quality and 50 percent price, and your preferred bidder wins by one point."],[309.87,311.36,"What happens at 60 to 40?"],[311.36,312.61,"Or at 40 to 60?"],[312.61,318.38,"If the recommendation flips when the weights move by ten points, the approvers need to know."],[318.66,320.79,"It does not make the decision wrong."],[320.79,326.16,"The weights were agreed before issue, and that is what protects the fairness of the event."],[326.16,332.38,"But it tells the approvers that the bids are close, and that the conditions in the contract matter a great deal."],[332.66,338.46,"A recommendation that flips at 60 to 40 needs to say so, in writing, on the first page."],[338.96,341.24,"One more point, on conditions."],[341.24,350.3,"A recommendation can say: award to this bidder, on condition that the KPIs, the indexation clause and the exit schedule are agreed as drafted."],[350.3,357.29,"That keeps your leverage in the final contract talks, because the approval itself depends on those terms."],[358.24,365.01,"Let's make this concrete with the Nordvia headquarters cleaning tender, project P1 in the pipeline."],[365.01,366.27,"These bids are fictional."],[366.27,368.87,"Bidder C has already been excluded."],[368.87,375.75,"Its staffing hours failed the wage-floor check, a classic abnormally low bid in a labour-intensive service."],[376.03,383.22,"If the hours cannot cover the cleaning, either the service fails or the staff are underpaid, and neither is acceptable."],[383.72,385.19,"Three bidders remain."],[385.19,391.84,"Look at the columns: price in thousands of euros, a quality score, and the total at 50 to 50."],[391.84,399.44,"The price score uses the formula in the footnote: the lowest compliant price divided by the bid price, times 100."],[399.94,406.69,"Bidder A is the incumbent, at 690 thousand euros, with a quality score of 62."],[406.69,412.61,"Its price score is 610 divided by 690, times 100, which is about 88.4."],[412.61,416.36,"Half of 62 plus half of 88.4 gives 75.2."],[416.36,418.3,"The incumbent is clearly third."],[418.3,425.9,"Bidder B is the cheapest, at 610 thousand euros, so it gets the full 100 points for price."],[426.18,433.58,"With quality at 74, its total is half of 74 plus half of 100, which is 87.0."],[433.58,439.98,"Bidder D bids 655 thousand euros, with the best quality, 81."],[439.98,446.72,"Its price score is 610 divided by 655, times 100, about 93.1."],[446.72,450.45,"Half of 81 plus half of 93.1 gives 87.1."],[450.45,457.47,"But D wants a four-year contract, which is not the term we asked for."],[457.98,460.55,"So B scores 87.0 and D scores 87.1."],[460.55,463.53,"A difference of a tenth of a point."],[463.53,464.34,"Is that a winner?"],[464.34,464.68,"No."],[464.68,466.58,"That is a statistical tie."],[466.58,468.65,"Scoring has a margin of error."],[468.65,473.93,"One evaluator marking one criterion one point differently could flip it."],[473.93,474.75,"So what do you do?"],[474.75,479.53,"This is the moment for a BAFO round, not for an auction."],[479.53,480.85,"Why not an auction?"],[481.13,484.25,"Because this is a quality-sensitive labour service."],[484.25,495.29,"The two bidders differ on quality and on terms, not only on price, and a price-only auction would push both of them toward the very wage floor we just used to exclude Bidder C."],[495.57,501.93,"A BAFO lets both bidders improve their price and terms once, in writing, under the same rules."],[501.93,505.57,"And it lets you answer D's four-year request directly."],[505.57,507.31,"The term is ours to set."],[507.31,514.71,"We can say what we would accept, for example three years plus two optional one-year extensions tied to performance."],[515.66,517.76,"After the decision come the debriefs."],[517.76,520.73,"There are four cards on the slide."],[520.73,524.3,"Always: debrief in writing, before or soon after signature."],[524.3,529.32,"Share: their scores, and their strengths and weaknesses against each criterion."],[529.32,533.64,"Never share: another bidder's prices or confidential solution."],[533.92,537.52,"And the courtesy period: a short pause before signing."],[537.52,541.94,"A mandatory standstill period exists only in public procurement."],[541.94,546.57,"In a private insurer, the courtesy period is good practice, not law."],[547.07,547.88,"Why bother?"],[547.88,549.04,"Three reasons."],[549.04,550.35,"First, fairness."],[550.35,556.22,"Bidders invested real time and money in their proposals, and a clear explanation respects that."],[556.22,558.13,"Second, market health."],[558.13,566.27,"Good suppliers who lose without understanding why often stop bidding, and in a narrow market that weakens your next tender."],[566.27,566.91,"Third, risk."],[567.19,576.88,"A short courtesy period gives a losing bidder the chance to point out a genuine error in the evaluation before the contract is signed, while it is still cheap to fix."],[576.88,583.0,"In the public sector, the Crown Commercial Service requires written feedback in further competitions."],[583.28,586.24,"We are not bound by that, but it is a good benchmark."],[586.75,588.51,"What does a good debrief sound like?"],[588.51,591.75,"For the Nordvia incumbent, something like this."],[591.75,596.08,"Your quality score was 62, against a best score of 81."],[596.08,600.9,"The main gaps were your staffing method and your KPI reporting proposal."],[600.9,604.44,"Your price was competitive but did not close the quality gap."],[604.72,608.58,"What it never says is: Bidder B was 80 thousand euros cheaper."],[608.58,612.11,"That is someone else's confidential information."],[612.11,617.98,"Give feedback on their own bid against the criteria, never a comparison with a competitor's price."],[618.93,622.42,"Now to the contract itself, starting with its architecture."],[622.42,627.24,"Before you draft a single clause, decide what shape the contract should have."],[627.24,630.13,"The table shows four typical situations."],[630.63,641.39,"For panel or project work, for example consulting, IT services or marketing agencies, the architecture is a master agreement plus statements of work, or call-offs."],[641.39,647.16,"The master agreement fixes the legal terms, the rate card and the governance once."],[647.44,653.64,"Each project then gets a short statement of work with scope, deliverables, price and timeline."],[653.64,658.98,"That way you do not renegotiate liability every time someone needs two weeks of work."],[659.26,674.15,"For cleaning or security, it is a single-supplier term contract with options: one supplier, one scope, a base term long enough to recover mobilisation, and extension options the buyer can exercise if performance is good."],[674.15,678.98,"For tail and catalogue items, it is a catalogue contract with a price list."],[679.26,683.94,"Office supplies, promotional items, small facilities consumables."],[683.94,689.16,"Here the value lies in easy buying and fixed prices, not in heavy governance."],[689.66,695.84,"For software, it is a licence or subscription, with support and services as separate schedules."],[695.84,696.78,"The note explains why."],[696.78,706.85,"When licence, support and services are bundled into one price, you cannot see what each costs, and at renewal you cannot negotiate them separately."],[707.13,712.28,"Keeping them separate keeps the unbundling lever alive for the next negotiation."],[712.28,715.56,"Think of the Kelvar case from earlier in this session."],[715.56,720.23,"The strongest arguments were about unused licences and the uplift cap."],[720.23,724.35,"You can only make those arguments if the licence line is visible."],[725.3,731.5,"Inside a services contract, most of the value sits in the schedules, not in the general terms."],[731.5,735.92,"Let's go through the six on the slide, and what you must get right in each."],[736.42,742.92,"Scope and specification: output-based or hybrid, and a clear statement of what is in and what is out."],[742.92,747.19,"Disputes in services contracts are very often scope disputes."],[747.19,750.62,"Is window cleaning above the second floor included?"],[750.62,753.56,"Is the deep clean after an office move included?"],[753.56,754.68,"Write it down."],[754.96,759.73,"Service levels: a few measurable KPIs, with the method and the reporting."],[759.73,761.8,"We come back to these shortly."],[761.8,766.83,"Pricing: the price mechanism, rates, indexation and invoicing."],[766.83,771.79,"If the invoicing rules are vague, invoice checking becomes a monthly argument."],[772.07,779.8,"Term and termination: the base term, the options, termination for cause, termination for convenience, and change of control."],[779.8,783.09,"Change of control matters more than people think."],[783.09,789.65,"If your cleaning supplier is bought by a company you excluded on quality, do you still want the contract?"],[790.15,796.59,"Exit and transition: the supplier's assistance at the end, the return of data and assets, and staff data."],[796.59,805.42,"In cleaning and security, staff may transfer to the next supplier, and the next supplier needs accurate staff data to price fairly."],[805.7,812.91,"If the exit schedule does not oblige the outgoing supplier to provide it, your next tender starts blind."],[812.91,825.2,"And protection: the data processing agreement under the GDPR, the General Data Protection Regulation, plus information security, liability, insurance, audit rights and subcontracting."],[825.71,830.14,"Now the note on this slide, which is one of the key points of the module."],[830.14,836.32,"If the draft contract was attached to the RFP, bidders have already marked their deviations."],[836.32,838.83,"The negotiation happened through the bids."],[839.11,846.94,"That is far better than negotiating the contract after award, when the winner knows it has won and your leverage is gone."],[846.94,854.88,"So attach the draft contract, with these schedules, to every RFP you can, and evaluate the deviations as part of the bid."],[855.82,857.6,"Let's look at price mechanisms."],[857.6,862.34,"There are five in the table, each with what it fits and what to watch."],[862.34,867.22,"A fixed periodic price fits a stable scope, such as cleaning or a managed service."],[867.22,870.98,"Watch two things: a clear scope, and change control."],[870.98,875.0,"A fixed price on a vague scope is an invitation to invoice extras."],[875.28,884.68,"Unit rates fit variations and extras, for example an hourly rate for additional guards at an event, or a price per square metre for deep cleaning."],[884.68,886.42,"Watch the volume forecasts."],[886.42,890.32,"Bidders price unit rates against the volumes you gave them."],[890.32,893.56,"If those volumes were wrong, the rates may not hold."],[894.06,903.29,"Capped time and materials fits an unclear scope, for example a discovery phase in an IT project, when nobody yet knows exactly what needs doing."],[903.29,905.71,"Watch the cap and the reporting."],[905.71,910.56,"Without a cap, time and materials rewards the supplier for taking longer."],[910.56,913.88,"Indexation fits multi-year labour services."],[914.16,920.88,"Watch four parameters: which index, what share of the price it applies to, how often, and with what cap."],[920.88,923.25,"The next slide is a worked example."],[923.25,926.12,"Gain-share fits productivity and energy."],[926.12,933.33,"For example, the supplier proposes changes that cut energy use in a building, and both sides share the savings."],[933.33,934.83,"Watch the baseline."],[935.11,938.53,"It must be agreed upfront, before anything changes."],[938.53,943.86,"Otherwise every gain-share discussion becomes an argument about what would have happened anyway."],[944.36,947.97,"The note brings back Ellram's total cost of ownership view."],[947.97,953.97,"Transition, contract management and exit costs belong in the comparison, not only the price."],[953.97,960.74,"A mechanism that looks cheap but needs someone checking complex invoices every month is not cheap."],[961.69,967.88,"Here is a worked example on indexation, using Nordvia security, project P6 in the pipeline."],[967.88,969.17,"It is fictional."],[969.17,974.64,"The situation today: hourly rates indexed twice in two years, and no KPIs."],[974.64,980.1,"So the price went up twice, and nobody can say whether the service got better or worse."],[980.1,981.86,"How common is that?"],[981.86,981.91,"Very."],[982.19,986.71,"Indexation is often where labour-service contracts lose their savings."],[986.71,994.31,"You run a good tender, you win a good rate, and within three years the indexation clause has quietly given most of it back."],[994.82,997.67,"Look at the five lines of indexation done right."],[997.67,999.84,"Once a year, on a fixed date."],[999.84,1002.11,"Not whenever the supplier asks."],[1002.11,1004.61,"On the labour share of the price only."],[1004.61,1008.67,"In security, labour is most of the cost, but not all of it."],[1008.67,1013.59,"Equipment, management overhead and margin do not rise with a wage agreement."],[1013.87,1016.29,"Linked to a named, published wage index."],[1016.29,1025.32,"Not to the supplier's own cost statement, and not to a vague phrase like market conditions, but a named index both sides can look up."],[1025.32,1027.86,"With a cap, and only if the KPIs were met."],[1027.86,1034.46,"The cap protects the budget in an unusual year, and the KPI condition links price to performance."],[1034.74,1045.85,"And recorded as a contract amendment in Coupa CLM, so that the new rates are the contracted rates, invoices are checked against them, and the history is visible at the next renewal."],[1046.35,1051.19,"Let me give you a simple illustration, with illustrative numbers."],[1051.19,1058.56,"Suppose the annual price is one million euros, labour is 80 percent of it, and the named wage index rises 4 percent."],[1058.56,1062.94,"Applied to the whole price, the increase would be 40 thousand euros."],[1062.94,1066.85,"Applied only to the labour share, it is 32 thousand euros."],[1067.13,1071.92,"With a cap of 3 percent on the labour share, it is 24 thousand euros."],[1071.92,1074.99,"And if the KPIs were missed, it is zero."],[1074.99,1077.17,"Same index, very different outcomes."],[1077.17,1080.12,"That is exactly what your clause should do."],[1081.07,1084.1,"Next, service levels, KPIs and service credits."],[1084.1,1085.05,"Four cards."],[1085.05,1087.55,"Few: five to eight KPIs that matter to the business."],[1087.55,1089.48,"Not thirty."],[1089.48,1096.72,"When there are thirty KPIs, nobody reads the report, and the supplier learns that none of them matter."],[1096.72,1102.06,"Measurable: the source of the data, the method and the frequency, agreed in the contract."],[1102.34,1113.48,"A KPI like user satisfaction means nothing until you say: measured by a quarterly survey of named users, on a five-point scale, with a target average of four."],[1113.98,1118.42,"At risk: service credits as a share of the monthly fee, with a cap."],[1118.42,1123.37,"If a KPI is missed, the supplier credits a defined share of the fee."],[1123.37,1128.3,"The cap keeps the commercial risk proportionate, which keeps the price reasonable."],[1128.3,1133.56,"Fair: earn-back for sustained recovery, and termination for persistent failure."],[1133.84,1142.64,"Earn-back means that if the supplier misses a KPI one month but then performs above target for the next few months, it can recover the credit."],[1142.64,1144.48,"That rewards fixing the problem."],[1144.48,1151.87,"And if the failure persists, you need a clear right to terminate, because credits alone never fix a broken service."],[1152.37,1154.42,"The note makes an important point."],[1154.42,1158.69,"The exact percentages are commercial choices, not norms."],[1158.69,1162.58,"No standard says credits must be a particular share of the fee."],[1162.58,1166.77,"And service credits are a lever for behaviour, not a revenue line."],[1167.05,1174.49,"If someone starts budgeting service credit income, something is wrong with the supplier or with the contract."],[1174.49,1176.18,"Think of Nordvia security again."],[1176.18,1177.12,"No KPIs today."],[1177.4,1189.04,"A good start would be five: shift coverage, response time to alarms, incident reports within an agreed time, staff turnover on site, and a quarterly user satisfaction score."],[1189.04,1190.87,"Each with a source and a method."],[1190.87,1194.64,"And the yearly indexation applies only if they are met."],[1195.59,1197.51,"Time for a short reflection."],[1197.51,1201.97,"The poll asks: which contract area is weakest in your entity today?"],[1201.97,1210.58,"The options are KPIs and service credits, indexation, exit and transition, regulatory clauses, and renewal management."],[1210.58,1214.09,"Take a moment to pick your own answer, and be honest."],[1214.37,1218.76,"If you are not sure, think of the last three services contracts you saw."],[1218.76,1222.65,"Which of these areas would you least like an auditor to look at?"],[1222.65,1224.1,"Keep that answer in mind."],[1224.1,1227.76,"Several of these weaknesses come back in the next two modules."],[1228.71,1230.3,"Now, where does all this live?"],[1230.3,1233.22,"In Coupa contract lifecycle management."],[1233.22,1233.79,"Four cards."],[1233.79,1238.03,"Templates: start from the Group template, not the supplier's paper."],[1238.03,1243.54,"When you start from the supplier's paper, you spend your time removing what favours them."],[1243.82,1248.99,"When you start from yours, they spend their time asking for changes, and you decide."],[1248.99,1261.61,"Clause library: approved clauses available inside Microsoft Word, so that a sourcing lead in any entity can insert the approved indexation or data processing clause without redrafting it."],[1261.89,1266.56,"Workflow: review, negotiation and approval with an audit trail."],[1266.56,1271.04,"And alerts: milestones, options, notice periods and expiry."],[1271.04,1276.82,"Those alerts are what prevents the silent roll-over we discuss in module 4.5."],[1277.32,1281.05,"All four features are in Coupa's public product description."],[1281.05,1286.41,"Which templates actually exist for each category in your entity is a different question."],[1286.41,1290.5,"Ask your Coupa administrators, and confirm it in your Coupa tenant."],[1291.46,1293.01,"Now it is your turn."],[1293.01,1297.2,"This is Practice 4.3 in your workbook, about twelve minutes."],[1297.2,1301.88,"We go back to the headquarters cleaning tender after the BAFO round."],[1301.88,1303.16,"The results are illustrative."],[1303.16,1308.36,"Bidder B now bids 600 thousand euros, with quality still at 74."],[1308.64,1319.92,"Bidder D bids 632 thousand euros, with quality 81, and now accepts a term of three years plus one plus one, with options based on KPIs."],[1320.42,1321.05,"Your tasks."],[1321.05,1330.07,"First, calculate both totals at 50 to 50, with the same price formula: lowest price divided by bid price, times 100."],[1330.07,1333.59,"Second, test the result at 60 to 40, quality to price."],[1333.59,1338.13,"Third, write your recommendation, with conditions for the contract."],[1338.13,1341.84,"Fourth, draft two lines of debrief for the incumbent."],[1342.12,1345.28,"The workbook has check fields that confirm your numbers."],[1345.28,1351.78,"If you want to do the exercise, pause the video now, open the Modules tab, and come back when you are done."],[1352.73,1353.96,"Welcome back."],[1353.96,1356.51,"Let's go through the numbers."],[1356.51,1364.44,"After the BAFO, Bidder B has the lowest price, 600 thousand euros, so it scores 100 on price."],[1364.44,1367.72,"Half of 74 plus half of 100 is 87.0."],[1367.72,1373.33,"Bidder D's price score is 600 divided by 632, times 100, about 94.9."],[1373.33,1378.14,"Half of 81 plus half of 94.9 is about 88.0."],[1378.42,1383.25,"So at 50 to 50, D leads by one point."],[1383.25,1396.13,"At 60 to 40, quality to price, B gets 0.6 times 74, which is 44.4, plus 0.4 times 100, which is 40, for 84.4."],[1396.13,1405.39,"D gets 0.6 times 81, which is 48.6, plus 0.4 times 94.9, about 38.0, for 86.6."],[1405.39,1408.19,"So D wins more clearly."],[1408.7,1414.88,"Now the slide adds one more test, and it is the point of the whole exercise."],[1414.88,1418.88,"At 40 to 60, quality to price, the order flips."],[1418.88,1421.59,"B scores 89.6 and D scores 89.4."],[1421.87,1432.6,"So in the recommendation you write: D is recommended at the agreed weights, its lead grows when quality weighs more, and the result would reverse if price weighed 60 percent."],[1432.6,1436.33,"That one sentence protects the approvers, and it protects you."],[1436.82,1437.93,"Why does this matter?"],[1437.93,1441.1,"Because it shows why weights are fixed before issue."],[1441.1,1450.42,"If they could change after the bids were opened, anyone who preferred B could argue for 40 to 60, and anyone who preferred D for 60 to 40."],[1450.42,1453.52,"Fixing the weights first removes that temptation."],[1453.8,1460.11,"And sensitivity belongs in every recommendation, so that approvers see how robust the decision is."],[1460.11,1468.0,"The conditions for the contract are the KPIs, indexation linked to a wage index, staff transfer data, and exit."],[1468.0,1474.56,"And the incumbent debrief covers the quality score gap and its reasons, never the other bidders' prices."],[1475.52,1478.85,"Let's recap module 4.3 in three lines."],[1478.85,1494.08,"First, an award recommendation can be signed without reopening the file: scope and value, process, evaluation with sensitivity, commercials against target and baseline, risk and regulation, and a clear decision with conditions."],[1494.36,1501.72,"Second, debrief every bidder in writing: their own scores, strengths and weaknesses, never another bidder's prices."],[1501.72,1506.22,"It keeps the market healthy and good suppliers coming back."],[1506.22,1512.15,"Third, the price mechanism, the KPIs, the term and the exit protect what the negotiation won."],[1512.43,1520.09,"A great price with a loose indexation clause, no KPIs and no exit plan is a great price for about eighteen months."],[1520.59,1524.18,"And here is the bridge to the next lesson."],[1524.18,1528.43,"In an insurer, some of the contract is not ours to negotiate at all."],[1528.43,1536.16,"It is set by regulation: the Solvency II outsourcing rules and DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act."],[1536.44,1547.42,"Module 4.4 shows which clauses those are, how to classify a contract early, and how to build the clauses into the draft so that they never become a negotiation issue."],[1547.42,1548.58,"See you there."]]},{"id":"s4-l5","session":"s4","title":"Contracts in a regulated insurer","summary":"Classify a sourcing project early under Solvency II outsourcing and DORA, and build the required clauses and a testable exit plan into the draft contract, to be confirmed with Legal and Compliance.","from":74,"to":90,"audio":"audio/s4-l5.mp3","duration":1598.0,"segs":[[74,0.6,34.95,"Welcome back. This lesson covers module 4.4 of Session 4: contracts in a regulated insurer. In the last lesson we built a contract that protects the value of the deal. Now we add a layer that most other industries do not have. As an insurer, the Group must meet the Solvency II rules on outsourcing, and DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act, for ICT services. Both reach right into Source to Contract: into intake, strategy, the RFx, evaluation and the contract itself."],[74,35.45,91.26,"Before we start, an important framing. Everything in this module is orientation, not legal advice. The aim is that you recognise when these rules apply, ask the right questions early, and know which clauses to expect. For each actual contract, Legal, Compliance and the owner of the DORA register confirm what applies. Here is the plan. We start with one principle about non-negotiable clauses. Then we classify. Then Solvency II, Article 49 of the Directive and Article 274 of the Delegated Regulation. Then DORA, Articles 28 and 30. Then a word on the EIOPA cloud guidelines, which are withdrawn. We then map regulation onto the S2C steps, classify the Nordvia pipeline, look at the Northbyte exit gap, and finish with an exit plan that would pass, your own checklist, and a debrief."],[75,92.21,109.11,"Here is the principle. Some clauses are not negotiable. Negotiate how they are met, never whether. Look at the second line. Pooled audits, certifications and reporting formats are negotiable. Audit rights, exit and cooperation with supervisors are not."],[75,109.61,165.96,"What does that mean in practice? Imagine a software supplier telling you: we never accept on-site audits from customers. If the service supports a critical or important function, you cannot simply drop the audit right. But you can discuss how the right is used. For example, you might rely first on independent certifications and audit reports, join a pooled audit with other customers of the same supplier, and agree a reporting format and a notice period for inspections. That is exactly how the Kelvar seller in Case 4 was briefed: it dislikes unlimited on-site audits, but accepts the clauses through certifications and pooled audits. The right stays in the contract. The way it is exercised becomes the negotiation. That framing changes the conversation. You stop arguing about principle, which the supplier can resist for weeks, and start solving a practical problem together."],[76,166.91,233.39,"Now, classify first. The slide gives five questions, in order. First: is it outsourcing? Under Solvency II, outsourcing means a provider performs a function or activity that the insurer would otherwise perform itself. Second: is that function critical or important? This is the insurer's own documented assessment. Typical examples from supervisory guidance include underwriting, claims handling, risk management, actuarial work and data storage. Third: is it an ICT service under DORA? DORA's definition is broad. It includes software as a service, cloud, managed IT and data services. Fourth: does that ICT service support a critical or important function? If yes, extra DORA clauses apply, and an exit strategy is required. Fifth: what about data and people? Personal data means a GDPR data processing agreement. Staff transfer rules may apply in cleaning and security. Agency-work rules may apply to IT staff augmentation."],[76,233.89,265.74,"This is the triage you met in Session 1, now confirmed with the people who own it. Most cleaning, catering, agency and travel contracts are usually not critical outsourcing. IT services and cloud may well be. And why first? Because classification changes everything downstream: the notification lead time, the clauses in the draft contract, the knock-out criteria in the RFx, and the due diligence. Discover it at contract stage, and you may have to reopen the whole event."],[76,266.24,306.4,"Let me give you a quick example of why the order of the questions matters. A Nordvia HR team wants a new payroll platform delivered as software as a service. Is it outsourcing? If the provider also runs the payroll calculations that Nordvia's own team used to do, quite possibly yes. Is it critical or important? That depends on the insurer's own assessment. Is it an ICT service? Certainly, because it is software as a service. Personal data? Obviously, salaries and bank details. Five questions, and in ten minutes you know which experts must join the project, and which clauses the draft contract needs."],[77,307.35,356.72,"Let's start with Solvency II, Article 49 of the Directive. Three rules on the slide. Full responsibility: the insurer remains fully responsible for outsourced functions. You can outsource the work. You cannot outsource the accountability. If a provider handling claims fails, the supervisor talks to the insurer, not to the provider. Four conditions: outsourcing a critical or important function must not materially harm the quality of governance, must not unduly increase operational risk, must not impair the supervisor's ability to monitor compliance, and must not undermine continuous and satisfactory service to policyholders. And prior notification: the supervisor is notified before a critical or important function is outsourced."],[77,357.22,386.94,"The note has the practical consequence for us. Notification lead time must be in the sourcing timeline. Picture a Nordvia project where everyone assumes a managed service is routine. Two weeks before the planned start, Compliance classifies it as critical outsourcing. Now the supervisor must be notified before the service begins, and the start date moves. A late classification can delay the start date. An early one costs a single conversation at intake."],[78,387.89,435.92,"Article 274 of the Solvency II Delegated Regulation sets out what the written agreement with the provider must contain. Look at the table. Duties and compliance: the provider's responsibilities, and its commitment to comply with the law and with the insurer's policies. Supervisor: the provider cooperates with the supervisor, and the supervisor may question the provider directly. Continuity: notice periods long enough for the insurer to find an alternative, and termination without detriment to the continuity and quality of service. Access: information and premises open to the insurer, its auditor and the supervisor. And sub-outsourcing: conditions for passing work on, with the provider remaining responsible."],[78,436.42,471.28,"Notice the continuity line. A thirty-day termination notice may look flexible, but if replacing the provider would take a year, it gives you no real protection. The test is whether you could find and move to an alternative in time. And the note adds two requirements that sit outside the contract. Article 274 also requires a written outsourcing policy, and a detailed examination of the provider before selection. That examination is your due diligence step in the sourcing process. It happens before award, not after."],[78,471.78,511.27,"How does this look in a real sourcing file? Suppose Nordvia outsources part of its claims handling to a specialist provider. The outsourcing policy tells you who must approve, which assessment is needed and what the contract must contain. The detailed examination covers the provider's financial strength, its capacity, its controls, its business continuity and any conflicts of interest. Write the result down in the award recommendation, in the risk and regulation section we met in the last lesson. Then the approvers can see that the examination happened before the selection, and the supervisor can see it later too."],[79,512.22,579.04,"Now DORA, Regulation 2022 2554, which has applied since 17 January 2025. Article 28 covers what happens before and around the contract. Article 28, paragraph 3: a register of information covering all ICT contractual arrangements. Every one, not just the critical ones. The templates for the register are set out in an Implementing Regulation from 2024. Paragraph 4: a pre-contract assessment. Does the service support a critical or important function? What are the risks, including concentration risk? Has due diligence been done? Are there conflicts of interest? Paragraph 5: contract only with providers that meet appropriate information security standards. Paragraph 7: mandatory termination rights, for example for a serious breach or where the provider's situation impedes supervision. And paragraph 8: exit strategies for services supporting critical or important functions, documented, tested and reviewed."],[79,579.54,609.67,"Read paragraph 4 with procurement eyes. A pre-contract assessment of risk, concentration and due diligence is, in our language, the market analysis, the strategy and the supplier qualification. DORA does not invent new steps. It makes existing good practice mandatory and auditable. And concentration risk is a real question for us. If several entities and several critical services depend on the same provider, a single failure hits all of them at once."],[80,610.62,661.46,"Article 30, paragraph 2, lists what every ICT contract must contain, critical or not. Services: a clear description, and the conditions for subcontracting. Locations: where the services run and where data is stored, with notice of any change. Data: availability, integrity and confidentiality, plus access to the data and its return, including on insolvency or termination. Service levels: a description, including updates. Incidents and authorities: assistance with ICT incidents at no cost or at a cost agreed in advance, and cooperation with the competent authorities. And exit and training: termination rights with notice periods, and the provider's participation in the insurer's security-awareness programmes."],[80,661.96,699.92,"Two practical points from the note. First, the contract must be one written document, under Article 30, paragraph 1. Not a master agreement plus a supplier's online terms that change whenever the supplier likes. Second, keep these clauses as Coupa CLM templates, so that nobody redrafts them. Think of a marketing team buying a small analytics tool as software as a service. Small value, not critical. It is still an ICT service, so it still needs a register entry and these baseline clauses. The template makes that a ten-minute task instead of a legal project."],[80,700.42,723.63,"One more angle on the locations clause. Location is not only a data protection question. It also determines which law applies to the data, how quickly a provider can restore a service, and whether the supervisor can actually inspect. A supplier that wants the freedom to move processing anywhere without telling you is asking you to give up something DORA expects you to keep."],[81,724.58,765.14,"Where the ICT service supports a critical or important function, Article 30, paragraph 3, adds six clauses. Service levels: precise quantitative and qualitative performance targets, so the insurer can take corrective action. Reporting: notice and reporting obligations on developments that may affect the service. Continuity: business contingency plans and security measures. Testing: participation in the insurer's threat-led penetration testing. Audit: unrestricted rights of access, inspection and audit. And exit: an exit strategy with a mandatory, adequate transition period."],[81,765.64,797.79,"The note mentions two Delegated Regulations, from 2024 and from 2025, which add detail on the contractual policy and on subcontracting for these functions. Your Legal colleagues will know them. What you need to remember is the practical point from Case 4. The Kelvar software supports critical functions, so these clauses were not negotiable. You could negotiate price, uplift caps, term and volume, but not whether the audit and exit clauses are in the contract."],[81,798.29,830.44,"Think about what each of these means for the sourcing timeline. Threat-led penetration testing and unrestricted audit rights are exactly the clauses suppliers push back on hardest. If they first appear after award, you negotiate them with no competition left. If they are in the draft contract attached to the RFP, every bidder has already said yes, no, or yes with conditions, and you can evaluate those answers. The same logic we saw in module 4.3 applies even more strongly here."],[82,831.39,856.14,"A short but important slide. The EIOPA cloud guidelines have been withdrawn. On the left, then. EIOPA's Guidelines on outsourcing to cloud service providers were published in 2020 and applied from 2021. They asked for a cloud register, a pre-outsourcing analysis and exit planning. They are often still quoted in old templates and old supplier questionnaires."],[82,856.64,894.21,"On the right, now. EIOPA announced on 19 December 2024 that it was withdrawing them, with effect from 17 January 2025, the day DORA started to apply, to avoid overlap with DORA. So for ICT and cloud, the regime is DORA, plus Solvency II where the arrangement is also critical outsourcing. The action for you is simple. If a template or a questionnaire cites the old cloud guidelines, flag it for update. And never present the cloud guidelines as current, in a strategy paper, a recommendation or a conversation with a supplier."],[82,894.71,928.51,"Why does this deserve its own slide? Because old guidance has a long afterlife. A template drafted in 2021 is copied into a 2023 contract, which becomes the starting point for a 2026 renewal. Each time, someone assumes the references were checked. In a regulated insurer, citing a withdrawn guideline as the basis for a clause does not make the clause wrong, but it signals to an auditor that nobody looked. A five-minute check of the references in your templates is time well spent."],[83,929.46,973.71,"Now let's map regulation across the S2C steps, because this is where procurement adds real value. At intake, classify early and note the notification lead time. In the market step, look at concentration risk and subcontracting chains. Who does your provider depend on? In strategy, make exit feasibility a criterion, and consider more than one vendor where relevant. In the RFx, put the mandatory clauses into the draft contract, and make acceptance of audit rights a knock-out. In evaluation, treat due diligence as a gate, not a weight. And at contract and supplier relationship management, the register entry, the exit plan, monitoring and exit tests."],[83,974.21,1002.75,"Why a gate and not a weight? Because a weight lets a cheap bid buy its way past a security failure. If a bidder cannot meet the security standard DORA requires, no price makes it acceptable. In Coupa, the practical tip is to build classification questions into the intake form, and DORA questionnaires into the supplier onboarding forms in supplier information management. Exactly how your tenant is configured, confirm with your Coupa administrators."],[83,1003.25,1031.59,"Let me make the strategy line concrete. For a claims IT managed service, exit feasibility as a criterion means asking, already in the strategy paper, how we would leave this provider. If the honest answer is that nobody else could take over in less than two years, that shapes the term, the exit clauses and perhaps the decision to split the service into lots. Regulation, here, simply forces a question good procurement should ask anyway."],[84,1032.54,1078.6,"Let's classify the Nordvia pipeline. This is fictional, and in real life it would be confirmed by Compliance. P1, headquarters cleaning: not outsourcing of a critical function, but staff transfer rules apply. P2, IT consulting: an ICT service if the consultants' staff access Nordvia systems, and agency-work rules may apply. P3, Kelvar: an ICT service supporting critical functions, so Article 30, paragraph 3, applies. P4, Telmo: an ICT service used by the claims systems, so its criticality must be assessed. And P8, Northbyte: critical ICT, and likely critical outsourcing too, so both Solvency II and DORA apply."],[84,1079.1,1121.62,"Now challenge P2 and P4, as the note suggests. Is IT consulting always an ICT service? If consultants only write a strategy document from their own laptops, perhaps not. If they log into the policy administration system and change configurations, very likely yes. And Telmo: if the telecom lines carry claims traffic, is that supporting a critical function? It depends on how the service is used and on what happens if it fails. That is exactly why the assessment is documented. The answer depends on facts about access and use, and two reasonable people can start with different answers until those facts are on the table."],[85,1122.57,1143.33,"Now Case 4, part 2: the Northbyte exit gap. Northbyte runs Nordvia's claims IT as a managed service, at 19.4 million euros a year. The contract runs to December 2027. It is in the DORA register. And there is no tested exit plan. Look at the four gaps on the slide."],[85,1143.83,1189.08,"Gap one: no documented exit strategy, as Article 28, paragraph 8, requires. Gap two: the alternatives and the transition plan have not been identified. Who could take over, and how long would it take? Gap three: the contract may lack the Article 30, paragraph 3, exit and transition clauses, because it was signed before DORA applied. Gap four: the renewal timing leaves no room for a tested exit. If the plan were tested in late 2027 and showed you need eighteen months to move, the test would come too late. And the open question at the bottom: does a renewal, or a material change to the contract, trigger a Solvency II notification? That is one for Compliance."],[85,1189.58,1222.53,"In the classroom, participants discuss this in pairs for five minutes. Do it yourself now. What has to happen before the end of 2027, and in what order? A reasonable sequence is: document the exit strategy, identify the alternatives, review the contract against the required clauses, plan the renewal or re-tender early enough for a tested exit, and ask Compliance about notification. Record your actions in the Case 4 exercise in your workbook, and if you can, involve a Compliance colleague."],[86,1223.48,1258.43,"So what does an exit plan that would pass look like? Five elements. Triggers: termination, insolvency, service failure, regulatory instruction. Alternatives: named options, such as other providers or bringing the service back in house. Not the market. Named options. Transition: an adequate period, clear roles and the supplier's assistance. Data and knowledge: return of data, the formats, documentation, and the knowledge held by the provider's staff. Testing: a test schedule, the last test result and the next review date."],[86,1258.93,1293.06,"The note is blunt. An exit plan that has never been tested does not meet DORA for critical or important functions. But testing does not have to start with a live migration. It can start with a desk-based walk-through. Put the business owner, IT, the contract owner and Compliance in a room. Simulate the trigger: Northbyte has just announced it is leaving the market. Walk through the plan step by step. Where does it break? Who calls whom? Where is the data, and in what format? You will learn more in two hours than from a year of reading the document."],[86,1293.56,1325.82,"Why are named alternatives so important? Because the market is not an exit plan. Saying there are many providers out there tells a supervisor nothing. Saying that two named providers have the capability, that one of them answered an RFI last year, and that insourcing was costed as a fallback, tells them the plan is real. And notice how this links back to negotiation. A tested exit plan is also your BATNA. The provider that knows you can leave negotiates differently from the one that knows you cannot."],[87,1326.77,1350.97,"Time for a reflection. The poll asks: does your own Sourcing Case fall under DORA or Solvency II outsourcing? The options are a DORA ICT service, DORA supporting a critical or important function, Solvency II outsourcing, none, or not yet classified. Pick your answer. If it is not yet classified, that is your first action after this lesson."],[88,1351.92,1401.52,"Here is the exercise: Practice 4.4 in your workbook, your regulatory annex checklist, about twelve minutes. First, classify your project on the six questions in the workbook. Then check eight core clauses against your draft contract, from services and subcontracting through to the exit strategy and transition period. Mark each one as in, partly, missing or not applicable, and note what requires it: DORA, Solvency II, the GDPR, Group policy or good practice. Name the owner of each gap. And book the Legal or Compliance review. If your project is cleaning or marketing, do not skip it. You still check the GDPR and staff transfer lines. Pause the video now if you want to do the exercise in the Modules tab, and continue when you are ready."],[88,1402.02,1424.15,"A tip for doing it well. Do not tick boxes from memory. Open the actual draft contract, or the supplier's paper if that is all you have, and find each clause. If you cannot find it in two minutes, mark it as missing or partly. That honesty is what makes the review with Legal or Compliance useful, because it shows them exactly where to spend their time."],[89,1425.1,1449.42,"Welcome back. Four debrief questions. First, whose project turned out to be an ICT service under DORA? Often it is a surprise. A marketing analytics tool, an HR platform, a print management service with an online portal. Second, which clause is most often missing from supplier paper? In practice, the usual suspects are data location, exit and audit rights."],[89,1449.92,1472.52,"Third, where will audit rights be hard to negotiate, and how will you meet them? Go back to the principle of this module: negotiate how, never whether. Certifications, pooled audits and agreed formats. Fourth, who owns the register entry for your contract? If you do not know, find out this week. A contract without a register entry is a finding waiting to happen."],[89,1473.02,1497.32,"A word on why data location, exit and audit rights are the usual gaps. Supplier paper is written to protect the supplier, and these three clauses limit the supplier's freedom: where it can run the service, how easily the customer can leave, and who can look inside. None of that is bad faith. It is simply why you start from the Group template, with the regulatory clauses already in."],[90,1498.27,1535.94,"Let's recap module 4.4 in three lines. First, classify early with the outsourcing and DORA owners. Is it outsourcing? Is it critical or important? Is it an ICT service, and does it support a critical function? The answers drive the timeline, the clauses and the due diligence. Second, the required clauses go into the draft contract, not into the negotiation. Put them in the RFx, make acceptance a knock-out, and negotiate only how they are met. Third, the EIOPA cloud guidelines are withdrawn. DORA and Solvency II apply."],[90,1536.44,1574.25,"Let me add the bigger picture. These rules can feel like a burden on procurement, one more set of boxes. But look at what they actually ask for: knowing whether a service matters, checking the provider before you choose it, writing down what you need in one contract, keeping access to your data, and being able to leave. That is good sourcing. Regulation makes it non-optional, and it gives procurement a strong argument with suppliers and with internal stakeholders who want to skip steps. When a supplier pushes back on an exit clause, you are not being difficult. You are meeting a legal requirement the Group cannot waive."],[90,1574.75,1596.79,"And one reminder, as at the start. This module is orientation only. Always confirm with Legal and Compliance for your actual contract. In the next lesson we move from signature to value: how to hand the contract over so that it is used, measured and renewed on purpose. Then the capstone briefing and the close of the course. See you there."]],"questions":[{"after":75,"q":"A cloud supplier supporting a critical Nordvia function refuses any on-site audit rights. What is the best negotiating position?","options":["Drop the audit right to close the deal","Keep the audit right, and negotiate how it is exercised, for example through certifications and pooled audits","Accept a promise to cooperate informally","Walk away immediately"],"answer":1,"explain":"Audit rights for critical functions are not negotiable, but how they are met is. Certifications, pooled audits and agreed formats solve the practical concern without removing the right.","t":165.96},{"after":77,"q":"Two weeks before go-live, Compliance classifies a new managed service as outsourcing of a critical function. What is the most likely consequence under Solvency II?","options":["None, as long as the contract is signed","The supplier becomes responsible for the function","The supervisor must be notified before the outsourcing starts, which may delay the start date","The EIOPA cloud guidelines now apply"],"answer":2,"explain":"Article 49 requires prior notification of the supervisor for critical or important outsourcing, and the insurer remains fully responsible. That is why notification lead time belongs in the sourcing timeline from intake.","t":386.94},{"after":79,"q":"Which statement about DORA's register of information is correct?","options":["It covers all ICT contractual arrangements, not only critical ones","It covers only cloud contracts","It replaced the need for any pre-contract assessment","It applies only to contracts above a value threshold"],"answer":0,"explain":"Article 28, paragraph 3, requires a register of all ICT contractual arrangements. Critical or important functions add further requirements, such as tested exit strategies, but the register itself is universal.","t":609.67},{"after":80,"q":"The marketing team wants a small, non-critical analytics tool sold as software as a service on the supplier's standard online terms. What applies?","options":["Nothing, because it is low value and not critical","Only the GDPR","The full set of critical-function clauses","It is an ICT service, so it needs a register entry and the baseline DORA contract clauses in one written document"],"answer":3,"explain":"DORA's definition of ICT services is broad. Every ICT contract needs the Article 30, paragraph 2, clauses and a register entry, and Coupa CLM templates make that quick.","t":723.63},{"after":82,"q":"An old supplier questionnaire in your entity cites the EIOPA Guidelines on outsourcing to cloud service providers. What do you do?","options":["Keep it, because the guidelines are still the main cloud rule","Flag it for update: the guidelines were withdrawn from 17 January 2025, and DORA plus Solvency II apply","Add DORA questions but keep presenting the guidelines as current","Remove all cloud questions"],"answer":1,"explain":"EIOPA withdrew the cloud guidelines to avoid overlap with DORA. For ICT and cloud the regime is DORA, plus Solvency II where it is also critical outsourcing.","t":928.51},{"after":83,"q":"In an IT services RFP, one bidder is 15 percent cheaper but fails the information security due diligence. How should the evaluation treat this?","options":["Due diligence is a gate, so the bidder cannot be awarded regardless of price","Deduct a few quality points and let price decide","Award, and fix security after signature","Run an e-auction to see if others match the price"],"answer":0,"explain":"Regulatory due diligence works as a gate, not a weight. Under DORA, contracts may only be concluded with providers that meet appropriate security standards, so no price compensates.","t":1031.59},{"after":84,"q":"Nordvia buys IT consulting. The consultants will log into the policy administration system and change configurations. How should you classify it, to confirm with Compliance?","options":["Not relevant, because consulting is a professional service","Solvency II outsourcing only","Likely an ICT service under DORA, with agency-work rules to check","Facilities services"],"answer":2,"explain":"Whether consulting is an ICT service depends on access and use. Consultants working inside core systems are likely an ICT service, which is why the assessment is documented case by case.","t":1121.62},{"after":86,"q":"Northbyte's exit plan is a well-written document that names two alternative providers but has never been tested. Does it meet DORA for a critical function?","options":["Yes, a written plan is enough","Yes, if the supplier signs it","Only if a live migration has been run","No; an untested plan does not meet DORA, but a desk-based walk-through is a valid first test"],"answer":3,"explain":"Article 28, paragraph 8, requires exit strategies that are documented, tested and reviewed. Testing can start as a desk-based walk-through and grow from there.","t":1325.82},{"after":89,"q":"Your cleaning Sourcing Case is not outsourcing of a critical function and not an ICT service. Which part of the regulatory checklist still matters most?","options":["None, so you can skip the checklist","The GDPR data processing and staff transfer lines","The DORA threat-led penetration testing clause","Supervisor notification"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cleaning and security typically involve staff transfer rules and personal data, such as staff and access records. Those lines apply even when Solvency II outsourcing and DORA do not.","t":1497.32}],"cues":[[0.6,1.4,"Welcome back."],[1.4,6.02,"This lesson covers module 4.4 of Session 4: contracts in a regulated insurer."],[6.02,12.61,"In the last lesson we built a contract that protects the value of the deal."],[12.61,16.43,"Now we add a layer that most other industries do not have."],[16.71,26.67,"As an insurer, the Group must meet the Solvency II rules on outsourcing, and DORA, the Digital Operational Resilience Act, for ICT services."],[26.67,34.95,"Both reach right into Source to Contract: into intake, strategy, the RFx, evaluation and the contract itself."],[35.45,37.73,"Before we start, an important framing."],[37.73,42.32,"Everything in this module is orientation, not legal advice."],[42.32,49.76,"The aim is that you recognise when these rules apply, ask the right questions early, and know which clauses to expect."],[49.76,56.36,"For each actual contract, Legal, Compliance and the owner of the DORA register confirm what applies."],[56.64,57.89,"Here is the plan."],[57.89,63.37,"We start with one principle about non-negotiable clauses."],[63.37,65.43,"Then we classify."],[65.43,71.59,"Then Solvency II, Article 49 of the Directive and Article 274 of the Delegated Regulation."],[71.59,73.98,"Then DORA, Articles 28 and 30."],[73.98,78.74,"Then a word on the EIOPA cloud guidelines, which are withdrawn."],[79.02,91.26,"We then map regulation onto the S2C steps, classify the Nordvia pipeline, look at the Northbyte exit gap, and finish with an exit plan that would pass, your own checklist, and a debrief."],[92.21,93.5,"Here is the principle."],[93.5,95.88,"Some clauses are not negotiable."],[95.88,98.76,"Negotiate how they are met, never whether."],[98.76,100.36,"Look at the second line."],[100.36,104.83,"Pooled audits, certifications and reporting formats are negotiable."],[104.83,109.11,"Audit rights, exit and cooperation with supervisors are not."],[109.61,111.33,"What does that mean in practice?"],[111.33,117.1,"Imagine a software supplier telling you: we never accept on-site audits from customers."],[117.1,123.08,"If the service supports a critical or important function, you cannot simply drop the audit right."],[123.08,125.57,"But you can discuss how the right is used."],[125.85,138.99,"For example, you might rely first on independent certifications and audit reports, join a pooled audit with other customers of the same supplier, and agree a reporting format and a notice period for inspections."],[139.27,150.3,"That is exactly how the Kelvar seller in Case 4 was briefed: it dislikes unlimited on-site audits, but accepts the clauses through certifications and pooled audits."],[150.3,152.49,"The right stays in the contract."],[152.49,156.0,"The way it is exercised becomes the negotiation."],[156.0,158.53,"That framing changes the conversation."],[158.81,165.96,"You stop arguing about principle, which the supplier can resist for weeks, and start solving a practical problem together."],[166.91,168.59,"Now, classify first."],[168.59,171.31,"The slide gives five questions, in order."],[171.31,173.73,"First: is it outsourcing?"],[173.73,182.09,"Under Solvency II, outsourcing means a provider performs a function or activity that the insurer would otherwise perform itself."],[182.09,185.81,"Second: is that function critical or important?"],[185.81,188.95,"This is the insurer's own documented assessment."],[189.23,198.62,"Typical examples from supervisory guidance include underwriting, claims handling, risk management, actuarial work and data storage."],[198.62,201.02,"Third: is it an ICT service under DORA?"],[201.02,203.04,"DORA's definition is broad."],[203.04,208.41,"It includes software as a service, cloud, managed IT and data services."],[208.69,214.15,"Fourth: does that ICT service support a critical or important function?"],[214.15,218.75,"If yes, extra DORA clauses apply, and an exit strategy is required."],[218.75,221.46,"Fifth: what about data and people?"],[221.46,225.33,"Personal data means a GDPR data processing agreement."],[225.33,229.23,"Staff transfer rules may apply in cleaning and security."],[229.51,233.39,"Agency-work rules may apply to IT staff augmentation."],[233.89,239.55,"This is the triage you met in Session 1, now confirmed with the people who own it."],[239.55,245.28,"Most cleaning, catering, agency and travel contracts are usually not critical outsourcing."],[245.28,248.05,"IT services and cloud may well be."],[248.05,249.21,"And why first?"],[249.49,261.25,"Because classification changes everything downstream: the notification lead time, the clauses in the draft contract, the knock-out criteria in the RFx, and the due diligence."],[261.25,265.74,"Discover it at contract stage, and you may have to reopen the whole event."],[266.24,271.45,"Let me give you a quick example of why the order of the questions matters."],[271.45,275.93,"A Nordvia HR team wants a new payroll platform delivered as software as a service."],[275.93,277.43,"Is it outsourcing?"],[277.43,285.11,"If the provider also runs the payroll calculations that Nordvia's own team used to do, quite possibly yes."],[285.11,286.76,"Is it critical or important?"],[287.04,290.26,"That depends on the insurer's own assessment."],[290.26,291.68,"Is it an ICT service?"],[291.68,294.81,"Certainly, because it is software as a service."],[294.81,296.14,"Personal data?"],[296.14,298.2,"Obviously, salaries and bank details."],[298.2,306.39,"Five questions, and in ten minutes you know which experts must join the project, and which clauses the draft contract needs."],[307.35,311.37,"Let's start with Solvency II, Article 49 of the Directive."],[311.37,314.12,"Three rules on the slide."],[314.12,318.93,"Full responsibility: the insurer remains fully responsible for outsourced functions."],[318.93,321.19,"You can outsource the work."],[321.19,323.67,"You cannot outsource the accountability."],[323.95,329.69,"If a provider handling claims fails, the supervisor talks to the insurer, not to the provider."],[329.97,349.47,"Four conditions: outsourcing a critical or important function must not materially harm the quality of governance, must not unduly increase operational risk, must not impair the supervisor's ability to monitor compliance, and must not undermine continuous and satisfactory service to policyholders."],[349.75,356.72,"And prior notification: the supervisor is notified before a critical or important function is outsourced."],[357.22,359.96,"The note has the practical consequence for us."],[359.96,363.99,"Notification lead time must be in the sourcing timeline."],[363.99,368.56,"Picture a Nordvia project where everyone assumes a managed service is routine."],[368.56,374.39,"Two weeks before the planned start, Compliance classifies it as critical outsourcing."],[374.67,380.63,"Now the supervisor must be notified before the service begins, and the start date moves."],[380.63,383.39,"A late classification can delay the start date."],[383.39,386.94,"An early one costs a single conversation at intake."],[387.89,395.71,"Article 274 of the Solvency II Delegated Regulation sets out what the written agreement with the provider must contain."],[395.71,397.24,"Look at the table."],[397.24,406.34,"Duties and compliance: the provider's responsibilities, and its commitment to comply with the law and with the insurer's policies."],[406.62,413.81,"Supervisor: the provider cooperates with the supervisor, and the supervisor may question the provider directly."],[413.81,423.67,"Continuity: notice periods long enough for the insurer to find an alternative, and termination without detriment to the continuity and quality of service."],[423.95,429.95,"Access: information and premises open to the insurer, its auditor and the supervisor."],[429.95,435.92,"And sub-outsourcing: conditions for passing work on, with the provider remaining responsible."],[436.42,438.25,"Notice the continuity line."],[438.25,446.53,"A thirty-day termination notice may look flexible, but if replacing the provider would take a year, it gives you no real protection."],[446.53,451.1,"The test is whether you could find and move to an alternative in time."],[451.1,454.79,"And the note adds two requirements that sit outside the contract."],[455.07,463.38,"Article 274 also requires a written outsourcing policy, and a detailed examination of the provider before selection."],[463.38,468.58,"That examination is your due diligence step in the sourcing process."],[468.58,471.28,"It happens before award, not after."],[471.78,474.21,"How does this look in a real sourcing file?"],[474.21,479.5,"Suppose Nordvia outsources part of its claims handling to a specialist provider."],[479.5,486.61,"The outsourcing policy tells you who must approve, which assessment is needed and what the contract must contain."],[486.89,497.13,"The detailed examination covers the provider's financial strength, its capacity, its controls, its business continuity and any conflicts of interest."],[497.13,504.1,"Write the result down in the award recommendation, in the risk and regulation section we met in the last lesson."],[504.38,511.27,"Then the approvers can see that the examination happened before the selection, and the supervisor can see it later too."],[512.22,517.62,"Now DORA, Regulation 2022 2554, which has applied since 17 January 2025."],[517.62,523.39,"Article 28 covers what happens before and around the contract."],[523.39,531.3,"Article 28, paragraph 3: a register of information covering all ICT contractual arrangements."],[531.3,535.2,"Every one, not just the critical ones."],[535.48,540.89,"The templates for the register are set out in an Implementing Regulation from 2024."],[540.89,543.72,"Paragraph 4: a pre-contract assessment."],[543.72,547.74,"Does the service support a critical or important function?"],[547.74,550.87,"What are the risks, including concentration risk?"],[550.87,552.22,"Has due diligence been done?"],[552.22,554.61,"Are there conflicts of interest?"],[554.89,561.23,"Paragraph 5: contract only with providers that meet appropriate information security standards."],[561.23,570.34,"Paragraph 7: mandatory termination rights, for example for a serious breach or where the provider's situation impedes supervision."],[570.62,579.04,"And paragraph 8: exit strategies for services supporting critical or important functions, documented, tested and reviewed."],[579.54,582.25,"Read paragraph 4 with procurement eyes."],[582.25,592.63,"A pre-contract assessment of risk, concentration and due diligence is, in our language, the market analysis, the strategy and the supplier qualification."],[592.63,594.67,"DORA does not invent new steps."],[594.67,598.33,"It makes existing good practice mandatory and auditable."],[598.61,602.0,"And concentration risk is a real question for us."],[602.0,609.67,"If several entities and several critical services depend on the same provider, a single failure hits all of them at once."],[610.62,616.75,"Article 30, paragraph 2, lists what every ICT contract must contain, critical or not."],[616.75,621.94,"Services: a clear description, and the conditions for subcontracting."],[621.94,627.86,"Locations: where the services run and where data is stored, with notice of any change."],[628.14,637.59,"Data: availability, integrity and confidentiality, plus access to the data and its return, including on insolvency or termination."],[637.59,641.28,"Service levels: a description, including updates."],[641.56,651.24,"Incidents and authorities: assistance with ICT incidents at no cost or at a cost agreed in advance, and cooperation with the competent authorities."],[651.24,661.46,"And exit and training: termination rights with notice periods, and the provider's participation in the insurer's security-awareness programmes."],[661.96,664.0,"Two practical points from the note."],[664.0,670.12,"First, the contract must be one written document, under Article 30, paragraph 1."],[670.12,676.41,"Not a master agreement plus a supplier's online terms that change whenever the supplier likes."],[676.41,681.95,"Second, keep these clauses as Coupa CLM templates, so that nobody redrafts them."],[682.23,687.01,"Think of a marketing team buying a small analytics tool as software as a service."],[687.01,689.38,"Small value, not critical."],[689.38,695.75,"It is still an ICT service, so it still needs a register entry and these baseline clauses."],[695.75,699.91,"The template makes that a ten-minute task instead of a legal project."],[700.42,703.1,"One more angle on the locations clause."],[703.1,706.54,"Location is not only a data protection question."],[706.54,715.5,"It also determines which law applies to the data, how quickly a provider can restore a service, and whether the supervisor can actually inspect."],[715.78,723.63,"A supplier that wants the freedom to move processing anywhere without telling you is asking you to give up something DORA expects you to keep."],[724.58,731.64,"Where the ICT service supports a critical or important function, Article 30, paragraph 3, adds six clauses."],[731.64,739.67,"Service levels: precise quantitative and qualitative performance targets, so the insurer can take corrective action."],[739.67,745.81,"Reporting: notice and reporting obligations on developments that may affect the service."],[746.09,750.29,"Continuity: business contingency plans and security measures."],[750.29,755.38,"Testing: participation in the insurer's threat-led penetration testing."],[755.38,759.95,"Audit: unrestricted rights of access, inspection and audit."],[759.95,765.14,"And exit: an exit strategy with a mandatory, adequate transition period."],[765.64,776.48,"The note mentions two Delegated Regulations, from 2024 and from 2025, which add detail on the contractual policy and on subcontracting for these functions."],[776.48,779.61,"Your Legal colleagues will know them."],[779.61,784.11,"What you need to remember is the practical point from Case 4."],[784.39,790.01,"The Kelvar software supports critical functions, so these clauses were not negotiable."],[790.01,797.79,"You could negotiate price, uplift caps, term and volume, but not whether the audit and exit clauses are in the contract."],[798.29,801.7,"Think about what each of these means for the sourcing timeline."],[801.7,809.4,"Threat-led penetration testing and unrestricted audit rights are exactly the clauses suppliers push back on hardest."],[809.4,814.5,"If they first appear after award, you negotiate them with no competition left."],[814.78,825.13,"If they are in the draft contract attached to the RFP, every bidder has already said yes, no, or yes with conditions, and you can evaluate those answers."],[825.13,830.44,"The same logic we saw in module 4.3 applies even more strongly here."],[831.39,833.37,"A short but important slide."],[833.37,837.28,"The EIOPA cloud guidelines have been withdrawn."],[837.28,838.01,"On the left, then."],[838.01,846.5,"EIOPA's Guidelines on outsourcing to cloud service providers were published in 2020 and applied from 2021."],[846.5,851.61,"They asked for a cloud register, a pre-outsourcing analysis and exit planning."],[851.89,856.14,"They are often still quoted in old templates and old supplier questionnaires."],[856.64,857.86,"On the right, now."],[857.86,870.15,"EIOPA announced on 19 December 2024 that it was withdrawing them, with effect from 17 January 2025, the day DORA started to apply, to avoid overlap with DORA."],[870.15,878.71,"So for ICT and cloud, the regime is DORA, plus Solvency II where the arrangement is also critical outsourcing."],[878.71,880.73,"The action for you is simple."],[881.01,886.33,"If a template or a questionnaire cites the old cloud guidelines, flag it for update."],[886.33,894.21,"And never present the cloud guidelines as current, in a strategy paper, a recommendation or a conversation with a supplier."],[894.71,897.53,"Why does this deserve its own slide?"],[897.53,900.0,"Because old guidance has a long afterlife."],[900.0,908.76,"A template drafted in 2021 is copied into a 2023 contract, which becomes the starting point for a 2026 renewal."],[908.76,913.29,"Each time, someone assumes the references were checked."],[913.57,923.87,"In a regulated insurer, citing a withdrawn guideline as the basis for a clause does not make the clause wrong, but it signals to an auditor that nobody looked."],[923.87,928.5,"A five-minute check of the references in your templates is time well spent."],[929.46,936.05,"Now let's map regulation across the S2C steps, because this is where procurement adds real value."],[936.05,940.72,"At intake, classify early and note the notification lead time."],[940.72,945.7,"In the market step, look at concentration risk and subcontracting chains."],[945.7,947.53,"Who does your provider depend on?"],[947.81,954.05,"In strategy, make exit feasibility a criterion, and consider more than one vendor where relevant."],[954.05,961.39,"In the RFx, put the mandatory clauses into the draft contract, and make acceptance of audit rights a knock-out."],[961.39,965.88,"In evaluation, treat due diligence as a gate, not a weight."],[966.16,973.71,"And at contract and supplier relationship management, the register entry, the exit plan, monitoring and exit tests."],[974.21,976.11,"Why a gate and not a weight?"],[976.11,980.62,"Because a weight lets a cheap bid buy its way past a security failure."],[980.62,985.99,"If a bidder cannot meet the security standard DORA requires, no price makes it acceptable."],[986.27,997.76,"In Coupa, the practical tip is to build classification questions into the intake form, and DORA questionnaires into the supplier onboarding forms in supplier information management."],[997.76,1002.76,"Exactly how your tenant is configured, confirm with your Coupa administrators."],[1003.25,1005.65,"Let me make the strategy line concrete."],[1005.65,1015.58,"For a claims IT managed service, exit feasibility as a criterion means asking, already in the strategy paper, how we would leave this provider."],[1015.86,1026.63,"If the honest answer is that nobody else could take over in less than two years, that shapes the term, the exit clauses and perhaps the decision to split the service into lots."],[1026.63,1031.58,"Regulation, here, simply forces a question good procurement should ask anyway."],[1032.54,1034.87,"Let's classify the Nordvia pipeline."],[1034.87,1039.5,"This is fictional, and in real life it would be confirmed by Compliance."],[1039.5,1046.28,"P1, headquarters cleaning: not outsourcing of a critical function, but staff transfer rules apply."],[1046.56,1055.78,"P2, IT consulting: an ICT service if the consultants' staff access Nordvia systems, and agency-work rules may apply."],[1055.78,1062.98,"P3, Kelvar: an ICT service supporting critical functions, so Article 30, paragraph 3, applies."],[1062.98,1069.68,"P4, Telmo: an ICT service used by the claims systems, so its criticality must be assessed."],[1069.96,1078.6,"And P8, Northbyte: critical ICT, and likely critical outsourcing too, so both Solvency II and DORA apply."],[1079.1,1082.38,"Now challenge P2 and P4, as the note suggests."],[1082.38,1085.5,"Is IT consulting always an ICT service?"],[1085.5,1091.57,"If consultants only write a strategy document from their own laptops, perhaps not."],[1091.57,1098.53,"If they log into the policy administration system and change configurations, very likely yes."],[1098.81,1104.9,"And Telmo: if the telecom lines carry claims traffic, is that supporting a critical function?"],[1104.9,1109.59,"It depends on how the service is used and on what happens if it fails."],[1109.59,1112.81,"That is exactly why the assessment is documented."],[1113.09,1121.62,"The answer depends on facts about access and use, and two reasonable people can start with different answers until those facts are on the table."],[1122.57,1126.02,"Now Case 4, part 2: the Northbyte exit gap."],[1126.02,1133.5,"Northbyte runs Nordvia's claims IT as a managed service, at 19.4 million euros a year."],[1133.5,1135.77,"The contract runs to December 2027."],[1135.77,1137.11,"It is in the DORA register."],[1137.11,1140.62,"And there is no tested exit plan."],[1140.62,1143.33,"Look at the four gaps on the slide."],[1143.83,1148.96,"Gap one: no documented exit strategy, as Article 28, paragraph 8, requires."],[1148.96,1153.88,"Gap two: the alternatives and the transition plan have not been identified."],[1153.88,1157.59,"Who could take over, and how long would it take?"],[1157.87,1167.03,"Gap three: the contract may lack the Article 30, paragraph 3, exit and transition clauses, because it was signed before DORA applied."],[1167.03,1170.8,"Gap four: the renewal timing leaves no room for a tested exit."],[1170.8,1178.41,"If the plan were tested in late 2027 and showed you need eighteen months to move, the test would come too late."],[1178.69,1187.16,"And the open question at the bottom: does a renewal, or a material change to the contract, trigger a Solvency II notification?"],[1187.16,1189.08,"That is one for Compliance."],[1189.58,1193.95,"In the classroom, participants discuss this in pairs for five minutes."],[1193.95,1195.46,"Do it yourself now."],[1195.46,1199.91,"What has to happen before the end of 2027, and in what order?"],[1200.19,1215.55,"A reasonable sequence is: document the exit strategy, identify the alternatives, review the contract against the required clauses, plan the renewal or re-tender early enough for a tested exit, and ask Compliance about notification."],[1215.83,1222.52,"Record your actions in the Case 4 exercise in your workbook, and if you can, involve a Compliance colleague."],[1223.48,1227.57,"So what does an exit plan that would pass look like?"],[1227.57,1228.53,"Five elements."],[1228.53,1233.34,"Triggers: termination, insolvency, service failure, regulatory instruction."],[1233.34,1239.41,"Alternatives: named options, such as other providers or bringing the service back in house."],[1239.41,1240.12,"Not the market."],[1240.12,1241.29,"Named options."],[1241.57,1246.23,"Transition: an adequate period, clear roles and the supplier's assistance."],[1246.23,1253.54,"Data and knowledge: return of data, the formats, documentation, and the knowledge held by the provider's staff."],[1253.54,1258.43,"Testing: a test schedule, the last test result and the next review date."],[1258.93,1260.3,"The note is blunt."],[1260.3,1266.33,"An exit plan that has never been tested does not meet DORA for critical or important functions."],[1266.33,1269.8,"But testing does not have to start with a live migration."],[1269.8,1272.57,"It can start with a desk-based walk-through."],[1272.57,1277.4,"Put the business owner, IT, the contract owner and Compliance in a room."],[1277.68,1282.15,"Simulate the trigger: Northbyte has just announced it is leaving the market."],[1282.15,1284.36,"Walk through the plan step by step."],[1284.36,1285.52,"Where does it break?"],[1285.52,1286.71,"Who calls whom?"],[1286.71,1288.91,"Where is the data, and in what format?"],[1288.91,1293.07,"You will learn more in two hours than from a year of reading the document."],[1293.56,1296.25,"Why are named alternatives so important?"],[1296.25,1298.41,"Because the market is not an exit plan."],[1298.41,1302.82,"Saying there are many providers out there tells a supervisor nothing."],[1303.1,1313.98,"Saying that two named providers have the capability, that one of them answered an RFI last year, and that insourcing was costed as a fallback, tells them the plan is real."],[1313.98,1317.65,"And notice how this links back to negotiation."],[1317.65,1320.17,"A tested exit plan is also your BATNA."],[1320.45,1325.82,"The provider that knows you can leave negotiates differently from the one that knows you cannot."],[1326.77,1328.37,"Time for a reflection."],[1328.37,1334.65,"The poll asks: does your own Sourcing Case fall under DORA or Solvency II outsourcing?"],[1334.65,1345.16,"The options are a DORA ICT service, DORA supporting a critical or important function, Solvency II outsourcing, none, or not yet classified."],[1345.16,1346.29,"Pick your answer."],[1346.57,1350.96,"If it is not yet classified, that is your first action after this lesson."],[1351.92,1358.9,"Here is the exercise: Practice 4.4 in your workbook, your regulatory annex checklist, about twelve minutes."],[1358.9,1363.26,"First, classify your project on the six questions in the workbook."],[1363.26,1372.63,"Then check eight core clauses against your draft contract, from services and subcontracting through to the exit strategy and transition period."],[1372.91,1382.86,"Mark each one as in, partly, missing or not applicable, and note what requires it: DORA, Solvency II, the GDPR, Group policy or good practice."],[1382.86,1385.13,"Name the owner of each gap."],[1385.13,1387.04,"And book the Legal or Compliance review."],[1387.04,1391.79,"If your project is cleaning or marketing, do not skip it."],[1391.79,1395.19,"You still check the GDPR and staff transfer lines."],[1395.47,1401.53,"Pause the video now if you want to do the exercise in the Modules tab, and continue when you are ready."],[1402.02,1403.55,"A tip for doing it well."],[1403.55,1405.84,"Do not tick boxes from memory."],[1405.84,1412.47,"Open the actual draft contract, or the supplier's paper if that is all you have, and find each clause."],[1412.47,1416.55,"If you cannot find it in two minutes, mark it as missing or partly."],[1416.83,1424.15,"That honesty is what makes the review with Legal or Compliance useful, because it shows them exactly where to spend their time."],[1425.1,1426.08,"Welcome back."],[1426.08,1427.9,"Four debrief questions."],[1427.9,1432.17,"First, whose project turned out to be an ICT service under DORA?"],[1432.17,1433.34,"Often it is a surprise."],[1433.34,1440.22,"A marketing analytics tool, an HR platform, a print management service with an online portal."],[1440.22,1444.28,"Second, which clause is most often missing from supplier paper?"],[1444.56,1449.42,"In practice, the usual suspects are data location, exit and audit rights."],[1449.92,1454.97,"Third, where will audit rights be hard to negotiate, and how will you meet them?"],[1454.97,1458.8,"Go back to the principle of this module: negotiate how, never whether."],[1458.8,1462.49,"Certifications, pooled audits and agreed formats."],[1462.49,1465.59,"Fourth, who owns the register entry for your contract?"],[1465.59,1468.05,"If you do not know, find out this week."],[1468.33,1472.51,"A contract without a register entry is a finding waiting to happen."],[1473.02,1477.5,"A word on why data location, exit and audit rights are the usual gaps."],[1477.5,1489.96,"Supplier paper is written to protect the supplier, and these three clauses limit the supplier's freedom: where it can run the service, how easily the customer can leave, and who can look inside."],[1489.96,1491.9,"None of that is bad faith."],[1492.18,1497.32,"It is simply why you start from the Group template, with the regulatory clauses already in."],[1498.27,1501.57,"Let's recap module 4.4 in three lines."],[1501.57,1505.7,"First, classify early with the outsourcing and DORA owners."],[1505.7,1507.25,"Is it outsourcing?"],[1507.25,1508.24,"Is it critical or important?"],[1508.24,1513.47,"Is it an ICT service, and does it support a critical function?"],[1513.47,1517.7,"The answers drive the timeline, the clauses and the due diligence."],[1517.98,1523.7,"Second, the required clauses go into the draft contract, not into the negotiation."],[1523.7,1530.39,"Put them in the RFx, make acceptance a knock-out, and negotiate only how they are met."],[1530.39,1533.19,"Third, the EIOPA cloud guidelines are withdrawn."],[1533.19,1535.95,"DORA and Solvency II apply."],[1536.44,1538.22,"Let me add the bigger picture."],[1538.22,1542.39,"These rules can feel like a burden on procurement, one more set of boxes."],[1542.39,1555.41,"But look at what they actually ask for: knowing whether a service matters, checking the provider before you choose it, writing down what you need in one contract, keeping access to your data, and being able to leave."],[1555.69,1557.22,"That is good sourcing."],[1557.22,1565.88,"Regulation makes it non-optional, and it gives procurement a strong argument with suppliers and with internal stakeholders who want to skip steps."],[1565.88,1570.53,"When a supplier pushes back on an exit clause, you are not being difficult."],[1570.53,1574.25,"You are meeting a legal requirement the Group cannot waive."],[1574.75,1576.96,"And one reminder, as at the start."],[1576.96,1580.12,"This module is orientation only."],[1580.12,1583.94,"Always confirm with Legal and Compliance for your actual contract."],[1583.94,1592.86,"In the next lesson we move from signature to value: how to hand the contract over so that it is used, measured and renewed on purpose."],[1593.14,1595.78,"Then the capstone briefing and the close of the course."],[1595.78,1596.79,"See you there."]]},{"id":"s4-l6","session":"s4","title":"Handover, capstone and course close","summary":"Hand a signed contract over so that it is used, measured and renewed on purpose, prepare the capstone Sourcing Case, and connect every decision in the Source to Contract chain.","from":91,"to":112,"audio":"audio/s4-l6.mp3","duration":1461.5,"segs":[[91,0.6,20.7,"Welcome back. This is the last lesson of Session 4, and of the Source to Contract course. It has three parts. First, module 4.5: handover, from contract to value. Then the briefing for your capstone, My Sourcing Case. And finally, a recap of the whole course, the decision chain from need to contract."],[91,21.2,56.59,"Look at the subtitle on this slide. A contract nobody buys against is a document, not a deal. Everything we did in the last four sessions, the category analysis, the strategy, the event, the negotiation and the contract, creates the possibility of value. Whether that value actually arrives depends on what happens after signature. In this module we look at mobilisation, at making the contract live in Coupa, at governance and how much of it each supplier deserves, at savings that Finance recognises, at renewals, and at the first ninety days."],[92,57.54,97.38,"Here is the statement. Value is realised in the months after signature, or lost there. Think about that for a moment. Can you name a good contract in your entity that delivered nothing, because no one used it? Most of us can. A travel agreement with excellent negotiated rates, while half the employees kept booking on public websites. A print framework that nobody in the regional offices knew existed. A marketing agency panel where one brand manager kept calling the agency she had always used. In each case, procurement reported a saving, and the budget never saw it. The contract was fine. The handover was missing."],[92,97.88,117.48,"Why does this happen so often? Because the sourcing team moves on to the next project the day after signature, and the business assumes Procurement will make the contract work. Each side thinks the other owns the handover. So nobody does. This module is about closing that gap on purpose, with a named owner, a plan and dates."],[93,118.43,138.36,"A quick reflection. The poll asks: where do signed contracts most often lose value in your entity? Nobody buys against them, no KPIs or reviews, savings never reach the budget, or silent roll-over at renewal. Pick the one you see most often. Each of these four has an answer in the next few slides."],[94,139.31,163.97,"Let's start with mobilisation, because the focus differs a lot by category. Look at the table. For cleaning and security: staff transfer, site induction, equipment, and a KPI baseline in month one. The baseline matters. If you do not measure performance in the first month, you have nothing to compare against in month six, and every service discussion becomes opinion against opinion."],[94,164.47,210.39,"For IT services: knowledge transfer, access rights, the DORA register entry, and naming the owner of the exit plan. Notice that last point. We just saw in module 4.4 that exit plans must be tested and reviewed. If nobody owns the plan from day one, it will not be tested. For panels: onboard every member, set up the call-off templates, and run the first mini-competitions quickly, so the business sees the panel working. For a software renewal: a rightsized user list, accurate licence records, and the dates for uplifts and volume reductions. If you negotiated the right to reduce volume by ten percent a year, as in the Kelvar case, someone has to put that date in the calendar and exercise it."],[94,210.89,237.6,"And the note on this slide: the incumbent's cooperation during transition is a contract obligation, not a favour. Think of the cleaning tender. Bidder D replaces the incumbent. The incumbent has just lost the business. How motivated is it to hand over accurate staff data, keys, and site knowledge? That is exactly why exit clauses matter. You wrote them into the last contract so that this transition would work."],[95,238.55,271.45,"Now, the contract live in Coupa. Four cards. The CLM record: the contract linked to the supplier, with date alerts. The buying channel: a catalogue, a punch-out to the supplier's site, or contract-backed requisitions, so that employees find the contract when they buy. Price lists: invoices validated against the contracted prices, so that a price creeping up shows as an exception instead of being paid. And supplier data: the supplier onboarded and kept current in supplier information management."],[95,271.95,299.02,"The principle behind all four is simple. Make the contract the easy way to buy. If an employee needs three emails to find out how to order from the new supplier, and one click to order from the old one, you know what will happen. Coupa describes contracts linked to purchasing so that employees buy against current terms. Which channels are actually set up in your entity, confirm with your Coupa administrators before you promise them to the business."],[95,299.52,333.13,"Let me give you a Nordvia example. The new headquarters cleaning contract has a fixed monthly fee and unit rates for extras such as a deep clean after an office move. If the unit rates are loaded as a price list, the facilities team orders extras through a contract-backed requisition, and an invoice at a higher rate is stopped automatically. If they are not loaded, the extras arrive by email, the invoices are paid on trust, and two years later nobody can say what the contract really cost. Same contract, very different control."],[96,334.08,387.16,"Next, the governance calendar. Three rhythms. Monthly, the contract owner and the supplier look at KPIs, issues and changes. Quarterly, the business owner, Procurement and the supplier's lead review performance, savings, improvement ideas and risks. Yearly, senior sponsors on both sides discuss strategy, the roadmap and the renewal decision. Each level has a different job. The monthly meeting keeps the service running. The quarterly review asks whether the contract is delivering what we sourced it for. The yearly meeting asks whether this is still the right supplier and the right deal. But here is the important note. Only the most important suppliers need all three levels. If you ran quarterly business reviews with every supplier, you would have no time for anything else. Effort follows segmentation, which is the next slide."],[96,387.66,412.75,"What makes these meetings useful rather than ritual? Three habits. Send the data before the meeting, so the time is spent on decisions, not on reading. Keep an action log with owners and dates, and open each meeting with it. And make the supplier present its own improvement ideas at the quarterly review. A supplier that never brings an idea in a strategic relationship is telling you something important."],[97,413.7,426.89,"This is Kraljic again, the matrix you met in Session 1, now used after signature. Supply risk on the vertical axis, profit impact on the horizontal one. Four quadrants, four levels of effort."],[97,427.39,468.44,"Strategic, top right, is highlighted. High risk and high impact. This is where full supplier relationship management belongs: joint plans and executive reviews. Northbyte is a good example. Bottleneck, top left: high risk, lower impact. Secure supply, monitor the risk, and keep alternatives alive. A specialist software tool with a single provider might sit here. Leverage, bottom right: lower risk, high impact. Performance and price reviews, and competition at renewal. Cleaning and security usually sit here. And non-critical, bottom left: automate. A catalogue, minimal reviews, and let the system do the work."],[97,468.94,501.0,"The note brings in O'Brien's point on supplier relationship management. Relationship effort goes where the value and the risk are. Most contracts need contract management, not SRM. That is a relief, not a shortcut. Good contract management, meaning KPIs measured, invoices checked and dates respected, is already a big step for most of a portfolio. Full SRM is reserved for the few suppliers where a closer relationship can create value that a contract alone cannot."],[98,501.95,543.22,"Now, savings that reach the budget. This is where Procurement's credibility with Finance is won or lost. On the left, before the event. Agree the baseline and the method with Finance. Separate price savings, cost avoidance and budget impact. And name who confirms the number. Why separate them? Because they are different things. A price saving lowers what you pay against the baseline. Cost avoidance stops an increase that would otherwise have happened, for example an uplift you refused. Budget impact is what actually changes in next year's budget line. Mixing them is how procurement ends up reporting savings that Finance cannot find."],[98,543.72,585.77,"On the right, after signature. Track the result in the first savings report at day ninety. Compare invoices with contract prices. And the Group planning assumption: about 6 percent per three-year cycle, made up of 2 percent savings plus 4 percent cost avoidance. Let me be precise about that figure, as the note is. The 6 percent is a planning assumption used in the Group, not a benchmark. It helps plan the pipeline. It is not a target you can promise for every category. Credibility with Finance depends on agreeing the method before the event. If Finance agreed the baseline in advance, the number at day ninety is a shared number, not a procurement claim."],[98,586.27,623.78,"Here is an illustrative example. A facilities contract with a baseline of one million euros a year. The new price is 970 thousand euros: a price saving of 30 thousand euros. The incumbent had announced a 4 percent increase, which we refused: that is cost avoidance of 40 thousand euros against the announced price, but it does not lower the budget, because the budget never included it. Report them separately, with the method Finance agreed, and both numbers are credible. Add them together and call them savings, and Finance will discount everything you report next."],[99,624.73,649.05,"Here is a slide I would like you to remember. Never roll over blindly again. Look at the Nordvia pipeline, which is fictional. Telmo: rolled over twice without review. Kelvar: auto-renewed with a 9 percent uplift last time. Security: indexed twice in two years, with no KPIs. Three different contracts, one pattern. Nobody decided. The contract decided for them."],[99,649.55,693.35,"The fix has two parts. First, a renewal decision date, well before every notice deadline, in the sourcing pipeline. Not the expiry date. Not the notice deadline. A decision date, earlier than both. Second, Coupa CLM alerts for options, notice and expiry. And how early is early enough? The note gives the rule. Set the decision date at least as early as the time a re-tender would need. For complex categories, that is often a year or more ahead. Remember Kelvar: notice nine months before renewal, and a migration of eighteen to twenty-four months. A decision taken at the notice deadline leaves you no real alternative. That is exactly how the 9 percent happened."],[100,694.3,725.16,"Now the first ninety days, in three steps. Days zero to thirty: the contract is live in Coupa, the kick-off meeting has happened, transition milestones are tracked, and KPI definitions are agreed in detail. Days thirty-one to sixty: the first KPI report arrives, an issue log is running, and users have moved to the new buying channel. Days sixty-one to ninety: the first review with the supplier, the first savings report with Finance, and lessons learned."],[100,725.66,756.8,"Why ninety days? Because that is roughly when habits form. If users are still ordering the old way after three months, they probably always will. If the first KPI report has not arrived by day sixty, the supplier has learned that nobody is checking. And notice the note. Lessons learned from the sourcing event itself are part of day ninety. What would you change in the next event? Write it down while it is fresh, for the colleague who will run the renewal in three years. That colleague might be you."],[100,757.3,789.29,"Think of the security contract, project P6. Days zero to thirty: the new rate card is loaded in Coupa, the five KPIs are defined with their sources, and the guards are inducted on site. Days thirty-one to sixty: the first KPI report shows shift coverage below target on weekends, and it goes into the issue log. Days sixty-one to ninety: at the first review, the supplier presents a fix, and Finance confirms the first savings figure. That is a contract becoming value."],[101,790.24,828.15,"Here is the exercise: Practice 4.5 in your workbook, your handover plan, about ten minutes. Name the contract owner after signature. Choose the buying channel in Coupa. Plan four activities for the first ninety days, each with an owner and a date. Define three KPIs and how they are measured. And set the renewal decision date, next to the notice deadline. One push from the note: the contract owner must be a named person, not a team. A team cannot be asked why the KPI report is late. Pause the video now if you want to do the exercise in the Modules tab, then continue."],[102,829.1,857.35,"Welcome back. Four debrief questions. Who has no obvious contract owner yet? If that is you, it becomes an action for Fieldwork 4. Which buying channel will you use, and is it actually set up? What is your renewal decision date, and why that date? Can you justify it from the time a re-tender would need? And which KPI would the business owner choose first? If your answer differs from the one you put first, talk to the business owner before you finalise the list."],[102,857.85,883.94,"A practical tip on the renewal date. Work backwards. Take the notice deadline. Subtract the time a re-tender would need: market analysis, strategy, the event, evaluation, approvals and mobilisation. Subtract a margin for the unexpected. That is your decision date. For a simple catalogue category it may be three months before notice. For a claims IT service it may be two years."],[103,884.89,900.4,"Module 4.5 in three lines. Make the contract the easy way to buy. Measure, review, and agree savings with Finance. And decide every renewal on purpose, well before the notice deadline. Now let's turn to the capstone."],[103,900.9,921.38,"Notice that each of these lines answers one of the four poll options from the start of the module. Nobody buys against it: make it the easy way to buy. No KPIs or reviews: measure and review. Savings never reach the budget: agree them with Finance. Silent roll-over: decide every renewal on purpose."],[104,922.33,936.11,"The capstone: My Sourcing Case, about four weeks from today. Throughout the course you have been building a Sourcing Case on a real project from your own entity. The capstone is where you present it, complete, from need to handover."],[105,937.06,973.37,"Before the capstone comes Fieldwork 4. Five steps. Mandate: agree the negotiation one-pager with the business owner. Compliance: review your regulatory checklist with Legal or Compliance. Handover: agree the plan with the future contract owner. Pipeline: put the renewal decision date into the sourcing pipeline. And rehearse: present to your trio and collect feedback. Record your progress in the Fieldwork 4 readiness check. Trios meet at least once before the capstone. A rehearsal with two colleagues who ask hard questions is the best preparation there is."],[105,973.87,996.79,"Why the business owner first? Because the mandate for the negotiation and the handover plan both depend on decisions the business owner has to make: what matters most, what can be traded, and who will own the contract after signature. If you agree those things in Fieldwork 4, the business owner can confirm them in front of the panel, which is exactly what the panel will want to see."],[106,997.74,1031.19,"What to present: ten minutes, plus five minutes of questions. Seven parts, which follow the course. One, the need, the category and the market. Two, your power position: Kraljic, supplier preferencing and the Purchasing Chessboard. Three, the sourcing strategy and the route to market. Four, the event or negotiation design, and the Coupa set-up. Five, the negotiation plan or its outcome. Six, the contract and the regulatory checklist. Seven, the handover, the first ninety days and the renewal date."],[106,1031.69,1055.18,"Ten minutes for seven parts is tight, and that is deliberate. You cannot read your documents to the panel. You have to show the logic that connects them. The panel is the Head of Procurement, a business owner, and a Risk or Compliance colleague. Each will listen for something different: the quality of the decision, whether it serves the business, and whether the risk and regulation are handled."],[107,1056.13,1092.12,"Here is the one rule of the capstone. The tool follows the strategy, never the reverse. Expect the question: why this route to market and not another? If your answer is because we always tender this category, or because Coupa has an auction module, the panel will push back. The answer has to come from your power position and your strategy. And the second line: a Sourcing Case the business owner has not seen does not pass. The business owner is the person who lives with the result. If they have not validated the need, the strategy and the handover, the case is not finished."],[107,1092.62,1119.25,"Here is how a strong answer sounds. For a cleaning case: the category is leverage, there are several capable suppliers, the specification is stable, but quality matters and the work is labour-intensive. So we chose an RFP with a quality threshold and a BAFO round, not an e-auction, which could push bids below sustainable wages. That answer takes thirty seconds, and every word of it comes from the analysis."],[108,1120.2,1158.01,"The assessment has five criteria. Category insight and power, 20 percent. Strategy and route to market, 25 percent. Event and negotiation design, 20 percent. Contract and regulation, 20 percent. And handover and value, 15 percent. The strategy criterion carries most weight because it is the central decision of Source to Contract. A brilliant event built on the wrong strategy is still the wrong event. The full descriptions of what good looks like are on the Capstone page. Read them before your rehearsal, and ask your trio to score you against them."],[108,1158.51,1183.38,"Look at what the weights reward. Handover and value carries 15 percent, which shows that the course takes life after signature seriously. And contract and regulation carries 20 percent, which includes whether your classification is confirmed and the required clauses are in. A case that is strong commercially but never checked the regulation will lose points the panel's Compliance colleague will notice."],[109,1184.33,1203.77,"The poll asks: how ready is your Sourcing Case? All parts drafted, three parts, two or fewer, or I need help. Answer honestly for yourself. If it is two or fewer, that is normal at this stage. Plan the remaining parts across the four weeks now, and if you need help, ask your facilitator this week, not the week before."],[110,1204.72,1234.16,"Now let's step back and look at the whole course. The whole decision chain, in four questions, one per session. Session 1: what are we sourcing? The need, the category, the market and the power position. Session 2: which route to market? Six decisions, captured in the strategy paper. Session 3: how do we run the event? The RFx, evaluation, and e-auctions in Coupa. Session 4: how do we close it well? Negotiate, contract and hand over."],[110,1234.66,1267.18,"The big idea of the course is on this slide. Source to Contract is a decision chain, not a document chain. Each step rests on the one before. A weak analysis gives a weak strategy. A weak strategy gives an event that cannot succeed. A strong event with a weak contract gives value that leaks away. And a strong contract nobody uses gives nothing at all. So here is the question for you, before we finish. Which one decision in this chain will you do differently from tomorrow? Name it to yourself now."],[110,1267.68,1297.48,"Notice how the later sessions keep calling back to the earlier ones. The Kraljic matrix from Session 1 decided how much governance a supplier gets in this lesson. The six decisions from Session 2 told us when an auction or a negotiation fits. The evaluation discipline of Session 3, weights fixed before issue, is what made the sensitivity analysis in the cleaning award meaningful. The chain is not a metaphor. It is how the work really fits together."],[111,1298.43,1305.58,"Here is the course in five lines. These are also the summary for your capstone panel. Let's go through them one by one."],[111,1306.08,1320.31,"One. Analyse the category and the power position first. Spend, need, market, Kraljic, supplier preferencing and the Chessboard. Your leverage is set before any event begins, and you can only use it if you know it."],[111,1320.81,1336.4,"Two. Choose the strategy, then the tool: tender, panel, negotiation or auction. The six decisions in the strategy paper come before the choice of RFx or auction. As we said in Session 2, the tool follows the strategy."],[111,1336.9,1358.9,"Three. Run fair, comparable events, and auction only when the preconditions hold. Weights fixed before issue, a draft contract attached to the RFP, abnormally low bids checked. And, from Session 3, an e-auction only for well-specified categories with enough qualified bidders, never for complex, quality-sensitive services."],[111,1359.4,1375.38,"Four. Negotiate on interests, data and an honest BATNA. That is Fisher and Ury's lesson, and the Kelvar role-play in this session. Prepare on one page, find the other side's interests with good questions, and know your walk-away before you sit down."],[111,1375.88,1397.87,"Five. Contract for regulation and value, and hand over so the contract is used. Price mechanism, KPIs, term and exit to protect the deal. Solvency II and DORA clauses in the draft, not in the negotiation. And a handover with a named owner, a buying channel, savings agreed with Finance and a renewal decision date."],[111,1398.37,1404.94,"If you can say those five lines with an example from your own Sourcing Case for each one, you are ready for the capstone."],[112,1405.89,1422.51,"And the final statement. Good sourcing is a chain of good decisions. Make each one on purpose. Not by habit, not because the tool offers it, not because the contract rolled over. On purpose, with the evidence in front of you and the business owner beside you."],[112,1423.01,1448.72,"Over these four sessions you have collected the tools: the matrices, the strategy paper, the evaluation model, the negotiation one-pager, the regulatory checklist and the handover plan. None of them is the point. Each one exists to help you make one decision well, and to show others why you made it. That is what the capstone panel will look for, and what your business owners will notice long after the course is over."],[112,1449.22,1460.34,"Thank you for your work across these four sessions. Complete Fieldwork 4, rehearse with your trio, check your capstone date and panel, and bring your Sourcing Case. See you at the capstone."]],"questions":[{"after":94,"q":"Nordvia's new cleaning supplier starts next month, replacing the incumbent. Which mobilisation action matters most to avoid a service discussion based on opinions in month six?","options":["Set the KPI baseline in month one","Wait for the first quarterly review","Ask the incumbent informally for help","Skip site induction to save time"],"answer":0,"explain":"A KPI baseline measured in month one gives a fact base for every later service discussion. Without it, performance reviews become opinion against opinion.","t":237.6},{"after":95,"q":"Employees keep ordering office furniture from an old supplier, even though a new contract with better prices is signed. What is the most effective fix?","options":["Send a reminder email every quarter","Make the contract the easy way to buy, with a catalogue or contract-backed channel in Coupa and price lists loaded","Report the employees to their managers","Renegotiate the contract"],"answer":1,"explain":"Value comes from use. A catalogue, punch-out or contract-backed requisition with loaded price lists makes the new contract the easiest path and lets invoices be checked against contracted prices.","t":333.13},{"after":97,"q":"Which supplier most deserves full supplier relationship management with joint plans and executive reviews?","options":["The office supplies catalogue supplier","The headquarters cleaning supplier","Northbyte, the claims IT managed service","A one-off event venue"],"answer":2,"explain":"Full SRM goes where value and risk are both high, the strategic quadrant. Most other contracts need good contract management, scaled to their Kraljic position.","t":501.0},{"after":98,"q":"A supplier announced a 4 percent increase, which you refused, and you also cut the price by 3 percent. How do you report this to Finance?","options":["As 7 percent savings","Only the refused increase","As savings plus avoidance, combined into one figure","Separately: the price saving and the cost avoidance, using the method agreed with Finance before the event"],"answer":3,"explain":"Price savings, cost avoidance and budget impact are different things. Reporting them separately, on a method Finance agreed in advance, keeps the numbers credible.","t":623.78},{"after":99,"q":"A complex IT contract has a notice deadline in twelve months, and a re-tender would take about eighteen months. When should the renewal decision date have been?","options":["At the notice deadline","At least as early as a re-tender would need, so already six months or more in the past","At contract expiry","One month before the notice deadline"],"answer":1,"explain":"The decision date must leave time for a real alternative. Taking the decision at the notice deadline, as happened with the Kelvar auto-renewal, leaves no BATNA.","t":693.35},{"after":101,"q":"Your business owner proposes that the contract owner after signature is the Facilities team. What do you do?","options":["Accept, because a team gives cover during holidays","Leave it to the supplier","Ask for a named person as contract owner, with the team supporting","Make Procurement the owner by default"],"answer":2,"explain":"A named person can be held to the KPI reports, the reviews and the renewal date. A team as owner usually means no owner.","t":828.15},{"after":107,"q":"At the capstone, a panel member asks why you chose an e-auction for your category. Which answer meets the capstone rule?","options":["Because Coupa offers an auction module","Because we always auction this category","Because auctions usually give the biggest savings","Because the category is well specified, has enough qualified bidders and is price-led, as the power analysis and strategy show"],"answer":3,"explain":"The tool follows the strategy. The answer must come from the power position and the preconditions, not from the tool or from habit.","t":1119.25},{"after":108,"q":"Which capstone criterion carries the most weight, and why?","options":["Strategy and route to market, because it is the central decision of Source to Contract","Event and negotiation design, because it creates the savings","Handover and value, because it comes last","Contract and regulation, because it is mandatory"],"answer":0,"explain":"Strategy and route to market carries 25 percent. A brilliant event built on the wrong strategy is still the wrong event.","t":1183.38},{"after":111,"q":"Looking back at the whole course, which sequence reflects the Source to Contract decision chain?","options":["Choose the tool, run the event, then analyse the category","Analyse category and power, choose the strategy, run a fair event, negotiate, then contract and hand over","Negotiate first, then decide the strategy","Sign the contract, then define KPIs at renewal"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each decision rests on the one before: analysis, strategy, event, negotiation, then contract and handover. That is why S2C is a decision chain, not a document chain.","t":1404.94}],"cues":[[0.6,1.41,"Welcome back."],[1.41,6.73,"This is the last lesson of Session 4, and of the Source to Contract course."],[6.73,7.75,"It has three parts."],[7.75,11.66,"First, module 4.5: handover, from contract to value."],[11.66,15.78,"Then the briefing for your capstone, My Sourcing Case."],[15.78,20.7,"And finally, a recap of the whole course, the decision chain from need to contract."],[21.2,23.6,"Look at the subtitle on this slide."],[23.6,27.48,"A contract nobody buys against is a document, not a deal."],[27.48,37.73,"Everything we did in the last four sessions, the category analysis, the strategy, the event, the negotiation and the contract, creates the possibility of value."],[38.01,42.34,"Whether that value actually arrives depends on what happens after signature."],[42.34,56.59,"In this module we look at mobilisation, at making the contract live in Coupa, at governance and how much of it each supplier deserves, at savings that Finance recognises, at renewals, and at the first ninety days."],[57.54,58.92,"Here is the statement."],[58.92,63.0,"Value is realised in the months after signature, or lost there."],[63.0,65.39,"Think about that for a moment."],[65.39,70.56,"Can you name a good contract in your entity that delivered nothing, because no one used it?"],[70.56,71.71,"Most of us can."],[71.99,79.15,"A travel agreement with excellent negotiated rates, while half the employees kept booking on public websites."],[79.15,83.44,"A print framework that nobody in the regional offices knew existed."],[83.44,89.52,"A marketing agency panel where one brand manager kept calling the agency she had always used."],[89.8,94.8,"In each case, procurement reported a saving, and the budget never saw it."],[94.8,95.82,"The contract was fine."],[95.82,97.37,"The handover was missing."],[97.88,99.61,"Why does this happen so often?"],[99.61,108.48,"Because the sourcing team moves on to the next project the day after signature, and the business assumes Procurement will make the contract work."],[108.48,110.34,"Each side thinks the other owns the handover."],[110.34,111.7,"So nobody does."],[111.98,117.49,"This module is about closing that gap on purpose, with a named owner, a plan and dates."],[118.43,119.65,"A quick reflection."],[119.65,124.75,"The poll asks: where do signed contracts most often lose value in your entity?"],[124.75,132.96,"Nobody buys against them, no KPIs or reviews, savings never reach the budget, or silent roll-over at renewal."],[132.96,135.17,"Pick the one you see most often."],[135.17,138.36,"Each of these four has an answer in the next few slides."],[139.31,143.86,"Let's start with mobilisation, because the focus differs a lot by category."],[143.86,145.53,"Look at the table."],[145.53,152.65,"For cleaning and security: staff transfer, site induction, equipment, and a KPI baseline in month one."],[152.65,154.22,"The baseline matters."],[154.5,163.97,"If you do not measure performance in the first month, you have nothing to compare against in month six, and every service discussion becomes opinion against opinion."],[164.47,172.59,"For IT services: knowledge transfer, access rights, the DORA register entry, and naming the owner of the exit plan."],[172.59,174.43,"Notice that last point."],[174.43,179.77,"We just saw in module 4.4 that exit plans must be tested and reviewed."],[179.77,183.71,"If nobody owns the plan from day one, it will not be tested."],[183.99,192.83,"For panels: onboard every member, set up the call-off templates, and run the first mini-competitions quickly, so the business sees the panel working."],[192.83,200.87,"For a software renewal: a rightsized user list, accurate licence records, and the dates for uplifts and volume reductions."],[201.15,210.38,"If you negotiated the right to reduce volume by ten percent a year, as in the Kelvar case, someone has to put that date in the calendar and exercise it."],[210.89,218.29,"And the note on this slide: the incumbent's cooperation during transition is a contract obligation, not a favour."],[218.29,219.97,"Think of the cleaning tender."],[219.97,222.51,"Bidder D replaces the incumbent."],[222.51,225.26,"The incumbent has just lost the business."],[225.26,230.41,"How motivated is it to hand over accurate staff data, keys, and site knowledge?"],[230.69,233.5,"That is exactly why exit clauses matter."],[233.5,237.6,"You wrote them into the last contract so that this transition would work."],[238.55,240.76,"Now, the contract live in Coupa."],[240.76,241.3,"Four cards."],[241.3,246.03,"The CLM record: the contract linked to the supplier, with date alerts."],[246.03,256.19,"The buying channel: a catalogue, a punch-out to the supplier's site, or contract-backed requisitions, so that employees find the contract when they buy."],[256.47,265.25,"Price lists: invoices validated against the contracted prices, so that a price creeping up shows as an exception instead of being paid."],[265.25,271.45,"And supplier data: the supplier onboarded and kept current in supplier information management."],[271.95,274.44,"The principle behind all four is simple."],[274.44,277.12,"Make the contract the easy way to buy."],[277.12,285.66,"If an employee needs three emails to find out how to order from the new supplier, and one click to order from the old one, you know what will happen."],[285.66,291.64,"Coupa describes contracts linked to purchasing so that employees buy against current terms."],[291.92,299.02,"Which channels are actually set up in your entity, confirm with your Coupa administrators before you promise them to the business."],[299.52,301.62,"Let me give you a Nordvia example."],[301.62,309.8,"The new headquarters cleaning contract has a fixed monthly fee and unit rates for extras such as a deep clean after an office move."],[310.08,320.64,"If the unit rates are loaded as a price list, the facilities team orders extras through a contract-backed requisition, and an invoice at a higher rate is stopped automatically."],[320.92,330.39,"If they are not loaded, the extras arrive by email, the invoices are paid on trust, and two years later nobody can say what the contract really cost."],[330.39,333.13,"Same contract, very different control."],[334.08,336.02,"Next, the governance calendar."],[336.02,337.32,"Three rhythms."],[337.32,342.98,"Monthly, the contract owner and the supplier look at KPIs, issues and changes."],[342.98,350.66,"Quarterly, the business owner, Procurement and the supplier's lead review performance, savings, improvement ideas and risks."],[350.94,357.35,"Yearly, senior sponsors on both sides discuss strategy, the roadmap and the renewal decision."],[357.35,359.56,"Each level has a different job."],[359.56,361.22,"The monthly meeting keeps the service running."],[361.22,367.09,"The quarterly review asks whether the contract is delivering what we sourced it for."],[367.37,372.02,"The yearly meeting asks whether this is still the right supplier and the right deal."],[372.02,374.3,"But here is the important note."],[374.3,377.47,"Only the most important suppliers need all three levels."],[377.47,383.62,"If you ran quarterly business reviews with every supplier, you would have no time for anything else."],[383.9,387.17,"Effort follows segmentation, which is the next slide."],[387.66,390.58,"What makes these meetings useful rather than ritual?"],[390.58,391.81,"Three habits."],[391.81,397.1,"Send the data before the meeting, so the time is spent on decisions, not on reading."],[397.1,401.45,"Keep an action log with owners and dates, and open each meeting with it."],[401.45,406.5,"And make the supplier present its own improvement ideas at the quarterly review."],[406.78,412.75,"A supplier that never brings an idea in a strategic relationship is telling you something important."],[413.7,419.26,"This is Kraljic again, the matrix you met in Session 1, now used after signature."],[419.26,424.51,"Supply risk on the vertical axis, profit impact on the horizontal one."],[424.51,426.88,"Four quadrants, four levels of effort."],[427.39,429.7,"Strategic, top right, is highlighted."],[429.7,431.81,"High risk and high impact."],[431.81,438.59,"This is where full supplier relationship management belongs: joint plans and executive reviews."],[438.59,440.06,"Northbyte is a good example."],[440.06,443.53,"Bottleneck, top left: high risk, lower impact."],[443.53,447.87,"Secure supply, monitor the risk, and keep alternatives alive."],[448.15,452.7,"A specialist software tool with a single provider might sit here."],[452.7,455.64,"Leverage, bottom right: lower risk, high impact."],[455.64,459.18,"Performance and price reviews, and competition at renewal."],[459.18,461.9,"Cleaning and security usually sit here."],[461.9,464.48,"And non-critical, bottom left: automate."],[464.48,468.44,"A catalogue, minimal reviews, and let the system do the work."],[468.94,473.45,"The note brings in O'Brien's point on supplier relationship management."],[473.45,478.51,"Relationship effort goes where the value and the risk are."],[478.51,481.19,"Most contracts need contract management, not SRM."],[481.19,483.53,"That is a relief, not a shortcut."],[483.81,493.08,"Good contract management, meaning KPIs measured, invoices checked and dates respected, is already a big step for most of a portfolio."],[493.08,501.01,"Full SRM is reserved for the few suppliers where a closer relationship can create value that a contract alone cannot."],[501.95,504.23,"Now, savings that reach the budget."],[504.23,508.76,"This is where Procurement's credibility with Finance is won or lost."],[508.76,510.81,"On the left, before the event."],[510.81,513.81,"Agree the baseline and the method with Finance."],[513.81,517.7,"Separate price savings, cost avoidance and budget impact."],[517.7,520.04,"And name who confirms the number."],[520.04,521.13,"Why separate them?"],[521.41,523.57,"Because they are different things."],[523.57,527.02,"A price saving lowers what you pay against the baseline."],[527.02,533.2,"Cost avoidance stops an increase that would otherwise have happened, for example an uplift you refused."],[533.2,537.56,"Budget impact is what actually changes in next year's budget line."],[537.84,543.21,"Mixing them is how procurement ends up reporting savings that Finance cannot find."],[543.72,545.63,"On the right, after signature."],[545.63,549.44,"Track the result in the first savings report at day ninety."],[549.44,552.31,"Compare invoices with contract prices."],[552.31,560.93,"And the Group planning assumption: about 6 percent per three-year cycle, made up of 2 percent savings plus 4 percent cost avoidance."],[560.93,564.16,"Let me be precise about that figure, as the note is."],[564.44,569.07,"The 6 percent is a planning assumption used in the Group, not a benchmark."],[569.07,570.99,"It helps plan the pipeline."],[570.99,574.42,"It is not a target you can promise for every category."],[574.42,578.86,"Credibility with Finance depends on agreeing the method before the event."],[579.14,585.77,"If Finance agreed the baseline in advance, the number at day ninety is a shared number, not a procurement claim."],[586.27,588.43,"Here is an illustrative example."],[588.43,594.03,"A facilities contract with a baseline of one million euros a year."],[594.03,599.65,"The new price is 970 thousand euros: a price saving of 30 thousand euros."],[599.93,612.66,"The incumbent had announced a 4 percent increase, which we refused: that is cost avoidance of 40 thousand euros against the announced price, but it does not lower the budget, because the budget never included it."],[612.66,618.04,"Report them separately, with the method Finance agreed, and both numbers are credible."],[618.32,623.78,"Add them together and call them savings, and Finance will discount everything you report next."],[624.73,628.06,"Here is a slide I would like you to remember."],[628.06,629.65,"Never roll over blindly again."],[629.65,632.9,"Look at the Nordvia pipeline, which is fictional."],[632.9,636.0,"Telmo: rolled over twice without review."],[636.0,639.57,"Kelvar: auto-renewed with a 9 percent uplift last time."],[639.57,643.47,"Security: indexed twice in two years, with no KPIs."],[643.47,645.74,"Three different contracts, one pattern."],[646.02,647.26,"Nobody decided."],[647.26,649.05,"The contract decided for them."],[649.55,651.15,"The fix has two parts."],[651.15,657.48,"First, a renewal decision date, well before every notice deadline, in the sourcing pipeline."],[657.48,658.76,"Not the expiry date."],[658.76,661.06,"Not the notice deadline."],[661.06,662.82,"A decision date, earlier than both."],[662.82,667.2,"Second, Coupa CLM alerts for options, notice and expiry."],[667.2,669.21,"And how early is early enough?"],[669.21,670.63,"The note gives the rule."],[670.91,675.75,"Set the decision date at least as early as the time a re-tender would need."],[675.75,679.21,"For complex categories, that is often a year or more ahead."],[679.21,685.89,"Remember Kelvar: notice nine months before renewal, and a migration of eighteen to twenty-four months."],[685.89,690.49,"A decision taken at the notice deadline leaves you no real alternative."],[690.77,693.35,"That is exactly how the 9 percent happened."],[694.3,697.1,"Now the first ninety days, in three steps."],[697.1,708.42,"Days zero to thirty: the contract is live in Coupa, the kick-off meeting has happened, transition milestones are tracked, and KPI definitions are agreed in detail."],[708.7,717.49,"Days thirty-one to sixty: the first KPI report arrives, an issue log is running, and users have moved to the new buying channel."],[717.49,725.15,"Days sixty-one to ninety: the first review with the supplier, the first savings report with Finance, and lessons learned."],[725.66,726.84,"Why ninety days?"],[726.84,729.32,"Because that is roughly when habits form."],[729.32,734.61,"If users are still ordering the old way after three months, they probably always will."],[734.61,741.89,"If the first KPI report has not arrived by day sixty, the supplier has learned that nobody is checking."],[741.89,743.2,"And notice the note."],[743.48,747.67,"Lessons learned from the sourcing event itself are part of day ninety."],[747.67,749.82,"What would you change in the next event?"],[749.82,755.06,"Write it down while it is fresh, for the colleague who will run the renewal in three years."],[755.06,756.81,"That colleague might be you."],[757.3,760.05,"Think of the security contract, project P6."],[760.05,770.09,"Days zero to thirty: the new rate card is loaded in Coupa, the five KPIs are defined with their sources, and the guards are inducted on site."],[770.09,778.59,"Days thirty-one to sixty: the first KPI report shows shift coverage below target on weekends, and it goes into the issue log."],[778.87,787.03,"Days sixty-one to ninety: at the first review, the supplier presents a fix, and Finance confirms the first savings figure."],[787.03,789.28,"That is a contract becoming value."],[790.24,796.45,"Here is the exercise: Practice 4.5 in your workbook, your handover plan, about ten minutes."],[796.45,799.09,"Name the contract owner after signature."],[799.09,801.38,"Choose the buying channel in Coupa."],[801.38,806.39,"Plan four activities for the first ninety days, each with an owner and a date."],[806.39,809.48,"Define three KPIs and how they are measured."],[809.76,813.96,"And set the renewal decision date, next to the notice deadline."],[813.96,819.15,"One push from the note: the contract owner must be a named person, not a team."],[819.15,822.62,"A team cannot be asked why the KPI report is late."],[822.62,828.15,"Pause the video now if you want to do the exercise in the Modules tab, then continue."],[829.1,829.86,"Welcome back."],[829.86,830.96,"Four debrief questions."],[830.96,833.39,"Who has no obvious contract owner yet?"],[833.39,837.23,"If that is you, it becomes an action for Fieldwork 4."],[837.23,840.52,"Which buying channel will you use, and is it actually set up?"],[840.52,844.13,"What is your renewal decision date, and why that date?"],[844.13,847.77,"Can you justify it from the time a re-tender would need?"],[848.05,851.3,"And which KPI would the business owner choose first?"],[851.3,857.35,"If your answer differs from the one you put first, talk to the business owner before you finalise the list."],[857.85,860.49,"A practical tip on the renewal date."],[860.49,861.39,"Work backwards."],[861.39,863.18,"Take the notice deadline."],[863.18,871.87,"Subtract the time a re-tender would need: market analysis, strategy, the event, evaluation, approvals and mobilisation."],[871.87,874.23,"Subtract a margin for the unexpected."],[874.23,876.18,"That is your decision date."],[876.46,881.18,"For a simple catalogue category it may be three months before notice."],[881.18,883.94,"For a claims IT service it may be two years."],[884.89,887.57,"Module 4.5 in three lines."],[887.57,890.11,"Make the contract the easy way to buy."],[890.11,893.65,"Measure, review, and agree savings with Finance."],[893.65,898.24,"And decide every renewal on purpose, well before the notice deadline."],[898.24,900.4,"Now let's turn to the capstone."],[900.9,907.78,"Notice that each of these lines answers one of the four poll options from the start of the module."],[907.78,910.61,"Nobody buys against it: make it the easy way to buy."],[910.61,913.68,"No KPIs or reviews: measure and review."],[913.68,917.63,"Savings never reach the budget: agree them with Finance."],[917.63,921.38,"Silent roll-over: decide every renewal on purpose."],[922.33,926.37,"The capstone: My Sourcing Case, about four weeks from today."],[926.37,931.77,"Throughout the course you have been building a Sourcing Case on a real project from your own entity."],[931.77,936.11,"The capstone is where you present it, complete, from need to handover."],[937.06,939.42,"Before the capstone comes Fieldwork 4."],[939.42,940.3,"Five steps."],[940.3,945.03,"Mandate: agree the negotiation one-pager with the business owner."],[945.03,949.74,"Compliance: review your regulatory checklist with Legal or Compliance."],[949.74,952.83,"Handover: agree the plan with the future contract owner."],[952.83,957.65,"Pipeline: put the renewal decision date into the sourcing pipeline."],[957.93,961.42,"And rehearse: present to your trio and collect feedback."],[961.42,965.03,"Record your progress in the Fieldwork 4 readiness check."],[965.03,968.02,"Trios meet at least once before the capstone."],[968.02,973.37,"A rehearsal with two colleagues who ask hard questions is the best preparation there is."],[973.87,975.73,"Why the business owner first?"],[975.73,988.1,"Because the mandate for the negotiation and the handover plan both depend on decisions the business owner has to make: what matters most, what can be traded, and who will own the contract after signature."],[988.38,996.78,"If you agree those things in Fieldwork 4, the business owner can confirm them in front of the panel, which is exactly what the panel will want to see."],[997.74,1001.33,"What to present: ten minutes, plus five minutes of questions."],[1001.33,1003.87,"Seven parts, which follow the course."],[1003.87,1006.85,"One, the need, the category and the market."],[1006.85,1012.36,"Two, your power position: Kraljic, supplier preferencing and the Purchasing Chessboard."],[1012.36,1015.81,"Three, the sourcing strategy and the route to market."],[1016.09,1020.21,"Four, the event or negotiation design, and the Coupa set-up."],[1020.21,1023.07,"Five, the negotiation plan or its outcome."],[1023.07,1026.5,"Six, the contract and the regulatory checklist."],[1026.5,1031.19,"Seven, the handover, the first ninety days and the renewal date."],[1031.69,1035.53,"Ten minutes for seven parts is tight, and that is deliberate."],[1035.53,1038.06,"You cannot read your documents to the panel."],[1038.06,1040.81,"You have to show the logic that connects them."],[1040.81,1046.45,"The panel is the Head of Procurement, a business owner, and a Risk or Compliance colleague."],[1046.73,1055.18,"Each will listen for something different: the quality of the decision, whether it serves the business, and whether the risk and regulation are handled."],[1056.13,1058.56,"Here is the one rule of the capstone."],[1058.56,1062.04,"The tool follows the strategy, never the reverse."],[1062.04,1065.11,"Expect the question: why this route to market and not another?"],[1065.11,1073.54,"If your answer is because we always tender this category, or because Coupa has an auction module, the panel will push back."],[1073.82,1077.52,"The answer has to come from your power position and your strategy."],[1077.52,1083.04,"And the second line: a Sourcing Case the business owner has not seen does not pass."],[1083.04,1086.4,"The business owner is the person who lives with the result."],[1086.4,1092.12,"If they have not validated the need, the strategy and the handover, the case is not finished."],[1092.62,1095.02,"Here is how a strong answer sounds."],[1095.02,1105.23,"For a cleaning case: the category is leverage, there are several capable suppliers, the specification is stable, but quality matters and the work is labour-intensive."],[1105.51,1114.43,"So we chose an RFP with a quality threshold and a BAFO round, not an e-auction, which could push bids below sustainable wages."],[1114.43,1119.25,"That answer takes thirty seconds, and every word of it comes from the analysis."],[1120.2,1122.55,"The assessment has five criteria."],[1122.55,1125.63,"Category insight and power, 20 percent."],[1125.63,1129.05,"Strategy and route to market, 25 percent."],[1129.05,1132.48,"Event and negotiation design, 20 percent."],[1132.48,1135.12,"Contract and regulation, 20 percent."],[1135.12,1137.99,"And handover and value, 15 percent."],[1138.27,1144.53,"The strategy criterion carries most weight because it is the central decision of Source to Contract."],[1144.53,1148.9,"A brilliant event built on the wrong strategy is still the wrong event."],[1148.9,1153.53,"The full descriptions of what good looks like are on the Capstone page."],[1153.81,1158.01,"Read them before your rehearsal, and ask your trio to score you against them."],[1158.51,1160.42,"Look at what the weights reward."],[1160.42,1167.39,"Handover and value carries 15 percent, which shows that the course takes life after signature seriously."],[1167.39,1175.96,"And contract and regulation carries 20 percent, which includes whether your classification is confirmed and the required clauses are in."],[1176.24,1183.39,"A case that is strong commercially but never checked the regulation will lose points the panel's Compliance colleague will notice."],[1184.33,1187.15,"The poll asks: how ready is your Sourcing Case?"],[1187.15,1191.01,"All parts drafted, three parts, two or fewer, or I need help."],[1191.01,1193.51,"Answer honestly for yourself."],[1193.51,1195.95,"If it is two or fewer, that is normal at this stage."],[1195.95,1203.76,"Plan the remaining parts across the four weeks now, and if you need help, ask your facilitator this week, not the week before."],[1204.72,1207.3,"Now let's step back and look at the whole course."],[1207.3,1212.34,"The whole decision chain, in four questions, one per session."],[1212.34,1214.23,"Session 1: what are we sourcing?"],[1214.23,1218.0,"The need, the category, the market and the power position."],[1218.0,1219.78,"Session 2: which route to market?"],[1219.78,1222.62,"Six decisions, captured in the strategy paper."],[1222.62,1225.18,"Session 3: how do we run the event?"],[1225.46,1229.02,"The RFx, evaluation, and e-auctions in Coupa."],[1229.02,1231.69,"Session 4: how do we close it well?"],[1231.69,1234.16,"Negotiate, contract and hand over."],[1234.66,1237.21,"The big idea of the course is on this slide."],[1237.21,1241.4,"Source to Contract is a decision chain, not a document chain."],[1241.4,1243.82,"Each step rests on the one before."],[1243.82,1246.54,"A weak analysis gives a weak strategy."],[1246.54,1250.02,"A weak strategy gives an event that cannot succeed."],[1250.02,1254.22,"A strong event with a weak contract gives value that leaks away."],[1254.5,1258.05,"And a strong contract nobody uses gives nothing at all."],[1258.05,1261.59,"So here is the question for you, before we finish."],[1261.59,1265.57,"Which one decision in this chain will you do differently from tomorrow?"],[1265.57,1267.17,"Name it to yourself now."],[1267.68,1271.69,"Notice how the later sessions keep calling back to the earlier ones."],[1271.69,1278.49,"The Kraljic matrix from Session 1 decided how much governance a supplier gets in this lesson."],[1278.49,1283.51,"The six decisions from Session 2 told us when an auction or a negotiation fits."],[1283.79,1292.7,"The evaluation discipline of Session 3, weights fixed before issue, is what made the sensitivity analysis in the cleaning award meaningful."],[1292.7,1295.05,"The chain is not a metaphor."],[1295.05,1297.49,"It is how the work really fits together."],[1298.43,1300.68,"Here is the course in five lines."],[1300.68,1303.52,"These are also the summary for your capstone panel."],[1303.52,1305.58,"Let's go through them one by one."],[1306.08,1306.56,"One."],[1306.56,1309.67,"Analyse the category and the power position first."],[1309.67,1314.67,"Spend, need, market, Kraljic, supplier preferencing and the Chessboard."],[1314.67,1320.31,"Your leverage is set before any event begins, and you can only use it if you know it."],[1320.81,1321.35,"Two."],[1321.35,1326.26,"Choose the strategy, then the tool: tender, panel, negotiation or auction."],[1326.26,1332.55,"The six decisions in the strategy paper come before the choice of RFx or auction."],[1332.55,1336.4,"As we said in Session 2, the tool follows the strategy."],[1336.9,1337.5,"Three."],[1337.5,1342.76,"Run fair, comparable events, and auction only when the preconditions hold."],[1342.76,1349.02,"Weights fixed before issue, a draft contract attached to the RFP, abnormally low bids checked."],[1349.3,1358.9,"And, from Session 3, an e-auction only for well-specified categories with enough qualified bidders, never for complex, quality-sensitive services."],[1359.4,1359.44,"Four."],[1359.44,1362.87,"Negotiate on interests, data and an honest BATNA."],[1362.87,1368.31,"That is Fisher and Ury's lesson, and the Kelvar role-play in this session."],[1368.31,1375.38,"Prepare on one page, find the other side's interests with good questions, and know your walk-away before you sit down."],[1375.88,1376.53,"Five."],[1376.53,1381.03,"Contract for regulation and value, and hand over so the contract is used."],[1381.03,1385.77,"Price mechanism, KPIs, term and exit to protect the deal."],[1385.77,1389.37,"Solvency II and DORA clauses in the draft, not in the negotiation."],[1389.37,1397.87,"And a handover with a named owner, a buying channel, savings agreed with Finance and a renewal decision date."],[1398.37,1404.94,"If you can say those five lines with an example from your own Sourcing Case for each one, you are ready for the capstone."],[1405.89,1407.46,"And the final statement."],[1407.46,1410.48,"Good sourcing is a chain of good decisions."],[1410.48,1411.71,"Make each one on purpose."],[1411.71,1417.01,"Not by habit, not because the tool offers it, not because the contract rolled over."],[1417.01,1422.51,"On purpose, with the evidence in front of you and the business owner beside you."],[1423.01,1435.04,"Over these four sessions you have collected the tools: the matrices, the strategy paper, the evaluation model, the negotiation one-pager, the regulatory checklist and the handover plan."],[1435.04,1436.3,"None of them is the point."],[1436.3,1441.98,"Each one exists to help you make one decision well, and to show others why you made it."],[1442.26,1448.72,"That is what the capstone panel will look for, and what your business owners will notice long after the course is over."],[1449.22,1452.06,"Thank you for your work across these four sessions."],[1452.06,1458.71,"Complete Fieldwork 4, rehearse with your trio, check your capstone date and panel, and bring your Sourcing Case."],[1458.71,1460.33,"See you at the capstone."]]}]};
